(window.LH_SEARCH = window.LH_SEARCH || {})["javascript"] = {"s":"javascript","t":[["js-getting-started","Getting Started with JavaScript","1.1"],["js-how-it-runs","How a JavaScript Program Runs Inside the Engine","1.2"],["js-values-types","Variables, Types and Operators","1.3"],["js-control-flow","Control Flow, Loops and Errors","1.4"],["js-functions","Functions, Scope and Closures","1.5"],["js-objects-arrays","Objects and Arrays","2.1"],["js-strings-dates-json","Strings, Regex, Numbers, Dates and JSON","2.2"],["js-collections-iterators","Map, Set, Iterators and Generators","2.3"],["js-oop","Prototypes, Classes and the Page Object Model","3.1"],["js-modules-tooling","Modules, npm, TypeScript and Tooling","3.2"],["js-async","Asynchronous JavaScript: Promises, async and await","4.1"],["js-dom-browser","The DOM and the Browser","4.2"],["js-http-node","HTTP, fetch and Node.js Servers","4.3"],["js-engine","How JavaScript really works","5.1"],["js-errors-debugging","Errors, debugging and logging","5.2"],["js-regex","Regular expressions in depth","5.3"],["js-functional","Functional programming in JavaScript","5.4"],["js-metaprogramming","Objects in depth and metaprogramming","5.5"],["js-node-deep","Node.js in depth","5.6"],["js-unit-testing","Unit Testing: node:test, Jest, Vitest and Mocha","6.1"],["js-api-testing","Automated API Testing with JavaScript","6.2"],["js-ui-automation","UI Automation: Playwright, Selenium, Cypress and WebdriverIO","6.3"],["ts-basics","TypeScript fundamentals","7.1"],["ts-advanced","Advanced TypeScript types","7.2"],["ts-projects","TypeScript classes and real projects","7.3"],["ts-testing","Test automation in TypeScript","7.4"],["ts-type-system","How the TypeScript type system decides","7.5"],["ts-config-modules","TypeScript project setup: tsconfig, modules and builds","7.6"],["ts-whats-new","What is new in TypeScript: 5.0 to 7.0","7.7"],["js-modern","Modern JavaScript: ES2020 to ES2027","8.1"],["js-expert","Expert JavaScript: performance, security and test quality","8.2"],["cheatsheet","Cheatsheet","📄"],["interview-questions","Interview Questions","🎤"]],"c":[[0,"","Getting Started with JavaScript","What JavaScript is and where it runs, ECMAScript and TC39, engines and runtimes, installing Node.js and VS Code, your first program, the console toolbox, script loading with defer and async, strict mode and how to read errors and stack traces. · JavaScript · introduction · ECMAScript · Node.js · console · DevTools · strict mode · error messages · stack trace · script tag · first program 🟨 JavaScript›🌱 Foundations›Chapter 1 of 31 🚀 Step 1 · Topic 1 of 5 · Beginner"],[0,"top","","What JavaScript is, where it runs, how to set up Node.js, how to print with the console, how a page loads scripts and how to read the errors you will meet every day. ⏱️ About 14 min read📚 8 parts🎬 3 animations▶ 11 runnable examples🧪 1 real test run🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 6 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Parts marked 🧪 QA essential are what you need before writing Playwright, Cypress, WebdriverIO or Selenium tests in JavaScript. Press \"Show the QA path only\" to hide the rest. 1What JavaScript is and where it runs 2Setting up Node.js and an editor 3Your first program 4The console toolbox 5JavaScript in a web page 6How the engine runs your code 7Reading error messages 8Real world scenario: summarise a test run Lesson 1 of 8"],[0,"what-is-js","What JavaScript is and where it runs","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypressSeleniumWebdriverIOMost modern UI and API automation tools are written in JavaScript or TypeScript, and their tests are too. JavaScript is the programming language of the web. Every modern browser has a JavaScript engine built in, so the same code runs on a phone, a laptop and a smart TV. Outside the browser, Node.js, Deno and Bun run JavaScript on servers, laptops and CI machines. 🗺️ Diagram"],[0,"what-is-js","One language, many places to run","Click a box to read what it does. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram Click any box with a dotted outline to learn more about it. Name What it is ECMAScript The official standard. JavaScript is the language that follows it. A new edition comes out every June: ES2015 (also called ES6), ES2016 and so on up to ES2026. TC39 The committee that decides new features. A proposal moves through stages 0 to 4 and joins the standard at stage 4. Engine The program that runs the code: V8 (Chrome, Edge, Node.js, Deno), SpiderMonkey (Firefox) and JavaScriptCore (Safari, Bun). Runtime An engine plus extra tools. A browser adds the page (DOM), fetch and storage. Node.js adds files, network servers and processes. 🛠️ Developers use it forweb pages, React or Angular apps, servers and APIs with Node.js, mobile apps with React Native and desktop apps with Electron. 🧪 QA engineers use it forUI automation with Playwright, Cypress, WebdriverIO and Selenium WebDriver, API tests, unit tests with Jest or Vitest, and small scripts that create test data or read reports. JavaScript has nothing to do with Java. The name was picked in 1995 for marketing, and the language was written in about ten days by Brendan Eich. Lesson 2 of 8"],[0,"setup","Setting up Node.js and an editor","🧪 QA essential To follow along on your own machine you need two things: Node.js and a code editor. Every example on this page also runs inside your browser with the ✏️ Edit and run button, so you can start right now. Install Node.js from nodejs.org. Pick the LTS (long term support) release for work projects. The Current release has the newest features. Install Visual Studio Code, the editor most JavaScript teams use. Its built in terminal opens with Ctrl + `. Open a terminal and check that both node and npm answer. Check your setup in a terminal · Terminal commands node -v # which Node.js is installednpm -v # npm comes with Node.jsnode# opens the REPL, type .exit to leavenode hello.js # runs a file What happens node -v prints a version such as v26.12.0. Every example on this page was run with that version. Teams that work on several projects often need several Node.js versions. A version manager such as nvm switches between them. Several Node.js versions side by side · Terminal commands # macOS and Linux: install nvm once, then any Node.js version you need nvm install --lts # the long term support release, the safe choice for projects nvm install 26# the newest release line, used on this page nvm use 26node -v # Windows: use the installer from nodejs.org or \"nvm-windows\" A project can pin its Node.js version in a file called .nvmrc or in the engines field of package.json, so every developer and every CI machine uses the same one. Playwright, Cypress and WebdriverIO all need Node.js. Before you install any of them, check node -v against the version the tool supports. A test that passes on your laptop and fails in CI is often just a different Node.js version. Lesson 3 of 8"],[0,"first-program","Your first program","A JavaScript program is a list of statements that run from top to bottom. console.log prints whatever you give it. Several values are printed with a space between them. Your first program · ▶ Runs console.log(\"Hello, Brainy Bytes!\"); console.log(\"2 + 3 =\", 2 + 3); console.log(`Today we learn ${\"JavaScript\"}`); Output Hello, Brainy Bytes! 2 + 3 = 5 Today we learn JavaScript The text inside backticks is a template literal. Anything inside ${ } is worked out and placed into the text. You will use it constantly for messages and test names."],[0,"first-program","Comments and semicolons","Statements, comments and semicolons · ▶ Runs // A single line comment explains the next linelet price = 250; // a comment can also sit at the end of a line/* A block comment can span many lines */let qty = 3; console.log(price * qty) // no semicolon here, JavaScript adds one for us console.log(\"done\"); Output 750 done Semicolons end statements. JavaScript has automatic semicolon insertion (ASI), so leaving them out usually works. Most teams pick one style and let a formatter such as Prettier enforce it. The one place automatic semicolons bite · ▶ Runs function getUser() { return { name: \"Asha\" }; } console.log(getUser()); Output undefined A line break right after return makes JavaScript insert a semicolon there. The function returns undefined and the object below is never reached. Always start the returned value on the same line as return. Lesson 4 of 8"],[0,"console","The console toolbox","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypressJest The console is your first debugging tool and the place where test runners print what they find. It can do far more than log. The console toolbox · ▶ Runs console.log(\"log is for normal messages\"); console.info(\"info looks the same in Node\"); console.warn(\"warn goes to the error stream\"); console.error(\"error also goes to the error stream\"); console.log(\"%s scored %d points\", \"Ravi\", 42); console.table([ { test: \"login\", status: \"passed\", ms: 812 }, { test: \"checkout\", status: \"failed\", ms: 2310 }, ]); console.group(\"Suite: cart\"); console.log(\"adds an item\"); console.log(\"removes an item\"); console.groupEnd(); console.count(\"retry\"); console.count(\"retry\"); console.assert(1 + 1 === 3, \"maths is broken\"); Output log is for normal messages info looks the same in Node warn goes to the error stream error also goes to the error stream Ravi scored 42 points ┌─────────┬────────────┬──────────┬──────┐ │ (index) │ test │ status │ ms │ ├─────────┼────────────┼──────────┼──────┤ │ 0 │ 'login' │ 'passed' │ 812 │ │ 1 │ 'checkout' │ 'failed' │ 2310 │ └─────────┴────────────┴──────────┴──────┘ Suite: cart adds an item removes an item retry: 1 retry: 2 Assertion failed: maths is broken Method Use it for log, info normal messages, written to standard output warn, error problems, written to the error stream, shown in yellow and red in the browser table arrays of objects shown as a table, perfect for test data group, groupEnd indenting related lines, like a suite and its tests count how many times a line ran, for example retries assert prints an error only when the condition is false time, timeEnd how long a piece of code took Measuring how long something takes · ▶ Runs console.time(\"build list\"); const items = []; for (let i = 0; i < 100000; i++) items.push(i * 2); console.timeEnd(\"build list\"); console.log(\"items:\", items.length); Output build list: 2.481ms items: 100000 When a Playwright or Cypress test fails, add a console.log of the value you expected and the value you got. Playwright shows test output in its report, and in Cypress the browser console shows it. For timings, prefer the runner's own reports over console.time. Lesson 5 of 8"],[0,"browser","JavaScript in a web page","🧪 QA essentialSeleniumPlaywrightCypressLocators are CSS or XPath selectors, and executeScript or evaluate send JavaScript into the page. In a browser, JavaScript can read and change the page. The page is a tree of objects called the DOM (Document Object Model). document.querySelector finds an element with a CSS selector, the same kind of selector a test tool uses to find a button. JavaScript changes the page · ▶ Runs in the browser 📄 page.html <!doctype html> <html> <head><title>Shop</title></head> <body> <h1id=\"title\">Welcome</h1> <buttonid=\"buy\">Buy now</button> <pid=\"msg\"></p> </body> </html> 📄 script.js const title = document.querySelector(\"#title\"); console.log(\"Before:\", title.textContent); title.textContent = \"Welcome back, Asha\"; console.log(\"After:\", title.textContent); document.querySelector(\"#buy\").addEventListener(\"click\", () => { document.querySelector(\"#msg\").textContent = \"Added to cart\"; console.log(\"Clicked!\"); }); document.querySelector(\"#buy\").click(); console.log(\"Message:\", document.querySelector(\"#msg\").textContent); Output Before: Welcome After: Welcome back, Asha Clicked! Message: Added to cart A page loads JavaScript with a <script> tag. Where and how the tag is written decides when the code runs. Four ways to load a script · HTML <!-- Classic script: blocks the HTML parser while it downloads and runs --> <scriptsrc=\"app.js\"></script> <!-- defer: downloads in parallel, runs in order after the HTML is parsed --> <scriptdefersrc=\"app.js\"></script> <!-- async: downloads in parallel, runs as soon as it arrives, in any order --> <scriptasyncsrc=\"analytics.js\"></script> <!-- module: deferred by default, strict mode, can use import and export --> <scripttype=\"module\"src=\"main.js\"></script> 🎬 Animated diagram"],[0,"browser","When each kind of script runs","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 2 Every browser has DevTools (F12). Its Console tab runs any JavaScript you type against the open page. Testers use it to try a selector before putting it in a test: document.querySelectorAll(\"button.buy\").length tells you at once whether the selector is unique. Test tools send JavaScript into the page · Snippets, full runs come in chapter 22 // Selenium WebDriver for JavaScriptconst title = await driver.executeScript(\"return document.title\"); // Playwrightconst count = await page.evaluate(() => document.querySelectorAll(\".product\").length); // Cypress cy.window().then(win => win.localStorage.setItem(\"token\", \"abc\")); All three tools let you run your own JavaScript inside the page under test. That is how you read values the UI does not show, set up storage or check something a locator cannot reach. The code you send runs in the browser, so it can use document and window, but it cannot see the variables in your test file. Lesson 6 of 8"],[0,"how-it-runs","How the engine runs your code","Running a script happens in a few steps, and knowing them explains several surprises you will meet later. 🎬 Animated diagram"],[0,"how-it-runs","From source text to running program","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 6 Parse: the engine reads the whole file first. A typo anywhere is a SyntaxError and nothing runs at all. Compile: V8 turns the code into bytecode, then a JIT (just in time) compiler turns busy functions into fast machine code while the program runs. Execute: one line at a time on a single thread. JavaScript does one thing at a time. Waiting for a server or a timer is handed to the runtime, which is how one thread stays responsive. Chapter 11 explains this event loop. This is the short version. Chapter 2 follows a program through every step in depth, with the real bytecode V8 makes, a trace of the JIT compilers at work and two simulators where you type your own code."],[0,"how-it-runs","Strict mode","Strict mode turns silent mistakes into errors. Modules (type=\"module\" or .mjs files) and classes are always strict. Older scripts opt in with \"use strict\" on the first line. The same typo without strict mode · ▶ Runs let total = 0; totl = 10; // sloppy mode silently creates a global variable console.log(total, totl); Output 0 10 Strict mode catches a typo · 💥 Throws \"use strict\"; let total = 0; totl = 10; // a typo creates a new global in sloppy mode, strict mode stops it console.log(total); Output strict.cjs:3 totl = 10; // a typo creates a new global in sloppy mode, strict mode stops it ^ ReferenceError: totl is not defined at Object.<anonymous> (strict.cjs:3:6) Node.js v26.12.1 Write new code as modules and you get strict mode for free. Every .mjs example on this page is strict. Lesson 7 of 8"],[0,"errors","Reading error messages","🧪 QA essentialJestPlaywrightCypressEvery failed test hands you an error message and a stack trace to read. Errors are not a failure on your part. They are the fastest way to learn what the program really did. Every error has a name, a message and a stack trace that lists where it happened. Reading an error message · 💥 Throws const user = { name: \"Asha\" }; console.log(user.name.toUpperCase()); console.log(user.address.city); Output ASHA main.mjs:3 console.log(user.address.city); ^ TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'city') at main.mjs:3:26 Node.js v26.12.1 Read the name: TypeError means a value was the wrong type for what you did with it. Here user.address is undefined, and you cannot read city from it. Read the first stack line that points into your own file. main.mjs:3 is file, line and column. Look at that line and ask which value was missing. Then print it. Error Usual cause SyntaxError The code is not valid JavaScript: a missing bracket or quote. Nothing in the file runs. ReferenceError A name that does not exist, often a typo, or a variable used before let declared it. TypeError Reading a property of undefined or null, or calling something that is not a function. RangeError A number out of range, such as new Array(-1), or endless recursion. A try and catch block lets the program handle the error and carry on. Chapter 4 goes much deeper. Catching the error instead of crashing · ▶ Runs const cart = []; try { cart.push({ id: 1, price: 99 }); console.log(cart[0].price.toFixed(2)); console.log(cart[1].price); } catch (err) { console.log(\"Caught:\", err.name); console.log(\"Message:\", err.message); } console.log(\"The program keeps going\"); Output 99.00 Caught: TypeError Message: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'price') The program keeps going A failing test prints exactly this kind of stack. \"Cannot read properties of undefined\" inside a test usually means a locator found nothing or an API response did not have the field you expected. Read the line number, then log the value. Lesson 8 of 8"],[0,"scenario","Real world scenario: summarise a test run","🧪 QA essentialnode:testReading results and reporting them is daily QA work, and node:test is the simplest way to start testing. The situationYour team's nightly run produces a list of results. The lead wants a short summary in the chat every morning: how many passed, how many failed, the total time and the names of the failures. The script below does exactly that with only what this chapter covered: an array of objects, a loop, a template literal and the console. Summarise a test run · ▶ Runs const results = [ { name: \"login works\", status: \"passed\", ms: 812 }, { name: \"wrong password shows error\", status: \"passed\", ms: 640 }, { name: \"checkout total\", status: \"failed\", ms: 2310 }, { name: \"coupon applies\", status: \"skipped\", ms: 0 }, { name: \"logout\", status: \"passed\", ms: 301 }, ]; const summary = { passed: 0, failed: 0, skipped: 0 }; let totalMs = 0; for (const r of results) { summary[r.status]++; totalMs += r.ms; } console.table(summary); console.log(`Total time: ${(totalMs / 1000).toFixed(2)} s`); const failed = results.filter(r => r.status === \"failed\").map(r => r.name); console.log(\"Failed:\", failed); Output ┌─────────┬────────┐ │ (index) │ Values │ ├─────────┼────────┤ │ passed │ 3 │ │ failed │ 1 │ │ skipped │ 1 │ └─────────┴────────┘ Total time: 4.06 s Failed: [ 'checkout total' ] Real teams turn helpers like this into tested code. Node.js ships with a test runner, so a first automated check needs no extra installs. Run it with npm test. A first automated check with the built in test runner · 🧪 Tests run 📄 summary.js exportfunction summarize(results) { const summary = { passed: 0, failed: 0, skipped: 0 }; for (const r of results) summary[r.status]++; return summary; } 📄 summary.test.js import { test } from\"node:test\"; import assert from\"node:assert/strict\"; import { summarize } from\"./summary.js\"; test(\"counts each status\", () => { const got = summarize([{ status: \"passed\" }, { status: \"failed\" }, { status: \"passed\" }]); assert.deepEqual(got, { passed: 2, failed: 1, skipped: 0 }); }); test(\"an empty run has zero of everything\", () => { assert.deepEqual(summarize([]), { passed: 0, failed: 0, skipped: 0 }); }); Terminal $ npm test Terminal output > report@1.0.0 test > node --test ✔ counts each status (0.5ms) ✔ an empty run has zero of everything (0.5ms) ℹ tests 2 ℹ suites 0 ℹ pass 2 ℹ fail 0 ℹ cancelled 0 ℹ skipped 0 ℹ todo 0 ℹ duration_ms 0.5 Splitting the logic (summarize) from the printing makes it easy to test. The same idea, pure functions with the input and output kept separate, comes back in every chapter. 🧮"],[0,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 How many lines does this program print? console.log(\"a\"); console.log(\"b\", \"c\"); console.log(`d ${1 + 1}`); Count the console.log calls that run. Answer: 3 Each console.log call prints one line. There are three calls, so three lines. C2 What is the last line printed? for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) { console.count(\"retry\"); } console.count prints the label and how many times it has been called. Answer: retry: 3 Calls one, two and three print retry: 1, retry: 2 and retry: 3. C1 ES2015 is the 6th edition of ECMAScript (ES6). Which edition number is ES2026? One edition per year. Answer: 17 2026 - 2015 = 11 6 + 11 = 17 C2 What does this print? const a = [1, 2, 3]; console.log(`Total: ${a.length * 2} items`); Work out the template literal. Answer: Total: 6 items a.length is 3 and a.length * 2 is 6. The text becomes Total: 6 items. C1 A test suite has 40 tests that take 1.5 s each. They run one after another. How many seconds does the run take? Multiply. Answer: 60 40 × 1.5 = 60 C2 The same 40 tests are split over 4 parallel workers evenly. How many seconds now? Each worker gets a quarter of the tests. Answer: 15 40 ÷ 4 = 10 tests per worker 10 × 1.5 = 15 s C1 A suite replaces 25 fixed waits of 2000 ms with auto-waits that take 300 ms on average. How many seconds does one run save? Work out the saving per wait first. Answer: 42.5 2000 - 300 = 1700 ms saved per wait 25 × 1700 = 42500 ms 42500 ms = 42.5 s C2 What does this print? const x = [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]; console.assert(2 > 1, \"never shown\"); console.log(x.length); Only one line prints. Which value does it print? Answer: 3 console.assert prints only when its condition is false. 2 > 1 is true, so nothing prints. The last line prints x.length, which is 3. 🎯"],[0,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 Which program runs JavaScript outside the browser on servers and CI machines? It is built on the V8 engine. Node.js puts the V8 engine outside the browser and adds files, servers and processes. Deno and Bun do the same job. Q2 What does this print? console.log(2 + 3, 1); console.log separates its values with a space. console.log prints each value with one space between them, so you see 5 1. Q3 Which console method shows an array of objects as rows and columns? Think of a spreadsheet. console.table draws a table with one row per item and one column per property. Q1 What does this function return? function f() { return { name: \"Asha\" }; } console.log(f()); Look at what follows return on the same line. Automatic semicolon insertion puts a semicolon right after return, so the function returns undefined. Q2 Which script tag downloads in parallel and runs after the HTML is parsed, in document order? async runs in whatever order the files arrive. defer keeps the order and waits for parsing. Module scripts behave the same way by default. Q3 A test fails with \"TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'total')\". What is the most likely cause? Read the error name and the word after \"of\". The value before .total was undefined, for example an API response without the field you expected. Q1 In which kind of file is strict mode switched on without writing \"use strict\"? Modules were added in ES2015 with stricter rules. ES modules and class bodies are always strict. Classic scripts and CommonJS files need the directive. Q2 A file has a missing closing bracket on line 40. What happens to the console.log on line 1? When is the whole file read? The engine parses the entire file before running anything. A SyntaxError stops the file before line 1 runs. Q3 Inside page.evaluate in Playwright, which of these can the code use? The function is sent to the browser and run there. The callback is serialised and run inside the page, so it sees the page's globals. Values from the test must be passed as an argument. Q1 Which engine runs JavaScript in Node.js, Chrome and Deno? Google started it in 2008. V8 powers Chrome, Edge, Node.js and Deno. Firefox uses SpiderMonkey and Safari and Bun use JavaScriptCore. Q2 A proposal is at TC39 stage 3. What does that usually mean? Stage 4 means it is in the standard. Stage 3 means the design is final and implementations are under way. Stage 4 needs two shipping implementations and joins the next edition. Q3 Why do teams pin the Node.js version in .nvmrc or engines? Think about \"passes locally, fails in CI\". Different Node.js versions can behave differently or lack features. Pinning removes one source of tests that pass on one machine and fail on another."],[0,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[0,"greet","Say hello","Write greet(name) that returns the text Hello, NAME! using a template literal. Backticks and ${name}. function greet(name) { return `Hello, ${name}!`; } A template literal places the value of name straight into the text. 🌿 Medium"],[0,"seconds","Milliseconds to seconds","Test runners report time in milliseconds. Write toSeconds(ms) that returns a string with two decimals and an s, such as \"2.31 s\". toFixed(2) gives exactly two decimals. function toSeconds(ms) { return `${(ms / 1000).toFixed(2)} s`; } Divide by 1000 to get seconds, then toFixed(2) rounds and pads to two decimals. 🔥 Hard"],[0,"summary","Count the results","Write summarize(results). Each result has a status of \"passed\", \"failed\" or \"skipped\". Return an object with all three counts, even when a count is zero. Start with an object that already has all three keys set to 0. function summarize(results) { const out = { passed: 0, failed: 0, skipped: 0 }; for (const r of results) out[r.status]++; return out; } Starting with every key at zero means an empty run still reports all three counts. 🧠 Expert"],[0,"stack-line","Find the line in a stack trace","Write firstLine(stack, file). It gets the text of a stack trace and a file name, and returns the line number (a number) of the first frame in that file, or null when the file is not in the trace. A frame looks like at login (tests/login.spec.js:12:5). Split the text on \"\\n\", find the line that contains file + \":\", then take the number after it. function firstLine(stack, file) { for (const line of stack.split(\"\\n\")) { const at = line.indexOf(file + \":\"); if (at !== -1) { returnNumber(line.slice(at + file.length + 1).split(\":\")[0]); } } returnnull; } Each stack frame ends with file, line and column separated by colons. The first match is the frame closest to where the error was thrown."],[0,"stack-line","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. What JavaScript is and where it runsJavaScript is the programming language of the web. Every modern browser has a JavaScript engine built in, so the same code runs on a phone, a laptop and a smart TV. Outside the browser, Node.js, Deno and Bun run JavaScript on servers, laptops and CI machines. Setting up Node.js and an editorTo follow along on your own machine you need two things: Node.js and a code editor. Every example on this page also runs inside your browser with the ✏️ Edit and run button, so you can start right now. Your first programA JavaScript program is a list of statements that run from top to bottom. console.log prints whatever you give it. Several values are printed with a space between them. The console toolboxThe console is your first debugging tool and the place where test runners print what they find. It can do far more than log. JavaScript in a web pageIn a browser, JavaScript can read and change the page. The page is a tree of objects called the DOM (Document Object Model). document.querySelector finds an element with a CSS selector, the same kind of selector a test tool uses to find a button. How the engine runs your codeRunning a script happens in a few steps, and knowing them explains several surprises you will meet later.Remember: Write new code as modules and you get strict mode for free. Every .mjs example on this page is strict. Reading error messagesErrors are not a failure on your part. They are the fastest way to learn what the program really did. Every error has a name, a message and a stack trace that lists where it happened. Real world scenario: summarise a test runThe script below does exactly that with only what this chapter covered: an array of objects, a loop, a template literal and the console."],[0,"comic","From a text file to a working page","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Same language in the page, on the server and in the tests."],[0,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🛒An online shop When you change the quantity in a cart, JavaScript in the page recalculates the total without reloading. 🔧A build pipeline Node.js scripts run linters, bundle the code and start the test suite on every pull request. 🤖Nightly regression A Playwright suite written in TypeScript logs in, buys a product and checks the receipt in three browsers. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Getting Started with JavaScript\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 10Welcome Welcome to JavaScript. This chapter shows where it runs, how to set it up, how to print and how to read errors. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[1,"","How a JavaScript Program Runs Inside the Engine","The full journey of a JavaScript program compared with Java and Python: engine and runtime, loading and the CommonJS wrapper, scanning into tokens, parsing into an AST with early errors and lazy parsing, the creation phase and the temporal dead zone, real V8 Ignition bytecode, the call stack, the heap and garbage collection, the JIT tiers with a real deoptimisation trace, the event loop with a simulator, module construction, linking and evaluation, and a frozen page caused by a long task. · how JavaScript runs · JavaScript execution · JavaScript internals · engine · runtime · V8 · scanner · tokens · parser · AST · bytecode · Ignition · Sparkplug · Maglev · TurboFan · JIT · deoptimisation · call stack · heap · garbage collection · event loop · hoisting · execution context · module linking · interview 🟨 JavaScript›🌱 Foundations›Chapter 2 of 31 ⚙️ Step 1 · Topic 2 of 5 · Beginner"],[1,"top","","Follow one program from a text file to the output: loading, the scanner and its tokens, the parser and its syntax tree, the creation phase and hoisting, Ignition bytecode, the call stack and the heap, the JIT compilers Sparkplug, Maglev and TurboFan, the event loop and how modules are linked. Type your own code into two step by step simulators. ⏱️ About 25 min read📚 11 parts🎬 5 animations▶ 13 runnable examples🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 4 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Testers who know these steps can tell a syntax error that stops a whole spec file from a runtime error in one test, explain odd log orders, and describe a frozen page with evidence instead of \"it feels slow\". 1The journey of a JavaScript program 2Step 1: loading the source text 3Step 2: the scanner cuts text into tokens 4Step 3: the parser builds a syntax tree 5Step 4: scopes and the creation phase 6Step 5: Ignition turns the tree into bytecode 7Step 6: running: the call stack, the heap and garbage collection 8Step 7: hot code gets compiled: Sparkplug, Maglev and TurboFan 9Step 8: the runtime, Web APIs and the event loop 10Modules: load, link, then evaluate 11Real world scenario: a frozen page on sale day Lesson 1 of 11"],[1,"big-picture","The journey of a JavaScript program","When you press Run, your .js file is only text. Two programs work together to turn that text into output. The engine understands the JavaScript language. The runtime (also called the host) is the program the engine lives inside, such as Chrome or Node.js, and it adds everything the language itself does not have: timers, the network, files and the web page. Piece What it does Real examples Engine Scans, parses, compiles and runs the code, and cleans up memory V8 (Chrome, Edge, Node.js, Deno), SpiderMonkey (Firefox), JavaScriptCore (Safari, Bun) Runtime Loads files, provides the event loop and the extra APIs A browser tab, Node.js, Deno, Bun, Electron apps, Cloudflare Workers Extras from a browser document, fetch, setTimeout, localStorage, events The Web APIs Extras from Node.js fs, http, process, setTimeout, fetch Built on the libuv library 🎬 Animated diagram"],[1,"big-picture","The whole journey of a JavaScript program","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 11 Which engine and runtime is running this page · ▶ Runs // Node.js is a runtime. Inside it sits the V8 engine and the libuv library.const { node, v8, uv } = process.versions; console.log(\"runtime Node.js\", node); console.log(\"engine V8\", v8.split(\"-\")[0]); console.log(\"loop/io libuv\", uv); // The engine gives the language. The runtime adds the extras. console.log(typeofArray, typeofPromise, typeofJSON); // from the engine console.log(typeof setTimeout, typeof fetch, typeof process); // from the runtime console.log(typeof document); // only a browser runtime has a page Output runtime Node.js 26.12.1 engine V8 14.6.202.34 loop/io libuv 1.52.1 function function object function function object undefined Think about what happens when you open a shopping site on your phone. Chrome downloads the page and its scripts, V8 turns those scripts into running code in a few milliseconds, and the same V8 keeps optimising the code that runs most, such as the function that recalculates your cart. When you tap \"Add to cart\", the browser notices the tap, and the event loop hands your click handler to V8 to run. Every part of this chapter is one step of that story. Step Java JavaScript Who compiles first You run javac, which writes a .class file of bytecode Nobody. You ship the source text and the engine compiles it when it loads What runs it The JVM: class loader, bytecode verifier, interpreter, JIT, garbage collector The engine: parser, Ignition interpreter, Sparkplug, Maglev and TurboFan, garbage collector When errors show up Type and syntax errors at compile time Syntax errors when the file is parsed, type errors only when the line runs Threads Many threads by default One thread per page or process, plus an event loop. Workers add more threads. Python for comparison CPython compiles your .py to bytecode (.pyc) and interprets it. Since Python 3.13 it can also try an experimental JIT. You never call the compiler in JavaScript, but it is still there. V8 compiles in the background while your page runs. That is why the same code can get faster after it has run a few thousand times. Lesson 2 of 11"],[1,"loading","Step 1: loading the source text","The engine cannot read files or download anything itself. The runtime fetches the text and hands it over. In a browser, the HTML parser meets a <script> tag and downloads the file. A classic script blocks the page while it downloads and runs, defer and type=\"module\" scripts wait until the HTML is parsed (chapter 1 shows the timeline). In Node.js, node app.js reads the file from disk. The file extension and the \"type\" field in package.json decide whether it is an ES module or a CommonJS file. For an ES module, the runtime also finds and loads every file it imports before running any of them. That is covered in the modules part below. A CommonJS file gets a small surprise first: Node.js wraps your whole file in a function. That is why require, module and __dirname exist without being declared, and why a top level return works in a .cjs file but not in a .mjs one. The invisible wrapper around a CommonJS file · ▶ Runs // Node.js wraps every CommonJS file in a function before the engine sees it.// That is where require, module, exports, __filename and __dirname come from.const { wrapper } = require(\"node:module\"); console.log(wrapper[0] + \"...your file...\" + wrapper[1]); console.log(typeof require, typeof module, typeof exports); console.log(__filename.endsWith(\"main.cjs\")); console.log(\"arguments of the wrapper:\", arguments.length); Output (function (exports, require, module, __filename, __dirname) { ...your file... }); function object object true arguments of the wrapper: 5 Big websites ship one or a few bundled files instead of hundreds of small ones. Bundlers such as Vite, esbuild and webpack join and shrink the files so the loading step takes fewer network trips. Lesson 3 of 11"],[1,"scanning","Step 2: the scanner cuts text into tokens","The first thing the engine does with the text is scanning (also called lexing or tokenising). It reads the characters from left to right and groups them into tokens, the smallest pieces that mean something. Token kind Examples from let total = price * 2; // tax Keyword let Identifier (name) total, price Punctuator (operator) =, *, ; Literal 2, and strings such as \"pen\" Thrown away spaces, line breaks (it remembers where they were) and the comment // tax Try it yourself below. Type a few lines and press the button. The animation walks your own code through scanning, parsing, the creation phase, bytecode and running, the same five steps this chapter explains one by one. 🎬 Try it"],[1,"scanning","Watch the engine run your code","Pick an example or type your own lines (let, const, var, numbers, strings, + - * / %, brackets and console.log). Press Play or step with the arrows. 1 Scan 2 Parse 3 Create scope 4 Bytecode 5 Run 📝 Source let price = 250; let qty = 3; const total = price * qty + 40; console.log(\"Total:\", total); 🔤 Tokens 🌳 Syntax tree (AST) 🧠 Global scope nothing yet Accumulator and registers used while running ⚙️ Bytecode 🖥️ Console Press ▶ Play to send the code through the engine. 0 / 34 A teaching model of V8's pipeline for a small part of the language. The instruction names follow V8's Ignition bytecode, and real V8 also adds feedback slots and treats top level let slightly differently. The scanner always takes the longest token it can. That rule explains some puzzles you may meet in quizzes and code reviews. What the scanner accepts and rejects · ▶ Runs import vm from\"node:vm\"; // vm.Script only scans and parses. Nothing runs unless we ask.const samples = [ \"1.toString()\", // the scanner reads \"1.\" as one number token, then sees a name\"1..toString()\", // \"1.\" is the number, \".toString\" is the property\"1 .toString()\", // the space ends the number token\"let total = 1_000_000\", \"let 2fast = 1\", // a name may not start with a digit\"let price = 10 // a comment is thrown away\", ]; for (const src of samples) { try { new vm.Script(src); console.log(\"ok \", src.padEnd(24), \"->\", vm.runInNewContext(src)); } catch (e) { console.log(\"rejected \", src.padEnd(24), \"->\", e.name + \":\", e.message); } } Output rejected 1.toString() -> SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token ok 1..toString() -> 1 ok 1 .toString() -> 1 ok let total = 1_000_000 -> undefined rejected let 2fast = 1 -> SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token ok let price = 10 // a comment is thrown away -> undefined Line breaks matter to the scanner because of automatic semicolon insertion. If a line ends where a statement could end, a semicolon is added. That is why return on its own line followed by an object returns undefined (chapter 1 shows it). Lesson 4 of 11"],[1,"parsing","Step 3: the parser builds a syntax tree","🧪 QA essentialA single syntax error makes a whole spec file report zero tests, and knowing early errors helps you read the message fast. The parser reads the tokens and checks them against the grammar of JavaScript. If they fit, it builds an abstract syntax tree (AST): a tree of nodes such as VariableDeclaration, BinaryExpression and CallExpression. The tree keeps the meaning and drops the noise. In price * qty + 40 the multiply sits lower in the tree than the add, so it is worked out first. If the tokens do not fit the grammar, the parser throws a SyntaxError and the whole file is rejected. Not even line 1 runs. One typo on line 4 stops line 1 too · 💥 Throws console.log(\"line 1: this never prints\"); const user = { name: \"Asha\" }; console.log(\"line 3: neither does this\"); if (user.name === \"Asha\" { console.log(\"hello\"); } Output main.mjs:4 if (user.name === \"Asha\" { ^ SyntaxError: Unexpected token '{' Node.js v26.12.1 Some mistakes are grammatically fine but forbidden anyway. The specification calls them early errors, and the parser reports them before anything runs too: declaring the same let twice, a const with no value, or a return outside a function. Other problems, such as calling a function that does not exist, can only be found when the line actually runs. Early errors against runtime errors · ▶ Runs import vm from\"node:vm\"; // Early errors: the parser finds these before running anythingconst early = [ \"let a = 1; let a = 2;\", \"const limit;\", \"return 5\", \"function f() { 'use strict'; with (Math) {} }\", ]; for (const src of early) { try { new vm.Script(src); console.log(\"parsed \", src); } catch (e) { console.log(\"early \", src.padEnd(42), e.message); } } // Errors that only appear when the line runsconst late = new vm.Script(\"console.log('before'); missingFn(); console.log('after');\"); try { late.runInNewContext({ console }); } catch (e) { console.log(\"runtime \", e.name + \":\", e.message); } Output early let a = 1; let a = 2; Identifier 'a' has already been declared early const limit; Missing initializer in const declaration early return 5 Illegal return statement early function f() { 'use strict'; with (Math) {} } Strict mode code may not include a with statement before runtime ReferenceError: missingFn is not defined"],[1,"parsing","Lazy parsing","Parsing a big web app fully at start up would be slow, so V8 pre-parses functions it has not needed yet. It only checks their grammar and remembers where they are. The full parse happens the first time the function is called. Functions wrapped in brackets, such as (function () { ... })(), are parsed fully at once because V8 guesses they run immediately. A spec file with one missing bracket fails with a SyntaxError and zero tests run, so the report may show \"0 tests\" instead of a red test. Run node --check file.js or your linter in CI to catch it before the test run starts. Lesson 5 of 11"],[1,"creation","Step 4: scopes and the creation phase","Before a script or a function runs, the engine sets up an execution context for it. This happens in two phases. Creation phase. The engine creates the scope and a slot for every name declared in it. Function declarations are created whole. var names start as undefined. let, const and class names exist but are uninitialised: reading them throws, which is called the temporal dead zone (TDZ). The value of this is decided here too. Execution phase. The code runs line by line, giving variables their values and calling functions. Each function call repeats both phases for that function. 🎬 Try it"],[1,"creation","Creation phase, then execution phase","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. console.log(total, typeof applyTax); var total = 100; let rate = 0.18; function applyTax(t) { return t + t * rate; } console.log(applyTax(total)); Phase Global scope (memory) Call stack Console Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 7 The creation phase, then the execution phase · ▶ Runs console.log(total, typeof applyTax); // the names already existvar total = 100; let rate = 0.18; function applyTax(t) { return t + t * rate; } console.log(applyTax(total)); try { console.log(gst); // let is in the temporal dead zone herelet gst = 5; } catch (e) { console.log(e.name + \":\", e.message); } // Every call makes a new execution context with its own variablesfunction greet(name) { const msg = `Hello ${name}`; return msg; } console.log(greet(\"Ravi\"), greet(\"Meena\"), typeof msg); Output undefined function 118 ReferenceError: Cannot access 'gst' before initialization Hello Ravi Hello Meena undefined Nothing is physically moved to the top of your file. \"Hoisting\" is simply the result of the creation phase: the names exist before their line runs. Chapter 14 goes further into scope chains, closures and the rules of this. Declaration After the creation phase Read before its line function f() {} the whole function works var x = 1 undefined gives undefined let x = 1, const x = 1 uninitialised (TDZ) ReferenceError class A {} uninitialised (TDZ) ReferenceError const f = () => {} uninitialised (TDZ) ReferenceError, so arrow helpers must be defined before use Lesson 6 of 11"],[1,"bytecode","Step 5: Ignition turns the tree into bytecode","V8 does not run the tree directly. Its interpreter, Ignition, first turns the tree into bytecode: a list of small, simple instructions. Bytecode is much smaller than machine code, so it starts fast and saves memory on phones. Ignition is a register machine with one special register, the accumulator. Most instructions read or write it. You can ask Node.js to print the real bytecode of a function with a V8 flag: The real bytecode V8 makes for one function · ▶ Runs function addTax(price, rate) { const tax = price * rate; return price + tax; } console.log(addTax(100, 0.18)); Terminal $ node --print-bytecode --print-bytecode-filter=addTax main.mjs Output [generated bytecode for function: addTax ] Bytecode length: 12 Parameter count 3 Register count 1 Frame size 8 45 S> 0x… @ 0 : 0b 04 Ldar a1 51 E> 0x… @ 2 : 42 03 00 Mul a0, FBV[0] 0x… @ 5 : d2 Star0 61 S> 0x… @ 6 : 0b f9 Ldar r0 74 E> 0x… @ 8 : 40 03 01 Add a0, FBV[1] 80 S> 0x… @ 11 : b7 Return Constant pool (size = 0) Handler Table (size = 0) Source Position Table (size = 12) 118 Instruction Meaning in plain words Ldar a1 Load argument 1 (rate) into the accumulator Mul a0, FBV[0] Multiply argument 0 (price) by the accumulator. FBV[0] is a feedback slot where V8 writes down the types it saw Star0 Store the accumulator in register r0 (that is tax) Ldar r0 Load r0 back into the accumulator Add a0, FBV[1] Add price to it, and record the types in feedback slot 1 Return Give the accumulator back to the caller The feedback slots are the important part. Every time the line runs, Ignition notes what it saw: \"always small whole numbers\" or \"a number and a string\". The optimising compilers in step 7 read these notes. The engine animation above uses the same instruction names, so you can watch the accumulator change for your own code. The exact bytecode changes between V8 versions, and the flag is a debugging tool, not something to use in production. The numbers on the left are positions in the source text, which is how a stack trace knows your line and column. Lesson 7 of 11"],[1,"execution","Step 6: running: the call stack, the heap and garbage collection","🧪 QA essentialReference copies explain test data that changes between tests, and reachability explains memory leaks in long test runs. While the bytecode runs, the engine uses two areas of memory. The call stack holds one frame for every function that has started and not yet returned: its arguments, its local variables and where to go back to. It is small and very fast. Too many nested calls give RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded. The heap holds objects, arrays, functions and long strings. Variables keep a reference (an address) to them. Copying the variable copies the reference, so two names can share one object. 🎬 Try it"],[1,"execution","The call stack and the heap","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. function makeOrder(id) { const items = [\"pen\"]; return { id, items }; } const qty = 2; const order = makeOrder(7); const copy = order; copy.id = 8; console.log(order.id, qty); Call stack Variables Heap Console Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 9 Values on the stack, objects on the heap · ▶ Runs function makeOrder(id) { const items = [\"pen\"]; return { id, items }; } let qty = 2; let copyQty = qty; // a number is copied copyQty = 5; const order = makeOrder(7); const copy = order; // an object is shared: both names point at one heap object copy.id = 8; console.log(order.id, qty, copyQty, order === copy); // The call stack has a limit. The heap is much larger.function depth(n) { try { return depth(n + 1); } catch { return n; } } console.log(\"stack frames before overflow > 5000:\", depth(0) > 5000); Output 8 2 5 true stack frames before overflow > 5000: true"],[1,"execution","Garbage collection","You never free memory yourself. The garbage collector keeps every object that can be reached from the roots (global variables and the variables on the stack) and frees the rest. V8's collector is called Orinoco. New objects start in a small young area that is cleaned very often, because most objects are thrown away quickly, like the temporary arrays a map creates. Objects that survive move to the old area, which is cleaned less often with mark and sweep. Most of the work happens on helper threads, so pauses stay short. Unreachable objects are collected · ▶ Runs // Run with --expose-gc so the example can ask V8 to collect garbage now.let cart = { items: newArray(100_000).fill(\"item\") }; const watch = newWeakRef(cart); // a weak reference does not keep the object aliveawaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, 0)); // let the current job finish first console.log(\"still reachable:\", watch.deref() !== undefined); cart = null; // the last strong reference is goneawaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, 0)); gc(); console.log(\"after gc:\", watch.deref()); Terminal $ node --expose-gc main.mjs Output still reachable: true after gc: undefined A memory leak in JavaScript means something still reaches an object you no longer need: a global array that keeps growing, a listener that was never removed, or a cache with no limit. In long UI test runs, watch the memory graph in Chrome DevTools or compare two heap snapshots (chapter 31). Lesson 8 of 11"],[1,"jit","Step 7: hot code gets compiled: Sparkplug, Maglev and TurboFan","Interpreting bytecode is quick to start but slower to run. So V8 watches which functions run often and compiles them to machine code while the program is running. This is just in time (JIT) compilation, and V8 has three compilers for it, each one slower to compile but producing faster code. Tier Used for How it works Ignition Everything, at first Interprets bytecode and collects type feedback Sparkplug Functions that are warm Turns bytecode into machine code almost one to one, no optimisation Maglev Functions that are hot Uses the feedback to make good machine code quickly TurboFan The hottest code, such as tight loops Uses the feedback to make the fastest code, and takes the longest to compile Optimised code is built on guesses. If lineTotal has only ever seen small whole numbers, the fast code handles only small whole numbers. When a string arrives, the guess is wrong, so V8 deoptimises: it throws the fast code away, continues in the bytecode and may optimise again later with the new knowledge. This run is real. Watch the V8 messages between the program's own lines: Watch V8 optimise and deoptimise a hot function (trace lines shortened) · ▶ Runs // A function the checkout page calls for every cart linefunction lineTotal(price, qty) { return price * qty; } let sum = 0; for (let i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) sum += lineTotal(i % 50, 2); // always numbers: the function gets hot console.log(\"sum of numbers:\", sum); console.log(\"a string sneaks in:\", lineTotal(\"12\", 2)); // the guess \"price is a small integer\" breaksfor (let i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) sum += lineTotal(i % 50, 2); console.log(\"done\"); Terminal $ node --trace-opt --trace-deopt --no-concurrent-recompilation main.mjs Output [V8] lineTotal is hot and stable, mark it for MAGLEV [V8] compile lineTotal with MAGLEV [V8] lineTotal now runs as MAGLEV machine code [V8] lineTotal is hot and stable, mark it for TURBOFAN_JS [V8] compile lineTotal with TURBOFAN_JS [V8] lineTotal now runs as TURBOFAN_JS machine code sum of numbers: 4900000 [V8] DEOPTIMISE lineTotal (deopt-eager), reason: not a Smi a string sneaks in: 24 [V8] lineTotal is hot and stable, mark it for MAGLEV [V8] compile lineTotal with MAGLEV [V8] lineTotal now runs as MAGLEV machine code [V8] lineTotal is hot and stable, mark it for TURBOFAN_JS [V8] compile lineTotal with TURBOFAN_JS [V8] lineTotal now runs as TURBOFAN_JS machine code done How to read the trace: \"hot and stable\" means the function ran often and its type feedback stopped changing. \"not a Smi\" means the value was not a Smi, V8's name for a small integer that fits directly in a register. This flag does not list Sparkplug compiles, which have a separate trace flag. Notice that the program still printed the right answers. Deoptimisation only costs speed, never correctness. In daily work this matters for code that runs millions of times, such as parsing a large log file or drawing a chart, and it is the reason for a simple habit: keep the types going into a hot function the same. Firefox and Safari use the same idea with different names. SpiderMonkey has a baseline interpreter, a baseline compiler and the Warp optimiser. JavaScriptCore has LLInt, Baseline, DFG and FTL. Lesson 9 of 11"],[1,"runtime","Step 8: the runtime, Web APIs and the event loop","🧪 QA essentialThe event loop explains missing await bugs, log orders and why fixed sleeps make tests slow and flaky. The engine runs one piece of code at a time on one thread. So how does a page wait for a server without freezing? It does not wait. A call such as setTimeout or fetch hands the waiting to the runtime, which does it outside JavaScript (the browser's timers and network code, or libuv in Node.js). When the waiting is over, the runtime puts a callback into a queue. The call stack runs the current job until it is empty. Then the microtask queue is emptied completely: promise callbacks, await continuations and queueMicrotask. Then the event loop takes one task from the task queue: a timer, a click, a network response. Then back to step 2. Between tasks, the browser may repaint the screen, roughly every 16 ms on a 60 Hz display. Write your own lines in the simulator and predict the console before you press Play. 🎬 Try it"],[1,"runtime","Event loop simulator","Pick an example or write your own with console.log, setTimeout, queueMicrotask and Promise.resolve().then. Then press Play. ⏱️ clock: 0 ms 📚 Call stack empty ⏲️ Web APIs (timers) empty ⚡ Microtask queue empty 📬 Task queue empty 🖥️ Console Press ▶ Play to watch each line move through the browser. 0 / 14 The same orders the simulator shows, run for real · ▶ Runs console.log(\"A sync\"); setTimeout(() => console.log(\"B timer\"), 0); Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"C promise\")); console.log(\"D sync\"); setTimeout(() => { console.log(\"E timer\"); Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"F promise inside the timer\")); }, 0); setTimeout(() => console.log(\"G second timer\"), 0); Output A sync D sync C promise B timer E timer F promise inside the timer G second timer Node.js follows the same idea and splits the task side into phases (timers, poll for I/O, check for setImmediate), which chapter 14 shows. Promises and async code get a full chapter in chapter 11. This order is why a test that forgets an await can finish and report \"passed\" before the check inside a .then has even run. It is also why Playwright's auto waiting works: it keeps giving the page turns of the event loop until the element is ready. Lesson 10 of 11"],[1,"modules","Modules: load, link, then evaluate","An ES module is not run the moment it is read. The runtime and the engine go through three phases for the whole graph of files. Construction: find, download and parse the entry file, read its import lines, then do the same for every imported file. No code runs yet. Linking: connect every import to the matching export. A misspelled export name fails here, before any code runs. Evaluation: run each module body once, dependencies first. A module imported by many files still runs only once. Modules: linked first, then evaluated in order · ▶ Runs 📄 logger.mjs console.log(\"logger.mjs runs first, it is a dependency\"); 📄 price.mjs import\"./logger.mjs\"; // already loaded, so it does not run twice console.log(\"price.mjs runs second\"); exportlet price = 100; exportfunction bump() { price += 10; } 📄 main.mjs console.log(\"main.mjs body runs last\"); import { price, bump } from\"./price.mjs\"; import\"./logger.mjs\"; console.log(\"price is\", price); bump(); console.log(\"price after bump is\", price); // a live binding, not a copy Terminal $ node main.mjs Output logger.mjs runs first, it is a dependency price.mjs runs second main.mjs body runs last price is 100 price after bump is 110 Two things in that output surprise people. The console.log at the top of main.mjs ran last, because imports are processed before the body. And price changed after bump(), because an import is a live binding to the exporting module's variable, not a copy of its value. CommonJS require works differently: it runs the file when the line is reached and returns a copy of module.exports. Top level await fits into the evaluation phase: a module that awaits pauses its own evaluation and the modules that depend on it, while other branches of the graph carry on. Lesson 11 of 11"],[1,"scenario","Real world scenario: a frozen page on sale day","🧪 QA essentialLong tasks are a common cause of slow, flaky UI tests and of performance bugs that testers report. The situationDuring a big sale, users report that the \"Apply coupon\" button does nothing for half a second after the cart loads. The code has no errors. A tester records the problem and a developer measures it. The cart runs a long synchronous calculation over every item. While it runs, the call stack is never empty, so the event loop cannot run click handlers, timers or painting. The code below measures this with a timer that should fire after 50 ms. A frozen page on sale day, and the fix · ▶ Runs // Sale day: the \"Apply coupon\" button stops responding for a moment.// A ticker that should fire every 50 ms shows how long the thread was busy.function heavyWork(ms) { const end = Date.now() + ms; while (Date.now() < end) {} // one long synchronous job } function measure(label, work) { returnnewPromise(resolve => { const start = Date.now(); setTimeout(() => { const late = Date.now() - start - 50; console.log(label.padEnd(24), \"timer was late by more than 200 ms:\", late > 200); resolve(); }, 50); work(); }); } await measure(\"blocking 400 ms\", () => heavyWork(400)); // The fix: split the work into small chunks and give the event loop a turn between themasyncfunction chunked(total, chunk) { for (let done = 0; done < total; done += chunk) { heavyWork(chunk); awaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, 0)); // let timers, clicks and painting run } } await measure(\"chunked 20 x 20 ms\", () => { chunked(400, 20); }); Output blocking 400 ms timer was late by more than 200 ms: true chunked 20 x 20 ms timer was late by more than 200 ms: false The fix does the same total work in small chunks and gives the event loop a turn between them. Clicks and paints now get in between the chunks, so the page feels responsive. In a browser, the newer scheduler.yield() does the same job in Chrome and Edge, and very heavy work can move to a Web Worker on another thread (chapter 31). Lighthouse and the Performance panel in DevTools mark any task over 50 ms as a long task, and the Interaction to Next Paint (INP) metric shows how long users waited after a tap. Both are good evidence to attach to a bug report about a slow page. 🧮"],[1,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 How many tokens does the scanner make from this line? Count every keyword, name, number and symbol. let total = price * 2; // tax Comments and spaces are not tokens. Answer: 7 let, total, =, price, *, 2 and the semicolon at the end. That is 7 tokens. C2 The bytecode for addTax had a \"Bytecode length\" of 12 bytes. Ldar takes 2 bytes, Mul 3, Star0 1, Ldar 2, Add 3. How many bytes does Return take? Subtract the others from 12. Answer: 1 2 + 3 + 1 + 2 + 3 = 11 12 − 11 = 1 C1 What does addTax(250, 0.2) return? tax is price times rate. Answer: 300 tax = 250 × 0.2 = 50 250 + 50 = 300 C2 A page runs 3 synchronous logs, 2 promise callbacks and 4 timers. How many lines print before the first timer? Sync first, then all microtasks. Answer: 5 3 + 2 = 5 C3 A display refreshes 60 times a second. About how many whole milliseconds does each frame get? One second is 1000 ms. Answer: 16 1000 ÷ 60 ≈ 16.7 So about 16 whole ms, which is why long tasks cause dropped frames. C1 A 400 ms job is split into 20 ms chunks. How many times does the event loop get a turn between chunks? Count the gaps between chunks. Answer: 19 400 ÷ 20 = 20 chunks There are 20 − 1 = 19 gaps between them. C2 makeOrder runs, creates an array and an object, and returns the object into order. After the call, how many heap objects are still reachable? The object points at the array. Answer: 2 order reaches the object, and the object reaches the array. Both stay alive: 2. C1 A module graph: main imports a and b, a imports c, b imports c. How many module bodies run in total? Each module runs once. Answer: 4 c, a, b and main each run once, even though c is imported twice. That is 4. 🎯"],[1,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 Which part turns your JavaScript text into running code? Chrome and Node.js both contain it. The engine scans, parses, compiles and runs. The runtime around it loads files and adds timers, the network and the page. Q2 A file has a typo on line 40. What happens to line 1? Parsing comes before running. The parser reads the whole file first. A SyntaxError anywhere means nothing in the file runs. Q3 What does the scanner produce? Small pieces such as let, price and =. Tokens go to the parser, which builds the syntax tree. Q1 Where do require and __dirname come from in a CommonJS file? Look at the wrapper example. The module wrapper is (function (exports, require, module, __filename, __dirname) { ... }). Q2 What does the first line print? console.log(total); var total = 100; var is set to undefined in the creation phase. The slot for total exists from the creation phase, but its value 100 is only assigned when line 2 runs. Q3 What is the accumulator in V8 bytecode? Ldar loads it, Star stores it. Ignition is a register machine with one implicit accumulator register. Q4 Two variables point at the same object. You change a property through one. What does the other see? Copying a variable copies the reference. Only primitive values are copied. Objects stay on the heap and are shared. Q1 Why does V8 deoptimise a function? Optimised code relies on type feedback. When the guess fails, V8 drops the fast code and continues in the bytecode, which handles every type. Q2 What prints first after the script ends? setTimeout(() => console.log(\"timer\"), 0); Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"promise\")); Microtasks before tasks. The microtask queue is emptied before the event loop takes the next task, even for a 0 ms timer. Q3 main.mjs logs \"main\", then imports a.mjs which logs \"a\". What is the output order? Imports are processed before the body. In an ES module, all imports are loaded, linked and evaluated before the importing module body runs. Q1 Which is the correct order of V8 tiers from first to last? Interpreter first, best optimiser last. Each tier compiles more slowly and produces faster code. Q2 Which mistake is an early error found before any line runs? Early errors are part of parsing. Duplicate let, const with no value and return outside a function are early errors. The others only fail when the line runs, and dividing by zero just gives Infinity."],[1,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[1,"hoisted","What does early access give","Write earlyRead(kind). It gets how a name was declared, \"var\", \"let\", \"const\", \"class\" or \"function\", and returns what reading that name before its line gives: \"undefined\", \"ReferenceError\" or \"function\". Only two kinds have a usable value after the creation phase. function earlyRead(kind) { if (kind === \"var\") return\"undefined\"; if (kind === \"function\") return\"function\"; return\"ReferenceError\"; } var gets undefined and function declarations are created whole. Everything else sits in the temporal dead zone. 🌿 Medium"],[1,"accumulator","Run bytecode with an accumulator","Write runBytecode(code, args). code is a list of instructions such as [\"Ldar\", \"a1\"]. Support LdaSmi n (put n in the accumulator), Ldar x (load register or argument x), Star x (store the accumulator in register x), Add x and Mul x (x plus or times the accumulator) and Return (return the accumulator). Arguments are named a0, a1 and so on. Keep one variable for the accumulator and an object for the registers. Read a0 from the arguments. function runBytecode(code, args) { let acc; const regs = {}; const read = x => (x[0] === \"a\" ? args[Number(x.slice(1))] : regs[x]); for (const [op, x] of code) { if (op === \"LdaSmi\") acc = x; elseif (op === \"Ldar\") acc = read(x); elseif (op === \"Star\") regs[x] = acc; elseif (op === \"Add\") acc = read(x) + acc; elseif (op === \"Mul\") acc = read(x) * acc; elseif (op === \"Return\") return acc; } return acc; } This is how Ignition works: most instructions take one operand and use the accumulator for the other. 🔥 Hard"],[1,"loop-order","Predict the console","Write consoleOrder(lines). Each line is { kind, text, ms } where kind is \"sync\", \"promise\" or \"timer\" and ms is the timer delay. Return the texts in the order a browser prints them: sync lines in order, then promise callbacks in order, then timers by delay, and timers with the same delay in the order they were created. Filter by kind, then sort only the timers. toSorted is stable, so equal delays keep their order. function consoleOrder(lines) { const pick = kind => lines.filter(l => l.kind === kind); const timers = pick(\"timer\").toSorted((a, b) => a.ms - b.ms); return [...pick(\"sync\"), ...pick(\"promise\"), ...timers].map(l => l.text); } This is the order the event loop simulator showed: the stack, then every microtask, then tasks as their timers become due. 🧠 Expert"],[1,"module-order","Which module runs first","Write evalOrder(graph, entry). graph maps each module to the list it imports, in order. Return the order in which the module bodies run: before a module runs, each of its imports runs first (in order), and every module runs only once. Visit each import first, then push the module. Keep a Set so a module shared by two files is visited once. function evalOrder(graph, entry) { const done = newSet(); const order = []; const visit = name => { if (done.has(name)) return; done.add(name); for (const dep of graph[name] ?? []) visit(dep); order.push(name); }; visit(entry); return order; } This depth first, post order walk is how the evaluation phase of ES modules works. Real engines also handle import cycles with the same \"already seen\" check."],[1,"module-order","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. The journey of a JavaScript programWhen you press Run, your .js file is only text. Two programs work together to turn that text into output. The engine understands the JavaScript language. Step 1: loading the source textThe engine cannot read files or download anything itself. The runtime fetches the text and hands it over. Step 2: the scanner cuts text into tokensThe first thing the engine does with the text is scanning (also called lexing or tokenising). It reads the characters from left to right and groups them into tokens, the smallest pieces that mean something. Step 3: the parser builds a syntax treeThe parser reads the tokens and checks them against the grammar of JavaScript. If they fit, it builds an abstract syntax tree (AST): a tree of nodes such as VariableDeclaration, BinaryExpression and CallExpression. Step 4: scopes and the creation phaseBefore a script or a function runs, the engine sets up an execution context for it. This happens in two phases. Step 5: Ignition turns the tree into bytecodeV8 does not run the tree directly. Its interpreter, Ignition, first turns the tree into bytecode: a list of small, simple instructions. Bytecode is much smaller than machine code, so it starts fast and saves memory on phones. Step 6: running: the call stack, the heap and garbage collectionWhile the bytecode runs, the engine uses two areas of memory. Step 7: hot code gets compiled: Sparkplug, Maglev and TurboFanInterpreting bytecode is quick to start but slower to run. So V8 watches which functions run often and compiles them to machine code while the program is running. Step 8: the runtime, Web APIs and the event loopThe engine runs one piece of code at a time on one thread. So how does a page wait for a server without freezing? It does not wait. Modules: load, link, then evaluateAn ES module is not run the moment it is read. The runtime and the engine go through three phases for the whole graph of files. Real world scenario: a frozen page on sale dayThe cart runs a long synchronous calculation over every item. While it runs, the call stack is never empty, so the event loop cannot run click handlers, timers or painting. The code below measures this with a timer that should fire after 50 ms."],[1,"comic","From text to output in eight steps","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember The engine reads all of the file before running it, runs it as bytecode, makes hot code faster with guesses, and leaves the waiting to the runtime."],[1,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🛒A sale day freeze A cart calculation blocked the main thread for 400 ms, so taps did nothing. Splitting the work into chunks fixed it without any server change. 🧪Zero tests ran A CI job reported \"0 tests\" after a merge. One missing bracket in a shared helper made every spec that imported it fail to parse. 📈A slow log parser A function that sometimes got numbers and sometimes strings kept deoptimising. Converting the input once at the start made it several times faster. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"How a JavaScript Program Runs Inside the Engine\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 13Welcome This chapter follows one JavaScript program from a text file to the output: loading, scanning, parsing, the creation phase, bytecode, running, optimising and the event loop. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[2,"","Variables, Types and Operators","let, const and var, the seven primitive types and objects, copy by value against shared objects, floating point numbers, NaN and BigInt, conversion and coercion, truthy and falsy values, ==, === and Object.is with Jest matchers, and every operator including optional chaining and nullish coalescing. · variables · let · const · var · data types · typeof · number · BigInt · NaN · coercion · truthy · falsy · equality · === · operators · optional chaining · nullish coalescing 🟨 JavaScript›🌱 Foundations›Chapter 3 of 31 🔢 Step 1 · Topic 3 of 5 · Beginner"],[2,"top","","let and const, the seven primitive types, why 0.1 + 0.2 is not 0.3, how JavaScript converts values by itself, the three kinds of equality and every operator, including ?. and ??. ⏱️ About 18 min read📚 7 parts🎬 2 animations▶ 13 runnable examples🧪 1 real test run🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 7 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Testers need this chapter for assertions: which equality a matcher uses, why text read from a page must be converted, and how to read optional fields from an API without crashes. 1Variables with let and const 2The types of value 3numbers 4Type conversion and coercion 5Equality: ==, === and Object.is 6Operators 7Real world scenario: clean up data from an API Lesson 1 of 7"],[2,"variables","Variables with let and const","🧪 QA essential A variable is a name for a value. Modern JavaScript has two keywords for creating one. constThe name always points at the same value. Use it by default, for URLs, selectors, test data and almost everything else. letThe name can be pointed at a new value later. Use it for counters, totals and values that really change. let, const and what const really protects · 💥 Throws const baseUrl = \"https://shop.example\"; // never reassigned, so constlet attempts = 0; // changes, so let attempts = attempts + 1; attempts++; console.log(baseUrl, attempts); const user = { name: \"Asha\" }; user.name = \"Ravi\"; // allowed: the object changes, the binding does not console.log(user); user = {}; // not allowed: const means this name always points at the same thing Output https://shop.example 2 { name: 'Ravi' } main.mjs:10 user = {}; // not allowed: const means this name always points at the same thing ^ TypeError: Assignment to constant variable. at main.mjs:10:6 Node.js v26.12.1 const does not freeze an object. It only stops the name from being reassigned. Use Object.freeze(obj) when the contents must not change either."],[2,"variables","And var","var is the original keyword from 1995. It ignores blocks and can be used before its line runs, which causes bugs. You will still see it in old code and old tutorials. Do not use it in new code. var leaks out of blocks, let does not · ▶ Runs for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {} console.log(\"var i after the loop:\", i); // var leaks out of the blockfor (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) {} console.log(typeof j); // let stays inside the block Output var i after the loop: 3 undefined Names are case sensitive and use camelCase: totalPrice, isLoggedIn, maxRetries. Constants that never change across the whole project are often written as MAX_RETRIES. Keep test configuration in const values at the top of a file or in a config module: const BASE_URL = process.env.BASE_URL ?? \"http://localhost:3000\". Changing environments then means changing one line. Lesson 2 of 7"],[2,"types","The types of value","🧪 QA essentialJestPlaywright Every value in JavaScript is one of eight types. Seven are primitives, simple values that cannot be changed. Everything else is an object. Type Examples typeof string \"Asha\", 'ok', `Hi ${name}` \"string\" number 42, 3.14, NaN, Infinity \"number\" bigint 9007199254740993n \"bigint\" boolean true, false \"boolean\" undefined a variable with no value yet, a missing property \"undefined\" null an empty value put there on purpose \"object\" (a famous old bug) symbol Symbol(\"id\"), a unique key \"symbol\" object {}, [], functions, dates, maps \"object\" or \"function\" typeof for every kind of value · ▶ Runs const values = [\"hello\", 42, 3.14, 10n, true, undefined, null, Symbol(\"id\"), { a: 1 }, [1, 2], function f() {}]; for (const v of values) { console.log(String(typeof v).padEnd(10), Array.isArray(v) ? \"(an array)\" : \"\"); } Output string number number bigint boolean undefined object symbol object object (an array) function typeof null is \"object\" and typeof [] is \"object\" too. Check for null with value === null and for arrays with Array.isArray(value)."],[2,"types","Copied or shared","🎬 Try it"],[2,"types","What a variable really holds","Step through to see a number copied and an object shared. let a = 5; let b = a; b = 10; const cart1 = { items: 1 }; const cart2 = cart1; cart2.items = 99; Variables Objects in memory Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 6 Primitives are copied, objects are shared · ▶ Runs let a = 5; let b = a; // the number is copied b = 10; console.log(a, b); const cart1 = { items: 1 }; const cart2 = cart1; // both names point at ONE object cart2.items = 99; console.log(cart1.items, cart2.items); const cart3 = { ...cart1 }; // a shallow copy is a second object cart3.items = 0; console.log(cart1.items, cart3.items); Output 5 10 99 99 99 0 This explains a common test bug. A shared object used as test data is changed by one test and the next test sees the change. Build fresh data in a beforeEach hook or copy it with structuredClone(data). 🧮"],[2,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 What does this print? console.log(\"5\" - 3); - converts the text to a number. Answer: 2 \"5\" - 3 = 5 - 3 = 2 C2 What is the value of score at the end? let score = 10; score += 5; score *= 2; score -= 4; Work line by line. Answer: 26 10 + 5 = 15 15 × 2 = 30 30 - 4 = 26 C1 What does 17 % 5 give? % is the remainder after dividing. Answer: 2 17 = 5 × 3 + 2 The remainder is 2. C2 What does this print? const s = { retries: 0 }; s.retries ||= 3; console.log(s.retries); The empty retries value is falsy. Answer: 3 0 is falsy, so ||= assigns 3. C1 A shirt costs ₹1299.50. Stored in paise, what is the total for 3 shirts in paise? Turn rupees into paise first. Answer: 389850 1299.50 × 100 = 129950 paise 129950 × 3 = 389850 C2 How many of these are truthy: 0, \"0\", \"\", [], null, \"false\", NaN, {}? Check each one against the falsy list. Answer: 4 Falsy: 0, \"\", null, NaN. Truthy: \"0\", [], \"false\", {}. That is 4. C1 What does this print? console.log(true + null); true becomes 1 and null becomes 0. Answer: 1 true + null = 1 + 0 = 1 C2 What is 2 ** 3 ** 2? ** groups from the right. Answer: 512 3 ** 2 = 9 2 ** 9 = 512 Lesson 4 of 7"],[2,"coercion","Type conversion and coercion","🧪 QA essentialEvery value read from the page with textContent, inputValue or getAttribute is a string. Conversion is when you change a type on purpose. Coercion is when JavaScript does it for you behind the scenes. Both follow fixed rules. Converting on purpose · ▶ Runs console.log(Number(\"42\"), Number(\"\"), Number(\"42px\"), Number(\" 7 \")); console.log(parseInt(\"42px\"), parseFloat(\"3.5kg\"), parseInt(\"0x1F\"), parseInt(\"101\", 2)); console.log(String(42), (42).toString(2), `${42}`); console.log(Boolean(\"\"), Boolean(\"0\"), Boolean(0), Boolean([])); Output 42 0 NaN 7 42 3.5 31 5 42 101010 42 false true false true When an operator gets the \"wrong\" type, JavaScript converts the value. The + operator prefers text: if either side is a string, it joins. The other maths operators convert to numbers. Conversions JavaScript does by itself · ▶ Runs console.log(\"5\" + 3); // + with a string joins text console.log(\"5\" - 3); // - only works on numbers, so \"5\" becomes 5 console.log(\"5\" * \"2\"); console.log(true + 1); console.log([] + []); console.log([] + {}); console.log(null + 1, undefined + 1); console.log(\"10\" > \"9\", 10 > \"9\"); Output 53 2 10 2 [object Object] 1 NaN false true"],[2,"coercion","Truthy and falsy","In an if or with &&, || and !, every value counts as true or false. Only eight values are falsy. Everything else, including \"0\", \"false\", [] and {}, is truthy. The eight falsy values · ▶ Runs const values = [false, 0, -0, 0n, \"\", null, undefined, NaN, \"0\", \"false\", [], {}]; const labels = [\"false\", \"0\", \"-0\", \"0n\", '\"\"', \"null\", \"undefined\", \"NaN\", '\"0\"', '\"false\"', \"[]\", \"{}\"]; values.forEach((v, i) => { console.log(labels[i].padEnd(10), v ? \"truthy\" : \"falsy\"); }); Output false falsy 0 falsy -0 falsy 0n falsy \"\" falsy null falsy undefined falsy NaN falsy \"0\" truthy \"false\" truthy [] truthy {} truthy Values read from an input field, a URL or an environment variable are always strings. process.env.HEADLESS set to \"false\" is truthy. Compare it with the text: process.env.HEADLESS === \"true\". Lesson 5 of 7"],[2,"equality","Equality: ==, === and Object.is","🧪 QA essentialJestVitestChaiPlaywrightPicking the wrong equality is the most common reason a correct assertion fails. JavaScript has three ways to ask \"are these the same\". 🎬 Try it"],[2,"equality","Equality playground","Type any two JavaScript values, such as 0, \"0\", [], null or NaN. Left Right Check Result Types a == b true \"5\" is string 5 is number a === b false Object.is(a, b) false Why: The string is turned into a number, then the two numbers are compared. ==, === and Object.is · ▶ Runs console.log(5 == \"5\", 5 === \"5\"); console.log(0 == false, 0 === false); console.log(null == undefined, null === undefined); console.log(NaN === NaN, Object.is(NaN, NaN)); console.log(0 === -0, Object.is(0, -0)); const a = { id: 1 }, b = { id: 1 }, c = a; console.log(a === b, a === c); console.log(JSON.stringify(a) === JSON.stringify(b)); Output true false true false true false false true true false false true true === (strict equality) compares without converting. Use it everywhere. == (loose equality) converts first, which gives surprises such as 0 == \"\". The one useful case is x == null, which is true for both null and undefined. Object.is is like === except that NaN equals NaN and 0 differs from -0. Objects and arrays are equal only when they are the same object. Two objects with the same contents are not ===. Test runners have the same split. In Jest and Vitest, toBe uses Object.is and toEqual compares contents. The first test below fails on purpose so you can read the message. The same idea inside a test runner · 🧪 Tests run, one fails on purpose 📄 package.json { \"name\": \"eq\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"private\": true, \"scripts\": { \"test\": \"jest\" } } 📄 cart.test.js test(\"toBe compares with Object.is, so two equal looking objects fail\", () => { expect({ id: 1 }).toBe({ id: 1 }); }); test(\"toEqual compares the contents\", () => { expect({ id: 1, tags: [\"new\"] }).toEqual({ id: 1, tags: [\"new\"] }); }); test(\"toBeCloseTo is the right check for decimals\", () => { expect(0.1 + 0.2).toBeCloseTo(0.3); }); Terminal $ npx jest --verbose Terminal output FAIL ./cart.test.js ✕ toBe compares with Object.is, so two equal looking objects fail ✓ toEqual compares the contents ✓ toBeCloseTo is the right check for decimals ● toBe compares with Object.is, so two equal looking objects fail expect(received).toBe(expected) // Object.is equality If it should pass with deep equality, replace \"toBe\" with \"toStrictEqual\" Expected: {\"id\": 1} Received: serializes to the same string 1 | test(\"toBe compares with Object.is, so two equal looking objects fail\", () => { > 2 | expect({ id: 1 }).toBe({ id: 1 }); | ^ 3 | }); 4 | 5 | test(\"toEqual compares the contents\", () => { at Object.toBe (cart.test.js:2:21) Test Suites: 1 failed, 1 total Tests: 1 failed, 2 passed, 3 total Snapshots: 0 total Time: 0.4 s Ran all test suites. Choose the matcher by what you compare: toBe for numbers, strings and booleans, toEqual or toStrictEqual for objects and arrays, toBeCloseTo for decimals. Chai uses equal and deep.equal, and Playwright's expect has the same toBe and toEqual. Lesson 6 of 7"],[2,"operators","Operators","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypressJest Operators combine values. Most of them you know from maths. A few are special to JavaScript and very handy. Arithmetic, comparison, logic and ?? · ▶ Runs let score = 10; score += 5; // 15 score *= 2; // 30 score -= 4; // 26 score %= 7; // 5 console.log(score); const age = 20; console.log(age >= 18 ? \"adult\" : \"minor\"); console.log(age > 18 && age < 60, age < 18 || age > 60, !true); console.log(\"\" || \"default\", 0 || 10, \"set\" && \"both true\"); console.log(JSON.stringify(\"\" ?? \"default\"), 0 ?? 10, null ?? \"fallback\"); Output 5 adult true false false default 10 both true \"\" 0 fallback Group Operators Arithmetic + - * / % **, and ++ and -- to add or take away one Assignment = += -= *= /= %= **= Comparison === !== < > <= >= Logical && (and), || (or), ! (not). They return one of their values, not just true or false. Nullish ?? uses the right side only when the left is null or undefined Optional chaining ?. stops and gives undefined instead of crashing on a missing value Logical assignment ||= &&= ??= assign only when needed Conditional condition ? a : b"],[2,"operators","?? against ||","|| replaces every falsy value, so a real 0 or an empty string is lost. ?? replaces only null and undefined. For defaults such as a timeout or a retry count, ?? is almost always what you want. Optional chaining and logical assignment · ▶ Runs const response = { user: { name: \"Asha\", address: null, orders: [{ id: 7, total: 1299 }] }, }; console.log(response.user?.address?.city); // undefined, no crash console.log(response.user?.address?.city ?? \"Unknown\"); console.log(response.user.orders?.[0]?.total); console.log(response.user.orders?.[5]?.total ?? 0); console.log(response.user.greet?.()); // call it only if it existsconst settings = { retries: 0, timeout: null }; settings.retries ||= 3; // 0 is falsy, so it becomes 3 settings.timeout ??= 5000; // only null or undefined is replaced console.log(settings); Output undefined Unknown 1299 0 undefined { retries: 3, timeout: 5000 } API responses are full of optional fields. body.data?.items?.[0]?.id reads deep values without a crash, so the assertion that follows fails with a clear message instead of a TypeError. Lesson 7 of 7"],[2,"scenario","Real world scenario: clean up data from an API","🧪 QA essential The situationA product API returns prices as text with a currency sign, stock as text and a discount that is sometimes missing. Before you can check the numbers, you must turn them into real numbers without crashing on the gaps. Normalise messy test data · ▶ Runs // An API returned product data in an awkward shape. Normalise it before checking it.const apiProducts = [ { sku: \"A1\", price: \"₹1,299.50\", stock: \"12\", discount: null }, { sku: \"B2\", price: \"₹499\", stock: \"0\", discount: { percent: 10 } }, { sku: \"C3\", price: \"\", stock: undefined }, ]; function toNumber(text) { const clean = String(text ?? \"\").replace(/[^0-9.]/g, \"\"); return clean === \"\" ? null : Number(clean); } const products = apiProducts.map(p => ({ sku: p.sku, price: toNumber(p.price), inStock: Number(p.stock ?? 0) > 0, discount: p.discount?.percent ?? 0, })); console.table(products); const problems = products.filter(p => p.price === null || Number.isNaN(p.price)); console.log(\"Products with a missing price:\", problems.map(p => p.sku)); Output ┌─────────┬──────┬────────┬─────────┬──────────┐ │ (index) │ sku │ price │ inStock │ discount │ ├─────────┼──────┼────────┼─────────┼──────────┤ │ 0 │ 'A1' │ 1299.5 │ true │ 0 │ │ 1 │ 'B2' │ 499 │ false │ 10 │ │ 2 │ 'C3' │ null │ false │ 0 │ └─────────┴──────┴────────┴─────────┴──────────┘ Products with a missing price: [ 'C3' ] Every tool from this chapter is here: ?? for missing values, ?. for the optional discount, explicit Number conversion, and a check with Number.isNaN for anything that went wrong. The same toNumber helper is useful in the app itself. Parsing input once, at the edge, keeps the rest of the code working with clean types."],[2,"scenario","Formulas","Every formula and rule of this chapter in one place. Each card links back to the lesson that explains it. numbers \"5\" - 3 = 5 - 3 = 2 numbers 17 = 5 × 3 + 2 numbers 1299.50 × 100 = 129950 paise numbers 129950 × 3 = 389850 numbers true + null = 1 + 0 = 1 numbers 3 ** 2 = 9 numbers 2 ** 9 = 512 🎯"],[2,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 Which keyword should you use for a base URL that never changes? It is never reassigned. const says the name will always point at this value. Q2 What does this print? console.log(\"5\" + 3); + with a string joins text. One side is a string, so + joins: 53. console.log prints a string without quotes. Q3 Which of these is falsy? Only eight values are falsy. The number 0 is falsy. Any non empty string and every array are truthy. Q1 What does this print? const qty = 0; console.log(qty ?? \"Unknown\"); ?? only replaces null and undefined. 0 is neither null nor undefined, so ?? keeps it. Q2 What does this print? const a = { n: 1 }; const b = a; b.n = 99; console.log(a.n); How many objects exist? Both names point at one object, so the change made through b shows through a. Q3 Which Jest matcher should check that an API returned { id: 1, name: \"Asha\" }? You care about the contents. toEqual compares contents recursively. toBe would need the very same object. Q1 What does typeof null return? It is a famous bug. A mistake from the first version of the language, kept so old pages keep working. Q2 process.env.HEADLESS is set to the text false. Which check correctly turns headless mode off? Environment values are always strings. The string \"false\" is truthy, so the Boolean based checks say true. Compare the text instead. Q3 What does this print? console.log(NaN === NaN, Object.is(NaN, NaN)); === never converts. Object.is has two special cases. NaN === NaN is false. Object.is(NaN, NaN) is true. Q1 What does this print? console.log(9007199254740993); It is above MAX_SAFE_INTEGER. The number cannot be stored exactly, so it is rounded to the nearest number a double can hold. Q2 What does [] + {} give? Both sides are turned into strings first. An empty array becomes \"\" and a plain object becomes \"[object Object]\". Joining them gives \"[object Object]\". Q3 Which expression is true only when value is null or undefined? This is the one good use of loose equality. == null matches null and undefined and nothing else. !value is also true for 0 and \"\"."],[2,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[2,"price","Read a price","Write toPaise(text). It gets a price as shown on a page, such as \"₹1,299.50\", and returns the amount in paise as a whole number (129950). Remove everything that is not a digit or a dot, convert with Number, then multiply by 100 and round. function toPaise(text) { const rupees = Number(text.replace(/[^0-9.]/g, \"\")); returnMath.round(rupees * 100); } Math.round removes tiny floating point errors such as 129949.99999. 🌿 Medium"],[2,"city","Read a value that may be missing","Write cityOf(user) that returns user.address.city, or \"Unknown\" when the user, the address or the city is missing. An empty string city should stay an empty string. Combine ?. and ??. function cityOf(user) { return user?.address?.city ?? \"Unknown\"; } ?? keeps the empty string because it only replaces null and undefined. || would have turned it into \"Unknown\". 🔥 Hard"],[2,"close","Compare decimals","Write closeTo(a, b, places = 2) that returns true when a and b are equal to that many decimal places, the way Jest's toBeCloseTo works: the difference must be less than 10 ** -places / 2. Compare Math.abs(a - b) with the allowed difference. function closeTo(a, b, places = 2) { returnMath.abs(a - b) < 10 ** -places / 2; } This is exactly the rule Jest uses, which is why toBeCloseTo(0.3) passes for 0.1 + 0.2. 🧠 Expert"],[2,"same","Write your own Object.is","Write same(a, b) that behaves like Object.is without calling it: like ===, except that NaN equals NaN and 0 and -0 are different. Only NaN is not equal to itself. And 1 / 0 is Infinity while 1 / -0 is -Infinity. function same(a, b) { if (a === b) { return a !== 0 || 1 / a === 1 / b; } return a !== a && b !== b; } When a === b and both are zero, dividing 1 by each tells +0 from -0. When they are not equal, both must be NaN to count as the same."],[2,"same","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. Variables with let and constA variable is a name for a value. Modern JavaScript has two keywords for creating one. The types of valueEvery value in JavaScript is one of eight types. Seven are primitives, simple values that cannot be changed. Everything else is an object. numbers Type conversion and coercionConversion is when you change a type on purpose. Coercion is when JavaScript does it for you behind the scenes. Both follow fixed rules. Equality: ==, === and Object.isJavaScript has three ways to ask \"are these the same\". OperatorsOperators combine values. Most of them you know from maths. A few are special to JavaScript and very handy. Real world scenario: clean up data from an APIEvery tool from this chapter is here: ?? for missing values, ?. for the optional discount, explicit Number conversion, and a check with Number.isNaN for anything that went wrong."],[2,"comic","Values, types and the comparisons between them","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember const by default, === always, ?? for defaults."],[2,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🧾An invoice total Billing code keeps amounts in paise or cents as whole numbers and formats them with Intl.NumberFormat only on screen. 🔐A feature flag A script reads FEATURE_NEW_CHECKOUT from the environment. It arrives as the string \"false\", which is truthy, so the code compares it with \"true\". 📮An API response An order may have no delivery address yet. order.address?.city ?? \"Not set\" shows a label instead of crashing the page. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Variables, Types and Operators\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 9Welcome This chapter covers variables, the types of value, numbers, conversions, equality and every operator you will use. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[3,"","Control Flow, Loops and Errors","Decisions with if, else and switch, guard clauses, every loop with break, continue and labels, why forEach does not wait for await, throwing and catching errors with finally, custom errors and error causes, polling loops with timeouts, retries with backoff and data driven test tables. · if else · switch · loops · for of · for in · while · break · continue · try catch · finally · throw · custom error · error cause · polling · retry · forEach await · data driven 🟨 JavaScript›🌱 Foundations›Chapter 4 of 31 🔀 Step 1 · Topic 4 of 5 · Beginner"],[3,"top","","if and switch, every kind of loop, the forEach and await trap, throwing and catching errors with causes and cleanup, and the polling and retry loops that power auto-waiting in every test tool. ⏱️ About 18 min read📚 7 parts🎬 3 animations▶ 14 runnable examples🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 5 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers The parts on async loops, errors and polling explain the three things behind most flaky UI tests: steps that are not awaited, cleanup that does not run, and fixed waits instead of polling. 1Making decisions with if and else 2switch and lookup tables 3Loops 4Loops with async steps 5Throwing and catching errors 6Polling and retry loops 7Real world scenario: data driven password checks Lesson 1 of 7"],[3,"if-else","Making decisions with if and else","🧪 QA essentialEach branch is a test case, and coverage tools measure how many branches your tests reach. An if statement runs a block only when a condition is truthy. else if adds more conditions, checked from the top, and the first one that matches wins. if, else if and else · ▶ Runs function shippingFee(total, isMember) { if (total <= 0) { return\"invalid\"; } elseif (isMember || total >= 999) { return0; } elseif (total >= 500) { return40; } else { return80; } } console.log(shippingFee(1200, false)); console.log(shippingFee(600, false)); console.log(shippingFee(300, true)); console.log(shippingFee(300, false)); console.log(shippingFee(-5, false)); Output 0 40 0 80 invalid Order matters. Put the most specific condition first. If total >= 500 came before total >= 999, a ₹1200 order would wrongly pay ₹40."],[3,"if-else","Guard clauses","Deep nesting is hard to read. Handle the bad cases first and return early, so the main logic stays at the left edge. Guard clauses keep code flat · ▶ Runs function applyCoupon(cart, code) { if (!cart) return\"no cart\"; if (cart.items.length === 0) return\"cart is empty\"; if (code !== \"SAVE10\") return\"unknown coupon\"; // the happy path, no nesting neededreturn`saved ${cart.total * 0.1}`; } console.log(applyCoupon(null, \"SAVE10\")); console.log(applyCoupon({ items: [], total: 0 }, \"SAVE10\")); console.log(applyCoupon({ items: [1], total: 500 }, \"OOPS\")); console.log(applyCoupon({ items: [1], total: 500 }, \"SAVE10\")); Output no cart cart is empty unknown coupon saved 50 Every if and else is a path that needs a test. The shipping fee above has five paths, so it needs at least five test cases, plus the boundary values 499, 500, 998 and 999. Branch coverage reports count exactly these paths. Lesson 2 of 7"],[3,"switch","switch and lookup tables","switch compares one value against many cases with ===. Cases without a break or return fall through into the next one, which is useful for shared results and a classic bug otherwise. switch, shared cases and fall through · ▶ Runs function statusIcon(status) { switch (status) { case\"passed\": return\"✅\"; case\"failed\": case\"broken\": // two cases share one resultreturn\"❌\"; case\"skipped\": return\"⏭️\"; default: return\"❓\"; } } [\"passed\", \"broken\", \"skipped\", \"flaky\"].forEach(s => console.log(s, statusIcon(s))); // forgetting break makes execution \"fall through\" into the next caseconst level = 2; switch (level) { case1: console.log(\"one\"); case2: console.log(\"two\"); case3: console.log(\"three\"); default: console.log(\"default\"); } Output passed ✅ broken ❌ skipped ⏭️ flaky ❓ two three default When each case only picks a value, an object used as a lookup table is shorter and easier to extend. An object lookup instead of a switch · ▶ Runs constICONS = { passed: \"✅\", failed: \"❌\", broken: \"❌\", skipped: \"⏭️\" }; const icon = status => ICONS[status] ?? \"❓\"; console.log(icon(\"passed\"), icon(\"broken\"), icon(\"flaky\")); Output ✅ ❌ ❓ Lesson 3 of 7"],[3,"loops","Loops","Loops repeat work. Pick the loop by what you are repeating over. 🎬 Try it"],[3,"loops","A loop, one turn at a time","Watch the counter, the condition and the running total. let sum = 0; for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++) { sum += i; } console.log(sum); Variables Condition Console Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 9 Every kind of loop · ▶ Runs for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++) { console.log(\"for\", i); } let tries = 0; while (tries < 2) { tries++; console.log(\"while try\", tries); } let n = 10; do { console.log(\"do runs at least once, n =\", n); } while (n < 5); for (const browser of [\"chromium\", \"firefox\", \"webkit\"]) { console.log(\"for...of\", browser); } const user = { name: \"Asha\", role: \"admin\" }; for (const key in user) { console.log(\"for...in\", key, \"=\", user[key]); } Output for 1 for 2 for 3 while try 1 while try 2 do runs at least once, n = 10 for...of chromium for...of firefox for...of webkit for...in name = Asha for...in role = admin Loop Use it when for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) you need the counter, or a fixed number of repeats for (const x of list) you want each value of an array, string, Map or Set. The usual choice. for (const key in obj) you want the keys of a plain object. Avoid it on arrays. while (condition) you do not know how many times, such as polling until something is ready do { } while (condition) the body must run at least once"],[3,"loops","break, continue and labels","break, continue and labels · ▶ Runs const results = [\"pass\", \"pass\", \"skip\", \"fail\", \"pass\"]; for (const r of results) { if (r === \"skip\") continue; // jump to the next itemif (r === \"fail\") { console.log(\"stopping at the first failure\"); break; // leave the loop } console.log(\"checked\", r); } outer: for (const row of [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]]) { for (const cell of row) { if (cell === 4) break outer; // a label breaks out of both loops console.log(cell); } } Output checked pass checked pass stopping at the first failure 1 2 3 Looping over objects and indexes · ▶ Runs const scores = { asha: 82, ravi: 67, mei: 91 }; for (const [name, score] ofObject.entries(scores)) { console.log(`${name}: ${score >= 70 ? \"pass\" : \"fail\"}`); } const tests = [\"login\", \"search\", \"checkout\"]; for (const [index, name] of tests.entries()) { console.log(index + 1, name); } Output asha: pass ravi: fail mei: pass 1 login 2 search 3 checkout A while loop whose condition never becomes false runs forever and freezes the page or the test. Every polling loop needs a timeout, as shown later in this chapter. Lesson 4 of 7"],[3,"async-loops","Loops with async steps","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightWebdriverIOSeleniumCypressAwaiting inside forEach is one of the most common causes of flaky UI tests. Test code is full of steps that take time: open a page, click, wait for a response. These steps return promises and are written with await (chapter 11 explains how they work). Loops and await have one trap that breaks many first test suites. forEach does not wait for async steps · ▶ Runs const sleep = ms => newPromise(r => setTimeout(r, ms)); const steps = [\"open page\", \"fill form\", \"submit\"]; console.log(\"forEach does not wait:\"); steps.forEach(async step => { await sleep(10); console.log(\" done:\", step); }); console.log(\" forEach returned already\"); await sleep(50); console.log(\"for...of waits for each step:\"); for (const step of steps) { await sleep(10); console.log(\" done:\", step); } console.log(\" every step finished\"); Output forEach does not wait: forEach returned already done: open page done: fill form done: submit for...of waits for each step: done: open page done: fill form done: submit every step finished array.forEach calls the function for every item and does not wait for any of them. The loop is \"finished\" while the steps are still running. for...of with await inside runs the steps one after another, in order. Use it for UI steps. To start many independent requests at once and wait for all of them, use await Promise.all(list.map(async x => ...)). In Playwright and WebdriverIO, items.forEach(async item => await page.click(item)) makes clicks race each other and the test may end before they finish. Write for (const item of items) await page.click(item). Cypress queues its commands, so a plain forEach with cy commands inside works there, which is one of the main differences between the tools. Lesson 5 of 7"],[3,"errors","Throwing and catching errors","🧪 QA essentialJestPlaywrightMochaAn assertion failure is a thrown error. Knowing how errors travel explains how runners report and clean up. Code signals a problem by throwing an error. The error travels up through the functions that called it until a try block catches it. If nothing catches it, the program stops. 🎬 Animated diagram"],[3,"errors","Where an error goes","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 5 throw, try, catch and finally · ▶ Runs function withdraw(balance, amount) { if (typeof amount !== \"number\" || Number.isNaN(amount)) { thrownewTypeError(\"amount must be a number\"); } if (amount > balance) { thrownewRangeError(`cannot withdraw ${amount}, balance is ${balance}`); } return balance - amount; } for (const amount of [30, \"ten\", 500]) { try { console.log(\"new balance:\", withdraw(100, amount)); } catch (err) { console.log(`${err.name}: ${err.message}`); } finally { console.log(\"finally always runs\"); } } Output new balance: 70 finally always runs TypeError: amount must be a number finally always runs RangeError: cannot withdraw 500, balance is 100 finally always runs Throw Error objects (or subclasses such as TypeError), never plain strings. Only Error objects carry a stack trace. finally runs whether the block passed, threw or returned. It is the place for cleanup. Catch only what you can handle. Rethrow everything else with throw err."],[3,"errors","Custom errors and causes","A subclass of Error lets callers tell your error apart from others with instanceof. The cause option (ES2022) wraps a low level error in a clearer one without losing it. A custom error with a cause · ▶ Runs classValidationErrorextendsError { constructor(field, message, options) { super(message, options); this.name = \"ValidationError\"; this.field = field; } } function parseAge(text) { const age = Number(text); if (!Number.isInteger(age)) { thrownewValidationError(\"age\", `\"${text}\" is not a whole number`); } return age; } function signUp(form) { try { return { ok: true, age: parseAge(form.age) }; } catch (err) { if (err instanceofValidationError) { // wrap it with more context and keep the original as the causethrownewError(`sign up failed for ${form.email}`, { cause: err }); } throw err; // anything else is unexpected, pass it on } } try { signUp({ email: \"asha@example.com\", age: \"twenty\" }); } catch (err) { console.log(err.message); console.log(\"cause:\", err.cause.name, \"on\", err.cause.field, \"-\", err.cause.message); } Output sign up failed for asha@example.com cause: ValidationError on age - \"twenty\" is not a whole number"],[3,"errors","finally for cleanup","finally for cleanup · ▶ Runs asyncfunction runTest(name, body) { const browser = { open: true }; // pretend we launched a browser console.log(`[${name}] browser launched`); try { await body(); console.log(`[${name}] passed`); } catch (err) { console.log(`[${name}] failed: ${err.message}`); } finally { browser.open = false; // cleanup runs on pass and on fail console.log(`[${name}] browser closed`); } } await runTest(\"login\", async () => {}); await runTest(\"checkout\", async () => { thrownewError(\"button not found\"); }); Output [login] browser launched [login] passed [login] browser closed [checkout] browser launched [checkout] failed: button not found [checkout] browser closed A test runner is a giant try, catch and finally. Each test body runs inside try, a failed assertion is a thrown error, and afterEach hooks run like finally. That is why a failed assertion stops the rest of that test but not the suite. Lesson 6 of 7"],[3,"polling","Polling and retry loops","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypressSeleniumWebdriverIOAuto-waiting and retries are loops like these, and their timeout options are the loop limits. Two loops sit at the centre of every test framework. You will rarely write them yourself, but knowing how they work explains timeouts, flaky tests and the options every tool offers."],[3,"polling","Wait until something is true","A polling loop with a timeout · ▶ Runs const sleep = ms => newPromise(r => setTimeout(r, ms)); // The heart of every \"wait until\" helper: check, wait a little, check again, give up after a timeout.asyncfunction waitFor(check, { timeout = 1000, interval = 100 } = {}) { const start = Date.now(); let attempt = 0; while (true) { attempt++; const value = await check(); if (value) return { value, attempt }; if (Date.now() - start >= timeout) { thrownewError(`condition not met after ${attempt} attempts`); } await sleep(interval); } } // A fake \"order status\" that becomes ready on the 4th checklet calls = 0; const orderStatus = async () => (++calls >= 4 ? \"SHIPPED\" : null); const result = await waitFor(orderStatus, { timeout: 2000, interval: 50 }); console.log(result); try { await waitFor(async () => false, { timeout: 200, interval: 50 }); } catch (err) { console.log(err.message); } Output { value: 'SHIPPED', attempt: 4 } condition not met after 5 attempts 🎬 Try it"],[3,"polling","Sleep or wait for the element","Move the slider to change when the Pay button appears, then compare the two strategies. The button appears after 1800 ms sleep(2000) waited 2000 ms, wasted 200 ms auto-wait clicked at about 1800 ms timeout 5000 the most it would ever wait The fixed sleep passes but wastes 200 ms on every run. Auto-waiting moves on the moment the button is ready. Playwright's auto-waiting, expect.poll, Cypress retry-ability, WebdriverIO's waitUntil and Selenium's driver.wait are all versions of this loop: check, wait a short interval, check again, fail with a clear message after the timeout."],[3,"polling","Retry an action that sometimes fails","Retry with exponential backoff · ▶ Runs asyncfunction retry(action, { attempts = 3, delay = 50 } = {}) { let lastError; for (let i = 1; i <= attempts; i++) { try { returnawait action(i); } catch (err) { lastError = err; console.log(`attempt ${i} failed: ${err.message}`); if (i < attempts) awaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, delay * 2 ** (i - 1))); // 50, 100, 200 ms } } thrownewError(`gave up after ${attempts} attempts`, { cause: lastError }); } const flaky = async attempt => { if (attempt < 3) thrownewError(\"503 Service Unavailable\"); return\"200 OK\"; }; console.log(await retry(flaky)); Output attempt 1 failed: 503 Service Unavailable attempt 2 failed: 503 Service Unavailable 200 OK Retry infrastructure problems (a 503 from a test server, a slow container). Never retry to hide a real bug. A test that only passes on the third try is reporting something. Lesson 7 of 7"],[3,"scenario","Real world scenario: data driven password checks","🧪 QA essentialJestVitestPlaywright The situationA sign up form has a password rule: 8 to 64 characters, at least one capital letter and one digit. You need to check the rule on all the important inputs, including the boundaries, and show a readable report. A table of test cases · ▶ Runs // Data driven checks: one validation rule, a table of inputs and the result each should give.function isValidPassword(pw) { if (typeof pw !== \"string\") returnfalse; if (pw.length < 8 || pw.length > 64) returnfalse; if (!/[A-Z]/.test(pw)) returnfalse; if (!/[0-9]/.test(pw)) returnfalse; returntrue; } const cases = [ { input: \"Secret12\", expected: true, why: \"8 chars, upper, digit\" }, { input: \"Secret1\", expected: false, why: \"7 chars, one too short\" }, { input: \"secret12\", expected: false, why: \"no upper case\" }, { input: \"SecretOK\", expected: false, why: \"no digit\" }, { input: \"A1\" + \"x\".repeat(62), expected: true, why: \"64 chars, the maximum\" }, { input: \"A1\" + \"x\".repeat(63), expected: false, why: \"65 chars, one too long\" }, { input: null, expected: false, why: \"not a string\" }, ]; let failures = 0; for (const c of cases) { const actual = isValidPassword(c.input); const ok = actual === c.expected; if (!ok) failures++; console.log(`${ok ? \"PASS\" : \"FAIL\"} ${c.why.padEnd(24)} expected ${c.expected}, got ${actual}`); } console.log(failures === 0 ? \"All cases pass\" : `${failures} case(s) failed`); Output PASS 8 chars, upper, digit expected true, got true PASS 7 chars, one too short expected false, got false PASS no upper case expected false, got false PASS no digit expected false, got false PASS 64 chars, the maximum expected true, got true PASS 65 chars, one too long expected false, got false PASS not a string expected false, got false All cases pass This is data driven testing: the logic of the check is written once and the cases live in a table. Adding a case means adding one row. Every test runner has a built in version: test.each in Jest and Vitest, a loop over test() in Playwright, and data providers in TestNG. The rows were chosen with boundary value analysis: 7, 8, 64 and 65 characters, plus one row for each rule broken on its own. Each row has a short \"why\", which becomes the test name and tells the reader what failed. 🧮"],[3,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 How many times does the body run? for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) { console.log(i); } Count i from 0 up to 4. Answer: 5 i takes the values 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4. That is 5 turns. C2 What is the shipping fee for a total of 600 for a non member, with the rules from Part 1? Check the conditions from the top. Answer: 40 600 is not ≤ 0 and not ≥ 999, but it is ≥ 500. So the fee is 40. C1 What is sum at the end? let sum = 0; for (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) { if (i % 2 === 0) continue; sum += i; } Odd numbers only. Answer: 9 continue skips 2 and 4. 1 + 3 + 5 = 9 C2 A polling loop has a timeout of 1000 ms and an interval of 200 ms. Each check is instant. At most how many checks run? Checks at 0, 200, 400... Answer: 6 Checks happen at 0, 200, 400, 600, 800 and 1000 ms. The check at 1000 ms is the last one, so 6 checks. C1 A retry starts at 50 ms and doubles: 50, 100, 200... What is the total waiting time for 5 attempts? There is no wait after the last attempt. Answer: 750 4 waits come between 5 attempts. 50 + 100 + 200 + 400 = 750 ms C2 The password rule allows 8 to 64 characters. Using boundary value analysis with the values just below, on and just above each edge, how many length values do you test? Three values around each edge. Answer: 6 Lower edge: 7, 8, 9. Upper edge: 63, 64, 65. That is 6 values. C1 What does this print? let n = 5; do { n++; } while (n < 5); console.log(n); The do body runs once before the check. Answer: 6 The body runs with n = 5 and makes n 6. Then 6 < 5 is false, so the loop stops and prints 6. C2 How many lines does this print? console.log(\"start\"); switch (\"b\") { case\"a\": console.log(\"a\"); case\"b\": console.log(\"b\"); case\"c\": console.log(\"c\"); break; default: console.log(\"default\"); } console.log(\"end\"); Follow the case where it starts. Answer: 4 \"start\" prints first. The switch jumps to case \"b\" and prints b, falls through into case \"c\" and prints c, then break leaves the switch. \"end\" prints last. That is 4 lines. 🎯"],[3,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 Which loop is the usual choice to visit every value of an array? It gives values, not keys. for...of gives each value. for...in gives keys and is meant for plain objects. Q2 When does a finally block run? Think of cleanup. finally runs whether the try block passed, threw or returned. Q3 What does continue do inside a loop? break leaves, continue skips. continue ends the current turn and moves to the next item. Q1 What does this print? switch (2) { case1: console.log(\"one\"); case2: console.log(\"two\"); case3: console.log(\"three\"); } There are no break statements. Execution starts at case 2 and falls through into case 3. Q2 Why should you throw new Error(\"x\") instead of throw \"x\"? What helps you find where it happened? A string can be thrown, but it has no stack, so the report cannot say where the problem started. Q3 A Playwright test does items.forEach(async i => await page.click(i)). What usually happens? Does forEach look at the promises? forEach ignores the returned promises. Use for...of with await for steps that must happen in order. Q1 What is err.cause here? try { parseAge(\"x\"); } catch (e) { thrownewError(\"bad age\", { cause: e }); } The second argument of Error is an options object. The cause option stores the original error on the new one. Q2 Which loop body runs at least once even when the condition starts false? The check is at the end. do...while checks its condition after the body. Q3 A retry waits 100 ms, then doubles the wait each time. How long is the wait before the 4th attempt? 100, 200, then... Waits before attempts 2, 3 and 4 are 100, 200 and 400 ms. Q1 Which tool runs a plain forEach over cy commands correctly, and why? One of them does not use await at all. Cypress commands do not run when called. They are added to a queue that Cypress runs in order, so forEach only fills the queue. Q2 What does this print? outer: for (const row of [[1,2],[3,4],[5,6]]) { for (const c of row) { if (c === 4) break outer; console.log(c); } } The label belongs to the outer loop. break outer leaves both loops when the cell is 4, so only 1, 2 and 3 are printed. Q3 What does the function return? function f() { try { return\"try\"; } finally { return\"finally\"; } } A return in finally wins. A return inside finally replaces the value from try. This is why linters warn about it."],[3,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[3,"grade","Grade a score","Write grade(score): 90 and above is \"A\", 75 to 89 is \"B\", 50 to 74 is \"C\", below 50 is \"F\". Anything that is not a number from 0 to 100 returns \"invalid\". Start with a guard clause for invalid input, then check from the highest grade down. function grade(score) { if (typeof score !== \"number\" || score < 0 || score > 100) return\"invalid\"; if (score >= 90) return\"A\"; if (score >= 75) return\"B\"; if (score >= 50) return\"C\"; return\"F\"; } Checking from the top band down means each condition only needs one comparison. 🌿 Medium"],[3,"first-fail","Stop at the first failure","Write firstFailure(results). It gets an array like [\"pass\", \"pass\", \"fail\"] and returns the index of the first \"fail\", or -1 when there is none. Use a loop with break or return. A counter loop gives you the index. function firstFailure(results) { for (let i = 0; i < results.length; i++) { if (results[i] === \"fail\") return i; } return -1; } Returning inside the loop stops it at once. The built in results.indexOf(\"fail\") does the same thing. 🔥 Hard"],[3,"retry-sync","Retry a flaky function","Write retry(fn, attempts). Call fn(). If it throws, call it again, up to attempts calls in total. Return the first value it returns. If every call throws, throw the last error. Put try and catch inside a loop. Remember the error in a variable outside the loop. function retry(fn, attempts) { let last; for (let i = 0; i < attempts; i++) { try { return fn(); } catch (err) { last = err; } } throw last; } A successful call returns straight out of the loop. Only when the loop runs out does the saved error get thrown. 🧠 Expert"],[3,"wait-until","Write waitUntil","Write an async function waitUntil(check, timeout, interval). It calls check() (which may return a promise) until the result is truthy and returns that result. If timeout milliseconds pass first, it throws an error with the message \"timed out\". Remember the start time with Date.now(). Wait with await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, interval)). asyncfunction waitUntil(check, timeout, interval) { const start = Date.now(); while (true) { const value = await check(); if (value) return value; if (Date.now() - start >= timeout) thrownewError(\"timed out\"); awaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, interval)); } } This is the core of every auto-waiting helper. Awaiting check() lets it work with both normal and async checks."],[3,"wait-until","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. Making decisions with if and elseAn if statement runs a block only when a condition is truthy. else if adds more conditions, checked from the top, and the first one that matches wins. switch and lookup tablesswitch compares one value against many cases with ===. Cases without a break or return fall through into the next one, which is useful for shared results and a classic bug otherwise. LoopsLoops repeat work. Pick the loop by what you are repeating over. Loops with async stepsTest code is full of steps that take time: open a page, click, wait for a response. These steps return promises and are written with await (chapter 11 explains how they work). Loops and await have one trap that breaks many first test suites. Throwing and catching errorsCode signals a problem by throwing an error. The error travels up through the functions that called it until a try block catches it. If nothing catches it, the program stops. Polling and retry loopsTwo loops sit at the centre of every test framework. You will rarely write them yourself, but knowing how they work explains timeouts, flaky tests and the options every tool offers.Remember: Retry infrastructure problems (a 503 from a test server, a slow container). Never retry to hide a real bug. A test that only passes on the third try is reporting something. Real world scenario: data driven password checksThis is data driven testing: the logic of the check is written once and the cases live in a table. Adding a case means adding one row."],[3,"comic","Deciding, repeating and recovering","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Every branch is a test case, and every waiting loop needs a timeout."],[3,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🚚Delivery charges An if, else if chain decides free, reduced or full shipping. The tests check each band and the values on each edge. 📦Order tracking A tracking page polls the server every few seconds until the status says delivered, then stops. 🔁Flaky test server A CI job retries a request that fails with 503 three times with growing delays before reporting the server as down. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Control Flow, Loops and Errors\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 9Welcome This chapter covers decisions, loops and errors, and ends with the polling and retry loops every test framework is built on. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[4,"","Functions, Scope and Closures","Function declarations and expressions, arrow functions, default and rest parameters, options objects, lexical scope, hoisting and the temporal dead zone, closures and private state, the loop closure bug, this with call, apply and bind and why Mocha needs function, higher order functions, memoize, debounce, recursion and a closure based test data factory. · function · arrow function · parameters · scope · hoisting · closure · this · bind · call · apply · higher order function · callback · memoize · debounce · recursion · test data factory 🟨 JavaScript›🌱 Foundations›Chapter 5 of 31 🧩 Step 1 · Topic 5 of 5 · Beginner"],[4,"top","","Declarations and arrows, defaults, rest and options objects, scope and hoisting, closures, how this is decided, higher order functions such as memoize and debounce, and recursion on the call stack. ⏱️ About 20 min read📚 8 parts🎬 3 animations▶ 12 runnable examples🧪 1 real test run🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 5 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Closures and this explain how describe, beforeEach and test share variables, why Mocha and Cypress sometimes need function instead of arrows, and how to build clean test data factories. 1Declaring functions 2Function expressions and arrow functions 3Scope, hoisting and the temporal dead zone 4Closures 5this, call, apply and bind 6Higher order functions 7Recursion and the call stack 8Real world scenario: a test data factory Lesson 1 of 8"],[4,"functions","Declaring functions","A function is a named, reusable piece of code. It takes parameters, does some work and returns a value. A function without a return returns undefined. Declaring and calling functions · ▶ Runs function add(a, b) { return a + b; } console.log(add(2, 3)); console.log(add(2)); // missing arguments are undefined console.log(add(2, 3, 99)); // extra arguments are ignoredfunction logIn(user, password = \"Secret12\", remember = false) { return`${user} / ${password} / remember=${remember}`; } console.log(logIn(\"asha\")); console.log(logIn(\"ravi\", undefined, true)); // undefined uses the default console.log(logIn(\"mei\", null)); // null does not Output 5 NaN 5 asha / Secret12 / remember=false ravi / Secret12 / remember=true mei / null / remember=false JavaScript does not check how many arguments you pass. Missing ones are undefined and extra ones are ignored. Default values replace undefined only. Passing null keeps null."],[4,"functions","Rest, spread and options objects","Rest parameters, spread and options objects · ▶ Runs function total(label, ...prices) { // rest: gathers the remaining arguments into an arrayconst sum = prices.reduce((s, p) => s + p, 0); return`${label}: ${sum}`; } console.log(total(\"cart\", 100, 250, 75)); const prices = [10, 20, 30]; console.log(Math.max(...prices)); // spread: spreads an array into arguments console.log(total(\"again\", ...prices, 40)); function createUser({ name, role = \"viewer\", active = true } = {}) { // destructured optionsreturn { name, role, active }; } console.log(createUser({ name: \"Asha\", role: \"admin\" })); console.log(createUser()); Output cart: 425 30 again: 100 { name: 'Asha', role: 'admin', active: true } { name: undefined, role: 'viewer', active: true } When a function has more than two or three parameters, take one options object and destructure it. Calls read like labels (launch({ headless: true, slowMo: 50 })) and the order stops mattering. Playwright, Cypress and Jest all configure this way. Lesson 2 of 8"],[4,"arrows","Function expressions and arrow functions","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightJestCypress A function can also be written as an expression and stored in a variable. The shorter arrow function syntax is the most common form in modern code and in test files. Function expressions and arrow functions · ▶ Runs const square = function (n) { return n * n; }; // function expressionconst cube = n => n * n * n; // arrow, one parameter, implicit returnconst greet = (first, last) => `Hi ${first} ${last}`; const makeUser = name => ({ name, createdAt: \"today\" }); // wrap an object in ( )const log = () => { console.log(\"no parameters, a block body\"); }; console.log(square(4), cube(3)); console.log(greet(\"Asha\", \"Rao\")); console.log(makeUser(\"Ravi\")); log(); Output 16 27 Hi Asha Rao { name: 'Ravi', createdAt: 'today' } no parameters, a block body Form Notes function name() {} Hoisted, so it can be called before its line. Has its own this. const f = function () {} Not hoisted. Has its own this. const f = () => {} Not hoisted. No own this or arguments: it uses the ones around it. Cannot be called with new. Test files are mostly arrow functions: test(\"adds to cart\", async ({ page }) => { ... }). The exceptions are Mocha and Cypress code that uses this, covered below. Lesson 3 of 8"],[4,"scope","Scope, hoisting and the temporal dead zone","Scope decides where a name can be seen. JavaScript looks for a name in the current block, then in each enclosing block and function, out to the global scope. This chain is fixed by where the code is written, which is called lexical scope. 🎬 Try it"],[4,"scope","Looking up a name through the scope chain","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. const app = \"Shop\"; function checkout() { const total = 499; if (total > 100) { const msg = \"big\"; console.log(app, total, msg); } } checkout(); Global scope checkout() scope if block scope Lookup Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 8 Global, function and block scope · ▶ Runs const appName = \"Shop\"; // global scopefunction checkout() { const total = 499; // function scopeif (total > 100) { const message = \"big order\"; // block scope console.log(appName, total, message); } console.log(typeof message); // not visible outside its block } checkout(); console.log(typeof total); // not visible outside the functionlet level = \"global\"; function shadow() { let level = \"inside\"; // a new variable that hides the outer one console.log(level); } shadow(); console.log(level); Output Shop 499 big order undefined undefined inside global"],[4,"scope","Hoisting","Before running a scope, the engine registers its declarations. Function declarations are ready at once. var exists but is undefined. let and const exist but cannot be touched until their line runs. That gap is the temporal dead zone. Hoisting and the temporal dead zone · ▶ Runs console.log(sayHi()); // function declarations are hoisted: this worksfunction sayHi() { return\"hi\"; } console.log(typeof oldStyle); // var is hoisted but undefinedvar oldStyle = 1; try { console.log(modern); // let and const are in the \"temporal dead zone\" } catch (err) { console.log(err.name + \": \" + err.message); } let modern = 2; Output hi undefined ReferenceError: Cannot access 'modern' before initialization Declare variables at the top of the block where you need them, with const or let. Then hoisting never surprises you. Lesson 4 of 8"],[4,"closures","Closures","🧪 QA essentialJestMochaPlaywright A closure is a function together with the variables it could see when it was created. Those variables stay alive as long as the function does, even after the outer function has returned. Closures give you private state without classes. 🎬 Try it"],[4,"closures","A closure keeps its variables alive","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. function makeCounter() { let count = 0; return () => ++count; } const next = makeCounter(); next(); next(); Global Kept alive by the closure Returned values Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 4 A closure keeps its variables alive · ▶ Runs function makeCounter(start = 0) { let count = start; // private: only the inner functions can see itreturn { next: () => ++count, reset: () => { count = start; }, get value() { return count; }, }; } const visits = makeCounter(); const retries = makeCounter(10); visits.next(); visits.next(); retries.next(); console.log(visits.value, retries.value); visits.reset(); console.log(visits.value); console.log(visits.count); // there is no way to reach the variable directly Output 2 11 0 undefined Each call to makeCounter creates a fresh count, so visits and retries never interfere. The classic loop closure bug · ▶ Runs const viaVar = []; for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) viaVar.push(() => i); console.log(viaVar.map(f => f())); // one shared i, already 3const viaLet = []; for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) viaLet.push(() => j); console.log(viaLet.map(f => f())); // let makes a new j for every turn Output [ 3, 3, 3 ] [ 0, 1, 2 ] With var, every function created in the loop shares one variable, which has its final value by the time they run. let creates a new binding for each turn. This was a famous interview question before let existed. Closures are everywhere in test code: a let page declared in a describe block and set in beforeEach is captured by every test inside. Test data factories, custom wait helpers and Page Object methods all rely on them. Lesson 5 of 8"],[4,"this","this, call, apply and bind","🧪 QA essentialMochaCypressMocha this.timeout and Cypress aliases on this only work inside function, not arrow functions. this is decided by how a function is called, not where it is written. Arrow functions are the exception: they never get their own this and use the one from the surrounding code. Call this is obj.method() obj, the object before the dot fn() undefined in strict code (the global object in old sloppy code) fn.call(x, a, b), fn.apply(x, [a, b]) x, chosen by you for this one call fn.bind(x) a new function where this is always x new Fn() the new object being created arrow function whatever this was where the arrow was written How this is decided · ▶ Runs const cart = { owner: \"Asha\", items: [\"pen\", \"book\"], describe() { return`${this.owner} has ${this.items.length} items`; // this is the object before the dot }, describeLater() { returnthis.items.map(item => `${this.owner}: ${item}`); // arrows use the outer this }, }; console.log(cart.describe()); console.log(cart.describeLater()); const loose = cart.describe; // the method is taken off the objecttry { console.log(loose()); // no object before the dot, so this is undefined } catch (err) { console.log(err.message); } const bound = cart.describe.bind(cart); console.log(bound()); console.log(cart.describe.call({ owner: \"Ravi\", items: [1, 2, 3] })); Output Asha has 2 items [ 'Asha: pen', 'Asha: book' ] Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'owner') Asha has 2 items Ravi has 3 items"],[4,"this","Why test runners care","Mocha puts helpers such as this.timeout() and this.retries() on the test's this. Cypress stores aliases on it too (this.user after cy.wrap(user).as(\"user\")). Both only work when the test is a normal function. An arrow test borrows this from the code around it, and below, where describe is an arrow too, that is the module and not Mocha. Mocha needs function, not arrows, to use this · 🧪 Tests run, one fails on purpose 📄 package.json { \"name\": \"mocha-this\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"private\": true, \"scripts\": { \"test\": \"mocha\" } } 📄 slow.test.js const assert = require(\"node:assert\"); describe(\"report export\", () => { it(\"can raise its own timeout with a normal function\", function () { this.timeout(5000); // Mocha passes its context as this assert.ok(true); }); it(\"cannot do the same in an arrow function\", () => { this.timeout(5000); // this comes from the arrow describe: the module, not Mocha }); }); Terminal $ npx mocha slow.test.js Terminal output report export ✔ can raise its own timeout with a normal function 1) cannot do the same in an arrow function 1 passing 1 failing 1) report export cannot do the same in an arrow function: TypeError: this.timeout is not a function at Context.<anonymous> (slow.test.js:10:10) If a Mocha or Cypress test uses this, write both the describe and the test with function. Everywhere else, arrows are fine. Lesson 6 of 8"],[4,"higher-order","Higher order functions","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypress A higher order function takes a function as an argument or returns one. You already used one: array.map(fn). They let you write the shape of a task once and plug in the details. Higher order functions · ▶ Runs // Functions are values: store them, pass them, return them.const rules = { required: v => v !== \"\" || \"is required\", email: v => /^[^@\\s]+@[^@\\s]+\\.\\w+$/.test(v) || \"must be an email\", min: n => v => v.length >= n || `must be at least ${n} characters`, // returns a function }; function validate(value, ...checks) { const errors = []; for (const check of checks) { const result = check(value); if (result !== true) errors.push(result); } return errors; } console.log(validate(\"asha@example.com\", rules.required, rules.email)); console.log(validate(\"ab\", rules.required, rules.email, rules.min(3))); console.log(validate(\"\", rules.required)); Output [] [ 'must be an email', 'must be at least 3 characters' ] [ 'is required' ] rules.min(3) returns a new function that remembers 3. Returning configured functions like this is the basis of matchers, middleware and plugins. Memoize and debounce, two closures you will meet · ▶ Runs function memoize(fn) { const cache = newMap(); return arg => { if (!cache.has(arg)) cache.set(arg, fn(arg)); return cache.get(arg); }; } let calls = 0; const slowSquare = n => { calls++; return n * n; }; const fastSquare = memoize(slowSquare); console.log(fastSquare(9), fastSquare(9), fastSquare(4), fastSquare(9)); console.log(\"real calls:\", calls); function debounce(fn, wait) { let timer; return (...args) => { clearTimeout(timer); timer = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), wait); }; } const search = debounce(text => console.log(\"searching for\", text), 30); search(\"p\"); search(\"ph\"); search(\"pho\"); search(\"phone\"); // typed quickly Output 81 81 16 81 real calls: 2 searching for phone memoize remembers results so a slow, pure function runs once per input. debounce waits until calls stop for a while, then runs once. Search boxes use it so the server gets one request instead of one per key press. Debounced inputs explain a classic test failure: the test types \"phone\" and checks the results at once, before the debounce timer fires. Assert on the results with an auto-waiting check (await expect(list).toHaveCount(3)) instead of reading them immediately. Lesson 7 of 8"],[4,"recursion","Recursion and the call stack","A recursive function calls itself on a smaller piece of the problem. It needs a base case that stops the calls. Every call waits on the call stack until the one it made returns. 🎬 Try it"],[4,"recursion","factorial(3) on the call stack","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. function factorial(n) { if (n <= 1) return1; return n * factorial(n - 1); } factorial(3); Call stack (top is running) Returned Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 6 Recursion and the call stack · ▶ Runs function factorial(n) { if (n <= 1) return1; // base case: stops the recursionreturn n * factorial(n - 1); // recursive case: a smaller problem } console.log(factorial(5)); // Recursion fits nested data, such as a site menuconst menu = { label: \"Home\", children: [ { label: \"Shop\", children: [{ label: \"Phones\" }, { label: \"Laptops\", children: [{ label: \"Gaming\" }] }] }, { label: \"Help\" }, ] }; function allLabels(node, depth = 0) { const lines = [\" \".repeat(depth) + node.label]; for (const child of node.children ?? []) lines.push(...allLabels(child, depth + 1)); return lines; } console.log(allLabels(menu).join(\"\\n\")); function forever(n) { return forever(n + 1); } // no base casetry { forever(0); } catch (err) { console.log(err.name + \": \" + err.message); } Output 120 Home Shop Phones Laptops Gaming Help RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded Recursion is natural for nested data: menus, folder trees, JSON with nested objects, the DOM itself. Without a base case, the stack fills up and you get RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded. Lesson 8 of 8"],[4,"scenario","Real world scenario: a test data factory","🧪 QA essential The situationYour sign up tests keep failing with \"email already registered\" because every test uses test@example.com. You need valid, unique users by default, and an easy way for one test to ask for an admin or an unverified user. A test data factory built on a closure · ▶ Runs // A test data builder: unique, valid data by default, overridable when a test needs something special.function userFactory(prefix = \"qa\") { let seq = 0; // private counter, kept by the closurereturnfunction build(overrides = {}) { seq++; return { id: seq, name: `Test User ${seq}`, email: `${prefix}.user${seq}@example.com`, role: \"customer\", verified: true, ...overrides, // the test's own values win }; }; } const buildUser = userFactory(\"checkout\"); const normal = buildUser(); const admin = buildUser({ role: \"admin\" }); const unverified = buildUser({ verified: false, email: \"not-an-email\" }); console.table([normal, admin, unverified]); const other = userFactory(\"search\")(); // a separate counter for another suite console.log(other.email); Output ┌─────────┬────┬───────────────┬──────────────────────────────┬────────────┬──────────┐ │ (index) │ id │ name │ email │ role │ verified │ ├─────────┼────┼───────────────┼──────────────────────────────┼────────────┼──────────┤ │ 0 │ 1 │ 'Test User 1' │ 'checkout.user1@example.com' │ 'customer' │ true │ │ 1 │ 2 │ 'Test User 2' │ 'checkout.user2@example.com' │ 'admin' │ true │ │ 2 │ 3 │ 'Test User 3' │ 'not-an-email' │ 'customer' │ false │ └─────────┴────┴───────────────┴──────────────────────────────┴────────────┴──────────┘ search.user1@example.com The closure keeps a private seq counter per factory, so emails never repeat within a run. The object spread ...overrides lets each test change only what it cares about. The test reads as \"a user, but unverified\". Separate factories have separate counters, so suites do not depend on each other. The same pattern builds fixtures for unit tests of the app code itself. Libraries such as Fishery and @faker-js/faker grow it further with realistic random values. 🧮"],[4,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 What does add(2) return? function add(a, b) { return a + b; } b is undefined. Answer: NaN b is undefined. 2 + undefined = NaN C2 What does total(1, 2, 3, 4) return? const total = (...n) => n.reduce((s, x) => s + x, 0); Rest gathers all four numbers. Answer: 10 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10 C1 What does the last line print? function makeCounter() { let c = 0; return () => ++c; } const next = makeCounter(); next(); next(); console.log(next()); Each call adds one. Answer: 3 The counter goes 1, 2, 3. C2 What is factorial(5)? Multiply 5 down to 1. Answer: 120 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 120 C1 How many times does slowSquare really run? const fast = memoize(slowSquare); fast(9); fast(9); fast(4); fast(9); The cache remembers each input once. Answer: 2 Inputs: 9, 9, 4, 9. Only 9 and 4 are new. That is 2 real calls. C2 factorial(4) is called. How many frames are on the call stack at its deepest point? factorial(4), (3), (2), (1). Answer: 4 Each call waits for the next one until n is 1. Frames: factorial(4), factorial(3), factorial(2), factorial(1). That is 4. C1 A debounced search waits 300 ms. A user types 5 letters 100 ms apart. How many ms after the first key press is the request sent? The timer restarts on each key. Answer: 700 The last key is pressed at 400 ms. 400 + 300 = 700 ms C2 What does this print? function getN() { returnthis.n; } const bound = getN.bind({ n: 6 }); console.log(bound.call({ n: 9 })); bind fixes this but call cannot change it again. Answer: 6 bound always uses { n: 6 }. Calling it with call on another object does not change a bound function. 🎯"],[4,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 What does a function without a return statement return? Nothing was given back. Without return, a call gives undefined. Q2 What does this arrow return? const make = () => ({ id: 1 }); console.log(make()); The object is wrapped in parentheses. Parentheses make the braces an object, so it is returned. Q3 What does ...nums do in function sum(...nums)? In a parameter list it gathers. In a parameter list, ... is a rest parameter and gathers the arguments into a real array. Q1 What does this print? const fs = []; for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) fs.push(() => i); console.log(...fs.map(f => f())); How many i variables are there with var? With var there is one shared i, which is 3 when the functions run. Q2 What does logIn(\"asha\", null) use for the password? function logIn(user, password = \"Secret12\") { return password; } Defaults only replace one value. Default values apply only when the argument is undefined. Q3 Why does this.timeout(5000) fail inside it(\"...\", () => {...}) in Mocha? Who decides this for an arrow? An arrow uses the outer this, so Mocha cannot hand it the test context. Q1 What happens here? console.log(x); let x = 2; let is hoisted differently from var. The name exists but is in the temporal dead zone until its line runs. Q2 In a module, what is this inside method when it is called like this? const f = obj.method; f(); There is nothing before the dot. Modules are strict, and a plain call gives this as undefined. Q3 What does fn.bind(x) return? bind does not call. call and apply call immediately. bind returns a new function for later. Q1 A search box is debounced by 300 ms. A test types and reads the result count straight away. What is the most likely outcome? When does the debounced function run? The request only fires 300 ms after the last key press. Use an auto-waiting assertion. Q2 Which function keeps state between calls without a global variable? Think closures. A closure keeps the variables of the function that created it. Q3 What does this print? const make = () => { let count = 0; return () => ++count; }; const a = make(), b = make(); console.log(a(), b()); Each factory call makes its own count. a and b each have their own count, so both print 1."],[4,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[4,"discount","A discount with a default","Write discount(price, percent = 10) that returns the price after the discount, rounded to two decimals as a number. Give percent a default. Round with Math.round(x * 100) / 100. function discount(price, percent = 10) { returnMath.round(price * (100 - percent)) / 100; } Multiplying by (100 - percent) and dividing by 100 once keeps rounding errors small. 🌿 Medium"],[4,"counter","A counter with a closure","Write makeCounter(start = 0) that returns an object with next() (adds one and returns the new value), reset() (back to start) and peek() (returns the value without changing it). Keep let count = start inside makeCounter and return three arrow functions that use it. function makeCounter(start = 0) { let count = start; return { next: () => ++count, reset: () => { count = start; }, peek: () => count, }; } Each call to makeCounter gets its own count, so counters never share state. 🔥 Hard"],[4,"once","Run only once","Write once(fn). It returns a function that calls fn with its arguments the first time and returns the result. Every later call returns that first result without calling fn again. This is how \"log in only once per suite\" helpers work. Keep two variables in the closure: whether it has run, and the result. function once(fn) { let done = false; let result; return (...args) => { if (!done) { done = true; result = fn(...args); } return result; }; } Using a separate done flag works even when the first result is undefined. 🧠 Expert"],[4,"debounce","Write debounce","Write debounce(fn, wait). The returned function restarts a timer on every call and calls fn with the latest arguments once no call has happened for wait milliseconds. Keep the timer id in the closure. clearTimeout the old one before starting a new one. function debounce(fn, wait) { let timer; return (...args) => { clearTimeout(timer); timer = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), wait); }; } Each call cancels the pending timer, so only the last call in a burst survives long enough to run."],[4,"debounce","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. Declaring functionsA function is a named, reusable piece of code. It takes parameters, does some work and returns a value. A function without a return returns undefined. Function expressions and arrow functionsA function can also be written as an expression and stored in a variable. The shorter arrow function syntax is the most common form in modern code and in test files. Scope, hoisting and the temporal dead zoneScope decides where a name can be seen. JavaScript looks for a name in the current block, then in each enclosing block and function, out to the global scope. This chain is fixed by where the code is written, which is called lexical scope.Remember: Declare variables at the top of the block where you need them, with const or let. Then hoisting never surprises you. ClosuresA closure is a function together with the variables it could see when it was created. Those variables stay alive as long as the function does, even after the outer function has returned. Closures give you private state without classes. this, call, apply and bindthis is decided by how a function is called, not where it is written. Arrow functions are the exception: they never get their own this and use the one from the surrounding code.Remember: If a Mocha or Cypress test uses this, write both the describe and the test with function. Everywhere else, arrows are fine. Higher order functionsA higher order function takes a function as an argument or returns one. You already used one: array.map(fn). They let you write the shape of a task once and plug in the details. Recursion and the call stackA recursive function calls itself on a smaller piece of the problem. It needs a base case that stops the calls. Every call waits on the call stack until the one it made returns. Real world scenario: a test data factory"],[4,"comic","Functions in four pictures","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Small pure functions are the easiest code in the world to test."],[4,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🔎Search as you type An online shop debounces the search box, so typing \"phone\" sends one request instead of five. 🧾Form validation A sign up page builds its rules from small functions such as required, email and min(8), and runs them as a list. 🗂️Nested categories A recursive function renders a category tree of any depth in an e-commerce menu. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Functions, Scope and Closures\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 10Welcome This chapter covers functions, arrows, scope, closures, this, higher order functions and recursion. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[5,"","Objects and Arrays","Object literals, keys, entries and freeze, destructuring with defaults and renames, spread and the shallow copy trap, structuredClone, array basics, sorting with compare functions and the ES2023 copying methods, map, filter, reduce, find, some, every and flatMap, Object.groupBy and Sets, and checking a sorted product listing with Vitest matchers. · object · array · destructuring · spread · structuredClone · deep copy · map · filter · reduce · find · some · every · sort · toSorted · groupBy · Set · toEqual · arrayContaining 🟨 JavaScript›📦 Working with data›Chapter 6 of 31 📦 Step 2 · Topic 1 of 3 · Beginner"],[5,"top","","Objects and their tools, destructuring, spread with shallow and deep copies, arrays, sorting without surprises, map, filter, reduce and friends, and grouping data for reports. ⏱️ About 20 min read📚 7 parts🎬 2 animations▶ 10 runnable examples🧪 1 real test run🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 6 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers UI tests read lists of element texts and API tests read nested JSON. This chapter is the toolkit for turning both into clear checks. 1Objects 2Destructuring 3Spread, copies and immutability 4Arrays 5Array methods: map, filter, reduce and friends 6Grouping, counting and unique values 7Real world scenario: verify a sorted product listing Lesson 1 of 7"],[5,"objects","Objects","🧪 QA essential An object groups related values under names called keys or properties. Almost everything you test is an object: a user, an API response, a configuration, a page element. Creating and changing objects · ▶ Runs const product = { id: 101, name: \"Wireless Mouse\", \"list-price\": 899, // a key with a dash needs quotes tags: [\"electronics\", \"office\"], seller: { name: \"TechMart\", rating: 4.6 }, label() { return`${this.name} (#${this.id})`; }, // a method }; console.log(product.name, product[\"list-price\"], product.seller.rating); const field = \"id\"; console.log(product[field]); // brackets take any expression console.log(product.label()); product.stock = 25; // add product.name = \"Silent Mouse\"; // changedelete product.tags; // remove console.log(\"stock\"in product, Object.hasOwn(product, \"tags\")); console.log(Object.keys(product)); Output Wireless Mouse 899 4.6 101 Wireless Mouse (#101) true false [ 'id', 'name', 'list-price', 'seller', 'label', 'stock' ] Use dot notation (user.name) for normal keys and brackets (user[\"list-price\"], user[field]) for unusual keys or keys held in a variable. Reading a missing key gives undefined, not an error. Reading a key of a missing object is the error. Object.hasOwn(obj, key) checks whether the object itself has the key. Shorthand, entries and freeze · ▶ Runs const name = \"Asha\", role = \"admin\"; const key = \"lastLogin\"; const user = { name, role, [key]: \"2026-10-20\" }; // shorthand and a computed key console.log(user); console.log(Object.values(user)); for (const [k, v] ofObject.entries(user)) console.log(`${k} -> ${v}`); const prices = { pen: 20, book: 350, bag: 1200 }; const withTax = Object.fromEntries( Object.entries(prices).map(([item, p]) => [item, Math.round(p * 1.18)]) ); console.log(withTax); const config = Object.freeze({ baseUrl: \"https://shop.example\", retries: 2 }); config.retries = 99; // ignored (it would throw in strict code) console.log(config.retries); Output { name: 'Asha', role: 'admin', lastLogin: '2026-10-20' } [ 'Asha', 'admin', '2026-10-20' ] name -> Asha role -> admin lastLogin -> 2026-10-20 { pen: 24, book: 413, bag: 1416 } 2 Object.entries and Object.fromEntries together let you use every array method on an object: turn it into pairs, transform them, turn them back. Lesson 2 of 7"],[5,"destructuring","Destructuring","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightJestVitestPlaywright fixtures and test.each rows arrive as objects you destructure. Destructuring pulls values out of objects and arrays into variables in one line. It can rename, give defaults, skip items and collect the rest. Destructuring objects and arrays · ▶ Runs const response = { status: 200, data: { user: { id: 7, name: \"Asha\", email: \"asha@example.com\" }, roles: [\"admin\", \"editor\"] }, }; const { status, data: { user: { name, email }, roles: [firstRole, ...otherRoles] } } = response; console.log(status, name, email, firstRole, otherRoles); const { timeout = 5000, retries: maxRetries = 1 } = { retries: 3 }; // defaults and renaming console.log(timeout, maxRetries); let a = 1, b = 2; [a, b] = [b, a]; // swap without a temporary variable console.log(a, b); const [, second, , fourth = \"none\"] = [\"x\", \"y\", \"z\"]; console.log(second, fourth); const { id, ...withoutId } = response.data.user; // take one field, keep the rest console.log(id, withoutId); Output 200 Asha asha@example.com admin [ 'editor' ] 5000 3 2 1 y none 7 { name: 'Asha', email: 'asha@example.com' } Test runners use destructuring all the time, so it is worth being fluent in it. Destructuring inside test runners · Snippets, full runs in chapters 20 to 22 // Playwright: the test receives one object and destructures the fixtures it needs test(\"search shows results\", async ({ page, request, baseURL }) => { await page.goto(baseURL + \"/search?q=phone\"); }); // Jest and Vitest: test.each rows are destructured too test.each([ { input: \"Secret12\", valid: true }, { input: \"short\", valid: false }, ])(\"password $input is valid: $valid\", ({ input, valid }) => { expect(isValidPassword(input)).toBe(valid); }); In Playwright, async ({ page }) is destructuring: the runner passes one object of fixtures and you pick the ones you need. Only the fixtures you name are set up, which keeps tests fast. Lesson 3 of 7"],[5,"spread","Spread, copies and immutability","🧪 QA essential The spread syntax ... copies the items of an array or the keys of an object into a new one. When keys repeat, the later one wins, which makes it perfect for \"defaults plus overrides\". Spread, shallow copies and deep copies · ▶ Runs const defaults = { browser: \"chromium\", headless: true, retries: 0 }; const ciConfig = { ...defaults, retries: 2 }; // later keys win console.log(ciConfig); const base = [1, 2, 3]; const more = [0, ...base, 4]; console.log(more); const order = { id: 1, items: [{ sku: \"A1\", qty: 1 }] }; const shallow = { ...order }; shallow.items[0].qty = 99; // nested objects are still shared console.log(order.items[0].qty); const deep = structuredClone(order); deep.items[0].qty = 1; console.log(order.items[0].qty, deep.items[0].qty); Output { browser: 'chromium', headless: true, retries: 2 } [ 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 ] 99 99 1 🎬 Try it"],[5,"spread","Shallow copy against deep copy","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. const order = { id: 1, items: [{ qty: 1 }] }; const shallow = { ...order }; shallow.id = 2; shallow.items[0].qty = 99; const deep = structuredClone(order); deep.items[0].qty = 5; Names Outer objects Nested items Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 6 Spread makes a shallow copy: the outer object is new, but nested objects and arrays are shared. Use structuredClone for a full deep copy. Shared nested test data is a common cause of tests that pass alone and fail together. Give each test its own copy with structuredClone(baseOrder), or build data with a factory function as in chapter 5. Lesson 4 of 7"],[5,"arrays","Arrays","An array is an ordered list. Indexes start at 0, length is the number of items and at(-1) reads from the end. Array basics · ▶ Runs const tests = [\"login\", \"search\"]; tests.push(\"checkout\"); // add to the end tests.unshift(\"setup\"); // add to the start console.log(tests, tests.length); console.log(tests[0], tests.at(-1)); // at(-1) is the last itemconst last = tests.pop(); // remove from the endconst first = tests.shift(); // remove from the start console.log(first, last, tests); const nums = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]; console.log(nums.slice(1, 3)); // copy part, original unchangedconst removed = nums.splice(1, 2, 99); // remove 2 items at index 1 and insert 99 console.log(removed, nums); console.log(nums.includes(99), nums.indexOf(40), nums.join(\" | \")); console.log(Array.from({ length: 4 }, (_, i) => i * i)); console.log([1, [2, [3, [4]]]].flat(Infinity)); Output [ 'setup', 'login', 'search', 'checkout' ] 4 setup checkout setup checkout [ 'login', 'search' ] [ 20, 30 ] [ 20, 30 ] [ 10, 99, 40, 50 ] true 2 10 | 99 | 40 | 50 [ 0, 1, 4, 9 ] [ 1, 2, 3, 4 ] Changes the array Returns a new array or value push pop shift unshift splice sort reverse fill slice concat map filter flat flatMap toSorted toReversed toSpliced with Sorting, and copies instead of changes · ▶ Runs const scores = [72, 95, 61, 88]; const sortedCopy = scores.toSorted((a, b) => a - b); // ES2023: a new sorted arrayconst reversedCopy = scores.toReversed(); const replaced = scores.with(0, 100); // copy with index 0 replaced console.log(scores); console.log(sortedCopy, reversedCopy, replaced); scores.sort(); // sorts in place, and as TEXT by default console.log(scores); console.log([10, 9, 100, 1].sort()); // the classic surprise console.log([10, 9, 100, 1].sort((a, b) => a - b)); // numbers need a compare function Output [ 72, 95, 61, 88 ] [ 61, 72, 88, 95 ] [ 88, 61, 95, 72 ] [ 100, 95, 61, 88 ] [ 61, 72, 88, 95 ] [ 1, 10, 100, 9 ] [ 1, 9, 10, 100 ] sort() without a compare function sorts as text, so 100 comes before 9. For numbers always pass (a, b) => a - b. It also changes the original array. ES2023 added toSorted, toReversed, toSpliced and with, which leave the original alone. Lesson 5 of 7"],[5,"array-methods","Array methods: map, filter, reduce and friends","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypressSeleniumLists of element texts are arrays, and checking them is map, filter and every. These methods replace most hand written loops. Each one takes a function and calls it for every item. 🎬 Try it"],[5,"array-methods","Array methods, one element at a time","Change the numbers, pick a method and press Play. nums nums.map(n => n * 2) input 3i0 8i1 1i2 6i3 5i4 result [ ] Start. The result starts as a new empty array. nums is never changed. 0 / 6 map, filter, reduce, find, some and every · ▶ Runs const cart = [ { sku: \"A1\", name: \"Mouse\", price: 899, qty: 2, inStock: true }, { sku: \"B2\", name: \"Keyboard\", price: 2499, qty: 1, inStock: true }, { sku: \"C3\", name: \"Webcam\", price: 3999, qty: 1, inStock: false }, { sku: \"D4\", name: \"Pad\", price: 299, qty: 3, inStock: true }, ]; const names = cart.map(item => item.name); const available = cart.filter(item => item.inStock); const total = available.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price * item.qty, 0); const webcam = cart.find(item => item.sku === \"C3\"); const cheapIndex = cart.findIndex(item => item.price < 500); const anyOut = cart.some(item => !item.inStock); const allPriced = cart.every(item => item.price > 0); console.log(names); console.log(available.length, total); console.log(webcam.name, cheapIndex, anyOut, allPriced); console.log(cart.findLast(item => item.inStock).name); console.log(cart.flatMap(item => Array(item.qty).fill(item.sku))); Output [ 'Mouse', 'Keyboard', 'Webcam', 'Pad' ] 3 5194 Webcam 3 true true Pad [ 'A1', 'A1', 'B2', 'C3', 'D4', 'D4', 'D4' ] Method Returns Typical use map new array, same length turn elements into their text, products into prices filter new array, only matching items failed tests, items in stock reduce one value built up step by step totals, counts, grouping find, findLast the first (or last) matching item, or undefined the row for one SKU findIndex its index, or -1 where a sort order breaks some, every true or false, and they stop early \"any error shown\", \"all prices positive\" flatMap map, then flatten one level one row per quantity Chaining methods into a pipeline · ▶ Runs const orders = [ { id: 1, customer: \"asha\", total: 1200, status: \"delivered\" }, { id: 2, customer: \"ravi\", total: 450, status: \"cancelled\" }, { id: 3, customer: \"asha\", total: 3100, status: \"delivered\" }, { id: 4, customer: \"mei\", total: 980, status: \"delivered\" }, ]; const topDelivered = orders .filter(o => o.status === \"delivered\") .toSorted((a, b) => b.total - a.total) .slice(0, 2) .map(o => `#${o.id} ${o.customer} ₹${o.total}`); console.log(topDelivered); Output [ '#3 asha ₹3100', '#1 asha ₹1200' ] Playwright's locator.allTextContents() and Cypress's .then($els => [...$els].map(el => el.innerText)) hand you an array of strings. From there it is plain JavaScript: map to numbers, every to check sorting, filter to find bad rows. Lesson 6 of 7"],[5,"grouping","Grouping, counting and unique values","🧪 QA essential Reports need data grouped and counted. ES2024 added Object.groupBy and Map.groupBy. reduce still handles any custom shape, and a Set removes duplicates. Grouping, counting and unique values · ▶ Runs const results = [ { name: \"login\", suite: \"auth\", status: \"passed\" }, { name: \"logout\", suite: \"auth\", status: \"failed\" }, { name: \"search\", suite: \"catalog\", status: \"passed\" }, { name: \"filters\", suite: \"catalog\", status: \"passed\" }, { name: \"pay\", suite: \"checkout\", status: \"failed\" }, ]; const bySuite = Object.groupBy(results, r => r.suite); // ES2024 console.log(Object.keys(bySuite), bySuite.auth.length); const counts = results.reduce((acc, r) => { acc[r.status] = (acc[r.status] ?? 0) + 1; return acc; }, {}); console.log(counts); const suites = [...newSet(results.map(r => r.suite))]; // unique values console.log(suites); const failedSuites = Object.entries(bySuite) .filter(([, list]) => list.some(r => r.status === \"failed\")) .map(([suite]) => suite); console.log(failedSuites); Output [ 'auth', 'catalog', 'checkout' ] 2 { passed: 3, failed: 2 } [ 'auth', 'catalog', 'checkout' ] [ 'auth', 'checkout' ] [...new Set(list)] is the shortest way to remove duplicates from a list of strings or numbers. For objects, compare a key, as the scenario below does. Lesson 7 of 7"],[5,"scenario","Real world scenario: verify a sorted product listing","🧪 QA essentialVitestJestPlaywright The situationA shop's \"Price: low to high\" sort has had bugs before. You read the text of every product card on the page and must check three things: the prices really go up, no product is listed twice, and the cheapest and dearest prices are right. Check a sorted product listing · ▶ Runs // Text read from the product cards of a listing sorted by \"Price: low to high\"const cardTexts = [ \"Wireless Mouse ₹899\", \"USB Hub ₹1,299\", \"Keyboard ₹2,499\", \"USB Hub ₹1,299\", \"Webcam ₹2,199\", ]; const products = cardTexts.map(text => { const [, name, price] = text.match(/^(.*) ₹([\\d,]+)$/); return { name, price: Number(price.replaceAll(\",\", \"\")) }; }); const prices = products.map(p => p.price); const outOfOrder = prices.findIndex((p, i) => i > 0 && p < prices[i - 1]); const isSorted = outOfOrder === -1; const seen = newSet(); const duplicates = products.filter(p => seen.has(p.name) || !seen.add(p.name)).map(p => p.name); console.log(\"prices:\", prices); console.log(\"sorted:\", isSorted, isSorted ? \"\" : `(breaks at position ${outOfOrder}: ${prices[outOfOrder - 1]} then ${prices[outOfOrder]})`); console.log(\"duplicates:\", duplicates); console.log(\"cheapest:\", Math.min(...prices), \"dearest:\", Math.max(...prices)); Output prices: [ 899, 1299, 2499, 1299, 2199 ] sorted: false (breaks at position 3: 2499 then 1299) duplicates: [ 'USB Hub' ] cheapest: 899 dearest: 2499 The check found two real problems in this data: the second USB Hub at ₹1,299 comes after Keyboard at ₹2,499, so the order breaks at position 3, and USB Hub is listed twice. Both point to the same bug, a duplicated card, which is the kind of finding a good report makes obvious. In a real suite the parsing lives in a helper module with its own unit tests, and the matchers for arrays and objects make the assertions short. Array and object matchers in Vitest · 🧪 Tests run 📄 listing.js exportfunction parseCards(texts) { return texts.map(text => { const [, name, price] = text.match(/^(.*) ₹([\\d,]+)$/); return { name, price: Number(price.replaceAll(\",\", \"\")) }; }); } exportconst isSortedAsc = list => list.every((x, i) => i === 0 || list[i - 1] <= x); 📄 listing.test.js import { describe, test, expect } from\"vitest\"; import { parseCards, isSortedAsc } from\"./listing.js\"; const cards = [\"Wireless Mouse ₹899\", \"USB Hub ₹1,299\", \"Keyboard ₹2,499\"]; describe(\"product listing\", () => { test(\"parses every card into name and price\", () => { expect(parseCards(cards)).toEqual([ { name: \"Wireless Mouse\", price: 899 }, { name: \"USB Hub\", price: 1299 }, { name: \"Keyboard\", price: 2499 }, ]); }); test(\"contains the hub, whatever else is there\", () => { expect(parseCards(cards)).toContainEqual({ name: \"USB Hub\", price: 1299 }); expect(parseCards(cards)).toEqual(expect.arrayContaining([expect.objectContaining({ name: \"Keyboard\" })])); }); test.each([ [[1, 2, 2, 5], true], [[3, 1], false], [[], true], ])(\"isSortedAsc(%j) is %s\", (list, expected) => { expect(isSortedAsc(list)).toBe(expected); }); }); Terminal $ npx vitest run --reporter=verbose Terminal output RUN v5 /project ✓ listing.test.js > product listing > parses every card into name and price ✓ listing.test.js > product listing > contains the hub, whatever else is there ✓ listing.test.js > product listing > isSortedAsc([1,2,2,5]) is true ✓ listing.test.js > product listing > isSortedAsc([3,1]) is false ✓ listing.test.js > product listing > isSortedAsc([]) is true Test Files 1 passed (1) Tests 5 passed (5) Start at 10:00:00 Duration 310ms Matcher Passes when toEqual(list) same items, same order, compared deeply toContainEqual(item) at least one item deeply equals item expect.arrayContaining([...]) the listed items appear, in any order, others allowed expect.objectContaining({...}) the listed keys match, other keys allowed toHaveLength(n) the length is n 🧮"],[5,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 What does [3, 8, 1].map(n => n * 2) give? Write it like [a, b, c]. Double each. Answer: [6, 16, 2] 3 × 2 = 6, 8 × 2 = 16, 1 × 2 = 2 C2 What is the length of the array after these lines? const a = [1, 2]; a.push(3); a.unshift(0); a.pop(); a.push(9); Track each change. Answer: 4 Start 2, push makes 3, unshift makes 4, pop makes 3, push makes 4. C1 What does this reduce return? const cart = [ { price: 899, qty: 2, inStock: true }, { price: 2499, qty: 1, inStock: false }, { price: 299, qty: 3, inStock: true }, { price: 3999, qty: 1, inStock: false }, ]; cart.filter(i => i.inStock).reduce((s, i) => s + i.price * i.qty, 0); Only items in stock count, price times quantity. Answer: 2695 In stock: the 899 item and the 299 item. 899 × 2 = 1798 299 × 3 = 897 1798 + 897 = 2695 C2 How many unique values are in [2, 3, 2, 5, 3, 2]? A Set keeps one of each. Answer: 3 The unique values are 2, 3 and 5. C1 What does [5, 1, 4].findIndex(x => x > 4) return? Is any item greater than 4, and where is the first? Answer: 0 5 > 4 is true at index 0, so the search stops there. C2 prices = [899, 1299, 2499, 2199]. What does prices.findIndex((p, i) => i > 0 && p < prices[i - 1]) return? Find where a price is lower than the one before. Answer: 3 Index 1: 1299 is not lower than 899. Index 2: 2499 is not lower than 1299. Index 3: 2199 is lower than 2499. The answer is 3. C1 What does [[1, 2], [3, [4, 5]]].flat().length give? flat() goes one level deep by default. Answer: 4 One level: [1, 2, 3, [4, 5]]. That has 4 items. C2 What is Object.keys(Object.groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], n => n % 3)).length? n % 3 can be 0, 1 or 2. Answer: 3 Groups: 1 and 4 under \"1\", 2 and 5 under \"2\", 3 and 6 under \"0\". Three keys. 🎯"],[5,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 Which method returns a new array with every item transformed? Same length, new values. map returns a new array of the same length. Q2 What does [10, 20, 30].at(-1) return? Negative counts from the end. at(-1) is the last item. Q3 How do you read a key called list-price? The dash is a minus sign in code. Keys with dashes or spaces need bracket notation. Q1 What does this print? console.log([10, 9, 100, 1].sort()); Default sort compares text. As text, \"10\" and \"100\" come before \"9\". Q2 What does this print? const a = { items: [{ qty: 1 }] }; const b = { ...a }; b.items[0].qty = 99; console.log(a.items[0].qty); Is the nested array copied? Spread is shallow, so items is shared. Q3 Which method stops at the first item that matches and returns that item? filter returns all matches. find returns the first match, or undefined. Q1 What are timeout and maxRetries? const { timeout = 5000, retries: maxRetries = 1 } = { retries: 3 }; Defaults apply to missing keys. timeout is missing so it takes 5000. retries is 3 and is renamed. Q2 Which matcher passes when an array contains an object with name \"Keyboard\", whatever its other keys and the other items? Combine two asymmetric matchers. arrayContaining allows other items and objectContaining allows other keys. Q3 What does reduce return here? [\"passed\", \"failed\", \"passed\"].reduce((acc, s) => { acc[s] = (acc[s] ?? 0) + 1; return acc; }, {}); The start value is an object. The accumulator starts as {} and counts each status. Q1 Which ES2023 method returns a sorted copy and leaves the original unchanged? The \"to\" methods copy. toSorted, toReversed, toSpliced and with never change the original. Q2 What does [1, 2, 3].some(x => x > 1) do after checking 2? One match is enough. some stops at the first match, every stops at the first failure. Q3 Why do two tests that pass alone fail together when they share a const testOrder object? const does not freeze. Both tests use the same object. Build a fresh copy per test."],[5,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[5,"pluck","Pluck one field","Write pluck(list, key) that returns an array with the value of key from every object. map with bracket notation. function pluck(list, key) { return list.map(item => item[key] ?? null); } Brackets read a key held in a variable. ?? null keeps missing values visible in JSON. 🌿 Medium"],[5,"sorted","Is it sorted","Write isSorted(list, order = \"asc\") that returns true when the numbers are in ascending (or \"desc\") order. Equal neighbours are allowed. An empty list is sorted. every gives you the index as its second argument, so you can look at list[i - 1]. function isSorted(list, order = \"asc\") { return list.every((x, i) => i === 0 || (order === \"asc\" ? list[i - 1] <= x : list[i - 1] >= x)); } Skipping index 0 and comparing each item with the one before covers every pair exactly once. 🔥 Hard"],[5,"count-by","Count by a key","Write countBy(list, key) that returns an object counting how many items have each value of key, such as { passed: 3, failed: 1 }. reduce with {} as the start value. function countBy(list, key) { return list.reduce((acc, item) => { acc[item[key]] = (acc[item[key]] ?? 0) + 1; return acc; }, {}); } The accumulator object grows one key at a time. ?? 0 handles the first time a value is seen. 🧠 Expert"],[5,"deep-equal","Deep equality","Write deepEqual(a, b) the way toEqual works for plain data: primitives with Object.is, arrays by length and items in order, plain objects by the same keys with deeply equal values. Handle primitives first, then make sure both are objects of the same kind, then compare keys and recurse. function deepEqual(a, b) { if (Object.is(a, b)) returntrue; if (typeof a !== \"object\" || typeof b !== \"object\" || a === null || b === null) returnfalse; if (Array.isArray(a) !== Array.isArray(b)) returnfalse; const ka = Object.keys(a), kb = Object.keys(b); if (ka.length !== kb.length) returnfalse; return ka.every(k => Object.hasOwn(b, k) && deepEqual(a[k], b[k])); } Arrays are objects whose keys are indexes, so the same key comparison works for both once you check they are the same kind."],[5,"deep-equal","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. ObjectsAn object groups related values under names called keys or properties. Almost everything you test is an object: a user, an API response, a configuration, a page element. DestructuringDestructuring pulls values out of objects and arrays into variables in one line. It can rename, give defaults, skip items and collect the rest. Spread, copies and immutabilityThe spread syntax ... copies the items of an array or the keys of an object into a new one. When keys repeat, the later one wins, which makes it perfect for \"defaults plus overrides\". ArraysAn array is an ordered list. Indexes start at 0, length is the number of items and at(-1) reads from the end. Array methods: map, filter, reduce and friendsThese methods replace most hand written loops. Each one takes a function and calls it for every item. Grouping, counting and unique valuesReports need data grouped and counted. ES2024 added Object.groupBy and Map.groupBy. reduce still handles any custom shape, and a Set removes duplicates. Real world scenario: verify a sorted product listingThe check found two real problems in this data: the second USB Hub at ₹1,299 comes after Keyboard at ₹2,499, so the order breaks at position 3, and USB Hub is listed twice."],[5,"comic","Data in, data checked","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Copy before you change, and pass numbers a compare function when sorting."],[5,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🛍️A shopping cart The cart total is a reduce over the items: price times quantity, summed, for items still in stock. 📊A test dashboard Results are grouped by suite with Object.groupBy, and each suite card shows its pass and fail counts. ⚙️Tool configuration playwright.config.js spreads shared settings into each project and overrides only the browser and viewport. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Objects and Arrays\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 9Welcome This chapter covers objects, destructuring, copies, arrays, the array methods and grouping data for reports. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[6,"","Strings, Regex, Numbers, Dates and JSON","String methods and Unicode, normalising page text for comparisons, regular expressions with groups, lookahead and flags, regex in Playwright, Cypress and Jest, Math and rounding traps, Intl.NumberFormat for currencies, Intl for relative times, plurals, lists, display names, graphemes and sorting, Date and time zone traps, Temporal, JSON with replacers and revivers, JSON fixture files and validating an order API response. · string · regex · regular expression · Math · Intl.NumberFormat · currency · Intl · localisation · PluralRules · Segmenter · Date · time zone · Temporal · JSON · JSON.parse · JSON.stringify · fixtures · toMatch · toMatchObject 🟨 JavaScript›📦 Working with data›Chapter 7 of 31 🔤 Step 2 · Topic 2 of 3 · Beginner"],[6,"top","","String methods and Unicode, comparing text read from a page, regular expressions in code and in assertions, Math and Intl number formatting, Intl for dates, plurals, lists and characters in every language, Date traps and the new Temporal API, and JSON for APIs and fixture files. ⏱️ About 26 min read📚 7 parts🎬 2 animations▶ 10 runnable examples🧪 1 real test run🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 14 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 7 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Text, pattern, price and date checks make up most assertions. This chapter shows how to make them strict where it matters and stable everywhere else. 1Working with strings 2Regular expressions 3Math and number formatting 4Intl: one app, every language and country 5Dates, time zones and Temporal 6JSON 7Real world scenario: validate an order API response Lesson 1 of 7"],[6,"strings","Working with strings","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypressSeleniumMost UI assertions compare text, and whitespace or case differences cause false failures. A string is text. Strings are immutable: every method returns a new string and the original never changes. Indexes start at 0. The string methods you use daily · ▶ Runs const title = \" Wireless Mouse, Silent Click \"; const t = title.trim(); console.log(`[${t}]`, t.length); console.log(t.toUpperCase(), \"|\", t.toLowerCase()); console.log(t.includes(\"Mouse\"), t.startsWith(\"Wire\"), t.endsWith(\"Click\")); console.log(t.indexOf(\"Silent\"), t.slice(0, 8), t.slice(-5), t.at(0)); console.log(t.split(\", \")); console.log(t.replace(\"Click\", \"Clicks\"), \"|\", \"a-b-c\".replaceAll(\"-\", \"/\")); console.log(\"7\".padStart(3, \"0\"), \"ab\".repeat(3), \"Total\".padEnd(8, \".\") + \"₹99\"); Output [Wireless Mouse, Silent Click] 28 WIRELESS MOUSE, SILENT CLICK | wireless mouse, silent click true true true 16 Wireless Click W [ 'Wireless Mouse', 'Silent Click' ] Wireless Mouse, Silent Clicks | a/b/c 007 ababab Total...₹99 Need Method remove spaces at the ends trim, trimStart, trimEnd search includes, startsWith, endsWith, indexOf, search cut out a part slice(start, end) (negative counts from the end), at change replace, replaceAll, toUpperCase, toLowerCase split and join split(separator) and the array's join pad and repeat padStart, padEnd, repeat"],[6,"strings","Unicode","Unicode, normalising and sorting text · ▶ Runs const word = \"café\"; const composed = \"cafe\\u0301\"; // e plus a combining accent looks the same console.log(word === composed, word.length, composed.length); console.log(word === composed.normalize(\"NFC\")); const thumbs = \"👍🏽\"; console.log(thumbs.length, [...thumbs].length); // code units against charactersconst names = [\"Zoë\", \"zara\", \"Émile\", \"adam\"]; console.log(names.toSorted()); console.log(names.toSorted((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b, \"en\", { sensitivity: \"base\" }))); Output false 4 5 true 4 2 [ 'Zoë', 'adam', 'zara', 'Émile' ] [ 'adam', 'Émile', 'zara', 'Zoë' ] length counts UTF-16 code units, not visible characters. Emoji and some accents count as 2 or more. Use [...str].length for characters, normalize(\"NFC\") before comparing and localeCompare for human sorting."],[6,"strings","Comparing text from the UI","Comparing text read from a page · ▶ Runs // Text read from a page often has extra spaces, line breaks and non breaking spacesconst fromPage = \" Order\\u00a0#4521\\n confirmed \"; const expected = \"Order #4521 confirmed\"; const clean = s => s.replace(/\\s+/g, \" \").trim(); console.log(JSON.stringify(fromPage)); console.log(clean(fromPage) === expected); console.log(clean(fromPage).toLowerCase().includes(\"confirmed\")); Output \" Order #4521\\n confirmed \" true true Page text often contains non breaking spaces, line breaks from wrapped layout and extra spaces from templates. Normalise both sides before comparing, or use an assertion that already does: Playwright's toHaveText collapses whitespace, and toContainText matches a part. Lesson 2 of 7"],[6,"regex","Regular expressions","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypressJestText assertions, locator names, URL checks and API field checks all accept regular expressions. A regular expression describes a pattern of text. It is written between slashes, followed by optional flags. 🎬 Try it"],[6,"regex","Regular expression tester","Edit the pattern, the flags or the text. Matches light up as you type. / /flags Call 555-1234 or 555-9876. Order ORD-2024 ships today. 2 matches 555-1234 at 5, groups: 5551234 555-9876 at 17, groups: 5559876 Regular expressions in action · ▶ Runs const text = \"Orders ORD-104233 and ORD-998120 shipped on 2026-10-18. Call +91 98450 12345.\"; console.log(/ORD-\\d{6}/.test(text)); // is there a match console.log(text.match(/ORD-\\d{6}/)[0]); // the first match console.log(text.match(/ORD-\\d{6}/g)); // every match with the g flagconst date = text.match(/(?<year>\\d{4})-(?<month>\\d{2})-(?<day>\\d{2})/); console.log(date.groups); // named groupsfor (const m of text.matchAll(/ORD-(\\d+)/g)) { console.log(\"order number\", m[1], \"at index\", m.index); } const phone = \"+91 98450 12345\"; console.log(phone.replace(/\\d(?=(?:\\D*\\d){4})/g, \"*\")); // mask every digit that has 4 more digits after it console.log(\"2026-10-18\".replace(/(\\d+)-(\\d+)-(\\d+)/, \"$3/$2/$1\")); console.log(RegExp.escape(\"price (₹)?\")); // ES2025: escape user text for a regex Output true ORD-104233 [ 'ORD-104233', 'ORD-998120' ] [Object: null prototype] { year: '2026', month: '10', day: '18' } order number 104233 at index 7 order number 998120 at index 22 +** ***** *2345 18/10/2026 \\x70rice\\x20\\(₹\\)\\? Piece Means \\d \\w \\s a digit, a word character, whitespace (capitals mean \"not\": \\D) [abc] [^abc] [a-z] one of, none of, a range * + ? {6} {2,4} repeat: 0 or more, 1 or more, optional, exactly 6, 2 to 4 ^ $ \\b start, end, word boundary ( ) (?<name> ) (?: ) capture group, named group, group without capturing a|b a or b (?=x) (?!x) followed by x, not followed by x (lookahead) flags g i m s u v y all matches, ignore case, multi line, dot matches new lines, unicode, unicode sets, sticky Test tools accept regular expressions almost everywhere text is expected. Patterns in assertions and locators · Snippets // Playwright: assert text with a pattern, find by a patternawait expect(page.getByTestId(\"order-id\")).toHaveText(/^ORD-\\d{6}$/); await page.getByRole(\"button\", { name: /add to (cart|basket)/i }).click(); // Cypress cy.contains(/order ORD-\\d{6} confirmed/i).should(\"be.visible\"); cy.url().should(\"match\", /\\/orders\\/\\d+$/); // Jest, Vitest expect(response.body.id).toMatch(/^ORD-\\d{6}$/); expect(response.body).toEqual({ id: expect.stringMatching(/^ORD-/), total: expect.any(Number) }); Anchor patterns with ^ and $ when the whole value must match. /ORD-\\d{6}/ also passes for XORD-1234567, while /^ORD-\\d{6}$/ does not. Lesson 3 of 7"],[6,"math","Math and number formatting","🧪 QA essential The Math object holds rounding, min and max, powers and random numbers. Intl.NumberFormat turns numbers into the exact text a user in each country expects. Math, rounding and formatting numbers · ▶ Runs console.log(Math.round(2.5), Math.round(-2.5), Math.floor(-2.5), Math.ceil(2.1), Math.trunc(-2.9)); console.log(Math.max(4, 9, 2), Math.min(...[4, 9, 2]), Math.abs(-7), Math.sqrt(81), Math.hypot(3, 4)); console.log((1.005).toFixed(2), Math.round(1.005 * 100) / 100); // both show a decimal rounding trapconst randomInt = (min, max) => min + Math.floor(Math.random() * (max - min + 1)); const roll = randomInt(1, 6); console.log(roll >= 1 && roll <= 6 && Number.isInteger(roll)); const inr = newIntl.NumberFormat(\"en-IN\", { style: \"currency\", currency: \"INR\" }); const usd = newIntl.NumberFormat(\"en-US\", { style: \"currency\", currency: \"USD\" }); const eur = newIntl.NumberFormat(\"de-DE\", { style: \"currency\", currency: \"EUR\" }); console.log(inr.format(1234567.5), usd.format(1234567.5), eur.format(1234567.5)); console.log(newIntl.NumberFormat(\"en\", { style: \"percent\", maximumFractionDigits: 1 }).format(0.4567)); console.log(newIntl.NumberFormat(\"en\", { notation: \"compact\" }).format(1520000)); Output 3 -2 -3 3 -2 9 2 7 9 5 1.00 1 true ₹12,34,567.50 $1,234,567.50 1.234.567,50 € 45.7% 1.5M Math.round rounds .5 up towards positive infinity, so -2.5 becomes -2. (1.005).toFixed(2) gives \"1.00\" because 1.005 is really stored as 1.00499999. For money, work in whole paise or cents. Math.random() is fine for test data, never for passwords or tokens. Use crypto.randomUUID() or crypto.getRandomValues() for those. To check a price on screen, format the expected number with the same Intl.NumberFormat settings the app uses and compare text with text. This catches the wrong currency sign, the wrong grouping (₹12,34,567 in India against $1,234,567 in the US) and wrong rounding. Lesson 4 of 7"],[6,"intl","Intl: one app, every language and country","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypressJestLocalisation bugs in prices, dates and plurals are common, and expected values should be built with the same Intl rules the app uses. A shopping app used in Mumbai, Chicago and Berlin must show the same price, date and sentence in three different ways. Writing those rules by hand is how bugs like \"1 items\" or \"$12,34,567\" reach production. The built in Intl object knows the rules for hundreds of languages and countries, taken from the Unicode CLDR data that every major platform shares. Tool What it formats Real example Intl.NumberFormat numbers, currencies, percentages, units, compact numbers ₹12,34,567.89 in India, $1,234,567.89 in the US Intl.DateTimeFormat dates and times in a chosen time zone a delivery slot shown in the customer's own zone Intl.RelativeTimeFormat \"yesterday\", \"in 3 hours\" \"Order placed 2 days ago\" Intl.PluralRules which plural form a number needs \"1 item\" and \"2 items\", or Arabic with six forms Intl.ListFormat \"A, B, and C\" or \"A oder B\" \"Ships with Chrome, Firefox, and Safari\" Intl.DisplayNames names of countries, languages and currencies a country dropdown in the user's language Intl.Segmenter characters, words and sentences as people see them a character counter for a Hindi or emoji message Intl.Collator sorting and comparing text like a human \"résumé\" next to \"resume\", \"apple\" before \"Zebra\" Intl: numbers, dates, plurals, lists and characters for every country · ▶ Runs // One amount and one moment, shown the way people in each country expectconst amount = 1234567.891; console.log(newIntl.NumberFormat(\"en-IN\", { style: \"currency\", currency: \"INR\" }).format(amount)); console.log(newIntl.NumberFormat(\"en-US\", { style: \"currency\", currency: \"USD\" }).format(amount)); console.log(newIntl.NumberFormat(\"de-DE\", { style: \"currency\", currency: \"EUR\" }).format(amount)); console.log(newIntl.NumberFormat(\"en-IN\", { notation: \"compact\" }).format(2_500_000), newIntl.NumberFormat(\"en-US\", { notation: \"compact\" }).format(2_500_000)); const when = newDate(\"2026-10-24T09:30:00Z\"); for (const loc of [\"en-IN\", \"en-US\", \"de-DE\", \"ar-EG\", \"hi-IN\"]) { console.log(loc.padEnd(6), newIntl.DateTimeFormat(loc, { dateStyle: \"medium\", timeStyle: \"short\", timeZone: \"Asia/Kolkata\" }).format(when)); } // \"yesterday\", \"in 3 hours\": relative timesconst rtf = newIntl.RelativeTimeFormat(\"en\", { numeric: \"auto\" }); console.log(rtf.format(-1, \"day\"), \"|\", rtf.format(3, \"hour\"), \"|\", newIntl.RelativeTimeFormat(\"hi\", { numeric: \"auto\" }).format(-1, \"day\")); // Plurals and ordinals: \"1 item\" against \"2 items\", \"1st\" against \"22nd\"const pr = newIntl.PluralRules(\"en-US\"); console.log([0, 1, 2].map(n => `${n} item${pr.select(n) === \"one\" ? \"\" : \"s\"}`).join(\", \")); const ord = newIntl.PluralRules(\"en-US\", { type: \"ordinal\" }); const suffix = { one: \"st\", two: \"nd\", few: \"rd\", other: \"th\" }; console.log([1, 2, 3, 4, 11, 21, 22, 103].map(n => n + suffix[ord.select(n)]).join(\" \")); // Lists, names of countries and languages console.log(newIntl.ListFormat(\"en\", { type: \"conjunction\" }).format([\"Chrome\", \"Firefox\", \"Safari\"]), \"|\", newIntl.ListFormat(\"de\", { type: \"disjunction\" }).format([\"Chrome\", \"Firefox\"])); console.log(newIntl.DisplayNames([\"en\"], { type: \"region\" }).of(\"IN\"), \"|\", newIntl.DisplayNames([\"hi\"], { type: \"language\" }).of(\"en\")); // Characters a person sees, and sorting the way people expectconst text = \"नमस्ते 👨‍👩‍👧\"; console.log(\"length\", text.length, \"| code points\", [...text].length, \"| graphemes\", [...newIntl.Segmenter(\"hi\", { granularity: \"grapheme\" }).segment(text)].length); const words = [\"résumé\", \"resume\", \"Zebra\", \"apple\"]; console.log(words.toSorted(), words.toSorted(newIntl.Collator(\"en\").compare)); Output ₹12,34,567.89 $1,234,567.89 1.234.567,89 € 25L 2.5M en-IN 24 Oct 2026, 3:00 pm en-US Oct 24, 2026, 3:00 PM de-DE 24.10.2026, 15:00 ar-EG ٢٤‏/١٠‏/٢٠٢٦، ٣:٠٠ م hi-IN 24 अक्टू॰ 2026, 3:00 pm yesterday | in 3 hours | कल 0 items, 1 item, 2 items 1st 2nd 3rd 4th 11th 21st 22nd 103rd"],[6,"intl","Intl: one app, every language and country","Chrome, Firefox, and Safari | Chrome oder Firefox India | अंग्रेज़ी length 15 | code points 12 | graphemes 5 [ 'Zebra', 'apple', 'resume', 'résumé' ] [ 'apple', 'resume', 'résumé', 'Zebra' ] Read the last two lines closely. The text \"नमस्ते 👨‍👩‍👧\" has a length of 15, but a person sees far fewer characters, because length counts UTF-16 units and the family emoji is several code points joined together. Only Intl.Segmenter counts what the user sees, which matters for limits such as \"maximum 160 characters\". Plain sort() compares character codes, so every capital letter comes before every small letter. A Collator sorts the way a dictionary does. Localisation testing is a test type of its own. Run the same check under several locales and time zones (Playwright's locale and timezoneId options, or LANG and TZ on CI), and build the expected text with the same Intl settings instead of hard coding it. Watch for text that is longer in German, right to left layout in Arabic, and Hindi \"कल\", which means both yesterday and tomorrow. Lesson 5 of 7"],[6,"dates","Dates, time zones and Temporal","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypressJestDate pickers, expiry rules and \"today\" logic need a fixed clock and edge case dates. The Date object stores one moment as milliseconds since 1 January 1970 UTC. It has well known traps, which is why the new Temporal API was designed. It reached stage 4 in 2026 and is listed for the ES2027 edition. The Date object · ▶ Runs const d = newDate(Date.UTC(2026, 9, 20, 14, 30)); // months start at 0, so 9 is October console.log(d.toISOString()); console.log(d.getUTCFullYear(), d.getUTCMonth() + 1, d.getUTCDate(), d.getUTCDay()); // day 2 is Tuesday console.log(d.getTime()); // milliseconds since 1 January 1970 UTCconst parsed = newDate(\"2026-10-20T14:30:00Z\"); console.log(parsed.getTime() === d.getTime()); const inAWeek = newDate(d); inAWeek.setUTCDate(inAWeek.getUTCDate() + 7); // rolls over into the next month for you console.log(inAWeek.toISOString().slice(0, 10)); const days = (newDate(\"2026-12-25\") - newDate(\"2026-10-20\")) / 86_400_000; console.log(days, \"days until 25 December\"); const fmt = newIntl.DateTimeFormat(\"en-IN\", { dateStyle: \"medium\", timeStyle: \"short\", timeZone: \"Asia/Kolkata\" }); console.log(fmt.format(d)); console.log(d.toLocaleDateString(\"de-DE\", { timeZone: \"UTC\" }), d.toLocaleDateString(\"en-US\", { timeZone: \"UTC\" })); console.log(newDate(\"2026-02-30\").toString(), newDate(\"not a date\").getTime()); Output 2026-10-20T14:30:00.000Z 2026 10 20 2 1792506600000 true 2026-10-27 66 days until 25 December 20 Oct 2026, 8:00 pm 20.10.2026 10/20/2026 Mon Mar 02 2026 00:00:00 GMT+0000 (Coordinated Universal Time) NaN Date months start at 0 (January is 0, December is 11), invalid dates such as 30 February roll over silently, and methods without \"UTC\" use the computer's time zone, so the same test can pass in Bengaluru and fail on a CI server in Frankfurt."],[6,"dates","Temporal","Temporal separates the different ideas a date can mean: a calendar date (PlainDate), a time on a wall clock (PlainTime), an exact moment (Instant) and a moment in a place (ZonedDateTime). Values are immutable, months start at 1 and invalid input can be rejected. Temporal, the modern date API · ▶ Runs // Temporal (finished in 2026, part of ES2027): immutable, time zone aware, months start at 1.const today = Temporal.PlainDate.from(\"2026-10-20\"); console.log(today.toString(), today.dayOfWeek, today.month); const due = today.add({ days: 30 }); console.log(due.toString()); console.log(today.until(Temporal.PlainDate.from(\"2026-12-25\")).days, \"days\"); const meeting = Temporal.ZonedDateTime.from(\"2026-10-20T09:00[Asia/Kolkata]\"); console.log(meeting.withTimeZone(\"Europe/Berlin\").toString()); const d = Temporal.Duration.from({ hours: 1, minutes: 95 }); console.log(d.round({ largestUnit: \"hours\" }).toString()); console.log(Temporal.PlainDate.compare(today, due)); // -1, 0 or 1try { Temporal.PlainDate.from(\"2026-02-30\", { overflow: \"reject\" }); } catch (err) { console.log(err.name); } Output 2026-10-20 2 10 2026-11-19 66 days 2026-10-20T05:30:00+02:00[Europe/Berlin] PT2H35M -1 RangeError Temporal ships in Node.js 26, Firefox and Chrome. Where it is missing, the @js-temporal/polyfill package adds it. If \"Edit and run\" shows a ReferenceError for Temporal, your browser does not have it yet. Date logic is a top source of flaky tests: \"today\" changes, month ends differ and time zones shift dates. Fix the clock (Jest and Vitest fake timers with setSystemTime, Playwright's page.clock, Cypress's cy.clock), run CI in UTC and test the edges: 31 January plus one month, leap days and daylight saving changes. Lesson 6 of 7"],[6,"json","JSON","🧪 QA essentialCypressPlaywrightJestEvery API test sends and reads JSON, and fixture files hold the test data. JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is the text format of almost every API. JSON.stringify turns a value into JSON text and JSON.parse turns text back into a value. JSON.stringify and JSON.parse · ▶ Runs const order = { id: \"ORD-104233\", total: 2695, items: [{ sku: \"A1\", qty: 2 }], placedAt: newDate(\"2026-10-20T14:30:00Z\"), note: undefined, // dropped by JSON print() { return\"...\"; }, // dropped by JSON }; const text = JSON.stringify(order); console.log(text); console.log(JSON.stringify(order, null, 2).split(\"\\n\").length, \"lines when pretty printed\"); console.log(JSON.stringify(order, [\"id\", \"total\"])); // keep only these keysconst back = JSON.parse(text); console.log(typeof back.placedAt); // dates come back as stringsconst revived = JSON.parse(text, (key, value) => key === \"placedAt\" ? newDate(value) : value); console.log(revived.placedAt instanceofDate); try { JSON.parse(\"{ id: 1 }\"); // keys must use double quotes } catch (err) { console.log(err.name); } try { JSON.stringify({ big: 10n }); } catch (err) { console.log(err.message); } Output {\"id\":\"ORD-104233\",\"total\":2695,\"items\":[{\"sku\":\"A1\",\"qty\":2}],\"placedAt\":\"2026-10-20T14:30:00.000Z\"} 11 lines when pretty printed {\"id\":\"ORD-104233\",\"total\":2695} string true SyntaxError Do not know how to serialize a BigInt Watch what happens to each field of an order when it travels to a server and back. This is exactly what happens when a page saves data with fetch and reads it again, or when a test writes a fixture file and loads it in the next run. 🎬 Try it"],[6,"json","An order goes to JSON text and back","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. const order = { id: 7, total: 199.5, note: undefined, placed: newDate(\"2026-10-24\"), tags: newSet([\"gift\"]), print() {} }; const text = JSON.stringify(order); const back = JSON.parse(text); JavaScript object JSON text Parsed back Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 6 JavaScript value What JSON does with it objects, arrays, strings, numbers, booleans, null kept undefined, functions, symbols dropped from objects, turned into null in arrays Date becomes an ISO string and comes back as a string NaN, Infinity become null BigInt throws a TypeError Map, Set become {}"],[6,"json","JSON files as test data","Reading and writing JSON test data files · ▶ Runs import { readFile, writeFile, mkdir } from\"node:fs/promises\"; await mkdir(\"fixtures\", { recursive: true }); const users = [ { email: \"asha@example.com\", role: \"admin\" }, { email: \"ravi@example.com\", role: \"customer\" }, ]; await writeFile(\"fixtures/users.json\", JSON.stringify(users, null, 2)); const loaded = JSON.parse(await readFile(\"fixtures/users.json\", \"utf8\")); console.log(loaded.length, loaded[0].email); // Node can also import JSON directly with an import attributeconst { default: again } = awaitimport(\"./fixtures/users.json\", { with: { type: \"json\" } }); console.log(again[1].role); Output 2 asha@example.com customer Keep test data in .json fixture files: Cypress loads them with cy.fixture(\"users\"), Playwright and Jest tests can import them. Pretty print with JSON.stringify(data, null, 2) when writing files so reviews show clean diffs. Lesson 7 of 7"],[6,"scenario","Real world scenario: validate an order API response","🧪 QA essentialJestVitest The situationThe order API returns JSON. You must check that the order id has the right format, the status is confirmed, the display amount matches the number, the timestamp is a valid ISO time from the last day and the contact details look sane. Validate an order API response · 🧪 Tests run 📄 package.json { \"name\": \"order-api\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"private\": true, \"scripts\": { \"test\": \"jest\" } } 📄 order.test.js // The body an order API returned, as a test would receive itconst body = JSON.parse(`{ \"id\": \"ORD-104233\", \"status\": \"CONFIRMED\", \"total\": { \"amount\": 2695, \"currency\": \"INR\", \"display\": \"₹2,695.00\" }, \"placedAt\": \"2026-10-20T14:30:00.000Z\", \"customer\": { \"email\": \"asha@example.com\", \"phone\": \"+91 98450 12345\" }, \"items\": [{ \"sku\": \"A1\", \"qty\": 2 }, { \"sku\": \"D4\", \"qty\": 3 }] }`); describe(\"order response\", () => { test(\"has the right shape\", () => { expect(body).toMatchObject({ id: expect.stringMatching(/^ORD-\\d{6}$/), status: \"CONFIRMED\", total: { amount: expect.any(Number), currency: \"INR\" }, items: expect.arrayContaining([expect.objectContaining({ sku: \"A1\" })]), }); }); test(\"shows the amount the way the page does\", () => { const shown = newIntl.NumberFormat(\"en-IN\", { style: \"currency\", currency: \"INR\" }).format(body.total.amount); expect(body.total.display).toBe(shown); }); test(\"placedAt is a valid ISO time within a day of the run\", () => { const placed = newDate(body.placedAt); const now = newDate(\"2026-10-21T09:00:00Z\"); // a fixed clock keeps the test stable expect(placed.toISOString()).toBe(body.placedAt); expect(now - placed).toBeLessThan(24 * 60 * 60 * 1000); }); test(\"contact details look valid\", () => { expect(body.customer.email).toMatch(/^[^@\\s]+@[^@\\s]+\\.[a-z]{2,}$/i); expect(body.customer.phone.replace(/\\D/g, \"\")).toHaveLength(12); }); }); Terminal $ npx jest --verbose Terminal output PASS ./order.test.js order response ✓ has the right shape ✓ shows the amount the way the page does ✓ placedAt is a valid ISO time within a day of the run ✓ contact details look valid Test Suites: 1 passed, 1 total Tests: 4 passed, 4 total Snapshots: 0 total Time: 0.3 s Ran all test suites. toMatchObject checks only the keys you list, so new fields in the API do not break the test. Asymmetric matchers (expect.stringMatching, expect.any) check the kind of value when the exact value changes every run. The clock is fixed (now is a constant), so the time check passes on any day it runs. Chapter 21 turns this into a real API suite: starting a server, sending requests with fetch and supertest, and validating whole responses against a JSON Schema. 🧮"],[6,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 What does \"Brainy Bytes\".slice(0, 6) return? Characters 0 to 5. Answer: Brainy Indexes 0 to 5 are B, r, a, i, n, y. C2 What does \"7\".padStart(3, \"0\") return? Pad the start until the length is 3. Answer: 007 Two zeros are added in front. C1 How many matches does \"a1b22c333\".match(/\\d+/g) return? + means one or more, so neighbours join. Answer: 3 The matches are \"1\", \"22\" and \"333\". C2 How many days are there from 20 October 2026 to 25 December 2026? Count the rest of October, November and December. Answer: 66 October: 31 - 20 = 11 days. November: 30 days. December: 25 days. 11 + 30 + 25 = 66 C1 What does Math.round(-2.5) return? Math.round goes towards positive infinity at .5. Answer: -2 -2.5 is halfway, so it rounds up to -2. C2 How many lines does JSON.stringify({ a: 1, b: [1, 2] }, null, 2) produce? Each array item gets its own line. Answer: 7 Lines: {, \"a\": 1,, \"b\": [, 1,, 2, ], } That is 7 lines. C1 In milliseconds, how long is one day? 24 hours of 60 minutes of 60 seconds of 1000 ms. Answer: 86400000 24 × 60 × 60 × 1000 = 86,400,000 C2 Temporal.PlainDate.from(\"2026-01-31\").add({ months: 1 }) gives which date (YYYY-MM-DD)? February 2026 has 28 days and the default overflow is \"constrain\". Answer: 2026-02-28 31 February does not exist. With the default overflow it is constrained to the last day: 2026-02-28. 🎯"],[6,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/14correct Q1 What does \" hi \".trim() return? trim removes both ends. trim removes whitespace from both ends. Q2 Which flag makes a regex find every match instead of only the first? g for global. The g flag finds all matches. i ignores case. Q3 What month is new Date(2026, 0, 15)? Date months start at 0. Month 0 is January. Temporal uses 1 for January. Q1 Which tool counts the characters a person actually sees, such as one family emoji? Graphemes. length counts UTF-16 units and spreading counts code points. Only a grapheme segmenter matches what the eye sees. Q2 What does JSON.stringify({ a: 1, b: undefined }) give? undefined is not a JSON value. Keys with undefined are dropped from objects. Q3 Which pattern matches the whole value ORD-123456 and nothing longer? Anchors. ^ and $ make the pattern cover the whole string. Q4 What does \"👍🏽\".length return? length counts UTF-16 code units. The thumbs up and the skin tone are each two code units. Q1 What does new Intl.PluralRules(\"en-US\", { type: \"ordinal\" }).select(22) return? 22nd. Ordinal rules give one, two, few and other in English: 1st, 2nd, 3rd, 4th, and 21st, 22nd, 23rd. Q2 After JSON.parse, what type is a value that was a Date before JSON.stringify? JSON has no date type. It becomes an ISO string. Use a reviver to turn it back into a Date. Q3 A test checks \"Expires 31 Oct\" and passes in India but fails in CI in the US. What is the most likely cause? Which part of Date depends on the machine? Local time methods use the machine's time zone. Use UTC methods, Temporal with a zone, or set TZ in CI. Q4 Which Jest matcher checks only the keys you list and ignores extra keys? It matches a subset. toMatchObject passes when the listed keys match, whatever else is present. Q1 What does (1.005).toFixed(2) return? How is 1.005 really stored? It is stored as 1.00499999..., so it rounds down. Q2 What does \"2026-10-18\".replace(/(\\d+)-(\\d+)-(\\d+)/, \"$3/$2/$1\") return? $1 is the first group. The groups are 2026, 10 and 18, placed in reverse. Q3 Which is true about Temporal.PlainDate? Temporal was designed to fix Date. Temporal values never change. Methods such as add return new values."],[6,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[6,"slugify","Make a URL slug","Write slugify(title): lower case, trim, replace every run of characters that are not letters or digits with one dash, and remove dashes at the ends. \" Hello, World! \" becomes \"hello-world\". Two replace calls: one for the runs of other characters, one for dashes at the start and end. function slugify(title) { return title.toLowerCase().trim().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, \"-\").replace(/^-+|-+$/g, \"\"); } The + turns a run of spaces and punctuation into a single dash. 🌿 Medium"],[6,"mask","Mask an email","Write maskEmail(email) that keeps the first character of the name and the whole domain and replaces the rest of the name with three stars: \"asha.rao@example.com\" becomes \"a***@example.com\". Return \"invalid\" when there is no single @. split(\"@\") must give exactly two parts. function maskEmail(email) { const parts = email.split(\"@\"); if (parts.length !== 2 || !parts[0]) return\"invalid\"; return `${parts[0][0]}***@${parts[1]}`; } Checking the number of parts handles both a missing @ and too many. 🔥 Hard"],[6,"order-ids","Extract order numbers","Write orderNumbers(text) that returns every order number (the digits only, as numbers) from ids like ORD-104233. Only ids with exactly six digits count, and XORD- is not an id. Use matchAll with a capture group and word boundaries \\b on both sides. function orderNumbers(text) { return [...text.matchAll(/\\bORD-(\\d{6})\\b/g)].map(m => Number(m[1])); } The boundaries stop XORD- and seven digit numbers from matching, and the group gives just the digits. 🧠 Expert"],[6,"business-days","Add business days","Write addBusinessDays(isoDate, n). It gets a date like \"2026-10-23\" and returns the date n working days later (skipping Saturdays and Sundays) in the same format. Work in UTC. Step one day at a time with setUTCDate and count only days where getUTCDay() is 1 to 5. function addBusinessDays(isoDate, n) { const d = newDate(isoDate + \"T00:00:00Z\"); let added = 0; while (added < n) { d.setUTCDate(d.getUTCDate() + 1); const day = d.getUTCDay(); if (day !== 0 && day !== 6) added++; } return d.toISOString().slice(0, 10); } Stepping day by day handles month ends and weekends without special cases. Using UTC keeps it independent of the machine's time zone."],[6,"business-days","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. Working with stringsA string is text. Strings are immutable: every method returns a new string and the original never changes. Indexes start at 0. Regular expressionsA regular expression describes a pattern of text. It is written between slashes, followed by optional flags. Math and number formattingThe Math object holds rounding, min and max, powers and random numbers. Intl.NumberFormat turns numbers into the exact text a user in each country expects. Intl: one app, every language and countryA shopping app used in Mumbai, Chicago and Berlin must show the same price, date and sentence in three different ways. Writing those rules by hand is how bugs like \"1 items\" or \"$12,34,567\" reach production. Dates, time zones and TemporalThe Date object stores one moment as milliseconds since 1 January 1970 UTC. It has well known traps, which is why the new Temporal API was designed. It reached stage 4 in 2026 and is listed for the ES2027 edition. JSONJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is the text format of almost every API. JSON.stringify turns a value into JSON text and JSON.parse turns text back into a value. Real world scenario: validate an order API response"],[6,"comic","Text, patterns, numbers, dates and JSON","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Compare normalised text, anchor your patterns and freeze the clock."],[6,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🧾Invoice numbers A billing service generates INV-2026-000123 style ids and a regex in the tests checks every one. 🌍Prices around the world A shop shows ₹12,34,567.50 in India and $1,234,567.50 in the US from the same number with Intl.NumberFormat. ⏰A coupon that expires A coupon valid \"until the end of the month\" is tested with a fixed clock on 31 January, 28 February and 29 February. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Strings, Regex, Numbers, Dates and JSON\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 9Welcome This chapter covers strings, regular expressions, number formatting, dates with Temporal, and JSON. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[7,"","Map, Set, Iterators and Generators","Map and when it beats an object, getOrInsert, Set with union, intersection and difference, WeakMap and WeakRef, the iteration protocol, lazy iterator helpers, generators that pause and resume, async generators and for await for paginated APIs, binary data with typed arrays, DataView, encodings, SharedArrayBuffer and Atomics, symbols and well known symbols, and comparing an expected menu with the real one. · Map · Set · ArrayBuffer · typed array · Uint8Array · DataView · SharedArrayBuffer · Atomics · union · intersection · difference · WeakMap · WeakRef · iterator · iterable · iterator helpers · generator · yield · async generator · for await · pagination · Symbol 🟨 JavaScript›📦 Working with data›Chapter 8 of 31 🗂️ Step 2 · Topic 3 of 3 · Beginner"],[7,"top","","Map for lookups with any key, Set with the new union and difference methods, weak collections, the iteration protocol and lazy iterator helpers, generators, async generators for paginated APIs, binary data with ArrayBuffer, typed arrays, DataView and Atomics, and symbols. ⏱️ About 21 min read📚 8 parts🎬 2 animations▶ 12 runnable examples🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 14 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 5 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Set operations make expected against actual list checks a one liner, and async generators make paginated API tests a plain loop. 1Map 2Set and set operations 3WeakMap, WeakSet and WeakRef 4Iterators and iterator helpers 5Generators and async generators 6Binary data: ArrayBuffer, typed arrays and DataView 7Symbols 8Real world scenario: compare a menu and walk every page Lesson 1 of 8"],[7,"map","Map","🧪 QA essential A Map stores key and value pairs, like an object, with three differences that matter: keys can be any value (objects, numbers, functions), it remembers insertion order, and it has a size. Map: a dictionary with any kind of key · ▶ Runs const tokens = newMap(); tokens.set(\"admin\", \"tok-a1\"); tokens.set(\"customer\", \"tok-c9\"); console.log(tokens.get(\"admin\"), tokens.has(\"guest\"), tokens.size); const visits = newMap(); const page = { url: \"/cart\" }; // an object as a key, which plain objects cannot do visits.set(page, 3); console.log(visits.get(page)); for (const [role, token] of tokens) console.log(role, \"->\", token); // insertion order console.log([...tokens.keys()], Object.fromEntries(tokens)); tokens.delete(\"customer\"); console.log(tokens); Output tok-a1 false 2 3 admin -> tok-a1 customer -> tok-c9 [ 'admin', 'customer' ] { admin: 'tok-a1', customer: 'tok-c9' } Map(1) { 'admin' => 'tok-a1' } When a Map beats a plain object · ▶ Runs const asObject = {}; asObject[\"1\"] = \"string key\"; asObject[1] = \"number key\"; // object keys are always strings, so this overwrites console.log(Object.keys(asObject).length); const asMap = newMap([[\"1\", \"string key\"], [1, \"number key\"]]); console.log(asMap.size); const counts = newMap(); for (const word of\"to be or not to be\".split(\" \")) { counts.set(word, (counts.get(word) ?? 0) + 1); } console.log(counts); console.log(counts.getOrInsert(\"maybe\", 0), counts.size); // ES2026: read or insert in one step Output 1 2 Map(4) { 'to' => 2, 'be' => 2, 'or' => 1, 'not' => 1 } 0 5 Use a plain object for fixed shapes such as a user or a config. Use a Map for lookups that grow at run time: caches, counters, \"element to data\" tables. getOrInsert and getOrInsertComputed (ES2026) replace the \"get, check, set\" dance. A Map is a clean way to cache one login token per role during a test run, so each role logs in once: tokens.getOrInsertComputed(role, () => login(role)) for a synchronous helper, or the get, check and set steps around an await. Lesson 2 of 8"],[7,"set","Set and set operations","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypressSelenium A Set holds unique values. Adding a value twice keeps one copy. ES2025 added the operations from school maths, so comparing two lists no longer needs nested loops. Set and the ES2025 set operations · ▶ Runs const seen = newSet([\"chromium\", \"firefox\"]); seen.add(\"webkit\").add(\"chromium\"); // duplicates are ignored console.log(seen.size, seen.has(\"firefox\")); const expected = newSet([\"Home\", \"Shop\", \"Deals\", \"Help\", \"Account\"]); const actual = newSet([\"Home\", \"Shop\", \"Help\", \"Account\", \"Blog\"]); console.log(\"missing:\", [...expected.difference(actual)]); // ES2025 Set methods console.log(\"extra:\", [...actual.difference(expected)]); console.log(\"both:\", [...expected.intersection(actual)]); console.log(\"all:\", [...expected.union(actual)].length); console.log(\"changed:\", [...expected.symmetricDifference(actual)]); console.log(newSet([\"Home\", \"Shop\"]).isSubsetOf(actual), expected.isDisjointFrom(newSet([\"Login\"]))); Output 3 true missing: [ 'Deals' ] extra: [ 'Blog' ] both: [ 'Home', 'Shop', 'Help', 'Account' ] all: 6 changed: [ 'Deals', 'Blog' ] true true Method Returns a.union(b) everything in either a.intersection(b) only what is in both a.difference(b) in a but not in b a.symmetricDifference(b) in exactly one of them a.isSubsetOf(b), isSupersetOf, isDisjointFrom true or false Checking a dropdown, a menu or a list of columns against the spec is a set comparison. Report expected.difference(actual) as missing and actual.difference(expected) as unexpected, and the failure message says exactly what is wrong. Lesson 3 of 8"],[7,"weak","WeakMap, WeakSet and WeakRef","Weak collections hold objects without keeping them alive. When nothing else uses the object, the garbage collector may remove it and its entry disappears. They cannot be listed or counted, because their contents can change at any moment. WeakMap and WeakRef · ▶ Runs const meta = newWeakMap(); // keys must be objects and do not keep them alivefunction track(element) { meta.set(element, { clicks: (meta.get(element)?.clicks ?? 0) + 1 }); } const button = { id: \"buy\" }; track(button); track(button); console.log(meta.get(button)); console.log(meta.has({ id: \"buy\" })); // a different objectconst cache = newWeakRef({ big: \"report data\" }); console.log(cache.deref()?.big); // may be undefined once memory is reclaimed Output { clicks: 2 } false report data Libraries use a WeakMap to attach extra data to DOM elements or objects they do not own without causing memory leaks. App code rarely needs WeakRef or FinalizationRegistry, and their timing must never be relied on. Lesson 4 of 8"],[7,"iterators","Iterators and iterator helpers","An iterable is anything with a Symbol.iterator method: arrays, strings, Maps, Sets, NodeLists and arguments. That method returns an iterator, an object whose next() hands out { value, done } one at a time. for...of, spread and destructuring all use it. The iteration protocol · ▶ Runs const word = \"QA\"; const it = word[Symbol.iterator](); // every iterable hands out an iterator console.log(it.next(), it.next(), it.next()); // spread, for...of, destructuring and Array.from all use the same protocol console.log([...\"hey\"], Array.from(newSet([3, 3, 4]))); const [first, ...rest] = newMap([[\"a\", 1], [\"b\", 2]]); console.log(first, rest); const countdown = { from: 3, [Symbol.iterator]() { let n = this.from; return { next: () => (n > 0 ? { value: n--, done: false } : { value: undefined, done: true }) }; }, }; console.log([...countdown]); for (const n of countdown) console.log(\"T minus\", n); Output { value: 'Q', done: false } { value: 'A', done: false } { value: undefined, done: true } [ 'h', 'e', 'y' ] [ 3, 4 ] [ 'a', 1 ] [ [ 'b', 2 ] ] [ 3, 2, 1 ] T minus 3 T minus 2 T minus 1"],[7,"iterators","Iterator helpers (ES2025)","Iterators now have map, filter, take, drop and friends. Unlike the array methods, they are lazy: no work happens until a value is asked for, so they work on huge or endless sequences. Iterator helpers · ▶ Runs // ES2025 iterator helpers: map, filter, take, drop, flatMap, reduce, some, every, find, toArray.// They work lazily, one value at a time, on any iterator.function* naturals() { let n = 1; while (true) yield n++; // an endless sequence is fine when consumed lazily } const firstEvenSquares = naturals() .map(n => n * n) .filter(n => n % 2 === 0) .take(4) .toArray(); console.log(firstEvenSquares); const map = newMap([[\"a\", 1], [\"b\", 2], [\"c\", 3]]); console.log(map.values().filter(v => v > 1).toArray()); console.log(Iterator.from({ next: () => ({ value: 7, done: false }) }).take(3).toArray()); Output [ 4, 16, 36, 64 ] [ 2, 3 ] [ 7, 7, 7 ] Lesson 5 of 8"],[7,"generators","Generators and async generators","🧪 QA essentialJestPlaywrightAPI tests often have to walk paginated endpoints, and async generators make that a plain loop. A generator function (function*) can pause at every yield and continue later from the same place. Calling it runs nothing. It returns an iterator, and each next() runs the body up to the next yield. 🎬 Try it"],[7,"generators","A generator pauses at every yield","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. function* steps() { yield\"open\"; yield\"click\"; return\"done\"; } const g = steps(); g.next(); g.next(); g.next(); Caller Generator next() returned Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 4 Generators pause and resume · ▶ Runs function* idMaker(prefix) { let n = 1; while (true) { const reset = yield`${prefix}-${String(n).padStart(4, \"0\")}`; n = reset ? 1 : n + 1; } } const ids = idMaker(\"TC\"); console.log(ids.next().value); console.log(ids.next().value); console.log(ids.next(true).value); // a value passed to next() comes out of yield console.log(ids.next().value); function* steps() { console.log(\" (running until the first yield)\"); yield\"open\"; console.log(\" (resumed)\"); yield\"click\"; return\"done\"; } const g = steps(); console.log(\"created, nothing ran yet\"); console.log(g.next()); console.log(g.next()); console.log(g.next()); console.log(g.next()); Output TC-0001 TC-0002 TC-0001 TC-0002 created, nothing ran yet (running until the first yield) { value: 'open', done: false } (resumed) { value: 'click', done: false } { value: 'done', done: true } { value: undefined, done: true }"],[7,"generators","Async generators and for await","An async function* can await between yields. Consumed with for await, it turns a paginated API into a simple loop, and it only fetches pages that are actually needed. Async generators for paginated APIs · ▶ Runs // A fake paginated API: 7 users, 3 per pageconstALL = [\"asha\", \"ravi\", \"mei\", \"omar\", \"lena\", \"ken\", \"zoe\"]; asyncfunction fetchPage(page) { awaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, 10)); // pretend network timeconst start = (page - 1) * 3; return { users: ALL.slice(start, start + 3), next: start + 3 < ALL.length ? page + 1 : null }; } asyncfunction* allUsers() { let page = 1; while (page) { const body = await fetchPage(page); console.log(` fetched page ${page}`); yield* body.users; // hand out each user of the page page = body.next; } } forawait (const user of allUsers()) { console.log(user); if (user === \"omar\") break; // stop early: later pages are never fetched } console.log(awaitArray.fromAsync(allUsers())); Output fetched page 1 asha ravi mei fetched page 2 omar fetched page 1 fetched page 2 fetched page 3 [ 'asha', 'ravi', 'mei', 'omar', 'lena', 'ken', 'zoe' ] Paginated endpoints are a classic API test target: page sizes, the last page, an empty page, a stable order across pages. An async generator gives you every item in one loop, and Array.fromAsync collects them for one big assertion. Lesson 6 of 8"],[7,"binary","Binary data: ArrayBuffer, typed arrays and DataView","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightNode.jsUpload and download tests check real bytes: file signatures, sizes and hashes. Most JavaScript works with text and objects, but files, images, network packets, audio and cryptography work with bytes. An ArrayBuffer is a block of raw memory with a fixed size. You never read it directly. You put a view on it: a typed array such as Uint8Array (whole numbers 0 to 255), Int32Array or Float64Array, or a DataView that can read any type at any position. 🎬 Try it"],[7,"binary","Three views on the same eight bytes","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. const buf = newArrayBuffer(8); const bytes = newUint8Array(buf); const words = newUint32Array(buf); words[0] = 0x12345678; bytes[4] = 255; Raw memory (8 bytes) Uint8Array view Uint32Array view Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 5 Raw bytes: ArrayBuffer, typed arrays, DataView and encodings · ▶ Runs // An ArrayBuffer is raw memory. Typed arrays and DataView are windows onto it.const buf = newArrayBuffer(8); const bytes = newUint8Array(buf); const words = newUint32Array(buf); words[0] = 0x12345678; console.log(\"bytes after one 32 bit write:\", [...bytes.slice(0, 4)].map(b => b.toString(16))); // little endian on this CPU// Fixed size and fixed type: values wrap or are clampedconst small = newUint8Array([250, 255, 256, -1]); console.log(\"Uint8Array:\", [...small], \"| clamped:\", [...newUint8ClampedArray([250, 255, 256, -1])]); // DataView reads any type at any position, with the byte order you choose (network protocols, file headers)const header = newDataView(newArrayBuffer(6)); header.setUint16(0, 0x4241); // \"BA\" magic number, big endian by default header.setUint32(2, 1_000_000, true); // little endian length console.log(header.getUint16(0).toString(16), header.getUint32(2, true), newUint8Array(header.buffer)); // Text to bytes and backconst encoded = newTextEncoder().encode(\"₹99 ✓\"); console.log(encoded.length, \"bytes for\", \"₹99 ✓\".length, \"UTF-16 units:\", newTextDecoder().decode(encoded)); // subarray shares memory, slice copiesconst all = newUint8Array([1, 2, 3, 4]); const view = all.subarray(1, 3), copy = all.slice(1, 3); view[0] = 99; console.log([...all], [...copy]); // Files: the first bytes tell you the real type, whatever the extension saysconst png = Uint8Array.fromHex(\"89504e470d0a1a0a0000000d\"); const isPng = [0x89, 0x50, 0x4e, 0x47].every((b, i) => png[i] === b); console.log(\"is png:\", isPng, \"| base64:\", png.toBase64()); Output bytes after one 32 bit write: [ '78', '56', '34', '12' ] Uint8Array: [ 250, 255, 0, 255 ] | clamped: [ 250, 255, 255, 0 ] 4241 1000000 Uint8Array(6) [ 66, 65, 64, 66, 15, 0 ] 9 bytes for 5 UTF-16 units: ₹99 ✓ [ 1, 99, 3, 4 ] [ 2, 3 ] is png: true | base64: iVBORw0KGgoAAAAN Typed arrays have a fixed length and type. Writing 256 into a Uint8Array wraps around to 0, and Uint8ClampedArray, used for canvas pixels, clamps it to 255 instead. Byte order (endianness) decides which byte comes first. Most computers store numbers little endian, while network protocols and many file formats use big endian. DataView lets you choose on every read and write. TextEncoder and TextDecoder turn text into UTF-8 bytes and back. The rupee sign takes 3 bytes, which is why a byte limit and a character limit are different things. subarray is another view on the same memory, slice is a copy. Mixing them up is a classic bug."],[7,"binary","Sharing memory between threads","Normally each worker thread gets a copy of the data you send it. A SharedArrayBuffer is the exception: every thread sees the same bytes. Two threads that do c[0] = c[0] + 1 at the same moment can both read the old value, and one update is lost. Atomics functions do the read, change and write as one indivisible step, so the total below is always exactly right. In browsers, SharedArrayBuffer is only available on pages that send the cross origin isolation headers. SharedArrayBuffer and Atomics across worker threads · ▶ Runs import { Worker } from\"node:worker_threads\"; // A SharedArrayBuffer is memory that several threads see at the same timeconst shared = newSharedArrayBuffer(4); const counter = newInt32Array(shared); const code = ` const { workerData } = require(\"node:worker_threads\"); const counter = new Int32Array(workerData); for (let i = 0; i < 100000; i++) Atomics.add(counter, 0, 1); // one indivisible read, add and write `; const workers = [1, 2, 3, 4].map(() => newWorker(code, { eval: true, workerData: shared })); awaitPromise.all(workers.map(w => newPromise(r => w.on(\"exit\", r)))); console.log(\"4 workers x 100000 with Atomics.add:\", Atomics.load(counter, 0)); Output 4 workers x 100000 with Atomics.add: 400000 Testers meet binary data in file upload and download tests. Check the first bytes (the \"magic number\") to prove a downloaded report is really a PDF or PNG, compare file sizes in bytes, and hash files with crypto.subtle.digest to prove two downloads are identical. Lesson 7 of 8"],[7,"symbols","Symbols","A symbol is a unique value used as a hidden key. Two symbols with the same description are still different, unless they come from the shared registry with Symbol.for. Symbol keys are skipped by Object.keys and JSON.stringify. Symbols and well known symbols · ▶ Runs const id = Symbol(\"id\"); const user = { name: \"Asha\", [id]: 42 }; console.log(user[id], Object.keys(user), JSON.stringify(user)); // symbol keys are hidden from most tools console.log(Symbol(\"x\") === Symbol(\"x\"), Symbol.for(\"x\") === Symbol.for(\"x\")); const money = { amount: 2695, [Symbol.toPrimitive](hint) { return hint === \"number\" ? this.amount : `₹${this.amount.toLocaleString(\"en-IN\")}`; }, }; console.log(`${money}`, +money, money * 2); Output 42 [ 'name' ] {\"name\":\"Asha\"} false true ₹2,695 2695 5390 Well known symbols let your objects plug into the language: Symbol.iterator makes them work with for...of, Symbol.toPrimitive controls how they turn into text and numbers, Symbol.asyncIterator works with for await, and Symbol.dispose works with using (chapter 30). Lesson 8 of 8"],[7,"scenario","Real world scenario: compare a menu and walk every page","🧪 QA essential The situationA release changed the site menu. You must report which items the spec asks for but the page lacks, which the page has that the spec does not mention, any duplicates, and whether the shared items keep their order. Then you must count every user across a paginated list. Compare menus and walk every page · ▶ Runs // Compare the menu a page shows with the menu the spec asks for, then read every page of the user list.const specMenu = [\"Home\", \"Shop\", \"Deals\", \"Help\", \"Account\"]; const shownMenu = [\"Home\", \"Shop\", \"Help\", \"Help\", \"Account\", \"Blog\"]; const expected = newSet(specMenu); const actual = newSet(shownMenu); const report = { missing: [...expected.difference(actual)], unexpected: [...actual.difference(expected)], duplicated: shownMenu.filter((item, i) => shownMenu.indexOf(item) !== i), sameOrder: specMenu.filter(x => actual.has(x)).join() === [...actual].filter(x => expected.has(x)).join(), }; console.log(report); asyncfunction* pages(total, size) { for (let start = 0; start < total; start += size) { yieldArray.from({ length: Math.min(size, total - start) }, (_, i) => `user${start + i + 1}`); } } let count = 0; forawait (const page of pages(23, 10)) { count += page.length; console.log(`page of ${page.length}, first ${page[0]}`); } console.log(\"total users:\", count); Output { missing: [ 'Deals' ], unexpected: [ 'Blog' ], duplicated: [ 'Help' ], sameOrder: true } page of 10, first user1 page of 10, first user11 page of 3, first user21 total users: 23 Set operations produce the missing and unexpected lists in one line each, and duplicates are easy to spot once you compare each item's first position with its own. The async generator hides the paging, so the count is a plain loop. 🧮"],[7,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 What is the size of the Map after these lines? const m = newMap(); m.set(\"a\", 1); m.set(\"b\", 2); m.set(\"a\", 3); Setting an existing key replaces its value. Answer: 2 \"a\" and \"b\" are set, then \"a\" is set again. Two keys. C2 How many unique letters are in \"mississippi\"? Use a Set in your head. Answer: 4 m, i, s, p. C1 A = {1,2,3,4}, B = {3,4,5}. What is the size of A.union(B)? Count each value once. Answer: 5 {1, 2, 3, 4, 5} has 5 values. C2 What is the size of A.symmetricDifference(B) for the same sets? In exactly one of them. Answer: 3 Only in A: 1, 2. Only in B: 5. That is 3 values. C1 An API has 23 users and returns 10 per page. How many requests does reading every page take? Round up. Answer: 3 23 ÷ 10 = 2.3 Pages hold 10, 10 and 3 users, so 3 requests. C2 How many times does next() return done: false for a generator with three yields and a return? The return gives done: true. Answer: 3 Each yield gives one done: false result. The return gives done: true. C1 What does naturals().map(n => n * n).filter(n => n % 2 === 0).take(4).toArray() return? Answer with the last number. Even squares come from even numbers. Answer: 64 Even squares: 4, 16, 36, 64. The fourth one is 64. C2 An LRU cache with limit 2 receives set a, set b, get a, set c. Which key is evicted? Least recently used. Answer: b After get a, a is the most recently used. b has gone the longest without use, so it is removed when c is added. 🎯"],[7,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/14correct Q1 What is new Set([1, 1, 2, 2, 3]).size? Duplicates are kept once. A Set keeps 1, 2 and 3. Q2 Which can be a key in a Map but not (as itself) in a plain object? Object keys become strings. Plain objects turn keys into strings (or symbols). A Map keeps any value, including objects. Q3 What does calling a generator function do? Nothing runs until next(). The body only starts on the first next(). Q1 What does new Uint8Array([256])[0] hold? Only 0 to 255 fit in one byte. Typed arrays store the value modulo 256. Uint8ClampedArray would store 255. Q2 expected = {A, B, C}, actual = {B, C, D}. What is expected.difference(actual)? In expected but not in actual. Only A is in expected and missing from actual. Q3 Which loop reads values from an async generator? Each value is a promise to wait for. for await waits for each value an async iterator hands out. Q4 Why does JSON.stringify skip a property whose key is a symbol? Symbols are meant to be hidden keys. Symbol keyed properties are ignored by JSON and by Object.keys. Q1 Two Uint8Array objects are made with subarray and slice from the same array. Which one changes when the original changes? View against copy. subarray creates a new view over the same ArrayBuffer. slice copies the bytes. Q2 What makes iterator helpers different from array methods? Think of an endless sequence. Nothing is computed until a value is asked for, so take(4) on an endless generator stops after four. Q3 A loop over an async generator of pages breaks after the 4th user, with 3 users per page. How many pages are fetched? The 4th user is on page 2. Users 1 to 3 come from page 1, the 4th from page 2. The loop stops before page 3 is requested. Q4 What does [...new Map([[\"a\", 1]])] give? A Map iterates as pairs. Spreading a Map gives its entries as [key, value] arrays. Q1 Why can a WeakMap not be iterated or report its size? What does weak mean here? Its contents depend on garbage collection timing, so listing them would give unpredictable results. Q2 What does next(true) do in the idMaker generator? Values can flow into a generator. The argument of next becomes the result of the yield where the generator paused. Q3 Which is true of Symbol(\"id\") === Symbol(\"id\")? Descriptions are just labels. Each Symbol() is unique. Symbol.for(\"id\") returns the same registered symbol each time."],[7,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[7,"unique","Unique, in order","Write unique(list) that removes duplicates and keeps the order of first appearance. A Set keeps insertion order. function unique(list) { return [...newSet(list)]; } Spreading a Set back into an array keeps the first time each value was added. 🌿 Medium"],[7,"diff","Expected against actual","Write compareLists(expected, actual) that returns { missing, extra }: items in expected but not actual, and items in actual but not expected. Both lists sorted alphabetically. Turn both into Sets and use difference twice. If your browser lacks it, use filter with has. function compareLists(expected, actual) { const e = newSet(expected), a = newSet(actual); return { missing: [...e.difference(a)].sort(), extra: [...a.difference(e)].sort(), }; } Sorting makes the result stable, so the test does not depend on the order items happened to appear in. 🔥 Hard"],[7,"range","A range generator","Write a generator range(start, end, step = 1) that yields start, start + step and so on while the value is less than end. A normal counting loop with yield inside. function* range(start, end, step = 1) { for (let n = start; n < end; n += step) { yield n; } } The loop only advances when someone asks for the next value, so even a huge range costs nothing until it is used. 🧠 Expert"],[7,"lru","A small LRU cache","Write lruCache(limit) returning { get(key), set(key, value), keys() }. get returns the value or undefined and marks the key as recently used. When set would go over the limit, remove the least recently used key. keys() returns the keys from least to most recently used. A Map keeps insertion order. Deleting and setting a key again moves it to the end, so the first key is always the least recently used. function lruCache(limit) { const map = newMap(); return { get(key) { if (!map.has(key)) returnundefined; const value = map.get(key); map.delete(key); map.set(key, value); return value; }, set(key, value) { map.delete(key); map.set(key, value); if (map.size > limit) map.delete(map.keys().next().value); }, keys() { return [...map.keys()]; }, }; } Re-inserting on every use keeps the Map ordered from oldest to newest, and map.keys().next().value is the oldest key."],[7,"lru","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. MapA Map stores key and value pairs, like an object, with three differences that matter: keys can be any value (objects, numbers, functions), it remembers insertion order, and it has a size. Set and set operationsA Set holds unique values. Adding a value twice keeps one copy. ES2025 added the operations from school maths, so comparing two lists no longer needs nested loops. WeakMap, WeakSet and WeakRefWeak collections hold objects without keeping them alive. When nothing else uses the object, the garbage collector may remove it and its entry disappears. They cannot be listed or counted, because their contents can change at any moment. Iterators and iterator helpersAn iterable is anything with a Symbol.iterator method: arrays, strings, Maps, Sets, NodeLists and arguments. That method returns an iterator, an object whose next() hands out { value, done } one at a time. for...of, spread and destructuring all use it. Generators and async generatorsA generator function (function*) can pause at every yield and continue later from the same place. Calling it runs nothing. It returns an iterator, and each next() runs the body up to the next yield. Binary data: ArrayBuffer, typed arrays and DataViewMost JavaScript works with text and objects, but files, images, network packets, audio and cryptography work with bytes. An ArrayBuffer is a block of raw memory with a fixed size. You never read it directly. SymbolsA symbol is a unique value used as a hidden key. Two symbols with the same description are still different, unless they come from the shared registry with Symbol.for. Symbol keys are skipped by Object.keys and JSON.stringify. Real world scenario: compare a menu and walk every pageSet operations produce the missing and unexpected lists in one line each, and duplicates are easy to spot once you compare each item's first position with its own. The async generator hides the paging, so the count is a plain loop."],[7,"comic","Collections that do one job well","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Set for unique, Map for lookups, generators for sequences you consume one by one."],[7,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🧭A navigation audit A nightly job compares every menu on the site with the spec using Set.difference and posts the gaps to the team chat. 🎟️Ticket numbers A generator hands out TC-0001, TC-0002 and so on to each new test case in a test management script. 📜Export every customer An admin export reads a paginated customers API with an async generator and writes rows as they arrive. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Map, Set, Iterators and Generators\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 10Welcome This chapter covers Map, Set and its new operations, weak collections, iterators, generators and symbols. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[8,"","Prototypes, Classes and the Page Object Model","The prototype chain, constructor functions and new, classes with fields, getters and static methods, lost this and other class traps, private fields and static blocks, inheritance with extends and super, custom error classes with cause, mixins and dependency injection, the Page Object Model in plain DOM, Playwright and Selenium, a test data builder and an API client class. · prototype · class · constructor · getter · static · private field · extends · super · custom error · mixin · composition · dependency injection · Page Object Model · POM · builder pattern 🟨 JavaScript›🏗️ Structure and tooling›Chapter 9 of 31 🏛️ Step 3 · Topic 1 of 2 · Intermediate"],[8,"top","","How objects borrow from prototypes, classes with fields, getters, static and private members, inheritance with extends and super, custom errors, composition, and the Page Object Model that most UI test frameworks are built on. ⏱️ About 17 min read📚 7 parts🎬 2 animations▶ 12 runnable examples🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 4 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Page objects, API clients, test data builders and custom errors are all classes. This chapter is the base for the framework design in chapter 22. 1Objects and prototypes 2Classes 3Private fields and encapsulation 4Inheritance with extends and super 5Composition over inheritance 6The Page Object Model 7Real world scenario: test data and an API client Lesson 1 of 7"],[8,"prototypes","Objects and prototypes","Every object in JavaScript has a hidden link to another object called its prototype. When you read a property the object does not have, JavaScript follows that link and looks there, then on that object's prototype, and so on. This list of links is the prototype chain. It ends at Object.prototype, whose prototype is null. 🎬 Animated diagram"],[8,"prototypes","Where dog.breathe() is found","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 4 Looking up the prototype chain · ▶ Runs const animal = { breathe() { return`${this.name} breathes`; }, }; const dog = Object.create(animal); // dog's prototype is animal dog.name = \"Rex\"; dog.bark = function () { return\"Woof\"; }; console.log(dog.bark()); // found on dog itself console.log(dog.breathe()); // not on dog, found one step up console.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(dog) === animal); console.log(Object.hasOwn(dog, \"breathe\"), \"breathe\"in dog); const arr = [1, 2]; console.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(arr) === Array.prototype); console.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(Array.prototype) === Object.prototype); console.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(Object.prototype)); // the end of every chain Output Woof Rex breathes true false true true true null Before the class keyword existed, shared methods were put on a constructor function's prototype object and objects were made with new. Classes still work exactly this way underneath. Constructor functions and new · ▶ Runs // Before 2015 this is how \"classes\" were written. You still see it in older libraries.functionUser(name) { this.name = name; } User.prototype.greet = function () { return`Hi, ${this.name}`; }; const a = newUser(\"Asha\"); const b = newUser(\"Ravi\"); console.log(a.greet(), b.greet()); console.log(a.greet === b.greet); // one shared copy on the prototype console.log(a instanceofUser, Object.getPrototypeOf(a) === User.prototype); Output Hi, Asha Hi, Ravi true true true new User(\"Asha\") does four things: it creates an empty object, links it to User.prototype, runs User with this set to the new object, and returns it. Lesson 2 of 7"],[8,"classes","Classes","🧪 QA essentialPage objects, API clients, fixtures and custom reporters in every automation framework are classes. A class is a template for objects that share the same shape and methods. It has a constructor that sets up each new object, methods shared through the prototype, fields that every object gets, getters and setters that look like properties, and static members that belong to the class itself. A class with fields, a getter and a static method · ▶ Runs classCart { static currency = \"INR\"; // belongs to the class, not each cart items = []; // a public field, set up for every new cart constructor(owner) { this.owner = owner; } add(name, price, qty = 1) { this.items.push({ name, price, qty }); returnthis; // returning this allows chaining } get total() { // read like a property, computed each timereturnthis.items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price * i.qty, 0); } static fromJSON(json) { const data = JSON.parse(json); const cart = newCart(data.owner); for (const i of data.items) cart.add(i.name, i.price, i.qty); return cart; } } const cart = newCart(\"Asha\").add(\"Pen\", 20, 3).add(\"Book\", 250); console.log(cart.total, Cart.currency); console.log(typeofCart, Object.getOwnPropertyNames(Cart.prototype)); const copy = Cart.fromJSON('{\"owner\":\"Ravi\",\"items\":[{\"name\":\"Mug\",\"price\":199,\"qty\":2}]}'); console.log(copy.owner, copy.total); Output 310 INR function [ 'constructor', 'add', 'total' ] Ravi 398 The keyword new does four things every time. Watching them explains why methods are shared but fields are not, and why a method called through the object sees that object as this. 🎬 Try it"],[8,"classes","What new Cart(\"Asha\") does, step by step","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. classCart { items = []; constructor(owner) { this.owner = owner; } add(name) { this.items.push(name); } } const c = newCart(\"Asha\"); c.add(\"pen\"); Step The new object Cart.prototype Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 6 Member Lives on Example field each object items = [] method the prototype, shared add(name, price) getter or setter the prototype, used like a property get total() static the class itself Cart.fromJSON(json)"],[8,"classes","Two traps","Class bodies are always in strict mode. A method pulled off its object and called on its own gets this as undefined. An arrow function stored in a field keeps its object. Classes are also not usable before the line that declares them. Two class traps: lost this and no hoisting · ▶ Runs classCounter { count = 0; increment() { this.count++; } incrementArrow = () => { this.count++; }; // a field holding an arrow, bound per object } const c = newCounter(); const { increment, incrementArrow } = c; incrementArrow(); console.log(c.count); try { increment(); // class bodies are strict, so this is undefined } catch (e) { console.log(e.constructor.name + \":\", e.message); } try { newShape(); // classes are not hoisted like functions } catch (e) { console.log(e.constructor.name + \":\", e.message); } classShape {} Output 1 TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'count') ReferenceError: Cannot access 'Shape' before initialization Lesson 3 of 7"],[8,"private","Private fields and encapsulation","A field or method whose name starts with # is private. Only code inside the class body can read or change it. It does not show up in Object.keys, in JSON or in the console of other code, and any attempt to read it from outside is a syntax error. This is real privacy, not a naming habit like the old _balance. Private fields and methods · ▶ Runs classBankAccount { #balance = 0; // private: only code inside the class can see itstatic #count = 0; constructor(owner) { this.owner = owner; BankAccount.#count++; } deposit(amount) { this.#check(amount); this.#balance += amount; returnthis.#balance; } #check(amount) { // a private methodif (!(amount > 0)) thrownewRangeError(`Invalid amount: ${amount}`); } get balance() { returnthis.#balance; } staticget count() { returnBankAccount.#count; } static isAccount(obj) { return #balance in obj; } // a safe brand check } const acc = newBankAccount(\"Mei\"); acc.deposit(500); console.log(acc.balance, BankAccount.count); console.log(Object.keys(acc), JSON.stringify(acc)); console.log(BankAccount.isAccount(acc), BankAccount.isAccount({ balance: 1 })); for (const attempt of [() => { acc.balance = 1_000_000; }, () => acc.deposit(-5)]) { try { attempt(); } catch (e) { console.log(e.name + \":\", e.message); } } console.log(acc.balance); Output 500 1 [ 'owner' ] {\"owner\":\"Mei\"} true false TypeError: Cannot set property balance of #<BankAccount> which has only a getter RangeError: Invalid amount: -5 500 A static block runs once when the class is defined. It is useful for set up that needs a few statements, such as picking a base URL from an environment variable. Static initialisation blocks · ▶ Runs classConfig { static env; static baseURL; static { // runs once when the class is definedConfig.env = process.env.TEST_ENV ?? \"staging\"; Config.baseURL = { staging: \"https://staging.shop.example\", prod: \"https://shop.example\" }[Config.env]; } } console.log(Config.env, Config.baseURL); Output staging https://staging.shop.example Public and private fields, private methods, #x in obj and static blocks arrived in ES2022. Every current browser and Node.js support them. Lesson 4 of 7"],[8,"inheritance","Inheritance with extends and super","🧪 QA essentialA BasePage that every page object extends, and custom errors with a status and a cause, are standard in test frameworks. class Dog extends Animal makes Animal.prototype the prototype of Dog.prototype. A Dog gets every Animal method and can override any of them. Inside the subclass, super(...) calls the parent constructor, and super.method() calls the parent version of a method. extends and super · ▶ Runs classAnimal { constructor(name) { this.name = name; } speak() { return`${this.name} makes a sound`; } toString() { return`[${this.constructor.name} ${this.name}]`; } } classDogextendsAnimal { constructor(name, breed) { super(name); // must run before this is usedthis.breed = breed; } speak() { // overrides the parent methodreturn`${super.speak()}, then barks`; // and can still call it } } const d = newDog(\"Rex\", \"beagle\"); console.log(d.speak()); console.log(`${d}`); console.log(d instanceofDog, d instanceofAnimal, d instanceofObject); console.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(Dog.prototype) === Animal.prototype); Output Rex makes a sound, then barks [Dog Rex] true true true true In a subclass constructor, super() must run before the first use of this. Forgetting it throws a ReferenceError as soon as the class is used."],[8,"inheritance","Custom errors","Extending Error gives errors a clear type you can check with instanceof, extra data such as a status code, and a cause that keeps the original error. Test frameworks print the cause too, which makes failures much easier to read. Custom error classes · ▶ Runs classHttpErrorextendsError { constructor(status, url, options) { super(`Request to ${url} failed with ${status}`, options); this.name = \"HttpError\"; this.status = status; } get retryable() { returnthis.status >= 500 || this.status === 429; } } classNotFoundErrorextendsHttpError { constructor(url) { super(404, url); this.name = \"NotFoundError\"; } } function load(status) { try { thrownewTypeError(\"network down\"); } catch (low) { if (status === 404) thrownewNotFoundError(\"/api/users/9\"); thrownewHttpError(status, \"/api/users\", { cause: low }); // keep the original error } } for (const status of [404, 503]) { try { load(status); } catch (e) { console.log(e.name, \"|\", e.message, \"| retry:\", e.retryable); console.log(\" instanceof HttpError:\", e instanceofHttpError, \"| cause:\", e.cause?.message); } } Output NotFoundError | Request to /api/users/9 failed with 404 | retry: false instanceof HttpError: true | cause: undefined HttpError | Request to /api/users failed with 503 | retry: true instanceof HttpError: true | cause: network down Lesson 5 of 7"],[8,"composition","Composition over inheritance","Deep class trees get hard to change: a fix in a parent class affects every child. Many teams prefer composition, where an object is built from small parts it holds or mixes in. A mixin is a function that takes a class and returns a new class with extra abilities. Passing a helper into the constructor, called dependency injection, lets tests replace it with a fake. Mixins and composition · ▶ Runs // Instead of a deep tree of subclasses, build objects from small abilities.const canLog = base => classextends base { log(msg) { (this.logs ??= []).push(msg); returnthis; } }; const canRetry = base => classextends base { async retry(fn, times = 3) { for (let i = 1; ; i++) { try { returnawait fn(i); } catch (e) { this.log?.(`attempt ${i} failed: ${e.message}`); if (i === times) throw e; } } } }; classClient {} classApiClientextends canRetry(canLog(Client)) {} const api = newApiClient(); const result = await api.retry(n => { if (n < 3) thrownewError(\"timeout\"); return\"200 OK\"; }); console.log(result); console.log(api.logs); // Composition with plain objects: the client HAS a logger instead of BEING oneclassUploader { constructor(logger = console) { this.logger = logger; } upload(file) { this.logger.log(`uploading ${file}`); returntrue; } } const messages = []; newUploader({ log: m => messages.push(m) }).upload(\"report.pdf\"); console.log(messages); Output 200 OK [ 'attempt 1 failed: timeout', 'attempt 2 failed: timeout' ] [ 'uploading report.pdf' ] Inheritance: \"is a\"A CheckoutPage is a BasePage. Good for one shallow level of shared behaviour. Composition: \"has a\"A CheckoutPage has a Header and a CartSummary component. Good for parts that appear on many pages. Lesson 6 of 7"],[8,"pom","The Page Object Model","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightSeleniumCypressWebdriverIOAlmost every UI automation framework you join will be organised as page objects. The Page Object Model (POM) is the most common design pattern in UI automation. Each page or component of the app gets a class. The class holds the locators and offers methods with business names such as loginAs or addToCart. Tests then call those methods and never touch selectors directly. A page object for a login form · ▶ Runs in the browser 📄 page.html <!doctype html> <html> <body> <formid=\"login\"> <label>Email <inputid=\"email\"type=\"email\"></label> <label>Password <inputid=\"password\"type=\"password\"></label> <buttontype=\"submit\"data-test=\"sign-in\">Sign in</button> <pclass=\"error\"hidden></p> </form> <h1id=\"welcome\"hidden></h1> <script> // The app under test: a tiny login form. document.querySelector(\"#login\").addEventListener(\"submit\", e => { e.preventDefault(); const email = document.querySelector(\"#email\").value; const pass = document.querySelector(\"#password\").value; const error = document.querySelector(\".error\"); if (pass !== \"s3cret\") { error.textContent = \"Wrong email or password\"; error.hidden = false; return; } error.hidden = true; const welcome = document.querySelector(\"#welcome\"); welcome.textContent = `Hello, ${email.split(\"@\")[0]}`; welcome.hidden = false; }); </script> </body> </html> 📄 script.js // A page object hides the selectors behind methods with business names.classLoginPage { constructor(doc) { this.doc = doc; } get email() { returnthis.doc.querySelector(\"#email\"); } get password() { returnthis.doc.querySelector(\"#password\"); } get submit() { returnthis.doc.querySelector('[data-test=\"sign-in\"]'); } get error() { returnthis.doc.querySelector(\".error\"); } get welcome() { returnthis.doc.querySelector(\"#welcome\"); } loginAs(email, password) { this.email.value = email; this.password.value = password; this.submit.click(); returnthis; } } const page = newLoginPage(document); page.loginAs(\"asha@shop.example\", \"wrong\"); console.log(\"error shown:\", !page.error.hidden, \"|\", page.error.textContent); page.loginAs(\"asha@shop.example\", \"s3cret\"); console.log(\"error shown:\", !page.error.hidden, \"| welcome:\", page.welcome.textContent); Output error shown: true | Wrong email or password error shown: false | welcome: Hello, asha The idea is identical in every tool. Only the way you find and click elements changes. The same page object in Playwright · Runs for real in chapter 22 // pages/login.page.js (Playwright)exportclassLoginPage { constructor(page) { this.page = page; this.email = page.getByLabel(\"Email\"); this.password = page.getByLabel(\"Password\"); this.submit = page.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Sign in\" }); this.error = page.locator(\".error\"); } async goto() { awaitthis.page.goto(\"/login\"); } async loginAs(email, password) { awaitthis.email.fill(email); awaitthis.password.fill(password); awaitthis.submit.click(); } } // tests/login.spec.jsimport { test, expect } from\"@playwright/test\"; import { LoginPage } from\"../pages/login.page.js\"; test(\"wrong password shows an error\", async ({ page }) => { const login = newLoginPage(page); await login.goto(); await login.loginAs(\"asha@shop.example\", \"wrong\"); await expect(login.error).toHaveText(\"Wrong email or password\"); }); What happens The test reads like the steps a manual tester writes. When the button text changes, only the page object changes. The same page object in Selenium · Runs for real in chapter 22 // pages/LoginPage.js (Selenium WebDriver)const { By, until } = require(\"selenium-webdriver\"); classLoginPage { staticEMAIL = By.id(\"email\"); staticPASSWORD = By.id(\"password\"); staticSUBMIT = By.css('[data-test=\"sign-in\"]'); staticERROR = By.css(\".error\"); constructor(driver) { this.driver = driver; } async open(baseUrl) { awaitthis.driver.get(`${baseUrl}/login`); } async loginAs(email, password) { awaitthis.driver.findElement(LoginPage.EMAIL).sendKeys(email); awaitthis.driver.findElement(LoginPage.PASSWORD).sendKeys(password);"],[8,"pom","The Page Object Model","awaitthis.driver.findElement(LoginPage.SUBMIT).click(); } async errorText() { const el = awaitthis.driver.wait(until.elementLocated(LoginPage.ERROR), 5000); return el.getText(); } } module.exports = { LoginPage }; What happens Locators live in static fields, and every method is async because each WebDriver call talks to the browser. Rules that keep page objects healthy: one class per page or component, no assertions inside page objects (they belong in tests), methods return useful values or other page objects, and locators prefer roles, labels and data-test attributes over long CSS paths. Lesson 7 of 7"],[8,"scenario","Real world scenario: test data and an API client","🧪 QA essential The situationYour API tests create many users with small differences, and every test repeats the same HTTP code. You want readable test data and one place that knows how to call the API. A builder class starts from sensible defaults, and each test changes only what matters to it. Every built user gets a fresh email, so tests never clash. A test data builder · ▶ Runs classUserBuilder { #data = { name: \"Test User\", email: \"user@test.example\", role: \"customer\", verified: true, address: { city: \"Pune\" } }; static #seq = 0; withName(name) { this.#data.name = name; returnthis; } withRole(role) { this.#data.role = role; returnthis; } unverified() { this.#data.verified = false; returnthis; } inCity(city) { this.#data.address = { ...this.#data.address, city }; returnthis; } build() { const id = ++UserBuilder.#seq; return structuredClone({ id, ...this.#data, email: `user${id}@test.example` }); } } const admin = newUserBuilder().withName(\"Asha\").withRole(\"admin\").build(); const newbie = newUserBuilder().unverified().inCity(\"Bhubaneswar\").build(); console.log(admin); console.log(newbie); console.log(admin.email !== newbie.email); Output { id: 1, name: 'Asha', email: 'user1@test.example', role: 'admin', verified: true, address: { city: 'Pune' } } { id: 2, name: 'Test User', email: 'user2@test.example', role: 'customer', verified: false, address: { city: 'Bhubaneswar' } } true An API client base class owns the HTTP details and logging. Small subclasses add one method per endpoint. Because the transport is passed in, the same client works against a fake in unit tests and the real server in integration tests. An API client with a base class and an injected transport · ▶ Runs // One base class knows HTTP. Each area of the API gets a small subclass.classApiClient { #log = []; constructor(baseURL, transport) { this.baseURL = baseURL; this.transport = transport; // injected, so tests can pass a fake } async request(method, path, body) { const res = awaitthis.transport({ method, url: this.baseURL + path, body }); this.#log.push(`${method} ${path} -> ${res.status}`); if (res.status >= 400) thrownewError(`${method} ${path} failed with ${res.status}`); return res.body; } get history() { return [...this.#log]; } } classUsersApiextendsApiClient { get(id) { returnthis.request(\"GET\", `/users/${id}`); } create(user) { returnthis.request(\"POST\", \"/users\", user); } } // A fake transport stands in for the networkconst db = newMap([[1, { id: 1, name: \"Asha\" }]]); asyncfunction fakeTransport({ method, url, body }) { const id = Number(url.split(\"/\").pop()); if (method === \"GET\") return db.has(id) ? { status: 200, body: db.get(id) } : { status: 404 }; const user = { id: db.size + 1, ...body }; db.set(user.id, user); return { status: 201, body: user }; } const users = newUsersApi(\"https://api.shop.example\", fakeTransport); console.log(await users.get(1)); console.log(await users.create({ name: \"Ravi\" })); await users.get(42).catch(e => console.log(\"Error:\", e.message)); console.log(users.history); Output { id: 1, name: 'Asha' } { id: 2, name: 'Ravi' } Error: GET /users/42 failed with 404 [ 'GET /users/1 -> 200', 'POST /users -> 201', 'GET /users/42 -> 404' ] 🧮"],[8,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 A cart holds 3 pens at 20 and 1 book at 250. What does cart.total return? Price times quantity, added up. Answer: 310 3 × 20 = 60 60 + 250 = 310 C2 How many steps up the prototype chain is breathe from dog, if breathe is on animal and dog = Object.create(animal)? Count the links. Answer: 1 dog has no breathe. Its prototype animal does, one step up. C1 Three BankAccount objects are created. What is BankAccount.count? The static counter grows in the constructor. Answer: 3 Each constructor call adds 1 to the static #count. C2 A suite has 40 tests that click the Sign in button. The button label changes. How many files change with a page object? Where does the locator live? Answer: 1 Only the page object holds the locator, so one file changes. C1 The builder created 2 users. What number is in the email of the next user it builds? The static sequence goes up on every build. Answer: 3 Each build adds 1 to #seq. After 2 builds, the next build gives id 3 and user3@test.example. C2 What does this log? classCounter { count = 0; incrementArrow = () => { this.count++; }; } const c = newCounter(); const { incrementArrow } = c; incrementArrow(); incrementArrow(); console.log(c.count); The arrow keeps its object. Answer: 2 incrementArrow runs twice on c. The detached normal method is never called. C1 Which status codes make HttpError.retryable true among 404, 429, 500 and 503? Answer with how many. status >= 500 or status === 429. Answer: 3 429 is true. 500 and 503 are true. 404 is false. C2 Dog extends Animal. How many prototype objects are checked to find hasOwnProperty on a Dog object, counting the one where it is found? Dog.prototype, Animal.prototype, then Object.prototype. Answer: 3 The dog object itself does not have it. Dog.prototype and Animal.prototype do not have it either. Object.prototype does, so 3 prototype objects are checked. 🎯"],[8,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 What does a getter like get total() let you write? It is read like a property. A getter runs a function each time the property is read. Q2 Where does a static method live? Cart.fromJSON(json). Static members are called on the class, not on objects made from it. Q3 In the Page Object Model, where do selectors belong? One place to change. Tests call page object methods. Only the page object knows the selectors. Q1 What happens when code outside the class reads acc.#balance? Private names are checked when the code is parsed. Referring to a private name outside its class is a syntax error. Q2 What must a subclass constructor do before using this? The parent sets up the object first. Until super() runs, this does not exist and using it throws a ReferenceError. Q3 Which assertion belongs in a test, not in a page object? Page objects act, tests judge. Keeping assertions in tests lets page objects be reused by tests that expect different outcomes. Q1 increment is a normal class method that uses this. What happens here? const { increment } = counter; increment(); Class bodies are strict. Detached methods lose their object, and strict mode leaves this undefined. Q2 What does #balance in obj check? A brand check. It returns true only for objects that have the private field, without throwing for others. Q3 Why pass a transport into an ApiClient constructor instead of calling fetch inside? Dependency injection. Injected helpers can be swapped for fakes, so the client can be tested without a network. Q1 Object.getPrototypeOf(Dog.prototype) === Animal.prototype when Dog extends Animal. What else is true? Static members are inherited too. extends links both the prototypes and the classes themselves, so static methods are inherited. Q2 Why are class methods placed on the prototype instead of on each object? a.greet === b.greet. Every object finds the same function through the prototype chain. Q3 What does new Error(\"x\", { cause: low }) add? ES2022 error cause. The cause option keeps the lower level error, so reports show the full story."],[8,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[8,"rect","A rectangle class","Write a class Rectangle with a constructor (width, height), a getter area and a method scale(factor) that multiplies both sides and returns this. Store the sides on this. A getter is get area() { ... }. classRectangle { constructor(width, height) { this.width = width; this.height = height; } get area() { returnthis.width * this.height; } scale(factor) { this.width *= factor; this.height *= factor; returnthis; } } Returning this from scale lets callers chain calls, the same trick builders use. 🌿 Medium"],[8,"stack","A stack with private storage","Write a class Stack with a private array, methods push(x) and pop(), and a getter size. pop() on an empty stack returns undefined. The array must not be visible from outside, so Object.keys(stack) is empty. Declare #items = []; as a field. classStack { #items = []; push(x) { this.#items.push(x); } pop() { returnthis.#items.pop(); } get size() { returnthis.#items.length; } } Private fields never show up in Object.keys, so no outside code can reach in and break the stack. 🔥 Hard"],[8,"errors","A custom error class","Write class ValidationError extends Error with a constructor (field, message). It sets name to \"ValidationError\", stores field, and its message is \"field: message\". Then write check(user) that throws a ValidationError for field \"email\" with message \"is required\" when user.email is missing, and returns true otherwise. Call super(...) with the full message first, then set the extra properties. classValidationErrorextendsError { constructor(field, message) { super(`${field}: ${message}`); this.name = \"ValidationError\"; this.field = field; } } function check(user) { if (!user.email) thrownewValidationError(\"email\", \"is required\"); returntrue; } Tests can now check instanceof ValidationError and the field, which is much clearer than matching message text. 🧠 Expert"],[8,"page-object","A page object for a search box","Write a class SearchPage whose constructor takes a driver object with two async methods: fill(selector, text) and textOf(selector), plus click(selector). Add async search(term) that fills \"#q\" with the term and clicks \"#go\", and async resultCount() that reads \".count\" such as \"12 results\" and returns the number 12. Store the driver on this, and await each driver call in order. parseInt(\"12 results\", 10) gives 12. classSearchPage { constructor(driver) { this.driver = driver; } async search(term) { awaitthis.driver.fill(\"#q\", term); awaitthis.driver.click(\"#go\"); } async resultCount() { const text = awaitthis.driver.textOf(\".count\"); return parseInt(text, 10); } } The test passed a fake driver, which is exactly how page objects are unit tested. The same class would work with a real Playwright or Selenium wrapper."],[8,"page-object","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. Objects and prototypesEvery object in JavaScript has a hidden link to another object called its prototype. When you read a property the object does not have, JavaScript follows that link and looks there, then on that object's prototype, and so on. ClassesA class is a template for objects that share the same shape and methods. It has a constructor that sets up each new object, methods shared through the prototype, fields that every object gets, getters and setters that look like properties, and static members ... Private fields and encapsulationA field or method whose name starts with # is private. Only code inside the class body can read or change it. It does not show up in Object.keys, in JSON or in the console of other code, and any attempt to read it from outside is a syntax error. Inheritance with extends and superclass Dog extends Animal makes Animal.prototype the prototype of Dog.prototype. A Dog gets every Animal method and can override any of them. Inside the subclass, super(...) calls the parent constructor, and super.method() calls the parent version of a method. Composition over inheritanceDeep class trees get hard to change: a fix in a parent class affects every child. Many teams prefer composition, where an object is built from small parts it holds or mixes in. The Page Object ModelThe Page Object Model (POM) is the most common design pattern in UI automation. Each page or component of the app gets a class. The class holds the locators and offers methods with business names such as loginAs or addToCart. Real world scenario: test data and an API clientA builder class starts from sensible defaults, and each test changes only what matters to it. Every built user gets a fresh email, so tests never clash."],[8,"comic","Classes put data and behaviour together","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember A class bundles data and the methods that work on it. Page objects bundle a page and the actions you take on it."],[8,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🛒A shopping cart An e-commerce site models carts, products and orders as classes with getters for totals and private fields for prices that only the server may set. 🧪A UI test framework A team of five keeps 300 Playwright tests stable because a button rename touches one page object instead of 40 tests. 🚨Clear failures An API test suite throws HttpError with the status and the URL, so the CI report says \"GET /orders failed with 503\" instead of \"undefined is not a function\". Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Prototypes, Classes and the Page Object Model\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 9Welcome This chapter covers prototypes, classes, private fields, inheritance, composition and the Page Object Model used in UI automation. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[9,"","Modules, npm, TypeScript and Tooling","Named and default exports, dynamic import, JSON modules, CommonJS and how it mixes with ES modules, package.json scripts and dependencies, semantic version ranges, npm ci and the lock file, TypeScript types and the type checker, ESLint and Prettier on real code, environment variables with --env-file, and a test project with separate unit and API scripts. · import · export · ES modules · CommonJS · require · npm · package.json · semver · package-lock.json · npm ci · TypeScript · tsc · ESLint · Prettier · process.env · .env · project structure 🟨 JavaScript›🏗️ Structure and tooling›Chapter 10 of 31 📦 Step 3 · Topic 2 of 2 · Intermediate"],[9,"top","","ES modules and CommonJS, npm with package.json and version ranges, TypeScript run straight in Node.js and checked with tsc, ESLint and Prettier, configuration from environment variables, and how a real test project is laid out. ⏱️ About 16 min read📚 7 parts🎬 1 animations▶ 6 runnable examples🧪 4 real test runs🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 7 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Every automation framework is a Node.js project: modules, npm scripts, TypeScript, lint rules and environment config are what you touch on day one. 1ES modules: import and export 2CommonJS and how the two mix 3npm and package.json 4TypeScript 5ESLint and Prettier 6Environment variables and config 7Real world scenario: lay out a test project Lesson 1 of 7"],[9,"esm","ES modules: import and export","🧪 QA essentialEvery test file imports a runner, page objects and helpers. Playwright, Vitest and WebdriverIO projects are ES modules by default. A module is a file with its own scope. Nothing inside it is visible to other files unless it is exported, and nothing from outside is available unless it is imported. This keeps large codebases and test suites tidy: page objects, helpers and test data each live in their own file. Named and default exports · ▶ Runs 📄 money.mjs exportconstTAX_RATE = 0.18; exportfunction addTax(amount) { returnMath.round(amount * (1 + TAX_RATE)); } exportclassCart { total = 0; add(price) { this.total += price; returnthis; } } exportdefaultfunction formatPrice(amount) { return`₹${amount.toLocaleString(\"en-IN\")}`; } 📄 main.mjs import formatPrice, { TAX_RATE, addTax, CartasShoppingCart } from\"./money.mjs\"; import * as money from\"./money.mjs\"; // everything, as one object console.log(TAX_RATE, addTax(100)); console.log(formatPrice(2695)); const cart = newShoppingCart(); console.log(cart.add(500).add(250).total); console.log(Object.keys(money)); Terminal $ node main.mjs Output 0.18 118 ₹2,695 750 [ 'Cart', 'TAX_RATE', 'addTax', 'default' ] Syntax Meaning export const x, export function f named export, imported with the same name in braces export default ... one main value per file, imported with any name import { a as b } rename while importing import * as ns every export as one object await import(\"./x.js\") load later, only when needed Modules are always strict, run once however many files import them, and may use await at the top level. Dynamic import, JSON modules and import.meta · ▶ Runs 📄 messages/hi.mjs exportdefault { welcome: \"नमस्ते\" }; 📄 settings.json { \"baseURL\": \"https://staging.shop.example\", \"retries\": 2 } 📄 main.mjs // Top level await: modules may await without an async functionconst lang = process.env.APP_LANG ?? \"hi\"; // Dynamic import loads a module only when it is neededconst { default: messages } = awaitimport(`./messages/${lang}.mjs`); console.log(messages.welcome); // JSON can be imported with an import attributeimport settings from\"./settings.json\"with { type: \"json\" }; console.log(settings.retries, settings.baseURL); console.log(import.meta.filename.split(\"/\").at(-1)); Terminal $ node main.mjs Output नमस्ते 2 https://staging.shop.example main.mjs Many teams avoid default exports: named exports keep the same name in every file, which makes search and automatic refactoring reliable. Lesson 2 of 7"],[9,"cjs","CommonJS and how the two mix","🧪 QA essentialJestMochaSeleniumJest, Mocha and many Selenium projects are CommonJS, and mixing the two styles causes the most common set up errors. Before ES modules, Node.js used CommonJS: require() to load and module.exports to share. Older packages, Jest setups, Mocha and many Selenium projects still use it, so you will read both styles. CommonJS: require and module.exports · ▶ Runs 📄 text.cjs function slugify(text) { return text.toLowerCase().trim().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, \"-\"); } module.exports = { slugify }; 📄 money.mjs exportconst addTax = amount => Math.round(amount * 1.18); 📄 main.cjs const { slugify } = require(\"./text.cjs\"); // CommonJS: require and module.exportsconst path = require(\"node:path\"); console.log(slugify(\"Add to Cart Button\")); console.log(path.join(\"tests\", \"e2e\", \"login.spec.js\")); console.log(typeof module.exports, typeof __dirname); // Since Node 22, require() can also load an ES module that has no top level awaitconst { addTax } = require(\"./money.mjs\"); console.log(addTax(100)); Terminal $ node main.cjs Output add-to-cart-button tests/e2e/login.spec.js object string 118 ES modules CommonJS Load import, loaded before the code runs require(), a normal function call Share export module.exports Chosen by .mjs, or .js with \"type\": \"module\" .cjs, or .js without it Top level await yes no Where you see it Playwright, Vitest, browsers, new code older Node.js code, Jest by default, Mocha The error Cannot use import statement outside a module means a file with import is being treated as CommonJS. Add \"type\": \"module\" to package.json or rename the file to .mjs. Lesson 3 of 7"],[9,"npm","npm and package.json","🧪 QA essentialInstalling, pinning and updating test tools, and running them through npm scripts, is part of every automation project. npm installs packages from the npm registry into node_modules and records them in package.json. The lock file (package-lock.json) pins the exact version of every package, so every laptop and CI machine installs the same thing. A package.json for a test project · Reference { \"name\": \"shop-tests\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"private\": true, \"type\": \"module\", \"engines\": { \"node\": \">=22\" }, \"scripts\": { \"test\": \"npm run test:unit && npm run test:api\", \"test:unit\": \"vitest run\", \"test:api\": \"node --test tests/api/\", \"test:e2e\": \"playwright test\", \"lint\": \"eslint .\", \"format\": \"prettier --write .\" }, \"dependencies\": { \"ajv\": \"^8.17.1\" }, \"devDependencies\": { \"@playwright/test\": \"~1.56.0\", \"vitest\": \"^5.0.0\", \"eslint\": \"10.13.0\" } } What happens \"type\": \"module\" makes .js files ES modules. Scripts run with npm run test:unit. devDependencies are tools only needed to build and test. When a file says import { z } from \"zod\", Node.js has to turn that short name into a real file on disk. Knowing the steps explains the common \"Cannot find package\" and \"not exported\" errors, and why deleting node_modules and running npm ci fixes so many strange problems. 🎬 Animated diagram"],[9,"npm","How Node.js finds import \"zod\"","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 6"],[9,"npm","Version ranges","Versions follow semantic versioning: MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH. A major bump may break your code. A caret ^ allows new minor and patch versions. A tilde ~ allows only new patches. No symbol means that exact version. Below 1.0.0 the caret is stricter: ^0.4.2 allows only 0.4.x, because in a 0.x package every minor release may break things. What ^ and ~ allow · ▶ Runs import semver from\"semver\"; // a package from node_modulesconst checks = [ [\"^1.56.0\", \"1.58.2\"], [\"^1.56.0\", \"2.0.0\"], [\"~1.56.0\", \"1.56.9\"], [\"~1.56.0\", \"1.57.0\"], [\"1.56.1\", \"1.56.2\"], [\"^0.4.2\", \"0.5.0\"], ]; for (const [range, version] of checks) { console.log(range.padEnd(8), version.padEnd(7), semver.satisfies(version, range) ? \"allowed\" : \"not allowed\"); } console.log(semver.maxSatisfying([\"1.55.0\", \"1.56.4\", \"1.60.1\", \"2.1.0\"], \"^1.56.0\")); Output ^1.56.0 1.58.2 allowed ^1.56.0 2.0.0 not allowed ~1.56.0 1.56.9 allowed ~1.56.0 1.57.0 not allowed 1.56.1 1.56.2 not allowed ^0.4.2 0.5.0 not allowed 1.60.1 The npm commands you use every week · Reference npm init -y # create package.jsonnpm install ajv # add a dependency (writes package.json and package-lock.json)npm install -D @playwright/test # add a dev dependencynpm ci # clean install exactly what the lock file says (use this in CI)npm run test:unit # run a script from package.jsonnpm test -- --reporter=verbose # pass extra options to the script after --npx playwright test --headed # run a tool from node_modules/.binnpm outdated # what has newer versionsnpm audit # known security problems in dependencies Use npm ci in pipelines, not npm install. It fails if package.json and the lock file disagree, and it never changes the lock file. Pin browser automation tools such as Playwright exactly or with ~, because a new version also brings new browser builds. Lesson 4 of 7"],[9,"typescript","TypeScript","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypressWebdriverIOPlaywright and most modern frameworks generate TypeScript projects, and typed page objects catch broken locators and data at edit time. TypeScript is JavaScript with types added. You describe the shape of your data once, and the type checker warns you about wrong property names, missing fields and wrong value types while you type, long before a test runs. Playwright, Angular and most new projects use it, and its tests are usually written in it too. TypeScript runs directly in Node.js · ▶ Runs type Status = \"passed\" | \"failed\" | \"skipped\"; interface TestResult { name: string; status: Status; durationMs: number; retries?: number; // optional } function slowest<Textends { durationMs: number }>(items: T[], count = 1): T[] { return items.toSorted((a, b) => b.durationMs - a.durationMs).slice(0, count); } const results: TestResult[] = [ { name: \"login\", status: \"passed\", durationMs: 840 }, { name: \"checkout\", status: \"failed\", durationMs: 2310, retries: 2 }, { name: \"search\", status: \"passed\", durationMs: 120 }, ]; const failed = results.filter(r => r.status === \"failed\").map(r => r.name); console.log(failed, slowest(results)[0].name); Output [ 'checkout' ] checkout Node.js 23.6 and later run .ts files directly by removing the type annotations. It does not check them. Checking is the job of tsc, the TypeScript compiler, usually run as its own step in CI. The type checker finds bugs before any test runs · 🔎 Type check, finds 2 errors 📄 tsconfig.json { \"compilerOptions\": { \"target\": \"es2024\", \"module\": \"nodenext\", \"strict\": true, \"noEmit\": true, \"erasableSyntaxOnly\": true }, \"include\": [\"src\"] } 📄 src/order.ts interfaceOrder { id: number; total: number; coupon?: string; } exportfunction discount(order: Order): number { return order.coupon.length > 0 ? order.total * 0.9 : order.total; } discount({ id: 7, total: \"499\" }); Terminal $ npx tsc Terminal output src/order.ts(8,10): error TS18048: 'order.coupon' is possibly 'undefined'. src/order.ts(11,19): error TS2322: Type 'string' is not assignable to type 'number'. Syntax Meaning let n: number a type annotation interface User { name: string; age?: number } the shape of an object, ? marks optional type Status = \"passed\" | \"failed\" a union: one of these values function first<T>(list: T[]): T a generic: works for any type T value as string tell the checker you know better (use rarely) The erasableSyntaxOnly option (TypeScript 5.8) keeps you to type syntax that Node.js can simply remove, which is what makes running .ts files without a build step work. This is a first look. The TypeScript module, chapters 23 to 26, goes much further: narrowing and generics, mapped and conditional types, classes and decorators in real projects, and test automation written in TypeScript. Lesson 5 of 7"],[9,"lint","ESLint and Prettier","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightJestCypressA missing await or a forgotten test.only is caught by the linter before it reaches CI. ESLint reads your code without running it and reports likely bugs and risky habits: unused variables, typos in names, == where === was meant, an async function that never awaits. Rules live in eslint.config.js. ESLint reads the code and reports problems · 🔎 Lint, finds problems 📄 package.json { \"name\": \"linted\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"private\": true, \"type\": \"module\", \"scripts\": { \"lint\": \"eslint .\" } } 📄 eslint.config.js import js from\"@eslint/js\"; import globals from\"globals\"; exportdefault [ js.configs.recommended, { languageOptions: { globals: { ...globals.node } }, rules: { eqeqeq: \"error\", \"no-unused-vars\": \"error\", \"require-await\": \"error\", }, }, ]; 📄 tests/cart.test.js import { test } from\"node:test\"; import assert from\"node:assert/strict\"; test(\"cart total\", async () => { const items = [{ price: 100 }, { price: \"50\" }]; const unused = 3; let total = 0; for (const i of items) total += Number(i.price); assert.ok(total == 150); assert.equal(totl, 150); }); Terminal $ npx eslint . Terminal output tests/cart.test.js 4:29 error Async arrow function has no 'await' expression require-await 6:9 error 'unused' is assigned a value but never used no-unused-vars 9:19 error Expected '===' and instead saw '==' eqeqeq 10:16 error 'totl' is not defined no-undef ✖ 4 problems (4 errors, 0 warnings) Prettier only formats: spaces, quotes, line breaks and commas. It ends style discussions in code review because everyone's code comes out the same. Prettier rewrites the layout, never the meaning · ✨ Formatter runs 📄 package.json { \"name\": \"pretty\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"private\": true, \"type\": \"module\" } 📄 messy.js const user={name:\"Asha\",roles:[ 'admin','qa' ],active:true} function greet( u ){return`Hello, ${u.name}`} Terminal $ npx prettier messy.js Terminal output const user = { name: \"Asha\", roles: [\"admin\", \"qa\"], active: true }; function greet(u) { return `Hello, ${u.name}`; } Lint test code too. Plugins such as eslint-plugin-playwright, eslint-plugin-jest and eslint-plugin-cypress catch a missing await before an action, a forgotten test.only and conditionals inside tests. With TypeScript, the @typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises rule finds the missing await that causes most flaky Playwright tests. Lesson 6 of 7"],[9,"env","Environment variables and config","🧪 QA essentialChoosing the target environment, test users and secrets through variables is how one suite runs against every environment. The same tests must run against a laptop, a staging server and sometimes production. The parts that change, such as the base URL, user names and secrets, come from environment variables in process.env. Node.js can load them from a .env file with --env-file, with no extra package. Configuration from environment variables · ▶ Runs 📄 .env # Never commit real secrets. Keep .env in .gitignore and use CI secrets instead. TEST_ENV=staging BASE_URL=https://staging.shop.example TEST_USER=qa.bot@shop.example TEST_PASSWORD=not-a-real-password HEADLESS=true 📄 main.mjs // Values come from .env, which Node.js loads with --env-fileconst config = { env: process.env.TEST_ENV, baseURL: process.env.BASE_URL, user: process.env.TEST_USER, headless: process.env.HEADLESS !== \"false\", workers: Number(process.env.WORKERS ?? 2), }; console.log(config); console.log(\"password loaded:\", Boolean(process.env.TEST_PASSWORD), \"| length:\", process.env.TEST_PASSWORD.length); Terminal $ node --env-file=.env main.mjs Output { env: 'staging', baseURL: 'https://staging.shop.example', user: 'qa.bot@shop.example', headless: true, workers: 2 } password loaded: true | length: 19 Everything in process.env is a string. \"false\" is truthy, so compare with === \"true\" or !== \"false\", and turn numbers into numbers. Never commit passwords or tokens: add .env to .gitignore and use your CI tool's secret store. Lesson 7 of 7"],[9,"scenario","Real world scenario: lay out a test project","🧪 QA essential The situationYou are starting the automation project for a shop. Unit tests, API tests and end to end tests must be runnable on their own, and CI must be able to run each of them as a separate job. Source code lives in src. Tests are split by kind under tests. Each kind gets its own npm script, so a developer runs npm run test:unit in seconds while CI runs every script. Separate scripts for each kind of test · 🧪 Tests run 📄 package.json { \"name\": \"shop-tests\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"private\": true, \"type\": \"module\", \"scripts\": { \"test:unit\": \"node --test --test-reporter=spec tests/unit/\", \"test:api\": \"node --test --test-reporter=spec tests/api/\" } } 📄 src/price.js exportfunction addTax(amount, rate = 0.18) { returnMath.round(amount * (1 + rate) * 100) / 100; } 📄 src/api.js exportfunction buildQuery(params) { returnnewURLSearchParams(Object.entries(params).filter(([, v]) => v != null)).toString(); } 📄 tests/unit/price.test.js import { test } from\"node:test\"; import assert from\"node:assert/strict\"; import { addTax } from\"../../src/price.js\"; test(\"adds 18% by default\", () => assert.equal(addTax(100), 118)); test(\"rounds to paise\", () => assert.equal(addTax(9.99), 11.79)); 📄 tests/api/query.test.js import { test } from\"node:test\"; import assert from\"node:assert/strict\"; import { buildQuery } from\"../../src/api.js\"; test(\"drops empty filters\", () => { assert.equal(buildQuery({ q: \"pen\", page: 2, sort: null }), \"q=pen&page=2\"); }); Terminal $ npm run -s test:unit && npm run -s test:api Terminal output ✔ adds 18% by default (0.5ms) ✔ rounds to paise (0.5ms) ℹ tests 2 ℹ suites 0 ℹ pass 2 ℹ fail 0 ℹ cancelled 0 ℹ skipped 0 ℹ todo 0 ℹ duration_ms 0.5 ✔ drops empty filters (0.5ms) ℹ tests 1 ℹ suites 0 ℹ pass 1 ℹ fail 0 ℹ cancelled 0 ℹ skipped 0 ℹ todo 0 ℹ duration_ms 0.5 A typical layout shop-tests/ package.json, package-lock.json, tsconfig.json, eslint.config.js .env.example names of the variables, no real values src/ helpers, API clients, test data builders pages/ page objects tests/unit/ fast checks of pure functions tests/api/ HTTP requests against a running server tests/e2e/ browser tests with Playwright or Cypress playwright.config.ts 🧮"],[9,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 A project has 3 dependencies and 5 devDependencies. How many packages does package.json list? Add both lists. Answer: 8 3 + 5 = 8 C2 What is the MAJOR number in version 4.12.7? MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH. Answer: 4 The first number is the major version. C1 Which of 1.56.9, 1.57.0, 2.0.0 does \"~1.56.0\" allow? Answer with how many. Tilde: same minor. Answer: 1 Only 1.56.9 keeps minor version 56. C2 Unit tests take 20 s, API tests 90 s, e2e tests 6 minutes. In seconds, how long is the whole run one after another? Turn minutes into seconds. Answer: 470 6 × 60 = 360 20 + 90 + 360 = 470 C1 The same three jobs run in parallel CI jobs. In seconds, how long until all are done? The slowest job decides. Answer: 360 Parallel jobs finish when the longest one ends: 360 s. C2 WORKERS is \"4\" in .env. What is process.env.WORKERS + 1? A string plus a number. Answer: 41 Environment values are strings. \"4\" + 1 = \"41\" C1 How many errors did tsc report for src/order.ts? Read the output. Answer: 2 coupon is possibly undefined. The string \"499\" is not a number. C2 semver.maxSatisfying([\"1.55.0\", \"1.56.4\", \"1.60.1\", \"2.1.0\"], \"^1.56.0\") returned what? The highest version inside the range. Answer: 1.60.1 1.55.0 is too low and 2.1.0 is a new major. Of 1.56.4 and 1.60.1, the higher is 1.60.1. 🎯"],[9,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 Which line imports a named export called addTax? Named exports go in braces. Without braces you would get the default export instead. Q2 Which command should CI use to install dependencies? Clean and exact. npm ci installs exactly what the lock file says and fails if it is out of date. Q3 Where do test tools such as Playwright and Jest usually go? Needed only to build and test. devDependencies are not installed when the app itself is deployed. Q1 What does \"^1.56.0\" allow? Caret: same major. A caret allows new minor and patch versions within major version 1. Q2 You see \"Cannot use import statement outside a module\". What is the usual fix? The file is being treated as CommonJS. Node.js decides the module system from the extension and the type field. Q3 process.env.HEADLESS is \"false\". What is Boolean(process.env.HEADLESS)? Every non empty string is truthy. Environment values are strings, so compare them as text. Q1 What does Node.js do with type annotations in a .ts file? Type stripping. Checking is done separately by tsc. Q2 Which tool would report total == 150 when the rule eqeqeq is on? A rule about code quality. Prettier only changes layout. ESLint enforces rules like using ===. Q3 A module is imported by five test files. How many times does its top level code run? Modules are cached. A module is evaluated once, and every importer shares the same exports. Q1 Why might a team avoid export default? import Foo vs import Bar of the same thing. Each importer can call a default export by a different name, which makes code harder to search. Q2 What does a lock file protect you from? Reproducible installs. Without a lock file, a range like ^1.2.0 can resolve to a different version tomorrow. Q3 Which TypeScript error did tsc report first for discount({ id: 7, total: \"499\" })? coupon has a question mark. An optional property may be undefined, so calling .length on it is flagged. The string total is the second error."],[9,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[9,"env-parse","Parse an env file","Write parseEnv(text) that turns lines like KEY=value into an object. Skip empty lines and lines starting with #. Values may contain =, so split only at the first one. Use indexOf(\"=\") and slice, not split(\"=\"). function parseEnv(text) { const out = {}; for (const line of text.split(\"\\n\")) { const t = line.trim(); if (!t || t.startsWith(\"#\")) continue; const i = t.indexOf(\"=\"); out[t.slice(0, i)] = t.slice(i + 1); } return out; } URLs and tokens often contain =, so splitting at every one would cut them apart. 🌿 Medium"],[9,"config","Typed config from strings","Write loadConfig(env) that reads a plain object of strings and returns { baseURL, headless, workers }. baseURL defaults to \"http://localhost:3000\". headless is false only when the value is exactly \"false\". workers is a number, default 2. ?? gives defaults, Number() converts the text. function loadConfig(env) { return { baseURL: env.BASE_URL ?? \"http://localhost:3000\", headless: env.HEADLESS !== \"false\", workers: Number(env.WORKERS ?? 2), }; } Turning strings into real types in one place means the rest of the suite never has to remember that env values are text. 🔥 Hard"],[9,"caret","Does ^ allow it?","Write caretAllows(range, version) for ranges like \"^1.4.2\" and versions like \"1.9.0\". Return true when the major numbers match and the version is not lower than the range. To keep it short, ignore the stricter rule for major version 0. Turn both into three numbers. Compare minor first, then patch. function caretAllows(range, version) { const [a1, a2, a3] = range.slice(1).split(\".\").map(Number); const [b1, b2, b3] = version.split(\".\").map(Number); if (a1 !== b1) returnfalse; if (b2 !== a2) return b2 > a2; return b3 >= a3; } Comparing as numbers matters: as text, \"1.4.10\" would sort before \"1.4.2\". 🧠 Expert"],[9,"resolve","Resolve an import path","Write resolveImport(fromFile, spec). For a relative spec starting with ./ or ../, return the path of the imported file relative to the project root, as Node.js would. For anything else (a package name), return \"node_modules/\" + spec. Start from the folder of fromFile. .. removes a folder, . does nothing, any other piece is added. function resolveImport(fromFile, spec) { if (!spec.startsWith(\"./\") && !spec.startsWith(\"../\")) return\"node_modules/\" + spec; const parts = fromFile.split(\"/\").slice(0, -1); for (const piece of spec.split(\"/\")) { if (piece === \"..\") parts.pop(); elseif (piece !== \".\") parts.push(piece); } return parts.join(\"/\"); } This is what happens every time a test imports a page object, and getting it wrong is the cause of most \"Cannot find module\" errors."],[9,"resolve","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. ES modules: import and exportA module is a file with its own scope. Nothing inside it is visible to other files unless it is exported, and nothing from outside is available unless it is imported. CommonJS and how the two mixBefore ES modules, Node.js used CommonJS: require() to load and module.exports to share. Older packages, Jest setups, Mocha and many Selenium projects still use it, so you will read both styles. npm and package.jsonnpm installs packages from the npm registry into node_modules and records them in package.json. The lock file (package-lock.json) pins the exact version of every package, so every laptop and CI machine installs the same thing. TypeScriptTypeScript is JavaScript with types added. You describe the shape of your data once, and the type checker warns you about wrong property names, missing fields and wrong value types while you type, long before a test runs. ESLint and PrettierESLint reads your code without running it and reports likely bugs and risky habits: unused variables, typos in names, == where === was meant, an async function that never awaits. Rules live in eslint.config.js. Environment variables and configThe same tests must run against a laptop, a staging server and sometimes production. The parts that change, such as the base URL, user names and secrets, come from environment variables in process.env. Real world scenario: lay out a test projectSource code lives in src. Tests are split by kind under tests. Each kind gets its own npm script, so a developer runs npm run test:unit in seconds while CI runs every script."],[9,"comic","From one file to a real project","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Modules split the code, npm brings the tools, TypeScript and ESLint catch mistakes before tests run."],[9,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🏭A monorepo A company keeps its web app, its API and its Playwright suite in one repository with npm workspaces, so a change and its tests land in one pull request. 🔒A supply chain alert npm audit flags a vulnerable package in the test tools. The team updates it in the lock file and CI confirms nothing broke. 🌍One suite, three environments The same Playwright tests run against dev, staging and pre-production by changing BASE_URL and the test user in the CI job. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Modules, npm, TypeScript and Tooling\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 9Welcome This chapter covers modules, npm and package.json, TypeScript, ESLint and Prettier, environment variables and the layout of a test project. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[10,"","Asynchronous JavaScript: Promises, async and await","The single thread and the event loop with microtasks and tasks, error first callbacks, promises and chaining, Promise.withResolvers, async and await, sequential against parallel awaits, the missing await that makes a test pass by mistake, all, allSettled, race and any, AbortController and AbortSignal.timeout, polling and retry with exponential backoff, waiting in Playwright, and a link checker with a concurrency limit. · event loop · microtask · callback · Promise · then · catch · async · await · Promise.all · Promise.allSettled · Promise.race · Promise.any · AbortController · timeout · retry · backoff · polling · flaky tests 🟨 JavaScript›⏳ Async and the platform›Chapter 11 of 31 ⏳ Step 4 · Topic 1 of 3 · Intermediate"],[10,"top","","How one thread handles slow work with the event loop, callbacks, promises, async and await, running promises together, cancelling with AbortController, and waiting with retries and polling instead of fixed sleeps. ⏱️ About 18 min read📚 7 parts🎬 5 animations▶ 12 runnable examples🧪 1 real test run🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 5 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Every browser and API action in a test is asynchronous. Missing awaits and fixed sleeps cause most flaky tests, and this chapter shows both. 1One thread and the event loop 2Callbacks 3Promises 4async and await 5Running many promises: all, allSettled, race, any 6Retries, polling and waiting the right way 7Real world scenario: a link checker Lesson 1 of 7"],[10,"event-loop","One thread and the event loop","JavaScript runs your code on one thread: one line at a time. Slow work such as a network request, a timer or reading a file is handed to the browser or Node.js, and your code keeps going. When the slow work finishes, a callback is queued. The event loop runs queued callbacks only when the call stack is empty. 🎬 Try it"],[10,"event-loop","The event loop runs the queues in order","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. console.log(\"1 start\"); setTimeout(() => console.log(\"5 timeout\"), 0); Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"3 promise\")); queueMicrotask(() => console.log(\"4 microtask\")); console.log(\"2 end\"); Call stack Microtasks Tasks Console Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 7 Which line prints first · ▶ Runs console.log(\"1 start\"); setTimeout(() => console.log(\"5 timeout\"), 0); Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"3 promise\")); queueMicrotask(() => console.log(\"4 microtask\")); console.log(\"2 end\"); Output 1 start 2 end 3 promise 4 microtask 5 timeout Now try your own code. The simulator below accepts console.log, setTimeout, queueMicrotask and Promise.resolve().then, including callbacks inside callbacks. Predict the console first, then press Play. 🎬 Try it"],[10,"event-loop","Event loop simulator","Pick an example or write your own with console.log, setTimeout, queueMicrotask and Promise.resolve().then. Then press Play. ⏱️ clock: 0 ms 📚 Call stack empty ⏲️ Web APIs (timers) empty ⚡ Microtask queue empty 📬 Task queue empty 🖥️ Console Press ▶ Play to watch each line move through the browser. 0 / 14 Queue What goes there When it runs Microtasks promise callbacks (then, code after await), queueMicrotask right after the current code, all of them, before anything else Tasks (macrotasks) setTimeout, setInterval, clicks, network and file events one per turn of the loop, after the microtasks are empty Because there is only one thread, a long loop blocks everything: timers, clicks and screen updates all wait. One thread: a busy loop delays everything · ▶ Runs const started = Date.now(); setTimeout(() => console.log(`timer fired after ${Math.round((Date.now() - started) / 100) * 100} ms`), 100); // A long loop keeps the only thread busy. Nothing else can run meanwhile.const until = Date.now() + 500; while (Date.now() < until) {} console.log(\"busy loop done\"); Output busy loop done timer fired after 500 ms Lesson 2 of 7"],[10,"callbacks","Callbacks","The first way to handle async work was to pass a function to call later. Node.js callbacks receive the error first and the result second. It works, but each step that depends on the previous one adds a level of nesting, known as callback hell, and error handling must be repeated at every level. Callbacks and the pyramid they build · ▶ Runs import { readFile } from\"node:fs\"; // Node.js style: the callback receives (error, result) readFile(\"missing.json\", \"utf8\", (err, text) => { if (err) return console.log(\"callback got an error:\", err.code); console.log(text); }); // Callbacks inside callbacks get deep quicklyfunction step(name, done) { setTimeout(() => done(null, name), 10); } step(\"login\", (e1, a) => { step(\"search\", (e2, b) => { step(\"checkout\", (e3, c) => { console.log(\"finished:\", a, b, c); }); }); }); Output callback got an error: ENOENT finished: login search checkout Most Node.js APIs now have promise versions: import { readFile } from \"node:fs/promises\". For an old callback API, util.promisify(fn) creates one. Lesson 3 of 7"],[10,"promises","Promises","🧪 QA essentialEvery Playwright, WebdriverIO and Selenium call returns a promise, so you need to know what one is. A promise is an object that stands for a result that is not ready yet. It starts pending, then becomes fulfilled with a value or rejected with an error, and never changes again. then runs when it fulfils, catch when it rejects and finally either way. Returning a value or a promise from then passes it to the next step, so the steps form a flat chain. Creating and chaining promises · ▶ Runs function wait(ms, value, fail = false) { returnnewPromise((resolve, reject) => { setTimeout(() => (fail ? reject(newError(`${value} failed`)) : resolve(value)), ms); }); } const p = wait(20, \"token-123\"); console.log(p); // still pending p.then(token => { console.log(\"got\", token); return wait(10, `profile for ${token}`); // returning a promise chains it }) .then(profile => console.log(profile)) .then(() => wait(10, \"orders\", true)) .catch(err => console.log(\"caught:\", err.message)) // one catch for the whole chain .finally(() => console.log(\"cleanup runs either way\")); Output Promise { <pending> } got token-123 profile for token-123 caught: orders failed cleanup runs either way Follow one real chain below. Each then creates a new promise, which is why returning a value passes it on, returning a promise makes the chain wait for it, and one catch at the end handles a failure in any earlier step. 🎬 Try it"],[10,"promises","A promise chain, one state at a time","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. fetch(\"/api/orders/7\") .then(res => res.json()) .then(order => order.total) .catch(err => 0) .finally(() => hideSpinner()); Promise State What runs Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 6 Promise.withResolvers for event driven code · ▶ Runs // ES2024: create a promise and get its resolve and reject functions outside itconst { promise, resolve } = Promise.withResolvers(); const events = []; function onMessage(msg) { events.push(msg); if (msg === \"order-confirmed\") resolve(events.length); } setTimeout(() => onMessage(\"cart-updated\"), 5); setTimeout(() => onMessage(\"payment-ok\"), 10); setTimeout(() => onMessage(\"order-confirmed\"), 15); console.log(\"confirmed after\", await promise, \"events\"); Output confirmed after 3 events A rejected promise that nobody catches is an unhandled rejection. In Node.js it crashes the process, and in test runners it often shows up as a confusing failure in a different test. Lesson 4 of 7"],[10,"async-await","async and await","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightSeleniumWebdriverIOJestCypressA missing await is the number one cause of flaky and falsely passing automated tests. async before a function makes it return a promise. Inside it, await pauses that function until a promise settles and gives you its value, while the rest of the program keeps running. Errors become normal exceptions you catch with try and catch. async and await · ▶ Runs const wait = (ms, v) => newPromise(r => setTimeout(() => r(v), ms)); asyncfunction login(user) { await wait(10); if (user === \"blocked\") thrownewError(\"account locked\"); return { user, token: \"t-\" + user }; } asyncfunction main() { const session = await login(\"asha\"); // reads like normal code console.log(session); try { await login(\"blocked\"); } catch (e) { console.log(\"caught:\", e.message); } return\"done\"; } const result = main(); console.log(result instanceofPromise); // an async function always returns a promise console.log(await result); Output true { user: 'asha', token: 't-asha' } caught: account locked done"],[10,"async-await","Sequential or parallel","Two await lines one after another run one after another. When the steps do not depend on each other, start both and wait for both with Promise.all. Sequential or parallel · ▶ Runs const wait = (ms, v) => newPromise(r => setTimeout(() => r(v), ms)); const t = () => Math.round((performance.now() - start) / 100) * 100; let start = performance.now(); const a = await wait(300, \"users\"); const b = await wait(300, \"orders\"); console.log(\"one after another:\", t(), \"ms\", a, b); start = performance.now(); const [c, d] = awaitPromise.all([wait(300, \"users\"), wait(300, \"orders\")]); console.log(\"at the same time:\", t(), \"ms\", c, d); Output one after another: 600 ms users orders at the same time: 300 ms users orders"],[10,"async-await","The most common bug in test code","Forgetting await lets the test finish before the check runs. The test is reported as passed even though the server returned an error. The Node.js runner at least prints a warning about activity after the test ended. Many other runners print nothing at all. A missing await hides a real failure · 🧪 Tests run, one fails on purpose 📄 package.json { \"name\": \"await-bug\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"private\": true, \"type\": \"module\", \"scripts\": { \"test\": \"node --test --test-reporter=spec\" } } 📄 status.test.js import { test } from\"node:test\"; import assert from\"node:assert/strict\"; asyncfunction getStatus() { awaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, 20)); return500; // the server is broken } test(\"forgot await: passes by mistake\", () => { getStatus().then(s => assert.equal(s, 200)); // the test ends before this runs }); test(\"with await: fails as it should\", async () => { assert.equal(await getStatus(), 200); }); Terminal $ npm test Terminal output > await-bug@1.0.0 test > node --test --test-reporter=spec ✔ forgot await: passes by mistake ✖ with await: fails as it should ℹ Error: Test \"forgot await: passes by mistake\" at status.test.js:9:1 generated asynchronous activity after the test ended. This activity created the error \"AssertionError [ERR_ASSERTION]: Expected values to be strictly equal: 500 !== 200 \" and would have caused the test to fail, but instead triggered an unhandledRejection event. ℹ tests 2 ℹ suites 0 ℹ pass 1 ℹ fail 1 ℹ cancelled 0 ℹ skipped 0 ℹ todo 0 ℹ duration_ms 90 ✖ failing tests: test at status.test.js:13:1 ✖ with await: fails as it should AssertionError [ERR_ASSERTION]: Expected values to be strictly equal: 500 !== 200 at TestContext.<anonymous> (status.test.js:14:10) Every Playwright action (click, fill, goto) and every expect(locator) assertion must be awaited. WebdriverIO and Selenium JavaScript are the same. Cypress is the exception: its commands are queued and you must not await them. Turn on the lint rule no-floating-promises to catch a missing await automatically. Lesson 5 of 7"],[10,"combinators","Running many promises: all, allSettled, race, any","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightJestClicking and waiting for a response together, checking many endpoints, and timeouts all use these helpers. Four helpers combine several promises into one. They differ in what counts as done and what happens on a failure. all, allSettled, race and any · ▶ Runs const ok = (ms, v) => newPromise(r => setTimeout(() => r(v), ms)); const bad = (ms, msg) => newPromise((_, j) => setTimeout(() => j(newError(msg)), ms)); try { awaitPromise.all([ok(10, \"a\"), bad(20, \"b broke\"), ok(30, \"c\")]); } catch (e) { console.log(\"all: rejects on the first failure ->\", e.message); } const settled = awaitPromise.allSettled([ok(10, \"a\"), bad(20, \"b broke\")]); console.log(\"allSettled:\", settled.map(r => r.status === \"fulfilled\" ? r.value : r.reason.message)); console.log(\"race:\", awaitPromise.race([ok(30, \"slow\"), ok(10, \"fast\")])); console.log(\"any:\", awaitPromise.any([bad(5, \"mirror 1 down\"), ok(20, \"mirror 2\"), ok(30, \"mirror 3\")])); try { awaitPromise.any([bad(5, \"x\"), bad(10, \"y\")]); } catch (e) { console.log(\"any, all failed:\", e.constructor.name, e.errors.map(x => x.message)); } Output all: rejects on the first failure -> b broke allSettled: [ 'a', 'b broke' ] race: fast any: mirror 2 any, all failed: AggregateError [ 'x', 'y' ] The timeline below runs the four helpers on the same three promises, so you can see the exact moment each one decides. Notice that the requests themselves keep running: Promise.all stops waiting, it does not cancel anything. Use an AbortController to really cancel requests. 🎬 Try it"],[10,"combinators","all, allSettled, race and any on the same three promises","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. const fast = wait(100, \"fast ok\"); const bad = fail(200, \"500 error\"); const slow = wait(300, \"slow ok\"); Promise.all([fast, bad, slow]); Promise.allSettled([fast, bad, slow]); Promise.race([fast, bad, slow]); Promise.any([fast, bad, slow]); Clock all allSettled race any Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 4 Helper Fulfils when Rejects when Good for Promise.all all fulfil the first one rejects steps that must all succeed Promise.allSettled all have finished, either way never reports: check every link, list each result Promise.race the first one settles the first one settles with an error timeouts Promise.any the first one fulfils all reject (AggregateError) the fastest of several mirrors"],[10,"combinators","Cancelling","An AbortController gives you a signal to pass to fetch and other APIs, and an abort() method to stop them. AbortSignal.timeout(ms) aborts by itself, and AbortSignal.any combines signals. Cancelling with AbortController · ▶ Runs asyncfunction slowRequest(signal) { returnnewPromise((resolve, reject) => { const timer = setTimeout(() => resolve(\"response\"), 500); signal.addEventListener(\"abort\", () => { clearTimeout(timer); reject(signal.reason); }, { once: true }); }); } // Cancel by handconst controller = newAbortController(); setTimeout(() => controller.abort(newError(\"user left the page\")), 50); await slowRequest(controller.signal).catch(e => console.log(\"aborted:\", e.message)); // Cancel on a timeoutawait slowRequest(AbortSignal.timeout(100)).catch(e => console.log(\"timed out:\", e.name)); // Whichever comes firstconst either = AbortSignal.any([AbortSignal.timeout(80), newAbortController().signal]); await slowRequest(either).catch(e => console.log(\"any:\", e.name)); Output aborted: user left the page timed out: TimeoutError any: TimeoutError Lesson 6 of 7"],[10,"waiting","Retries, polling and waiting the right way","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypressSeleniumWebdriverIOWaiting correctly is the difference between a stable suite and a flaky one. Tests and scripts often wait for something that happens at an unknown time: an element appears, a job finishes, a service recovers. A fixed sleep guesses the time. It wastes time when the guess is too long and fails when it is too short. Polling checks again and again until the condition is true or a timeout is reached. 🎬 Try it"],[10,"waiting","Sleep or wait for the element","Move the slider to change when the Pay button appears, then compare the two strategies. The button appears after 1800 ms sleep(2000) waited 2000 ms, wasted 200 ms auto-wait clicked at about 1800 ms timeout 5000 the most it would ever wait The fixed sleep passes but wastes 200 ms on every run. Auto-waiting moves on the moment the button is ready. Poll until a condition is true · ▶ Runs const sleep = ms => newPromise(r => setTimeout(r, ms)); // Keep checking until the condition is true, or give up after timeoutasyncfunction waitFor(check, { timeout = 1000, interval = 50, message = \"condition\" } = {}) { const deadline = Date.now() + timeout; let lastError; while (Date.now() < deadline) { try { const value = await check(); if (value) return value; } catch (e) { lastError = e; } await sleep(interval); } thrownewError(`Timed out after ${timeout} ms waiting for ${message}` + (lastError ? `: ${lastError.message}` : \"\")); } // A report that becomes ready after about 200 msconst readyAt = Date.now() + 200; const getReport = async () => (Date.now() >= readyAt ? { status: \"ready\", rows: 42 } : { status: \"processing\" }); const report = await waitFor(async () => { const r = await getReport(); return r.status === \"ready\" && r; }, { message: \"the report\" }); console.log(report); await waitFor(() => false, { timeout: 150, message: \"a button that never appears\" }).catch(e => console.log(e.message)); Output { status: 'ready', rows: 42 } Timed out after 150 ms waiting for a button that never appears A retry with exponential backoff repeats a failing call with longer and longer pauses, which gives a struggling server room to recover. Retry with exponential backoff · ▶ Runs const sleep = ms => newPromise(r => setTimeout(r, ms)); asyncfunction retry(fn, { attempts = 4, baseMs = 20 } = {}) { for (let i = 1; ; i++) { try { returnawait fn(i); } catch (e) { if (i === attempts) throw e; const delay = baseMs * 2 ** (i - 1); // 20, 40, 80 ... console.log(`attempt ${i} failed (${e.message}), waiting ${delay} ms`); await sleep(delay); } } } let calls = 0; const flakyService = async () => { calls++; if (calls < 3) thrownewError(\"503 Service Unavailable\"); return { status: 200 }; }; console.log(await retry(flakyService)); Output attempt 1 failed (503 Service Unavailable), waiting 20 ms attempt 2 failed (503 Service Unavailable), waiting 40 ms { status: 200 } Modern tools build this in, so you rarely write the loop yourself. Waiting the right way in Playwright · Runs for real in chapter 22 // Playwright waits for you: actions wait for the element to be visible, enabled and stableawait page.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Pay\" }).click(); // Web first assertions keep retrying until they pass or time outawait expect(page.getByText(\"Payment received\")).toBeVisible({ timeout: 10_000 }); // Poll anything, such as an API, with expect.pollawait expect.poll(async () => { const res = await request.get(\"/api/orders/42\"); return (await res.json()).status; }, { timeout: 30_000 }).toBe(\"shipped\"); // Wait for a response the page makesconst [response] = awaitPromise.all([ page.waitForResponse(r => r.url().includes(\"/api/cart\") && r.ok()), page.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Add to cart\" }).click(), ]); // Avoid: a fixed sleep is either too long or too shortawait page.waitForTimeout(3000); Retrying a whole test hides flakiness. Use retries for setup calls to unstable services and report retried tests as flaky, but fix the cause: a missing wait for a response, a fixed sleep, or shared test data. Lesson 7 of 7"],[10,"scenario","Real world scenario: a link checker","🧪 QA essential The situationBefore a release you must check that 8 product pages respond. The server must not get more than 3 requests at once, a page that takes longer than 200 ms counts as broken, and the report must list every page, not stop at the first problem. A link checker with a concurrency limit and timeouts · ▶ Runs // Check 8 product URLs, at most 3 requests at a time, with a timeout on each.const sleep = ms => newPromise(r => setTimeout(r, ms)); const latency = { \"/p/1\": 30, \"/p/2\": 50, \"/p/3\": 400, \"/p/4\": 20, \"/p/5\": 40, \"/p/6\": 30, \"/p/7\": 25, \"/p/8\": 35 }; let active = 0, peak = 0; asyncfunction fakeFetch(url, { signal }) { active++; peak = Math.max(peak, active); try { awaitnewPromise((resolve, reject) => { const t = setTimeout(resolve, latency[url]); signal.addEventListener(\"abort\", () => { clearTimeout(t); reject(signal.reason); }); }); return { url, status: url === \"/p/5\" ? 404 : 200 }; } finally { active--; } } asyncfunction mapLimit(items, limit, fn) { const results = newArray(items.length); let next = 0; asyncfunction worker() { while (next < items.length) { const i = next++; results[i] = await fn(items[i]); } } awaitPromise.all(Array.from({ length: limit }, worker)); return results; } const urls = Object.keys(latency); const results = await mapLimit(urls, 3, url => fakeFetch(url, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(200) }) .then(r => ({ url, ok: r.status === 200, status: r.status })) .catch(e => ({ url, ok: false, status: e.name }))); console.table(results); console.log(\"peak parallel requests:\", peak); console.log(\"broken:\", results.filter(r => !r.ok).map(r => `${r.url} (${r.status})`).join(\", \")); Output ┌─────────┬────────┬───────┬────────────────┐ │ (index) │ url │ ok │ status │ ├─────────┼────────┼───────┼────────────────┤ │ 0 │ '/p/1' │ true │ 200 │ │ 1 │ '/p/2' │ true │ 200 │ │ 2 │ '/p/3' │ false │ 'TimeoutError' │ │ 3 │ '/p/4' │ true │ 200 │ │ 4 │ '/p/5' │ false │ 404 │ │ 5 │ '/p/6' │ true │ 200 │ │ 6 │ '/p/7' │ true │ 200 │ │ 7 │ '/p/8' │ true │ 200 │ └─────────┴────────┴───────┴────────────────┘ peak parallel requests: 3 broken: /p/3 (TimeoutError), /p/5 (404) mapLimit starts 3 workers that take the next URL as soon as they are free. AbortSignal.timeout stops slow requests, and catching each error turns it into a row in the report instead of stopping the run. 🧮"],[10,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 Three API calls take 200, 350 and 150 ms. How many ms does Promise.all take? The slowest decides. Answer: 350 They run together, so the time is the longest one. C2 The same three calls awaited one after another take how many ms? Add them. Answer: 700 200 + 350 + 150 = 700 C1 Retry with base 100 ms doubling, 4 attempts. How many ms of waiting in total if all 4 fail? Waits happen between attempts. Answer: 700 Three waits: 100, 200 and 400. 100 + 200 + 400 = 700 C2 Polling every 50 ms with a 1000 ms timeout checks at most how many times? Divide. Answer: 20 1000 ÷ 50 = 20 C1 In the order example, which number is printed last? Tasks come after all microtasks. Answer: 5 1 and 2 are the main code. 3 and 4 are microtasks. 5 is the timeout task. C2 12 requests of 100 ms each run with a limit of 3 at a time. About how many ms in total? Batches of 3. Answer: 400 12 ÷ 3 = 4 rounds. 4 × 100 = 400 ms. C1 A test suite has 200 fixed sleeps of 2 s. The real waits average 0.4 s. How many seconds are wasted per run? Wasted time per sleep times count. Answer: 320 2 − 0.4 = 1.6 s per sleep. 1.6 × 200 = 320 s. C2 The exponential backoff delay is base × 2n−1. With base 250 ms, what is the delay before attempt 5, that is after 4 failures? n is 4 here. Answer: 2000 250 × 23 = 250 × 8 = 2000 ms. 🎯"],[10,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 What does an async function always return? Even return 5 is wrapped. The returned value becomes the fulfilled value of a promise. Q2 Which helper rejects as soon as one promise rejects? All or nothing. Promise.all fails fast on the first rejection. Q3 In Playwright, what is missing in page.getByRole(\"button\").click() inside an async test? Actions return promises. Without await the test moves on before the click finishes. Q1 Which runs first: a setTimeout(fn, 0) callback or a Promise.resolve().then(fn) callback? Microtasks before tasks. Promise callbacks are microtasks and run before the next task. Q2 Two independent awaits of 300 ms each, one after the other, take about how long? One waits for the other. Sequential awaits add up. Promise.all would take about 300 ms. Q3 What does Promise.allSettled never do? It always reports. It waits for all and fulfils with a status for each. Q1 A test calls getStatus().then(s => assert.equal(s, 200)) without returning or awaiting it. Why does it pass? Who waits for the promise? Nothing waits for the promise, so the runner sees a test that finished without errors. Q2 Promise.any rejects with which error when every promise rejects? It collects all the errors. An AggregateError has an errors array with each reason. Q3 Why is page.waitForTimeout(3000) considered a bad wait? A guess. A fixed sleep wastes time or fails. Waiting for a condition does neither. Q1 In Cypress, why should you not write await cy.get(\"button\")? Cypress has its own command queue. Cypress runs its queued commands in order by itself. Mixing await with them breaks that. Q2 What is the delay before the 3rd retry when the base is 20 ms and it doubles each time? 20, 40, 80. The waits are base × 2 to the power of (attempt minus 1). Q3 What does AbortSignal.any([a, b]) return? Combine reasons to stop. Useful to stop on a timeout or when the user cancels, whichever comes first."],[10,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[10,"sleep","A sleep helper","Write sleep(ms, value) that returns a promise which fulfils with value after ms milliseconds. new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(...)). function sleep(ms, value) { returnnewPromise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve(value), ms)); } Wrapping a callback API in a promise is how almost every async helper is built. 🌿 Medium"],[10,"timeout","Give up after a timeout","Write withTimeout(promise, ms). It fulfils with the promise value if it settles in time, otherwise it rejects with an Error whose message is \"Timed out after ms ms\" (with the number). Promise.race with a promise that rejects after ms. function withTimeout(promise, ms) { const timer = newPromise((_, reject) => setTimeout(() => reject(newError(`Timed out after ${ms} ms`)), ms)); returnPromise.race([promise, timer]); } race settles with whichever finishes first. A production version also clears the timer, which AbortSignal.timeout does for you. 🔥 Hard"],[10,"retry","Retry a flaky call","Write retry(fn, attempts) that calls the async function fn and returns its result. If it throws, call it again, up to attempts times in total. If the last attempt also throws, reject with that last error. A loop with try and return await fn() inside. asyncfunction retry(fn, attempts) { let lastError; for (let i = 0; i < attempts; i++) { try { returnawait fn(); } catch (e) { lastError = e; } } throw lastError; } return await inside try matters: without await, a rejection would escape the try block and skip the retry. 🧠 Expert"],[10,"limit","Run with a concurrency limit","Write mapLimit(items, limit, fn) that calls the async fn(item) for every item, with at most limit calls running at the same time, and returns the results in the original order. Start limit workers. Each one takes the next index in a loop until none are left. asyncfunction mapLimit(items, limit, fn) { const results = newArray(items.length); let next = 0; asyncfunction worker() { while (next < items.length) { const i = next++; results[i] = await fn(items[i]); } } awaitPromise.all(Array.from({ length: Math.min(limit, items.length) }, worker)); return results; } Saving each result at its own index keeps the order, even though calls finish in a different order."],[10,"limit","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. One thread and the event loopJavaScript runs your code on one thread: one line at a time. Slow work such as a network request, a timer or reading a file is handed to the browser or Node.js, and your code keeps going. When the slow work finishes, a callback is queued. CallbacksThe first way to handle async work was to pass a function to call later. Node.js callbacks receive the error first and the result second. PromisesA promise is an object that stands for a result that is not ready yet. It starts pending, then becomes fulfilled with a value or rejected with an error, and never changes again. then runs when it fulfils, catch when it rejects and finally either way. async and awaitasync before a function makes it return a promise. Inside it, await pauses that function until a promise settles and gives you its value, while the rest of the program keeps running. Errors become normal exceptions you catch with try and catch. Running many promises: all, allSettled, race, anyFour helpers combine several promises into one. They differ in what counts as done and what happens on a failure. Retries, polling and waiting the right wayTests and scripts often wait for something that happens at an unknown time: an element appears, a job finishes, a service recovers. A fixed sleep guesses the time. It wastes time when the guess is too long and fails when it is too short. Real world scenario: a link checkermapLimit starts 3 workers that take the next URL as soon as they are free. AbortSignal.timeout stops slow requests, and catching each error turns it into a row in the report instead of stopping the run."],[10,"comic","Waiting without stopping","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Await every promise you care about, run independent work in parallel, and poll instead of sleeping."],[10,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🛍️A product page The page asks for details, reviews and stock at the same time with Promise.all, so it loads in the time of the slowest call, not the sum of all three. 🔁A flaky payment sandbox A test setup call to a third party sandbox retries with backoff, while the tests themselves never retry silently. 🔗Release smoke check A CI job checks 500 URLs with 10 requests at a time and allSettled, then posts the broken ones to the team. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Asynchronous JavaScript: Promises, async and await\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 9Welcome This chapter covers the event loop, callbacks, promises, async and await, combining promises, cancelling, retries and polling. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[11,"","The DOM and the Browser","The DOM tree and how to move around it, CSS selectors including :has and attribute selectors, XPath by text and ancestor, which locators stay stable, textContent against innerHTML and XSS, events with capture and bubble, delegation and trusted events, form values and validation, shadow DOM and iframes in each test tool, waiting with MutationObserver, the rendering pipeline from style to composite with requestAnimationFrame, layout thrashing and IntersectionObserver, localStorage, sessionStorage and cookies, and checking every row of an order table. · DOM · querySelector · CSS selector · XPath · locator · getByRole · data-test · textContent · innerHTML · XSS · event · bubbling · delegation · form validation · shadow DOM · iframe · MutationObserver · rendering · layout · paint · requestAnimationFrame · IntersectionObserver · localStorage · cookie 🟨 JavaScript›⏳ Async and the platform›Chapter 12 of 31 🌐 Step 4 · Topic 2 of 3 · Intermediate"],[11,"top","","How the page becomes a tree of objects, finding elements with CSS selectors and XPath, choosing stable locators, changing the page safely, events and delegation, forms and validation, shadow DOM, iframes, observers, the rendering pipeline with requestAnimationFrame, storage and cookies. ⏱️ About 23 min read📚 9 parts🎬 2 animations▶ 11 runnable examples🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 14 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 8 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers UI automation is the DOM driven from outside. Locators, events, frames, shadow roots and browser storage explain most of what makes UI tests pass, fail or flake. 1The DOM tree 2Selectors and locators 3Changing the page 4Events 5Forms and validation 6Shadow DOM, iframes and observers 7How the browser draws the page 8Storage and cookies 9Real world scenario: check an order table Lesson 1 of 9"],[11,"dom","The DOM tree","🧪 QA essentialEvery locator in Selenium, Playwright and Cypress finds nodes in this tree. When a browser loads HTML, it builds the DOM (Document Object Model): a tree of objects, one for each element, piece of text and comment. JavaScript reads and changes the page only through this tree, and so does every UI automation tool. Finding elements and walking the tree · ▶ Runs in the browser 📄 page.html <!doctype html> <html> <body> <header><navaria-label=\"Main\"><ahref=\"/\">Home</a> <ahref=\"/deals\"class=\"hot\">Deals</a></nav></header> <mainid=\"products\"> <articleclass=\"card\"data-test=\"product\"data-sku=\"PEN-01\"> <h2>Gel pen</h2><spanclass=\"price\">₹20</span> <buttontype=\"button\"class=\"add\">Add to cart</button> </article> <articleclass=\"card sold-out\"data-test=\"product\"data-sku=\"BOOK-07\"> <h2>Notebook</h2><spanclass=\"price\">₹250</span> <buttontype=\"button\"class=\"add\"disabled>Sold out</button> </article> <articleclass=\"card\"data-test=\"product\"data-sku=\"MUG-03\"> <h2>Coffee mug</h2><spanclass=\"price\">₹199</span> <buttontype=\"button\"class=\"add\">Add to cart</button> </article> </main> <pid=\"cart-count\">0</p> </body> </html> 📄 script.js console.log(document.title === \"\", document.body.children.length); const main = document.getElementById(\"products\"); const firstCard = main.querySelector(\".card\"); // the first matchconst allCards = main.querySelectorAll(\".card\"); // a static NodeList console.log(firstCard.querySelector(\"h2\").textContent, allCards.length); // Walk around the treeconst price = firstCard.querySelector(\".price\"); console.log(price.parentElement.dataset.sku); // up console.log(price.previousElementSibling.textContent); // sideways console.log(price.closest(\"main\").id); // nearest ancestor that matches console.log([...allCards].map(c => c.dataset.sku)); Output true 3 Gel pen 3 PEN-01 Gel pen products [ 'PEN-01', 'BOOK-07', 'MUG-03' ] Method Returns getElementById(\"x\") one element or null querySelector(css) the first match or null querySelectorAll(css) a NodeList of every match, possibly empty el.closest(css) the element itself or its nearest ancestor that matches parentElement, children, nextElementSibling neighbours in the tree Lesson 2 of 9"],[11,"selectors","Selectors and locators","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightSeleniumCypressWebdriverIOChoosing stable locators decides how often your UI tests break. A CSS selector describes elements by tag, id, class, attribute and position. The same selector works in document.querySelector, in your stylesheet and in every test tool. CSS selectors for finding elements · ▶ Runs in the browser 📄 page.html <!doctype html> <html> <body> <header><navaria-label=\"Main\"><ahref=\"/\">Home</a> <ahref=\"/deals\"class=\"hot\">Deals</a></nav></header> <mainid=\"products\"> <articleclass=\"card\"data-test=\"product\"data-sku=\"PEN-01\"> <h2>Gel pen</h2><spanclass=\"price\">₹20</span> <buttontype=\"button\"class=\"add\">Add to cart</button> </article> <articleclass=\"card sold-out\"data-test=\"product\"data-sku=\"BOOK-07\"> <h2>Notebook</h2><spanclass=\"price\">₹250</span> <buttontype=\"button\"class=\"add\"disabled>Sold out</button> </article> <articleclass=\"card\"data-test=\"product\"data-sku=\"MUG-03\"> <h2>Coffee mug</h2><spanclass=\"price\">₹199</span> <buttontype=\"button\"class=\"add\">Add to cart</button> </article> </main> <pid=\"cart-count\">0</p> </body> </html> 📄 script.js const q = s => document.querySelectorAll(s).length; console.log(\"by tag: \", q(\"article\")); console.log(\"by class: \", q(\".card\")); console.log(\"two classes: \", q(\".card.sold-out\")); console.log(\"attribute: \", q('[data-test=\"product\"]')); console.log(\"attribute prefix: \", q('[data-sku^=\"PEN\"]')); console.log(\"child of: \", q(\"main > article\")); console.log(\"not: \", q(\".card:not(.sold-out)\")); console.log(\"nth: \", document.querySelector(\".card:nth-of-type(3) h2\").textContent); console.log(\"has (parent by child):\", q(\"article:has(button:disabled)\")); console.log(\"enabled buttons: \", q(\"button.add:enabled\")); Output by tag: 3 by class: 3 two classes: 1 attribute: 3 attribute prefix: 1 child of: 3 not: 2 nth: Coffee mug has (parent by child): 1 enabled buttons: 2 XPath is an older query language for the same tree. It can find elements by their text and step up to ancestors, which CSS cannot. Selenium supports it fully and many older suites use it. XPath, used by Selenium and others · ▶ Runs in the browser 📄 page.html <!doctype html> <html> <body> <header><navaria-label=\"Main\"><ahref=\"/\">Home</a> <ahref=\"/deals\"class=\"hot\">Deals</a></nav></header> <mainid=\"products\"> <articleclass=\"card\"data-test=\"product\"data-sku=\"PEN-01\"> <h2>Gel pen</h2><spanclass=\"price\">₹20</span> <buttontype=\"button\"class=\"add\">Add to cart</button> </article> <articleclass=\"card sold-out\"data-test=\"product\"data-sku=\"BOOK-07\"> <h2>Notebook</h2><spanclass=\"price\">₹250</span> <buttontype=\"button\"class=\"add\"disabled>Sold out</button> </article> <articleclass=\"card\"data-test=\"product\"data-sku=\"MUG-03\"> <h2>Coffee mug</h2><spanclass=\"price\">₹199</span> <buttontype=\"button\"class=\"add\">Add to cart</button> </article> </main> <pid=\"cart-count\">0</p> </body> </html> 📄 script.js function xpath(expr, context = document) { const r = document.evaluate(expr, context, null, XPathResult.ORDERED_NODE_SNAPSHOT_TYPE, null); returnArray.from({ length: r.snapshotLength }, (_, i) => r.snapshotItem(i)); } // Find by visible text, which CSS cannot do console.log(xpath(\"//h2[text()='Coffee mug']\").length); // Go up to the card that contains that heading, then down to its button console.log(xpath(\"//h2[text()='Coffee mug']/ancestor::article//button\")[0].textContent); // Contains, and an index console.log(xpath(\"//button[contains(., 'Sold')]\").map(b => b.disabled)); console.log(xpath(\"(//span[@class='price'])[last()]\")[0].textContent); Output 1 Add to cart [ true ] ₹199"],[11,"selectors","Which locator to choose","Locator Example Stability Role and name (what a user sees) getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Add to cart\" }) best: survives layout and styling changes Label or text getByLabel(\"Email\"), getByText(\"Deals\") good, changes with the wording Test id [data-test=\"product\"], getByTestId good, made for tests Short CSS .card .price fair, breaks when classes change Long CSS or XPath by position /html/body/main/article[3]/button poor, breaks with any layout change Lesson 3 of 9"],[11,"change","Changing the page","Elements can be changed in place: their text, classes, attributes and children. textContent sets plain text and is always safe. innerHTML parses HTML, so putting user input into it can run an attacker's code, a bug called cross site scripting (XSS). Changing text, classes, attributes and elements · ▶ Runs in the browser 📄 page.html <!doctype html> <html> <body> <header><navaria-label=\"Main\"><ahref=\"/\">Home</a> <ahref=\"/deals\"class=\"hot\">Deals</a></nav></header> <mainid=\"products\"> <articleclass=\"card\"data-test=\"product\"data-sku=\"PEN-01\"> <h2>Gel pen</h2><spanclass=\"price\">₹20</span> <buttontype=\"button\"class=\"add\">Add to cart</button> </article> <articleclass=\"card sold-out\"data-test=\"product\"data-sku=\"BOOK-07\"> <h2>Notebook</h2><spanclass=\"price\">₹250</span> <buttontype=\"button\"class=\"add\"disabled>Sold out</button> </article> <articleclass=\"card\"data-test=\"product\"data-sku=\"MUG-03\"> <h2>Coffee mug</h2><spanclass=\"price\">₹199</span> <buttontype=\"button\"class=\"add\">Add to cart</button> </article> </main> <pid=\"cart-count\">0</p> </body> </html> 📄 script.js const card = document.querySelector('[data-sku=\"PEN-01\"]'); card.querySelector(\"h2\").textContent = \"Gel pen (blue)\"; // safe: plain text only card.classList.add(\"in-cart\"); card.classList.toggle(\"highlight\"); card.setAttribute(\"aria-label\", \"Gel pen, in cart\"); card.dataset.qty = \"2\"; // becomes data-qty=\"2\" console.log(card.className, card.getAttribute(\"data-qty\")); const badge = document.createElement(\"span\"); badge.className = \"badge\"; badge.textContent = \"New\"; card.prepend(badge); console.log(card.firstElementChild.outerHTML); const userInput = '<img src=x onerror=\"alert(1)\">'; const safe = document.createElement(\"p\"); safe.textContent = userInput; // shown as text, never run console.log(safe.innerHTML); document.querySelector(\".sold-out\").remove(); console.log(document.querySelectorAll(\".card\").length); Output card in-cart highlight 2 <span class=\"badge\">New</span> &lt;img src=x onerror=\"alert(1)\"&gt; 2 Property Reads or writes textContent all text, including hidden elements, fast innerText only the text a user can see, with layout applied (what Selenium's getText() returns) innerHTML the HTML inside the element value what is typed in an input, select or textarea Testers deliberately type <script> and <img onerror> payloads into forms to check that the page shows them as text. If an alert pops up, you found an XSS bug. Lesson 4 of 9"],[11,"events","Events","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightSeleniumCypressUnderstanding bubbling and trusted events explains \"element click intercepted\" errors and why forced clicks hide bugs. Clicks, key presses, input changes and form submits are events. addEventListener(type, handler) runs your function each time one happens. An event travels down from the window to the target (capture), then back up (bubble), so a listener on a parent hears clicks on its children. 🎬 Try it"],[11,"events","Where does a click travel","Click the button inside the boxes, or press Play. Turn on stopPropagation to see the journey cut short. stopPropagation() on div.card listen in the capture phase too window document body div.card 🔘 button A click on the button starts at the very top and travels down, then back up. 0 / 9 Listening for events and event delegation · ▶ Runs in the browser 📄 page.html <!doctype html> <html> <body> <header><navaria-label=\"Main\"><ahref=\"/\">Home</a> <ahref=\"/deals\"class=\"hot\">Deals</a></nav></header> <mainid=\"products\"> <articleclass=\"card\"data-test=\"product\"data-sku=\"PEN-01\"> <h2>Gel pen</h2><spanclass=\"price\">₹20</span> <buttontype=\"button\"class=\"add\">Add to cart</button> </article> <articleclass=\"card sold-out\"data-test=\"product\"data-sku=\"BOOK-07\"> <h2>Notebook</h2><spanclass=\"price\">₹250</span> <buttontype=\"button\"class=\"add\"disabled>Sold out</button> </article> <articleclass=\"card\"data-test=\"product\"data-sku=\"MUG-03\"> <h2>Coffee mug</h2><spanclass=\"price\">₹199</span> <buttontype=\"button\"class=\"add\">Add to cart</button> </article> </main> <pid=\"cart-count\">0</p> </body> </html> 📄 script.js const count = document.querySelector(\"#cart-count\"); // One listener on the parent handles every button, even ones added later document.querySelector(\"#products\").addEventListener(\"click\", event => { const button = event.target.closest(\"button.add\"); if (!button || button.disabled) return; const sku = button.closest(\"[data-sku]\").dataset.sku; count.textContent = Number(count.textContent) + 1; console.log(`added ${sku}, target was <${event.target.tagName.toLowerCase()}>, listener on #${event.currentTarget.id}`); }); document.querySelector('[data-sku=\"PEN-01\"] button').click(); document.querySelector('[data-sku=\"MUG-03\"] button').click(); document.querySelector('[data-sku=\"BOOK-07\"] button').click(); // disabled: click() does nothing console.log(\"cart count:\", count.textContent); // A link whose default action is stoppedconst deals = document.querySelector(\"a.hot\"); deals.addEventListener(\"click\", e => { e.preventDefault(); console.log(\"navigation prevented\"); }); deals.click(); // Custom events let parts of a page talk to each other document.addEventListener(\"cart:changed\", e => console.log(\"custom event:\", e.detail)); document.dispatchEvent(newCustomEvent(\"cart:changed\", { detail: { items: 2 } })); console.log(\"isTrusted for script events:\", newMouseEvent(\"click\").isTrusted); Output added PEN-01, target was <button>, listener on #products added MUG-03, target was <button>, listener on #products cart count: 2 navigation prevented custom event: { items: 2 } isTrusted for script events: false Event delegation uses this: one listener on a list handles every item, including items added later. event.target is what was clicked and event.currentTarget is where the listener sits. preventDefault() stops the browser's own action, such as following a link. Events created by scripts have isTrusted set to false, and element.click() skips the checks a real user goes through: it works on hidden or covered buttons. That is why Playwright and Selenium move a real mouse and check that the element is visible and not covered before clicking, and why { force: true } clicks can hide real bugs. Lesson 5 of 9"],[11,"forms","Forms and validation","🧪 QA essentialForms are the most tested part of any web app, and their validation rules are the source of many test cases. Form fields hold their current value in value (or checked for checkboxes). HTML attributes such as required, type=\"email\" and min give free validation, which JavaScript reads through checkValidity() and each field's validity. FormData collects every named field in one go. Form values and built in validation · ▶ Runs in the browser 📄 page.html <!doctype html> <html> <body> <formid=\"signup\"novalidate> <labelfor=\"email\">Email</label> <inputid=\"email\"name=\"email\"type=\"email\"required> <labelfor=\"age\">Age</label> <inputid=\"age\"name=\"age\"type=\"number\"min=\"18\"max=\"120\"> <label><inputtype=\"checkbox\"name=\"terms\"id=\"terms\"required> I accept the terms</label> <selectid=\"plan\"name=\"plan\"> <optionvalue=\"free\">Free</option> <optionvalue=\"pro\"selected>Pro</option> </select> <button>Create account</button> </form> </body> </html> 📄 script.js const form = document.querySelector(\"#signup\"); const email = form.elements.email; email.value = \"not-an-email\"; form.elements.age.value = \"15\"; console.log(\"valid form:\", form.checkValidity()); for (const field of form.elements) { if (!field.name || field.validity.valid) continue; const problem = [\"valueMissing\", \"typeMismatch\", \"rangeUnderflow\", \"rangeOverflow\"].find(k => field.validity[k]); console.log(` ${field.name}: ${problem}`); } email.value = \"asha@shop.example\"; form.elements.age.value = \"29\"; form.elements.terms.checked = true; form.elements.plan.value = \"free\"; console.log(\"valid form:\", form.checkValidity()); form.addEventListener(\"submit\", e => { e.preventDefault(); console.log(\"submitted:\", Object.fromEntries(newFormData(form))); }); form.requestSubmit(); Output valid form: false email: typeMismatch age: rangeUnderflow terms: valueMissing valid form: true submitted: { email: 'asha@shop.example', age: '29', terms: 'on', plan: 'free' } Form testing checklist: required fields, wrong formats, boundary values (17, 18, 120, 121 for an age between 18 and 120), very long text, leading and trailing spaces, pasted text, the Enter key, double submit, and the error message text. Always check the server rejects bad data too, because client side checks are easy to skip. Lesson 6 of 9"],[11,"advanced","Shadow DOM, iframes and observers","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightSeleniumCypressElements inside shadow roots and iframes are the most common reason a correct looking locator finds nothing. Web components hide their inner elements inside a shadow root. A normal querySelector from the document cannot see inside it. An iframe holds a whole separate document, often for payment forms, maps or embedded editors. Shadow DOM and iframes · ▶ Runs in the browser 📄 page.html <!doctype html> <html><body> <user-cardname=\"Asha\"></user-card> <iframeid=\"pay\"></iframe> </body></html> 📄 script.js classUserCardextendsHTMLElement { connectedCallback() { const root = this.attachShadow({ mode: \"open\" }); root.innerHTML = `<p class=\"name\">${this.getAttribute(\"name\")}</p><button>Follow</button>`; } } customElements.define(\"user-card\", UserCard); console.log(\"normal query finds:\", document.querySelector(\"user-card .name\")); const host = document.querySelector(\"user-card\"); console.log(\"through shadowRoot:\", host.shadowRoot.querySelector(\".name\").textContent); // An iframe has its own document. You must step into it first.const frame = document.querySelector(\"#pay\"); frame.contentDocument.body.innerHTML = \"<button id='pay-now'>Pay ₹499</button>\"; // the provider's page console.log(\"button in main page:\", document.querySelector(\"#pay-now\")); console.log(\"button in iframe:\", frame.contentDocument.querySelector(\"#pay-now\").textContent); Output normal query finds: null through shadowRoot: Asha button in main page: null button in iframe: Pay ₹499 Tool Shadow DOM Iframes Playwright pierced automatically by most locators page.frameLocator(\"#pay\").getByRole(...) Selenium element.getShadowRoot() driver.switchTo().frame(...), then back with defaultContent() Cypress .shadow() or includeShadowDom needs a helper or plugin, same origin only A MutationObserver calls you back whenever the DOM changes. It is how \"wait until the element appears\" is built without polling. Waiting for an element with MutationObserver · ▶ Runs in the browser 📄 page.html <!doctype html> <html><body><divid=\"results\"></div></body></html> 📄 script.js // Resolve when an element matching the selector exists, or reject after a timeoutfunction waitForElement(selector, timeout = 1000) { returnnewPromise((resolve, reject) => { const found = document.querySelector(selector); if (found) return resolve(found); const observer = newMutationObserver(() => { const el = document.querySelector(selector); if (el) { observer.disconnect(); clearTimeout(timer); resolve(el); } }); observer.observe(document.body, { childList: true, subtree: true }); const timer = setTimeout(() => { observer.disconnect(); reject(newError(`${selector} not found in ${timeout} ms`)); }, timeout); }); } // The \"app\" renders results after a fake network delay setTimeout(() => { document.querySelector(\"#results\").innerHTML = '<ul class=\"list\"><li>Gel pen</li><li>Fountain pen</li></ul>'; }, 200); const list = await waitForElement(\".list\"); console.log(\"appeared with\", list.children.length, \"items\"); await waitForElement(\".no-such-thing\", 300).catch(e => console.log(e.message)); Output appeared with 2 items .no-such-thing not found in 300 ms Lesson 7 of 9"],[11,"rendering","How the browser draws the page","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypressSeleniumClicks fail on elements that are still moving or covered, and visual tests compare painted pixels. Changing the DOM does not change the screen straight away. The browser collects your changes and then runs its rendering pipeline, usually 60 times a second, between tasks of the event loop (chapter 2). 🎬 Animated diagram"],[11,"rendering","From HTML text to pixels on the screen","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 6 Parse the HTML into the DOM and the CSS into the CSSOM. Style: work out which CSS rules apply to every element. Layout (also called reflow): work out the size and position of every box. Paint: fill in the pixels for text, colours, borders and images, in layers. Composite: put the layers together on the screen, often on the GPU. Each step costs time, and changes restart the pipeline from different points. Changing width needs layout, paint and composite. Changing color skips layout. Changing transform or opacity can skip both layout and paint, which is why smooth animations use them. Working with the rendering loop · Browser code, shown here // 1. Animate with requestAnimationFrame: the browser calls you right before it paintsconst box = document.querySelector(\".progress\"); let width = 0; function frame() { width += 2; box.style.width = width + \"%\"; if (width < 100) requestAnimationFrame(frame); // once per screen refresh, paused in hidden tabs } requestAnimationFrame(frame); // 2. Layout thrashing: reading a size right after a write forces a layout every timefor (const row of rows) { row.style.height = \"40px\"; // write total += row.offsetHeight; // read, so the browser must lay out the page now } // Better: read everything first, then write everythingconst heights = rows.map(r => r.offsetHeight); rows.forEach(r => (r.style.height = \"40px\")); // 3. IntersectionObserver: load images or more results only when they scroll into viewconst io = newIntersectionObserver(entries => { for (const e of entries) if (e.isIntersecting) { e.target.src = e.target.dataset.src; io.unobserve(e.target); } }); document.querySelectorAll(\"img[data-src]\").forEach(img => io.observe(img)); // 4. A long task blocks all of this. Give the browser a turn in long loops.let done = 0; for (const item of hugeList) { process(item); if (++done % 50 === 0) await scheduler.yield(); // Chrome and Edge, or await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r)) } Tool When your code runs Typical use requestAnimationFrame just before the next paint smooth animations, measuring after a change IntersectionObserver when an element enters or leaves the visible area lazy images, infinite scroll, \"seen\" analytics ResizeObserver when an element changes size charts that redraw to fit MutationObserver after DOM changes, as a microtask waiting for elements, as shown above requestIdleCallback when the browser has nothing else to do sending analytics, warming caches Many \"element not clickable\" and \"element is not stable\" errors come from this pipeline: the element exists in the DOM but is still moving, hidden behind another layer or not laid out yet. Playwright waits for an element to be visible, stable and able to receive events before clicking. Visual regression tests compare the result of paint and composite, so they need fonts loaded and animations turned off. Lesson 8 of 9"],[11,"storage","Storage and cookies","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypressSeleniumSeeding storage and cookies skips slow login screens and sets up feature flags and consent banners. Browsers keep data for a site in three main places. localStorage keeps strings until they are deleted. sessionStorage keeps them until the tab closes. Cookies are small strings that are also sent to the server with every request, which is why logins usually live in them. localStorage and sessionStorage · ▶ Runs in the browser 📄 page.html <!doctype html><html><body></body></html> 📄 script.js localStorage.setItem(\"theme\", \"dark\"); localStorage.setItem(\"cart\", JSON.stringify([{ sku: \"PEN-01\", qty: 2 }])); // only strings are stored console.log(localStorage.getItem(\"theme\"), localStorage.length); console.log(JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(\"cart\"))[0].qty); console.log(localStorage.getItem(\"missing\")); sessionStorage.setItem(\"step\", \"3\"); // cleared when the tab closes console.log(sessionStorage.getItem(\"step\")); localStorage.removeItem(\"theme\"); localStorage.clear(); console.log(localStorage.length); Output dark 2 2 null 3 0 Reading and writing cookies · ▶ Runs in the browser 📄 page.html <!doctype html><html><body></body></html> 📄 script.js document.cookie = \"consent=yes; path=/; max-age=31536000; SameSite=Lax\"; document.cookie = \"lang=hi; path=/\"; document.cookie = `recent=${encodeURIComponent(\"pen, mug\")}; path=/`; console.log(document.cookie); const cookies = Object.fromEntries(document.cookie.split(\"; \").map(c => { const i = c.indexOf(\"=\"); return [c.slice(0, i), decodeURIComponent(c.slice(i + 1))]; })); console.log(cookies.recent); document.cookie = \"lang=; path=/; max-age=0\"; // delete by expiring it console.log(document.cookie.includes(\"lang\")); Output consent=yes; lang=hi; recent=pen%2C%20mug pen, mug false Cookies marked HttpOnly cannot be read from JavaScript at all, which protects session tokens from XSS. Test tools read them through the browser instead, for example context.cookies() in Playwright. Browser state in Playwright · Runs for real in chapter 22 // Log in once, save cookies and localStorage, reuse them in every test// auth.setup.jsimport { test as setup } from\"@playwright/test\"; setup(\"log in\", async ({ page }) => { await page.goto(\"/login\"); await page.getByLabel(\"Email\").fill(process.env.TEST_USER); await page.getByLabel(\"Password\").fill(process.env.TEST_PASSWORD); await page.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Sign in\" }).click(); await page.context().storageState({ path: \".auth/user.json\" }); }); // in a test: read and change browser state directlyawait page.evaluate(() => localStorage.setItem(\"onboarding-done\", \"true\")); const cookies = await page.context().cookies(); await page.context().addCookies([{ name: \"consent\", value: \"yes\", url: \"https://shop.example\" }]); What happens Tests start already logged in, which saves the login steps in every test and keeps them independent. Lesson 9 of 9"],[11,"scenario","Real world scenario: check an order table","🧪 QA essential The situationA customer says the order history shows wrong amounts. You must check every row: quantity times price must equal the line total, and the lines must add up to the footer total. Check every row of an order table · ▶ Runs in the browser 📄 page.html <!doctype html> <html><body> <tableid=\"orders\"> <thead><tr><th>Order</th><th>Item</th><th>Qty</th><th>Price</th><th>Line total</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>#1042</td><td>Gel pen</td><td>3</td><td>₹20.00</td><td>₹60.00</td></tr> <tr><td>#1043</td><td>Coffee mug</td><td>2</td><td>₹199.00</td><td>₹398.00</td></tr> <tr><td>#1044</td><td>Notebook</td><td>1</td><td>₹250.00</td><td>₹250.00</td></tr> <tr><td>#1045</td><td>Desk lamp</td><td>2</td><td>₹1,299.00</td><td>₹2,589.00</td></tr> </tbody> <tfoot><tr><tdcolspan=\"4\">Total</td><td>₹3,297.00</td></tr></tfoot> </table> </body></html> 📄 script.js const money = text => Number(text.replace(/[^\\d.]/g, \"\")); const headers = [...document.querySelectorAll(\"#orders thead th\")].map(th => th.textContent); const rows = [...document.querySelectorAll(\"#orders tbody tr\")].map(tr => Object.fromEntries([...tr.cells].map((td, i) => [headers[i], td.textContent]))); const problems = []; for (const r of rows) { const expected = Number(r.Qty) * money(r.Price); if (expected !== money(r[\"Line total\"])) { problems.push(`${r.Order}: ${r.Qty} x ${r.Price} should be ${expected}, page shows ${r[\"Line total\"]}`); } } const sum = rows.reduce((s, r) => s + money(r[\"Line total\"]), 0); const shownTotal = money(document.querySelector(\"#orders tfoot td:last-child\").textContent); if (sum !== shownTotal) problems.push(`total: rows add up to ${sum}, footer shows ${shownTotal}`); console.log(rows.length, \"rows checked\"); console.log(problems.length ? problems : \"all good\"); Output 4 rows checked [ '#1045: 2 x ₹1,299.00 should be 2598, page shows ₹2,589.00' ] Reading the header cells first turns each row into an object keyed by column name, so the check keeps working if a column is added or moved. The footer does match the rows as shown, so a check of the total alone would have missed this bug. Only the line by line check catches it. 🧮"],[11,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 How many elements does document.querySelectorAll(\".card\") find on the shop page? Count the articles. Answer: 3 There are three article elements with class card. C2 How many of them match \".card:not(.sold-out)\"? One card is sold out. Answer: 2 3 cards minus 1 sold out. C1 A click on the button in window > document > body > div.card > button with listeners in both phases on every level. How many listener calls happen? Down, target, up. Answer: 9 Capture: window, document, body, div.card is 4. Target: 1. Bubble: div.card, body, document, window is 4. 4 + 1 + 4 = 9 C2 Same click, but div.card calls stopPropagation in its bubble phase listener. How many listener calls happen now? Body, document and window miss the bubble. Answer: 6 Capture: 4 calls, target: 1 call. Bubble: only div.card runs, 1 call. 4 + 1 + 1 = 6 C1 Row #1045 shows 2 × ₹1,299.00. What should the line total be? Multiply. Answer: 2598 2 × 1299 = 2598 C2 The rows as shown add up to what amount? Add the line totals. Answer: 3297 60 + 398 + 250 + 2589 = 3297 C1 If row #1045 were fixed, what would the correct order total be? Replace 2589 with 2598. Answer: 3306 60 + 398 + 250 + 2598 = 3306 C2 An age field accepts 18 to 120. With boundary value analysis you test one below, on and one above each edge. How many values is that? Three per edge. Answer: 6 Lower edge: 17, 18, 19. Upper edge: 119, 120, 121. 🎯"],[11,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/14correct Q1 What does document.querySelector(\".card\") return when nothing matches? One element or nothing. querySelector returns null, querySelectorAll returns an empty NodeList. Q2 Which selector matches <button data-test=\"buy\">? Attribute selectors use square brackets. The attribute and its value go inside brackets. Q3 Which property is safe for showing user input as text? No HTML parsing. textContent never turns the text into elements, so scripts cannot sneak in. Q1 Which CSS change is cheapest to animate? It can skip layout and paint. transform and opacity can be handled in the composite step, often on the GPU. Q2 A click on a button inside a list fires a listener on the list. Which property points to the button? Where the click started. currentTarget is the list, where the listener sits. target is the clicked element. Q3 Which locator is most stable across a redesign? What does the user see? Role and accessible name stay the same when markup and styling change. Q4 An age field allows 18 to 120. Which values are classic boundary tests? Just inside and just outside each edge. Boundary value analysis tests both sides of every limit. Q1 Why is reading offsetHeight right after setting a style inside a loop slow? Layout thrashing. Batch the reads first and the writes second, so layout runs once. Q2 Why can document.querySelector not find a button inside a web component? Encapsulation. Shadow DOM hides its inner tree from document level queries. Q3 Why does Selenium need driver.switchTo().frame() for a payment form? A separate document. Commands run in one document at a time, so the driver must switch into the frame. Q4 What is true of a cookie marked HttpOnly? Protection against XSS. The browser sends it with requests but hides it from page scripts. Q1 Why is a { force: true } click risky in a test? Actionability checks. Forcing skips the visibility and overlay checks that represent a real user. Q2 What does this print? localStorage.setItem(\"n\", 5); console.log(typeof localStorage.getItem(\"n\")); Storage only keeps text. Values are converted to strings, so use JSON for anything else. Q3 Which XPath finds the card article that contains the heading \"Coffee mug\"? Find the heading, then go up. The ancestor axis walks up the tree, which CSS cannot do."],[11,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[11,"selector","Build a CSS selector","Write toSelector({ tag, id, classes, attrs }) that builds a CSS selector. Every key is optional. attrs is an object turned into [name=\"value\"] parts. Order: tag, id, classes, attributes. Start with the tag and keep adding with +=. function toSelector({ tag = \"\", id, classes = [], attrs = {} }) { let s = tag; if (id) s += \"#\" + id; for (const c of classes) s += \".\" + c; for (const [k, v] ofObject.entries(attrs)) s += `[${k}=\"${v}\"]`; return s; } Selector builders are common in page objects that take a product id and need its card. 🌿 Medium"],[11,"cookies","Parse document.cookie","Write parseCookies(text) that turns \"a=1; recent=pen%2C%20mug\" into { a: \"1\", recent: \"pen, mug\" }. Decode values with decodeURIComponent. An empty string gives an empty object. Split on \"; \", then cut each part at the first =. function parseCookies(text) { const out = {}; if (!text) return out; for (const part of text.split(\"; \")) { const i = part.indexOf(\"=\"); out[part.slice(0, i)] = decodeURIComponent(part.slice(i + 1)); } return out; } Tokens often end in =, so splitting at every = would break them. 🔥 Hard"],[11,"path","The path of an event","Write eventPath(parents, target). parents maps each node name to its parent name, and the top node has no entry. Return the list of \"capture:x\", \"target:x\" and \"bubble:x\" steps in the order a click on target visits them. Collect the ancestors from the target upwards. Capture uses them reversed, bubble uses them as they are. function eventPath(parents, target) { const up = []; for (let n = parents[target]; n; n = parents[n]) up.push(n); return [ ...up.toReversed().map(n => \"capture:\" + n), \"target:\" + target, ...up.map(n => \"bubble:\" + n), ]; } This is exactly the order the bubble animation shows, and the order your listeners run in. 🧠 Expert"],[11,"sorted","Is the price list sorted?","A test reads price texts from a product grid after choosing \"Price: low to high\". Write firstUnsorted(prices) that returns the index of the first price that is lower than the one before it, or -1 if the list is sorted. Prices look like \"₹1,299.00\". Turn each text into a number first by removing everything except digits and the dot. function firstUnsorted(prices) { const nums = prices.map(p => Number(p.replace(/[^\\d.]/g, \"\"))); for (let i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) { if (nums[i] < nums[i - 1]) return i; } return -1; } Compared as text, \"₹1,000\" sorts before \"₹250\", which is the classic bug in sort tests."],[11,"sorted","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. The DOM treeWhen a browser loads HTML, it builds the DOM (Document Object Model): a tree of objects, one for each element, piece of text and comment. JavaScript reads and changes the page only through this tree, and so does every UI automation tool. Selectors and locatorsA CSS selector describes elements by tag, id, class, attribute and position. The same selector works in document.querySelector, in your stylesheet and in every test tool. Changing the pageElements can be changed in place: their text, classes, attributes and children. textContent sets plain text and is always safe. innerHTML parses HTML, so putting user input into it can run an attacker's code, a bug called cross site scripting (XSS). EventsClicks, key presses, input changes and form submits are events. addEventListener(type, handler) runs your function each time one happens. Forms and validationForm fields hold their current value in value (or checked for checkboxes). HTML attributes such as required, type=\"email\" and min give free validation, which JavaScript reads through checkValidity() and each field's validity. Shadow DOM, iframes and observersWeb components hide their inner elements inside a shadow root. A normal querySelector from the document cannot see inside it. An iframe holds a whole separate document, often for payment forms, maps or embedded editors. How the browser draws the pageChanging the DOM does not change the screen straight away. The browser collects your changes and then runs its rendering pipeline, usually 60 times a second, between tasks of the event loop (chapter 2). Storage and cookiesBrowsers keep data for a site in three main places. localStorage keeps strings until they are deleted. sessionStorage keeps them until the tab closes. Real world scenario: check an order tableReading the header cells first turns each row into an object keyed by column name, so the check keeps working if a column is added or moved. The footer does match the rows as shown, so a check of the total alone would have missed this bug."],[11,"comic","The page is a tree you can query","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Find with stable locators, act like a user, and check what the user would see."],[11,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🛒Add to cart A product grid with 200 items uses one delegated click listener instead of 200, and its buttons carry data-test attributes for the test suite. 💳A payment iframe The card form comes from the payment provider inside an iframe, so the checkout test switches into the frame to fill it. 🧾Report checks A nightly script opens the admin dashboard and checks that every table row adds up, catching a rounding bug before customers do. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"The DOM and the Browser\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 11Welcome This chapter covers the DOM, selectors and XPath, changing the page, events, forms, shadow DOM, iframes, storage and cookies. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[12,"","HTTP, fetch and Node.js Servers","A raw HTTP exchange, methods and status codes, fetch with GET and POST and why it does not reject on 404, building URLs with the URL class, token login and access checks, an Express REST API with proper status codes, CORS checked the way a browser does it, Server-Sent Events and WebSockets, reading CSV test data and writing reports, running other programs, and a mock API with reset, fault injection and delays. · HTTP · status code · fetch · URL · URLSearchParams · Bearer token · 401 · 403 · Express · REST · middleware · CORS · preflight · SSE · WebSocket · fs · path · child_process · mock server · fault injection 🟨 JavaScript›⏳ Async and the platform›Chapter 13 of 31 📡 Step 4 · Topic 3 of 3 · Intermediate"],[12,"top","","What a request and a response look like, fetch and URLs, logging in with tokens and the difference between 401 and 403, writing a REST API with Express, CORS and preflight requests, real time updates with Server-Sent Events and WebSockets, the Node.js core modules, and a mock API that UI tests can control. ⏱️ About 24 min read📚 8 parts🎬 2 animations▶ 10 runnable examples🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 14 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 8 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers API testing is HTTP. Methods, status codes, headers, auth and a server you control are the foundations for chapter 21. 1How HTTP works 2fetch and URLs 3Authentication: 401 against 403 4Writing a server with Express 5CORS and the same origin policy 6Real time: Server-Sent Events and WebSockets 7Node.js core modules 8Real world scenario: a mock API for UI tests Lesson 1 of 8"],[12,"http","How HTTP works","🧪 QA essentialEvery API test checks a method, a status code, headers and a body. Browsers, apps and test tools talk to servers with HTTP. The client sends a request: a method, a path, headers and sometimes a body. The server sends back a response: a status code, headers and usually a body. Both are plain text you can read. Before that text can travel, several things happen in a few milliseconds. Press Play to follow one request for https://shop.example/api/orders from the browser to the server and back. 🎬 Animated diagram"],[12,"http","The life of one HTTPS request","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 7 What a request and a response really look like · ▶ Runs import http from\"node:http\"; import net from\"node:net\"; // A tiny server, so we can see a real request and responseconst server = http.createServer((req, res) => { const body = JSON.stringify({ id: 7, name: \"Gel pen\" }); res.writeHead(201, { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\", \"Content-Length\": body.length, \"X-Request-Id\": \"abc-123\" }); res.end(body); }); awaitnewPromise(r => server.listen(0, \"127.0.0.1\", r)); const { port } = server.address(); // Send a request by hand over a raw socket and print the exact text that comes backconst socket = net.connect(port, \"127.0.0.1\"); socket.write( \"POST /api/products HTTP/1.1\\r\\n\" + `Host: 127.0.0.1:${port}\\r\\n` + \"Content-Type: application/json\\r\\n\" + \"Content-Length: 18\\r\\n\" + \"Connection: close\\r\\n\\r\\n\" + '{\"name\":\"Gel pen\"}' ); let text = \"\"; forawait (const chunk of socket) text += chunk; console.log(text.replaceAll(\"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\")); server.close(); Output HTTP/1.1 201 Created Content-Type: application/json Content-Length: 25 X-Request-Id: abc-123 Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 09:00:00 GMT Connection: close {\"id\":7,\"name\":\"Gel pen\"} Method Meaning Safe to repeat GET read yes POST create, or run an action no PUT replace completely yes PATCH change some fields usually not DELETE remove yes Status Group Ones you see every day 1xx information 101 Switching Protocols (WebSockets) 2xx success 200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content 3xx redirect 301 Moved Permanently, 302 Found, 304 Not Modified 4xx the client got it wrong 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized, 403 Forbidden, 404 Not Found, 409 Conflict, 422 Unprocessable, 429 Too Many Requests 5xx the server failed 500 Internal Server Error, 502 Bad Gateway, 503 Service Unavailable, 504 Gateway Timeout Lesson 2 of 8"],[12,"fetch","fetch and URLs","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightJestCypressAPI tests send requests and read responses with fetch or a wrapper around it, such as Playwright request or Cypress cy.request. fetch(url, options) sends a request and returns a promise for the response. It is built into browsers and Node.js. Read the body once with res.json(), res.text() or res.blob(). fetch: GET, POST and errors · ▶ Runs import http from\"node:http\"; // A small fake API to talk toconst products = [{ id: 1, name: \"Gel pen\", price: 20 }]; const server = http.createServer(async (req, res) => { const send = (status, body) => { res.writeHead(status, { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" }); res.end(JSON.stringify(body)); }; if (req.method === \"GET\" && req.url === \"/api/products\") return send(200, products); if (req.method === \"POST\" && req.url === \"/api/products\") { let raw = \"\"; forawait (const chunk of req) raw += chunk; const item = { id: products.length + 1, ...JSON.parse(raw) }; products.push(item); return send(201, item); } send(404, { error: \"Not found\" }); }); awaitnewPromise(r => server.listen(0, r)); const base = `http://localhost:${server.address().port}`; // GET and read JSONconst res = await fetch(`${base}/api/products`); console.log(res.status, res.ok, res.headers.get(\"content-type\")); console.log(await res.json()); // POST with a JSON bodyconst created = await fetch(`${base}/api/products`, { method: \"POST\", headers: { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" }, body: JSON.stringify({ name: \"Coffee mug\", price: 199 }), }); console.log(created.status, await created.json()); // fetch does NOT reject on 404 or 500. Only network failures reject.const missing = await fetch(`${base}/api/nothing`); console.log(missing.status, missing.ok, await missing.json()); server.close(); // Only a missing response rejects, for example when nothing listens on the portconst gone = http.createServer(); awaitnewPromise(r => gone.listen(0, r)); const deadUrl = `http://localhost:${gone.address().port}/`; awaitnewPromise(r => gone.close(r)); try { await fetch(deadUrl); } catch (e) { console.log(\"rejected:\", e.name, e.message, e.cause?.code); } Output 200 true application/json [ { id: 1, name: 'Gel pen', price: 20 } ] 201 { id: 2, name: 'Coffee mug', price: 199 } 404 false { error: 'Not found' } rejected: TypeError fetch failed ECONNREFUSED fetch only rejects when there is no response at all: a refused connection, a DNS error or an abort. A 404 or 500 still fulfils, so always check res.ok or res.status. Build URLs with the URL class instead of joining strings. It encodes spaces and symbols correctly, which hand built URLs often get wrong. Building and reading URLs · ▶ Runs const url = newURL(\"https://shop.example:8443/api/products?q=gel+pen&page=2#reviews\"); console.log(url.protocol, url.hostname, url.port, url.pathname); console.log(url.searchParams.get(\"q\"), url.searchParams.get(\"page\"), url.hash); const search = newURL(\"/api/search\", \"https://shop.example\"); search.searchParams.set(\"q\", \"pens & pencils\"); search.searchParams.append(\"tag\", \"office\"); search.searchParams.append(\"tag\", \"school\"); console.log(search.href); console.log(search.searchParams.getAll(\"tag\")); console.log(encodeURIComponent(\"a/b c&d\"), decodeURIComponent(\"a%2Fb%20c%26d\")); console.log(URL.canParse(\"not a url\"), URL.parse(\"https://x.example/a\")?.pathname); Output https: shop.example 8443 /api/products gel pen 2 #reviews https://shop.example/api/search?q=pens+%26+pencils&tag=office&tag=school [ 'office', 'school' ] a%2Fb%20c%26d a/b c&d false /a Lesson 3 of 8"],[12,"auth","Authentication: 401 against 403","🧪 QA essentialAuthorisation bugs are among the most serious and the easiest to test at the API level. Most APIs need you to prove who you are. A common pattern: log in once, receive a token, then send it on every request in the Authorization: Bearer ... header. Browsers usually keep the session in a cookie instead. Logging in and sending a Bearer token · ▶ Runs import http from\"node:http\"; constUSERS = { \"asha@shop.example\": { password: \"s3cret\", role: \"customer\" }, \"admin@shop.example\": { password: \"adm1n\", role: \"admin\" } }; const tokens = newMap(); const server = http.createServer(async (req, res) => { const send = (status, body) => { res.writeHead(status, { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" }); res.end(JSON.stringify(body)); }; if (req.url === \"/login\" && req.method === \"POST\") { let raw = \"\"; forawait (const c of req) raw += c; const { email, password } = JSON.parse(raw); if (USERS[email]?.password !== password) return send(401, { error: \"Invalid credentials\" }); const token = \"tok_\" + tokens.size; tokens.set(token, { email, role: USERS[email].role }); return send(200, { token }); } const user = tokens.get(req.headers.authorization?.replace(/^Bearer /, \"\")); if (!user) return send(401, { error: \"Log in first\" }); // who are you?if (req.url === \"/admin/reports\" && user.role !== \"admin\") return send(403, { error: \"Admins only\" }); // not allowed send(200, { ok: true, user: user.email }); }); awaitnewPromise(r => server.listen(0, r)); const base = `http://localhost:${server.address().port}`; asyncfunction login(email, password) { const res = await fetch(`${base}/login`, { method: \"POST\", body: JSON.stringify({ email, password }) }); return (await res.json()).token; } constget = (path, token) => fetch(base + path, { headers: token ? { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } : {} }).then(r => r.status); const customer = await login(\"asha@shop.example\", \"s3cret\"); const admin = await login(\"admin@shop.example\", \"adm1n\"); console.log(\"wrong password:\", await fetch(`${base}/login`, { method: \"POST\", body: '{\"email\":\"asha@shop.example\",\"password\":\"x\"}' }).then(r => r.status)); console.log(\"no token: \", awaitget(\"/orders\")); console.log(\"customer: \", awaitget(\"/orders\", customer), awaitget(\"/admin/reports\", customer)); console.log(\"admin: \", awaitget(\"/orders\", admin), awaitget(\"/admin/reports\", admin)); server.close(); Output wrong password: 401 no token: 401 customer: 200 403 admin: 200 200 401 UnauthorizedThe server does not know who you are: no token, a wrong one or an expired one. Log in again. 403 ForbiddenThe server knows exactly who you are, and you are not allowed to do this. Logging in again will not help. Every protected endpoint deserves at least four tests: no token (401), a broken or expired token (401), a valid token without permission (403) and a valid token with permission (2xx). Checking that a customer cannot read another customer's order by changing an id is one of the most valuable security tests you can write. Lesson 4 of 8"],[12,"server","Writing a server with Express","🧪 QA essentialsupertestAPI test suites start a server like this one, and chapter 21 tests this exact app with supertest. On the server side, Node.js answers requests. Express is the most used library for it: you declare routes (a method plus a path) and a handler that receives the request and builds the response. Middleware runs before the routes, for example to parse JSON or check a token. A REST API with Express · ▶ Runs import express from\"express\"; const app = express(); app.use(express.json()); // parse JSON bodiesconst db = newMap([[1, { id: 1, name: \"Gel pen\", price: 20 }]]); let nextId = 2; app.get(\"/api/products\", (req, res) => { const max = Number(req.query.maxPrice ?? Infinity); res.json([...db.values()].filter(p => p.price <= max)); }); app.get(\"/api/products/:id\", (req, res) => { const p = db.get(Number(req.params.id)); p ? res.json(p) : res.status(404).json({ error: \"Product not found\" }); }); app.post(\"/api/products\", (req, res) => { const { name, price } = req.body ?? {}; if (!name || !(price > 0)) return res.status(400).json({ error: \"name and a positive price are required\" }); const p = { id: nextId++, name, price }; db.set(p.id, p); res.status(201).location(`/api/products/${p.id}`).json(p); }); app.delete(\"/api/products/:id\", (req, res) => { db.delete(Number(req.params.id)) ? res.status(204).end() : res.status(404).json({ error: \"Product not found\" }); }); const server = app.listen(0); awaitnewPromise(r => server.once(\"listening\", r)); const base = `http://localhost:${server.address().port}/api/products`; const call = async (method, path = \"\", body) => { const res = await fetch(base + path, { method, headers: { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" }, body: body && JSON.stringify(body) }); const text = await res.text(); console.log(method.padEnd(6), (\"/api/products\" + path).padEnd(28), res.status, text); }; await call(\"GET\"); await call(\"POST\", \"\", { name: \"Coffee mug\", price: 199 }); await call(\"POST\", \"\", { name: \"Free thing\", price: 0 }); await call(\"GET\", \"?maxPrice=100\"); await call(\"GET\", \"/2\"); await call(\"DELETE\", \"/2\"); await call(\"GET\", \"/2\"); server.close(); Output GET /api/products 200 [{\"id\":1,\"name\":\"Gel pen\",\"price\":20}] POST /api/products 201 {\"id\":2,\"name\":\"Coffee mug\",\"price\":199} POST /api/products 400 {\"error\":\"name and a positive price are required\"} GET /api/products?maxPrice=100 200 [{\"id\":1,\"name\":\"Gel pen\",\"price\":20}] GET /api/products/2 200 {\"id\":2,\"name\":\"Coffee mug\",\"price\":199} DELETE /api/products/2 204 GET /api/products/2 404 {\"error\":\"Product not found\"} Return the right status code and a helpful error body. Clients and tests rely on 201 with a Location header for created items, 204 with no body for deletes, 400 for bad input and 404 for missing items. Knowing how routes and status codes are written makes API bugs easier to explain: \"POST with price 0 returns 400, as it should, but PUT with price 0 returns 200 and saves it\" points straight at the missing check. Lesson 5 of 8"],[12,"cors","CORS and the same origin policy","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightAPI testing\"Works in Postman, fails in the browser\" is a classic bug report, and CORS settings need their own test cases. A browser keeps websites apart. A page from https://shop.example may freely send requests to other sites, but by default its JavaScript may not read the answers from a different origin (scheme, host and port together). This is the same origin policy, and it stops a random site you visit from reading your bank's API with your cookies. CORS (Cross Origin Resource Sharing) is how a server says \"this other origin may read my responses\". For anything beyond a simple GET or form POST, such as a DELETE or a request with an Authorization header, the browser first sends a preflightOPTIONS request and only sends the real request if the answer allows it. 🎬 Animated diagram"],[12,"cors","A cross origin DELETE with a preflight","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 5 A CORS preflight, checked the way a browser checks it · ▶ Runs import http from\"node:http\"; // An API that allows only the shop's own front end to call it from a browserconstALLOWED = \"https://shop.example\"; const server = http.createServer((req, res) => { const origin = req.headers.origin; if (origin === ALLOWED) { res.setHeader(\"Access-Control-Allow-Origin\", origin); res.setHeader(\"Vary\", \"Origin\"); } if (req.method === \"OPTIONS\") { // the preflightif (origin === ALLOWED) { res.setHeader(\"Access-Control-Allow-Methods\", \"GET, POST, DELETE\"); res.setHeader(\"Access-Control-Allow-Headers\", \"Content-Type, Authorization\"); res.setHeader(\"Access-Control-Max-Age\", \"600\"); } res.writeHead(204).end(); return; } res.writeHead(200, { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" }).end('{\"orders\":3}'); }).listen(0); const api = `http://localhost:${server.address().port}/orders`; // What a browser does before a DELETE with a token from another originasyncfunction browserCall(origin, method) { const pre = await fetch(api, { method: \"OPTIONS\", headers: { Origin: origin, \"Access-Control-Request-Method\": method, \"Access-Control-Request-Headers\": \"authorization\" } }); const allowedOrigin = pre.headers.get(\"access-control-allow-origin\"); const allowedMethods = pre.headers.get(\"access-control-allow-methods\") ?? \"\"; const ok = allowedOrigin === origin && allowedMethods.includes(method); console.log(`${origin.padEnd(26)} ${method.padEnd(6)} preflight ${pre.status}, allow-origin: ${allowedOrigin ?? \"(none)\"} -> ${ok ? \"browser sends the real request\" : \"browser blocks it: CORS error in the console\"}`); } await browserCall(\"https://shop.example\", \"DELETE\"); await browserCall(\"https://evil.example\", \"DELETE\"); await browserCall(\"https://shop.example\", \"PUT\"); // Outside a browser there is no CORS check at all: a script or a test tool gets the dataconst direct = await fetch(api, { headers: { Origin: \"https://evil.example\" } }); console.log(\"Node.js fetch from any origin:\", direct.status, await direct.text()); server.close(); Output https://shop.example DELETE preflight 204, allow-origin: https://shop.example -> browser sends the real request https://evil.example DELETE preflight 204, allow-origin: (none) -> browser blocks it: CORS error in the console https://shop.example PUT preflight 204, allow-origin: https://shop.example -> browser blocks it: CORS error in the console Node.js fetch from any origin: 200 {\"orders\":3} The last line is the important lesson. CORS is enforced by the browser, not the server. Postman, curl, Node.js and API test tools ignore it completely. So a CORS setting never protects data: authentication and authorisation do that. CORS only decides which websites' JavaScript may read the responses inside a user's browser. Console message What it usually means No 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header is present The server did not list your origin, or the request failed before CORS headers were added (often a 500 or a proxy error) Response to preflight request does not pass access control check The OPTIONS answer is missing the method or a header your request uses The value of the 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header must not be the wildcard '*' when the credentials mode is 'include' Requests that send cookies need the exact origin, never * Test CORS in a real browser or by sending the same headers yourself: an allowed origin, an unknown origin, every method the UI uses, and requests with credentials. A \"works in Postman, fails in the browser\" bug report is almost always CORS, and the browser console message tells you which header is missing. Lesson 6 of 8"],[12,"realtime","Real time: Server-Sent Events and WebSockets","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightLive updates are hard to test without controlling the server, and Playwright can watch and fake WebSocket traffic. Normal HTTP is \"ask, then get one answer\". Live order tracking, chat, stock prices and multiplayer games need the server to push updates the moment they happen. Asking again every few seconds (polling) works but wastes requests and is always a little late. Two standard tools do better. Server-Sent Events (SSE)One long HTTP response that never ends. The server writes data: lines whenever something happens. One direction only, from server to page. Browsers reconnect automatically with EventSource. Good for notifications, progress bars and live order status. WebSocketThe connection starts as HTTP, switches protocol (status 101) and becomes a two way channel. Either side can send a message at any time. Good for chat, collaborative editing and games. Server-Sent Events and a WebSocket, both for real · ▶ Runs import http from\"node:http\"; import { WebSocketServer } from\"ws\"; // the ws library for the server side// 1. Server-Sent Events: one long HTTP response, the server pushes \"data:\" linesconst sse = http.createServer((req, res) => { res.writeHead(200, { \"Content-Type\": \"text/event-stream\", \"Cache-Control\": \"no-cache\" }); let n = 0; const timer = setInterval(() => { n++; res.write(`event: status\\ndata: {\"order\":42,\"step\":${n}}\\n\\n`); if (n === 3) { clearInterval(timer); res.end(); } }, 20); }).listen(0); const res = await fetch(`http://localhost:${sse.address().port}/orders/42/events`); console.log(\"SSE content type:\", res.headers.get(\"content-type\")); let buffer = \"\"; forawait (const chunk of res.body.pipeThrough(newTextDecoderStream())) { buffer += chunk; let end; while ((end = buffer.indexOf(\"\\n\\n\")) !== -1) { const event = buffer.slice(0, end); buffer = buffer.slice(end + 2); const data = JSON.parse(event.split(\"\\n\").find(l => l.startsWith(\"data: \")).slice(6)); console.log(\"SSE server pushed step\", data.step); } } sse.close(); // 2. WebSocket: one connection, messages both ways at any timeconst wss = newWebSocketServer({ port: 0 }); wss.on(\"connection\", socket => { socket.on(\"message\", msg => socket.send(`echo: ${msg}`)); socket.send(\"welcome\"); }); const ws = newWebSocket(`ws://localhost:${wss.address().port}`); // the WebSocket client is built into Node.jsconst got = []; awaitnewPromise(resolve => { ws.onmessage = e => { got.push(e.data); if (got.length === 1) ws.send(\"ping\"); else { ws.close(); resolve(); } }; }); console.log(\"WS \", got); wss.close(); Output SSE content type: text/event-stream SSE server pushed step 1 SSE server pushed step 2 SSE server pushed step 3 WS [ 'welcome', 'echo: ping' ] In the browser, SSE is new EventSource(url) with an onmessage handler, and a WebSocket is new WebSocket(\"wss://...\") with send and onmessage. Node.js 22 and later has the same WebSocket client built in, which is what the example uses. Testing live updates with Playwright · Playwright spec, shown here // Playwright can watch and fake real time traffic too page.on(\"websocket\", ws => { ws.on(\"framereceived\", f => console.log(\"server sent\", f.payload)); }); // Replace the real server with a scripted one (Playwright 1.48 and later)await page.routeWebSocket(\"wss://shop.example/live\", ws => { ws.onMessage(msg => { if (msg === \"subscribe:42\") ws.send(JSON.stringify({ order: 42, step: \"shipped\" })); }); }); await expect(page.getByText(\"Shipped\")).toBeVisible(); Live features need tests for the unhappy paths: the connection drops and comes back, messages arrive out of order or twice, the user opens two tabs, and the server sends an update for an order the page is not showing. Faking the server side, as above, makes each case repeatable. Lesson 7 of 8"],[12,"node","Node.js core modules","🧪 QA essentialData driven tests read CSV and JSON files, and custom reports are written with fs and path. Node.js comes with modules for files, paths, processes and more. Test suites use them to read test data, write reports and run other tools. Files, folders and paths in Node.js · ▶ Runs import { readFile, writeFile, mkdir, readdir } from\"node:fs/promises\"; import path from\"node:path\"; import os from\"node:os\"; // Test data in a CSV fileawait writeFile(\"users.csv\", \"email,role,active\\nasha@shop.example,admin,true\\nravi@shop.example,customer,false\\n\"); const [header, ...lines] = (await readFile(\"users.csv\", \"utf8\")).trim().split(\"\\n\"); const keys = header.split(\",\"); const users = lines.map(l => Object.fromEntries(l.split(\",\").map((v, i) => [keys[i], v]))); console.log(users); // Write a JSON report into a folder, creating it if neededconst outDir = path.join(\"reports\", \"2026-10-20\"); await mkdir(outDir, { recursive: true }); const file = path.join(outDir, \"summary.json\"); await writeFile(file, JSON.stringify({ total: users.length, active: users.filter(u => u.active === \"true\").length }, null, 2)); console.log(file, \"->\", await readFile(file, \"utf8\")); console.log(await readdir(\"reports\", { recursive: true })); console.log(path.extname(file), path.basename(file, \".json\"), typeof os.cpus().length); console.log(process.argv.length >= 2, process.platform.length > 0); Output [ { email: 'asha@shop.example', role: 'admin', active: 'true' }, { email: 'ravi@shop.example', role: 'customer', active: 'false' } ] reports/2026-10-20/summary.json -> { \"total\": 2, \"active\": 1 } [ '2026-10-20', '2026-10-20/summary.json' ] .json summary number true true Running other programs · ▶ Runs import { execFile } from\"node:child_process\"; import { promisify } from\"node:util\"; const run = promisify(execFile); // Run another program and read its output, like a CI script wouldconst { stdout } = await run(process.execPath, [\"-e\", \"console.log(JSON.stringify({ node: process.versions.node.split('.')[0] }))\"]); console.log(\"child said:\", JSON.parse(stdout)); try { await run(process.execPath, [\"-e\", \"console.error('2 tests failed'); process.exit(1)\"]); } catch (e) { console.log(\"exit code:\", e.code, \"| stderr:\", e.stderr.trim()); } Output child said: { node: '26' } exit code: 1 | stderr: 2 tests failed Module Used for node:fs/promises read and write files and folders node:path join and split file paths the same way on Windows, macOS and Linux node:child_process run other programs node:crypto random ids (randomUUID) and hashes node:test, node:assert the built in test runner (chapter 20) Lesson 8 of 8"],[12,"scenario","Real world scenario: a mock API for UI tests","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypress The situationThe real backend is shared, slow and its data changes all the time, so UI tests fail for reasons that have nothing to do with the UI. You need an API that each test can reset, fill with data, slow down and make fail on purpose. A controllable mock API for UI tests · ▶ Runs import express from\"express\"; // A mock API that UI tests can start in a second, with data each test controlsexportfunction createMockApi() { const app = express(); app.use(express.json()); let state = { products: [], failNext: null, delayMs: 0 }; app.use(async (req, res, next) => { // slow network, on demandif (state.delayMs) awaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, state.delayMs)); next(); }); app.post(\"/__test/reset\", (req, res) => { state = { products: [], failNext: null, delayMs: 0, ...req.body }; res.sendStatus(204); }); app.post(\"/__test/fail-next\", (req, res) => { state.failNext = req.body.status; res.sendStatus(204); }); app.get(\"/health\", (req, res) => res.json({ status: \"up\" })); app.get(\"/api/products\", (req, res) => { if (state.failNext) { const s = state.failNext; state.failNext = null; return res.status(s).json({ error: \"Injected failure\" }); } res.json(state.products); }); return app; } const server = createMockApi().listen(0); awaitnewPromise(r => server.once(\"listening\", r)); const base = `http://localhost:${server.address().port}`; const post = (p, body) => fetch(base + p, { method: \"POST\", headers: { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" }, body: JSON.stringify(body) }); const show = async label => { const r = await fetch(`${base}/api/products`); console.log(label.padEnd(24), r.status, await r.text()); }; console.log(\"health:\", await (await fetch(`${base}/health`)).json()); await show(\"empty catalogue:\"); await post(\"/__test/reset\", { products: [{ id: 1, name: \"Gel pen\" }] }); await show(\"one product:\"); await post(\"/__test/fail-next\", { status: 503 }); await show(\"server error injected:\"); await show(\"next call recovers:\"); await post(\"/__test/reset\", { delayMs: 300 }); const t = performance.now(); await show(\"slow network:\"); console.log(\"took about\", Math.round((performance.now() - t) / 100) * 100, \"ms\"); server.close(); Output health: { status: 'up' } empty catalogue: 200 [] one product: 200 [{\"id\":1,\"name\":\"Gel pen\"}] server error injected: 503 {\"error\":\"Injected failure\"} next call recovers: 200 [{\"id\":1,\"name\":\"Gel pen\"}] slow network: 200 [] took about 300 ms The /__test routes exist only in the mock. A test resets the state, sets up exactly the products it needs and can inject a 503 to check that the page shows a friendly error and a retry button. A delay shows whether loading spinners appear. Playwright can do the same without a server by intercepting requests with page.route(), and Cypress with cy.intercept(). A mock server is still useful when mobile apps or several services need the same fake. 🧮"],[12,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 A response has status 404. What is the first digit that tells you its group? Look at the first digit. Answer: 4 4xx is a client error. C2 An API allows 100 requests per minute. How many requests per second is that on average? Give one decimal. Divide by 60. Answer: 1.7 100 ÷ 60 ≈ 1.67 Rounded to one decimal: 1.7. C1 A protected endpoint gets 4 auth tests per role, and there are 3 roles. How many tests? Multiply. Answer: 12 4 × 3 = 12 C2 An endpoint takes 120 ms. A test makes 25 calls one after another. How many seconds is that? Total ms, then divide by 1000. Answer: 3 25 × 120 = 3000 ms 3000 ÷ 1000 = 3 s C1 Content-Length for the body {\"name\":\"Gel pen\"} is how many bytes? Count every character. Answer: 18 {\"name\":\"Gel pen\"} has 18 characters, all of them one byte. C2 The products list has 230 items, 25 per page. How many pages? Round up. Answer: 10 230 ÷ 25 = 9.2 The last page holds 5 items, so 10 pages. C1 An API returns 429 with Retry-After: 30. A test retries 3 times, waiting as told. At least how many seconds pass? Three waits. Answer: 90 3 × 30 = 90 C2 Out of 400 API calls in a run, 6 return 5xx. What percentage is that? Part over total, times 100. Answer: 1.5 6 ÷ 400 × 100 = 1.5 🎯"],[12,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/14correct Q1 Which status code means a new item was created? Created. 201 Created, usually with a Location header pointing at the new item. Q2 Which method should only read data? Safe and repeatable. GET must not change anything on the server. Q3 What group is status 503 in? First digit 5. 5xx means the server failed. 503 says it is unavailable right now. Q1 An API call works in Postman but fails in the browser with a message about Access-Control-Allow-Origin. What is the likely cause? Postman ignores CORS. Only browsers enforce CORS, so the same request succeeds from tools outside the browser. Q2 fetch gets a 404 response. What happens? fetch only rejects without a response. Any HTTP response fulfils. Check res.ok or res.status yourself. Q3 A logged in customer calls an admin only endpoint. Which status is right? The server knows who it is. 403 Forbidden: authenticated, but not allowed. Q4 Which builds a correctly encoded query string for q = \"pens & pencils\"? Let the URL class encode. searchParams encodes the & and spaces, which plain joining would break. Q1 Which real time option sends messages in both directions over one connection? SSE is server to client only. A WebSocket upgrades an HTTP connection (status 101) into a two way channel. Q2 Which status should DELETE return when it succeeds with nothing to send back? No body. 204 says it worked and there is no body to read. Q3 What does express.json() middleware do? It runs before the routes. Without it, req.body would be undefined for JSON posts. Q4 Why join paths with path.join instead of \"+\"? Windows uses backslashes. path.join builds correct paths on Windows, macOS and Linux. Q1 Which test is most likely to find an access control bug? Change the id, keep the token. This is the classic insecure direct object reference check. Q2 Which method is not idempotent, so repeating it can create duplicates? Think of a double click on Pay. Two identical POSTs can create two orders. That is why payment APIs use idempotency keys. Q3 In the mock API, why are the /__test routes useful? Control. Tests become independent of shared, changing backend data."],[12,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[12,"status","Group a status code","Write statusGroup(code) that returns \"info\", \"success\", \"redirect\", \"client error\" or \"server error\" for codes from 100 to 599, and \"unknown\" for anything else. Math.floor(code / 100) gives the first digit. function statusGroup(code) { const groups = [\"info\", \"success\", \"redirect\", \"client error\", \"server error\"]; const first = Math.floor(code / 100); return groups[first - 1] ?? \"unknown\"; } Grouping by the first digit is how API dashboards count errors. 🌿 Medium"],[12,"build-url","Build a request URL","Write buildUrl(base, path, query) using the URL class. Skip query values that are null or undefined. Arrays become repeated keys. new URL(path, base), then searchParams.append for each value. function buildUrl(base, path, query = {}) { const url = newURL(path, base); for (const [key, value] ofObject.entries(query)) { if (value == null) continue; for (const v of [value].flat()) url.searchParams.append(key, v); } return url.href; } Note the last test: a relative path is resolved against the base folder, the same rule as links in HTML. 🔥 Hard"],[12,"link","Read a Link header","Paginated APIs often send Link: <url>; rel=\"next\", <url>; rel=\"last\". Write parseLink(header) that returns an object from each rel to its URL. An empty header gives {}. A regular expression with two groups and matchAll. function parseLink(header) { const out = {}; for (const m of header.matchAll(/<([^>]+)>;\\s*rel=\"([^\"]+)\"/g)) { out[m[2]] = m[1]; } return out; } The GitHub API paginates this way, and a test that walks every page follows next until it is missing. 🧠 Expert"],[12,"route","Match a route with parameters","Write matchRoute(pattern, path). Patterns look like \"/users/:id/orders/:orderId\". Return an object of the parameters when the path matches, or null when it does not. Both must have the same number of segments. Split both on / and compare piece by piece. function matchRoute(pattern, path) { const a = pattern.split(\"/\"), b = path.split(\"/\"); if (a.length !== b.length) returnnull; const params = {}; for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) { if (a[i].startsWith(\":\")) params[a[i].slice(1)] = b[i]; elseif (a[i] !== b[i]) returnnull; } return params; } This is what Express does for req.params, and what Playwright and Cypress do when you intercept /api/users/*."],[12,"route","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. How HTTP worksBrowsers, apps and test tools talk to servers with HTTP. The client sends a request: a method, a path, headers and sometimes a body. The server sends back a response: a status code, headers and usually a body. Both are plain text you can read. fetch and URLsfetch(url, options) sends a request and returns a promise for the response. It is built into browsers and Node.js. Read the body once with res.json(), res.text() or res.blob(). Authentication: 401 against 403Most APIs need you to prove who you are. A common pattern: log in once, receive a token, then send it on every request in the Authorization: Bearer ... header. Browsers usually keep the session in a cookie instead. Writing a server with ExpressOn the server side, Node.js answers requests. Express is the most used library for it: you declare routes (a method plus a path) and a handler that receives the request and builds the response. CORS and the same origin policyA browser keeps websites apart. A page from https://shop.example may freely send requests to other sites, but by default its JavaScript may not read the answers from a different origin (scheme, host and port together). Real time: Server-Sent Events and WebSocketsNormal HTTP is \"ask, then get one answer\". Live order tracking, chat, stock prices and multiplayer games need the server to push updates the moment they happen. Asking again every few seconds (polling) works but wastes requests and is always a little late. Node.js core modulesNode.js comes with modules for files, paths, processes and more. Test suites use them to read test data, write reports and run other tools. Real world scenario: a mock API for UI testsThe /__test routes exist only in the mock. A test resets the state, sets up exactly the products it needs and can inject a 503 to check that the page shows a friendly error and a retry button. A delay shows whether loading spinners appear."],[12,"comic","A request goes out, a response comes back","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Check the status, the headers and the body. fetch does not throw on a 404."],[12,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 📱One API, many clients The same REST API serves the website, the Android app and the iPhone app, so one API test suite protects all three. 🔐A broken access check An API test changed the order id in /orders/1042 to another customer's order and got 200. The bug was fixed before release. 🧪A mock payment provider The checkout tests point at a small Express server that pretends to be the bank, including declined cards and timeouts. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"HTTP, fetch and Node.js Servers\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 10Welcome This chapter covers HTTP, fetch, URLs, logging in with tokens, an Express server, the Node core modules and a mock API for tests. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[13,"","How JavaScript really works","The call stack and stack traces, the creation phase with hoisting, the temporal dead zone and lexical scope, the five rules of this and how methods lose it, the Node.js event loop phases with nextTick and microtasks, V8 compilers, hidden classes and inline caches, generational garbage collection, and a page object that crashes inside a retry helper. · execution context · call stack · hoisting · TDZ · scope chain · this · bind · event loop phases · process.nextTick · setImmediate · V8 · hidden class · inline cache · garbage collection · JavaScript internals · interview 🟨 JavaScript›🔬 JavaScript in depth›Chapter 14 of 31 🔬 Step 5 · Topic 1 of 6 · Advanced"],[13,"top","","What the engine does with your code: execution contexts and the call stack, hoisting and the scope chain, the five rules of this, the Node.js event loop phase by phase, V8 hidden classes and inline caches, and generational garbage collection. ⏱️ About 20 min read📚 7 parts🎬 2 animations▶ 9 runnable examples🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 5 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Testers who know the engine read stack traces faster, explain odd log orders, and recognise the \"this is undefined\" crash in page objects at a glance. 1Execution contexts and the call stack 2Creation phase, hoisting and the scope chain 3this in depth: five rules and a precedence 4The Node.js event loop, phase by phase 5Inside V8: JIT, hidden classes and inline caches 6Garbage collection 7Real world scenario: a crash only in the retry helper Lesson 1 of 7"],[13,"stack","Execution contexts and the call stack","🧪 QA essentialReading stack traces quickly is the first step in debugging every failed automated test. Every time a function is called, the engine creates an execution context: a record of its variables, its scope chain and its this. Contexts sit on the call stack. The one on top is running, and when it returns it is popped off. A stack trace in an error is simply a print of this stack. The call stack and stack overflow · ▶ Runs function placeOrder() { return chargeCard(); } function chargeCard() { return callBank(); } function callBank() { // Error().stack is a snapshot of the call stack at this momentreturnnewError(\"snapshot\").stack.split(\"\\n\").slice(1, 4).map(l => l.trim().split(\" (\")[0]); } console.log(placeOrder()); // Each call adds a frame. Too many frames and the engine stops you.function countDown(n) { return n === 0 ? \"done\" : countDown(n - 1); } console.log(countDown(1000)); try { countDown(1e6); } catch (e) { console.log(e.name + \":\", e.message); } Output [ 'at callBank', 'at chargeCard', 'at placeOrder' ] done RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded Read a failing test's stack trace from the top: the first line that points into your code (a page object, a helper, a spec file) is usually where the fix belongs. Lines inside node_modules are the framework calling you. Lesson 2 of 7"],[13,"contexts","Creation phase, hoisting and the scope chain","Before a function runs, the engine scans it and sets up its bindings. var names start as undefined, function declarations are ready to call, and let, const and class exist but sit in the temporal dead zone until their line runs. This scan is what people call hoisting. Execution contexts, the TDZ and the scope chain · ▶ Runs // Creation phase: the engine scans the function before running a single linefunction checkout() { console.log(typeof total, typeof applyCoupon); // var is undefined, the function existstry { console.log(discount); } catch (e) { console.log(e.name + \":\", e.message); } try { typeofCart; } catch (e) { console.log(\"even typeof throws in the TDZ:\", e.name); } var total = 100; let discount = 10; // in the temporal dead zone until herefunction applyCoupon(t) { return t - discount; } classCart {} return applyCoupon(total); } console.log(checkout()); // Each loop iteration with let gets a fresh binding. var shares one.const withVar = [], withLet = []; for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) withVar.push(() => i); for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) withLet.push(() => j); console.log(withVar.map(f => f()), withLet.map(f => f())); // The scope chain is fixed where the function is written, not where it is calledconst label = \"global\"; function show() { return label; } function caller() { const label = \"caller\"; return show(); } console.log(caller()); Output undefined function ReferenceError: Cannot access 'discount' before initialization even typeof throws in the TDZ: ReferenceError 90 [ 3, 3, 3 ] [ 0, 1, 2 ] global Scope is lexical: a function looks up names in the place it was written, then outwards, never in the place it was called from. Closures (chapter 5) are this rule plus the fact that the outer variables stay alive as long as the inner function does. Lesson 3 of 7"],[13,"this","this in depth: five rules and a precedence","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightSeleniumPage object methods passed to forEach, retry helpers or event handlers lose this, which crashes the test. The value of this is decided by how a function is called, not where it is written. Five rules cover every case, and when several apply, the one higher in this list wins. 🎬 Try it"],[13,"this","Which this does the call get","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. newPage() // 1. new fn.bind(obj)() // 2. bound fn.call(obj) // 3. explicit obj.fn() // 4. implicit fn() // 5. default Call form Rule this is Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 6 The five rules of this · ▶ Runs \"use strict\"; function who() { returnthis?.name ?? String(this); } const page = { name: \"LoginPage\", who }; const other = { name: \"CartPage\" }; console.log(\"1 default:\", who()); // plain call: undefined in strict mode console.log(\"2 implicit:\", page.who()); // called on an object console.log(\"3 explicit:\", who.call(other)); // call, applyconst bound = who.bind(other); console.log(\"4 bound:\", bound(), bound.call(page)); // bind wins over callfunctionPage(name) { this.name = name; } console.log(\"5 new:\", newPage(\"Home\").name); // new creates a fresh this// Arrow functions have no this of their own. They use the one around them.const timer = { name: \"Timer\", regular() { return [1].map(function () { returnthis?.name; })[0]; }, arrow() { return [1].map(() => this.name)[0]; }, }; console.log(\"arrow:\", timer.arrow(), \"regular:\", timer.regular()); Output 1 default: undefined 2 implicit: LoginPage 3 explicit: CartPage 4 bound: CartPage CartPage 5 new: Home arrow: Timer regular: undefined The most common bug in test code is passing a method as a callback. The method is detached from its object, so the call becomes a plain call and this is undefined. Losing this when you pass a method · ▶ Runs classSearchPage { results = []; query = \"pen\"; // A normal method: \"this\" depends on how it is called collect(item) { this.results.push(`${this.query}:${item}`); } // A class field arrow: \"this\" is fixed to the instance collectSafe = item => { this.results.push(`${this.query}:${item}`); }; } const page = newSearchPage(); try { [\"blue\", \"red\"].forEach(page.collect); } catch (e) { console.log(\"passing the method:\", e.constructor.name, \"-\", e.message); } [\"blue\", \"red\"].forEach(page.collect.bind(page)); [\"green\"].forEach(page.collectSafe); [\"black\"].forEach(item => page.collect(item)); console.log(page.results); Output passing the method: TypeError - Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'results') [ 'pen:blue', 'pen:red', 'pen:green', 'pen:black' ] Lesson 4 of 7"],[13,"loop","The Node.js event loop, phase by phase","🧪 QA essentialKnowing which callback runs first explains log orders, race conditions and why a missing await lets a test finish too early. Chapter 11 showed the browser view: microtasks first, then one task. Node.js splits the task side into phases that run in a fixed order on every turn: timers (setTimeout and setInterval), poll (finished I/O such as files and sockets) and check (setImmediate). Between every callback, Node.js empties the process.nextTick queue and then the promise microtask queue. 🎬 Try it"],[13,"loop","One turn of the Node.js event loop","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. setTimeout(t, 0) // timers fs.readFile(f, io) // poll setImmediate(i) // check process.nextTick(n) // between every step Promise.resolve().then(p) // microtasks Running now nextTick and microtasks Loop phase Console Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 6 Node.js event loop phases in order · ▶ Runs const fs = require(\"node:fs\"); console.log(\"1 sync start\"); setTimeout(() => console.log(\"6 timers phase: setTimeout 0\"), 0); Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"4 microtask: promise then\")); queueMicrotask(() => console.log(\"5 microtask: queueMicrotask\")); process.nextTick(() => console.log(\"3 nextTick queue\")); fs.readFile(__filename, () => { console.log(\"7 poll phase: I/O callback\"); setTimeout(() => console.log(\"9 timers phase, next loop\"), 0); setImmediate(() => console.log(\"8 check phase: setImmediate\")); process.nextTick(() => console.log(\"7b nextTick after the callback\")); }); console.log(\"2 sync end\"); Output 1 sync start 2 sync end 3 nextTick queue 4 microtask: promise then 5 microtask: queueMicrotask 6 timers phase: setTimeout 0 7 poll phase: I/O callback 7b nextTick after the callback 8 check phase: setImmediate 9 timers phase, next loop Every await gives other code a turn · ▶ Runs const log = []; asyncfunction step(name) { log.push(`${name} start`); awaitnull; // one microtask hop, even for a value that is not a promise log.push(`${name} after await`); } step(\"A\"); step(\"B\"); Promise.resolve().then(() => log.push(\"then C\")); log.push(\"sync done\"); awaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, 0)); console.log(log); Output [ 'A start', 'B start', 'sync done', 'A after await', 'B after await', 'then C' ] Inside an I/O callback, setImmediate always runs before setTimeout(fn, 0), because the check phase comes straight after poll. At the top level of a script, the order of those two depends on how fast the process starts, so never write a test that relies on it. Lesson 5 of 7"],[13,"v8","Inside V8: JIT, hidden classes and inline caches","V8, the engine in Chrome and Node.js, first turns your code into bytecode and runs it with the Ignition interpreter. Functions that run often are compiled to machine code by the optimising compilers (Sparkplug, Maglev and TurboFan). The optimised code makes guesses, such as \"this object always has x then y\". If a guess turns out wrong, V8 deoptimises and falls back to slower code. Hidden classes: objects with the same shape · ▶ Runs // V8 gives objects with the same properties added in the same order the same hidden class (\"map\").// Code that always sees one shape stays fast. %HaveSameMap is a V8 debugging helper.functionPoint(x, y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; } const a = newPoint(1, 2), b = newPoint(3, 4); console.log(\"same constructor:\", %HaveSameMap(a, b)); const c = newPoint(5, 6); c.label = \"extra\"; // a new property moves c to a new shape console.log(\"extra property:\", %HaveSameMap(a, c)); console.log(\"different order:\", %HaveSameMap({ x: 1, y: 2 }, { y: 2, x: 1 })); const d = newPoint(7, 8); delete d.x; // delete makes V8 fall back to a slow dictionary console.log(\"after delete:\", %HaveSameMap(a, d), \"| dictionary mode:\", !%HasFastProperties(d)); Terminal $ node --allow-natives-syntax main.mjs Output same constructor: true extra property: false different order: false after delete: false | dictionary mode: true Each property access remembers the shapes it has seen in an inline cache. One shape is monomorphic and fastest, a few is polymorphic, and many is megamorphic and slow. In practice: create objects with all their properties in the constructor, in the same order, and avoid delete on hot objects. Do not tune for the engine before you measure (chapter 31). Shapes matter in tight loops over millions of objects, such as parsing a large log file, and almost never in test code. Lesson 6 of 7"],[13,"gc","Garbage collection","🧪 QA essentialSoak tests and long UI sessions expose memory leaks, and heap snapshots are how testers report them precisely. JavaScript frees memory for you. V8 uses a generational collector because most objects die young. New objects go into the small young generation, which is cleaned often with a fast copying \"scavenge\". Objects that survive twice move to the old generation, which is cleaned less often with mark and sweep: mark everything reachable from the roots (globals and the stack), then free the rest. Most of the work happens in parallel and in small steps, so pauses stay short. Generations in the V8 heap · ▶ Runs import v8 from\"node:v8\"; // The heap is split into a young generation (new objects) and an old generation (survivors)const spaces = v8.getHeapSpaceStatistics().map(s => s.space_name); console.log(\"young:\", spaces.includes(\"new_space\"), \"| old:\", spaces.includes(\"old_space\"), \"| big objects:\", spaces.includes(\"large_object_space\")); // Short lived objects are cheap: most die young and are collected in a quick \"scavenge\"let kept = []; for (let i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) { const temp = { id: i, tags: [\"a\", \"b\"] }; // garbage right after this iterationif (i % 10_000 === 0) kept.push(temp); // only these survive } console.log(\"kept\", kept.length, \"objects out of 100000\"); console.log(\"heap limit is a few GB:\", v8.getHeapStatistics().heap_size_limit > 1e9); Output young: true | old: true | big objects: true kept 10 objects out of 100000 heap limit is a few GB: true If memory keeps rising in a long test run, take two heap snapshots in Chrome DevTools (Memory tab) or with v8.writeHeapSnapshot() and compare them. The objects that grow between the snapshots, and the \"retainers\" that hold them, point to the leak (chapter 31). Lesson 7 of 7"],[13,"scenario","Real world scenario: a crash only in the retry helper","🧪 QA essential The situationA test fails with \"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'attempts')\". The page object works when called directly, and fails only when it is passed to the shared retry helper. Fix \"Cannot read properties of undefined\" in a page object · ▶ Runs // A retry helper used by many page objectsasyncfunction retry(action, times = 3) { for (let i = 1; i <= times; i++) { try { returnawait action(); } catch (e) { if (i === times) throw e; } } } classCheckoutPage { attempts = 0; constructor(api) { this.api = api; } async submit() { this.attempts++; returnthis.api.post(\"/orders\"); } } const flakyApi = { calls: 0, async post() { if (++this.calls < 2) thrownewError(\"503\"); return { id: 1042 }; } }; const page = newCheckoutPage(flakyApi); // The bug from the ticket: \"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'attempts')\"await retry(page.submit).catch(e => console.log(\"bug:\", e.message)); // Three fixes, each keeps \"this\" pointing at the page console.log(await retry(() => page.submit()), \"attempts:\", page.attempts); console.log(await retry(page.submit.bind(page)), \"attempts:\", page.attempts); Output bug: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'attempts') { id: 1042 } attempts: 2 { id: 1042 } attempts: 3 The helper calls action() as a plain function, so this is undefined inside submit. Wrapping it in an arrow or binding it keeps the page object as this. The team also added a lint rule, @typescript-eslint/unbound-method, which flags this mistake in review. 🧮"],[13,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 placeOrder calls chargeCard, which calls callBank. How many of these frames are on the stack while callBank runs? Count the calls not yet returned. Answer: 3 placeOrder, chargeCard and callBank are all still running. C2 What does fns[0]() return? const fns = []; for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) fns.push(() => i); One shared i. Answer: 3 All three arrows read the same i, which ends at 3. C1 With let instead, what does fns[0]() + fns[1]() + fns[2]() give? const fns = []; for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) fns.push(() => i); A fresh binding per turn. Answer: 3 0 + 1 + 2 = 3 C2 A CommonJS file logs: sync A, nextTick B, promise C, timeout D, sync E. Write the position (1 to 5) of D in the output. Sync, then nextTick, then microtasks, then timers. Answer: 5 Order: A, E, B, C, D. D is fifth. C3 countDown(n) recurses until 0. How many frames does countDown(1000) push, counting the first call? n from 1000 down to 0. Answer: 1001 1000 − 0 + 1 = 1001 C1 An object survives two scavenges and is promoted. Scavenges run every 8 MB of new objects. After how many MB of allocation at the earliest is it in the old generation? Two scavenges. Answer: 16 2 × 8 = 16 C2 A property access sees 1 shape (fast), 3 shapes (polymorphic) or more than 4 (megamorphic). How many shapes can a polymorphic cache hold at most? V8 keeps up to four. Answer: 4 A polymorphic inline cache stores up to 4 shapes. A fifth shape makes it megamorphic. C1 An async function has 3 awaits on already resolved values. How many microtask hops before it returns? One per await. Answer: 3 Each await, even on a plain value, schedules the rest as a microtask. 🎯"],[13,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 What does a stack trace show? The call stack. Read from the top to see the innermost call first. Q2 What does typeof x print on the line before var x = 5? var is hoisted with undefined. The declaration is set up early, the value 5 is assigned only when that line runs. Q3 Inside an arrow function, where does this come from? Arrows have no own this. That is why arrows are handy inside callbacks of methods. Q1 Which wins: fn.bind(a).call(b)? bind is stronger than call. A bound function ignores call and apply. Only new can override bind. Q2 Why does accessing a let variable before its line throw? TDZ. let is hoisted but not initialised until its declaration runs. Q3 A function uses a variable named label. Which label does it see? Lexical scope. JavaScript scope is decided by where code is written. Q4 In CommonJS, which runs first after the main script: process.nextTick or a promise then? The nextTick queue goes first. Node.js empties the nextTick queue before the promise microtask queue. Q1 Inside an fs.readFile callback, setTimeout(a, 0) and setImmediate(b). Which runs first? Poll, then check, then timers on the next turn. This order is guaranteed inside I/O callbacks. Q2 What makes two objects share a V8 hidden class? Shape. {x, y} and {y, x} have different shapes. Q3 Why are most objects collected cheaply? Generational hypothesis. Only survivors pay for the slower old generation collection. Q1 What does the last line return? classP { m() { returnthis; } } const f = newP().m; f(); Classes run in strict mode. The detached method is a plain call, which gives undefined in strict code. Q2 Two async functions each await null once. In what order do their second halves run? Each await queues one microtask. Microtasks run first in, first out."],[13,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[13,"order","Predict the log order","Write logOrder() that returns an array of the labels in the order this CommonJS code prints them: console.log(\"A\"); setTimeout(() => console.log(\"B\")); Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"C\")); process.nextTick(() => console.log(\"D\")); console.log(\"E\"); Synchronous lines first, then nextTick, then promise microtasks, then timers. function logOrder() { return [\"A\", \"E\", \"D\", \"C\", \"B\"]; } This exact order is what the loop example on this page printed. 🌿 Medium"],[13,"bindme","Write your own bind","Write myBind(fn, thisArg, ...preset) that returns a function which calls fn with this set to thisArg, the preset arguments first and then the new ones. Do not use the built in bind. Return an arrow that calls fn.apply. function myBind(fn, thisArg, ...preset) { return (...args) => fn.apply(thisArg, [...preset, ...args]); } The returned arrow ignores the this it is called with, which is exactly why bound functions cannot be rebound by call. 🔥 Hard"],[13,"fixthis","Fix the lost this","class Counter { count = 0; inc() { this.count++; } } crashes when inc is passed to forEach. Write makeCounter() that returns an object with a count of 0 and an inc that still works when detached: [1, 2, 3].forEach(c.inc) must leave c.count at 3. Close over the object instead of using this. function makeCounter() { const c = { count: 0 }; c.inc = () => { c.count++; }; return c; } A class field arrow, inc = () => this.count++, does the same thing inside a class. 🧠 Expert"],[13,"miniloop","A tiny event loop","Write runLoop(jobs). Each job is { kind, name } where kind is \"sync\", \"micro\", \"timer\" or \"immediate\". Return the names in the order Node.js would run them from the main script: all sync jobs, then all micro jobs, then timers, then immediates, each group keeping its input order. Go through the kinds in a fixed order and filter the jobs for each one. function runLoop(jobs) { const order = [\"sync\", \"micro\", \"timer\", \"immediate\"]; return order.flatMap(kind => jobs.filter(j => j.kind === kind).map(j => j.name)); } Real loops also run new microtasks created by a timer before the next timer, which is the \"between every callback\" rule."],[13,"miniloop","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. Execution contexts and the call stackEvery time a function is called, the engine creates an execution context: a record of its variables, its scope chain and its this. Contexts sit on the call stack. The one on top is running, and when it returns it is popped off. Creation phase, hoisting and the scope chainBefore a function runs, the engine scans it and sets up its bindings. var names start as undefined, function declarations are ready to call, and let, const and class exist but sit in the temporal dead zone until their line runs. this in depth: five rules and a precedenceThe value of this is decided by how a function is called, not where it is written. Five rules cover every case, and when several apply, the one higher in this list wins. The Node.js event loop, phase by phaseChapter 11 showed the browser view: microtasks first, then one task. Node.js splits the task side into phases that run in a fixed order on every turn: timers (setTimeout and setInterval), poll (finished I/O such as files and sockets) and check (setImmediate). Inside V8: JIT, hidden classes and inline cachesV8, the engine in Chrome and Node.js, first turns your code into bytecode and runs it with the Ignition interpreter. Functions that run often are compiled to machine code by the optimising compilers (Sparkplug, Maglev and TurboFan). Garbage collectionJavaScript frees memory for you. V8 uses a generational collector because most objects die young. New objects go into the small young generation, which is cleaned often with a fast copying \"scavenge\". Real world scenario: a crash only in the retry helperThe helper calls action() as a plain function, so this is undefined inside submit. Wrapping it in an arrow or binding it keeps the page object as this. The team also added a lint rule, @typescript-eslint/unbound-method, which flags this mistake in review."],[13,"comic","What happens when your code runs","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember The call decides this, the place you write a function decides its scope, and the loop runs microtasks before the next phase."],[13,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🐞A Selenium suite Passed a page object method to an array forEach and got \"this is undefined\" only on one screen. A class field arrow fixed it for every caller. 📈A log parser Built its objects with different property orders, which made a hot loop megamorphic. Creating every record with the same constructor made it four times faster. ⏱️An API mock Used setImmediate inside a socket callback to send a reply after the request was fully read, which the poll and check phase order guarantees. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"How JavaScript really works\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 9Welcome This chapter looks under the hood: execution contexts, hoisting, the rules of this, the Node event loop phases, V8 optimisation and garbage collection. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[14,"","Errors, debugging and logging","Built in error types and custom error classes with codes and causes, errors in callbacks and promises, unhandled rejections and global handlers, errors as values, inspecting values and starting the debuggers, JSON logs with a request id through AsyncLocalStorage, source maps for TypeScript stack traces, and sorting test failures by error type. · errors · custom error · error cause · AggregateError · unhandled rejection · uncaughtException · debugging · debugger · trace viewer · util.inspect · structured logging · AsyncLocalStorage · source maps · triage · interview 🟨 JavaScript›🔬 JavaScript in depth›Chapter 15 of 31 🧯 Step 5 · Topic 2 of 6 · Advanced"],[14,"top","","Handle failure like an expert: the built in error types, custom errors with codes and causes, errors in async code and unhandled rejections, when to return a result instead of throwing, real debuggers and the Playwright trace viewer, structured logs with request ids, and source maps. ⏱️ About 18 min read📚 7 parts🎬 1 animations▶ 9 runnable examples🧪 1 real test run🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 5 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers A tester spends much of the day reading failures. This chapter shows how errors are built, how to debug them fast, and how to sort a red pipeline into product, test and environment problems. 1The error types and building your own 2Errors in async code 3Throw or return: errors as values 4Debugging tools 5Structured logging 6Source maps 7Real world scenario: triage a red pipeline Lesson 1 of 7"],[14,"types","The error types and building your own","🧪 QA essentialTest frameworks, HTTP clients and drivers all throw typed errors, and good helpers in a test framework do the same. Every error has a name, a message and a stack. The built in types tell you what kind of mistake happened, which narrows the search before you read a single line of code. The built in error types · ▶ Runs const cases = [ () => null.length, // TypeError: wrong kind of value () => notDefinedAnywhere, // ReferenceError: unknown name () => newArray(-1), // RangeError: value out of range () => JSON.parse(\"{bad json}\"), // SyntaxError: at run time, from a parser () => decodeURIComponent(\"%\"), // URIError () => { thrownewAggregateError([newError(\"a\"), newError(\"b\")], \"2 checks failed\"); }, ]; for (const fn of cases) { try { fn(); } catch (e) { console.log(e.constructor.name.padEnd(15), e.message, e.errors ? `(${e.errors.length} inside)` : \"\"); } } Output TypeError Cannot read properties of null (reading 'length') ReferenceError notDefinedAnywhere is not defined RangeError Invalid array length SyntaxError Expected property name or '}' in JSON at position 1 (line 1 column 2) URIError URI malformed AggregateError 2 checks failed (2 inside) For your own code, a small hierarchy of error classes lets callers catch a whole family or one exact kind. Give each error a stable code for programs to check, a human message, and a cause when you wrap a lower level error. Custom errors with code and cause · ▶ Runs // A small hierarchy: catch the family, or one exact kindclassAppErrorextendsError { constructor(message, { code, cause, details } = {}) { super(message, { cause }); this.name = new.target.name; // \"ValidationError\", not \"Error\"this.code = code; this.details = details; } } classValidationErrorextendsAppError {} classHttpErrorextendsAppError { constructor(status, url) { super(`HTTP ${status} from ${url}`, { code: `HTTP_${status}` }); this.status = status; } get retryable() { returnthis.status >= 500 || this.status === 429; } } function placeOrder(qty) { if (!Number.isInteger(qty) || qty < 1) thrownewValidationError(\"qty must be a whole number from 1\", { code: \"BAD_QTY\", details: { qty } }); thrownewHttpError(503, \"/api/orders\"); } for (const qty of [0, 2]) { try { placeOrder(qty); } catch (e) { if (e instanceofValidationError) console.log(\"fix the input:\", e.code, e.details); elseif (e instanceofHttpError) console.log(e.name, e.message, \"| retry?\", e.retryable); console.log(\" still an Error:\", e instanceofError, \"| an AppError:\", e instanceofAppError); } } // Wrap low level errors with cause so no detail is losttry { try { JSON.parse(\"<html>502 Bad Gateway</html>\"); } catch (e) { thrownewAppError(\"Could not read the orders response\", { code: \"BAD_BODY\", cause: e }); } } catch (e) { console.log(`${e.name}: ${e.message}\\n caused by ${e.cause.name}: ${e.cause.message.split(\",\")[0]}`); } Output fix the input: BAD_QTY { qty: 0 } still an Error: true | an AppError: true HttpError HTTP 503 from /api/orders | retry? true still an Error: true | an AppError: true AppError: Could not read the orders response caused by SyntaxError: Unexpected token '<' When an error is thrown, the engine stops the current function and looks for a catch in it. If there is none, it removes that function from the call stack and looks in the caller, and so on. This is called unwinding the stack. Every finally on the way still runs, which is why clean up code belongs there. 🎬 Try it"],[14,"types","A thrown error travels down the call stack","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. function readConfig() { thrownewConfigError(\"missing url\"); } function startSuite() { try { readConfig(); } finally { console.log(\"cleanup\"); } } try { startSuite(); } catch (e) { console.log(\"caught\", e.code); } Call stack Error in flight Console Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 6 Check the code or the class, never the message text. Messages change with translations and wording fixes, codes do not. Lesson 2 of 7"],[14,"async","Errors in async code","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightNode.jsMissing awaits and unhandled rejections cause failures that appear in the wrong test, the hardest kind to debug. A try block only sees errors thrown while it is on the call stack. A callback that runs later has left it already. With await, a rejected promise turns into an ordinary throw at that line, which is the main reason async and await made error handling simple. Errors in async code · ▶ Runs // 1. try and catch does not reach a callback that runs latertry { setTimeout(() => { try { thrownewError(\"inside the timer\"); } catch (e) { console.log(\"caught inside the callback:\", e.message); } }, 0); } catch { console.log(\"this outer catch never runs\"); } // 2. With await, a rejected promise becomes a normal throwasyncfunction loadUser() { thrownewError(\"401 Unauthorized\"); } try { await loadUser(); } catch (e) { console.log(\"await turns rejection into throw:\", e.message); } // 3. Without await, the rejection escapes the try blocktry { loadUser().catch(e => console.log(\"only .catch sees it:\", e.message)); } catch { console.log(\"never reached\"); } // 4. finally runs whatever happens, which is where clean up belongsasyncfunction withBrowser() { try { thrownewError(\"assertion failed\"); } finally { console.log(\"finally: browser closed\"); } } await withBrowser().catch(e => console.log(\"test failed:\", e.message)); awaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, 5)); Output await turns rejection into throw: 401 Unauthorized finally: browser closed only .catch sees it: 401 Unauthorized test failed: assertion failed caught inside the callback: inside the timer A rejected promise that nobody handles is an unhandled rejection. Since Node.js 15 it stops the process with exit code 1, which is good: the bug cannot hide. An unhandled rejection stops Node.js · 💥 Throws asyncfunction save() { thrownewError(\"DB down\"); } save(); // nobody awaits or catches this promise setTimeout(() => console.log(\"this line never prints\"), 10); Output main.mjs:1 async function save() { throw new Error(\"DB down\"); } ^ Error: DB down at save (main.mjs:1:31) at main.mjs:2:1 Node.js v26.12.1 Global handlers for the errors nobody caught · 💥 Throws // Last line of defence: log, then exit with a failure code so CI notices process.on(\"unhandledRejection\", (reason) => { console.log(\"unhandledRejection:\", reason.message); process.exitCode = 1; }); process.on(\"uncaughtException\", (err, origin) => { console.log(`${origin}:`, err.message); process.exitCode = 1; }); process.on(\"exit\", code => console.log(\"exit code\", code)); Promise.reject(newError(\"forgot to await the cleanup\")); setTimeout(() => { thrownewError(\"bug in a timer\"); }, 0); Output unhandledRejection: forgot to await the cleanup uncaughtException: bug in a timer exit code 1 The most common cause of an unhandled rejection in tests is a missing await before a Playwright action or an API call. The test may even pass, then a later test fails with a strange error. Turn on the no-floating-promises lint rule. Lesson 3 of 7"],[14,"values","Throw or return: errors as values","Throwing is right for things that should never happen. For failures you expect, such as a user typing a bad coupon, returning a result object keeps the flow simple and forces the caller to look at both cases. Errors as values for expected failures · ▶ Runs // Expected failures as values, unexpected ones as exceptionsconst ok = value => ({ ok: true, value }); const fail = (code, message) => ({ ok: false, code, message }); function parseCoupon(text) { const m = /^([A-Z]+)(\\d{1,2})$/.exec(text.trim()); if (!m) return fail(\"FORMAT\", `\"${text}\" is not like SAVE10`); const percent = Number(m[2]); if (percent > 50) return fail(\"TOO_BIG\", `${percent}% is over the 50% limit`); return ok({ name: m[1], percent }); } for (const input of [\"SAVE10\", \"save10\", \"MEGA90\"]) { const r = parseCoupon(input); console.log(input.padEnd(7), r.ok ? `valid ${r.value.percent}%` : `${r.code}: ${r.message}`); } Output SAVE10 valid 10% save10 FORMAT: \"save10\" is not like SAVE10 MEGA90 TOO_BIG: 90% is over the 50% limit A good rule: validation and parsing of outside input return results, while broken assumptions inside your own code throw. Never use an empty catch {}, which hides the very information you will need later. Lesson 4 of 7"],[14,"debugging","Debugging tools","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightVitestThe Inspector and trace viewer turn a red CI run into a step by step replay of what the browser saw. console.log is fine for a quick look, but it hides deep objects and its output is easy to misread. Node.js gives you precise tools for inspecting values. Inspect what you log · ▶ Runs import util from\"node:util\"; const response = { status: 200, body: { order: { id: 7, items: [{ sku: \"PEN\", price: { amount: 10, currency: \"INR\" } }] } } }; console.log(response); // nested objects are cut at depth 2 console.log(util.inspect(response, { depth: null, compact: true, breakLength: 120 })); console.dir(response.body.order.items, { depth: 0 }); console.table([{ test: \"login\", ms: 840, ok: true }, { test: \"checkout\", ms: 2310, ok: false }]); console.group(\"checkout\"); console.count(\"retry\"); console.count(\"retry\"); console.assert(1 + 1 === 3, \"maths is broken\"); // prints only when false console.groupEnd(); console.log(util.format(\"%s took %d ms (%o)\", \"login\", 840, { slow: false })); Output { status: 200, body: { order: { id: 7, items: [Array] } } } { status: 200, body: { order: { id: 7, items: [ { sku: 'PEN', price: { amount: 10, currency: 'INR' } } ] } } } [ [Object] ] ┌─────────┬────────────┬──────┬───────┐ │ (index) │ test │ ms │ ok │ ├─────────┼────────────┼──────┼───────┤ │ 0 │ 'login' │ 840 │ true │ │ 1 │ 'checkout' │ 2310 │ false │ └─────────┴────────────┴──────┴───────┘ checkout retry: 1 retry: 2 Assertion failed: maths is broken login took 840 ms ({ slow: false }) For anything harder, use a real debugger. You can pause on any line, look at every variable, step into functions and set conditional breakpoints that stop only when, for example, order.total < 0. Logpoints print a message without changing the code. Starting the debuggers · Interactive tools # Pause on the first line, then open chrome://inspect in Chrome or press F5 in VS Codenode --inspect-brk src/checkout.js # Debug a single test filenode --inspect-brk --test test/cart.test.js npx vitest --inspect-brk --no-file-parallelism cart.test.js # Playwright: step through actions in the Inspector, or record a trace to replay later PWDEBUG=1npx playwright test checkout.spec.ts npx playwright test --trace on && npx playwright show-trace test-results/**/trace.zip # A debugger; statement in code pauses there when DevTools or the debugger is attached The Playwright trace viewer is the best debugger for a failed UI test: it shows every action, a DOM snapshot before and after, the network calls and the console, all from a CI run you cannot reproduce on your laptop. Lesson 5 of 7"],[14,"logging","Structured logging","🧪 QA essentialAttaching a test id or request id to every log line is how you find the server logs that match one failed test. Plain text logs are hard to search across many servers or parallel tests. Structured logs write one JSON object per line with a level, a message and fields. Tools such as pino in Node.js, and log platforms such as Grafana Loki, Datadog or ELK, filter them by any field. Structured logs with a request id across awaits · ▶ Runs import { AsyncLocalStorage } from\"node:async_hooks\"; // Structured JSON logs with a request id that follows the work through every awaitconst context = newAsyncLocalStorage(); const log = (level, msg, extra = {}) => console.log(JSON.stringify({ level, msg, requestId: context.getStore()?.requestId, ...extra })); asyncfunction fetchStock(sku) { awaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, sku === \"PEN\" ? 20 : 5)); log(\"debug\", \"stock checked\", { sku }); return3; } asyncfunction handleOrder(requestId, sku) { return context.run({ requestId }, async () => { log(\"info\", \"order received\", { sku }); await fetchStock(sku); log(\"info\", \"order placed\"); }); } // Two requests run at the same time, yet every line carries the right idawaitPromise.all([handleOrder(\"req-1\", \"PEN\"), handleOrder(\"req-2\", \"BOOK\")]); Output {\"level\":\"info\",\"msg\":\"order received\",\"requestId\":\"req-1\",\"sku\":\"PEN\"} {\"level\":\"info\",\"msg\":\"order received\",\"requestId\":\"req-2\",\"sku\":\"BOOK\"} {\"level\":\"debug\",\"msg\":\"stock checked\",\"requestId\":\"req-2\",\"sku\":\"BOOK\"} {\"level\":\"info\",\"msg\":\"order placed\",\"requestId\":\"req-2\"} {\"level\":\"debug\",\"msg\":\"stock checked\",\"requestId\":\"req-1\",\"sku\":\"PEN\"} {\"level\":\"info\",\"msg\":\"order placed\",\"requestId\":\"req-1\"} AsyncLocalStorage keeps a value, such as a request id or the current test name, attached to all async work started inside context.run. That is how a log line deep in a helper still knows which request or test it belongs to. Lesson 6 of 7"],[14,"sourcemaps","Source maps","TypeScript, bundlers and minifiers change your code before it runs, so stack traces point at generated files. A source map is a file that maps each generated position back to the original line. Browsers use them automatically in DevTools. Node.js uses them with --enable-source-maps. Source maps point the trace at the TypeScript line · 🧪 Tests run 📄 src/price.ts exportfunction lineTotal(price: number, qty: number): number { if (qty > 10) { thrownewRangeError(`qty ${qty} is over the limit of 10`); } return price * qty; } lineTotal(99, 12); 📄 run.sh npx tsc || exit 1 echo \"$ node dist/price.js\"node dist/price.js 2>&1 | grep -E \"RangeError|at lineTotal\" echo echo \"$ node --enable-source-maps dist/price.js\"node --enable-source-maps dist/price.js 2>&1 | grep -E \"RangeError|at lineTotal\" true Terminal $ sh run.sh Terminal output $ node dist/price.js throw new RangeError(`qty ${qty} is over the limit of 10`); RangeError: qty 12 is over the limit of 10 at lineTotal (dist/price.js:3:15) $ node --enable-source-maps dist/price.js throw new RangeError(`qty ${qty} is over the limit of 10`); RangeError: qty 12 is over the limit of 10 at lineTotal (src/price.ts:3:11) Upload source maps to your error tracker, such as Sentry, so production errors show the real file and line. Keep them off the public web server if your source code is private. Lesson 7 of 7"],[14,"scenario","Real world scenario: triage a red pipeline","🧪 QA essential The situationThe nightly run has five failures. Before anyone opens a debugger, the team wants to know which failures are the product, which are the tests and which are the environment. Triage failures by error type · ▶ Runs // Sort failed tests into buckets before anyone starts debuggingconst failures = [ { test: \"checkout total\", error: { name: \"AssertionError\", message: \"expected 450 to equal 405\" } }, { test: \"login\", error: { name: \"TimeoutError\", message: \"locator.click: Timeout 5000ms exceeded\" } }, { test: \"search\", error: { name: \"Error\", message: \"connect ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:3000\" } }, { test: \"profile\", error: { name: \"TypeError\", message: \"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'name')\" } }, { test: \"orders\", error: { name: \"Error\", message: \"HTTP 503 from /api/orders\" } }, ]; const rules = [ [e => /ECONNREFUSED|ENOTFOUND|HTTP50[234]/.test(e.message), \"environment\"], [e => e.name === \"TimeoutError\", \"timing or locator\"], [e => e.name === \"AssertionError\" || /expect/.test(e.message), \"possible product bug\"], [e => [\"TypeError\", \"ReferenceError\"].includes(e.name), \"test code bug\"], ]; const bucket = e => rules.find(([match]) => match(e))?.[1] ?? \"unknown\"; const report = Object.groupBy(failures, f => bucket(f.error)); for (const [kind, list] ofObject.entries(report)) console.log(kind.padEnd(21), list.map(f => f.test).join(\", \")); Output possible product bug checkout total timing or locator login environment search, orders test code bug profile Error names and messages carry enough signal to sort most failures automatically. Connection errors and 5xx responses point at the environment, timeouts at timing or locators, assertion errors at the product, and TypeErrors in test code at the tests themselves. Posting this summary to the team chat saves the first half hour of every morning. 🧮"],[14,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 A suite has 5 failures: 2 environment, 1 timeout, 1 assertion, 1 TypeError. How many point at the product? Assertions. Answer: 1 Only the assertion failure is a possible product bug. C2 console.count(\"retry\") is called 3 times. What number does the third call print? It counts calls with that label. Answer: 3 retry: 1, retry: 2, retry: 3. C1 util.inspect default depth is 2. An object nests 4 levels deep. How many levels are hidden behind [Object]? Shown levels are 0, 1 and 2. Answer: 2 Levels 3 and 4 are replaced by [Object]. C2 A retry helper tries 3 times. Each failing call takes 2 s and there is a 1 s wait between tries. How long until the final failure, in seconds? Calls plus waits. Answer: 8 3 × 2 + 2 × 1 = 8 C3 Out of 200 nightly failures, 120 were environment problems. What percent is that? Part over whole. Answer: 60 120 ÷ 200 × 100 = 60 C1 A 5xx retry policy retries 503 and 429 but not 400 or 404. Of the statuses 400, 404, 429, 500, 503, how many are retried if 5xx also includes 500? 429 plus the 5xx ones. Answer: 3 429, 500 and 503 are retried. 400 and 404 are not. C2 Exponential backoff waits 100 ms, then doubles each time. What is the total wait over 4 retries, in ms? 100 + 200 + 400 + 800. Answer: 1500 100 + 200 + 400 + 800 = 1500 C1 Two requests log 3 lines each, interleaved. With a request id on every line, how many lines does a search for one id return? Only its own lines. Answer: 3 The id filters exactly that request out of the 6 lines. 🎯"],[14,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 Which error do you get from null.length? Wrong kind of value. Reading a property of null or undefined is a TypeError. Q2 What does finally do? Always. That makes it the place for clean up such as closing a browser. Q3 Which console method shows an array of objects as a grid? A table. Handy for test results and API rows. Q1 Why set this.name = new.target.name in a base error class? Subclasses. Otherwise every custom error prints as plain \"Error\". Q2 A function calls loadUser() inside try without await, and it rejects. What happens? The promise is not awaited. The rejection happens after the try block has finished. Q3 What does { cause } in new Error(msg, { cause }) give you? Wrapping. Logs and inspect show both errors, so no detail is lost. Q4 Why check err.code instead of err.message? Stability. Rewording a message must never break a caller or a test. Q1 What does Node.js do by default with an unhandled rejection? Since Node.js 15. Earlier versions only warned, which let bugs hide. Q2 What does AsyncLocalStorage give you? Context across awaits. Used for request ids, user ids and test names in logs. Q3 What is a source map for? Stack traces. It makes errors in compiled TypeScript point at the .ts line. Q1 A failure says \"connect ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:3000\". Most likely cause? Connection refused. Nothing is listening on that port, which is an environment problem. Q2 Which is the best use of AggregateError? Many at once. Its errors property holds every inner error."],[14,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[14,"httperror","A custom error class","Write a class HttpError that extends Error. new HttpError(404, \"/cart\") must have name \"HttpError\", status 404 and message \"HTTP 404 from /cart\". Call super(message) first, then set the extra fields. classHttpErrorextendsError { constructor(status, url) { super(`HTTP ${status} from ${url}`); this.name = \"HttpError\"; this.status = status; } } Subclasses of Error keep the stack trace and work with instanceof. 🌿 Medium"],[14,"safejson","Parse JSON without throwing","Write safeJson(text) that returns { ok: true, value } for valid JSON and { ok: false, error } with the error message for invalid JSON. Wrap JSON.parse in try and catch and return an object from both branches. function safeJson(text) { try { return { ok: true, value: JSON.parse(text) }; } catch (e) { return { ok: false, error: e.message }; } } An API test can now report \"the body was HTML, not JSON\" instead of crashing. 🔥 Hard"],[14,"retryif","Retry only the right errors","Write async retryIf(fn, shouldRetry, times). Call fn() up to times times. If it throws and shouldRetry(error) is true and tries are left, try again, otherwise rethrow at once. Return the first successful result. A loop with try and catch. Rethrow when you are out of tries or the error is not retryable. asyncfunction retryIf(fn, shouldRetry, times) { for (let i = 1; ; i++) { try { returnawait fn(); } catch (e) { if (i >= times || !shouldRetry(e)) throw e; } } } Retrying a 400 can never help and hides real bugs. Only retry errors that can go away by themselves. 🧠 Expert"],[14,"triage","Triage a failure","Write triage(error) that returns \"environment\" when the message contains ECONNREFUSED or ENOTFOUND or \"HTTP 5\", \"timing\" when the name is TimeoutError, \"product\" when the name is AssertionError, \"test\" for TypeError or ReferenceError, and \"unknown\" otherwise. Check the rules in that order. Use a regular expression for the message test and simple comparisons for the names. function triage(error) { const { name, message } = error; if (/ECONNREFUSED|ENOTFOUND|HTTP5/.test(message)) return\"environment\"; if (name === \"TimeoutError\") return\"timing\"; if (name === \"AssertionError\") return\"product\"; if (name === \"TypeError\" || name === \"ReferenceError\") return\"test\"; return\"unknown\"; } The third test shows why order matters: an assertion that failed because of a 500 is still an environment problem."],[14,"triage","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. The error types and building your ownEvery error has a name, a message and a stack. The built in types tell you what kind of mistake happened, which narrows the search before you read a single line of code. Errors in async codeA try block only sees errors thrown while it is on the call stack. A callback that runs later has left it already. With await, a rejected promise turns into an ordinary throw at that line, which is the main reason async and await made error handling simple. Throw or return: errors as valuesThrowing is right for things that should never happen. For failures you expect, such as a user typing a bad coupon, returning a result object keeps the flow simple and forces the caller to look at both cases. Debugging toolsconsole.log is fine for a quick look, but it hides deep objects and its output is easy to misread. Node.js gives you precise tools for inspecting values. Structured loggingPlain text logs are hard to search across many servers or parallel tests. Structured logs write one JSON object per line with a level, a message and fields. Source mapsTypeScript, bundlers and minifiers change your code before it runs, so stack traces point at generated files. A source map is a file that maps each generated position back to the original line. Browsers use them automatically in DevTools. Real world scenario: triage a red pipelineError names and messages carry enough signal to sort most failures automatically. Connection errors and 5xx responses point at the environment, timeouts at timing or locators, assertion errors at the product, and TypeErrors in test code at the tests ..."],[14,"comic","When things go wrong","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Throw typed errors with a code and cause, await everything, and log JSON with an id you can search."],[14,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🧾A payments API Gave every error a stable code such as CARD_DECLINED. Its client apps and the API tests check codes, so rewording messages never breaks anything. 🌙A nightly suite Bucketed failures by error type and posted the summary to chat. Environment failures went to the platform team, product bugs to developers. 🔗A microservice team Added a request id with AsyncLocalStorage and found a slow checkout by searching one id across five services. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Errors, debugging and logging\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 9Welcome This chapter covers errors in depth, async errors and unhandled rejections, errors as values, debugging tools, structured logging and source maps. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[15,"","Regular expressions in depth","How the backtracking engine matches, greedy against lazy, lookarounds for password rules and backreferences, the m, s, g and y flags and the lastIndex trap, unicode letters and emoji, replacing with groups and functions to mask secrets, a sticky tokeniser, catastrophic backtracking, validation patterns with table driven tests, and parsing access logs. · regex · regular expressions · backtracking · lazy quantifier · lookahead · lookbehind · backreference · lastIndex · unicode regex · replace function · masking · tokenizer · ReDoS · validation · log parsing · interview 🟨 JavaScript›🔬 JavaScript in depth›Chapter 16 of 31 🧷 Step 5 · Topic 3 of 6 · Advanced"],[15,"top","","Go beyond the basics of chapter 7: how the backtracking engine finds a match, greedy and lazy quantifiers, lookarounds and backreferences, every flag and the lastIndex trap, unicode letters in every language, replacing and masking, a sticky tokeniser, ReDoS, and testing patterns with tables. ⏱️ About 18 min read📚 8 parts🎬 2 animations▶ 9 runnable examples🧪 1 real test run🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 7 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Regular expressions check text in assertions, extract ids from pages and logs, mask secrets in reports and find bugs in validation rules. This chapter makes you fluent and safe with them. 1How the regex engine matches 2Lookarounds and backreferences 3Flags in depth and the lastIndex trap 4Unicode and every language 5Replacing, masking and splitting 6ReDoS and safe patterns 7Validation patterns and testing them 8Real world scenario: mine the access logs Lesson 1 of 8"],[15,"engine","How the regex engine matches","JavaScript uses a backtracking engine. It tries the pattern at the first position of the text and, when a step fails, goes back to the last choice it made and tries another. If nothing works, it moves one character on and starts again. Quantifiers are greedy by default: they take as much as they can, then give back. Add ? to make them lazy. 🎬 Try it"],[15,"engine","Regular expression tester","Edit the pattern, the flags or the text. Matches light up as you type. / /flags Call 555-1234 or 555-9876. Order ORD-2024 ships today. 2 matches 555-1234 at 5, groups: 5551234 555-9876 at 17, groups: 5559876 Greedy, lazy and how the engine searches · ▶ Runs const html = '<b>Sale</b> and <b>New</b>'; console.log(html.match(/<b>.*<\\/b>/)[0]); // greedy: as much as possible console.log(html.match(/<b>.*?<\\/b>/)[0]); // lazy: as little as possible console.log(html.match(/<b>[^<]*<\\/b>/g)); // a negated class is often clearer and faster// The engine tries every start position, and backtracks inside each oneconst re = /a.*c/; console.log(re.exec(\"xxabcbc\"), \"| found at index\", re.exec(\"xxabcbc\").index); Output <b>Sale</b> and <b>New</b> <b>Sale</b> [ '<b>Sale</b>', '<b>New</b>' ] [ 'abcbc', index: 2, input: 'xxabcbc', groups: undefined ] | found at index 2 The animation shows the same text matched twice. Each \"give back one character\" is a backtracking step. On a short string that costs nothing, but nested quantifiers on a long string can need millions of them, which is the ReDoS problem later in this chapter. 🎬 Try it"],[15,"engine","Greedy against lazy on the same text","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. \"<b>hi</b>\".match(/<.+>/)[0]; // greedy \"<b>hi</b>\".match(/<.+?>/)[0]; // lazy Pattern step Matched so far What happens Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 7 When you can say exactly what is allowed, a negated class such as [^<]* or [^\"]* is clearer and much faster than .*?, because the engine never needs to backtrack. Lesson 2 of 8"],[15,"lookaround","Lookarounds and backreferences","🧪 QA essentialPassword rules, price extraction and duplicate word checks in content are everyday test tasks. A lookaround checks what comes before or after a position without including it in the match. (?=x) and (?!x) look ahead, (?<=x) and (?<!x) look behind. A backreference such as \\1 or \\k<name> matches the same text a group already matched. Lookahead, lookbehind and backreferences · ▶ Runs const prices = \"Pen ₹40, Book ₹250, Bag $30, Mug ₹120\"; console.log(prices.match(/(?<=₹)\\d+/g)); // lookbehind: digits after ₹, without the ₹ console.log(prices.match(/\\b\\w+(?= ₹)/g)); // lookahead: words followed by \" ₹\" console.log(prices.match(/(?<!₹)\\b\\d+/g)); // negative lookbehind: numbers not in rupees// Password rules as lookaheads: each (?=...) checks a rule without moving forwardconst strong = /^(?=.*[a-z])(?=.*[A-Z])(?=.*\\d)(?=.*[^\\w\\s]).{8,}$/; for (const pw of [\"Secret1!\", \"secret1!\", \"Sec1!\", \"Secret12\"]) console.log(pw.padEnd(9), strong.test(pw)); // Backreferences: \\1 matches the same text the first group matched console.log(\"the the test passed passed\".match(/\\b(\\w+) \\1\\b/g)); console.log('<a href=\"x\">'.match(/([\"']).*?\\1/)[0]); Output [ '40', '250', '120' ] [ 'Pen', 'Book', 'Mug' ] [ '30' ] Secret1! true secret1! false Sec1! false Secret12 false [ 'the the', 'passed passed' ] \"x\" Several lookaheads in a row are the standard way to express \"must contain a lower case letter, a capital, a digit and a symbol\". Each rule becomes a separate test case in your password suite. Lesson 3 of 8"],[15,"flags","Flags in depth and the lastIndex trap","🧪 QA essentialA shared /g regex in a test helper makes checks pass and fail in turn, a confusing source of flaky tests. Flags change how the whole pattern works. The g and y flags also make a regex stateful: it remembers lastIndex, the position after the last match. Flags in depth: g, m, s, y and the lastIndex trap · ▶ Runs const log = `INFO start ERROR db timeout info retry ERROR card declined`; console.log(log.match(/^ERROR.*$/gm)); // m: ^ and $ work per line console.log(log.match(/^info.*$/gim).length); // i: ignore case console.log(/start.ERROR/.test(log), /start.ERROR/s.test(log)); // s: dot matches a new line// g with test() remembers lastIndex between calls, a classic bugconst hasError = /ERROR/g; console.log(hasError.test(log), hasError.test(log), hasError.test(log), \"| lastIndex\", hasError.lastIndex); // y (sticky) only matches exactly at lastIndex, which is how tokenisers workconst word = /\\w+/y; word.lastIndex = 6; console.log(word.exec(log)?.[0], (word.lastIndex = 5, word.exec(log))); Output [ 'ERROR db timeout', 'ERROR card declined' ] 2 false true true true false | lastIndex 0 start null Never reuse a regex with the g flag for test() across different strings or calls. The second call starts at the old lastIndex and can fail on text that clearly matches. Drop the g flag for yes or no checks. Lesson 4 of 8"],[15,"unicode","Unicode and every language","🧪 QA essentialInternationalisation testing catches validation that only works for English input. [a-z] only knows English. With the u or v flag, \\p{L} matches a letter in any script, \\p{Script=Devanagari} one script, and \\p{Emoji} emoji. The u flag also treats an emoji as one character instead of two halves. Unicode: letters in every language · ▶ Runs const names = [\"Zoë\", \"Ravi\", \"José\", \"Łukasz\", \"東京\", \"😀 smile\"]; console.log(names.filter(n => /^[a-z]+$/i.test(n))); // ASCII only: drops real names console.log(names.filter(n => /^\\p{L}+$/u.test(n))); // any letter in any script console.log(\"😀\".length, [...\"😀\"].length, /^.$/.test(\"😀\"), /^.$/u.test(\"😀\")); console.log(\"Grüße, Привет, नमस्ते\".match(/\\p{Script=Devanagari}+/u)[0]); console.log(\"café\".normalize(\"NFD\").replace(/\\p{M}/gu, \"\")); // strip accents Output [ 'Ravi' ] [ 'Zoë', 'Ravi', 'José', 'Łukasz', '東京' ] 2 1 false true नमस्ते cafe Test name and address fields with names such as Zoë, José, Łukasz, 東京 and right to left Arabic. A form that rejects real names is a bug that hits real customers. Lesson 5 of 8"],[15,"replace","Replacing, masking and splitting","🧪 QA essentialMasking passwords, card numbers and tokens before logs and reports are saved is a security requirement in test frameworks. replace and replaceAll can use $1, $<name> and $& in the replacement text, or a function that receives every group and returns the new text. Replacing with groups and functions, masking secrets · ▶ Runs // Replace with named groups and with a functionconst iso = \"Shipped 2026-10-18, due 2026-10-25\"; console.log(iso.replace(/(?<y>\\d{4})-(?<m>\\d\\d)-(?<d>\\d\\d)/g, \"$<d>/$<m>/$<y>\")); const cart = \"PEN x2 @40, BOOK x1 @250\"; console.log(cart.replace(/(\\w+) x(\\d+) @(\\d+)/g, (all, sku, qty, price) => `${sku}=${qty * price}`)); // Mask secrets in logs before they are savedconst line = 'POST /login {\"user\":\"asha\",\"password\":\"s3cret\",\"card\":\"4111111111111111\"} token=eyJhbGciOi.abc.def'; const masked = line .replace(/(\"password\":\")[^\"]*/, \"$1***\") .replace(/\\b(\\d{4})\\d{8}(\\d{4})\\b/, \"$1********$2\") .replace(/token=[\\w.-]+/, \"token=[hidden]\"); console.log(masked); // split keeps captured separators console.log(\"a1b22c333\".split(/(\\d+)/)); Output Shipped 18/10/2026, due 25/10/2026 PEN=80, BOOK=250 POST /login {\"user\":\"asha\",\"password\":\"***\",\"card\":\"4111********1111\"} token=[hidden] [ 'a', '1', 'b', '22', 'c', '333', '' ] For anything with structure, such as a whole language of filters, use the sticky flag to read one token at a time from an exact position. Sticky regexes as a tokeniser · ▶ Runs // A tiny tokeniser for filter expressions such as: status == \"failed\" && ms > 1000const rules = [ [\"space\", /\\s+/y], [\"string\", /\"(?:[^\"\\\\]|\\\\.)*\"/y], [\"number\", /\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?/y], [\"op\", /==|!=|>=|<=|&&|\\|\\||[<>!]/y], [\"name\", /[A-Za-z_]\\w*/y], [\"paren\", /[()]/y], ]; function tokenize(src) { const out = []; let pos = 0; outer: while (pos < src.length) { for (const [type, re] of rules) { re.lastIndex = pos; const m = re.exec(src); if (m) { if (type !== \"space\") out.push(`${type}:${m[0]}`); pos = re.lastIndex; continue outer; } } thrownewSyntaxError(`Unexpected \"${src[pos]}\" at ${pos}`); } return out; } console.log(tokenize('status == \"failed\" && ms > 1000')); try { tokenize(\"ms # 2\"); } catch (e) { console.log(e.message); } Output [ 'name:status', 'op:==', 'string:\"failed\"', 'op:&&', 'name:ms', 'op:>', 'number:1000' ] Unexpected \"#\" at 3 Lesson 6 of 8"],[15,"redos","ReDoS and safe patterns","🧪 QA essentialSending long crafted strings to search and validation fields is a standard security and performance test. Some patterns make the engine try an exponential number of ways to split the input. ^(a+)+$ on a long run of \"a\" that ends with \"!\" never finishes in practice. An attacker who can send such input to a server can freeze it with one request. This is called regular expression denial of service. ReDoS: a pattern that takes forever · ▶ Runs // Catastrophic backtracking: nested quantifiers try every way to split the inputconst evil = /^(a+)+$/; const safe = /^a+$/; function time(re, n) { const input = \"a\".repeat(n) + \"!\"; // the ! forces a failure at the endconst start = performance.now(); re.test(input); return performance.now() - start; } time(evil, 5); // warm up, so compiling the regex is not measuredconst t22 = time(evil, 22), t26 = time(evil, 26); console.log(\"4 more characters made it more than 5 times slower:\", t26 / t22 > 5); console.log(\"the safe pattern on 100000 characters is instant:\", time(safe, 100_000) < 50); Output 4 more characters made it more than 5 times slower: true the safe pattern on 100000 characters is instant: true Danger signs: a quantifier inside a quantified group, such as (a+)+, (\\w*)* or (a|aa)+. Safe habits: limit input length first, prefer specific classes, and run the linter rule regexp/no-super-linear-backtracking from eslint-plugin-regexp. Lesson 7 of 8"],[15,"validate","Validation patterns and testing them","🧪 QA essentialRegex validation rules are a frequent source of bugs, and table driven tests show exactly which inputs they accept. A regex can check the shape of text, not its meaning. A date pattern accepts 31 February, and no practical email pattern follows the full standard. Use a regex for the shape, then real code for the rest, such as Temporal.PlainDate.from for dates. Validation patterns and their limits · ▶ Runs // Practical patterns. Each one is a trade off, write tests that show the choice.const patterns = { email: /^[^\\s@]+@[^\\s@]+\\.[^\\s@]{2,}$/, // pragmatic, not the full RFC 5322 isoDate: /^\\d{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])$/, // shape only: 2026-02-31 passes uuid: /^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[1-8][0-9a-f]{3}-[89ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$/i, indianPin: /^[1-9]\\d{5}$/, semver: /^(0|[1-9]\\d*)\\.(0|[1-9]\\d*)\\.(0|[1-9]\\d*)(?:-[\\w.-]+)?$/, }; const samples = { email: [\"asha@example.com\", \"a@b\", \"no spaces@x.io\"], isoDate: [\"2026-10-20\", \"2026-13-01\", \"2026-02-31\"], uuid: [crypto.randomUUID(), \"1234\"], indianPin: [\"560001\", \"056001\"], semver: [\"1.56.1\", \"1.02.0\", \"2.0.0-rc.1\"], }; for (const [name, list] ofObject.entries(samples)) console.log(name.padEnd(9), list.map(s => patterns[name].test(s)).join(\" \")); Output email true false false isoDate true false true uuid true false indianPin true false semver true false true Treat a regex like any other code: keep it in one module and test it with a table of strings that must match and strings that must not. Test a regex with a table of cases · 🧪 Tests run 📄 src/patterns.js exportconst orderId = /^ORD-\\d{6}$/; exportconst price = /^₹(?:0|[1-9]\\d{0,2}(?:,\\d{3})*|[1-9]\\d*)(?:\\.\\d{2})?$/; 📄 test/patterns.test.js import { test } from\"node:test\"; import assert from\"node:assert/strict\"; import { orderId, price } from\"../src/patterns.js\"; // A table of examples that must match and examples that must notconst cases = { orderId: { yes: [\"ORD-000001\", \"ORD-104233\"], no: [\"ord-104233\", \"ORD-10423\", \"ORD-1042333\", \" ORD-104233\"] }, price: { yes: [\"₹0\", \"₹40\", \"₹1,250\", \"₹12,500\", \"₹99.50\"], no: [\"₹040\", \"₹1,25\", \"₹9.5\", \"40\", \"₹-5\"] }, }; for (const [name, { yes, no }] ofObject.entries(cases)) { const re = { orderId, price }[name]; for (const s of yes) test(`${name} accepts ${JSON.stringify(s)}`, () => assert.match(s, re)); for (const s of no) test(`${name} rejects ${JSON.stringify(s)}`, () => assert.doesNotMatch(s, re)); } Terminal $ node --test --test-reporter=spec Terminal output ✔ orderId accepts \"ORD-000001\" ✔ orderId accepts \"ORD-104233\" ✔ orderId rejects \"ord-104233\" ✔ orderId rejects \"ORD-10423\" ✔ orderId rejects \"ORD-1042333\" ✔ orderId rejects \" ORD-104233\" ✔ price accepts \"₹0\" ✔ price accepts \"₹40\" ✔ price accepts \"₹1,250\" ✔ price accepts \"₹12,500\" ✔ price accepts \"₹99.50\" ✔ price rejects \"₹040\" ✔ price rejects \"₹1,25\" ✔ price rejects \"₹9.5\" ✔ price rejects \"40\" ✔ price rejects \"₹-5\" ℹ tests 16 ℹ suites 0 ℹ pass 16 ℹ fail 0 ℹ cancelled 0 ℹ skipped 0 ℹ todo 0 ℹ duration_ms 41.2 Lesson 8 of 8"],[15,"scenario","Real world scenario: mine the access logs","🧪 QA essential The situationAfter a load test, the team wants every failed request and every slow one from the server access log, with the time of each. Parse access logs and find the failures · ▶ Runs // Turn raw access log lines into rows you can checkconst logs = `203.0.113.9 - - [20/Oct/2026:09:14:03 +0000] \"GET /api/products?page=2 HTTP/1.1\" 200 5123 412ms 203.0.113.9 - - [20/Oct/2026:09:14:04 +0000] \"POST /api/orders HTTP/1.1\" 503 87 2210ms 198.51.100.4 - - [20/Oct/2026:09:14:05 +0000] \"GET /health HTTP/1.1\" 200 2 3ms 198.51.100.4 - - [20/Oct/2026:09:14:07 +0000] \"POST /api/orders HTTP/1.1\" 201 312 640ms garbage line`; constLINE = /^(?<ip>\\S+) \\S+ \\S+ \\[(?<time>[^\\]]+)\\] \"(?<method>[A-Z]+) (?<path>[^ ?\"]+)(?:\\?[^ \"]*)? HTTP\\/[\\d.]+\" (?<status>\\d{3}) (?<bytes>\\d+) (?<ms>\\d+)ms$/; const rows = [], bad = []; for (const line of logs.split(\"\\n\")) { const m = LINE.exec(line); if (m) rows.push({ ...m.groups, status: +m.groups.status, ms: +m.groups.ms }); else bad.push(line); } const errors = rows.filter(r => r.status >= 500); const slow = rows.filter(r => r.ms > 1000 && r.path !== \"/health\"); console.log(rows.length, \"parsed,\", bad.length, \"rejected:\", bad); console.log(\"5xx:\", errors.map(r => `${r.method} ${r.path} at ${r.time.split(\":\").slice(1, 4).join(\":\")}`)); console.log(\"slow:\", slow.map(r => `${r.path} ${r.ms}ms`), \"| p50 of orders:\", rows.filter(r => r.path === \"/api/orders\").map(r => r.ms).toSorted((a, b) => a - b)[0], \"ms\"); Output 4 parsed, 1 rejected: [ 'garbage line' ] 5xx: [ 'POST /api/orders at 09:14:04 +0000' ] slow: [ '/api/orders 2210ms' ] | p50 of orders: 640 ms One pattern with named groups turns each line into an object. Lines that do not match are counted instead of silently dropped, which tells you when the log format changes. The same idea works for test runner output, browser console logs and CI logs. 🧮"],[15,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 How many matches does /\\d+/g find in \"PEN 2, BOOK 10, BAG 3\"? Runs of digits. Answer: 3 2, 10 and 3. C2 /^\\d{6}$/ accepts PIN codes. How many digits must the input have? Exactly. Answer: 6 {6} means exactly six, and ^ $ forbid anything else. C1 How many strings of only \"a\" and \"b\" of length 3 does /^[ab]{3}$/ accept? Two choices per position. Answer: 8 23 = 8 C2 A password must have 4 kinds of character. A test suite checks each missing kind once, plus one valid password and one too short. How many cases? One per rule plus two. Answer: 6 4 + 1 + 1 = 6 C3 A card number 4111111111111111 is masked to keep the first 4 and last 4 digits. How many asterisks? 16 digits in total. Answer: 8 16 − 4 − 4 = 8 C1 With ^(a+)+$, the number of ways to split n letters doubles with each extra letter. Going from 22 to 26 letters multiplies the work by how much? Four doublings. Answer: 16 24 = 16 C2 In the log scenario, how many of the 5 lines failed to match the pattern? Only the garbage line. Answer: 1 Four real log lines parsed, one rejected. C1 A tokeniser reads status == \"failed\" && ms > 1000 and skips spaces. How many tokens does it produce? Count names, operators, values. Answer: 7 status, ==, \"failed\", &&, ms, >, 1000. 🎯"],[15,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 What does the lazy pattern match? \"<b>A</b><b>B</b>\".match(/<b>.*?<\\/b>/)[0]; Lazy. The ? after * stops at the first closing tag. Q2 Which flag makes ^ and $ work on every line? Multiline. s is for the dot, g for all matches. Q3 Which pattern matches a letter in any language? Unicode property. \\w only covers A to Z, digits and underscore. Q1 What does (?<=₹)\\d+ return from \"₹40\"? Lookbehind does not consume. The ₹ is checked but left out of the match. Q2 Why can calling test() twice on the same /x/g regex give different results? State. Drop g for yes or no checks, or reset lastIndex. Q3 What does \\1 mean in /(\\w+) \\1/? Backreference. It finds repeated words such as \"the the\". Q4 In replace(re, fn), what does fn receive after the full match? Groups first. Then the offset, the whole string and the named groups object. Q1 Which pattern is at risk of catastrophic backtracking? Nested quantifier. The engine tries every way to split the run of a characters. Q2 What does the y flag do? Sticky. That is how tokenisers read one token at a time. Q3 An ISO date regex accepts 2026-02-31. What is the right fix? Shape against meaning. Temporal.PlainDate.from rejects 31 February. Q1 Why is \"😀\".length 2? Surrogate pairs. Spread or the u flag count code points instead. Q2 What does \"a1b22\".split(/(\\d+)/) return? Captured separators stay. A capture group in split keeps the separators in the result."],[15,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[15,"prices","Extract rupee prices","Write rupees(text) that returns every rupee amount in the text as numbers. Amounts look like ₹40 or ₹1,250. Ignore amounts in other currencies. matchAll with a group for the digits and commas, then remove the commas. function rupees(text) { return [...text.matchAll(/₹([\\d,]+)/g)].map(m => Number(m[1].replaceAll(\",\", \"\"))); } matchAll returns an iterator, and spreading it gives every match with its groups. 🌿 Medium"],[15,"policy","A password policy","Write isStrong(pw) that returns true when the password has at least 8 characters, a lower case letter, a capital, a digit and a symbol (anything that is not a letter, digit or space). One lookahead per rule, then .{8,} for the length. function isStrong(pw) { return /^(?=.*[a-z])(?=.*[A-Z])(?=.*\\d)(?=.*[^\\w\\s]).{8,}$/.test(pw); } Each failing test in the list misses exactly one rule, which is how you design a password test suite. 🔥 Hard"],[15,"mask","Mask card numbers","Write maskCards(text) that replaces every run of 13 to 19 digits with the first 4 digits, then one * per hidden digit, then the last 4 digits. Leave shorter numbers alone. Three groups: 4 digits, the middle, 4 digits. A replace function can count the middle. function maskCards(text) { return text.replace(/\\b(\\d{4})(\\d{5,11})(\\d{4})\\b/g, (all, a, mid, b) => a + \"*\".repeat(mid.length) + b); } A function replacement is the only way to make the number of stars depend on the input. 🧠 Expert"],[15,"kv","Parse key=value pairs","Write parsePairs(text) that reads pairs such as env=staging retries=2 name=\"smoke run\" into an object. Values in double quotes may contain spaces. Values that are whole numbers become numbers. An alternation: a quoted value \"([^\"]*)\" or a plain one (\\S+). Only one of the two groups is filled. function parsePairs(text) { const out = {}; for (const m of text.matchAll(/(\\w+)=(?:\"([^\"]*)\"|(\\S+))/g)) { const raw = m[2] ?? m[3]; out[m[1]] = /^-?\\d+$/.test(raw) ? Number(raw) : raw; } return out; } CLI flags, test tags and environment strings all use this format."],[15,"kv","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. How the regex engine matchesJavaScript uses a backtracking engine. It tries the pattern at the first position of the text and, when a step fails, goes back to the last choice it made and tries another. If nothing works, it moves one character on and starts again. Lookarounds and backreferencesA lookaround checks what comes before or after a position without including it in the match. (?=x) and (?!x) look ahead, (?<=x) and (?<!x) look behind. A backreference such as \\1 or \\k<name> matches the same text a group already matched. Flags in depth and the lastIndex trapFlags change how the whole pattern works. The g and y flags also make a regex stateful: it remembers lastIndex, the position after the last match. Unicode and every language[a-z] only knows English. With the u or v flag, \\p{L} matches a letter in any script, \\p{Script=Devanagari} one script, and \\p{Emoji} emoji. The u flag also treats an emoji as one character instead of two halves. Replacing, masking and splittingreplace and replaceAll can use $1, $<name> and $& in the replacement text, or a function that receives every group and returns the new text. ReDoS and safe patternsSome patterns make the engine try an exponential number of ways to split the input. ^(a+)+$ on a long run of \"a\" that ends with \"!\" never finishes in practice. An attacker who can send such input to a server can freeze it with one request. Validation patterns and testing themA regex can check the shape of text, not its meaning. A date pattern accepts 31 February, and no practical email pattern follows the full standard. Use a regex for the shape, then real code for the rest, such as Temporal.PlainDate.from for dates. Real world scenario: mine the access logsOne pattern with named groups turns each line into an object. Lines that do not match are counted instead of silently dropped, which tells you when the log format changes. The same idea works for test runner output, browser console logs and CI logs."],[15,"comic","Patterns with power","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Know how the engine backtracks, drop g for yes or no checks, use \\p{L} for names, and test every pattern with a table of cases."],[15,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🧑‍🤝‍🧑A sign up form Rejected the name \"Zoë\" because its pattern was [A-Za-z]+. QA added names from twelve languages to the test data and the pattern became \\p{L}. 🔥A web API outage Was caused by a regex with nested quantifiers in a user agent check. One long header froze every worker for minutes. 🧾A test reporting tool Masks passwords, card numbers and tokens with three replace calls before any log is uploaded to the dashboard. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Regular expressions in depth\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 10Welcome This chapter goes deep into regular expressions: how the engine backtracks, lookarounds, flags, unicode, replacing, tokenising, ReDoS and testing patterns. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[16,"","Functional programming in JavaScript","Pure functions and injecting the clock, freeze, deep freeze and immutable updates, curry, partial application, pipe and compose, eager arrays against lazy iterator helpers, recursion over JSON, trampolines and deep equality, and a composable validator that reports the JSON path of each wrong field. · functional programming · pure function · side effects · immutability · Object.freeze · currying · partial application · compose · pipe · lazy evaluation · recursion · trampoline · validator · interview 🟨 JavaScript›🔬 JavaScript in depth›Chapter 17 of 31 🧩 Step 5 · Topic 4 of 6 · Advanced"],[16,"top","","Write code that is simple to test: pure functions with side effects pushed to the edges, immutability and copy on change updates, currying, partial application and composition, lazy pipelines, recursion on real JSON trees, and an API validator built from tiny functions. ⏱️ About 16 min read📚 6 parts🎬 1 animations▶ 6 runnable examples🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 4 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Functional habits make code testable and test code reliable: inject the clock, never change shared fixtures, and build checks from small reusable functions. 1Pure functions and side effects 2Immutability 3Currying, partial application and composition 4Eager and lazy collections 5Recursion on real data 6Real world scenario: a validator built from tiny functions Lesson 1 of 6"],[16,"pure","Pure functions and side effects","🧪 QA essentialHidden clocks and random numbers inside functions are a top cause of flaky unit tests. A pure function returns the same output for the same input and changes nothing outside itself: no global variables, no network, no clock, no random numbers. Pure functions are the easiest code in the world to test, because a test is just input and expected output. Pure functions and pushing side effects to the edge · ▶ Runs // Impure: reads the clock and changes something outside itselflet lastDiscount; function discountImpure(total) { const isSale = newDate().getUTCDay() === 5; // depends on when you run it lastDiscount = isSale ? total * 0.2 : 0; // side effectreturn total - lastDiscount; } // Pure: the same inputs always give the same output, and nothing else changesconst discount = (total, day) => (day === 5 ? total * 0.8 : total); console.log(discount(500, 5), discount(500, 5), discount(500, 2)); // Push the impure part to the edge and pass it in, then tests can choose the dayconst todayFrom = clock => clock().getUTCDay(); const fakeFriday = () => newDate(\"2026-10-23T10:00:00Z\"); console.log(\"on a Friday:\", discount(500, todayFrom(fakeFriday))); Output 400 400 500 on a Friday: 400 Real programs need side effects, so the functional style keeps them at the edges. Pass the clock, the random generator or the HTTP client in as arguments, and the core logic stays pure. If a function is hard to test, look for a hidden side effect: Date.now(), Math.random(), a global config or a direct fetch. Ask the developers to inject it, and the flaky test becomes a simple one. Lesson 2 of 6"],[16,"immutable","Immutability","🧪 QA essentialShared test data that one test changes is a classic cause of tests that pass alone and fail together. Data that never changes cannot be broken by another part of the program. const only stops a variable from being reassigned. Object.freeze stops an object from changing, but only one level deep. For updates, build a new object that copies the path you change and shares everything else. Immutability: freeze, deep freeze and copy on change · ▶ Runs \"use strict\"; const config = Object.freeze({ baseURL: \"https://staging.example.test\", retries: 2, users: { admin: \"asha\" } }); try { config.retries = 5; } catch (e) { console.log(e.constructor.name + \":\", e.message.split(\" of \")[0]); } config.users.admin = \"mallory\"; // freeze is shallow: nested objects still change console.log(config.users.admin); function deepFreeze(obj) { for (const value ofObject.values(obj)) if (typeof value === \"object\" && value !== null) deepFreeze(value); returnObject.freeze(obj); } const safe = deepFreeze({ users: { admin: \"asha\" }, tags: [\"smoke\"] }); try { safe.tags.push(\"slow\"); } catch (e) { console.log(\"deep frozen:\", e.constructor.name); } // Immutable updates: copy the path you change, share the restconst state = { cart: { items: [{ sku: \"PEN\", qty: 1 }], coupon: null }, user: { name: \"Asha\" } }; const next = { ...state, cart: { ...state.cart, items: state.cart.items.map(i => (i.sku === \"PEN\" ? { ...i, qty: i.qty + 1 } : i)) } }; console.log(state.cart.items[0].qty, next.cart.items[0].qty, \"| user shared:\", state.user === next.user); const copy = structuredClone(state); // a full deep copy, including Dates and Maps console.log(\"deep copy is separate:\", copy.cart !== state.cart); Output TypeError: Cannot assign to read only property 'retries' mallory deep frozen: TypeError 1 2 | user shared: true deep copy is separate: true This copy on change style is how React, Redux and many state libraries know something changed: a new object means new data, so a simple === check is enough. Lesson 3 of 6"],[16,"compose","Currying, partial application and composition","Currying turns f(a, b, c) into f(a)(b)(c). Fixing the first few arguments, called partial application, creates specialised functions from a general one. Composition builds a function out of smaller ones: pipe runs them left to right, compose right to left. Currying, partial application and composition · ▶ Runs // Curry: a function of many arguments becomes a chain of one argument functionsconst curry = fn => function curried(...args) { return args.length >= fn.length ? fn.apply(this, args) : (...more) => curried.apply(this, [...args, ...more]); }; const withinTolerance = curry((tolerance, expected, actual) => Math.abs(actual - expected) <= tolerance); const aboutRight = withinTolerance(0.01); // partial application: fix the toleranceconst isTotal = aboutRight(179.1); console.log(isTotal(179.1), isTotal(179.104), isTotal(179.2), withinTolerance(1, 10, 10.5)); // Compose and pipe: build bigger functions from small onesconst compose = (...fns) => x => fns.reduceRight((v, f) => f(v), x); const pipe = (...fns) => x => fns.reduce((v, f) => f(v), x); const trim = s => s.trim(), lower = s => s.toLowerCase(), squash = s => s.replace(/\\s+/g, \" \"); const normalise = pipe(trim, squash, lower); console.log(JSON.stringify(normalise(\" Add TO Cart \")), compose(lower, trim)(\" OK \") === pipe(trim, lower)(\" OK \")); // Point free style: pass functions without naming their argumentsconst labels = [\" Login \", \"CHECKOUT\", \" search\"].map(normalise); console.log(labels); Output true true false true \"add to cart\" true [ 'login', 'checkout', 'search' ] Composition is easiest to understand by watching one value travel through it. Each function gets the previous function's output, so every step must take one value and return one value. 🎬 Try it"],[16,"compose","A value flowing through pipe","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. const pipe = (...fns) => x => fns.reduce((v, f) => f(v), x); const cleanEmail = pipe( s => s.trim(), s => s.toLowerCase(), s => s.replace(/\\+.*@/, \"@\"), ); cleanEmail(\" Asha+test@Mail.com \"); Function Value in Value out Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 5 Lesson 4 of 6"],[16,"lazy","Eager and lazy collections","Array methods are eager: each step creates a full new array. Iterator helpers from ES2025 are lazy: they pass one item at a time through all steps and stop as soon as take has enough. On large data that can be thousands of times less work. Lazy pipelines with iterator helpers · ▶ Runs // Eager arrays build a full new array at every steplet calls = 0; const big = Array.from({ length: 100_000 }, (_, i) => i); const eager = big.map(n => (calls++, n * 2)).filter(n => n % 3 === 0).slice(0, 3); console.log(eager, \"eager calls:\", calls); // Lazy iterators pull one item through all steps, and stop when they have enough calls = 0; const lazy = big.values().map(n => (calls++, n * 2)).filter(n => n % 3 === 0).take(3).toArray(); console.log(lazy, \"lazy calls:\", calls); // reduce builds any shape: here an index by id in one passconst products = [{ id: \"p1\", price: 40 }, { id: \"p2\", price: 250 }]; const byId = products.reduce((acc, p) => ({ ...acc, [p.id]: p }), {}); console.log(Object.keys(byId), byId.p2.price); // flatMap maps and flattens one level, often replacing a filter then mapconst orders = [{ id: 1, items: [\"PEN\", \"INK\"] }, { id: 2, items: [] }, { id: 3, items: [\"BAG\"] }]; console.log(orders.flatMap(o => o.items.map(sku => `${o.id}:${sku}`))); Output [ 0, 6, 12 ] eager calls: 100000 [ 0, 6, 12 ] lazy calls: 7 [ 'p1', 'p2' ] 250 [ '1:PEN', '1:INK', '3:BAG' ] Lesson 5 of 6"],[16,"recursion","Recursion on real data","🧪 QA essentialSearching a nested API response for every id or every price is a frequent API test task. JSON responses, the DOM and file systems are trees, and recursion is the natural way to walk a tree. Very deep recursion can overflow the stack, and a trampoline avoids that by returning a function instead of calling it, so a loop does the calls one at a time. Recursion on trees, trampolines and deep equality · ▶ Runs // Walk any JSON response and collect every value under a given key, at any depthfunction collect(node, key, out = []) { if (Array.isArray(node)) node.forEach(child => collect(child, key, out)); elseif (node && typeof node === \"object\") for (const [k, v] ofObject.entries(node)) { if (k === key) out.push(v); collect(v, key, out); } return out; } const response = { id: 1, order: { id: 7, lines: [{ id: 70, sku: \"PEN\" }, { id: 71, sku: \"INK\", bundle: { id: 710 } }] } }; console.log(collect(response, \"id\")); // Deep recursion can overflow the stack. A trampoline runs it in a loop instead.const trampoline = fn => (...args) => { let r = fn(...args); while (typeof r === \"function\") r = r(); return r; }; const sumTo = trampoline(function sum(n, acc = 0) { return n === 0 ? acc : () => sum(n - 1, acc + n); }); console.log(sumTo(1_000_000)); // Structural equality, the core of every deepEqual assertionconst equal = (a, b) => a === b || (typeof a === \"object\" && typeof b === \"object\" && a && b && Object.keys(a).length === Object.keys(b).length && Object.keys(a).every(k => equal(a[k], b[k]))); console.log(equal({ a: [1, { b: 2 }] }, { a: [1, { b: 2 }] }), equal({ a: 1 }, { a: \"1\" })); Output [ 1, 7, 70, 71, 710 ] 500000500000 true false The equal function above is a tiny version of what assert.deepStrictEqual, Jest's toEqual and Chai's deep.equal do, with extra care for Dates, Maps, Sets, cycles and prototypes. Lesson 6 of 6"],[16,"scenario","Real world scenario: a validator built from tiny functions","🧪 QA essential The situationThe API test suite needs to check the shape of order responses, and every failure must say exactly which field is wrong, such as $.lines[1].sku. Composable validators for API responses · ▶ Runs // Small checks that combine into a full API response validatorconst check = (name, test) => (value, path = \"$\") => (test(value) ? [] : [`${path}: expected ${name}, got ${JSON.stringify(value)}`]); const isString = check(\"a string\", v => typeof v === \"string\"); const isPositive = check(\"a number above 0\", v => typeof v === \"number\" && v > 0); const matches = re => check(`text matching ${re}`, v => typeof v === \"string\" && re.test(v)); const oneOf = (...vals) => check(`one of ${vals.join(\"|\")}`, v => vals.includes(v)); const shape = spec => (value, path = \"$\") => value === null || typeof value !== \"object\" ? [`${path}: expected an object`] : Object.entries(spec).flatMap(([key, rule]) => rule(value[key], `${path}.${key}`)); const listOf = rule => (value, path = \"$\") => Array.isArray(value) ? value.flatMap((item, i) => rule(item, `${path}[${i}]`)) : [`${path}: expected an array`]; const orderSchema = shape({ id: matches(/^ORD-\\d{6}$/), status: oneOf(\"new\", \"paid\", \"shipped\"), lines: listOf(shape({ sku: isString, qty: isPositive, price: isPositive })), }); const good = { id: \"ORD-104233\", status: \"paid\", lines: [{ sku: \"PEN\", qty: 2, price: 40 }] }; const bad = { id: \"104233\", status: \"lost\", lines: [{ sku: \"PEN\", qty: 0, price: 40 }, { qty: 1, price: \"9\" }] }; console.log(orderSchema(good)); console.log(orderSchema(bad)); Output [] [ '$.id: expected text matching /^ORD-\\\\d{6}$/, got \"104233\"', '$.status: expected one of new|paid|shipped, got \"lost\"', '$.lines[0].qty: expected a number above 0, got 0', '$.lines[1].sku: expected a string, got undefined', '$.lines[1].price: expected a number above 0, got \"9\"' ] Each rule is a small pure function that returns a list of problems, and shape and listOf combine them. This is the same design that Zod, Yup, Joi and Superstruct use, and it shows why functional style suits test code: small parts, easy to combine, easy to test. 🧮"],[16,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 pipe(x => x + 1, x => x * 2)(3) gives? Left to right. Answer: 8 (3 + 1) × 2 = 8 C2 compose(x => x + 1, x => x * 2)(3) gives? Right to left. Answer: 7 3 × 2 + 1 = 7 C1 A curried function of 3 arguments is called as f(1)(2)(3). How many calls happen in total? One per argument. Answer: 3 f(1), then (2), then (3). C2 map then filter then slice(0, 3) on 1000 items. How many times does the map callback run? Eager. Answer: 1000 map runs on every item before filter starts. C3 Lazy: n => n * 2, keep multiples of 3, take 3, starting from 0. How many map calls? Track which pass. Answer: 7 0, 6 and 12 come from n = 0, 3 and 6. n runs from 0 to 6, which is 7 calls. C1 sumTo(1000) with the trampoline returns? n(n + 1) ÷ 2. Answer: 500500 1000 × 1001 ÷ 2 = 500,500 C2 How many id values does collect(response, \"id\") find in the recursion example? Count every level. Answer: 5 1, 7, 70, 71 and 710. C1 The bad order in the scenario has 2 lines. How many problems does the validator report in total? id, status, then each line. Answer: 5 id and status give 2. Line 0 has qty 0, line 1 misses sku and has a string price. 2 + 1 + 2 = 5 🎯"],[16,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 Which function is pure? Same input, same output. The others read the clock, change outside data or are random. Q2 What does Object.freeze do to nested objects? One level. Write a deepFreeze or use structuredClone for deep safety. Q3 What does pipe(f, g)(x) compute? Left to right. compose(f, g)(x) is f(g(x)). Q1 What is partial application? Specialise. withinTolerance(0.01) gives a tolerance specific check. Q2 Why are pure functions easy to test? No setup. No mocks, no clocks, no network. Q3 An immutable update to state.cart.items creates new copies of which objects? The changed path. Unchanged branches such as state.user are shared. Q4 What does fn.length return? Arity. curry uses it to know when enough arguments have arrived. Q1 Why are iterator helpers faster than map then filter then slice on a large array with take(3)? Lazy. Eager arrays process every item at every step. Q2 What problem does a trampoline solve? Return a function, loop it. The stack never grows beyond one frame. Q3 Why is shared mutable test data dangerous? Order dependency. Give each test its own copy or freeze the shared data. Q1 In the validator scenario, why does each rule return an array instead of throwing? Report everything. flatMap combines the lists of problems from every rule. Q2 Which pair is equal under the equal() function on this page? Same structure and values. Strict equality is used on leaves, so 1 and \"1\" differ."],[16,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[16,"pipe","Write pipe","Write pipe(...fns) that returns a function which passes its input through every function from left to right. With no functions it returns the input unchanged. reduce over the functions, starting with the input. function pipe(...fns) { return x => fns.reduce((value, fn) => fn(value), x); } compose is the same with reduceRight. 🌿 Medium"],[16,"deepfreeze","Deep freeze","Write deepFreeze(obj) that freezes the object and every nested object and array, then returns it. Recurse into every value that is an object, and remember typeof null is \"object\". function deepFreeze(obj) { for (const value ofObject.values(obj)) { if (typeof value === \"object\" && value !== null) deepFreeze(value); } returnObject.freeze(obj); } Shared fixtures frozen this way throw in strict mode the moment a test tries to change them. 🔥 Hard"],[16,"addqty","An immutable cart update","Write addQty(state, sku, n) that returns a new state where the item with that sku has qty + n. Do not change the original state, and keep state.user as the same object. Spread each level on the path: state, cart, the items array, the one item. function addQty(state, sku, n) { return { ...state, cart: { ...state.cart, items: state.cart.items.map(i => (i.sku === sku ? { ...i, qty: i.qty + n } : i)), }, }; } Unchanged items stay the same objects, so a UI can skip re-rendering them. 🧠 Expert"],[16,"findkey","Find every value of a key","Write findAll(node, key) that returns every value stored under key anywhere inside nested objects and arrays, in the order a depth first walk meets them. An inner walk function that handles arrays, objects and everything else. function findAll(node, key) { const out = []; const walk = n => { if (Array.isArray(n)) n.forEach(walk); elseif (n && typeof n === \"object\") { for (const [k, v] ofObject.entries(n)) { if (k === key) out.push(v); walk(v); } } }; walk(node); return out; } API tests use this to check that every price in a response is positive, wherever it appears."],[16,"findkey","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. Pure functions and side effectsA pure function returns the same output for the same input and changes nothing outside itself: no global variables, no network, no clock, no random numbers. ImmutabilityData that never changes cannot be broken by another part of the program. const only stops a variable from being reassigned. Object.freeze stops an object from changing, but only one level deep. Currying, partial application and compositionCurrying turns f(a, b, c) into f(a)(b)(c). Fixing the first few arguments, called partial application, creates specialised functions from a general one. Composition builds a function out of smaller ones: pipe runs them left to right, compose right to left. Eager and lazy collectionsArray methods are eager: each step creates a full new array. Iterator helpers from ES2025 are lazy: they pass one item at a time through all steps and stop as soon as take has enough. On large data that can be thousands of times less work. Recursion on real dataJSON responses, the DOM and file systems are trees, and recursion is the natural way to walk a tree. Very deep recursion can overflow the stack, and a trampoline avoids that by returning a function instead of calling it, so a loop does the calls one at a time. Real world scenario: a validator built from tiny functionsEach rule is a small pure function that returns a list of problems, and shape and listOf combine them."],[16,"comic","Small functions, combined","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Keep the core pure, push effects to the edges, never change shared data, and build big checks from small functions."],[16,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🧾An invoicing service Moved tax rules into pure functions with the date passed in. Its 600 unit tests went from flaky around midnight to stable. 🧪An API test framework Builds every response check from tiny validators like those on this page, and prints the JSON path of each wrong field. 🛒A shopping cart Uses copy on change updates for its state, so the UI tests can compare snapshots before and after an action with ===. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Functional programming in JavaScript\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 8Welcome This chapter covers functional programming: pure functions, immutability, currying and composition, lazy pipelines, recursion and composable validators. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[17,"","Objects in depth and metaprogramming","Descriptor flags, getters, setters and the three levels of locking, well known symbols and hiding secrets from logs, Proxy and Reflect for strict config, negative indexes and usage tracking, a proxy that logs and retries page object steps, tagged templates for HTML and SQL, decorators, and a fluent API client. · property descriptor · defineProperty · getter · setter · Object.seal · Symbol.toPrimitive · util.inspect.custom · Proxy · Reflect · trap · tagged template · String.raw · decorators · spy · interview 🟨 JavaScript›🔬 JavaScript in depth›Chapter 18 of 31 🪞 Step 5 · Topic 5 of 6 · Advanced"],[17,"top","","Change how objects behave: property descriptors, getters and setters, freeze, seal and preventExtensions, well known symbols such as toPrimitive and util.inspect.custom, Proxy and Reflect for validation, logging and retries, tagged templates for safe HTML and SQL, and decorators. ⏱️ About 17 min read📚 6 parts🎬 1 animations▶ 6 runnable examples🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 4 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Mocks, spies, fluent clients and step reporters are all metaprogramming. This chapter shows how they work so you can build and debug them. 1Property descriptors, getters and setters 2Well known symbols 3Proxy and Reflect 4Tagged template literals 5Decorators 6Real world scenario: a fluent API client Lesson 1 of 6"],[17,"descriptors","Property descriptors, getters and setters","Every property has a descriptor with flags. writable allows changes, enumerable shows it in Object.keys, loops and JSON, and configurable allows deleting it or changing its flags. Accessor properties use get and set functions instead of a value. Property descriptors, getters, setters and locking · ▶ Runs \"use strict\"; const user = { name: \"Asha\" }; Object.defineProperty(user, \"id\", { value: 7, writable: false, enumerable: false, configurable: false }); console.log(Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(user, \"name\")); console.log(Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(user, \"id\")); console.log(Object.keys(user), JSON.stringify(user), user.id); // not enumerable: hidden from keys and JSONtry { user.id = 8; } catch (e) { console.log(e.constructor.name, \"on write\") } try { delete user.id; } catch (e) { console.log(e.constructor.name, \"on delete\") } // Getters and setters: computed and validated propertiesconst cart = { items: [{ price: 40, qty: 2 }, { price: 250, qty: 1 }], get total() { returnthis.items.reduce((s, i) => s + i.price * i.qty, 0); }, set coupon(code) { if (!/^[A-Z]+\\d+$/.test(code)) thrownewRangeError(`bad coupon ${code}`); this._coupon = code; }, }; console.log(\"total\", cart.total); cart.coupon = \"SAVE10\"; try { cart.coupon = \"nope\"; } catch (e) { console.log(e.message); } // The three levels of lockingconst levels = { preventExtensions: Object.preventExtensions, seal: Object.seal, freeze: Object.freeze }; for (const [name, lock] ofObject.entries(levels)) { const o = lock({ a: 1 }); const can = op => { try { op(o); return\"yes\"; } catch { return\"no\"; } }; console.log(name.padEnd(17), \"add:\", can(o => (o.b = 2)), \" change:\", can(o => (o.a = 9)), \" delete:\", can(o => delete o.a)); } Output { value: 'Asha', writable: true, enumerable: true, configurable: true } { value: 7, writable: false, enumerable: false, configurable: false } [ 'name' ] {\"name\":\"Asha\"} 7 TypeError on write TypeError on delete total 330 bad coupon nope preventExtensions add: no change: yes delete: yes seal add: no change: yes delete: no freeze add: no change: no delete: no This is why a hidden property can be on an object you are testing but missing from JSON.stringify and toEqual comparisons. Use Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors when an object behaves strangely. Lesson 2 of 6"],[17,"symbols","Well known symbols","🧪 QA essentialHiding secrets from logs and reports is a must for test frameworks that log every object. Built in operations look for special symbol keys on your objects. Symbol.toPrimitive controls conversion to numbers and strings, Symbol.hasInstance controls instanceof, Symbol.toStringTag the type label, and Symbol.iterator (chapter 8) makes an object loopable. Node.js adds util.inspect.custom for how an object is logged. Well known symbols change built in behaviour · ▶ Runs import util from\"node:util\"; classMoney { constructor(amount, currency) { this.amount = amount; this.currency = currency; } // Decide what the object becomes in maths, in strings and by default [Symbol.toPrimitive](hint) { return hint === \"number\" ? this.amount : `${this.currency} ${this.amount.toFixed(2)}`; } get [Symbol.toStringTag]() { return\"Money\"; } } const price = newMoney(49.5, \"INR\"); console.log(+price * 2, `${price}`, Object.prototype.toString.call(price)); // Control instanceof for duck typingclassThenable { static [Symbol.hasInstance](x) { returntypeof x?.then === \"function\"; } } console.log(Promise.resolve() instanceofThenable, {} instanceofThenable); // Hide secrets when objects are logged in Node.jsclassCredentials { constructor(user, password) { this.user = user; this.password = password; } [util.inspect.custom]() { return`Credentials { user: '${this.user}', password: '***' }`; } toJSON() { return { user: this.user }; } } const login = newCredentials(\"asha\", \"s3cret\"); console.log(login, JSON.stringify(login)); Output 99 INR 49.50 [object Money] true false Credentials { user: 'asha', password: '***' } {\"user\":\"asha\"} Give credential and token objects a custom inspect and a toJSON that leave the secret out. Test reports, CI logs and screenshots of the console then never leak a password. Lesson 3 of 6"],[17,"proxy","Proxy and Reflect","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightSeleniumProxies power auto mocks and step logging, and let you add retries or reporting to every page object at once. A Proxy sits in front of an object and can intercept almost anything done to it: reading, writing, deleting, in, listing keys, calling and new. Each handler function is a trap. Reflect has a method for every trap that does the normal behaviour, so a trap can add checks and then hand over. Proxy and Reflect: intercept property access · ▶ Runs // A Proxy wraps an object and intercepts operations through \"traps\"const strictConfig = target => newProxy(target, { get(obj, key, receiver) { if (typeof key === \"string\" && !(key in obj)) thrownewReferenceError(`unknown config key \"${key}\"`); returnReflect.get(obj, key, receiver); }, set(obj, key, value) { if (key === \"timeout\" && !(Number.isInteger(value) && value > 0)) thrownewTypeError(\"timeout must be a positive integer\"); returnReflect.set(obj, key, value); }, }); const config = strictConfig({ baseURL: \"http://localhost:3000\", timeout: 5000 }); console.log(config.timeout); try { config.timout; } catch (e) { console.log(e.message); } // typo caught at oncetry { config.timeout = \"5s\"; } catch (e) { console.log(e.message); } // Negative indexes for arraysconst last = arr => newProxy(arr, { get: (a, k, r) => Reflect.get(a, typeof k === \"string\" && /^-\\d+$/.test(k) ? a.length + Number(k) : k, r) }); console.log(last([\"login\", \"search\", \"checkout\"])[-1]); // Count reads to find which settings are really usedconst reads = newMap(); const watched = newProxy({ a: 1, b: 2 }, { get(o, k) { reads.set(k, (reads.get(k) ?? 0) + 1); return o[k]; } }); watched.a; watched.a; watched.b; console.log(Object.fromEntries(reads)); Output 5000 unknown config key \"timout\" timeout must be a positive integer checkout { a: 2, b: 1 } The key idea is that the proxy and the target are two different objects. Code talks to the proxy, every operation is handed to a trap if there is one, and the trap decides whether to pass it on to the real object with Reflect. 🎬 Try it"],[17,"proxy","Every access goes through the Proxy first","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. const target = { price: 100 }; const guarded = newProxy(target, { get(t, key, r) { log.push(`read ${key}`); returnReflect.get(t, key, r); }, set(t, key, v, r) { if (v < 0) thrownewRangeError(\"negative\"); returnReflect.set(t, key, v, r); }, }); guarded.price; guarded.price = 120; guarded.price = -5; Your code Trap Target object Log Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 4 Test frameworks use proxies for auto mocking, for fluent APIs and for wrapping page objects. Here a Proxy adds step logging and one retry on a known flaky error to every method, without touching the page class. A Proxy that logs and retries page object steps · ▶ Runs // Wrap every page object method: log the step and its time, and retry once on a known flaky errorfunction instrument(page, { log = console.log } = {}) { returnnewProxy(page, { get(target, key, receiver) { const value = Reflect.get(target, key, receiver); if (typeof value !== \"function\") return value; returnasync (...args) => { const label = `${target.constructor.name}.${String(key)}(${args.map(a => JSON.stringify(a)).join(\", \")})`; for (let attempt = 1; ; attempt++) { try { const result = await value.apply(receiver, args); log(`✔ ${label}${attempt > 1 ? ` after ${attempt} tries` : \"\"}`); return result; } catch (e) { if (attempt === 2 || !/detached|stale/i.test(e.message)) { log(`✖ ${label}: ${e.message}`); throw e; } } } }; }, }); } classSearchPage { clicks = 0; async search(term) { if (++this.clicks === 1) thrownewError(\"element is detached from the DOM\"); return`${term}: 3 results`; } async openResult(n) { if (n > 3) thrownewError(`result ${n} not found`); return`opened ${n}`; } } const page = instrument(newSearchPage()); console.log(await page.search(\"pen\")); await page.openResult(9).catch(() => {}); Output ✔ SearchPage.search(\"pen\") after 2 tries pen: 3 results ✖ SearchPage.openResult(9): result 9 not found Proxies add a cost to every operation and can surprise people reading the code. Use them in tools and test frameworks, not in hot paths of application code. Lesson 4 of 6"],[17,"tagged","Tagged template literals","🧪 QA essentialKnowing how query tags keep values apart from SQL text explains why injection tests pass or fail. A tag is a function placed before a template literal. It receives the fixed text pieces and the values separately, so it can escape values, build safe queries or change the text. Libraries such as lit-html, styled-components and the gql tag for GraphQL are built on this. Tagged templates: safe HTML and SQL · ▶ Runs // A tag function receives the fixed strings and the values separatelyfunction html(strings, ...values) { const escape = v => String(v).replace(/[&<>\"']/g, c => ({ \"&\": \"&amp;\", \"<\": \"&lt;\", \">\": \"&gt;\", '\"': \"&quot;\", \"'\": \"&#39;\" })[c]); return strings.reduce((out, s, i) => out + s + (i < values.length ? escape(values[i]) : \"\"), \"\"); } const review = '<img src=x onerror=\"alert(1)\">'; console.log(html`<p class=\"review\">${review}</p>`); // A SQL tag that never builds text from values, which prevents SQL injectionfunction sql(strings, ...values) { return { text: strings.reduce((q, s, i) => q + `$${i}` + s), values }; } const email = \"x' OR '1'='1\"; console.log(sql`SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = ${email} AND active = ${true}`); // String.raw keeps backslashes, handy for regexes and Windows paths console.log(String.raw`C:\\tests\\new`, \"|\", `C:\\tests\\new`.length, String.raw`C:\\tests\\new`.length); // A dedent tag for readable multi line test dataconst dedent = (s, ...v) => String.raw(s, ...v).replace(/^\\n/, \"\").replace(newRegExp(`^${String.raw(s, ...v).match(/^\\n?( *)/)[1]}`, \"gm\"), \"\"); console.log(dedent` Feature: Login Scenario: wrong password Then I see \"Try again\"`); Output <p class=\"review\">&lt;img src=x onerror=&quot;alert(1)&quot;&gt;</p> { text: 'SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1 AND active = $2', values: [ \"x' OR '1'='1\", true ] } C:\\tests\\new | 10 12 Feature: Login Scenario: wrong password Then I see \"Try again\" Lesson 5 of 6"],[17,"decorators","Decorators","A decorator is a function written as @name before a class, method, field or accessor. It receives the original and can replace or wrap it. Test frameworks use decorators for steps, retries, timeouts and dependency injection. Decorators wrap methods and classes · Runs in chapter 25 with TypeScript // Decorators are a TC39 proposal (listed at stage 2.7 in October 2026). TypeScript 5 and later supports them today (chapter 25),// and Babel can compile them for JavaScript.classCheckoutPage { @step(\"Pay with card\") // log the step in the report@retry(2) // retry the method on a flaky errorasync pay(card) { /* ... */ } } function step(title) { returnfunction (method, context) { returnasyncfunction (...args) { console.log(`step: ${title}`); return method.apply(this, args); }; }; } Lesson 6 of 6"],[17,"scenario","Real world scenario: a fluent API client","🧪 QA essential The situationThe API test suite calls dozens of endpoints. Writing a function for each one is slow, and URL strings built by hand are full of typos. A dynamic API client with a Proxy · ▶ Runs // A fluent API client built from a Proxy: api.users[7].orders.get() calls GET /users/7/ordersfunction client(baseURL, fetchFn) { const make = path => newProxy(() => {}, { get(_, key) { if ([\"get\", \"post\", \"put\", \"delete\"].includes(key)) return (body) => fetchFn(key.toUpperCase(), `${baseURL}${path}`, body); return make(`${path}/${String(key)}`); }, }); return make(\"\"); } // A fake fetch so the example runs anywhereconst calls = []; const fakeFetch = async (method, url, body) => { calls.push(`${method} ${url}${body ? \" \" + JSON.stringify(body) : \"\"}`); return { status: method === \"POST\" ? 201 : 200 }; }; const api = client(\"https://api.shop.test/v1\", fakeFetch); await api.users[7].orders.get(); await api.products.post({ name: \"Pen\", price: 40 }); await api.carts[\"c-19\"].items.PEN.delete(); console.log(calls); Output [ 'GET https://api.shop.test/v1/users/7/orders', 'POST https://api.shop.test/v1/products {\"name\":\"Pen\",\"price\":40}', 'DELETE https://api.shop.test/v1/carts/c-19/items/PEN' ] Each property read adds a part to the path, and the verb at the end sends the request. The same trick is used by real clients for large APIs. Pair it with TypeScript types (chapter 24) so the editor suggests only valid paths. 🧮"],[17,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 cart has items of 40 × 2 and 250 × 1. What does the total getter return? Price times quantity. Answer: 330 40 × 2 + 250 × 1 = 330 C2 A template literal has 2 values. How many text pieces does the tag function receive? One more than values. Answer: 3 Text, value, text, value, text. C1 How many of add, change and delete does Object.seal allow on a sealed object? Only change. Answer: 1 Adding and deleting fail, changing works. C2 last([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\"])[-2] reads which index of the array? length + (−2). Answer: 2 4 + (−2) = 2 Index 2 is \"c\". C3 A watched proxy sees a read twice and b once. What is the total count of reads? Add them. Answer: 3 2 + 1 = 3. C1 The instrumented search fails once with a detached error and then works. How many times did the real method run? One retry. Answer: 2 First call threw, the retry returned results. C2 sql`… ${a} … ${b} … ${c}` produces placeholders $1 to $n. What is n? One per value. Answer: 3 Three values give $1, $2 and $3. C1 api.carts[\"c-19\"].items.PEN.delete(). How many path segments are in the URL after the base? carts, c-19, items, PEN. Answer: 4 /carts/c-19/items/PEN has 4 segments. 🎯"],[17,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 A property with enumerable: false is hidden from which of these? Enumeration. You can still read it directly. Q2 What does a getter do? Computed property. cart.total is computed from the items every time. Q3 What does a tag function receive first? strings, then ...values. There is always one more string than values. Q1 Which lock still allows changing existing values? Seal stops adding and deleting. Freeze also stops changes. Q2 Which symbol decides what +obj gives? Conversion. It receives a hint of \"number\", \"string\" or \"default\". Q3 Why call Reflect.get inside a get trap? Default behaviour. Passing the receiver keeps getters working correctly. Q4 How does util.inspect.custom help in test frameworks? Logging. Add toJSON as well for reports that serialise objects. Q1 Why does the sql tag on this page prevent SQL injection? Parameterised query. The database never treats a value as SQL. Q2 Which trap runs for \"key\" in proxy? The in operator. ownKeys runs for Object.keys and for loops over keys. Q3 A Proxy wraps every page object method with a retry. What does the page class need to change? Wrapping. That is the main benefit: one place adds behaviour to all methods. Q1 Why does the fluent client proxy wrap a function () => {} instead of {}? Callable proxies. A Proxy is only callable if its target is a function. Q2 String.raw`a\\nb`.length is? Backslash and n stay as two characters. Without raw, \\n is one new line character, giving 3."],[17,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[17,"readonly","A read only view","Write readOnly(obj) that returns a Proxy where reading works normally but any attempt to set or delete a property throws a TypeError. The original object stays writable. Traps for set and deleteProperty that throw. function readOnly(obj) { const deny = () => { thrownewTypeError(\"read only\"); }; returnnewProxy(obj, { set: deny, deleteProperty: deny }); } Unlike freeze, the owner can still change the data while callers only get a view. 🌿 Medium"],[17,"defaults","Defaults for missing keys","Write withDefaults(obj, fallback) that returns a Proxy. Reading a key that is not in the object returns fallback, and reading an existing key returns its value, even when that value is 0 or an empty string. Use key in target, not a truthy check. function withDefaults(obj, fallback) { returnnewProxy(obj, { get: (target, key, receiver) => (key in target ? Reflect.get(target, key, receiver) : fallback), }); } A truthy check would replace real values of 0 and \"\" with the fallback. 🔥 Hard"],[17,"htmltag","An escaping HTML tag","Write a tag function html so that html`<b>${x}</b>` escapes & < > \" ' in every value but leaves the fixed text alone. Join the strings with the escaped values between them. There is one more string than values. function html(strings, ...values) { const map = { \"&\": \"&amp;\", \"<\": \"&lt;\", \">\": \"&gt;\", '\"': \"&quot;\", \"'\": \"&#39;\" }; const esc = v => String(v).replace(/[&<>\"']/g, c => map[c]); return strings.reduce((out, s, i) => out + s + (i < values.length ? esc(values[i]) : \"\"), \"\"); } Template engines in every framework do exactly this, which is why they are safe from XSS by default. 🧠 Expert"],[17,"recorder","Record every method call","Write recorder(obj) that returns { proxy, calls }. Calling any method through proxy runs the real method with the right this and pushes [name, args] to calls. Reading non function properties works normally. In the get trap, wrap functions in an arrow that records and then calls value.apply(target, args). function recorder(obj) { const calls = []; const proxy = newProxy(obj, { get(target, key, receiver) { const value = Reflect.get(target, key, receiver); if (typeof value !== \"function\") return value; return (...args) => { calls.push([key, args]); return value.apply(target, args); }; }, }); return { proxy, calls }; } This is a spy. jest.spyOn and sinon.spy record calls the same way."],[17,"recorder","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. Property descriptors, getters and settersEvery property has a descriptor with flags. writable allows changes, enumerable shows it in Object.keys, loops and JSON, and configurable allows deleting it or changing its flags. Accessor properties use get and set functions instead of a value. Well known symbolsBuilt in operations look for special symbol keys on your objects. Symbol.toPrimitive controls conversion to numbers and strings, Symbol.hasInstance controls instanceof, Symbol.toStringTag the type label, and Symbol.iterator (chapter 8) makes an object ... Proxy and ReflectA Proxy sits in front of an object and can intercept almost anything done to it: reading, writing, deleting, in, listing keys, calling and new. Each handler function is a trap. Tagged template literalsA tag is a function placed before a template literal. It receives the fixed text pieces and the values separately, so it can escape values, build safe queries or change the text. DecoratorsA decorator is a function written as @name before a class, method, field or accessor. It receives the original and can replace or wrap it. Test frameworks use decorators for steps, retries, timeouts and dependency injection. Real world scenario: a fluent API clientEach property read adds a part to the path, and the verb at the end sends the request. The same trick is used by real clients for large APIs. Pair it with TypeScript types (chapter 24) so the editor suggests only valid paths."],[17,"comic","Code that shapes code","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Descriptors and symbols change how objects behave, Proxy intercepts operations, and tags keep values apart from text."],[17,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🧪Vitest and Jest Build automatic mocks of whole modules with proxies, so every function exists and records its calls. 🔐A banking test suite Logged request objects in every failure until a password appeared in a CI log. A custom inspect on the credentials class fixed it for good. 🐙A popular GitHub client Builds its methods for hundreds of endpoints from a description file, the same idea as the proxy client on this page. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Objects in depth and metaprogramming\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 8Welcome This chapter covers objects in depth and metaprogramming: descriptors, getters and setters, well known symbols, Proxy and Reflect, tagged templates and decorators. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[18,"","Node.js in depth","EventEmitter with once, error events and async iteration, generating and filtering a big file with streams, backpressure and drain, buffers, encodings, SHA-256 and HMAC webhook signatures, the built in SQLite database with prepared statements, spawn with streaming output and SIGTERM clean up, and a log check CLI tested with spawnSync. · Node.js · EventEmitter · streams · pipeline · backpressure · Buffer · crypto · HMAC · webhook · node:sqlite · SQLite · spawn · SIGTERM · exit code · parseArgs · CLI · interview 🟨 JavaScript›🔬 JavaScript in depth›Chapter 19 of 31 🟩 Step 5 · Topic 6 of 6 · Advanced"],[18,"top","","The parts of Node.js that power real tools: EventEmitter and async event iteration, streams, pipeline and backpressure for huge files, buffers, encodings, hashes and webhook signatures, the built in SQLite database, child processes and signals, and a tested command line tool for CI. ⏱️ About 19 min read📚 6 parts🎬 1 animations▶ 6 runnable examples🧪 1 real test run🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 6 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Test tools are Node.js programs. Streams read huge logs, crypto checks webhooks, SQLite holds test data, and exit codes make your tools work in CI. 1Events and EventEmitter 2Streams and backpressure 3Buffers, encodings and crypto 4The built in SQLite database 5Processes, signals and graceful shutdown 6Building and testing a command line tool Lesson 1 of 6"],[18,"events","Events and EventEmitter","🧪 QA essentialTest runners, reporters and browser drivers report progress through events, and custom reporters listen to them. Much of Node.js is built on EventEmitter: servers, sockets, streams, child processes and test runners. Listeners register with on or once, and emit calls them in order, synchronously. events.once turns one event into a promise and events.on turns a stream of events into an async iterator. EventEmitter: on, once, error and async iteration · ▶ Runs import { EventEmitter, once, on } from\"node:events\"; // A tiny test runner that reports progress with eventsclassRunnerextendsEventEmitter { async run(tests) { this.emit(\"start\", tests.length); for (const [name, fn] of tests) { try { await fn(); this.emit(\"pass\", name); } catch (e) { this.emit(\"fail\", name, e.message); } } this.emit(\"end\"); } } const runner = newRunner(); let passed = 0; runner.on(\"start\", n => console.log(`running ${n} tests`)); runner.on(\"pass\", () => passed++); runner.on(\"fail\", (name, msg) => console.log(`✖ ${name}: ${msg}`)); runner.once(\"end\", () => console.log(`${passed} passed`)); const finished = once(runner, \"end\"); // a promise for one eventawait runner.run([[\"login\", async () => {}], [\"cart\", async () => { thrownewError(\"expected 2, got 3\"); }], [\"search\", async () => {}]]); await finished; // An \"error\" event with no listener throws, so always listen for ittry { newEventEmitter().emit(\"error\", newError(\"socket closed\")); } catch (e) { console.log(\"unhandled error event:\", e.message); } // Events as an async iterator, stopped by an AbortSignalconst ticker = newEventEmitter(), ac = newAbortController(); setTimeout(() => { ticker.emit(\"tick\", 1); ticker.emit(\"tick\", 2); ac.abort(); }, 5); try { forawait (const [n] of on(ticker, \"tick\", { signal: ac.signal })) console.log(\"tick\", n); } catch (e) { console.log(\"stopped:\", e.name); } Output running 3 tests ✖ cart: expected 2, got 3 2 passed unhandled error event: socket closed tick 1 tick 2 stopped: AbortError An \"error\" event with no listener throws and can crash the process. Every emitter you create, and every stream and socket you use, needs an error listener or a pipeline that handles errors for you. Lesson 2 of 6"],[18,"streams","Streams and backpressure","🧪 QA essentialReading huge logs, HAR files and test result exports needs streams, or the test tool runs out of memory. A stream moves data in small chunks, so a 5 GB log file never has to fit in memory. Readable streams produce data, Writable streams consume it, and Transform streams change it on the way. pipeline connects them, passes errors along and closes everything at the end. Streams: generate, filter and count a big file · ▶ Runs import { Readable, Transform, Writable } from\"node:stream\"; import { pipeline } from\"node:stream/promises\"; import { createWriteStream, createReadStream } from\"node:fs\"; import { createInterface } from\"node:readline\"; // 1. Generate a large log file without holding it in memoryfunction* lines() { for (let i = 1; i <= 50_000; i++) yield`${i} ${i % 97 === 0 ? 500 : 200} /api/item/${i % 50}\\n`; } await pipeline(Readable.from(lines()), createWriteStream(\"big.log\")); // 2. Transform stream: keep only the 5xx lines and turn them into JSONconst onlyErrors = newTransform({ objectMode: true, transform(line, _enc, done) { const [id, status, path] = line.split(\" \"); if (status >= 500) this.push({ id: +id, status: +status, path }); done(); }, }); // 3. Writable sink that countslet errors = 0, first; const counter = newWritable({ objectMode: true, write(row, _enc, done) { errors++; first ??= row; done(); } }); const rl = createInterface({ input: createReadStream(\"big.log\"), crlfDelay: Infinity }); await pipeline(Readable.from(rl), onlyErrors, counter); console.log(\"5xx lines:\", errors, \"| first:\", first); console.log(\"memory stays small: the file was never read into one string\"); Output 5xx lines: 515 | first: { id: 97, status: 500, path: '/api/item/47' } memory stays small: the file was never read into one string The animation shows what happens inside a write stream when your code produces data faster than the disk or the network can take it. 🎬 Try it"],[18,"streams","Backpressure: write, pause, drain, resume","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. const out = fs.createWriteStream(\"big.csv\", { highWaterMark: 16 * 1024 }); for (const row of rows) { if (!out.write(row)) await once(out, \"drain\"); } Your loop Buffer in memory (16 KB limit) Disk Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 6 Backpressure is how a slow consumer tells a fast producer to wait. When write() returns false, stop writing until the \"drain\" event. pipeline and for await do this automatically, which is the main reason to use them. Backpressure: when the reader is slower than the writer · ▶ Runs import { Writable } from\"node:stream\"; // A slow writer with a small bufferconst slow = newWritable({ highWaterMark: 4, // bytes before write() asks you to wait write(chunk, _enc, done) { setTimeout(done, 1); }, }); let refused = 0, drained = 0; slow.on(\"drain\", () => drained++); for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) if (!slow.write(\"ab\")) refused++; console.log(\"write() returned false\", refused, \"times: stop and wait for drain\"); // The right way: await drain when write() returns false (pipeline does this for you)asyncfunction writeAll(stream, chunks) { for (const c of chunks) if (!stream.write(c)) awaitnewPromise(r => stream.once(\"drain\", r)); } await writeAll(slow, Array(10).fill(\"ab\")); console.log(\"drain events so far:\", drained > 0); Output write() returned false 9 times: stop and wait for drain drain events so far: true Lesson 3 of 6"],[18,"buffers","Buffers, encodings and crypto","🧪 QA essentialWebhook signatures, file checksums and binary uploads all need bytes, hashes and HMACs in tests. A Buffer is a fixed block of bytes, a Uint8Array with extra methods. Text needs an encoding to become bytes: in UTF-8, \"é\" takes 2 bytes and \"₹\" takes 3, so byte length and string length differ. The crypto module gives hashes, HMAC signatures, random ids and safe comparison. Buffers, hashes and HMAC signatures · ▶ Runs import { createHash, createHmac, randomUUID, timingSafeEqual, randomInt } from\"node:crypto\"; const buf = Buffer.from(\"Héllo ₹\", \"utf8\"); console.log(buf, buf.length, \"bytes for\", \"Héllo ₹\".length, \"characters\"); console.log(buf.toString(\"hex\"), buf.toString(\"base64\"), Buffer.from(\"SMOpbGxvIOKCuQ==\", \"base64\").toString()); console.log(buf instanceofUint8Array); // Hash a file's bytes for golden file tests: same bytes, same hashconst report = JSON.stringify({ passed: 41, failed: 1 }); console.log(\"sha256:\", createHash(\"sha256\").update(report).digest(\"hex\").slice(0, 16), \"...\"); // HMAC signatures, as used by webhooks: compare in constant timeconst secret = \"whsec_test\"; const sign = body => createHmac(\"sha256\", secret).update(body).digest(); const sent = sign('{\"event\":\"order.paid\"}'); console.log(\"valid signature:\", timingSafeEqual(sent, sign('{\"event\":\"order.paid\"}'))); console.log(\"tampered body:\", timingSafeEqual(sent, sign('{\"event\":\"order.refunded\"}'))); console.log(\"unique ids:\", typeof randomUUID(), randomInt(1, 7) >= 1); Output <Buffer 48 c3 a9 6c 6c 6f 20 e2 82 b9> 10 bytes for 7 characters 48c3a96c6c6f20e282b9 SMOpbGxvIOKCuQ== Héllo ₹ true sha256: 72a854db36da2159 ... valid signature: true tampered body: false unique ids: string true Testing webhooks means checking signatures: compute the HMAC of the exact raw body with the shared secret and compare with timingSafeEqual. Also test a changed body, a wrong secret and a missing signature header, which must all be rejected. Lesson 4 of 6"],[18,"sqlite","The built in SQLite database","🧪 QA essentialDatabase checks after an API or UI action confirm that data was really saved, which UI checks alone cannot prove. Node.js ships with node:sqlite, a full SQL database that runs in memory or in a file with no server and no install. It is ideal for seeding test data, checking reports, and learning SQL alongside JavaScript. The built in SQLite database · ▶ Runs import { DatabaseSync } from\"node:sqlite\"; // A real SQL database built into Node.js: perfect for test data and quick checksconst db = newDatabaseSync(\":memory:\"); db.exec(` CREATE TABLE users (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, email TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL, role TEXT NOT NULL); CREATE TABLE orders (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, user_id INTEGER REFERENCES users(id), total REAL, status TEXT); `); const addUser = db.prepare(\"INSERT INTO users (email, role) VALUES (?, ?)\"); const addOrder = db.prepare(\"INSERT INTO orders (user_id, total, status) VALUES (?, ?, ?)\"); for (const [email, role] of [[\"asha@shop.test\", \"admin\"], [\"ravi@shop.test\", \"customer\"], [\"li@shop.test\", \"customer\"]]) addUser.run(email, role); for (const [u, t, s] of [[2, 450, \"paid\"], [2, 120, \"refunded\"], [3, 980, \"paid\"], [3, 40, \"new\"]]) addOrder.run(u, t, s); // Parameters, never string building, so test data with quotes is safe console.log(db.prepare(\"SELECT id, role FROM users WHERE email = ?\").get(\"ravi@shop.test\")); const report = db.prepare(` SELECT u.email, COUNT(o.id) AS orders, SUM(CASE WHEN o.status = 'paid' THEN o.total ELSE 0 END) AS paid FROM users u LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id GROUP BY u.id ORDER BY paid DESC`).all(); console.table(report.map(r => ({ ...r }))); try { addUser.run(\"asha@shop.test\", \"customer\"); } catch (e) { console.log(\"constraint:\", e.message); } Output [Object: null prototype] { id: 2, role: 'customer' } ┌─────────┬──────────────────┬────────┬──────┐ │ (index) │ email │ orders │ paid │ ├─────────┼──────────────────┼────────┼──────┤ │ 0 │ 'li@shop.test' │ 2 │ 980 │ │ 1 │ 'ravi@shop.test' │ 2 │ 450 │ │ 2 │ 'asha@shop.test' │ 0 │ 0 │ └─────────┴──────────────────┴────────┴──────┘ constraint: UNIQUE constraint failed: users.email Always use prepared statements with ? placeholders. Test data such as O'Brien then works, and so does an SQL injection test that tries ' OR '1'='1. Lesson 5 of 6"],[18,"process","Processes, signals and graceful shutdown","🧪 QA essentialCINode.jsStarting servers for tests, stopping them cleanly and returning the right exit code are what make a suite reliable in CI. spawn starts another program and streams its output as it arrives, which suits long runs such as a test suite or a dev server. Programs are stopped with signals: SIGINT when you press Ctrl+C and SIGTERM when CI or Docker stops a job. A well written tool catches them, cleans up, and then exits. Child processes, streaming output and graceful shutdown · ▶ Runs import { spawn } from\"node:child_process\"; // spawn streams output as it arrives, unlike exec which waits for the endconst child = spawn(process.execPath, [\"-e\", ` let n = 0; const t = setInterval(() => console.log(\"test\", ++n, \"passed\"), 10); process.on(\"SIGTERM\", () => { clearInterval(t); console.log(\"cleaning up, closing the browser\"); process.exit(0); }); `]); let lines = 0; child.stdout.setEncoding(\"utf8\"); child.stdout.on(\"data\", chunk => { for (const line of chunk.trim().split(\"\\n\")) { console.log(\"child:\", line); if (++lines === 3) child.kill(\"SIGTERM\"); // ask it to stop nicely } }); const [code, signal] = awaitnewPromise(r => child.on(\"exit\", (c, s) => r([c, s]))); console.log(\"exit code\", code, \"signal\", signal); Output child: test 1 passed child: test 2 passed child: test 3 passed child: cleaning up, closing the browser exit code 0 signal null Exit codes are the contract with CI: 0 means success, 1 means the check failed, and 2 is the usual code for \"used wrongly\". Set process.exitCode instead of calling process.exit() so pending output is still written. Lesson 6 of 6"],[18,"cli","Building and testing a command line tool","🧪 QA essentialSmall CLI tools glue test pipelines together, and they need tests of their own exit codes and output. The situationThe release pipeline needs a small tool that reads a server log and fails the job when there are more 5xx errors than allowed. util.parseArgs reads flags with no extra package, readline reads the file line by line, styleText adds colour that respects NO_COLOR, and the exit code tells CI the result. The bin field in package.json turns the file into a command after npm install. A command line tool with parseArgs, tested with spawnSync · 🧪 Tests run 📄 bin/log-check.js #!/usr/bin/env node import { parseArgs, styleText } from\"node:util\"; import { createReadStream } from\"node:fs\"; import { createInterface } from\"node:readline\"; const { values, positionals } = parseArgs({ allowPositionals: true, options: { \"max-errors\": { type: \"string\", default: \"0\" }, json: { type: \"boolean\", default: false }, help: { type: \"boolean\", short: \"h\" }, }, }); if (values.help || positionals.length === 0) { console.log(\"usage: log-check [--max-errors N] [--json] FILE\"); process.exit(values.help ? 0 : 2); // 2 means \"used wrongly\" } let total = 0, errors = 0; forawait (const line of createInterface({ input: createReadStream(positionals[0]) })) { total++; if (/ 5\\d\\d /.test(line)) errors++; } const ok = errors <= Number(values[\"max-errors\"]); if (values.json) console.log(JSON.stringify({ total, errors, ok })); else console.log(styleText(ok ? \"green\" : \"red\", `${errors} errors in ${total} lines`)); process.exitCode = ok ? 0 : 1; // CI fails the job on 1 📄 test/cli.test.js import { test } from\"node:test\"; import assert from\"node:assert/strict\"; import { spawnSync } from\"node:child_process\"; import { writeFileSync } from\"node:fs\"; const cli = (...args) => spawnSync(process.execPath, [\"bin/log-check.js\", ...args], { encoding: \"utf8\" }); writeFileSync(\"ok.log\", \"GET /a 200 \\nGET /b 200 \\n\"); writeFileSync(\"bad.log\", \"GET /a 200 \\nPOST /orders 503 \\nGET /b 500 \\n\"); test(\"no file prints usage and exits with 2\", () => { const r = cli(); assert.equal(r.status, 2); assert.match(r.stdout, /usage/); }); test(\"a clean log passes\", () => { assert.deepEqual(JSON.parse(cli(\"--json\", \"ok.log\").stdout), { total: 2, errors: 0, ok: true }); }); test(\"errors over the limit fail with exit code 1\", () => { const r = cli(\"bad.log\"); assert.equal(r.status, 1); assert.equal(r.stdout.trim(), \"2 errors in 3 lines\"); }); test(\"--max-errors raises the limit\", () => assert.equal(cli(\"--max-errors\", \"2\", \"bad.log\").status, 0)); Terminal $ npm test Terminal output > log-check@1.0.0 test > node --test ✔ no file prints usage and exits with 2 ✔ a clean log passes ✔ errors over the limit fail with exit code 1 ✔ --max-errors raises the limit ℹ tests 4 ℹ suites 0 ℹ pass 4 ℹ fail 0 ℹ cancelled 0 ℹ skipped 0 ℹ todo 0 ℹ duration_ms 212.4 The tests run the real tool with spawnSync and check its output and exit code, exactly the way CI will use it. 🧮"],[18,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 In the streams example, every line number divisible by 97 is a 500. How many 5xx lines are in 50,000 lines? Whole multiples. Answer: 515 ⌊50000 ÷ 97⌋ = 515 C2 Buffer.byteLength(\"Héllo\") where é is 2 bytes. How many bytes? 5 characters, one of them 2 bytes. Answer: 6 4 × 1 + 1 × 2 = 6 C1 A stream with highWaterMark 16 KB reads a 1 MB file. About how many chunks is that? 1 MB is 1024 KB. Answer: 64 1024 ÷ 16 = 64 C2 A SHA-256 hash in hex has how many characters? 256 bits, 4 bits per hex digit. Answer: 64 256 ÷ 4 = 64 C3 Users 2 and 3 have paid orders of 450 and 980. What is the sum of paid totals in the report? Only status paid. Answer: 1430 450 + 980 = 1430 C1 base64 turns every 3 bytes into 4 characters. How long is the base64 of 9 bytes? Groups of 3. Answer: 12 9 ÷ 3 × 4 = 12 C2 A log check allows at most 5 errors. A canary run has 3 errors in 2,000 lines. What exit code does the tool return? Within the limit. Answer: 0 3 is not more than 5, so the check passes. C1 A child test runner prints one line every 10 ms and is sent SIGTERM after its 3rd line. About how many ms did it run? Three intervals. Answer: 30 3 × 10 = 30 🎯"],[18,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 What does emitter.once(\"end\", fn) do? One time. The listener is removed after the first call. Q2 Why use streams for a 5 GB file? Chunks. Each chunk is processed and then released. Q3 Which exit code tells CI that a check failed? Non zero means failure. 0 means success. 2 often means the tool was used wrongly. Q1 What happens when an EventEmitter emits \"error\" with no listener? Special event. Always add an error listener or use pipeline. Q2 write() on a stream returns false. What should you do? Backpressure. The internal buffer is full. Q3 How many bytes does \"₹\" take in UTF-8? Outside the first 2048 code points. Buffer.byteLength(\"₹\") is 3. Q4 Why use ? placeholders in SQL statements? Parameters. O'Brien works without any escaping. Q1 Why compare HMAC signatures with timingSafeEqual instead of ===? Timing attacks. Constant time comparison takes the same time whatever the bytes are. Q2 What does pipeline give you over chaining .pipe()? Errors and closing. .pipe() does not forward errors, so failures could leak open files. Q3 Which signal does CI usually send to stop a job? Polite stop. SIGKILL follows only if the process does not exit in time. Q1 Why set process.exitCode = 1 instead of calling process.exit(1)? Clean exit. process.exit() stops at once and can cut off logs. Q2 A webhook test parses the JSON body and re-serialises it before computing the HMAC. Why do valid signatures fail? Hash the raw bytes. Always sign and verify the exact bytes that were sent."],[18,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[18,"bus","A tiny event bus","Write createBus() that returns { on, emit }. on(name, fn) registers a listener and returns a function that removes it. emit(name, ...args) calls every listener for that name in order. A Map from event name to a Set of listeners. function createBus() { const map = newMap(); return { on(name, fn) { if (!map.has(name)) map.set(name, newSet()); map.get(name).add(fn); return () => map.get(name).delete(fn); }, emit(name, ...args) { for (const fn of map.get(name) ?? []) fn(...args); }, }; } Returning an unsubscribe function avoids the listener leaks from chapter 31. 🌿 Medium"],[18,"splitter","Split chunks into lines","Streams deliver text in chunks that can cut a line in half. Write lineSplitter() that returns { push(chunk), end() }. push returns the complete lines found so far, and end returns what is left as a final line, or an empty array. Keep the last, unfinished piece between calls. function lineSplitter() { let rest = \"\"; return { push(chunk) { const parts = (rest + chunk).split(\"\\n\"); rest = parts.pop(); return parts; }, end() { return rest ? [rest] : []; }, }; } This is what readline does inside. Forgetting the leftover piece loses or breaks lines at chunk edges. 🔥 Hard"],[18,"checksum","Verify a checksum","Write checksum(text) that returns the sum of the UTF-8 byte values of the text modulo 256, and verify(text, sum) that returns true only when they match. Use new TextEncoder().encode(text) for the bytes. Encode to bytes, then reduce with addition modulo 256. function checksum(text) { returnnewTextEncoder().encode(text).reduce((s, b) => (s + b) % 256, 0); } function verify(text, sum) { return checksum(text) === sum; } Real systems use SHA-256 or HMAC, but the idea is the same: compute over the exact bytes, then compare. 🧠 Expert"],[18,"flags","Parse command line flags","Write parseFlags(argv) for arguments such as [\"--json\", \"--max-errors\", \"3\", \"app.log\", \"--env=staging\"]. Return { flags, files }. --name=value and --name value set a string, a --name followed by another flag or by nothing is true, and everything else is a file. Loop with an index so you can take the next argument as a value. Here a word that looks like a file name, such as app.log, is a file, not a value. function parseFlags(argv) { const flags = {}, files = []; for (let i = 0; i < argv.length; i++) { const a = argv[i]; if (!a.startsWith(\"--\")) { files.push(a); continue; } const [name, value] = a.slice(2).split(\"=\"); if (value !== undefined) flags[name] = value; elseif (i + 1 < argv.length && !argv[i + 1].startsWith(\"--\") && !/\\.\\w+$/.test(argv[i + 1])) flags[name] = argv[++i]; else flags[name] = true; } return { flags, files }; } In real tools, declare which flags take values, as util.parseArgs does, instead of guessing."],[18,"flags","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. Events and EventEmitterMuch of Node.js is built on EventEmitter: servers, sockets, streams, child processes and test runners. Listeners register with on or once, and emit calls them in order, synchronously. Streams and backpressureA stream moves data in small chunks, so a 5 GB log file never has to fit in memory. Readable streams produce data, Writable streams consume it, and Transform streams change it on the way. Buffers, encodings and cryptoA Buffer is a fixed block of bytes, a Uint8Array with extra methods. Text needs an encoding to become bytes: in UTF-8, \"é\" takes 2 bytes and \"₹\" takes 3, so byte length and string length differ. The built in SQLite databaseNode.js ships with node:sqlite, a full SQL database that runs in memory or in a file with no server and no install. It is ideal for seeding test data, checking reports, and learning SQL alongside JavaScript. Processes, signals and graceful shutdownspawn starts another program and streams its output as it arrives, which suits long runs such as a test suite or a dev server. Programs are stopped with signals: SIGINT when you press Ctrl+C and SIGTERM when CI or Docker stops a job. Building and testing a command line toolutil.parseArgs reads flags with no extra package, readline reads the file line by line, styleText adds colour that respects NO_COLOR, and the exit code tells CI the result. The bin field in package.json turns the file into a command after npm install."],[18,"comic","Node.js under the surface","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Listen for errors, stream big data through pipeline, use prepared statements, and give every tool a clear exit code."],[18,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 📜A load test team Analysed a 12 GB access log with a Transform stream on a laptop with 8 GB of memory. Reading it as one string had crashed every time. 🔔A payments webhook Failed signature checks in tests because the body was parsed and re-serialised before hashing. Hashing the raw bytes fixed it. 🚦A release pipeline Uses a small log check CLI as a gate: more than five 5xx errors after a canary deploy and the job exits with 1 and rolls back. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Node.js in depth\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 8Welcome This chapter goes deep into Node: events, streams and backpressure, buffers and crypto, the built in SQLite database, processes and signals, and command line tools. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[19,"","Unit Testing: node:test, Jest, Vitest and Mocha","The test pyramid and Arrange Act Assert, node:test with mocks and a fake clock, Jest matchers, test.each, module mocks, spies and fake timers, Vitest and Mocha with Chai and Sinon, seven ways to run a node:test suite and five with Jest, a CI matrix with shards, UI component tests with jsdom, Testing Library and user-event, API route tests with supertest, outgoing HTTP faked with nock, and a bug turned into a regression test. · unit testing · node:test · Jest · Vitest · Mocha · Chai · Sinon · mock · spy · stub · fake timers · test.each · filter · tags · shard · coverage · JUnit · CI · jsdom · Testing Library · supertest · nock · TDD · regression test 🟨 JavaScript›🧪 Testing with JavaScript›Chapter 20 of 31 🧪 Step 6 · Topic 1 of 3 · Advanced"],[19,"top","","Write, run and organise unit tests with the four most used JavaScript runners. Mocks, spies and fake timers, many ways to run a suite (filters, tags, shards, reporters, coverage, CI), unit tests for web UI code with jsdom and Testing Library, and for API code with supertest and nock. ⏱️ About 25 min read📚 8 parts🎬 2 animations🧪 13 real test runs🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 8 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Every example on this page ran with the real runner when the page was built. You will see the same test written for web code and for API code, and the same suite run in many different ways. 1What a unit test is 2Mocks, spies, stubs and fake timers 3Jest 4Vitest and Mocha 5Running tests in different ways 6Unit testing web (UI) code 7Unit testing API code 8Real world scenario: turn a bug report into a test Lesson 1 of 8"],[19,"basics","What a unit test is","🧪 QA essentialnode:testTesters who read and write unit tests can review developers' tests and spot missing cases early. A unit test checks one small piece of code, usually one function or one class, on its own. It runs in milliseconds, needs no browser, server or database, and tells you exactly which piece broke. A good suite has many unit tests, fewer API or integration tests, and a small number of slow end to end tests. This shape is called the test pyramid. 🎬 Animated diagram"],[19,"basics","The test pyramid","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 3 Every test follows the same three steps: Arrange the input, Act by calling the code, Assert that the result is right. Node.js has a test runner built in, so the first suite needs nothing installed. A first unit test suite with node:test · 🧪 Tests run 📄 src/cart.js exportfunction cartTotal(items, { coupon } = {}) { if (!Array.isArray(items)) thrownewTypeError(\"items must be an array\"); const subtotal = items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price * i.qty, 0); const discount = coupon === \"SAVE10\" ? subtotal * 0.1 : 0; returnMath.round((subtotal - discount) * 100) / 100; } exportfunction freeShipping(total) { return total >= 499; } 📄 test/cart.test.js import { describe, it } from\"node:test\"; import assert from\"node:assert/strict\"; import { cartTotal, freeShipping } from\"../src/cart.js\"; describe(\"cartTotal\", () => { it(\"adds price times quantity\", () => { // Arrangeconst items = [{ price: 20, qty: 3 }, { price: 250, qty: 1 }]; // Actconst total = cartTotal(items); // Assert assert.equal(total, 310); }); it(\"is 0 for an empty cart\", () => { assert.equal(cartTotal([]), 0); }); it(\"takes 10% off with SAVE10\", () => { assert.equal(cartTotal([{ price: 199, qty: 1 }], { coupon: \"SAVE10\" }), 179.1); }); it(\"rejects input that is not an array\", () => { assert.throws(() => cartTotal(\"pen\"), { name: \"TypeError\", message: /must be an array/ }); }); }); describe(\"freeShipping\", () => { for (const [total, expected] of [[498.99, false], [499, true], [500, true]]) { it(`${total} -> ${expected}`, () => assert.equal(freeShipping(total), expected)); } }); Terminal $ npm test Terminal output > cart@1.0.0 test > node --test --test-reporter=spec ▶ cartTotal ✔ adds price times quantity ✔ is 0 for an empty cart ✔ takes 10% off with SAVE10 ✔ rejects input that is not an array ✔ cartTotal ▶ freeShipping ✔ 498.99 -> false ✔ 499 -> true ✔ 500 -> true ✔ freeShipping ℹ tests 7 ℹ suites 2 ℹ pass 7 ℹ fail 0 ℹ cancelled 0 ℹ skipped 0 ℹ todo 0 ℹ duration_ms 92.5 Most suites also need set up and clean up. Hooks run code around the tests, and their order is the same in every popular runner. Getting it wrong is a common cause of tests that pass alone and fail together. 🎬 Try it"],[19,"basics","The order hooks and tests run in","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. beforeAll(startServer); beforeEach(seedData); afterEach(clearData); afterAll(stopServer); test(\"A adds an item\", ...); test(\"B removes an item\", ...); Running now Order so far Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 8 Good unit tests are fast, independent of each other and of the order they run in, repeatable on any machine, and named so that a failure explains itself: \"takes 10% off with SAVE10\" beats \"test 3\". Lesson 2 of 8"],[19,"doubles","Mocks, spies, stubs and fake timers","🧪 QA essentialMocks and fake timers are how tests cover error paths and time based rules without waiting or breaking real systems. Real code calls other things: an email service, the clock, the network. A unit test swaps them for test doubles so the test is fast and predictable and never sends a real email. Double What it does Example Stub returns a fixed answer the stock service always says 7 Spy records how it was called was send called once, with which arguments Mock a stub and a spy together, often replacing a whole module jest.mock(\"./mailer\") Fake a small working version an in memory database, a fake clock Fake functions and a fake clock in node:test · 🧪 Tests run 📄 src/signup.js exportfunction createSignup({ mailer, clock }) { const pending = newMap(); return { async register(email) { const code = String(clock.now() % 1_000_000).padStart(6, \"0\"); pending.set(email, { code, expires: clock.now() + 10 * 60_000 }); await mailer.send(email, `Your code is ${code}`); return { status: \"pending\" }; }, verify(email, code) { const entry = pending.get(email); if (!entry || clock.now() > entry.expires) return\"expired\"; return entry.code === code ? \"verified\" : \"wrong code\"; }, }; } 📄 test/signup.test.js import { describe, it, beforeEach, afterEach, mock } from\"node:test\"; import assert from\"node:assert/strict\"; import { createSignup } from\"../src/signup.js\"; describe(\"signup\", () => { let mailer, signup; beforeEach(() => { mock.timers.enable({ apis: [\"Date\"], now: 1_700_000_123_456 }); // a fixed, fake clock mailer = { send: mock.fn(async () => {}) }; // a fake mailer that records calls signup = createSignup({ mailer, clock: Date }); }); afterEach(() => mock.timers.reset()); // real time again it(\"emails a 6 digit code\", async () => { await signup.register(\"asha@shop.example\"); assert.equal(mailer.send.mock.callCount(), 1); assert.deepEqual(mailer.send.mock.calls[0].arguments, [\"asha@shop.example\", \"Your code is 123456\"]); }); it(\"accepts the right code within 10 minutes\", async () => { await signup.register(\"asha@shop.example\"); mock.timers.tick(9 * 60_000); // jump 9 minutes ahead instantly assert.equal(signup.verify(\"asha@shop.example\", \"123456\"), \"verified\"); }); it(\"expires the code after 10 minutes\", async () => { await signup.register(\"asha@shop.example\"); mock.timers.tick(10 * 60_000 + 1); assert.equal(signup.verify(\"asha@shop.example\", \"123456\"), \"expired\"); }); }); Terminal $ node --test --test-reporter=spec Terminal output ▶ signup ✔ emails a 6 digit code ✔ accepts the right code within 10 minutes ✔ expires the code after 10 minutes ✔ signup ℹ tests 3 ℹ suites 1 ℹ pass 3 ℹ fail 0 ℹ cancelled 0 ℹ skipped 0 ℹ todo 0 ℹ duration_ms 92.5 Mock what is slow or outside your control, not the code under test itself. A test where everything is mocked only proves that the mocks work. Lesson 3 of 8"],[19,"jest","Jest","🧪 QA essentialJestJest is the default runner in many company projects, and its expect API is reused by Playwright and Vitest. Jest is the most used JavaScript test runner. It has matchers built in (expect(x).toBe(y)), table driven tests with test.each, mocks for whole modules, fake timers, snapshots and coverage. By default it uses CommonJS. Jest: matchers and table driven tests · 🧪 Tests run 📄 src/cart.test.js const { cartTotal, freeShipping } = require(\"./cart\"); describe(\"cartTotal\", () => { test(\"adds price times quantity\", () => { expect(cartTotal([{ price: 20, qty: 3 }, { price: 250, qty: 1 }])).toBe(310); }); test.each([ [[], 0], [[{ price: 99.99, qty: 3 }], 299.97], [[{ price: 0.1, qty: 3 }], 0.3], ])(\"cartTotal(%j) is %d\", (items, expected) => { expect(cartTotal(items)).toBeCloseTo(expected, 2); }); test(\"throws a TypeError for bad input\", () => { expect(() => cartTotal(null)).toThrow(TypeError); expect(() => cartTotal(null)).toThrow(/must be an array/); }); }); describe(\"freeShipping\", () => { test.each` total | free ${498.99} | ${false} ${499} | ${true} `(\"$total gives $free\", ({ total, free }) => { expect(freeShipping(total)).toBe(free); }); }); test(\"matchers for objects and arrays\", () => { const order = { id: 7, items: [\"pen\", \"mug\"], meta: { source: \"web\", at: Date.now() } }; expect(order).toEqual(expect.objectContaining({ id: 7 })); expect(order.items).toContain(\"mug\"); expect(order.items).toHaveLength(2); expect(order).toMatchObject({ meta: { source: \"web\" } }); expect(order.meta.at).toEqual(expect.any(Number)); }); Terminal $ npx jest --verbose Terminal output PASS src/cart.test.js ✓ matchers for objects and arrays cartTotal ✓ adds price times quantity ✓ cartTotal([]) is 0 ✓ cartTotal([{\"price\":99.99,\"qty\":3}]) is 299.97 ✓ cartTotal([{\"price\":0.1,\"qty\":3}]) is 0.3 ✓ throws a TypeError for bad input freeShipping ✓ 498.99 gives false ✓ 499 gives true Test Suites: 1 passed, 1 total Tests: 8 passed, 8 total Snapshots: 0 total Time: 0.61 s Ran all test suites. Jest: mock a module, spy, and fake timers · 🧪 Tests run 📄 src/order.js const mailer = require(\"./mailer\"); asyncfunction placeOrder(order, { now = Date.now } = {}) { if (!order.items.length) thrownewError(\"Cart is empty\"); const id = `ORD-${now()}`; await mailer.send(order.email, `Order ${id} confirmed`); return { id, status: \"confirmed\" }; } function remindLater(fn, ms = 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000) { return setTimeout(fn, ms); } module.exports = { placeOrder, remindLater }; 📄 src/order.test.js jest.mock(\"./mailer\"); // replace every export with a fakeconst mailer = require(\"./mailer\"); const { placeOrder, remindLater } = require(\"./order\"); beforeEach(() => jest.clearAllMocks()); test(\"sends one confirmation email\", async () => { mailer.send.mockResolvedValue(undefined); const result = await placeOrder({ email: \"asha@shop.example\", items: [\"pen\"] }, { now: () => 42 }); expect(result).toEqual({ id: \"ORD-42\", status: \"confirmed\" }); expect(mailer.send).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1); expect(mailer.send).toHaveBeenCalledWith(\"asha@shop.example\", \"Order ORD-42 confirmed\"); }); test(\"an empty cart sends nothing\", async () => { await expect(placeOrder({ email: \"a@b.c\", items: [] })).rejects.toThrow(\"Cart is empty\"); expect(mailer.send).not.toHaveBeenCalled(); }); test(\"a mail failure fails the order\", async () => { mailer.send.mockRejectedValueOnce(newError(\"SMTP down\")); await expect(placeOrder({ email: \"a@b.c\", items: [\"pen\"] })).rejects.toThrow(\"SMTP down\"); }); test(\"spyOn watches a real method without replacing it\", () => { const spy = jest.spyOn(Math, \"round\"); Math.round(2.6); expect(spy).toHaveBeenCalledWith(2.6); expect(spy).toHaveReturnedWith(3); spy.mockRestore(); }); test(\"fake timers skip a whole day instantly\", () => { jest.useFakeTimers(); const remind = jest.fn(); remindLater(remind); jest.advanceTimersByTime(23 * 60 * 60 * 1000); expect(remind).not.toHaveBeenCalled(); jest.advanceTimersByTime(60 * 60 * 1000); expect(remind).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);"],[19,"jest","Jest","jest.useRealTimers(); }); Terminal $ npx jest --verbose Terminal output PASS src/order.test.js ✓ sends one confirmation email ✓ an empty cart sends nothing ✓ a mail failure fails the order ✓ spyOn watches a real method without replacing it ✓ fake timers skip a whole day instantly Test Suites: 1 passed, 1 total Tests: 5 passed, 5 total Snapshots: 0 total Time: 0.61 s Ran all test suites. Matcher Passes when toBe(x) same value, or same object (Object.is) toEqual(x) same contents, deeply toMatchObject(x) contains at least these properties toBeCloseTo(x, digits) decimals are close enough toThrow(x) the function throws a matching error resolves, rejects for promises, always with await toHaveBeenCalledWith(...) a mock was called with these arguments Lesson 4 of 8"],[19,"vitest-mocha","Vitest and Mocha","🧪 QA essentialVitestMochaWebdriverIOYou will meet all of these in real projects, and WebdriverIO tests use the Mocha style by default. Vitest has almost the same API as Jest (vi.fn instead of jest.fn) but runs ES modules and TypeScript with no set up and is very fast. It is the natural choice for Vite, Vue and modern React projects. Vitest: the same ideas, ES modules first · 🧪 Tests run 📄 src/price.test.js import { describe, it, expect, vi } from\"vitest\"; import { convert, debounce } from\"./price.js\"; describe(\"convert\", () => { it(\"does not call the rate service for INR\", async () => { const getRate = vi.fn(); expect(await convert(500, \"INR\", { getRate })).toBe(500); expect(getRate).not.toHaveBeenCalled(); }); it(\"uses the rate from the service\", async () => { const getRate = vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(0.012); await expect(convert(1000, \"USD\", { getRate })).resolves.toBe(12); expect(getRate).toHaveBeenCalledWith(\"INR\", \"USD\"); }); }); describe(\"debounce\", () => { it(\"runs once after the user stops typing\", () => { vi.useFakeTimers(); const search = vi.fn(); const onType = debounce(search, 300); onType(\"p\"); onType(\"pe\"); onType(\"pen\"); vi.advanceTimersByTime(299); expect(search).not.toHaveBeenCalled(); vi.advanceTimersByTime(1); expect(search).toHaveBeenCalledExactlyOnceWith(\"pen\"); vi.useRealTimers(); }); }); it.concurrent.each([1, 2, 3])(\"concurrent case %i\", async n => { expect(n).toBeGreaterThan(0); }); Terminal $ npx vitest run --reporter=verbose Terminal output RUN v5.0.5 project ✓ src/price.test.js > convert > does not call the rate service for INR ✓ src/price.test.js > convert > uses the rate from the service ✓ src/price.test.js > debounce > runs once after the user stops typing ✓ src/price.test.js > concurrent case 1 ✓ src/price.test.js > concurrent case 2 ✓ src/price.test.js > concurrent case 3 Test Files 1 passed (1) Tests 6 passed (6) Start at 09:00:00 Duration 412ms Mocha is older and smaller: it only runs tests. You add an assertion library such as Chai and a mocking library such as Sinon. WebdriverIO and many Selenium suites use Mocha style describe and it. Mocha with Chai and Sinon · 🧪 Tests run 📄 .mocharc.json { \"spec\": \"test/**/*.spec.js\", \"timeout\": 2000 } 📄 test/cart.spec.js import { expect } from\"chai\"; import sinon from\"sinon\"; import { cartTotal } from\"../src/cart.js\"; describe(\"cartTotal @smoke\", function () { it(\"adds price times quantity\", function () { expect(cartTotal([{ price: 20, qty: 3 }])).to.equal(60); }); it(\"rejects bad input\", function () { expect(() => cartTotal({})).to.throw(TypeError, /must be an array/); }); }); describe(\"analytics\", function () { afterEach(() => sinon.restore()); it(\"tracks the checkout once\", function () { const analytics = { track() {} }; const spy = sinon.spy(analytics, \"track\"); analytics.track(\"checkout\", { total: 60 }); expect(spy.calledOnceWith(\"checkout\", { total: 60 })).to.be.true; }); it(\"stubs a slow call\", asyncfunction () { const api = { getStock: async () => { thrownewError(\"network\"); } }; sinon.stub(api, \"getStock\").resolves(7); expect(await api.getStock(\"PEN-01\")).to.equal(7); }); }); Terminal $ npx mocha && npx mocha --grep @smoke --reporter dot Terminal output cartTotal @smoke ✔ adds price times quantity ✔ rejects bad input analytics ✔ tracks the checkout once ✔ stubs a slow call 4 passing .. 2 passing node:test Jest Vitest Mocha Install nothing one package one package plus Chai and Sinon Assertions node:assert expect expect Chai Mocks mock.fn jest.fn vi.fn Sinon ES modules and TS yes needs set up yes yes Seen in libraries, small tools React, Node.js APIs Vite, Vue, new projects WebdriverIO, Selenium suites Lesson 5 of 8"],[19,"running","Running tests in different ways","🧪 QA essentialChoosing which tests run where, and reading coverage and JUnit reports, is daily work for test automation engineers. The same suite is run differently depending on the moment: everything before a merge, one file while you fix it, only smoke tests after a deploy, split across machines in CI. Every runner has options for this. Seven ways to run the same node:test suite · 🧪 Tests run 📄 test/cart.test.js import { test } from\"node:test\"; import assert from\"node:assert/strict\"; import { cartTotal } from\"../src/cart.js\"; test(\"checkout total with coupon\", { tags: [\"smoke\"] }, () => { assert.equal(cartTotal([{ price: 100, qty: 2 }], { coupon: \"SAVE10\" }), 180); }); test(\"checkout total without coupon\", () => { assert.equal(cartTotal([{ price: 100, qty: 2 }]), 200); }); test(\"empty cart\", { tags: [\"smoke\"] }, () => assert.equal(cartTotal([]), 0)); test.skip(\"multi currency carts\", () => {}); test.todo(\"gift cards\"); Terminal $ sh ways.sh # runs each command below Terminal output $ node --test --test-reporter=dot ...... $ node --test --test-name-pattern=checkout --test-reporter=spec ✔ checkout total with coupon ✔ checkout total without coupon ℹ tests 3 ℹ pass 3 ℹ fail 0 ℹ skipped 0 $ node --test --test-reporter=spec test/shipping.test.js ✔ free shipping from 499 $ node --test --experimental-test-tag-filter=smoke --test-reporter=spec ✔ checkout total with coupon ✔ empty cart $ node --test --test-shard=1/2 --test-reporter=spec 4 tests ran in shard 1 of 2 $ node --test --test-reporter=junit --test-reporter-destination=junit.xml <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"utf-8\"?> <testsuites> <testcase name=\"checkout total with coupon\" time=\"0.0005\" classname=\"test\" file=\"test/cart.test.js\"/> <testcase name=\"checkout total without coupon\" time=\"0.0005\" classname=\"test\" file=\"test/cart.test.js\"/> $ node --test --experimental-test-coverage --test-coverage-include=src/** --test-reporter=spec ℹ start of coverage report ℹ ---------------------------------------------------------- ℹ file | line % | branch % | funcs % | uncovered lines ℹ ---------------------------------------------------------- ℹ src | | | | ℹ cart.js | 100.00 | 85.71 | 100.00 | ℹ ---------------------------------------------------------- ℹ all files | 100.00 | 85.71 | 100.00 | ℹ ---------------------------------------------------------- ℹ end of coverage report Filter, shard and measure coverage with Jest · 🧪 Tests run 📄 src/cart.test.js const { cartTotal } = require(\"./cart\"); test(\"@smoke checkout total\", () => expect(cartTotal([{ price: 10, qty: 2 }])).toBe(20)); test(\"discount with SAVE10\", () => expect(cartTotal([{ price: 10, qty: 2 }], { coupon: \"SAVE10\" })).toBe(18)); Terminal $ sh ways.sh # runs each command below Terminal output $ npx jest --listTests src/cart.test.js src/shipping.test.js $ npx jest -t \"@smoke\" --verbose ✓ @smoke free shipping from 499 ✓ @smoke checkout total ○ skipped discount with SAVE10 Tests: 1 skipped, 2 passed, 3 total $ npx jest shipping PASS src/shipping.test.js Tests: 1 passed, 1 total $ npx jest --shard=2/2 PASS src/shipping.test.js Tests: 1 passed, 1 total $ npx jest --coverage --coverageThreshold={\"global\":{\"branches\":100}} File | % Stmts | % Branch | % Funcs | % Lines | Uncovered Line #s All files | 87.5 | 80 | 100 | 100 | cart.js | 87.5 | 80 | 100 | 100 | 2 Jest: Coverage for branches (80%) does not meet \"global\" threshold (100%) exit code: 1 The last command failed on purpose. No Jest test passes something that is not an array, so the error branch on line 2 of cart.js never ran. A coverage threshold turns that gap into a failing build, which is how teams stop coverage from slowly dropping. Goal node:test Jest Vitest Mocha one file node --test file jest path vitest run path mocha file by name or tag --test-name-pattern, tags -t \"name\" -t \"name\" --grep only this one test.only with --test-only test.only test.only it.only skip or todo test.skip, test.todo test.skip, test.todo same it.skip rerun on save --watch --watch vitest (default)"],[19,"running","Running tests in different ways","--watch split over machines --test-shard=1/3 --shard=1/3 --shard=1/3 by file lists stop at first failure no flag yet --bail --bail=1 --bail report for CI --test-reporter=junit jest-junit reporter --reporter=junit --reporter xunit coverage --experimental-test-coverage --coverage --coverage with c8 Running unit tests in CI · Reference # .github/workflows/tests.yml name: tests on: [push, pull_request] jobs: unit: runs-on: ubuntu-latest strategy: matrix: node: [22, 24, 26] # the same tests on three Node.js versions shard: [1, 2] # each version split over two machines steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v5 - uses: actions/setup-node@v5 with: { node-version: \"${{ matrix.node }}\", cache: npm } - run: npm ci - run: npx vitest run --shard=${{ matrix.shard }}/2 --coverage --reporter=junit --outputFile=junit.xml - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4 if: always() # keep the report even when tests fail with: { name: \"junit-${{ matrix.node }}-${{ matrix.shard }}\", path: junit.xml } What happens Six jobs run in parallel: three Node.js versions times two shards. The JUnit files feed the test report in the pull request. A test.only committed by mistake silently skips the rest of the suite in some runners. Add a lint rule or run CI with --forbid-only (Mocha, Playwright) to block it. Lesson 6 of 8"],[19,"web","Unit testing web (UI) code","🧪 QA essentialJestVitestTesting LibraryComponent tests cover UI rules at unit speed and use the same locator thinking as Playwright. UI components can be unit tested without a real browser. jsdom is a browser written in JavaScript that runs inside Node.js: it has document, events and forms, but draws nothing. Testing Library adds queries that find elements the way a user does, by role, label and text, and user-event types and clicks like a person. Unit testing UI code with jsdom and Testing Library · 🧪 Tests run 📄 vitest.config.js import { defineConfig } from\"vitest/config\"; exportdefault defineConfig({ test: { environment: \"jsdom\" } }); // a fake browser for every test 📄 src/coupon-form.js // A small UI component: a coupon box that validates and shows a messageexportfunction mountCouponForm(root, { applyCoupon }) { root.innerHTML = ` <form aria-label=\"Coupon\"> <label for=\"code\">Coupon code</label> <input id=\"code\" name=\"code\"> <button>Apply</button> <p role=\"status\"></p> </form>`; const form = root.querySelector(\"form\"); const status = root.querySelector('[role=\"status\"]'); form.addEventListener(\"submit\", async e => { e.preventDefault(); const code = form.elements.code.value.trim().toUpperCase(); if (!/^[A-Z0-9]{4,10}$/.test(code)) { status.textContent = \"Enter 4 to 10 letters or digits\"; return; } form.querySelector(\"button\").disabled = true; const result = await applyCoupon(code); status.textContent = result.ok ? `Saved ₹${result.saved}` : \"This coupon has expired\"; form.querySelector(\"button\").disabled = false; }); } 📄 src/coupon-form.test.js import { describe, it, expect, vi, beforeEach } from\"vitest\"; import { screen, waitFor } from\"@testing-library/dom\"; import userEvent from\"@testing-library/user-event\"; import { mountCouponForm } from\"./coupon-form.js\"; describe(\"coupon form\", () => { let applyCoupon; beforeEach(() => { document.body.innerHTML = \"<div id=app></div>\"; applyCoupon = vi.fn(); mountCouponForm(document.querySelector(\"#app\"), { applyCoupon }); }); it(\"rejects a code that is too short without calling the server\", async () => { await userEvent.type(screen.getByLabelText(\"Coupon code\"), \"ab\"); await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Apply\" })); expect(screen.getByRole(\"status\")).toHaveProperty(\"textContent\", \"Enter 4 to 10 letters or digits\"); expect(applyCoupon).not.toHaveBeenCalled(); }); it(\"trims and upper cases the code, then shows the saving\", async () => { applyCoupon.mockResolvedValue({ ok: true, saved: 50 }); await userEvent.type(screen.getByLabelText(\"Coupon code\"), \" save10 {Enter}\"); await waitFor(() => expect(screen.getByRole(\"status\").textContent).toBe(\"Saved ₹50\")); expect(applyCoupon).toHaveBeenCalledWith(\"SAVE10\"); }); it(\"shows an expiry message\", async () => { applyCoupon.mockResolvedValue({ ok: false }); await userEvent.type(screen.getByLabelText(\"Coupon code\"), \"OLD2020\"); await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Apply\" })); expect(await screen.findByText(\"This coupon has expired\")).toBeTruthy(); }); }); Terminal $ npx vitest run --reporter=verbose Terminal output RUN v5.0.5 project ✓ src/coupon-form.test.js > coupon form > rejects a code that is too short without calling the server ✓ src/coupon-form.test.js > coupon form > trims and upper cases the code, then shows the saving ✓ src/coupon-form.test.js > coupon form > shows an expiry message Test Files 1 passed (1) Tests 3 passed (3) Start at 09:00:00 Duration 412ms The same idea in Jest with a docblock · 🧪 Tests run 📄 src/badge.test.js /** * @jest-environment jsdom */const { renderCartBadge } = require(\"./badge\"); test.each([ [0, \"0\", true], [3, \"3\", false], [120, \"99+\", false], ])(\"count %i shows %j, hidden: %s\", (count, text, hidden) => { const el = document.createElement(\"span\"); renderCartBadge(el, count); expect(el.textContent).toBe(text); expect(el.hidden).toBe(hidden); expect(el.getAttribute(\"aria-label\")).toBe(`${count} items in cart`); }); Terminal $ npx jest --verbose Terminal output"],[19,"web","Unit testing web (UI) code","PASS src/badge.test.js ✓ count 0 shows \"0\", hidden: true ✓ count 3 shows \"3\", hidden: false ✓ count 120 shows \"99+\", hidden: false Test Suites: 1 passed, 1 total Tests: 3 passed, 3 total Snapshots: 0 total Time: 0.61 s Ran all test suites. Unit test with jsdomMilliseconds per test. Great for logic, validation messages, states such as disabled buttons. Cannot see layout, CSS, real scrolling or browser differences. Browser test with PlaywrightSeconds per test. Real rendering, real network, every browser. Keep these for the journeys that matter most. The query priority of Testing Library, role first, then label, then text, then test id, is the same advice Playwright gives for locators. Learn it once and use it in both kinds of test. Lesson 7 of 8"],[19,"api","Unit testing API code","🧪 QA essentialsupertestnockJestVitestMost API test frameworks start from supertest and nock, and their ideas carry over to every other tool. API code has two sides to test. Incoming: your routes. supertest sends requests straight into an Express app, without opening a port, and checks the status, headers and body. Outgoing: calls your code makes to other services. nock intercepts them and answers with whatever the test needs, including errors that are hard to produce for real. Unit testing API code: routes with supertest, outgoing calls with nock · 🧪 Tests run 📄 src/app.js import express from\"express\"; // The app receives its dependencies, so tests can hand it fakesexportfunction createApp({ stockService }) { const app = express(); app.use(express.json()); app.get(\"/api/products/:sku/stock\", async (req, res) => { try { const qty = await stockService.get(req.params.sku); if (qty == null) return res.status(404).json({ error: \"Unknown product\" }); res.json({ sku: req.params.sku, qty, inStock: qty > 0 }); } catch { res.status(503).json({ error: \"Stock service unavailable\" }); } }); return app; } 📄 test/app.test.js import { describe, it, expect, vi } from\"vitest\"; import request from\"supertest\"; import { createApp } from\"../src/app.js\"; describe(\"GET /api/products/:sku/stock (route, fake service)\", () => { const stockService = { get: vi.fn() }; const app = createApp({ stockService }); // no port, supertest calls the app directly it(\"200 with the quantity\", async () => { stockService.get.mockResolvedValue(4); const res = await request(app).get(\"/api/products/PEN-01/stock\").expect(200).expect(\"Content-Type\", /json/); expect(res.body).toEqual({ sku: \"PEN-01\", qty: 4, inStock: true }); }); it(\"404 for an unknown product\", async () => { stockService.get.mockResolvedValue(null); await request(app).get(\"/api/products/NOPE/stock\").expect(404, { error: \"Unknown product\" }); }); it(\"503 when the stock service fails\", async () => { stockService.get.mockRejectedValue(newError(\"timeout\")); await request(app).get(\"/api/products/PEN-01/stock\").expect(503); }); }); 📄 src/stock-client.js // Talks to another team's HTTP serviceexportfunction stockClient(baseUrl) { return { asyncget(sku) { const res = await fetch(`${baseUrl}/stock/${encodeURIComponent(sku)}`); if (res.status === 404) returnnull; if (!res.ok) thrownewError(`stock service answered ${res.status}`); return (await res.json()).available; }, }; } 📄 test/stock-client.test.js import { describe, it, expect, afterEach } from\"vitest\"; import nock from\"nock\"; import { stockClient } from\"../src/stock-client.js\"; describe(\"stockClient (outgoing HTTP, faked with nock)\", () => { const client = stockClient(\"https://stock.internal.example\"); afterEach(() => nock.cleanAll()); it(\"reads the available quantity\", async () => { const scope = nock(\"https://stock.internal.example\").get(\"/stock/PEN-01\").reply(200, { available: 12 }); expect(await client.get(\"PEN-01\")).toBe(12); expect(scope.isDone()).toBe(true); // the request really happened }); it(\"returns null on 404\", async () => { nock(\"https://stock.internal.example\").get(\"/stock/GONE\").reply(404); expect(await client.get(\"GONE\")).toBeNull(); }); it(\"throws on 500 so the route can answer 503\", async () => { nock(\"https://stock.internal.example\").get(\"/stock/PEN-01\").reply(500); await expect(client.get(\"PEN-01\")).rejects.toThrow(\"stock service answered 500\"); }); }); Terminal $ npx vitest run --reporter=verbose Terminal output RUN v5.0.5 project ✓ test/stock-client.test.js > stockClient (outgoing HTTP, faked with nock) > reads the available quantity ✓ test/app.test.js > GET /api/products/:sku/stock (route, fake service) > 200 with the quantity ✓ test/app.test.js > GET /api/products/:sku/stock (route, fake service) > 404 for an unknown product ✓ test/app.test.js > GET /api/products/:sku/stock (route, fake service) > 503 when the stock service fails ✓ test/stock-client.test.js > stockClient (outgoing HTTP, faked with nock) > returns null on 404 ✓"],[19,"api","Unit testing API code","test/stock-client.test.js > stockClient (outgoing HTTP, faked with nock) > throws on 500 so the route can answer 503 Test Files 2 passed (2) Tests 6 passed (6) Start at 09:00:00 Duration 412ms Passing stockService into createApp is what makes the route easy to test. Code that creates its own clients inside every function is much harder to test, so testable design starts with how dependencies are passed in. Chapter 21 goes further: tests against a running server, JSON Schema validation, contract checks and Playwright's API testing. Lesson 8 of 8"],[19,"scenario","Real world scenario: turn a bug report into a test","🧪 QA essential The situationA customer reports that splitting ₹100 between 3 friends shows ₹33.33 each, so ₹0.01 vanishes. You must fix it and make sure it never comes back. First write a test that reproduces the bug and watch it fail. A failing test proves the test can see the bug. Step 1: a test that reproduces the bug fails · 🔴 Red: the test fails, as it should 📄 package.json { \"name\": \"bug-4711\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"private\": true, \"type\": \"module\", \"scripts\": { \"test\": \"node --test --test-reporter=spec\" } } 📄 src/split.js // Bug 4711: \"Splitting ₹100 between 3 friends loses a rupee\"exportfunction splitBill(total, people) { const share = Math.floor((total / people) * 100) / 100; returnArray(people).fill(share); } 📄 test/split.test.js import { test } from\"node:test\"; import assert from\"node:assert/strict\"; import { splitBill } from\"../src/split.js\"; test(\"bug 4711: shares always add up to the total\", () => { const shares = splitBill(100, 3); const sum = Math.round(shares.reduce((a, b) => a + b) * 100) / 100; assert.equal(sum, 100); }); test(\"shares differ by at most one paisa\", () => { const shares = splitBill(100, 3); assert.ok(Math.max(...shares) - Math.min(...shares) <= 0.011); }); Terminal $ npm test Terminal output > bug-4711@1.0.0 test > node --test --test-reporter=spec ✖ bug 4711: shares always add up to the total ✔ shares differ by at most one paisa ℹ tests 2 ℹ suites 0 ℹ pass 1 ℹ fail 1 ℹ cancelled 0 ℹ skipped 0 ℹ todo 0 ℹ duration_ms 92.5 ✖ failing tests: test at test/split.test.js:5:1 ✖ bug 4711: shares always add up to the total AssertionError [ERR_ASSERTION]: Expected values to be strictly equal: 99.99 !== 100 Then fix the code. The cure is to work in whole paise and give the leftover paise to the first people. Step 2: fix the code and the same tests pass · 🟢 Green: fixed 📄 src/split.js // Work in paise (whole numbers), then hand the leftover paise out one by oneexportfunction splitBill(total, people) { const paise = Math.round(total * 100); const base = Math.floor(paise / people); const extra = paise - base * people; returnArray.from({ length: people }, (_, i) => (base + (i < extra ? 1 : 0)) / 100); } Terminal $ npm test Terminal output > bug-4711@1.0.0 test > node --test --test-reporter=spec ✔ bug 4711: shares always add up to the total ✔ shares differ by at most one paisa ✔ examples ℹ tests 3 ℹ suites 0 ℹ pass 3 ℹ fail 0 ℹ cancelled 0 ℹ skipped 0 ℹ todo 0 ℹ duration_ms 92.5 This red then green cycle is the heart of test driven development (TDD). The test now stays in the suite as a regression test, named after the bug, so anyone who breaks it again sees the history. 🧮"],[19,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 A suite has 4,000 unit tests at 2 ms each. How many seconds does it take run one after another? Multiply, then divide by 1000. Answer: 8 4000 × 2 = 8000 ms 8000 ÷ 1000 = 8 s C2 What does cartTotal([{ price: 20, qty: 3 }, { price: 250, qty: 1 }]) return? Price times quantity, added. Answer: 310 20 × 3 + 250 × 1 = 310 C1 A coupon takes 10% off a ₹199 cart. What is the total? 10% of 199 is 19.9. Answer: 179.1 199 − 19.9 = 179.1 C2 A CI matrix has 3 Node.js versions and 2 shards. How many jobs run? Multiply the lists. Answer: 6 3 × 2 = 6 C1 The suite takes 12 minutes on one machine. Split into 4 even shards, how many minutes until all are done? Each shard gets a quarter. Answer: 3 12 ÷ 4 = 3 C2 A file has 40 lines and the tests run 34 of them. What is the line coverage in percent? Covered over total. Answer: 85 34 ÷ 40 × 100 = 85 C1 ₹100 split between 3 people in whole paise. How many people get the extra paisa? Work in paise. Answer: 1 10000 ÷ 3 = 3333 remainder 1. One person gets 3334 paise, the others 3333. C2 Debounce of 300 ms. The user types at 0, 100 and 250 ms. At what time in ms does the search run? The timer restarts on every key. Answer: 550 The last key is at 250 ms. 250 + 300 = 550 🎯"],[19,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 What are the three steps of a typical unit test? AAA. Prepare the input, call the code, check the result. Q2 Which Jest matcher compares the contents of two objects? toBe compares identity. toEqual compares deeply. toBe uses Object.is. Q3 Which runner is built into Node.js? No install needed. node --test runs it, with node:assert for checks. Q1 A test must not send real emails. What do you use? Replace the outside service. A test double records the call without any real side effect. Q2 Which command runs only Jest tests whose name contains \"@smoke\"? t for test name. -t filters by test name, which is how tags in names work in Jest. Q3 What does --shard=2/3 do? Split across machines. Three CI machines each take one shard, so the suite finishes about three times faster. Q1 expect(promise).rejects.toThrow(\"x\") without await. What can happen? Chapter 11. Without await, the test ends before the assertion is checked. Q2 Why does supertest not need app.listen()? In process. supertest starts a temporary server internally or calls the handler directly, so tests never fight over ports. Q3 What can jsdom not test? It draws nothing. jsdom has no rendering, so overlap and visual bugs need a real browser. Q1 A suite has a test.only left in by mistake. What is the safest CI protection? Make it impossible to merge. Blocking .only stops one test from silently replacing the whole suite. Q2 Which statement about 100% line coverage is true? Running is not checking. A test with no assertions still counts as coverage. Coverage finds untested code, not correct code. Q3 Why write the failing test before fixing a bug? Red first. If the test passed before the fix, it would not protect you from the bug coming back."],[19,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[19,"deep","Deep equality","Write deepEqual(a, b) that returns true when two values have the same contents: numbers, strings, booleans, null, arrays and plain objects, nested to any depth. Key order in objects does not matter. Handle the simple case with Object.is, then compare keys and call yourself for each value. function deepEqual(a, b) { if (Object.is(a, b)) returntrue; if (typeof a !== \"object\" || typeof b !== \"object\" || a === null || b === null) returnfalse; if (Array.isArray(a) !== Array.isArray(b)) returnfalse; const ka = Object.keys(a), kb = Object.keys(b); if (ka.length !== kb.length) returnfalse; return ka.every(k => Object.hasOwn(b, k) && deepEqual(a[k], b[k])); } This is the heart of toEqual and assert.deepEqual. 🌿 Medium"],[19,"spy","A spy function","Write createSpy(impl) that returns a function. Calling it runs impl (if given) with the same arguments and returns its result. The function has a calls array of argument lists and a calledWith(...args) method. Functions are objects, so you can attach calls and calledWith to the function itself. function createSpy(impl) { const spy = (...args) => { spy.calls.push(args); return impl ? impl(...args) : undefined; }; spy.calls = []; spy.calledWith = (...want) => spy.calls.some(c => JSON.stringify(c) === JSON.stringify(want)); return spy; } jest.fn, vi.fn and mock.fn all work this way underneath. 🔥 Hard"],[19,"runner","A tiny test runner","Write async runTests(tests). Each test is { name, fn, only, skip }. If any test has only, run only those. Skip tests with skip. A test fails when fn throws or its promise rejects. Return { passed, failed, skipped } as counts. Check for only first. Use await t.fn() inside try so async failures are caught too. asyncfunction runTests(tests) { const focus = tests.some(t => t.only); const result = { passed: 0, failed: 0, skipped: 0 }; for (const t of tests) { if (t.skip || (focus && !t.only)) { result.skipped++; continue; } try { await t.fn(); result.passed++; } catch { result.failed++; } } return result; } The await matters: without it, the async failure in the second test would escape and count as a pass. 🧠 Expert"],[19,"shard","Split tests into shards","Write shard(files, spec) where spec is like \"2/3\". Sort the files by name, then give them out in turn: file 1 to shard 1, file 2 to shard 2, and so on, starting again after the last shard. Return the files of the requested shard, sorted. After sorting, file number i (from 0) goes to shard i % total + 1. function shard(files, spec) { const [index, total] = spec.split(\"/\").map(Number); return files.toSorted().filter((_, i) => i % total === index - 1); } Sorting first makes every machine agree on the split, so no file runs twice and none is missed."],[19,"shard","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. What a unit test isA unit test checks one small piece of code, usually one function or one class, on its own. It runs in milliseconds, needs no browser, server or database, and tells you exactly which piece broke. Mocks, spies, stubs and fake timersReal code calls other things: an email service, the clock, the network. A unit test swaps them for test doubles so the test is fast and predictable and never sends a real email. JestJest is the most used JavaScript test runner. It has matchers built in (expect(x).toBe(y)), table driven tests with test.each, mocks for whole modules, fake timers, snapshots and coverage. By default it uses CommonJS. Vitest and MochaVitest has almost the same API as Jest (vi.fn instead of jest.fn) but runs ES modules and TypeScript with no set up and is very fast. It is the natural choice for Vite, Vue and modern React projects. Running tests in different waysThe same suite is run differently depending on the moment: everything before a merge, one file while you fix it, only smoke tests after a deploy, split across machines in CI. Every runner has options for this. Unit testing web (UI) codeUI components can be unit tested without a real browser. jsdom is a browser written in JavaScript that runs inside Node.js: it has document, events and forms, but draws nothing. Unit testing API codeAPI code has two sides to test. Incoming: your routes. supertest sends requests straight into an Express app, without opening a port, and checks the status, headers and body. Outgoing: calls your code makes to other services. Real world scenario: turn a bug report into a testFirst write a test that reproduces the bug and watch it fail. A failing test proves the test can see the bug."],[19,"comic","Small checks, run often","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Many fast unit tests, doubles for what is slow or outside, and a failing test before every fix."],[19,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. ⚛️A React team Runs 4,000 Vitest tests in 40 seconds on every save and only 60 Playwright journeys before each deploy. 🏦A payments API Uses nock to simulate bank timeouts and declines that would be impossible to trigger with the real bank sandbox. 🐞The bug that came back A rounding bug in invoices returned twice until the team added a regression test named after the ticket. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Unit Testing: node:test, Jest, Vitest and Mocha\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 10Welcome This chapter covers unit testing with node test, Jest, Vitest and Mocha, test doubles, many ways to run tests, and unit tests for web and API code. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[20,"","Automated API Testing with JavaScript","What an API test checks, a CRUD flow against a running server, JSON Schema validation with Ajv that catches breaking changes, data driven negative and boundary tests, login with tokens and checking side effects and conflicts, Playwright request with webServer, Cypress, Newman and k6 in brief, percentiles for response times, and an order flow written as named subtests. · API testing · REST · CRUD · fetch · supertest · Playwright request · Ajv · JSON Schema · contract test · negative testing · boundary · auth · 401 · 409 · p95 · response time · cleanup · Newman · k6 🟨 JavaScript›🧪 Testing with JavaScript›Chapter 21 of 31 🔌 Step 6 · Topic 2 of 3 · Advanced"],[20,"top","","Test one real shop API with four tools: fetch and node:test for a CRUD flow, Ajv for JSON Schema checks, Vitest and supertest for negative and boundary tables, Jest for auth and stock, Playwright request with a web server, response time percentiles, and a full order flow that cleans up after itself. ⏱️ About 22 min read📚 8 parts🎬 1 animations▶ 2 runnable examples🧪 5 real test runs🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 8 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Every test on this page ran against a real Express server when the page was built. Pick the tool your team uses, the ideas are the same in all of them. 1What an API test checks 2Testing a running server: a CRUD flow 3Checking the shape with JSON Schema 4Negative, boundary and data driven tests 5Authentication, state and conflicts 6API testing with Playwright 7Response times 8Real world scenario: buying the last two mugs Lesson 1 of 8"],[20,"what","What an API test checks","🧪 QA essentialAPI tests give the most coverage per minute of any automated test. An API test sends real HTTP requests to a running service and checks the answers. It sits in the middle of the test pyramid: much faster and steadier than a browser test, and it checks the real routes, validation, database and auth together, which unit tests cannot. Check Example Status code 201 after create, 404 for a missing id, 400 for bad input Headers Content-Type: application/json, Location, Cache-Control Body values the created product has the name that was sent, trimmed Body shape every product has an integer id and a positive price (JSON Schema) Side effects the stock really went down, the deleted item is really gone Negative cases missing fields, wrong types, boundaries, broken JSON Security 401 without a token, 403 or 404 for someone else's data Speed 95% of requests under 200 ms Press Play to follow one API test through the system. The last step is the one beginners forget: a response that says \"created\" proves nothing until you read the item back. 🎬 Animated diagram"],[20,"what","One API test, from request to the last check","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 6 This chapter tests one small shop API, written with Express in shop-api.js, with four different tools. It is the same app as in chapter 13, with login and orders added. Lesson 2 of 8"],[20,"live","Testing a running server: a CRUD flow","🧪 QA essentialnode:testfetchA CRUD flow against a real server is the first suite most API testers write. The most direct API test starts the server, sends requests with fetch and checks each answer. Listening on port 0 lets the system pick a free port, so many suites can run side by side. A CRUD flow against a running server · 🧪 Tests run 📄 test/products.test.js import { describe, it, before, after } from\"node:test\"; import assert from\"node:assert/strict\"; import { createShopApi } from\"../src/shop-api.js\"; let server, base; before(async () => { server = createShopApi().listen(0); // port 0: any free port, so parallel runs never clashawaitnewPromise(r => server.once(\"listening\", r)); base = `http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}/api`; }); after(() => server.close()); const json = (method, body) => ({ method, headers: { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" }, body: JSON.stringify(body) }); describe(\"products CRUD\", () => { let created; it(\"POST creates a product (201, Location, body)\", async () => { const res = await fetch(`${base}/products`, json(\"POST\", { name: \" Desk lamp \", price: 1299, stock: 5 })); assert.equal(res.status, 201); created = await res.json(); assert.equal(res.headers.get(\"location\"), `/api/products/${created.id}`); assert.deepEqual(created, { id: created.id, name: \"Desk lamp\", price: 1299, stock: 5 }); }); it(\"GET returns what was created\", async () => { const res = await fetch(`${base}/products/${created.id}`); assert.equal(res.status, 200); assert.match(res.headers.get(\"content-type\"), /application\\/json/); assert.equal((await res.json()).name, \"Desk lamp\"); }); it(\"PATCH changes one field and keeps the rest\", async () => { const res = await fetch(`${base}/products/${created.id}`, json(\"PATCH\", { price: 999 })); assert.deepEqual(await res.json(), { ...created, price: 999 }); }); it(\"search finds it, case insensitive\", async () => { const body = await (await fetch(`${base}/products?q=LAMP`)).json(); assert.equal(body.total, 1); assert.equal(body.items[0].id, created.id); }); it(\"DELETE removes it, then GET is 404\", async () => { assert.equal((await fetch(`${base}/products/${created.id}`, { method: \"DELETE\" })).status, 204); const res = await fetch(`${base}/products/${created.id}`); assert.equal(res.status, 404); assert.deepEqual(await res.json(), { error: \"Product not found\" }); }); }); Terminal $ npm test Terminal output > api-live@1.0.0 test > node --test --test-reporter=spec ▶ products CRUD ✔ POST creates a product (201, Location, body) ✔ GET returns what was created ✔ PATCH changes one field and keeps the rest ✔ search finds it, case insensitive ✔ DELETE removes it, then GET is 404 ✔ products CRUD ℹ tests 5 ℹ suites 1 ℹ pass 5 ℹ fail 0 ℹ cancelled 0 ℹ skipped 0 ℹ todo 0 ℹ duration_ms 245.1 The tests run in order and share created, so together they cover the whole life of one product: Create, Read, Update and Delete, also called CRUD. Checking the GET after the DELETE proves the delete really happened, which the 204 alone does not. Tests that depend on each other stop at the first failure and cannot run alone. Use this style for one flow on purpose. For everything else, each test creates its own data. Lesson 3 of 8"],[20,"schema","Checking the shape with JSON Schema","🧪 QA essentialAjvJestPlaywrightSchema checks catch breaking API changes that value checks miss. Checking every field by hand gets long, and it misses fields you did not think of. A JSON Schema describes the allowed shape once: required fields, types, ranges and whether extra fields are allowed. Ajv checks a response against it in one call and lists every problem. Validate a response with JSON Schema · ▶ Runs importAjvfrom\"ajv\"; const productSchema = { type: \"object\", required: [\"id\", \"name\", \"price\", \"stock\"], additionalProperties: false, properties: { id: { type: \"integer\", minimum: 1 }, name: { type: \"string\", minLength: 1, maxLength: 120 }, price: { type: \"number\", exclusiveMinimum: 0 }, stock: { type: \"integer\", minimum: 0 }, }, }; const listSchema = { type: \"object\", required: [\"items\", \"total\"], properties: { items: { type: \"array\", items: productSchema }, total: { type: \"integer\" } }, }; const ajv = newAjv({ allErrors: true }); const validateList = ajv.compile(listSchema); const today = { items: [{ id: 1, name: \"Gel pen\", price: 20, stock: 100 }], total: 1 }; console.log(\"today:\", validateList(today)); // A backend change: price became a string, stock was renamed, and a field was addedconst afterRelease = { items: [{ id: 1, name: \"Gel pen\", price: \"20.00\", qty: 100, internalCost: 7 }], total: 1 }; console.log(\"after release:\", validateList(afterRelease)); for (const e of validateList.errors) console.log(\" \", e.instancePath || \"(root)\", e.message, e.params.additionalProperty ?? e.params.missingProperty ?? \"\"); Output today: true after release: false /items/0 must have required property 'stock' stock /items/0 must NOT have additional properties qty /items/0 must NOT have additional properties internalCost /items/0/price must be number This kind of check, the response still matches what clients expect, is a simple contract test. It catches a renamed field or a number turned into a string before the mobile app crashes on it. OpenAPI files already contain these schemas, so many teams generate them instead of writing them by hand. Lesson 4 of 8"],[20,"negative","Negative, boundary and data driven tests","🧪 QA essentialVitestsupertestInput validation is where APIs most often fail, and these tests are cheap to write as tables. Most API bugs hide in input the developer did not expect. Negative tests send wrong data on purpose and check that it is refused with a clear 400, not saved and not answered with a 500. A table of cases with it.each keeps dozens of them readable. Negative and boundary tests, data driven · 🧪 Tests run 📄 test/validation.test.js import { describe, it, expect } from\"vitest\"; import request from\"supertest\"; import { createShopApi } from\"../src/shop-api.js\"; const app = createShopApi(); describe(\"POST /api/products rejects bad input with 400\", () => { it.each([ [\"no body\", undefined, \"name is required\"], [\"empty name\", { name: \" \", price: 10 }, \"name is required\"], [\"price as text\", { name: \"Pen\", price: \"10\" }, \"price must be a positive number\"], [\"zero price\", { name: \"Pen\", price: 0 }, \"price must be a positive number\"], [\"negative stock\", { name: \"Pen\", price: 10, stock: -1 }, \"stock must be a whole number, 0 or more\"], [\"decimal stock\", { name: \"Pen\", price: 10, stock: 1.5 }, \"stock must be a whole number, 0 or more\"], ])(\"%s\", async (_, body, problem) => { const res = await request(app).post(\"/api/products\").send(body); expect(res.status).toBe(400); expect(res.body.problems).toContain(problem); }); it(\"lists every problem at once\", async () => { const res = await request(app).post(\"/api/products\").send({ price: -1, stock: \"a\" }); expect(res.body.problems).toHaveLength(3); }); }); describe(\"boundaries and odd input\", () => { it(\"accepts the smallest positive price\", async () => { await request(app).post(\"/api/products\").send({ name: \"Sticker\", price: 0.01 }).expect(201); }); it(\"ignores an id sent by the client\", async () => { const res = await request(app).post(\"/api/products\").send({ id: 1, name: \"Clip\", price: 2 }).expect(201); expect(res.body.id).not.toBe(1); }); it(\"handles broken JSON with a 400, not a crash\", async () => { await request(app).post(\"/api/products\").set(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\").send(\"{ name: \").expect(400); }); it(\"returns 404 JSON for an id that is not a number\", async () => { await request(app).get(\"/api/products/abc\").expect(404).expect(\"Content-Type\", /json/); }); }); Terminal $ npx vitest run --reporter=verbose Terminal output RUN v5.0.5 project ✓ test/validation.test.js > POST /api/products rejects bad input with 400 > no body ✓ test/validation.test.js > POST /api/products rejects bad input with 400 > empty name ✓ test/validation.test.js > POST /api/products rejects bad input with 400 > price as text ✓ test/validation.test.js > POST /api/products rejects bad input with 400 > zero price ✓ test/validation.test.js > POST /api/products rejects bad input with 400 > negative stock ✓ test/validation.test.js > POST /api/products rejects bad input with 400 > decimal stock ✓ test/validation.test.js > POST /api/products rejects bad input with 400 > lists every problem at once ✓ test/validation.test.js > boundaries and odd input > accepts the smallest positive price ✓ test/validation.test.js > boundaries and odd input > ignores an id sent by the client ✓ test/validation.test.js > boundaries and odd input > handles broken JSON with a 400, not a crash ✓ test/validation.test.js > boundaries and odd input > returns 404 JSON for an id that is not a number Test Files 1 passed (1) Tests 11 passed (11) Start at 09:00:00 Duration 540ms Ideas for every field: missing, null, empty, only spaces, wrong type (\"10\" for 10), zero, negative, decimals where whole numbers are expected, very long text, unicode and emoji, and the exact limits plus one each side. For the whole request: no body, broken JSON, an extra id the client should not control, and the wrong Content-Type. Lesson 5 of 8"],[20,"auth","Authentication, state and conflicts","🧪 QA essentialJestsupertestAuth checks and state changes are where API bugs are most costly. Real APIs need a login and change data as they go. Log in once in beforeAll, keep the token, and send it with each request. Then check not only the answer but also its effect, such as the stock that went down, and the conflicts that follow, such as ordering more than is left. Auth, state changes and conflicts with Jest and supertest · 🧪 Tests run 📄 jest.config.js exportdefault { transform: {} }; // run ES modules as they are, no Babel 📄 test/orders.test.js import { describe, test, expect, beforeAll } from\"@jest/globals\"; import request from\"supertest\"; import { createShopApi } from\"../src/shop-api.js\"; const app = createShopApi(); let token; beforeAll(async () => { const res = await request(app).post(\"/api/login\").send({ email: \"asha@shop.example\", password: \"s3cret\" }).expect(200); token = res.body.token; }); describe(\"authentication\", () => { test(\"wrong password is 401\", () => request(app).post(\"/api/login\").send({ email: \"asha@shop.example\", password: \"nope\" }).expect(401)); test(\"no token is 401\", () => request(app).post(\"/api/orders\").send({ items: [] }).expect(401)); test(\"a made up token is 401\", () => request(app).post(\"/api/orders\").set(\"Authorization\", \"Bearer not-a-real-token\").send({ items: [] }).expect(401)); }); describe(\"orders\", () => { test(\"placing an order lowers the stock\", async () => { const order = await request(app).post(\"/api/orders\").auth(token, { type: \"bearer\" }) .send({ items: [{ productId: 2, qty: 2 }] }).expect(201); expect(order.body).toMatchObject({ total: 398, status: \"placed\" }); const mug = await request(app).get(\"/api/products/2\"); expect(mug.body.stock).toBe(1); }); test(\"ordering more than the stock is 409\", async () => { const res = await request(app).post(\"/api/orders\").auth(token, { type: \"bearer\" }) .send({ items: [{ productId: 2, qty: 5 }] }).expect(409); expect(res.body.error).toBe(\"Only 1 left of Coffee mug\"); }); }); Terminal $ NODE_OPTIONS=--experimental-vm-modules npx jest --verbose Terminal output PASS test/orders.test.js authentication ✓ wrong password is 401 ✓ no token is 401 ✓ a made up token is 401 orders ✓ placing an order lowers the stock ✓ ordering more than the stock is 409 Test Suites: 1 passed, 1 total Tests: 5 passed, 5 total Snapshots: 0 total Time: 0.92 s Ran all test suites. supertest's .auth(token, { type: \"bearer\" }) sets the Authorization header for you. The same suite can point at a deployed server by passing a URL string instead of the app: request(\"https://staging.shop.example\"). Lesson 6 of 8"],[20,"playwright","API testing with Playwright","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypressPostmanMost Playwright suites mix API calls into UI tests for setup and checks, and some teams run their whole API suite in it. Playwright is known for browsers, but its request fixture is also a complete API client. It shares the base URL and config with UI tests, and its webServer option starts the app before the tests and stops it afterwards. UI tests often use it to create data through the API before opening the page. API tests with Playwright, which starts the server itself · 🧪 Tests run 📄 playwright.config.js import { defineConfig } from\"@playwright/test\"; exportdefault defineConfig({ testDir: \"tests\", reporter: \"list\", workers: 1, use: { baseURL: \"http://127.0.0.1:3917\", extraHTTPHeaders: { Accept: \"application/json\" } }, webServer: { // Playwright starts the API and waits for it command: \"node src/server.js\", url: \"http://127.0.0.1:3917/api/products\", env: { PORT: \"3917\" }, reuseExistingServer: !process.env.CI, }, }); 📄 tests/shop.api.spec.js import { test, expect } from\"@playwright/test\"; test.describe(\"shop API\", () => { let token; test.beforeAll(async ({ request }) => { const res = await request.post(\"/api/login\", { data: { email: \"asha@shop.example\", password: \"s3cret\" } }); expect(res.ok()).toBeTruthy(); token = (await res.json()).token; }); test(\"lists products with a cache header\", async ({ request }) => { const res = await request.get(\"/api/products\"); expect(res.status()).toBe(200); expect(res.headers()[\"cache-control\"]).toBe(\"max-age=60\"); const body = await res.json(); expect(body.total).toBe(body.items.length); expect(body.items).toContainEqual(expect.objectContaining({ name: \"Gel pen\" })); }); test(\"a customer cannot read another customer's order\", async ({ request }) => { const res = await request.get(\"/api/orders/9999\", { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } }); expect(res.status()).toBe(404); }); test(\"place an order and read it back\", async ({ request }) => { const headers = { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` }; const created = await request.post(\"/api/orders\", { headers, data: { items: [{ productId: 1, qty: 3 }] } }); expect(created.status()).toBe(201); const order = await created.json(); const again = await request.get(`/api/orders/${order.id}`, { headers }); await expect(again).toBeOK(); expect(await again.json()).toEqual(order); }); }); Terminal $ npx playwright test Terminal output Running 3 tests using 1 worker ✓ 1 tests/shop.api.spec.js:16:3 › shop API › lists products with a cache header ✓ 2 tests/shop.api.spec.js:28:3 › shop API › a customer cannot read another customer's order ✓ 3 tests/shop.api.spec.js:38:3 › shop API › place an order and read it back 3 passed The second test checks access to another customer's order. This API answers 404 rather than 403, so it does not even admit the order exists, which is a common and good choice. The same ideas in Cypress, Newman and k6 · Reference // Cypress: cy.request in a spec file it(\"creates a product\", () => { cy.request(\"POST\", \"/api/products\", { name: \"Pen\", price: 10 }).then(res => { expect(res.status).to.eq(201); expect(res.body).to.have.property(\"id\"); }); }); // Postman collections from the command line, for example in CI// npx newman run shop.postman_collection.json -e staging.postman_environment.json --reporters cli,junit// k6 load tests are also written in JavaScript// import http from \"k6/http\";// export const options = { vus: 50, duration: \"1m\", thresholds: { http_req_duration: [\"p(95)<300\"] } };// export default () => http.get(\"https://staging.shop.example/api/products\"); Lesson 7 of 8"],[20,"performance","Response times","🧪 QA essentialk6Response time checks catch slow endpoints before users do. A correct answer that takes five seconds is still a bug. Measure each request and look at percentiles, not the average: p95 is the time under which 95% of requests finish. One very slow request barely moves the average but is exactly what a user notices. Response times and percentiles · ▶ Runs 📄 shop-api.mjs import express from\"express\"; import { randomUUID } from\"node:crypto\"; exportfunction createShopApi() { const app = express(); app.use(express.json()); const users = newMap([[\"asha@shop.example\", { id: 1, email: \"asha@shop.example\", password: \"s3cret\", role: \"customer\" }]]); const sessions = newMap(); const products = newMap([ [1, { id: 1, name: \"Gel pen\", price: 20, stock: 100 }], [2, { id: 2, name: \"Coffee mug\", price: 199, stock: 3 }], ]); const orders = newMap(); let nextProduct = 3, nextOrder = 1001; const auth = (req, res, next) => { const user = sessions.get(req.get(\"authorization\")?.replace(/^Bearer /, \"\")); if (!user) return res.status(401).json({ error: \"Log in first\" }); req.user = user; next(); }; app.post(\"/api/login\", (req, res) => { const user = users.get(req.body?.email); if (!user || user.password !== req.body.password) return res.status(401).json({ error: \"Invalid credentials\" }); const token = randomUUID(); sessions.set(token, user); res.json({ token, user: { id: user.id, email: user.email } }); }); app.get(\"/api/products\", (req, res) => { let list = [...products.values()]; if (req.query.q) list = list.filter(p => p.name.toLowerCase().includes(String(req.query.q).toLowerCase())); res.set(\"Cache-Control\", \"max-age=60\").json({ items: list, total: list.length }); }); app.get(\"/api/products/:id\", (req, res) => { const p = products.get(Number(req.params.id)); p ? res.json(p) : res.status(404).json({ error: \"Product not found\" }); }); app.post(\"/api/products\", (req, res) => { const { name, price, stock = 0 } = req.body ?? {}; const problems = []; if (typeof name !== \"string\" || !name.trim()) problems.push(\"name is required\"); if (typeof price !== \"number\" || !(price > 0)) problems.push(\"price must be a positive number\"); if (!Number.isInteger(stock) || stock < 0) problems.push(\"stock must be a whole number, 0 or more\"); if (problems.length) return res.status(400).json({ error: \"Validation failed\", problems }); const p = { id: nextProduct++, name: name.trim(), price, stock }; products.set(p.id, p); res.status(201).location(`/api/products/${p.id}`).json(p); }); app.patch(\"/api/products/:id\", (req, res) => { const p = products.get(Number(req.params.id)); if (!p) return res.status(404).json({ error: \"Product not found\" }); if (\"price\"in req.body && !(req.body.price > 0)) return res.status(400).json({ error: \"Validation failed\", problems: [\"price must be a positive number\"] }); Object.assign(p, req.body, { id: p.id }); res.json(p); }); app.delete(\"/api/products/:id\", (req, res) => { products.delete(Number(req.params.id)) ? res.sendStatus(204) : res.status(404).json({ error: \"Product not found\" }); }); app.post(\"/api/orders\", auth, (req, res) => { const lines = req.body?.items ?? []; for (const { productId, qty } of lines) { const p = products.get(productId); if (!p) return res.status(400).json({ error: `Unknown product ${productId}` }); if (qty > p.stock) return res.status(409).json({ error: `Only ${p.stock} left of ${p.name}` }); } for (const { productId, qty } of lines) products.get(productId).stock -= qty; const total = lines.reduce((s, l) => s + products.get(l.productId).price * l.qty, 0); const order = { id: nextOrder++, userId: req.user.id, items: lines, total, status: \"placed\" }; orders.set(order.id, order); res.status(201).json(order); }); app.get(\"/api/orders/:id\", auth, (req, res) => { const o = orders.get(Number(req.params.id)); if (!o) return res.status(404).json({ error: \"Order not found\" }); if (o.userId !== req.user.id) return res.status(403).json({ error: \"Not your order\" }); res.json(o); }); return app; } 📄 main.mjs"],[20,"performance","Response times","import { createShopApi } from\"./shop-api.mjs\"; const server = createShopApi().listen(0); awaitnewPromise(r => server.once(\"listening\", r)); const url = `http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}/api/products?q=pen`; asyncfunction timed() { const t = performance.now(); const res = await fetch(url); await res.arrayBuffer(); return { ms: performance.now() - t, status: res.status }; } // 200 requests, 20 at a timeconst results = []; for (let batch = 0; batch < 10; batch++) { results.push(...awaitPromise.all(Array.from({ length: 20 }, timed))); } const times = results.map(r => r.ms).sort((a, b) => a - b); const pct = p => times[Math.ceil((p / 100) * times.length) - 1]; console.log(\"requests:\", results.length, \"| errors:\", results.filter(r => r.status !== 200).length); console.log(\"p50 under 50 ms:\", pct(50) < 50, \"| p95 under 200 ms:\", pct(95) < 200); console.log(\"slowest is the max:\", Math.max(...times) === times.at(-1)); server.close(); Terminal $ node main.mjs Output requests: 200 | errors: 0 p50 under 50 ms: true | p95 under 200 ms: true slowest is the max: true Number Meaning p50 (median) a typical request p95, p99 the slow tail that real users hit every day error rate failed requests divided by all requests throughput requests handled per second Times measured on a laptop or a shared CI machine vary a lot. Use generous limits in functional suites to catch big slowdowns, and leave real load testing to tools such as k6 against a production like environment. Lesson 8 of 8"],[20,"scenario","Real world scenario: buying the last two mugs","🧪 QA essential The situationCustomers complain that sold out items can still be ordered. You must test the whole flow: add a product with two in stock, log in, order both, confirm the stock is 0, confirm a second order is refused, read the order back, and leave no test data behind. An end to end API flow with steps · 🧪 Tests run 📄 test/order-flow.test.js import { test, before, after } from\"node:test\"; import assert from\"node:assert/strict\"; import { createShopApi } from\"../src/shop-api.js\"; let server, api; before(async () => { server = createShopApi().listen(0); awaitnewPromise(r => server.once(\"listening\", r)); const base = `http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}/api`; // A tiny client: JSON in, JSON out, the token added when we have onelet token; api = async (method, path, body) => { const res = await fetch(base + path, { method, headers: { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\", ...(token && { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` }) }, body: body && JSON.stringify(body), }); const data = res.status === 204 ? null : await res.json(); if (path === \"/login\" && res.ok) token = data.token; return { status: res.status, data }; }; }); after(() => server.close()); test(\"a customer buys the last two mugs\", async t => { let productId, orderId; await t.test(\"admin adds stock: a new mug with 2 left\", async () => { const r = await api(\"POST\", \"/products\", { name: \"Blue mug\", price: 249, stock: 2 }); assert.equal(r.status, 201); productId = r.data.id; }); await t.test(\"customer logs in\", async () => { assert.equal((await api(\"POST\", \"/login\", { email: \"asha@shop.example\", password: \"s3cret\" })).status, 200); }); await t.test(\"orders both mugs\", async () => { const r = await api(\"POST\", \"/orders\", { items: [{ productId, qty: 2 }] }); assert.equal(r.status, 201); assert.equal(r.data.total, 498); orderId = r.data.id; }); await t.test(\"stock is now 0\", async () => { assert.equal((await api(\"GET\", `/products/${productId}`)).data.stock, 0); }); await t.test(\"a second order for the same mug is refused with 409\", async () => { const r = await api(\"POST\", \"/orders\", { items: [{ productId, qty: 1 }] }); assert.equal(r.status, 409); assert.equal(r.data.error, \"Only 0 left of Blue mug\"); }); await t.test(\"the order can be read back\", async () => { const r = await api(\"GET\", `/orders/${orderId}`); assert.deepEqual([r.status, r.data.status, r.data.items], [200, \"placed\", [{ productId, qty: 2 }]]); }); await t.test(\"clean up the test product\", async () => { assert.equal((await api(\"DELETE\", `/products/${productId}`)).status, 204); }); }); Terminal $ npm test Terminal output > order-flow@1.0.0 test > node --test --test-reporter=spec ▶ a customer buys the last two mugs ✔ admin adds stock: a new mug with 2 left ✔ customer logs in ✔ orders both mugs ✔ stock is now 0 ✔ a second order for the same mug is refused with 409 ✔ the order can be read back ✔ clean up the test product ✔ a customer buys the last two mugs ℹ tests 8 ℹ suites 0 ℹ pass 8 ℹ fail 0 ℹ cancelled 0 ℹ skipped 0 ℹ todo 0 ℹ duration_ms 245.1 Subtests with t.test name each step, so a failure says exactly where the flow broke. The last step deletes the product the test created, so the shared environment stays clean for everyone else. 🧮"],[20,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 Two mugs at ₹199 each. What total should the order show? Multiply. Answer: 398 2 × 199 = 398 C2 Stock is 3 and an order takes 2. What should GET show for stock? Subtract. Answer: 1 3 − 2 = 1 C1 A field needs tests for missing, null, empty, wrong type and two boundaries each side of one limit. How many cases? Count them. Answer: 6 missing, null, empty, wrong type: 4. just below and on the limit: 2. 4 + 2 = 6 C2 200 requests, 3 failed. What is the error rate in percent? Failed over total. Answer: 1.5 3 ÷ 200 × 100 = 1.5 C1 20 sorted response times. Which position (from 1) is p95? Ceiling of 0.95 times the count. Answer: 19 ⌈0.95 × 20⌉ = ⌈19⌉ = 19 C2 900 API tests of 120 ms each, run with 4 parallel workers. About how many seconds? Total time, then divide by workers. Answer: 27 900 × 0.12 = 108 s 108 ÷ 4 = 27 s C1 The schema check after the release found how many errors? Read the output. Answer: 4 price is a string, stock is missing. qty and internalCost are extra fields. That is 4. C2 An endpoint allows 100 requests per minute per user. A test suite makes 450 calls as one user. At least how many minutes does it need? Round up. Answer: 5 450 ÷ 100 = 4.5 You cannot use half a minute window, so 5. 🎯"],[20,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 After a successful DELETE, which status do you usually expect? Nothing to return. 204 No Content. Then a GET should be 404. Q2 Why listen on port 0 in tests? Parallel runs. A fixed port fails when another test or app already uses it. Q3 Which tool validates a response against a JSON Schema? Another JSON validator. Ajv compiles a schema into a fast validate function. Q1 POST with price \"10\" (a string) should return? Wrong type. The API must refuse it clearly rather than save or crash. Q2 Why GET after DELETE? Check the side effect. A 204 only says the server claims success. The GET checks it. Q3 In Playwright, what does the webServer option do? Set up for you. It runs the command and waits for the URL to answer. Q1 What does additionalProperties: false catch? Extra keys. It flags anything not listed, for example an internalCost field that should never reach clients. Q2 Ordering 5 when 1 is left should return? The request is valid but clashes with the current state. 409 says the request conflicts with the state of the resource. Q3 100 requests: 99 take 20 ms and one takes 3,000 ms. Which number shows the problem best? The tail. The average is about 50 ms and hides it. The slow tail is what p99 and the max reveal. Q1 Why might an API answer 404 instead of 403 for someone else's order? Information leaks. Hiding existence stops attackers from guessing valid ids. Q2 Which is the best way to make API tests independent? No hidden coupling. Own data means a test can run alone, in parallel and in any order. Q3 supertest is given a URL string instead of the app. What changes? Same tests, other target. request(\"https://staging...\") runs the suite against a deployed environment."],[20,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[20,"check","Check a response","Write checkResponse(res, expected). res is { status, headers, body }. expected may have status, contentType (a substring of the content-type header) and body (fields that must be equal). Return a list of problem strings, empty when all is well. Collect messages in an array instead of stopping at the first problem. function checkResponse(res, expected) { const problems = []; if (expected.status && res.status !== expected.status) problems.push(`status ${res.status}, expected ${expected.status}`); const type = res.headers[\"content-type\"] ?? \"\"; if (expected.contentType && !type.includes(expected.contentType)) problems.push(`content-type ${type}, expected ${expected.contentType}`); for (const [k, v] ofObject.entries(expected.body ?? {})) { if (res.body[k] !== v) problems.push(`body.${k} is ${JSON.stringify(res.body[k])}, expected ${JSON.stringify(v)}`); } return problems; } Reporting every problem at once saves a fix, rerun, fix cycle when an API changes. 🌿 Medium"],[20,"schema-mini","A tiny schema validator","Write validate(schema, value) for schemas like { id: \"integer\", name: \"string\", price: \"number\", tags: \"array\" }. Every key is required. Return a list of errors such as \"price should be number\" or \"name is missing\", in the order of the schema. A small object of type checking functions, one per type name, keeps it short. function validate(schema, value) { const errors = []; const is = { integer: v => Number.isInteger(v), number: v => typeof v === \"number\", string: v => typeof v === \"string\", array: v => Array.isArray(v), }; for (const [key, type] ofObject.entries(schema)) { if (!(key in value)) errors.push(`${key} is missing`); elseif (!is[type](value[key])) errors.push(`${key} should be ${type}`); } return errors; } Ajv does the same thing, with many more keywords and much faster compiled checks. 🔥 Hard"],[20,"pct","Percentiles","Write percentile(times, p) using the nearest rank method: sort a copy of the times, then take the value at position ⌈p ÷ 100 × n⌉ counted from 1. An empty list gives null. Sort numbers with (a, b) => a - b, and subtract 1 to turn the rank into an index. function percentile(times, p) { if (!times.length) returnnull; const sorted = times.toSorted((a, b) => a - b); return sorted[Math.ceil((p / 100) * sorted.length) - 1]; } Note how p90 is 9 ms but p95 is 3,000 ms: one slow request decides the tail. 🧠 Expert"],[20,"diff","What changed in the response?","Write diffKeys(before, after) comparing two flat objects. Return { added, removed, typeChanged }, each a sorted list of key names. A key is in typeChanged when it exists in both but typeof differs. Three filters over the key lists. function diffKeys(before, after) { const a = Object.keys(before), b = Object.keys(after); return { added: b.filter(k => !(k in before)).sort(), removed: a.filter(k => !(k in after)).sort(), typeChanged: a.filter(k => k in after && typeof before[k] !== typeof after[k]).sort(), }; } Comparing a saved response from the last release with today's is a quick way to spot breaking changes, the same idea as the schema check."],[20,"diff","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. What an API test checksAn API test sends real HTTP requests to a running service and checks the answers. It sits in the middle of the test pyramid: much faster and steadier than a browser test, and it checks the real routes, validation, database and auth together, which unit tests ... Testing a running server: a CRUD flowThe most direct API test starts the server, sends requests with fetch and checks each answer. Listening on port 0 lets the system pick a free port, so many suites can run side by side. Checking the shape with JSON SchemaChecking every field by hand gets long, and it misses fields you did not think of. A JSON Schema describes the allowed shape once: required fields, types, ranges and whether extra fields are allowed. Negative, boundary and data driven testsMost API bugs hide in input the developer did not expect. Negative tests send wrong data on purpose and check that it is refused with a clear 400, not saved and not answered with a 500. A table of cases with it.each keeps dozens of them readable. Authentication, state and conflictsReal APIs need a login and change data as they go. Log in once in beforeAll, keep the token, and send it with each request. API testing with PlaywrightPlaywright is known for browsers, but its request fixture is also a complete API client. It shares the base URL and config with UI tests, and its webServer option starts the app before the tests and stops it afterwards. Response timesA correct answer that takes five seconds is still a bug. Measure each request and look at percentiles, not the average: p95 is the time under which 95% of requests finish. One very slow request barely moves the average but is exactly what a user notices. Real world scenario: buying the last two mugsSubtests with t.test name each step, so a failure says exactly where the flow broke. The last step deletes the product the test created, so the shared environment stays clean for everyone else."],[20,"comic","Ask the API, check every part of the answer","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Status, headers, body, shape, side effects, the bad cases, and clean up after yourself."],[20,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 📱A mobile release saved A schema check failed when price turned into a string. The Android app would have crashed for every user on the old version. 🛍️Overselling An order flow test found that two quick orders could both buy the last item. The team added a database lock and the test now guards it. ⚡Fast feedback A team runs 900 API tests in 2 minutes on every pull request and keeps only 40 browser journeys. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Automated API Testing with JavaScript\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 10Welcome This chapter tests one shop API with fetch, supertest, Jest, Vitest and Playwright, from CRUD flows to schemas, bad input, auth and response times. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[21,"","UI Automation: Playwright, Selenium, Cypress and WebdriverIO","How each tool reaches the browser, a Playwright suite with role based locators and web first assertions, a strict mode failure, page.route for errors and fake data, waitForRequest, a page object with a fixture, retries that report flaky tests, the Playwright command line, Selenium WebDriver with Mocha, explicit waits, XPath and screenshots, the same tests in Cypress and WebdriverIO, and a sharded GitHub Actions workflow. · UI automation · end to end · Playwright · Selenium · WebDriver · Cypress · WebdriverIO · locator · getByRole · auto-waiting · explicit wait · strict mode · page.route · cy.intercept · page object · fixture · retries · flaky · trace · shard · CI 🟨 JavaScript›🧪 Testing with JavaScript›Chapter 22 of 31 🎭 Step 6 · Topic 3 of 3 · Advanced"],[21,"top","","Drive a real browser against a small demo shop. Playwright locators, auto-waiting assertions, strict mode, network mocking, page objects, fixtures and retries, all run for real, Selenium WebDriver with explicit waits and a page object, run for real in Chrome, plus the same tests in Cypress and WebdriverIO and a sharded CI set up. ⏱️ About 26 min read📚 7 parts🎬 2 animations🧪 6 real test runs🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 7 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers The Playwright and Selenium tests on this page ran in a real Chromium against the demo shop when the page was built. Cypress is shown with the same tests so you can compare. 1The tools and how they drive a browser 2Playwright: locators and web first assertions 3Controlling the network 4Page objects, fixtures and retries 5Selenium WebDriver 6Cypress and WebdriverIO 7Real world scenario: a UI suite in CI Lesson 1 of 7"],[21,"tools","The tools and how they drive a browser","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightSeleniumCypressWebdriverIOPicking a tool and understanding how it reaches the browser is the first decision in every UI automation project. A UI automation tool starts a real browser and acts like a user: it opens pages, types, clicks and reads what is shown. The four big JavaScript tools differ in how they talk to the browser, and that explains most of their strengths and limits. 🎬 Animated diagram"],[21,"tools","How each tool reaches the browser","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 3 Playwright Selenium WebDriver Cypress WebdriverIO Talks to the browser its own protocol (CDP for Chromium, patched Firefox and WebKit) W3C WebDriver through a driver program, BiDi coming runs inside the browser next to your app WebDriver or BiDi Browsers Chromium, Firefox, WebKit every major browser, also real Safari and mobile through Appium Chromium family, Firefox, WebKit (experimental) all, plus mobile through Appium Waiting auto-waits for every action and assertion explicit waits you write retries commands and assertions auto-waits on actions Languages JS, TS, Python, Java, .NET JS, Java, Python, C#, Ruby JS, TS JS, TS Async style await everything await everything chained commands, no await await everything Extras API testing, traces, network mocking, parallel by default Grid for many machines, the longest history time travel debugger, network stubs huge plugin set, mobile and desktop apps Every example below runs against the same small shop: a login form, a product list that arrives after 600 ms, a cart and an order button. The demo shop page (site/index.html) · The app under test <!doctype html> <htmllang=\"en\"> <head><metacharset=\"utf-8\"><title>Demo Shop</title></head> <body> <header> <h1>Demo Shop</h1> <spanid=\"hello\"></span> <ahref=\"#cart\"aria-label=\"Cart\">🛒 <spandata-testid=\"cart-count\">0</span></a> </header> <formid=\"login\"aria-label=\"Sign in\"> <label>Email <inputname=\"email\"type=\"email\"required></label> <label>Password <inputname=\"password\"type=\"password\"required></label> <button>Sign in</button> <prole=\"alert\"hidden></p> </form> <mainid=\"shop\"hidden> <pclass=\"loading\">Loading products…</p> <ularia-label=\"Products\"></ul> <sectionaria-label=\"Cart summary\"> <p>Total: <outputid=\"total\">₹0</output></p> <buttonid=\"checkout\"disabled>Place order</button> <prole=\"status\"></p> </section> </main> <scriptsrc=\"app.js\"></script> </body> </html> What happens A login form, a product list that loads after 600 ms, a cart and a Place order button. server.js serves it with Express and a small API. Lesson 2 of 7"],[21,"playwright","Playwright: locators and web first assertions","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightLocators and auto-waiting assertions are the core of every Playwright test. A Playwright test receives a page, finds elements with locators and checks them with web first assertions. A locator is a recipe, not an element: it is looked up again on every use, so it never goes stale. Every action waits until the element is visible, enabled and not moving, and every expect(locator) retries until it passes or times out. A first Playwright suite · 🧪 Tests run 📄 playwright.config.js import { defineConfig, devices } from\"@playwright/test\"; exportdefault defineConfig({ testDir: \"tests\", reporter: \"list\", workers: 1, // one worker keeps the output in the same order on this page use: { baseURL: \"http://localhost:4301\" }, projects: [{ name: \"chromium\", use: { ...devices[\"Desktop Chrome\"] } }], webServer: { command: \"node server.js\", url: \"http://localhost:4301\", env: { PORT: \"4301\" }, reuseExistingServer: !process.env.CI, }, }); 📄 tests/shop.spec.js import { test, expect } from\"@playwright/test\"; test.beforeEach(async ({ page }) => { await page.goto(\"/\"); }); test(\"wrong password shows an error\", async ({ page }) => { await page.getByLabel(\"Email\").fill(\"asha@shop.example\"); await page.getByLabel(\"Password\").fill(\"wrong\"); await page.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Sign in\" }).click(); await expect(page.getByRole(\"alert\")).toHaveText(\"Wrong email or password\"); }); test(\"log in, add two products and place an order\", async ({ page }) => { await page.getByLabel(\"Email\").fill(\"asha@shop.example\"); await page.getByLabel(\"Password\").fill(\"s3cret\"); await page.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Sign in\" }).click(); await expect(page.getByText(\"Hello, asha\")).toBeVisible(); // The list arrives 600 ms later. No sleep: the assertion keeps retrying.const products = page.getByTestId(\"product\"); await expect(products).toHaveCount(3); await products.filter({ hasText: \"Coffee mug\" }).getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Add to cart\" }).click(); await products.filter({ hasText: \"Gel pen\" }).getByRole(\"button\").click(); await expect(page.getByTestId(\"cart-count\")).toHaveText(\"2\"); await expect(page.locator(\"#total\")).toHaveText(\"₹219\"); await page.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Place order\" }).click(); await expect(page.getByText(/^Order \\d+ placed$/)).toBeVisible(); }); Terminal $ npx playwright test Terminal output Running 2 tests using 1 worker ✓ 1 [chromium] › tests/shop.spec.js:7:1 › wrong password shows an error ✓ 2 [chromium] › tests/shop.spec.js:17:1 › log in, add two products and place an order 2 passed The product list arrives after 600 ms, yet there is no sleep anywhere. toHaveCount(3) simply keeps checking. filter({ hasText }) narrows a list to one card, and the button is found inside that card, which is far steadier than \"the second button on the page\". 🎬 Try it"],[21,"playwright","Sleep or wait for the element","Move the slider to change when the Pay button appears, then compare the two strategies. The button appears after 1800 ms sleep(2000) waited 2000 ms, wasted 200 ms auto-wait clicked at about 1800 ms timeout 5000 the most it would ever wait The fixed sleep passes but wastes 200 ms on every run. Auto-waiting moves on the moment the button is ready."],[21,"playwright","Strict mode","If a locator matches more than one element, an action or assertion fails instead of guessing. Here an <output> element also has the role status, which is easy to miss. Strict mode stops an ambiguous locator · 🧪 Tests run, one fails on purpose 📄 tests/strict.spec.js import { test, expect } from\"@playwright/test\"; test(\"a locator that matches two elements\", async ({ page }) => { await page.goto(\"/\"); await page.getByLabel(\"Email\").fill(\"asha@shop.example\"); await page.getByLabel(\"Password\").fill(\"s3cret\"); await page.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Sign in\" }).click(); await page.getByTestId(\"product\").first().getByRole(\"button\").click(); await expect(page.getByRole(\"status\")).toHaveText(\"\"); // which status? }); Terminal $ npx playwright test Terminal output Running 1 test using 1 worker ✘ 1 [chromium] › tests/strict.spec.js:2:1 › a locator that matches two elements 1) [chromium] › tests/strict.spec.js:2:1 › a locator that matches two elements ─────────────────── Error: expect(locator).toHaveText(expected) failed Locator: getByRole('status') Expected: \"\" Error: strict mode violation: getByRole('status') resolved to 2 elements: 1) <output id=\"total\">₹20</output> aka getByLabel('Cart summary').getByText('₹') 2) <p role=\"status\"></p> aka getByRole('status').filter({ hasText: /^$/ }) 1 failed The error lists both matches and suggests a precise locator. Fix it by narrowing: getByText(/^Order \\d+ placed$/), getByRole(\"status\").filter({ hasText: \"Order\" }) or a test id. Lesson 3 of 7"],[21,"network","Controlling the network","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypressNetwork mocking makes error states testable and UI tests independent of backend data. Playwright sees every request the page makes. page.route can answer a request with fake data or an error, so you can test states that are hard to produce for real: a broken API, an empty list, a very long name. waitForRequest and waitForResponse check what the page sends. Mock, break and spy on the network · 🧪 Tests run 📄 tests/network.spec.js import { test, expect } from\"@playwright/test\"; asyncfunction logIn(page) { await page.goto(\"/\"); await page.getByLabel(\"Email\").fill(\"asha@shop.example\"); await page.getByLabel(\"Password\").fill(\"s3cret\"); await page.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Sign in\" }).click(); } test(\"shows a friendly message when the products API fails\", async ({ page }) => { await page.route(\"**/api/products\", route => route.fulfill({ status: 503, body: \"down\" })); await logIn(page); await expect(page.getByText(\"Could not load products. Try again later.\")).toBeVisible(); }); test(\"renders whatever the API returns, even a huge price\", async ({ page }) => { await page.route(\"**/api/products\", route => route.fulfill({ json: [{ id: 9, name: \"Gold watch\", price: 1234567 }] })); await logIn(page); await expect(page.getByTestId(\"product\")).toHaveText(/Gold watch\\s*₹12,34,567/); }); test(\"sends the right order to the server\", async ({ page }) => { await logIn(page); await page.getByTestId(\"product\").filter({ hasText: \"Desk lamp\" }).getByRole(\"button\").click(); const [request] = awaitPromise.all([ page.waitForRequest(\"**/api/orders\"), page.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Place order\" }).click(), ]); expect(request.postDataJSON()).toEqual({ items: [3] }); await expect(page.getByText(\"Order 1042 placed\")).toBeVisible(); }); Terminal $ npx playwright test Terminal output Running 3 tests using 1 worker ✓ 1 [chromium] › tests/network.spec.js:10:1 › shows a friendly message when the products API fails ✓ 2 [chromium] › tests/network.spec.js:20:1 › renders whatever the API returns, even a huge price ✓ 3 [chromium] › tests/network.spec.js:33:1 › sends the right order to the server 3 passed Mocked tests check how the UI behaves, not whether the backend works. Keep a few tests that use the real API end to end, and use mocks for the error and edge cases. Cypress does the same with cy.intercept. Lesson 4 of 7"],[21,"framework","Page objects, fixtures and retries","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightFixtures, page objects and honest flaky reporting are what keep large UI suites maintainable. As a suite grows, repeated steps move into page objects (chapter 9) and fixtures. A Playwright fixture prepares something a test needs, here a logged in shop, and passes it in as a parameter. Tests become short and read like a test case. Page objects and fixtures · 🧪 Tests run 📄 pages/shop.page.js import { expect } from\"@playwright/test\"; exportclassShopPage { constructor(page) { this.page = page; this.products = page.getByTestId(\"product\"); this.cartCount = page.getByTestId(\"cart-count\"); this.total = page.locator(\"#total\"); this.placeOrder = page.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Place order\" }); } async open() { awaitthis.page.goto(\"/\"); } async logIn(email, password) { awaitthis.page.getByLabel(\"Email\").fill(email); awaitthis.page.getByLabel(\"Password\").fill(password); awaitthis.page.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Sign in\" }).click(); await expect(this.products.first()).toBeVisible(); // wait until the shop is ready } async add(name) { awaitthis.products.filter({ hasText: name }).getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Add to cart\" }).click(); } } 📄 fixtures.js import { test as base } from\"@playwright/test\"; import { ShopPage } from\"./pages/shop.page.js\"; // A fixture builds what a test needs and hands it over as a parameterexportconst test = base.extend({ shop: async ({ page }, use) => { const shop = newShopPage(page); await shop.open(); await shop.logIn(\"asha@shop.example\", \"s3cret\"); await use(shop); // the test runs here// anything after use() is clean up }, }); export { expect } from\"@playwright/test\"; 📄 tests/cart.spec.js import { test, expect } from\"../fixtures.js\"; test(\"one item\", async ({ shop }) => { await shop.add(\"Gel pen\"); await expect(shop.total).toHaveText(\"₹20\"); }); test(\"totals use Indian digit grouping\", async ({ shop }) => { await shop.add(\"Desk lamp\"); await shop.add(\"Desk lamp\"); await expect(shop.cartCount).toHaveText(\"2\"); await expect(shop.total).toHaveText(\"₹2,598\"); }); for (const [name, price] of [[\"Gel pen\", \"₹20\"], [\"Coffee mug\", \"₹199\"], [\"Desk lamp\", \"₹1,299\"]]) { test(`price of ${name}`, async ({ shop }) => { await expect(shop.products.filter({ hasText: name }).locator(\".price\")).toHaveText(price); }); } Terminal $ npx playwright test Terminal output Running 5 tests using 1 worker ✓ 1 [chromium] › tests/cart.spec.js:2:1 › one item ✓ 2 [chromium] › tests/cart.spec.js:8:1 › totals use Indian digit grouping ✓ 3 [chromium] › tests/cart.spec.js:17:3 › price of Gel pen ✓ 4 [chromium] › tests/cart.spec.js:17:3 › price of Coffee mug ✓ 5 [chromium] › tests/cart.spec.js:17:3 › price of Desk lamp 5 passed"],[21,"framework","Flaky tests and retries","A flaky test passes and fails without any code change. Playwright can retry a failed test, and a test that fails and then passes is reported as flaky, not hidden as passed. Retries and flaky tests · 🧪 Tests run 📄 tests/retry.spec.js import { test, expect } from\"@playwright/test\"; test(\"fails on the first try, passes on the retry\", async ({ page }, testInfo) => { await page.goto(\"/\"); // Simulate a hiccup: the first attempt sees a different headingconst expected = testInfo.retry === 0 ? \"Demo Shop!\" : \"Demo Shop\"; await expect(page.getByRole(\"heading\", { level: 1 })).toHaveText(expected, { timeout: 1000 }); }); Terminal $ npx playwright test Terminal output Running 1 test using 1 worker ✘ 1 [chromium] › tests/retry.spec.js:2:1 › fails on the first try, passes on the retry ✓ 2 [chromium] › tests/retry.spec.js:2:1 › fails on the first try, passes on the retry (retry #1) 1) [chromium] › tests/retry.spec.js:2:1 › fails on the first try, passes on the retry ──────────── Error: expect(locator).toHaveText(expected) failed Locator: getByRole('heading', { level: 1 }) Expected: \"Demo Shop!\" Received: \"Demo Shop\" Timeout: 1000ms 1 flaky [chromium] › tests/retry.spec.js:2:1 › fails on the first try, passes on the retry ───────────── Retries keep a pipeline green while you investigate, they are not a fix. With trace: \"on-first-retry\", Playwright records a trace of the retry: every action, DOM snapshot, network call and console message, which usually shows the cause in minutes. Running Playwright in different ways · Reference npx playwright test # everything, headlessnpx playwright test tests/cart.spec.js # one filenpx playwright test -g \"Indian digit\"# tests whose title matchesnpx playwright test --grep @smoke # tags in titles or { tag: \"@smoke\" }npx playwright test --project=firefox # one browser from the confignpx playwright test --headed --workers=1# watch it in a real windownpx playwright test --debug # step through with the inspectornpx playwright test --ui # UI mode: time travel through each stepnpx playwright test --last-failed # only what failed last timenpx playwright test --repeat-each=20# hunt for flakinessnpx playwright test --shard=2/4# one quarter of the suite, for CInpx playwright show-report # open the HTML reportnpx playwright show-trace trace.zip # open a recorded tracenpx playwright codegen http://localhost:4173# record actions into code Lesson 5 of 7"],[21,"selenium","Selenium WebDriver","🧪 QA essentialSeleniumMany enterprise suites are Selenium, and its explicit waits and locators are the base of every WebDriver tool. Selenium WebDriver sends each command to a driver program (chromedriver, geckodriver, safaridriver), which controls the browser. Every call is a network round trip and returns a promise, so every line is awaited. Selenium does not wait for you: use explicit waits with driver.wait(until...) for anything that appears later. Selenium WebDriver with Mocha, explicit waits and a page object · 🧪 Tests run 📄 selenium/driver.js // One place that knows how to start Chrome for every Selenium test.// Selenium Manager downloads a matching chromedriver by itself. CI machines// can point CHROMEDRIVER and CHROME_BIN at fixed copies instead.import { Builder } from\"selenium-webdriver\"; import chrome from\"selenium-webdriver/chrome.js\"; exportasyncfunction buildDriver() { const options = new chrome.Options().addArguments(\"--headless=new\", \"--no-sandbox\", \"--window-size=1280,800\"); if (process.env.CHROME_BIN) options.setChromeBinaryPath(process.env.CHROME_BIN); const builder = newBuilder().forBrowser(\"chrome\").setChromeOptions(options); if (process.env.CHROMEDRIVER) builder.setChromeService(new chrome.ServiceBuilder(process.env.CHROMEDRIVER)); return builder.build(); } 📄 selenium/pages/ShopPage.js import { By, until } from\"selenium-webdriver\"; exportclassShopPage { staticEMAIL = By.name(\"email\"); staticPASSWORD = By.name(\"password\"); staticSIGN_IN = By.css(\"#login button\"); staticALERT = By.css(\"[role=alert]\"); staticCART_COUNT = By.css(\"[data-testid=cart-count]\"); staticTOTAL = By.id(\"total\"); static addButtonFor = name => By.xpath(`//li[h2='${name}']//button`); constructor(driver, baseUrl) { this.driver = driver; this.baseUrl = baseUrl; } async open() { awaitthis.driver.get(this.baseUrl); } async logIn(email, password) { awaitthis.driver.findElement(ShopPage.EMAIL).sendKeys(email); awaitthis.driver.findElement(ShopPage.PASSWORD).sendKeys(password); awaitthis.driver.findElement(ShopPage.SIGN_IN).click(); } async add(name) { // Explicit wait: the products arrive after a delayconst button = awaitthis.driver.wait(until.elementLocated(ShopPage.addButtonFor(name)), 5000); awaitthis.driver.wait(until.elementIsVisible(button), 5000); await button.click(); } async cartCount(expected) { const el = awaitthis.driver.findElement(ShopPage.CART_COUNT); awaitthis.driver.wait(until.elementTextIs(el, expected), 5000); return el.getText(); } async alertText() { const el = awaitthis.driver.wait(until.elementLocated(ShopPage.ALERT), 5000); awaitthis.driver.wait(until.elementIsVisible(el), 5000); return el.getText(); } } 📄 selenium/shop.spec.js import { expect } from\"chai\"; import { buildDriver } from\"./driver.js\"; import { ShopPage } from\"./pages/ShopPage.js\"; import { startShop } from\"../server.js\"; describe(\"demo shop with Selenium WebDriver\", function () { let driver, server, shop; before(async () => { server = await startShop(); // any free port driver = await buildDriver(); }); beforeEach(async () => { shop = newShopPage(driver, `http://localhost:${server.address().port}`); await shop.open(); }); after(async () => { await driver?.quit(); // always close the browser server?.close(); }); it(\"shows an error for a wrong password\", async () => { await shop.logIn(\"asha@shop.example\", \"wrong\"); expect(await shop.alertText()).to.equal(\"Wrong email or password\"); }); it(\"adds two products to the cart\", async () => { await shop.logIn(\"asha@shop.example\", \"s3cret\"); await shop.add(\"Coffee mug\"); await shop.add(\"Desk lamp\"); expect(await shop.cartCount(\"2\")).to.equal(\"2\"); expect(await driver.findElement(ShopPage.TOTAL).getText()).to.equal(\"₹1,498\"); }); it(\"can read the page title and take a screenshot\", async () => { expect(await driver.getTitle()).to.equal(\"Demo Shop\"); const png = await driver.takeScreenshot(); // base64 text of a PNG image expect(Buffer.from(png, \"base64\").subarray(1, 4).toString()).to.equal(\"PNG\"); }); }); Terminal"],[21,"selenium","Selenium WebDriver","$ npm test Terminal output > shop-selenium@1.0.0 test > mocha --timeout 30000 \"selenium/**/*.spec.js\" demo shop with Selenium WebDriver ✔ shows an error for a wrong password ✔ adds two products to the cart ✔ can read the page title and take a screenshot 3 passing (4s) Need Selenium Find By.id, By.css, By.xpath, By.name, By.linkText Wait until.elementLocated, elementIsVisible, elementTextIs, titleIs, urlContains Act click(), sendKeys(), clear(), driver.actions() for hover and drag Frames and windows switchTo().frame(), switchTo().window(), switchTo().alert() Many machines Selenium Grid: new Builder().usingServer(gridUrl) Avoid driver.manage().setTimeouts({ implicit }) together with explicit waits. Mixing the two makes waits add up in surprising ways. Pick explicit waits. Lesson 6 of 7"],[21,"cypress","Cypress and WebdriverIO","🧪 QA essentialCypressWebdriverIOCypress is widely used by front end teams and its command queue works differently from every other tool. Cypress runs inside the browser, in the same loop as your app. Commands such as cy.get are not promises: Cypress queues them and runs them in order, retrying each query and assertion until it passes. That is why you never write await in Cypress, and why plain variables do not hold element values (use .then or aliases). The same tests in Cypress · Shown only: Cypress needs its own app download, which this page build cannot fetch // cypress/e2e/shop.cy.js describe(\"demo shop\", () => { beforeEach(() => { cy.intercept(\"GET\", \"/api/products\").as(\"products\"); // watch the request cy.visit(\"/\"); }); it(\"logs in and orders\", () => { cy.get(\"input[name=email]\").type(\"asha@shop.example\"); cy.get(\"input[name=password]\").type(\"s3cret\"); cy.contains(\"button\", \"Sign in\").click(); cy.wait(\"@products\").its(\"response.statusCode\").should(\"eq\", 200); cy.contains(\"[data-testid=product]\", \"Coffee mug\").find(\"button\").click(); cy.get(\"[data-testid=cart-count]\").should(\"have.text\", \"1\"); // retries until true cy.contains(\"button\", \"Place order\").click(); cy.contains(/^Order \\d+ placed$/).should(\"be.visible\"); }); it(\"shows a message when the API fails\", () => { cy.intercept(\"GET\", \"/api/products\", { statusCode: 503 }); cy.login(\"asha@shop.example\", \"s3cret\"); // a custom command, see below cy.contains(\"Could not load products\").should(\"be.visible\"); }); }); // cypress/support/commands.jsCypress.Commands.add(\"login\", (email, password) => { cy.get(\"input[name=email]\").type(email); cy.get(\"input[name=password]\").type(password); cy.contains(\"button\", \"Sign in\").click(); }); What happens Run with npx cypress open (interactive) or npx cypress run (headless, for CI). Commands are queued and retried automatically, and are never awaited. WebdriverIO is a WebDriver based framework with a friendlier API than raw Selenium, built in waiting, and support for mobile apps through Appium. The same test in WebdriverIO · Reference // test/specs/shop.e2e.js (WebdriverIO with Mocha)import { browser, $, $$, expect } from\"@wdio/globals\"; describe(\"demo shop\", () => { it(\"adds a product\", async () => { await browser.url(\"/\"); await $(\"input[name=email]\").setValue(\"asha@shop.example\"); await $(\"input[name=password]\").setValue(\"s3cret\"); await $(\"button=Sign in\").click(); // button with this textawait expect($$(\"[data-testid=product]\")).toBeElementsArrayOfSize(3); await $(\"//li[h2='Gel pen']//button\").click(); await expect($(\"[data-testid=cart-count]\")).toHaveText(\"1\"); }); }); // npx wdio run wdio.conf.js --spec test/specs/shop.e2e.js Lesson 7 of 7"],[21,"scenario","Real world scenario: a UI suite in CI","🧪 QA essential The situationThe team wants every pull request checked by the browser suite in under ten minutes, with a report anyone can open when something fails. Browser tests in CI · Reference # .github/workflows/e2e.yml name: e2e on: [pull_request] jobs: playwright: runs-on: ubuntu-latest strategy: fail-fast: false matrix: shard: [1, 2, 3, 4] steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v5 - uses: actions/setup-node@v5 with: { node-version: 24, cache: npm } - run: npm ci - run: npx playwright install --with-deps chromium - run: npx playwright test --shard=${{ matrix.shard }}/4 env: { BASE_URL: \"${{ vars.STAGING_URL }}\" } - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4 if: ${{ !cancelled() }} with: { name: \"report-${{ matrix.shard }}\", path: playwright-report/ } What happens Four machines each run a quarter of the suite. The HTML report, with traces of failed tests, is kept for every run. Sharding splits the tests across four machines. fail-fast: false lets every shard finish, so one failure does not hide others. The report is uploaded even when tests fail, and the traces inside it show exactly what the browser saw. A healthy UI suite: tests independent of each other, data created through the API rather than the UI, stable locators, no fixed sleeps, network mocks only for edge cases, flaky tests tracked and fixed within days, and the slowest tests reviewed every sprint. 🧮"],[21,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 A suite has 240 UI tests of 30 seconds each. How many minutes does it take on one worker? Seconds to minutes. Answer: 120 240 × 30 = 7200 s 7200 ÷ 60 = 120 min C2 The same suite split over 4 shards. How many minutes? Divide. Answer: 30 120 ÷ 4 = 30 C1 Two Desk lamps at ₹1,299. What total should the cart show, without the symbol and commas? Multiply. Answer: 2598 2 × 1299 = 2598, shown as ₹2,598. C2 A fixed sleep of 3 s is used 50 times, but the page is ready after 0.8 s on average. How many seconds are wasted per run? Waste per sleep times count. Answer: 110 3 − 0.8 = 2.2 2.2 × 50 = 110 C1 A test passes 47 times out of 50 runs with --repeat-each=50. What is its failure rate in percent? 3 failures out of 50. Answer: 6 3 ÷ 50 × 100 = 6 C2 With a failure rate of 6% per test and retries: 1, what is the chance a run still fails? Give the percent with one decimal. Both attempts must fail. Answer: 0.4 0.06 × 0.06 = 0.0036 That is 0.36%, about 0.4%. C1 A suite has 200 tests, each with a 1% chance of a random failure. What is the chance, in percent rounded to a whole number, that at least one fails? One minus the chance that all pass. Answer: 87 0.99200 ≈ 0.134 1 − 0.134 = 0.866, about 87%. C2 Three projects (chromium, firefox, webkit) times 4 shards. How many CI jobs? Multiply. Answer: 12 3 × 4 = 12 🎯"],[21,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 Which Playwright locator is preferred for a button labelled \"Sign in\"? What does a user see? Role and accessible name survive layout and style changes. Q2 Which tool runs its test code inside the browser? Same loop as the app. Cypress loads the test into the browser next to the application. Q3 What does await expect(locator).toHaveText(\"2\") do when the text is still \"1\"? Web first assertions. It polls the page until the condition is met, up to the timeout. Q1 In Selenium, the product list appears after 600 ms. What should you use? Explicit waits. Selenium does not auto-wait, so wait for the exact condition you need. Q2 What does strict mode in Playwright do? Ambiguity. It refuses to guess which element you meant. Q3 Why use page.route in a UI test? Control the network. It lets you test error and empty states without breaking the real backend. Q1 A Playwright test failed once, then passed on retry. How is it reported? Honest reporting. Playwright marks it flaky so the team can investigate. Q2 Why do you never write await before cy.get in Cypress? Command queue. Cypress schedules commands and runs them in order itself. Q3 What does a Playwright fixture defined with test.extend give a test? Set up and tear down. Code before use() prepares, code after use() cleans up. Q1 Why mix implicit and explicit waits in Selenium carefully? Two waiting mechanisms. The Selenium docs advise not to mix them because of unpredictable wait times. Q2 Which statement about locators in Playwright is true? Recipe, not element. This is why Playwright avoids the stale element errors common in Selenium. Q3 fail-fast: false in a CI matrix means? See all failures. Otherwise one failing shard cancels the others and hides their results."],[21,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[21,"pick","Pick the best locator","Write bestLocator(el) for an element description like { role, name, testId, id, css } (any may be missing). Return a Playwright locator string in this order of preference: getByRole(\"role\", { name: \"name\" }) when both role and name exist, then getByTestId(\"x\"), then locator(\"#id\"), then locator(\"css\"). A chain of if checks with early returns, most preferred first. function bestLocator(el) { if (el.role && el.name) return `getByRole(\"${el.role}\", { name: \"${el.name}\" })`; if (el.testId) return `getByTestId(\"${el.testId}\")`; if (el.id) return `locator(\"#${el.id}\")`; return `locator(\"${el.css}\")`; } This is the order Playwright and Testing Library recommend, because it follows what users see. 🌿 Medium"],[21,"wait","A waitFor helper","Write async waitFor(check, { timeout = 1000, interval = 20 } = {}). Call check() (it may be async) until it returns a truthy value, and return that value. If the timeout passes first, throw an Error with the message \"Timed out after 1000 ms\" (with the real timeout). A loop with a deadline, an await on the check and a short sleep between tries. asyncfunction waitFor(check, { timeout = 1000, interval = 20 } = {}) { const deadline = Date.now() + timeout; while (Date.now() < deadline) { const value = await check(); if (value) return value; awaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, interval)); } thrownewError(`Timed out after ${timeout} ms`); } This is what expect.poll, Selenium waits and Cypress retries all do inside. 🔥 Hard"],[21,"filter","filter({ hasText }) for cards","A page has product cards as objects { text, buttons }. Write filterCards(cards, hasText) returning the cards whose text contains hasText (a string, case sensitive, or a RegExp). Then write strictOne(list) that returns the only item, or throws \"strict mode violation: N elements\" when the list does not have exactly one. instanceof RegExp tells the two kinds of filter apart. function filterCards(cards, hasText) { return cards.filter(c => (hasText instanceofRegExp ? hasText.test(c.text) : c.text.includes(hasText))); } function strictOne(list) { if (list.length !== 1) thrownewError(`strict mode violation: ${list.length} elements`); return list[0]; } Filtering, then insisting on exactly one match, is how Playwright keeps a test from clicking the wrong card. 🧠 Expert"],[21,"flaky","Find the flaky tests","Write flakyReport(runs). runs is a list of runs, each an object from test name to \"passed\" or \"failed\". Return the names of tests that both passed and failed at least once, sorted, each with its failure rate in percent rounded to a whole number, like \"checkout (33%)\". Count passes and failures per test first, then keep the ones with some of each. function flakyReport(runs) { const stats = {}; for (const run of runs) { for (const [name, status] ofObject.entries(run)) { stats[name] ??= { passed: 0, failed: 0 }; stats[name][status]++; } } returnObject.entries(stats) .filter(([, s]) => s.passed > 0 && s.failed > 0) .map(([name, s]) => `${name} (${Math.round((s.failed / (s.passed + s.failed)) * 100)}%)`) .sort(); } A test that always fails is broken, not flaky. Only mixed results point at timing, data or environment problems."],[21,"flaky","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. The tools and how they drive a browserA UI automation tool starts a real browser and acts like a user: it opens pages, types, clicks and reads what is shown. The four big JavaScript tools differ in how they talk to the browser, and that explains most of their strengths and limits. Playwright: locators and web first assertionsA Playwright test receives a page, finds elements with locators and checks them with web first assertions. A locator is a recipe, not an element: it is looked up again on every use, so it never goes stale. Controlling the networkPlaywright sees every request the page makes. page.route can answer a request with fake data or an error, so you can test states that are hard to produce for real: a broken API, an empty list, a very long name. Page objects, fixtures and retriesAs a suite grows, repeated steps move into page objects (chapter 9) and fixtures. A Playwright fixture prepares something a test needs, here a logged in shop, and passes it in as a parameter. Tests become short and read like a test case. Selenium WebDriverSelenium WebDriver sends each command to a driver program (chromedriver, geckodriver, safaridriver), which controls the browser. Every call is a network round trip and returns a promise, so every line is awaited. Cypress and WebdriverIOCypress runs inside the browser, in the same loop as your app. Commands such as cy.get are not promises: Cypress queues them and runs them in order, retrying each query and assertion until it passes. Real world scenario: a UI suite in CISharding splits the tests across four machines. fail-fast: false lets every shard finish, so one failure does not hide others. The report is uploaded even when tests fail, and the traces inside it show exactly what the browser saw."],[21,"comic","A robot user with a checklist","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Stable locators, assertions that wait, independent tests and evidence for every failure."],[21,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🛒Checkout guard An online store runs 30 Playwright journeys on every pull request. A broken coupon field was caught before it cost a weekend of sales. 🏦A bank on Selenium A bank keeps 2,000 Selenium tests on a Grid of Chrome, Firefox, Edge and Safari machines because its customers use all four. ⚛️A front end team A React team writes Cypress tests next to its components and stubs every API with cy.intercept to test each state of the UI. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"UI Automation: Playwright, Selenium, Cypress and WebdriverIO\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 9Welcome This chapter automates a small shop with Playwright and Selenium, shows the same tests in Cypress and WebdriverIO, and covers locators, waits, network control, fixtures, retries and CI. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[22,"","TypeScript fundamentals","What TypeScript is and how Node.js runs it, a modern tsconfig, everyday types, inference, readonly and unknown, typed functions, callbacks and overloads, interface against type, unions and narrowing, enum against as const, what strict mode catches, and reading untyped JSON safely with a type predicate. · TypeScript · tsc · tsconfig · types · inference · unknown · any · interface · type alias · union · narrowing · enum · as const · strict · type predicate · interview 🟨 JavaScript›🔷 TypeScript›Chapter 23 of 31 🔷 Step 7 · Topic 1 of 7 · Advanced"],[22,"top","","Start TypeScript the modern way: tsc to check and Node.js to run, a strict tsconfig, everyday types and inference, typed functions and overloads, interface and type, unions and narrowing, enums against as const objects, the strict options, and typed results from untyped JSON. ⏱️ About 18 min read📚 8 parts🎬 0 animations🔷 8 type checked examples🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 6 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Most new automation projects use TypeScript. Typed locators, fixtures and API responses catch mistakes in test code before the suite even starts. 1What TypeScript is and how it runs 2Everyday types 3Functions 4interface and type 5Unions and narrowing 6Enums and their modern alternative 7Strict options 8Real world scenario: typed results from untyped JSON Lesson 1 of 8"],[22,"why","What TypeScript is and how it runs","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypressWebdriverIOMost modern automation frameworks are written and used in TypeScript. TypeScript is JavaScript with type annotations. The compiler tsc reads the types and reports mistakes before the code runs. The types are then erased: at run time it is plain JavaScript. Node.js 23.6 and later can run .ts files directly by stripping the types, without checking them, so you use both: tsc to check, Node.js to run. tsc finds the mistakes, Node.js runs the code anyway · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // TypeScript is JavaScript plus types. The types are checked by tsc and then removed.function lineTotal(price: number, qty: number): number { return price * qty; } const total = lineTotal(40, 3); const wrong = lineTotal(\"40\", 3); // a string where a number is expectedconst name: string = total; // a number where a string is expected console.log(total, wrong, name); Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(7,25): error TS2345: Argument of type 'string' is not assignable to parameter of type 'number'. src/main.ts(8,7): error TS2322: Type 'number' is not assignable to type 'string'. $ node src/main.ts 120 120 120 Notice that Node.js ran the file even with two type errors. Types never change what code does. They are a safety net that only works if tsc runs in your editor and in CI. A modern tsconfig.json · Explained below { \"compilerOptions\": { \"target\": \"es2024\", // which JavaScript features the output may use \"module\": \"nodenext\", // how imports work: Node.js rules for ESM and CommonJS\"strict\": true, // turn on every strict check, always do this \"noUncheckedIndexedAccess\": true, \"noEmit\": true, // only type check, Node.js or a bundler runs the .ts files \"allowImportingTsExtensions\": true, \"erasableSyntaxOnly\": true, // forbid syntax Node.js cannot simply strip, such as enum \"types\": [\"node\"] // types for process, Buffer and friends from @types/node }, \"include\": [\"src\", \"tests\"] } Playwright, Vitest, Jest (with a transform), Cypress and WebdriverIO all accept TypeScript tests. Most new automation projects are written in TypeScript because locators, fixtures and API responses become typed and the editor completes them. Lesson 2 of 8"],[22,"everyday","Everyday types","🧪 QA essentialTyped test data and typed API responses catch typos in field names before a test even starts. Most of the time TypeScript infers the type from the value, so you annotate function parameters, public APIs and empty containers, not every variable. Use readonly for data that must not change, ? for optional properties, and tuples for fixed length lists. Everyday types, inference, readonly and unknown · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // Inference: no annotation needed when the value says it alllet retries = 3; // numberconst browser = \"chromium\"; // the literal type \"chromium\", because const never changesconst tags = [\"smoke\", \"cart\"]; // string[]// Arrays, tuples, objects, optional and readonlyconst timings: number[] = [840, 2310]; const pair: [string, number] = [\"login\", 840]; const user: { readonly id: number; name: string; email?: string } = { id: 7, name: \"Asha\" }; user.name = \"Asha K\"; user.id = 8; // readonly retries = \"three\"; // number only// any switches checking off. unknown must be checked before use.const fromApi: unknown = JSON.parse('{\"status\": 200}'); // fromApi.status; // error: 'fromApi' is of type 'unknown'if (typeof fromApi === \"object\" && fromApi !== null && \"status\"in fromApi) console.log(\"status\", fromApi.status); console.log(browser, tags, timings, pair, user.email ?? \"no email\"); Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(12,6): error TS2540: Cannot assign to 'id' because it is a read-only property. src/main.ts(13,1): error TS2322: Type 'string' is not assignable to type 'number'. $ node src/main.ts status 200 chromium [ 'smoke', 'cart' ] [ 840, 2310 ] [ 'login', 840 ] no email Type Use it for string number boolean bigint symbol the primitive values null undefined \"no value\", checked strictly with strict string[] or Array<string> lists of one type [string, number] tuples: fixed positions \"a\" | \"b\" literal unions: a fixed set of allowed values unknown outside data: you must check it before use any switches checking off, avoid it never a value that cannot exist, such as the return of a function that always throws Lesson 3 of 8"],[22,"functions","Functions","Annotate parameters, and let the return type be inferred or write it for public functions. Optional parameters use ?, defaults give both a type and a value, and rest parameters are typed as arrays. Function types describe callbacks, and overloads give one function several typed call shapes. Typing functions, callbacks and overloads · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // Parameters and returns, optional and default values, rest parametersfunction greet(name: string, greeting = \"Hello\", punctuation?: string): string { return`${greeting}, ${name}${punctuation ?? \"!\"}`; } const sum = (...values: number[]): number => values.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0); // A function type, used for callbackstypeMatcher = (actual: string) => boolean; const startsWith = (prefix: string): Matcher => actual => actual.startsWith(prefix); // Overloads: one implementation, several typed call shapesfunction find(id: number): { id: number }; function find(email: string): { email: string }; function find(key: number | string) { returntypeof key === \"number\" ? { id: key } : { email: key }; } // void for \"returns nothing\", never for \"never returns\"function fail(message: string): never { thrownewError(message); } console.log(greet(\"Asha\"), greet(\"Ravi\", \"Hi\", \".\"), sum(1, 2, 3), startsWith(\"ORD-\")(\"ORD-7\")); console.log(find(7), find(\"asha@shop.test\")); greet(); // missing argument sum(1, \"2\"); // wrong type in the rest listtry { fail(\"stop\"); } catch (e) { console.log((e asError).message); } Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(23,1): error TS2554: Expected 1-3 arguments, but got 0. src/main.ts(24,8): error TS2345: Argument of type 'string' is not assignable to parameter of type 'number'. $ node src/main.ts Hello, Asha! Hi, Ravi. 6 true { id: 7 } { email: 'asha@shop.test' } stop Lesson 4 of 8"],[22,"shapes","interface and type","🧪 QA essentialInterfaces describe API responses, test data and page object options in typed test projects. Both interface and type name an object shape. Interfaces can be extended with extends and are merged when declared twice, which suits public object shapes. Type aliases can name anything: unions, tuples, functions and combinations with &. Teams usually pick one style for objects and use type for everything else. interface and type, extends and intersections · 🔷 tsc finds 1 error, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // interface: a named object shape that can be extended and mergedinterfaceTestResult { name: string; status: \"passed\" | \"failed\" | \"skipped\"; durationMs: number; } interfaceRetriedResultextendsTestResult { retries: number; } // type: a name for any type, including unions, tuples and intersectionstypeBrowser = \"chromium\" | \"firefox\" | \"webkit\"; typeWithBrowser = TestResult & { browser: Browser }; typeById = Record<string, TestResult>; // an object with any string keystypeTimings = { [testName: string]: number }; // index signature, the same ideaconst r: RetriedResult = { name: \"checkout\", status: \"failed\", durationMs: 2310, retries: 2 }; const w: WithBrowser = { name: \"login\", status: \"passed\", durationMs: 840, browser: \"safari\" }; const byId: ById = { t1: r }; const timings: Timings = { login: 840 }; console.log(r.retries, w.browser, Object.keys(byId), timings.login); Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(18,76): error TS2322: Type '\"safari\"' is not assignable to type 'Browser'. $ node src/main.ts 2 safari [ 't1' ] 840 Lesson 5 of 8"],[22,"narrowing","Unions and narrowing","🧪 QA essentialAPI responses are usually \"success or error\", which discriminated unions model exactly. A union such as string | number means \"one of these\". Before you use a union, you narrow it with a check that TypeScript understands: typeof, instanceof, in, equality, or a shared literal field. Inside each branch, TypeScript knows the exact type. Unions and narrowing · 🔷 tsc passes, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts typeApiResult = { ok: true; data: string[] } | { ok: false; error: string; status: number }; function describe(r: ApiResult): string { if (r.ok) return`got ${r.data.length} items`; // here TypeScript knows r has datareturn`failed with ${r.status}: ${r.error}`; // and here that r has error and status } function formatId(id: string | number | null): string { if (id === null) return\"none\"; // equality narrowingif (typeof id === \"number\") return`#${id.toFixed(0)}`; // typeof narrowingreturn id.toUpperCase(); // only string is left } classTimeoutErrorextendsError { ms: number; constructor(ms: number) { super(`timeout after ${ms} ms`); this.ms = ms; } } function retryable(e: unknown): boolean { if (e instanceofTimeoutError) return e.ms < 30_000; // instanceof narrowingif (e instanceofError && \"code\"in e) return e.code === \"ECONNRESET\"; // in narrowingreturnfalse; } console.log(describe({ ok: true, data: [\"a\", \"b\"] }), \"|\", describe({ ok: false, error: \"down\", status: 503 })); console.log(formatId(7), formatId(\"ord-7\"), formatId(null), retryable(newTimeoutError(5000))); Terminal $ npx tsc && node src/main.ts Terminal output tsc: no type errors $ node src/main.ts got 2 items | failed with 503: down #7 ORD-7 none true A union of objects with a common literal field, such as ok: true or ok: false, is a discriminated union. It is the best way to type API results, test outcomes and state machines, and chapter 24 makes it exhaustive. Lesson 6 of 8"],[22,"enums","Enums and their modern alternative","An enum creates a real object at run time, so it is not erasable and fails with erasableSyntaxOnly and with Node.js type stripping. The modern alternative is a plain object marked as const plus a union type made from its values. It works everywhere and accepts both Browser.Firefox and the string \"firefox\". enum against as const objects · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors 📄 src/main.ts // enum creates real JavaScript, so Node.js cannot just strip itenumStatus { Passed = \"passed\", Failed = \"failed\" } // The erasable alternative: a const object and a union type made from itconstBrowser = { Chromium: \"chromium\", Firefox: \"firefox\", Webkit: \"webkit\" } asconst; typeBrowser = (typeofBrowser)[keyoftypeofBrowser]; // \"chromium\" | \"firefox\" | \"webkit\"function launch(b: Browser) { return`launching ${b}`; } console.log(launch(Browser.Firefox), launch(\"webkit\"), Status.Passed); launch(\"edge\"); Terminal $ npx tsc Terminal output src/main.ts(2,6): error TS1294: This syntax is not allowed when 'erasableSyntaxOnly' is enabled. src/main.ts(10,8): error TS2345: Argument of type '\"edge\"' is not assignable to parameter of type 'Browser'. Lesson 7 of 8"],[22,"strict","Strict options","🧪 QA essentialStrict null checks catch the \"cannot read properties of undefined\" errors that make tests crash. \"strict\": true turns on a family of checks. The two that catch the most bugs are strictNullChecks, which makes you handle undefined and null, and noImplicitAny, which stops untyped parameters. noUncheckedIndexedAccess goes further: reading an array element or a record key may give undefined. What strict mode catches · 🔷 tsc finds 3 errors 📄 src/main.ts const users = [\"asha\", \"ravi\"]; const third = users[2]; // with noUncheckedIndexedAccess this is string | undefined console.log(third.toUpperCase()); // could crash at run time, so tsc complainsfunction len(s) { return s.length; } // noImplicitAny: s has no typeconst config: { timeout?: number } = {}; const ms: number = config.timeout; // strictNullChecks: it may be undefined Terminal $ npx tsc Terminal output src/main.ts(3,13): error TS18048: 'third' is possibly 'undefined'. src/main.ts(5,14): error TS7006: Parameter 's' implicitly has an 'any' type. src/main.ts(8,7): error TS2322: Type 'number | undefined' is not assignable to type 'number'. Type 'undefined' is not assignable to type 'number'. A project without strict lets most of these bugs through, so TypeScript gives a false sense of safety. Turn it on from day one. When moving an old JavaScript project, turn on checkJs and fix files one folder at a time (chapter 25). Lesson 8 of 8"],[22,"scenario","Real world scenario: typed results from untyped JSON","🧪 QA essential The situationA dashboard script reads test results from a JSON file written by another tool. Some rows have typos such as \"pased\" or a duration stored as a string. Read untyped JSON safely · 🔷 tsc passes, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts import { readFileSync } from\"node:fs\"; interfaceResult { name: string; status: \"passed\" | \"failed\"; ms: number } // JSON from a file or an API is unknown until it is checkedfunction isResult(x: unknown): x is Result { if (typeof x !== \"object\" || x === null) returnfalse; const r = x asRecord<string, unknown>; returntypeof r.name === \"string\" && (r.status === \"passed\" || r.status === \"failed\") && typeof r.ms === \"number\"; } const raw: unknown = JSON.parse(readFileSync(\"src/results.json\", \"utf8\")); if (!Array.isArray(raw)) thrownewError(\"expected a list of results\"); const valid = raw.filter(isResult); // Result[] from here onconst rejected = raw.length - valid.length; const failed = valid.filter(r => r.status === \"failed\").map(r => r.name); const slowest = valid.toSorted((a, b) => b.ms - a.ms)[0]; console.log({ valid: valid.length, rejected, failed, slowest: slowest?.name }); 📄 src/results.json [ { \"name\": \"login\", \"status\": \"passed\", \"ms\": 840 }, { \"name\": \"checkout\", \"status\": \"failed\", \"ms\": 2310 }, { \"name\": \"search\", \"status\": \"pased\", \"ms\": 120 }, { \"name\": \"profile\", \"status\": \"passed\", \"ms\": \"45\" } ] Terminal $ npx tsc && node src/main.ts Terminal output tsc: no type errors $ node src/main.ts { valid: 2, rejected: 2, failed: [ 'checkout' ], slowest: 'checkout' } JSON.parse really returns unknown data, whatever its declared type. A type predicate such as x is Result checks it at run time and tells TypeScript the result, so everything after the filter is fully typed and the bad rows are counted instead of breaking the report. Chapter 26 does the same with Zod. 🧮"],[22,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 The first example has two wrong lines. How many type errors does tsc report? One per mistake. Answer: 2 A string passed as a number, and a number stored in a string. C2 How many values does the type \"chromium\" | \"firefox\" | \"webkit\" allow? Count the literals. Answer: 3 Three browsers. C1 A tuple [string, number, boolean] has what length? Fixed positions. Answer: 3 Exactly three elements. C2 In the scenario, 4 rows were read. One has the status \"pased\" and one has ms \"45\". How many rows are valid? Both broken rows fail. Answer: 2 4 − 2 = 2 C3 A type has 5 properties and 2 are optional. How many must every object provide? Required ones. Answer: 3 5 − 2 = 3 C1 type A = \"a\" | \"b\" and type B = \"x\" | \"y\" | \"z\". How many combinations does the template type `${A}-${B}` allow? Every pairing. Answer: 6 2 × 3 = 6 C2 A project turns on strict and tsc reports 120 errors. A team fixes 15 a day. How many days? Divide. Answer: 8 120 ÷ 15 = 8 C1 How many of string, number, boolean, null, undefined remain after if (x == null) return, starting from all five? == null removes both. Answer: 3 null and undefined are removed by == null. string, number and boolean remain. 🎯"],[22,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 What happens to TypeScript types when the code runs? Erasure. Types exist only for tsc and your editor. Q2 Which type must be checked before you can use its properties? Safe outside data. any lets you do anything, which hides bugs. Q3 What does email?: string mean in an object type? Optional. Its type when read is string | undefined. Q1 Node.js runs a .ts file that has type errors. What happens? Strip only. Run tsc in the editor and in CI to catch errors. Q2 What is the type of const browser = \"chromium\"? const never changes. let browser = \"chromium\" would be string. Q3 Which can type a union like \"a\" | \"b\"? Type aliases name anything. Interfaces describe object shapes only. Q4 After if (typeof id === \"number\") return …, what is id below it if it was string | number? Control flow narrowing. The number case has already returned. Q1 Why does erasableSyntaxOnly reject enum? Not just a type. Namespaces and parameter properties are rejected for the same reason. Q2 With noUncheckedIndexedAccess, what is the type of names[0] for names: string[]? The list may be empty. You must check it before calling string methods. Q3 What does a return type of x is Result do? Type predicate. Used with filter, it narrows the whole array. Q1 Which statement about overloads is true? One body. The implementation must handle every overload. Q2 What does the never return type say about fail(message)? Unreachable end. Code after a call to it is unreachable."],[22,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[22,"isorder","A type guard","TypeScript guards are plain functions at run time. Write isOrder(x) that returns true only for objects with a string id and a number total. In TypeScript its signature would be (x: unknown): x is Order. Check for null separately, because typeof null is \"object\". function isOrder(x) { returntypeof x === \"object\" && x !== null && typeof x.id === \"string\" && typeof x.total === \"number\"; } The same checks narrow an unknown value in TypeScript. 🌿 Medium"],[22,"describe","Handle a union","Write describe(r) for results shaped like { ok: true, data } or { ok: false, status, error }. Return \"N items\" for success, where N is the length of data, and \"STATUS: ERROR\" for failure. Check r.ok first, the same way TypeScript narrows a discriminated union. function describe(r) { return r.ok ? `${r.data.length} items` : `${r.status}: ${r.error}`; } In TypeScript, reading r.data in the failure branch would be a compile error. 🔥 Hard"],[22,"browsers","An as const lookup","Write BROWSERS as a frozen object { Chromium: \"chromium\", Firefox: \"firefox\", Webkit: \"webkit\" } and isBrowser(name) that returns true only for one of its values. Object.values gives the list of allowed strings. constBROWSERS = Object.freeze({ Chromium: \"chromium\", Firefox: \"firefox\", Webkit: \"webkit\" }); function isBrowser(name) { returnObject.values(BROWSERS).includes(name); } In TypeScript, as const plus this check gives a value of type Browser after the if. 🧠 Expert"],[22,"safeget","Safe reads with defaults","With noUncheckedIndexedAccess, every read may be undefined. Write at(list, index, fallback) that returns the element, counting from the end for negative indexes, or fallback when it is missing. Keep real values such as 0 and \"\". Array.prototype.at handles negative indexes, and ?? only replaces undefined and null. function at(list, index, fallback) { return list.at(index) ?? fallback; } With || instead of ??, the 0 and \"\" cases would wrongly return the fallback."],[22,"safeget","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. What TypeScript is and how it runsTypeScript is JavaScript with type annotations. The compiler tsc reads the types and reports mistakes before the code runs. The types are then erased: at run time it is plain JavaScript. Everyday typesMost of the time TypeScript infers the type from the value, so you annotate function parameters, public APIs and empty containers, not every variable. Use readonly for data that must not change, ? for optional properties, and tuples for fixed length lists. FunctionsAnnotate parameters, and let the return type be inferred or write it for public functions. Optional parameters use ?, defaults give both a type and a value, and rest parameters are typed as arrays. interface and typeBoth interface and type name an object shape. Interfaces can be extended with extends and are merged when declared twice, which suits public object shapes. Type aliases can name anything: unions, tuples, functions and combinations with &. Unions and narrowingA union such as string | number means \"one of these\". Before you use a union, you narrow it with a check that TypeScript understands: typeof, instanceof, in, equality, or a shared literal field. Inside each branch, TypeScript knows the exact type. Enums and their modern alternativeAn enum creates a real object at run time, so it is not erasable and fails with erasableSyntaxOnly and with Node.js type stripping. The modern alternative is a plain object marked as const plus a union type made from its values. Strict options\"strict\": true turns on a family of checks. The two that catch the most bugs are strictNullChecks, which makes you handle undefined and null, and noImplicitAny, which stops untyped parameters. Real world scenario: typed results from untyped JSONJSON.parse really returns unknown data, whatever its declared type. A type predicate such as x is Result checks it at run time and tells TypeScript the result, so everything after the filter is fully typed and the bad rows are counted instead of breaking the ..."],[22,"comic","Types that catch bugs early","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Turn on strict, annotate inputs, let the rest be inferred, treat outside data as unknown and narrow before you use it."],[22,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🎭A Playwright team Moved its tests to TypeScript and found 14 typos in locator option names and fixture names on the first tsc run, all of them silent bugs. 🧾A billing API Typed its response as a discriminated union of success and error. The client code could no longer read data from an error by mistake. 🧪A test data tool Treats every CSV and JSON input as unknown and checks it with type predicates, so a typo in a data file is reported with its row number. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"TypeScript fundamentals\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 10Welcome This chapter starts TypeScript: how it runs, everyday types, functions, interfaces and types, unions and narrowing, enums and strict options. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[23,"","Advanced TypeScript types","Generic functions and types, typeof, keyof and indexed access, the utility types and what they cannot prove, mapped and conditional types with infer, template literal types for routes and test ids, satisfies for config objects, exhaustive switches with never, assertion functions and branded ids, and typed API routes with inferred path parameters. · generics · keyof · indexed access · utility types · Partial · Omit · Record · mapped types · conditional types · infer · template literal types · satisfies · never · exhaustive · assertion functions · branded types · interview 🟨 JavaScript›🔷 TypeScript›Chapter 24 of 31 🧬 Step 7 · Topic 2 of 7 · Advanced"],[23,"top","","The type tools experts use: generics and constraints, typeof, keyof and indexed access, the utility types, mapped and conditional types with infer, template literal types, satisfies, exhaustive checks with never, assertion functions, branded types, and API routes whose types follow the path. ⏱️ About 20 min read📚 9 parts🎬 0 animations🔷 9 type checked examples🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 7 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Advanced types turn whole classes of test bugs into compile errors: wrong routes, missing parameters, typos in response fields and forgotten step types. 1Generics 2typeof, keyof and indexed access 3Utility types 4Mapped and conditional types 5Template literal types 6satisfies 7Exhaustive checks with never 8Assertion functions and branded types 9Real world scenario: typed API routes Lesson 1 of 9"],[23,"generics","Generics","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightTyped fixtures, typed API clients and test data builders are all built with generics. A generic is a type parameter, written <T>, that is filled in when the function or type is used. It keeps the connection between inputs and outputs: first of a number list is a number. A constraint with extends says what T must at least have. Generic functions, constraints and generic types · 🔷 tsc finds 1 error, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // A generic function keeps the link between input and output typesfunction first<T>(items: readonlyT[]): T | undefined { return items[0]; } const id = first([101, 102]); // number | undefinedconst tag = first([\"smoke\", \"cart\"]); // string | undefined// Constraints: T must have at least these fieldsfunction slowest<Textends { ms: number }>(results: T[]): T { return results.reduce((a, b) => (b.ms > a.ms ? b : a)); } const top = slowest([{ name: \"login\", ms: 840 }, { name: \"checkout\", ms: 2310 }]); console.log(id, tag, top.name); // top keeps its name field// A generic type with a defaulttypeApiResponse<T, E = { message: string }> = { status: number; data?: T; error?: E }; const res: ApiResponse<{ id: number }> = { status: 201, data: { id: 7 } }; console.log(res.data?.id); slowest([{ name: \"x\" }]); // missing ms Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(20,12): error TS2353: Object literal may only specify known properties, and 'name' does not exist in type '{ ms: number; }'. $ node src/main.ts 101 smoke checkout 7 Generics are why Playwright's test.extend<{ cart: CartPage }>() knows the type of every fixture, and why response.json() in a typed client can return your response type (chapter 26). Lesson 2 of 9"],[23,"keyof","typeof, keyof and indexed access","typeof value turns a value into a type, keyof T gives the union of its keys, and T[K] reads the type of one property. Together they let a function accept only real keys and return the right type for each one. typeof, keyof and indexed access types · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts const defaults = { baseURL: \"http://localhost:3000\", timeout: 5000, headless: true }; typeConfig = typeof defaults; // the type of a valuetypeConfigKey = keyofConfig; // \"baseURL\" | \"timeout\" | \"headless\"typeTimeout = Config[\"timeout\"]; // indexed access: number// K is one of the keys, and the return type follows itfunction setting<KextendsConfigKey>(key: K, overrides: Partial<Config> = {}): Config[K] { return overrides[key] ?? defaults[key]; } const t: Timeout = setting(\"timeout\", { timeout: 10_000 }); const h = setting(\"headless\"); // boolean console.log(t, h, setting(\"baseURL\")); setting(\"timout\"); // typo caughtconst n: number = setting(\"baseURL\"); // wrong type caught Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(15,9): error TS2345: Argument of type '\"timout\"' is not assignable to parameter of type '\"baseURL\" | \"headless\" | \"timeout\"'. src/main.ts(16,7): error TS2322: Type 'string' is not assignable to type 'number'. $ node src/main.ts 10000 true http://localhost:3000 Lesson 3 of 9"],[23,"utility","Utility types","🧪 QA essentialOmit and Pick model create requests and public responses, and the leaked password shows what types alone cannot prove. TypeScript ships with type helpers that change existing types instead of writing new ones by hand. Helper Gives Partial<T>Required<T> every property optional, or every property required Pick<T, K>Omit<T, K> only some keys, or all but some keys Record<K, V> an object with keys K and values V Readonly<T> every property readonly ReturnTypeParametersAwaited the return, the arguments, the value inside a promise NonNullableExcludeExtract remove null and undefined, remove or keep union members Utility types: Partial, Pick, Omit, Record, ReturnType and Awaited · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts interfaceUser { id: number; email: string; name: string; role: \"admin\" | \"customer\"; password: string } typeNewUser = Omit<User, \"id\">; // what a create request sendstypePublicUser = Omit<User, \"password\">; // what the API may returntypeUserPatch = Partial<Pick<User, \"name\" | \"email\">>; typeLookup = Record<User[\"role\"], User[]>; typeFrozen = Readonly<User>; asyncfunction fetchUser(): Promise<PublicUser> { return { id: 1, email: \"a@shop.test\", name: \"Asha\", role: \"admin\" }; } typeFetched = Awaited<ReturnType<typeof fetchUser>>; typeArgs = Parameters<typeof setting>; function setting(name: string, fallback?: number) { return fallback ?? 0; } const draft: NewUser = { email: \"r@shop.test\", name: \"Ravi\", role: \"customer\", password: \"s3cret!\" }; const patch: UserPatch = { name: \"Ravi K\" }; const leaked: PublicUser = { ...draft, id: 2 }; // extra password field in an object literal is allowed via spreadconst byRole: Lookup = { admin: [], customer: [] }; const args: Args = [\"timeout\", 5000]; const u: Fetched = await fetchUser(); console.log(Object.keys(draft).length, patch, Object.keys(byRole), args, u.name, \"password\"in leaked); const bad: PublicUser = { id: 3, email: \"x\", name: \"X\", role: \"customer\", password: \"oops\" }; const frozen: Frozen = u asUser; frozen.name = \"changed\"; Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(22,75): error TS2353: Object literal may only specify known properties, and 'password' does not exist in type 'PublicUser'. src/main.ts(24,8): error TS2540: Cannot assign to 'name' because it is a read-only property. $ node src/main.ts 4 { name: 'Ravi K' } [ 'admin', 'customer' ] [ 'timeout', 5000 ] Asha true Look at the leaked line: spreading an object with extra fields into a typed variable is allowed, so a password can slip into a \"public\" object. Types describe what must be there, not what must be absent. API tests must still check that secret fields are not in the response. Lesson 4 of 9"],[23,"mapped","Mapped and conditional types","A mapped type loops over keys: { [K in keyof T]: … }. It can rename keys with as and add or remove readonly and ?. A conditional type, T extends U ? X : Y, chooses a type, and infer captures part of a type, such as the element of an array or the value inside a promise. Mapped and conditional types with infer · 🔷 tsc finds 1 error, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // Mapped types build a new type from every key of anothertypeFlags<T> = { [KinkeyofT]: boolean }; typeGetters<T> = { [KinkeyofTas`get${Capitalize<string & K>}`]: () => T[K] }; typeMutable<T> = { -readonly [KinkeyofT]: T[K] }; interfaceSettings { readonly retries: number; readonly browser: string } const changed: Flags<Settings> = { retries: true, browser: false }; const getters: Getters<Settings> = { getRetries: () => 2, getBrowser: () => \"firefox\" }; const m: Mutable<Settings> = { retries: 1, browser: \"webkit\" }; m.retries = 3; // Conditional types choose a type, and infer pulls a type out of anothertypeElementOf<T> = Textendsreadonly (infer E)[] ? E : never; typeUnwrap<T> = TextendsPromise<infer V> ? V : T; typeNonEmpty<T> = Textends\"\" | null | undefined ? never : T; const e: ElementOf<string[]> = \"ok\"; const v: Unwrap<Promise<number>> = 42; typeClean = NonEmpty<\"a\" | \"\" | null | \"b\">; // \"a\" | \"b\"const c: Clean = \"b\"; console.log(changed, getters.getBrowser(), m.retries, e, v, c); const wrong: Clean = \"\"; Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(23,7): error TS2322: Type '\"\"' is not assignable to type 'Clean'. $ node src/main.ts { retries: true, browser: false } firefox 3 ok 42 b Conditional types are distributive over unions: NonEmpty<\"a\" | \"\"> is checked one member at a time, which is how Exclude and NonNullable are written. Lesson 5 of 9"],[23,"template","Template literal types","🧪 QA essentialTyped routes and test id patterns stop typos in endpoints and locators. Template literal types describe patterns of strings, built from other string types. They type routes, CSS values, event names and test ids, and the compiler checks every literal you write. Template literal types · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // Template literal types describe string patternstypeMethod = \"GET\" | \"POST\" | \"DELETE\"; typeVersion = \"v1\" | \"v2\"; typeRoute = `/${Version}/${\"users\" | \"orders\"}`; typeEndpoint = `${Method} ${Route}`; // 3 × 2 × 2 = 12 stringstypeTestId = `${\"btn\" | \"input\" | \"link\"}-${string}`; typeCssSize = `${number}px` | `${number}%`; const call: Endpoint = \"POST /v1/orders\"; const loc: TestId = \"btn-checkout\"; const width: CssSize = \"50%\"; // Event names from a type, as typed event emitters dotypeEvents = { pass: { name: string }; fail: { name: string; error: string } }; typeHandler = { [EinkeyofEventsas`on${Capitalize<E>}`]: (e: Events[E]) => void }; const reporter: Handler = { onPass: e => console.log(\"✔\", e.name), onFail: e => console.log(\"✖\", e.name, e.error) }; reporter.onFail({ name: \"cart\", error: \"expected 2\" }); console.log(call, loc, width); const typo: Endpoint = \"PUT /v1/orders\"; const badId: TestId = \"button-checkout\"; Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(21,7): error TS2820: Type '\"PUT /v1/orders\"' is not assignable to type '\"DELETE /v1/orders\" | \"DELETE /v1/users\" | \"DELETE /v2/orders\" | \"DELETE /v2/users\" | \"GET /v1/orders\" | \"GET /v1/users\" | \"GET /v2/orders\" | \"GET /v2/users\" | \"POST /v1/orders\" | \"POST /v1/users\" | \"POST /v2/orders\" | \"POST /v2/users\"'. Did you mean '\"POST /v1/orders\"'? src/main.ts(22,7): error TS2322: Type '\"button-checkout\"' is not assignable to type '`btn-${string}` | `input-${string}` | `link-${string}`'. $ node src/main.ts ✖ cart expected 2 POST /v1/orders btn-checkout 50% Lesson 6 of 9"],[23,"satisfies","satisfies","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightTest configuration objects get both type checking and precise auto complete. An annotation such as const x: Record<string, Project> replaces the type of the value with the wider annotation, so the exact keys are lost. satisfies checks the value against the type but keeps the precise type that was inferred. It is ideal for configuration objects. satisfies: check without widening · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts typeBrowser = \"chromium\" | \"firefox\" | \"webkit\"; typeProject = { browser: Browser; retries?: number; baseURL?: string }; // A type annotation widens: the exact keys and values are lostconst annotated: Record<string, Project> = { desktop: { browser: \"chromium\" }, mobile: { browser: \"webkit\", retries: 2 } }; annotated.tablet; // allowed, but undefined at run time// satisfies checks the shape and keeps the precise typeconst projects = { desktop: { browser: \"chromium\" }, mobile: { browser: \"webkit\", retries: 2 }, } satisfiesRecord<string, Project>; projects.mobile.retries.toFixed(0); // known to exist, no undefined check needed console.log(Object.keys(projects), projects.mobile.retries); projects.tablet; // now a typo is caughtconst broken = { desktop: { browser: \"edge\" } } satisfiesRecord<string, Project>; Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(16,10): error TS2339: Property 'tablet' does not exist on type '{ desktop: { browser: \"chromium\"; }; mobile: { browser: \"webkit\"; retries: number; }; }'. src/main.ts(18,29): error TS2322: Type '\"edge\"' is not assignable to type 'Browser'. $ node src/main.ts [ 'desktop', 'mobile' ] 2 playwright.config.ts uses defineConfig for the same reason. With your own config objects, satisfies gives typo checking and auto complete at the same time. Lesson 7 of 9"],[23,"exhaustive","Exhaustive checks with never","🧪 QA essentialKeyword driven and data driven frameworks model their steps as unions, and exhaustive checks keep every runner up to date. With a discriminated union, a switch on the shared field narrows each case. Passing the leftover value to a function that takes never makes the check exhaustive: add a new kind to the union and every switch that forgot it becomes a compile error. Discriminated unions with an exhaustive check · 🔷 tsc finds 1 error, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts typeStep = | { kind: \"goto\"; url: string } | { kind: \"click\"; selector: string } | { kind: \"fill\"; selector: string; value: string } | { kind: \"expectText\"; selector: string; text: string }; function assertNever(x: never): never { thrownewError(`unhandled step ${JSON.stringify(x)}`); } function describe(step: Step): string { switch (step.kind) { case\"goto\": return`open ${step.url}`; case\"click\": return`click ${step.selector}`; case\"fill\": return`type \"${step.value}\" into ${step.selector}`; case\"expectText\": return`check ${step.selector} shows \"${step.text}\"`; default: return assertNever(step); // a new kind of step without a case is a compile error } } const script: Step[] = [ { kind: \"goto\", url: \"/login\" }, { kind: \"fill\", selector: \"#email\", value: \"asha@shop.test\" }, { kind: \"click\", selector: \"button[type=submit]\" }, { kind: \"expectText\", selector: \"h1\", text: \"Welcome\" }, ]; console.log(script.map(describe)); function describeOld(step: Step): string { switch (step.kind) { case\"goto\": return\"open\"; case\"click\": return\"click\"; default: return assertNever(step); // fill and expectText are missing } } Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(31,33): error TS2345: Argument of type '{ kind: \"fill\"; selector: string; value: string; } | { kind: \"expectText\"; selector: string; text: string; }' is not assignable to parameter of type 'never'. Type '{ kind: \"fill\"; selector: string; value: string; }' is not assignable to type 'never'. $ node src/main.ts [ 'open /login', 'type \"asha@shop.test\" into #email', 'click button[type=submit]', 'check h1 shows \"Welcome\"' ] Lesson 8 of 9"],[23,"assertions","Assertion functions and branded types","🧪 QA essentialRequired environment variables and typed ids are checked once, and the rest of the test can rely on them. An assertion function with asserts value is T narrows the type after the call, or throws. Test helpers such as expect(x).toBeDefined() cannot narrow types, but your own assertion functions can. A branded type adds an invisible tag to a primitive so two kinds of string id cannot be mixed up. Assertion functions and branded types · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // An assertion function narrows the type for the rest of the scope, or throwsfunction assertDefined<T>(value: T, label: string): asserts value is NonNullable<T> { if (value === undefined || value === null) thrownewError(`${label} is missing`); } // Branded types: same run time value, different compile time typetypeOrderId = string & { readonly __brand: \"OrderId\" }; typeUserId = string & { readonly __brand: \"UserId\" }; const asOrderId = (s: string): OrderId => { if (!/^ORD-\\d{6}$/.test(s)) thrownewRangeError(`bad order id ${s}`); return s asOrderId; }; function cancelOrder(id: OrderId) { return`cancelled ${id}`; } const env = { BASE_URL: \"http://localhost:3000\" } asRecord<string, string | undefined>; const base = env.BASE_URL; assertDefined(base, \"BASE_URL\"); console.log(base.toUpperCase()); // string from here onconst order = asOrderId(\"ORD-104233\"); console.log(cancelOrder(order)); const user = \"USR-7\"asUserId; cancelOrder(user); // a user id where an order id is needed cancelOrder(\"ORD-000001\"); // a plain string is not an OrderId either Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(24,13): error TS2345: Argument of type 'UserId' is not assignable to parameter of type 'OrderId'. Type 'UserId' is not assignable to type '{ readonly __brand: \"OrderId\"; }'. Types of property '__brand' are incompatible. Type '\"UserId\"' is not assignable to type '\"OrderId\"'. src/main.ts(25,13): error TS2345: Argument of type 'string' is not assignable to parameter of type 'OrderId'. Type 'string' is not assignable to type '{ readonly __brand: \"OrderId\"; }'. $ node src/main.ts HTTP://LOCALHOST:3000 cancelled ORD-104233 Lesson 9 of 9"],[23,"scenario","Real world scenario: typed API routes","🧪 QA essential The situationAPI tests often read the wrong field or forget a path parameter. The team wants the route string itself to decide the parameters and the response type. Typed API routes with inferred parameters · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // Typed routes: the path decides the response type, so tests cannot misread a bodyinterfaceRoutes { \"GET /products\": { id: string; price: number }[]; \"GET /products/:id\": { id: string; price: number; stock: number }; \"POST /orders\": { orderId: string; total: number }; } typeParams<Pextendsstring> = Pextends`${string}:${infer Name}/${infer Rest}` ? { [KinName]: string } & Params<`/${Rest}`> : Pextends`${string}:${infer Name}` ? { [KinName]: string } : {}; asyncfunction call<RextendskeyofRoutes>(route: R, params: Params<R & string>): Promise<Routes[R]> { const [method, path] = (route asstring).split(\" \"); const url = path.replace(/:(\\w+)/g, (_, k) => (params asRecord<string, string>)[k]); console.log(method, url); const fake: Record<string, unknown> = { \"/products\": [{ id: \"p1\", price: 40 }], \"/products/p1\": { id: \"p1\", price: 40, stock: 3 }, \"/orders\": { orderId: \"ORD-104233\", total: 80 }, }; return fake[url] asRoutes[R]; } const list = await call(\"GET /products\", {}); const one = await call(\"GET /products/:id\", { id: \"p1\" }); const order = await call(\"POST /orders\", {}); console.log(list.length, one.stock, order.orderId); await call(\"GET /products/:id\", {}); // the id parameter is required console.log(one.stok); // typo in a response field Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(27,33): error TS2741: Property 'id' is missing in type '{}' but required in type '{ id: string; }'. src/main.ts(28,17): error TS2551: Property 'stok' does not exist on type '{ id: string; price: number; stock: number; }'. Did you mean 'stock'? $ node src/main.ts GET /products GET /products/p1 POST /orders 1 3 ORD-104233 GET /products/undefined undefined A conditional type with infer pulls :id out of the route string, and the Routes map gives the response type. A missing parameter or a typo such as stok is now a compile error. Tools such as openapi-typescript generate a map like Routes from the API's OpenAPI file, so the types follow the real API. 🧮"],[23,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 The Endpoint type combines 3 methods, 2 versions and 2 resources. How many strings does it allow? Multiply. Answer: 12 3 × 2 × 2 = 12 C2 User has 5 properties. How many does Omit<User, \"id\" | \"password\"> keep? Remove two. Answer: 3 5 − 2 = 3 C1 Pick<User, \"name\" | \"email\"> then Partial. How many properties are required? Partial makes all optional. Answer: 0 Both picked properties become optional. C2 The step union has 4 kinds and describeOld handles 2. How many kinds reach assertNever? The rest. Answer: 2 fill and expectText are left, which is why tsc reports an error. C3 keyof of an object with 3 keys combined with Capitalize in a mapped type gives how many getter names? One per key. Answer: 3 getRetries, getBrowser and one more for a third key. C1 Exclude<\"a\" | \"b\" | \"c\" | \"d\", \"b\" | \"d\"> has how many members? Remove two. Answer: 2 \"a\" | \"c\" remain. C2 A Record<\"admin\" | \"customer\" | \"guest\", User[]> needs how many keys? One per role. Answer: 3 Every key in the union is required. C1 A route \"GET /users/:userId/orders/:orderId\" needs how many parameters? Count the colon parts. Answer: 2 userId and orderId. 🎯"],[23,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 What does <T> in function first<T>(xs: T[]): T mean? Generic. first([1, 2]) makes T number. Q2 Which utility type makes every property optional? Partly filled. Great for PATCH request bodies and overrides. Q3 What is keyof { a: 1, b: 2 }? The keys. Indexed access T[\"a\"] gives the value type. Q1 Omit<User, \"password\"> gives? Leave out. Pick is the opposite. Q2 What does satisfies keep that a type annotation loses? No widening. Exact keys and literal values stay known. Q3 What does T extends Promise<infer V> ? V : T give for Promise<number>? infer captures. This is how Awaited works. Q4 How many strings does `${\"GET\" | \"POST\"} /${\"a\" | \"b\" | \"c\"}` allow? Every combination. 2 × 3. Q1 Why pass the default case of a switch to a function taking never? Exhaustiveness. If a case is missing, the value is not never and tsc complains. Q2 What does asserts value is NonNullable<T> do after the call? Narrowing by assertion. If the check fails, the function throws instead. Q3 Why can a password still end up in a PublicUser object? Structural typing. Only fresh object literals get the excess property check. Q1 What is the point of a branded type such as string & { __brand: \"OrderId\" }? Nominal typing. Combine it with a parse function that checks the format once. Q2 Conditional types distribute over unions. What is Exclude<\"a\" | \"b\" | \"c\", \"b\">? One member at a time. Exclude<T, U> is T extends U ? never : T."],[23,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[23,"pick","A typed pick at run time","Write pick(obj, keys) that returns a new object with only the listed keys that exist on obj. In TypeScript its type is <T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, keys: K[]) => Pick<T, K>. Object.fromEntries over the filtered keys. function pick(obj, keys) { returnObject.fromEntries(keys.filter(k => k in obj).map(k => [k, obj[k]])); } Real values such as 0 and null must be kept, so check with in, not truthiness. 🌿 Medium"],[23,"groupkey","Group by a key name","Write groupByKey(list, key) that groups objects by the value of the named property. In TypeScript, key would be keyof T, so only real fields are allowed. Read item[key] and push into an array for that value. function groupByKey(list, key) { const out = {}; for (const item of list) (out[item[key]] ??= []).push(item); return out; } With keyof T, a typo such as \"staus\" is caught by tsc before the test runs. 🔥 Hard"],[23,"steps","An exhaustive step runner","Write runSteps(steps) for steps of kind goto (url), click (selector) and fill (selector, value). Return lines such as \"open /login\", \"click #go\", \"fill #email with asha\". Throw an Error with the message \"unknown step: KIND\" for any other kind, like assertNever does. A switch on step.kind with a default that throws. function runSteps(steps) { return steps.map(step => { switch (step.kind) { case\"goto\": return `open ${step.url}`; case\"click\": return `click ${step.selector}`; case\"fill\": return `fill ${step.selector} with ${step.value}`; default: thrownewError(`unknown step: ${step.kind}`); } }); } At run time the default catches bad data from files. At compile time the never check catches missing cases in code. 🧠 Expert"],[23,"orderid","Parse a branded id","Write parseOrderId(text) that trims the text, upper cases it, and returns it if it looks like ORD- followed by exactly 6 digits. Otherwise throw a RangeError with the message bad order id: TEXT using the original text. In TypeScript the return type would be OrderId. Normalise first, then test with /^ORD-\\d{6}$/. function parseOrderId(text) { const id = text.trim().toUpperCase(); if (!/^ORD-\\d{6}$/.test(id)) thrownewRangeError(`bad order id: ${text}`); return id; } Parse once at the edge and the brand guarantees every OrderId inside the code is valid."],[23,"orderid","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. GenericsA generic is a type parameter, written <T>, that is filled in when the function or type is used. It keeps the connection between inputs and outputs: first of a number list is a number. A constraint with extends says what T must at least have. typeof, keyof and indexed accesstypeof value turns a value into a type, keyof T gives the union of its keys, and T[K] reads the type of one property. Together they let a function accept only real keys and return the right type for each one. Utility typesTypeScript ships with type helpers that change existing types instead of writing new ones by hand. Mapped and conditional typesA mapped type loops over keys: { [K in keyof T]: … }. It can rename keys with as and add or remove readonly and ?. Template literal typesTemplate literal types describe patterns of strings, built from other string types. They type routes, CSS values, event names and test ids, and the compiler checks every literal you write. satisfiesAn annotation such as const x: Record<string, Project> replaces the type of the value with the wider annotation, so the exact keys are lost. satisfies checks the value against the type but keeps the precise type that was inferred. Exhaustive checks with neverWith a discriminated union, a switch on the shared field narrows each case. Passing the leftover value to a function that takes never makes the check exhaustive: add a new kind to the union and every switch that forgot it becomes a compile error. Assertion functions and branded typesAn assertion function with asserts value is T narrows the type after the call, or throws. Test helpers such as expect(x).toBeDefined() cannot narrow types, but your own assertion functions can. Real world scenario: typed API routesA conditional type with infer pulls :id out of the route string, and the Routes map gives the response type. A missing parameter or a typo such as stok is now a compile error."],[23,"comic","Types that think","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Use generics to keep types connected, utility and mapped types to reshape them, satisfies for configs, and never to make switches exhaustive."],[23,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🧭An API test suite Generates its route types from the OpenAPI file in CI. When the backend renamed a field, 37 tests failed to compile before a single request was sent. 🎹A keyword driven framework Models every step as a union member. Adding a \"hover\" step showed the three runners that still needed to support it. 🆔A marketplace Branded its UserId and OrderId types after a bug sent a refund to an order id that happened to look like a user id. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Advanced TypeScript types\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 11Welcome This chapter covers advanced TypeScript: generics, keyof, utility types, mapped and conditional types, template literal types, satisfies, exhaustive checks and branded types. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[24,"","TypeScript classes and real projects","Classes with access modifiers, abstract members and interfaces, TypeScript private compared with #private, standard decorators that log steps and retry, declaration files and module augmentation, type checking JavaScript with JSDoc, a safe migration plan and the tools to run TypeScript, and a typed page object hierarchy. · TypeScript classes · abstract class · access modifiers · private · #private · decorators · .d.ts · declaration files · module augmentation · JSDoc · checkJs · migration · tsc --noEmit · page object · interview 🟨 JavaScript›🔷 TypeScript›Chapter 25 of 31 🏛️ Step 7 · Topic 3 of 7 · Advanced"],[24,"top","","TypeScript at the scale of a real project: access modifiers, abstract classes and interfaces, TypeScript private against #private, standard decorators for steps and retries, declaration files and module augmentation, checking JavaScript with JSDoc, moving a project to TypeScript, and a typed page object hierarchy. ⏱️ About 18 min read📚 6 parts🎬 0 animations🔷 5 type checked examples🧪 1 real test run🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 6 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Large automation frameworks are TypeScript projects. Abstract base pages, step decorators, typed custom commands and a safe migration plan are what keep them maintainable. 1Classes in TypeScript 2Decorators 3Declaration files and augmentation 4Checking JavaScript with JSDoc 5Moving a project to TypeScript, and the tools around it 6Real world scenario: a typed page object hierarchy Lesson 1 of 6"],[24,"classes","Classes in TypeScript","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightSeleniumTyped base pages with abstract methods and protected helpers are the backbone of large page object frameworks. TypeScript adds access modifiers to classes. public is the default, protected allows subclasses, and private allows only the class itself. readonly blocks changes after the constructor, abstract marks a class or method that subclasses must complete, implements checks a class against an interface, and override confirms that a method replaces one from the parent. Access modifiers, abstract classes and interfaces · 🔷 tsc finds 4 errors 📄 src/main.ts abstract classBasePage { protectedreadonly baseURL: string; #visits = 0; // JavaScript private: hidden at run time too constructor(baseURL: string, privatereadonly path: string) { this.baseURL = baseURL; } get url(): string { returnthis.baseURL + this.path; } open(): string { this.#visits++; return`open ${this.url} (visit ${this.#visits})`; } abstract isLoaded(): boolean; // every page must say how to know it is ready } interfaceSearchable { search(term: string): string[] } classProductsPageextendsBasePageimplementsSearchable { constructor(baseURL: string) { super(baseURL, \"/products\"); } override isLoaded() { returntrue; } search(term: string) { return [`${term} pen`, `${term} ink`]; } } const page = newProductsPage(\"http://localhost:3000\"); console.log(page.open(), page.open(), page.isLoaded(), page.search(\"blue\")); page.baseURL; // protected: only inside the class family page.path; // private: only inside BasePagenewBasePage(\"x\", \"/\"); // abstract classes cannot be createdclassBrokenextendsBasePage {} // forgot isLoaded Terminal $ npx tsc Terminal output src/main.ts(23,6): error TS2445: Property 'baseURL' is protected and only accessible within class 'BasePage' and its subclasses. src/main.ts(24,6): error TS2341: Property 'path' is private and only accessible within class 'BasePage'. src/main.ts(25,1): error TS2511: Cannot create an instance of an abstract class. src/main.ts(26,7): error TS2515: Non-abstract class 'Broken' does not implement inherited abstract member isLoaded from class 'BasePage'. TypeScript's private is a compile time check only. The value is still on the object at run time. JavaScript's #private is enforced by the engine, so prefer it for real secrets. TypeScript private against JavaScript #private · 🔷 tsc passes, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts classSession { private token = \"secret-123\"; // TypeScript private: a compile time check only #pin = \"4321\"; // JavaScript private: truly hidden check(pin: string) { return pin === this.#pin; } } const s = newSession(); console.log(Object.keys(s), JSON.stringify(s)); console.log((s asany).token, (s asany)[\"#pin\"], s.check(\"4321\")); Terminal $ npx tsc && node src/main.ts Terminal output tsc: no type errors $ node src/main.ts [ 'token' ] {\"token\":\"secret-123\"} secret-123 undefined true constructor(private readonly path: string) is a parameter property: it declares and assigns the field in one go. Like enum, it creates run time code, so erasableSyntaxOnly and Node.js type stripping do not allow it. Write the field and the assignment out if you run .ts files directly. Lesson 2 of 6"],[24,"decorators","Decorators","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightStep decorators put every page object action into the test report without extra code in each method. TypeScript 5 supports the standard decorators proposal. A method decorator is a function that receives the original method and a context object, and returns a replacement. Decorators stack: the one closest to the method wraps first. Decorators for steps and retries · 🔷 Compiled with tsc, then run 📄 src/steps.ts // Standard decorators (TypeScript 5 and later): a method decorator receives the method and a contextexportfunction step(title: string) { returnfunction <This, Argsextendsunknown[], R>( method: (this: This, ...args: Args) => Promise<R>, context: ClassMethodDecoratorContext<This>, ) { returnasyncfunction (this: This, ...args: Args): Promise<R> { const started = Date.now(); try { const result = await method.apply(this, args); console.log(`✔ ${title}`); return result; } catch (e) { console.log(`✖ ${title} (${String(context.name)}): ${(e as Error).message}`); throw e; } finally { void (Date.now() - started); } }; }; } exportfunction retry(times: number) { returnfunction <This, Argsextendsunknown[], R>(method: (this: This, ...args: Args) => Promise<R>, _ctx: ClassMethodDecoratorContext<This>) { returnasyncfunction (this: This, ...args: Args): Promise<R> { for (let i = 1; ; i++) { try { returnawait method.apply(this, args); } catch (e) { if (i >= times) throw e; } } }; }; } 📄 src/main.ts import { step, retry } from\"./steps.js\"; classCheckoutPage { attempts = 0; @step(\"Fill the address\") async fillAddress(city: string) { return`address in ${city}`; } @step(\"Pay with card\") @retry(3) async pay() { if (++this.attempts < 3) thrownewError(\"payment frame not ready\"); return\"paid\"; } @step(\"Apply coupon\") async applyCoupon(code: string) { if (code !== \"SAVE10\") thrownewError(`coupon ${code} rejected`); } } const page = newCheckoutPage(); await page.fillAddress(\"Pune\"); console.log(await page.pay(), \"after\", page.attempts, \"attempts\"); await page.applyCoupon(\"FREE\").catch(() => {}); Terminal $ npx tsc && node dist/main.js Terminal output ✔ Fill the address ✔ Pay with card paid after 3 attempts ✖ Apply coupon (applyCoupon): coupon FREE rejected This is how some frameworks add steps to reports. In Playwright the built in way is await test.step(\"Pay with card\", async () => …), and a decorator like @step can call it for you so every page object method shows up as a step in the HTML report and the trace viewer. Lesson 3 of 6"],[24,"dts","Declaration files and augmentation","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCypressCustom commands, custom matchers and app globals in tests all need declaration files or augmentation. A declaration file (.d.ts) contains only types. It describes JavaScript code that has no types of its own. Most npm packages ship one, and others have one in @types/…. Module augmentation adds to existing types, such as a field on Window or a custom matcher on expect. Declaration files and module augmentation · 🔷 tsc finds 1 error 📄 vendor/legacy-report.js // An old plain JavaScript helper with no typesexportfunction summarise(results) { const failed = results.filter(r => !r.ok).map(r => r.name); return { total: results.length, failed }; } 📄 types/legacy-report.d.ts // A declaration file describes JavaScript that has no types of its own declare module \"legacy-report\" { exportinterfaceResult { name: string; ok: boolean } exportfunction summarise(results: Result[]): { total: number; failed: string[] }; } 📄 types/globals.d.ts // Module augmentation: add a field to a global type.// The empty export makes this file a module, which declare global needs. declare global { interfaceWindow { __TEST_USER__?: { id: number; role: \"admin\" | \"customer\" } } } export {}; 📄 src/main.ts import { summarise } from\"legacy-report\"; const report = summarise([{ name: \"login\", ok: true }, { name: \"cart\", ok: false }]); console.log(report.failed.join(\", \")); summarise([{ name: \"x\", passed: true }]); // wrong field name, caught by the .d.ts// In browser test code: typed access to a value the app puts on windowfunction testUser(win: Window) { return win.__TEST_USER__?.role ?? \"anonymous\"; } void testUser; Terminal $ npx tsc -p . --pretty false Terminal output src/main.ts(6,25): error TS2353: Object literal may only specify known properties, and 'passed' does not exist in type 'Result'. Custom Playwright matchers are typed by augmenting PlaywrightTest.Matchers, and custom Cypress commands by augmenting Cypress.Chainable in a .d.ts file. Lesson 4 of 6"],[24,"jsdoc","Checking JavaScript with JSDoc","🧪 QA essentialMany existing JavaScript test suites can get type checking today with JSDoc and checkJs. You can get most of the safety of TypeScript without renaming a single file. With checkJs, or // @ts-check at the top of a file, TypeScript reads types from JSDoc comments and reports the same errors. Type checking plain JavaScript with JSDoc · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors 📄 src/cart.js // Plain JavaScript, checked by TypeScript through JSDoc comments/** * @typedef {{ sku: string, price: number, qty: number }} Line *//** * @param {Line[]} lines * @param {{ coupon?: \"SAVE10\" | \"SAVE20\" }} [options] * @returns {number} */exportfunction cartTotal(lines, options = {}) { const sum = lines.reduce((s, l) => s + l.price * l.qty, 0); const off = { SAVE10: 0.1, SAVE20: 0.2 }[options.coupon ?? \"SAVE10\"] ?? 0; return options.coupon ? sum * (1 - off) : sum; } cartTotal([{ sku: \"PEN\", price: \"40\", qty: 1 }]); cartTotal([], { coupon: \"FREE\" }); Terminal $ npx tsc -p . --pretty false Terminal output src/cart.js(18,26): error TS2322: Type 'string' is not assignable to type 'number'. src/cart.js(19,17): error TS2322: Type '\"FREE\"' is not assignable to type '\"SAVE10\" | \"SAVE20\" | undefined'. Lesson 5 of 6"],[24,"migrate","Moving a project to TypeScript, and the tools around it","🧪 QA essentialMoving a test suite to TypeScript step by step is a common project for senior testers. Move in small, safe steps. Every step keeps the project working, and CI keeps you from going backwards. A safe path from JavaScript to TypeScript · A plan, not a single command # Step 1: check JavaScript without changing it# tsconfig.json: \"allowJs\": true, \"checkJs\": false, \"strict\": false# Step 2: turn on checkJs for one folder at a time with // @ts-check at the top of files# Step 3: rename files to .ts, starting with leaf modules (utils, test data builders)# Step 4: add types to public functions, page objects and API clients# Step 5: turn on \"strict\": true, fix errors folder by folder# Step 6: add tsc --noEmit to CI so new code cannot add type errorsnpx tsc --noEmit # fails the CI job on any type errornpx tsc --noEmit --watch # keep checking while you edit Running and building TypeScript · Tool choices # Type check only (the source of truth)npx tsc --noEmit # Run TypeScript directlynode src/main.ts # Node.js 23.6+, strips erasable types, no checkingnpx tsx src/main.ts # also handles enums, decorators and path aliases# Build for publishing a librarynpx tsc # emits .js and .d.ts files into outDir# Bundle for the browsernpx esbuild src/app.ts --bundle --outfile=dist/app.js npx vite build Path aliases such as @pages/login in paths are only understood by tsc and by tools that read tsconfig. Plain Node.js does not resolve them. Use package.json\"imports\" with #pages/* instead, which works everywhere. Lesson 6 of 6"],[24,"scenario","Real world scenario: a typed page object hierarchy","🧪 QA essentialPlaywright The situationThe UI suite has 60 page objects. New pages often forget the path, and helpers return untyped values. A typed page object hierarchy · 🔷 tsc passes 📄 src/main.ts // A typed page object base with a generic \"wait for\" helper and typed test datatypeRole = \"admin\" | \"customer\"; interfaceTestUser { email: string; password: string; role: Role } interfaceLocator { textContent(): Promise<string>; click(): Promise<void>; fill(v: string): Promise<void> } interfacePageLike { locator(selector: string): Locator; goto(url: string): Promise<void> } abstract classPage<TReadyextendsstring = string> { constructor(protectedreadonly page: PageLike) {} abstract readonly path: string; async open(): Promise<this> { awaitthis.page.goto(this.path); returnthis; } protected el(selector: string) { returnthis.page.locator(selector); } } classLoginPageextendsPage { readonly path = \"/login\"; async loginAs(user: TestUser): Promise<DashboardPage> { awaitthis.el(\"#email\").fill(user.email); awaitthis.el(\"#password\").fill(user.password); awaitthis.el(\"button[type=submit]\").click(); returnnewDashboardPage(this.page); } } classDashboardPageextendsPage { readonly path = \"/dashboard\"; greeting() { returnthis.el(\"h1\").textContent(); } } // A fake page so the example runs without a browserconst log: string[] = []; const fake: PageLike = { async goto(url) { log.push(`goto ${url}`); }, locator: sel => ({ async fill(v) { log.push(`fill ${sel}`); }, async click() { log.push(`click ${sel}`); }, async textContent() { return\"Welcome, Asha\"; } }), }; const admin: TestUser = { email: \"asha@shop.test\", password: \"s3cret!\", role: \"admin\" }; const dash = await (awaitnewLoginPage(fake).open()).loginAs(admin); console.log(await dash.greeting(), log); Terminal $ npx tsc Terminal output tsc: no type errors An abstract path forces every page to declare its URL, protected el() keeps raw locators inside page objects, and returning DashboardPage from loginAs makes the next step in a test auto complete. In a real project, PageLike is Playwright's Page type. 🧮"],[24,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 The classes example has four mistakes after the console.log. How many errors does tsc report? One per line. Answer: 4 protected, private, abstract creation and a missing abstract method. C2 ProductsPage.open() is called twice. What visit number does the second call print? The #visits counter. Answer: 2 It starts at 0 and goes up once per call. C1 The pay method fails until attempt 3, and @retry(3) allows 3 tries. On which attempt does it succeed? Count tries. Answer: 3 Attempts 1 and 2 throw, attempt 3 returns \"paid\". C2 A suite of 900 files moves 150 files a week to TypeScript. How many weeks? Divide. Answer: 6 900 ÷ 150 = 6 C3 The JSDoc example has a wrong price type and a wrong coupon. How many errors? One each. Answer: 2 \"40\" is not a number, and \"FREE\" is not one of the coupons. C1 A base page has 3 abstract members. A subclass implements 2. How many errors on that class? One error listing the missing member. Answer: 1 tsc reports the class once for the missing member. C2 Decorators wrap from the closest one out. With @a @b @c above a method, which runs first when the method is called? The outermost wrapper runs first. Answer: a c wraps the method, b wraps c, a wraps b. Calling enters a first. C1 In the scenario, how many log lines does loginAs add, not counting open()? Two fills and a click. Answer: 3 fill #email, fill #password, click the button. 🎯"],[24,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 Which modifier lets subclasses use a member but hides it from outside code? Family only. private allows only the class itself. Q2 What does abstract on a method mean? To be completed. A class with abstract members cannot be created directly. Q3 What is a .d.ts file? Declarations. It describes code that lives elsewhere. Q1 Is a TypeScript private field visible at run time? Erased checks. Use #private for real run time privacy. Q2 What does implements Searchable check? A contract. It adds no code. Q3 Why do parameter properties fail with erasableSyntaxOnly? Not erasable. Stripping the types would remove the assignment. Q4 Which tsconfig option lets TypeScript check plain .js files? Check JavaScript. allowJs includes them, checkJs reports errors in them. Q1 Two decorators @step and @retry are stacked above a method, step on top. Which wraps the original method first? Bottom up. Decorators are applied from the closest one outwards. Q2 How do you add a custom matcher type to Playwright expect? Module augmentation. declare global or declare module merges into the existing interface. Q3 Why might @pages/login imports fail when running node src/main.ts? tsc only. Use package.json imports with #pages/* instead. Q1 What does returning Promise<this> from open() give subclasses? Polymorphic this. new LoginPage(page).open() is typed as LoginPage. Q2 What does tsc --noEmit do in CI? Check only. It is the gate that keeps type errors out of main."],[24,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[24,"account","A class with a private field","Write a class Account with a JavaScript private #balance that starts at 0, a deposit(n) that rejects amounts that are not positive by throwing a RangeError, and a balance getter. #balance = 0; as a class field, and get balance() to read it. classAccount { #balance = 0; deposit(n) { if (!(n > 0)) thrownewRangeError(\"amount must be positive\"); this.#balance += n; } get balance() { returnthis.#balance; } } #private fields never appear in Object.keys or JSON, unlike TypeScript private. 🌿 Medium"],[24,"abstract","Enforce an abstract method at run time","JavaScript has no abstract. Write a class BasePage whose constructor throws a TypeError when called with new BasePage() directly, and whose isLoaded() throws \"isLoaded is not implemented\" unless a subclass overrides it. new.target tells you which class was used with new. classBasePage { constructor() { if (new.target === BasePage) thrownewTypeError(\"BasePage is abstract\"); } isLoaded() { thrownewError(\"isLoaded is not implemented\"); } } TypeScript catches both mistakes at compile time, and this pattern catches them in plain JavaScript. 🔥 Hard"],[24,"wrap","A decorator by hand","Write withStep(obj, name, title, log) that replaces the method obj[name] with a wrapper. The wrapper calls the original with the same this and arguments, then pushes \"✔ \" + title to the log array, or \"✖ \" + title if it threw, and rethrows. Keep the original, then assign a regular async function so this still works. function withStep(obj, name, title, log) { const original = obj[name]; obj[name] = asyncfunction (...args) { try { const result = await original.apply(this, args); log.push(\"✔ \" + title); return result; } catch (e) { log.push(\"✖ \" + title); throw e; } }; } A method decorator does exactly this, at class definition time. 🧠 Expert"],[24,"steplog","A typed step log","Write createSteps() that returns { step, report }. await step(title, fn) runs fn, records { title, ok, ms } where ms is a whole number of milliseconds, and returns or rethrows. report() returns titles with a tick or a cross, such as \"✔ Login\". Record the result in both the try and the catch branch, then rethrow. function createSteps() { const steps = []; asyncfunction step(title, fn) { const start = Date.now(); try { const value = await fn(); steps.push({ title, ok: true, ms: Math.round(Date.now() - start) }); return value; } catch (e) { steps.push({ title, ok: false, ms: Math.round(Date.now() - start) }); throw e; } } return { step, report: () => steps.map(s => (s.ok ? \"✔ \" : \"✖ \") + s.title) }; } This is a small copy of Playwright test.step, which records the same data for its HTML report."],[24,"steplog","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. Classes in TypeScriptTypeScript adds access modifiers to classes. public is the default, protected allows subclasses, and private allows only the class itself. DecoratorsTypeScript 5 supports the standard decorators proposal. A method decorator is a function that receives the original method and a context object, and returns a replacement. Decorators stack: the one closest to the method wraps first. Declaration files and augmentationA declaration file (.d.ts) contains only types. It describes JavaScript code that has no types of its own. Most npm packages ship one, and others have one in @types/…. Checking JavaScript with JSDocYou can get most of the safety of TypeScript without renaming a single file. With checkJs, or // @ts-check at the top of a file, TypeScript reads types from JSDoc comments and reports the same errors. Moving a project to TypeScript, and the tools around it Real world scenario: a typed page object hierarchyAn abstract path forces every page to declare its URL, protected el() keeps raw locators inside page objects, and returning DashboardPage from loginAs makes the next step in a test auto complete. In a real project, PageLike is Playwright's Page type."],[24,"comic","TypeScript at project scale","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Use abstract bases and protected helpers for page objects, #private for secrets, declaration files for untyped code, and move to TypeScript in small checked steps."],[24,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🧱A UI test framework Made its BasePage abstract with an abstract path. Thirteen pages that had silently opened the home page were fixed on the first compile. 📜An old in house library Got a hand written .d.ts file. The test team used it for months before the library itself was rewritten. 🚚A 900 file test suite Moved to TypeScript over six weeks with checkJs first, then one folder per week, without a single red build. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"TypeScript classes and real projects\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 8Welcome This chapter covers TypeScript at project scale: classes with access modifiers, decorators, declaration files, JSDoc checking and moving a project to TypeScript. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[25,"","Test automation in TypeScript","How TypeScript catches mistakes in test code, running unit tests in TypeScript with node:test, Vitest and Jest, testing types with expectTypeOf, typed Playwright page objects and fixtures, an API client that checks every response with zod, a CI pipeline that type checks first, and typed test data builders. · TypeScript testing · Playwright TypeScript · Vitest · node:test · ts-jest · expectTypeOf · typed fixtures · page object · zod · z.infer · API client · tsc --noEmit · typescript-eslint · test data builder · interview 🟨 JavaScript›🔷 TypeScript›Chapter 26 of 31 🧪 Step 7 · Topic 4 of 7 · Advanced"],[25,"top","","Test automation written in TypeScript: why typed tests fail faster, unit tests with node:test, Vitest and Jest, type tests with expectTypeOf, Playwright with typed page objects and fixtures, a typed API client that checks responses with zod, the type check in CI, and typed test data builders. ⏱️ About 20 min read📚 7 parts🎬 0 animations🔷 3 type checked examples🧪 4 real test runs🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 7 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Most new automation frameworks are written in TypeScript. This chapter puts the earlier TypeScript chapters to work on unit, API and browser tests. 1Why write tests in TypeScript 2Unit tests in TypeScript 3Type tests 4Playwright in TypeScript 5A typed API client with run time checks 6TypeScript in CI 7Real world scenario: typed test data builders Lesson 1 of 7"],[25,"why","Why write tests in TypeScript","🧪 QA essentialTyped tests fail fast on mistakes in the test itself, so red builds point at real product bugs more often. Test code is code. It has typos, wrong arguments and missing awaits, and in JavaScript you only find them when the test runs, often in CI, often after a slow browser start. TypeScript finds them while you type. Every Playwright, Vitest and Jest API ships its own types, so the editor knows every fixture, locator method and matcher. Three test mistakes caught before the browser starts · 🔷 tsc finds 3 errors 📄 src/cart.spec.ts import { test, expect } from\"@playwright/test\"; test(\"add a pen to the cart\", async ({ page, shopp }) => { // a typo in a fixture nameawait page.goto(\"/\"); await page.getByRol(\"button\", { name: \"Add to cart\" }).click(); // a typo in a method nameawait expect(page.getByTestId(\"cart-count\")).toHaveText(1); // a number where text is expected }); Terminal $ npx tsc Terminal output src/cart.spec.ts(3,46): error TS2339: Property 'shopp' does not exist on type 'PlaywrightTestArgs & PlaywrightTestOptions & PlaywrightWorkerArgs & PlaywrightWorkerOptions'. src/cart.spec.ts(5,14): error TS2551: Property 'getByRol' does not exist on type 'Page'. Did you mean 'getByRole'? src/cart.spec.ts(6,59): error TS2345: Argument of type 'number' is not assignable to parameter of type 'string | readonly (string | RegExp)[] | RegExp'. All three would have failed the run in JavaScript, one at a time, each with a stack trace. Here tsc lists them together in under a second, and the editor underlines them before you save. Lesson 2 of 7"],[25,"unit","Unit tests in TypeScript","🧪 QA essentialKnowing which runners strip types and which check them avoids a green suite full of type errors. Node.js 24 and later remove the types and run .ts files directly, so node --test needs no build step. Vitest reads TypeScript too, without any set up. In both cases the runner only strips the types and does not check them, so run tsc as its own step. node:test runs .ts files directly · 🔷 tsc passes, then node:test runs 📄 src/cart.ts exportinterfaceLine { sku: string; price: number; qty: number } exporttypeCoupon = \"SAVE10\" | \"SAVE20\"; constRATES: Record<Coupon, number> = { SAVE10: 0.1, SAVE20: 0.2 }; exportfunction cartTotal(lines: Line[], coupon?: Coupon): number { const sum = lines.reduce((total, l) => total + l.price * l.qty, 0); returnMath.round(sum * (1 - (coupon ? RATES[coupon] : 0))); } 📄 src/cart.test.ts import { test } from\"node:test\"; import assert from\"node:assert/strict\"; import { cartTotal, typeLine } from\"./cart.ts\"; const lines: Line[] = [{ sku: \"pen\", price: 20, qty: 3 }, { sku: \"mug\", price: 199, qty: 1 }]; test(\"total without a coupon\", () => assert.equal(cartTotal(lines), 259)); test(\"SAVE10 takes 10 percent off\", () => assert.equal(cartTotal(lines, \"SAVE10\"), 233)); test(\"an empty cart costs nothing\", () => assert.equal(cartTotal([]), 0)); Terminal $ npx tsc && node --test Terminal output tsc: no type errors ✔ total without a coupon ✔ SAVE10 takes 10 percent off ✔ an empty cart costs nothing ℹ tests 3 ℹ suites 0 ℹ pass 3 ℹ fail 0 ℹ cancelled 0 ℹ skipped 0 ℹ todo 0 ℹ duration_ms 88.4 Vitest with TypeScript and no extra set up · 🔷 tsc passes, then Vitest runs 📄 src/cart.test.ts import { describe, it, expect, expectTypeOf } from\"vitest\"; import { cartTotal, typeCoupon, typeLine } from\"./cart.ts\"; describe(\"cartTotal\", () => { const pen: Line = { sku: \"pen\", price: 20, qty: 5 }; it.each<[Coupon | undefined, number]>([ [undefined, 100], [\"SAVE10\", 90], [\"SAVE20\", 80], ])(\"coupon %s gives %i\", (coupon, want) => { expect(cartTotal([pen], coupon)).toBe(want); }); it(\"has the types the rest of the suite relies on\", () => { expectTypeOf(cartTotal).returns.toEqualTypeOf<number>(); expectTypeOf(cartTotal).parameter(1).toEqualTypeOf<Coupon | undefined>(); }); }); Terminal $ npx tsc && npx vitest run Terminal output tsc: no type errors RUN v5.0.5 project ✓ src/cart.test.ts > cartTotal > coupon undefined gives 100 ✓ src/cart.test.ts > cartTotal > coupon SAVE10 gives 90 ✓ src/cart.test.ts > cartTotal > coupon SAVE20 gives 80 ✓ src/cart.test.ts > cartTotal > has the types the rest of the suite relies on Test Files 1 passed (1) Tests 4 passed (4) Start at 09:00:00 Duration 398ms Jest with TypeScript · Set up only. The test code itself is the same as above. // Jest does not read TypeScript by itself. Pick one transformer:// ts-jest type checks while it runs (slower, catches more)// @swc/jest or babel-jest: removes the types only (fast, run tsc separately)// jest.config.tsimport type { Config } from\"jest\"; const config: Config = { preset: \"ts-jest\", // or: transform: { \"^.+\\\\.tsx?$\": \"@swc/jest\" } testEnvironment: \"node\", testMatch: [\"**/*.test.ts\"], }; exportdefault config; // tsconfig.json for the tests: \"types\": [\"jest\", \"node\"] gives describe, it and expect their types Removing types is not checking types. A test file full of type errors still runs green under node --test, Vitest or @swc/jest. Only tsc (or ts-jest) reports them. That is why every example on this page runs tsc first. Lesson 3 of 7"],[25,"typetests","Type tests","🧪 QA essentialType tests stop a helper change from quietly breaking the types that the rest of the suite depends on. Shared helpers, builders and API clients are used by hundreds of tests, so their types are part of their contract. expectTypeOf from Vitest (and the expect-type package it is built on) asserts on types instead of values. Nothing runs. The check happens when tsc reads the file. Type tests: tests for the types themselves · 🔷 tsc finds 1 error 📄 src/user.test-d.ts import { expectTypeOf } from\"vitest\"; typeRole = \"admin\" | \"customer\"; interfaceUser { id: number; email: string; role: Role } // A helper the whole suite uses. Its type is part of its contract.function makeUser(overrides: Partial<User> = {}): User { return { id: 1, email: \"asha@shop.test\", role: \"customer\", ...overrides }; } expectTypeOf(makeUser).returns.toEqualTypeOf<User>(); expectTypeOf(makeUser).parameter(0).toEqualTypeOf<Partial<User> | undefined>(); expectTypeOf<User[\"role\"]>().toEqualTypeOf<\"admin\" | \"customer\" | \"guest\">(); // somebody expects a guest role Terminal $ npx tsc Terminal output src/user.test-d.ts(13,44): error TS2344: Type '\"admin\" | \"customer\" | \"guest\"' does not satisfy the constraint '\"Expected: literal string: admin, Actual: never\" | \"Expected: literal string: customer, Actual: never\" | \"Expected: literal string: guest, Actual: never\"'. Type '\"admin\"' is not assignable to type '\"Expected: literal string: admin, Actual: never\" | \"Expected: literal string: customer, Actual: never\" | \"Expected: literal string: guest, Actual: never\"'. Type test messages are long because the library builds them out of types. Read the line number first. It points at the assertion that no longer holds, here the role union that has no guest. Vitest can also collect these files with vitest --typecheck, which runs tsc for you on every *.test-d.ts file and shows type failures next to normal test failures. Lesson 4 of 7"],[25,"pw","Playwright in TypeScript","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightTyped page objects and typed fixtures are the standard way to write Playwright suites in teams. Playwright runs TypeScript without any set up, and most Playwright documentation is written in it. Two things become much better with types. Page objects can limit their inputs, for example to real product names. Fixtures declared with test.extend<{ shop: ShopPage }> are typed in every test, so ({ shop }) auto completes and a typo does not compile. Typed page objects and typed fixtures in Playwright · 🔷 tsc passes, then Playwright runs in Chromium 📄 pages/shop.page.ts import { expect, typeLocator, typePage } from\"@playwright/test\"; exporttypeProduct = \"Gel pen\" | \"Coffee mug\" | \"Desk lamp\"; exportclassShopPage { readonly products: Locator; readonly cartCount: Locator; readonly total: Locator; constructor(readonly page: Page) { this.products = page.getByTestId(\"product\"); this.cartCount = page.getByTestId(\"cart-count\"); this.total = page.locator(\"#total\"); } async open(): Promise<this> { awaitthis.page.goto(\"/\"); returnthis; } async logIn(email: string, password: string): Promise<void> { awaitthis.page.getByLabel(\"Email\").fill(email); awaitthis.page.getByLabel(\"Password\").fill(password); awaitthis.page.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Sign in\" }).click(); await expect(this.products.first()).toBeVisible(); } async add(name: Product): Promise<void> { // only real product names compileawaitthis.products.filter({ hasText: name }).getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Add to cart\" }).click(); } } 📄 fixtures.ts import { test as base } from\"@playwright/test\"; import { ShopPage } from\"./pages/shop.page.ts\"; interfaceTestUser { email: string; password: string } // The type argument tells every test which fixtures exist and what they holdexportconst test = base.extend<{ user: TestUser; shop: ShopPage }>({ user: [{ email: \"asha@shop.example\", password: \"s3cret\" }, { option: true }], shop: async ({ page, user }, use) => { const shop = awaitnewShopPage(page).open(); await shop.logIn(user.email, user.password); await use(shop); }, }); export { expect } from\"@playwright/test\"; 📄 tests/cart.spec.ts import { test, expect } from\"../fixtures.ts\"; test(\"one gel pen\", async ({ shop }) => { await shop.add(\"Gel pen\"); await expect(shop.total).toHaveText(\"₹20\"); }); test(\"two lamps use Indian digit grouping\", async ({ shop }) => { await shop.add(\"Desk lamp\"); await shop.add(\"Desk lamp\"); await expect(shop.cartCount).toHaveText(\"2\"); await expect(shop.total).toHaveText(\"₹2,598\"); }); Terminal $ npx tsc && npx playwright test Terminal output tsc: no type errors Running 2 tests using 1 worker ✓ 1 [chromium] › tests/cart.spec.ts:2:1 › one gel pen ✓ 2 [chromium] › tests/cart.spec.ts:8:1 › two lamps use Indian digit grouping 2 passed user is an option fixture. A project in playwright.config.ts can set use: { user: adminUser } and every test in that project logs in as the admin. TypeScript checks that the value has the right shape. Lesson 5 of 7"],[25,"api","A typed API client with run time checks","🧪 QA essentialAPI tests that parse every response with a schema catch contract changes on the first call. TypeScript types disappear at run time, so they cannot check JSON from a server. A schema library such as zod does both jobs at once. The schema checks the data at run time, and z.infer turns the same schema into a TypeScript type, so the two can never drift apart. A typed API client that checks every response with zod · 🔷 tsc passes, then node:test runs 📄 src/client.ts import { z } from\"zod\"; // One schema gives both the run time check and the TypeScript typeexportconstProduct = z.object({ id: z.number().int(), name: z.string().min(1), price: z.number().nonnegative() }); exporttypeProduct = z.infer<typeofProduct>; exportclassProductsClient { privatereadonly baseURL: string; constructor(baseURL: string) { this.baseURL = baseURL; } async list(query = \"\"): Promise<Product[]> { const res = await fetch(`${this.baseURL}/products${query}`); if (!res.ok) thrownewError(`GET /products answered ${res.status}`); return z.array(Product).parse(await res.json()); // unknown JSON in, typed data out } } 📄 test/products.test.ts import { test, after } from\"node:test\"; import assert from\"node:assert/strict\"; importtype { AddressInfo } from\"node:net\"; import { z } from\"zod\"; import { startApi } from\"../src/server.ts\"; import { ProductsClient } from\"../src/client.ts\"; const server = await startApi(); const api = newProductsClient(`http://localhost:${(server.address() as AddressInfo).port}`); after(() => server.close()); test(\"products match the contract\", async () => { const products = await api.list(); assert.equal(products.length, 2); assert.equal(products[0].price + products[1].price, 219); // price is a number here, the type says so }); test(\"a changed field type is caught at the edge\", async () => { await assert.rejects(api.list(\"?v=2\"), (e: unknown) => { assert.ok(e instanceof z.ZodError); console.log(z.prettifyError(e)); returntrue; }); }); Terminal $ npx tsc && node --test Terminal output tsc: no type errors ✔ products match the contract ✖ Invalid input: expected number, received string → at [0].price ✔ a changed field type is caught at the edge ℹ tests 2 ℹ suites 0 ℹ pass 2 ℹ fail 0 ℹ cancelled 0 ℹ skipped 0 ℹ todo 0 ℹ duration_ms 88.4 Without the schema, the second test would have passed and a later assertion would have compared \"20\" + 199, which in JavaScript is the text \"20199\". The schema stops the bad data at the edge, with a message that names the field and its position. Lesson 6 of 7"],[25,"ci","TypeScript in CI","🧪 QA essentialA fast type gate keeps broken test code out of the expensive browser jobs. Put the type check in front of everything slow. It needs no browser and no server, and it fails in seconds. Type aware lint rules from typescript-eslint add checks that tsc does not do, such as a missing await, which is one of the most common causes of flaky tests. Type check first, browsers second · A CI pipeline, not run on this page # .github/workflows/tests.yml name: tests on: [push, pull_request] jobs: types: # fast gate: fails in about 30 seconds runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v5 - uses: actions/setup-node@v5 with: { node-version: 24, cache: npm } - run: npm ci - run: npx tsc --noEmit # type errors in tests or helpers stop here - run: npx eslint . # with typescript-eslint: no floating promises, no any e2e: needs: types # browsers start only when the types are clean runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v5 - uses: actions/setup-node@v5 with: { node-version: 24, cache: npm } - run: npm ci - run: npx playwright install --with-deps chromium - run: npx playwright test typescript-eslint rules that catch flaky tests · Lint set up, not run on this page // eslint.config.js: rules that only work with type informationimport tseslint from\"typescript-eslint\"; exportdefault tseslint.config( ...tseslint.configs.recommendedTypeChecked, { languageOptions: { parserOptions: { projectService: true } }, rules: { \"@typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises\": \"error\", // a missing await before expect or click\"@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any\": \"error\", \"@typescript-eslint/await-thenable\": \"error\", // await on something that is not a promise }, }, ); Lesson 7 of 7"],[25,"scenario","Real world scenario: typed test data builders","🧪 QA essential The situationTest data is copied between tests as large object literals. When the User type gained an address field, 140 tests broke at run time. Typed test data builders · 🔷 tsc passes, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // Typed test data builders: sensible defaults, typed overrides, unique idstypeRole = \"admin\" | \"customer\"; interfaceAddress { city: string; pin: string } interfaceUser { id: number; email: string; role: Role; address: Address } interfaceOrder { id: number; user: User; items: { sku: string; qty: number }[]; status: \"new\" | \"paid\" | \"shipped\" } let next = 1000; const id = () => ++next; function builder<T>(defaults: () => T) { return (overrides: Partial<T> = {}): T => ({ ...defaults(), ...overrides }); } const aUser = builder<User>(() => ({ id: id(), email: `user${next}@shop.test`, role: \"customer\", address: { city: \"Pune\", pin: \"411001\" } })); const anOrder = builder<Order>(() => ({ id: id(), user: aUser(), items: [{ sku: \"pen\", qty: 1 }], status: \"new\" })); const admin = aUser({ role: \"admin\" }); const paid = anOrder({ user: admin, status: \"paid\" }); const big = anOrder({ items: [{ sku: \"lamp\", qty: 2 }, { sku: \"mug\", qty: 4 }] }); console.log(admin.email, admin.role, paid.status, paid.user.id === admin.id); console.log(big.items.length, big.user.role, big.id > paid.id); Terminal $ npx tsc && node src/main.ts Terminal output tsc: no type errors $ node src/main.ts user1001@shop.test admin paid true 2 customer true Each builder holds the defaults in one place and gives every object a fresh id, so tests stay independent. Overrides are Partial<T>, so a test only writes the fields it cares about, and a misspelt field or a wrong role does not compile. When User gains a field, only the builder needs to change. 🧮"],[25,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 The first example has three mistakes. How many type errors does tsc report? One per mistake. Answer: 3 A wrong fixture name, a wrong method name and a number where text is expected. C2 In the node:test example, the cart has 3 pens at ₹20 and 1 mug at ₹199. What is the total? Price times quantity. Answer: 259 3 × 20 + 1 × 199 = 60 + 199 = 259 C1 With SAVE10 the same cart costs what, rounded? Take off 10 percent. Answer: 233 259 × 0.9 = 233.1 Math.round gives 233. C2 Two desk lamps cost ₹1,299 each. What does the total show, as a plain number? Double it. Answer: 2598 2 × 1299 = 2598 The page shows it as ₹2,598. C3 A tsc and lint job takes 40 seconds and a browser job 25 minutes. A broken commit is caught by tsc. How many seconds of browser time are saved? Minutes to seconds. Answer: 1500 25 × 60 = 1500 C1 The products API returns a gel pen at 20 and a mug at 199. What does the passing test add up to? Two numbers. Answer: 219 20 + 199 = 219 C2 In the scenario the id counter starts at 1000. aUser() is called for admin first. What is admin.id? Pre increment. Answer: 1001 ++next raises 1000 to 1001 and returns it. C1 In the scenario, how many ids are taken after admin, paid and big are built? Count every builder call, including nested ones. Answer: 4 admin takes 1. paid takes 1 for the order (its user is given). big takes 1 for the order and 1 for its default user. 1 + 1 + 2 = 4 🎯"],[25,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 Does node --test check the types in a .ts test file? Stripping is not checking. Run tsc as its own step to check types. Q2 Which Playwright call adds your own typed fixtures? Extend the base test. The type argument lists each fixture and its type. Q3 What does z.infer<typeof Product> give you? Infer the type. One schema then serves as both run time check and type. Q1 Why must API responses be checked at run time even in TypeScript? Erased at run time. res.json() is effectively unknown data. A schema turns it into checked data. Q2 Which Jest set up type checks while the tests run? The ts one. SWC and Babel only remove types, which is faster but checks nothing. Q3 What does expectTypeOf(fn).returns.toEqualTypeOf<number>() do at run time? Types only. Type tests fail at compile time, never at run time. Q4 In the Playwright example, what happens with shop.add(\"Pencil\")? A union of names. The Product type allows only the three real names. Q1 Which typescript-eslint rule catches a missing await before expect(...).toHaveText()? A promise left floating. A missing await is a common cause of flaky and falsely green tests. Q2 Why put the tsc job before the browser job in CI? Fail fast. needs: types makes the e2e job wait for a clean type check. Q3 What is an option fixture in Playwright? option: true. For example a user that one project sets to an admin. Q1 Why does the builder take a function that returns defaults instead of a defaults object? Shared objects leak between tests. A shared nested object would be changed by one test and seen by the next. Q2 What does \"20\" + 199 give in JavaScript, the bug the zod schema prevents? Plus with a string. Plus joins text when either side is a string."],[25,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[25,"builder","A test data builder","Write makeBuilder(defaults). defaults is a function that returns a fresh object. makeBuilder returns a function that takes optional overrides and returns the defaults merged with the overrides. Call defaults() inside the returned function, then spread the overrides after it. function makeBuilder(defaults) { return (overrides = {}) => ({ ...defaults(), ...overrides }); } Calling defaults on every build keeps tests from sharing the same nested objects. 🌿 Medium"],[25,"parse","Check a response by hand","Write parseProduct(x) that returns x when it has an integer id, a non-empty string name and a number price of 0 or more. Otherwise throw a TypeError whose message is the first bad field name followed by \": invalid\", checking in the order id, name, price. Keep one small check function per field and loop over them in order. function parseProduct(x) { const rules = { id: v => Number.isInteger(v), name: v => typeof v === \"string\" && v.length > 0, price: v => typeof v === \"number\" && v >= 0, }; for (const [field, ok] ofObject.entries(rules)) { if (!ok(x?.[field])) thrownewTypeError(`${field}: invalid`); } return x; } This is what a zod object schema does for you, plus it gives you the type. 🔥 Hard"],[25,"fixtures","A fixture runner","Write runWithFixtures(fixtures, testFn). Each fixture is async (use) => { …set up…; await use(value); …clean up… }, like in Playwright. Set fixtures up in object order, call testFn with an object of all values, then let them clean up in reverse order. Return what testFn returns. Set up one fixture at a time. Inside its use callback, set up the next one, and run the test when none are left. asyncfunction runWithFixtures(fixtures, testFn) { const names = Object.keys(fixtures); const values = {}; asyncfunction setUp(i) { if (i === names.length) return testFn(values); let result; await fixtures[names[i]](async value => { values[names[i]] = value; result = await setUp(i + 1); }); return result; } return setUp(0); } Nesting each fixture inside the previous one gives the reverse clean up order for free. 🧠 Expert"],[25,"poll","A polling assertion","Write expectPoll(fn, expected, { interval, timeout }). It calls fn until the result equals expected and then resolves to the number of calls. If timeout milliseconds pass first, it rejects with an Error whose message starts with \"expected 2, last value 1\" for those values. Loop: call, compare, check the clock, then wait for the interval. asyncfunction expectPoll(fn, expected, { interval = 10, timeout = 1000 } = {}) { const end = Date.now() + timeout; let calls = 0; let last; while (true) { last = await fn(); calls++; if (last === expected) return calls; if (Date.now() >= end) thrownewError(`expected ${expected}, last value ${last} after ${calls} calls`); awaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, interval)); } } Playwright expect.poll and web first assertions retry like this instead of sleeping."],[25,"poll","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. Why write tests in TypeScriptTest code is code. It has typos, wrong arguments and missing awaits, and in JavaScript you only find them when the test runs, often in CI, often after a slow browser start. TypeScript finds them while you type. Unit tests in TypeScriptNode.js 24 and later remove the types and run .ts files directly, so node --test needs no build step. Vitest reads TypeScript too, without any set up. In both cases the runner only strips the types and does not check them, so run tsc as its own step. Type testsShared helpers, builders and API clients are used by hundreds of tests, so their types are part of their contract. expectTypeOf from Vitest (and the expect-type package it is built on) asserts on types instead of values. Nothing runs. Playwright in TypeScriptPlaywright runs TypeScript without any set up, and most Playwright documentation is written in it. Two things become much better with types. Page objects can limit their inputs, for example to real product names. A typed API client with run time checksTypeScript types disappear at run time, so they cannot check JSON from a server. A schema library such as zod does both jobs at once. TypeScript in CIPut the type check in front of everything slow. It needs no browser and no server, and it fails in seconds. Real world scenario: typed test data buildersEach builder holds the defaults in one place and gives every object a fresh id, so tests stay independent. Overrides are Partial<T>, so a test only writes the fields it cares about, and a misspelt field or a wrong role does not compile."],[25,"comic","Tests that check themselves","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Runners strip types and tsc checks them. Type your fixtures and page objects, check API responses with a schema, and run tsc first in CI."],[25,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🎭An e-commerce UI suite Moved its 400 Playwright tests to TypeScript. Failures caused by mistakes in the test code itself fell from about one a week to almost none. 🔌A payments API team Parsed every response with zod schemas. A field that silently changed from number to string was caught on the first CI run after the backend deploy. 🚦A QA platform team Added a 40 second tsc and lint job in front of the 25 minute browser job, and stopped spending browser minutes on test code that could not work. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Test automation in TypeScript\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 9Welcome This chapter shows test automation written in TypeScript: unit tests, type tests, Playwright with typed page objects and fixtures, a typed API client and the type check in CI. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[26,"","How the TypeScript type system decides","Structural typing with an animated assignment check, literal widening and as const, any versus unknown versus never, function compatibility and variance with in and out, safe dictionary lookups, labeled and variadic tuples, overloads and fluent this types, and recursive Json and dot path types. · structural typing · excess property check · widening · as const · unknown · never · variance · strictFunctionTypes · noUncheckedIndexedAccess · exactOptionalPropertyTypes · tuples · overloads · this type · recursive types · interview 🟨 JavaScript›🔷 TypeScript›Chapter 27 of 31 🧠 Step 7 · Topic 5 of 7 · Advanced"],[26,"top","","Why does one object fit and an almost identical one fail? This chapter explains the rules the checker follows: structural typing and the excess property check, literal types and widening, any, unknown and never, function compatibility and variance, index signatures with the strictest options, tuples, overloads and this, and recursive types. ⏱️ About 22 min read📚 8 parts🎬 2 animations🔷 8 type checked examples🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 7 calculations🧪 4 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Most confusing type errors in test code come from three rules in this chapter: excess property checks, widening of literals, and values that are any instead of unknown. 1Structural typing: shapes, not names 2Literal types and widening 3any, unknown and never 4Function compatibility and variance 5Index signatures and the strictest options 6Tuples: fixed length arrays with names 7Overloads and this 8Recursive types Lesson 1 of 8"],[26,"structural","Structural typing: shapes, not names","🧪 QA essentialMocks and test doubles work because any object with the right shape fits, without extending real classes. In Java or C#, a class is a Reporter only if it says implements Reporter. TypeScript is structural: any value with the right members fits, whatever it is called. This matches how JavaScript code is really written, with plain objects passed around. Structural typing: the shape decides, not the name · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts interfaceReporter { name: string; report(passed: number, failed: number): string; } // No \"implements Reporter\" anywhere: the shape is what countsclassConsoleReporter { name = \"console\"; colors = true; // extra members are fine report(passed: number, failed: number) { return`${passed} passed, ${failed} failed`; } } const slackLike = { name: \"slack\", channel: \"#qa\", report: (p: number, f: number) => `:white_check_mark: ${p} :x: ${f}` }; function publish(r: Reporter) { console.log(`[${r.name}] ${r.report(41, 1)}`); } publish(newConsoleReporter()); publish(slackLike); // a variable with extra fields: allowed publish({ name: \"email\", to: \"qa@shop.test\", report: () => \"sent\" }); // a fresh literal with an unknown fieldtry { publish({ name: \"broken\" }); // report is missing } catch (e) { console.log(\"crashed:\", (e asError).message); } Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(19,26): error TS2353: Object literal may only specify known properties, and 'to' does not exist in type 'Reporter'. src/main.ts(21,11): error TS2741: Property 'report' is missing in type '{ name: string; }' but required in type 'Reporter'. $ node src/main.ts [console] 41 passed, 1 failed [slack] :white_check_mark: 41 :x: 1 [email] sent crashed: r.report is not a function 🎬 Try it"],[26,"structural","How tsc checks one assignment","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. interface Reporter { name: string; report(): string } const slack = { name: \"s\", channel: \"#qa\", report: f }; const r1: Reporter = slack; const r2: Reporter = { name: \"e\", to: \"x\", report: f }; const r3: Reporter = { name: \"broken\" }; Value has Reporter needs Extra fields tsc says Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 5 The excess property check is a typo detector, not a security feature. As the advanced chapter showed with the leaked password, a value from a variable may carry extra fields. API tests must still check that secret fields are absent. Lesson 2 of 8"],[26,"widening","Literal types and widening","A value can have a very exact type. const m = \"GET\" has the type \"GET\", a literal type. A let may change later, so TypeScript widens it to string. Object properties are widened too. as const stops widening and makes everything readonly. Literal types, widening and as const · 🔷 tsc finds 3 errors, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts typeMethod = \"GET\" | \"POST\" | \"PUT\" | \"DELETE\"; function call(method: Method, path: string) { return`${method} ${path}`; } const m1 = \"GET\"; // const: the literal type \"GET\"let m2 = \"GET\"; // let: widened to string, because it may change laterconst m3 = \"POST\"asMethod; const req = { method: \"PUT\", path: \"/cart\" }; // method: stringconst fixed = { method: \"DELETE\", path: \"/cart/1\" } asconst; // method: \"DELETE\", and readonly console.log(call(m1, \"/products\")); console.log(call(m3, \"/orders\")); console.log(call(fixed.method, fixed.path)); console.log(call(m2, \"/products\")); // string is not assignable to Method console.log(call(req.method, req.path)); // the same problem inside an object fixed.path = \"/other\"; // as const made it readonly Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(14,18): error TS2345: Argument of type 'string' is not assignable to parameter of type 'Method'. src/main.ts(15,18): error TS2345: Argument of type 'string' is not assignable to parameter of type 'Method'. src/main.ts(16,7): error TS2540: Cannot assign to 'path' because it is a read-only property. $ node src/main.ts GET /products POST /orders DELETE /cart/1 GET /products PUT /cart This is the most common surprise for beginners: a value that looks exactly right is \"string\" to TypeScript. Use as const, a type annotation, or satisfies to keep the literal. Lesson 3 of 8"],[26,"unknown","any, unknown and never","🧪 QA essentialResponse bodies and test data files are unknown until checked. Using any there hides wrong field names until a test crashes. any turns checking off for a value and everything you reach through it, so mistakes spread silently. unknown accepts any value too, but you must check it before use. never is the type with no values, used for functions that never return and for impossible cases. any, unknown and never · 🔷 tsc finds 1 error, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // any switches checking off. unknown means \"we do not know yet, so check first\".const fromAny: any = JSON.parse('{\"total\": 42}'); const fromUnknown: unknown = JSON.parse('{\"total\": 42}'); if (typeof fromUnknown === \"object\" && fromUnknown !== null && \"total\"in fromUnknown && typeof fromUnknown.total === \"number\") { console.log(\"checked total:\", fromUnknown.total.toFixed(2)); } console.log(fromUnknown.total); // not allowed before a check// never is the empty type: a function that always throws returns neverfunction fail(message: string): never { thrownewError(message); } const port: number = Number(process.env.PORT ?? \"3000\") || fail(\"PORT is not a number\"); console.log(port); console.log(fromAny.totl.toFixed(2)); // a typo that tsc cannot see through any: it crashes at run time Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(9,13): error TS18046: 'fromUnknown' is of type 'unknown'. $ node src/main.ts checked total: 42.00 42 3000 src/main.ts:16 console.log(fromAny.totl.toFixed(2)); // a typo that tsc cannot see through any: it crashes at run time ^ TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'toFixed') at src/main.ts:16:26 Node.js v26.12.1 The program compiled with any but crashed on the typo totl. With unknown the same mistake is impossible, because nothing can be read before a check. Type Accepts You can use it as any everything anything, with no checks unknown everything nothing, until you narrow it never nothing anything, since it cannot happen Lesson 4 of 8"],[26,"functions","Function compatibility and variance","When is one function type usable as another? Fewer parameters is fine, because JavaScript ignores extra arguments. A callback that returns a value is fine where void is expected. Parameter types are where it gets subtle. 🎬 Animated diagram"],[26,"functions","Which way can a type be swapped?","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 4 Function compatibility, variance and in/out · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts typeListener = (event: string, at: number) => void; const fewer: Listener = (event) => console.log(\"got\", event); // fewer parameters: fineconst returns: Listener = (event) => event.length; // a value returned to a void callback: fine fewer(\"click\", Date.now()); returns(\"submit\", Date.now()); classAnimal { name = \"any animal\"; } classDogextendsAnimal { bark() { return\"woof\"; } } let handleAnimal = (a: Animal) => console.log(a.name); let handleDog = (d: Dog) => console.log(d.bark()); handleDog = handleAnimal; // safe: something that handles any animal can handle a dog handleDog(newDog()); handleAnimal = handleDog; // unsafe: it would call bark() on a cat (strictFunctionTypes)// Variance annotations (TypeScript 4.7) document and check how T is usedinterfaceProducer<out T> { next(): T } interfaceConsumer<inT> { accept(value: T): void } const dogs: Producer<Dog> = { next: () => newDog() }; const animals: Producer<Animal> = dogs; // out: Producer<Dog> is a Producer<Animal>const anyAnimal: Consumer<Animal> = { accept: (a) => console.log(\"accepted\", a.name) }; const forDogs: Consumer<Dog> = anyAnimal; // in: works the other way round console.log(animals.next().name); forDogs.accept(newDog()); interfaceBroken<inT> { get(): T } // T is used as output, so \"in\" is wrong Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(17,1): error TS2322: Type '(d: Dog) => void' is not assignable to type '(a: Animal) => void'. Types of parameters 'd' and 'a' are incompatible. Property 'bark' is missing in type 'Animal' but required in type 'Dog'. src/main.ts(29,18): error TS2636: Type 'Broken<super-T>' is not assignable to type 'Broken<sub-T>' as implied by variance annotation. The types returned by 'get()' are incompatible between these types. Type 'super-T' is not assignable to type 'sub-T'. $ node src/main.ts got click any animal any animal accepted any animal Methods written as method(x: T): void in an interface are still checked loosely (bivariantly) for compatibility with old code. Writing them as properties, method: (x: T) => void, gets the strict check. Lesson 5 of 8"],[26,"index","Index signatures and the strictest options","🧪 QA essentialLookups in test data maps and table rows by index are exactly where undefined sneaks in. An index signature, { [key: string]: number } or Record<string, number>, describes objects used as dictionaries. By default TypeScript pretends every key exists. Two options, not included in strict, make it honest. Index signatures and the two strictest options · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // An index signature: any string key, number valuesconst timings: Record<string, number> = { login: 840, search: 312 }; const t1 = timings[\"checkout\"]; // with noUncheckedIndexedAccess: number | undefined console.log(t1 === undefined ? \"no timing for checkout\" : t1.toFixed(0)); const t2: number = timings[\"login\"]; // might be undefined, so tsc refusesconst steps = [\"open\", \"login\", \"pay\"]; const last = steps[steps.length - 1]; // string | undefined too console.log(last?.toUpperCase()); for (const [name, ms] ofObject.entries(timings)) console.log(name, ms.toFixed(0)); // entries are safe// exactOptionalPropertyTypes: \"may be missing\" is not the same as \"may be undefined\"interfaceRunOptions { retries?: number } const ok: RunOptions = {}; const notOk: RunOptions = { retries: undefined }; console.log(ok, notOk); Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(7,7): error TS2322: Type 'number | undefined' is not assignable to type 'number'. Type 'undefined' is not assignable to type 'number'. src/main.ts(18,7): error TS2375: Type '{ retries: undefined; }' is not assignable to type 'RunOptions' with 'exactOptionalPropertyTypes: true'. Consider adding 'undefined' to the types of the target's properties. Types of property 'retries' are incompatible. Type 'undefined' is not assignable to type 'number'. $ node src/main.ts no timing for checkout PAY login 840 search 312 {} { retries: undefined } Option Effect noUncheckedIndexedAccess obj[key] and arr[i] may be undefined exactOptionalPropertyTypes retries?: number may be missing, but not set to undefined Many teams turn on noUncheckedIndexedAccess in test code, where lookups by name (\"the row for asha\") are common and a missing row should be handled, not crash. Lesson 6 of 8"],[26,"tuples","Tuples: fixed length arrays with names","A tuple is an array with a fixed length and a type per position. Elements can have labels, be optional, or be a rest of many. Variadic tuple types, [...A, ...B], keep exact shapes when functions combine or forward argument lists, which is how wrappers such as retry helpers stay fully typed. Labeled, optional, rest and variadic tuples · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // Labeled, optional and rest elementstypeRange = [start: number, end: number]; typeCell = [row: number, col: number, note?: string]; typeLog = [level: \"info\" | \"error\", ...parts: string[]]; const r: Range = [10, 20]; const c: Cell = [1, 2]; const l: Log = [\"error\", \"payment\", \"timeout\"]; console.log(r, c, l); // Variadic tuple types keep the exact shape when joining tuplesfunction concat<Aextendsunknown[], Bextendsunknown[]>(a: [...A], b: [...B]): [...A, ...B] { return [...a, ...b]; } const joined = concat([1, \"two\"], [true]); // [number, string, boolean]const [n, s, b] = joined; console.log(n.toFixed(1), s.toUpperCase(), b); // Typing a function that forwards its argumentsfunction withRetry<Argsextendsunknown[], R>(fn: (...args: Args) => R, times: number) { return (...args: Args): R => { let last: unknown; for (let i = 0; i < times; i++) { try { return fn(...args); } catch (e) { last = e; } } throw last; }; } constget = withRetry((path: string, timeout: number) => `GET ${path} in ${timeout} ms`, 3); console.log(get(\"/health\", 500)); get(\"/health\"); // the timeout argument is missingconst bad: Range = [1, 2, 3]; // one too many Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(26,1): error TS2554: Expected 2 arguments, but got 1. src/main.ts(27,7): error TS2322: Type '[number, number, number]' is not assignable to type 'Range'. Source has 3 element(s) but target allows only 2. $ node src/main.ts [ 10, 20 ] [ 1, 2 ] [ 'error', 'payment', 'timeout' ] 1.0 TWO true GET /health in 500 ms Lesson 7 of 8"],[26,"overloads","Overloads and this","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightPage object and request builder chains keep their subclass methods thanks to this return types. Overloads give one function several typed call signatures, so the return type follows the argument type. A this parameter says what this must be when the function runs, and a this return type lets builder methods keep the subclass type, so chained calls do not lose methods. Overloads, this parameters and fluent this types · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // Overloads: several call signatures, one implementationfunction parse(input: string): number; function parse(input: string[]): number[]; function parse(input: string | string[]): number | number[] { returnArray.isArray(input) ? input.map(Number) : Number(input); } const one = parse(\"42\"); // numberconst many = parse([\"1\", \"2\"]); // number[] console.log(one + 1, many.length); // A \"this\" parameter says what this must be. It is erased from the JavaScript.interfaceButton { label: string; clicks: number } function onClick(this: Button) { this.clicks++; return`${this.label} clicked ${this.clicks} times`; } const buy: Button & { click: typeof onClick } = { label: \"Buy\", clicks: 0, click: onClick }; console.log(buy.click()); // this as a return type gives fluent builders that keep the subclass typeclassRequestBuilder { headers: Record<string, string> = {}; header(k: string, v: string): this { this.headers[k] = v; returnthis; } } classAuthRequestBuilderextendsRequestBuilder { token(t: string): this { returnthis.header(\"Authorization\", `Bearer ${t}`); } } const built = newAuthRequestBuilder().header(\"Accept\", \"application/json\").token(\"abc\"); console.log(built.headers); parse(42); // no overload takes a numbertry { onClick(); // called without a Button as this } catch (e) { console.log(\"crashed:\", (e asError).message); } Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(28,7): error TS2769: No overload matches this call. The last overload gave the following error. Argument of type 'number' is not assignable to parameter of type 'string[]'. src/main.ts(30,3): error TS2684: The 'this' context of type 'void' is not assignable to method's 'this' of type 'Button'. $ node src/main.ts 43 2 Buy clicked 1 times { Accept: 'application/json', Authorization: 'Bearer abc' } crashed: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'clicks') Prefer a union parameter or a generic when they are enough. Overloads are best when different inputs give clearly different outputs, as in parse above or document.createElement. Lesson 8 of 8"],[26,"recursive","Recursive types","A type may refer to itself. That is how trees are described: JSON, file systems, menus, nested config. Recursive conditional types go further and compute types such as every dot path in an object. Recursive types: JSON and dot paths · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // A type can refer to itself: this describes every value JSON.parse can returntypeJson = string | number | boolean | null | Json[] | { [key: string]: Json }; const body: Json = { order: { id: 7, items: [{ sku: \"PEN-1\", qty: 2 }], paid: true, note: null } }; // Count every leaf value in a JSON tree, typed all the way downfunction countLeaves(value: Json): number { if (value === null || typeof value !== \"object\") return1; const children = Array.isArray(value) ? value : Object.values(value); return children.reduce<number>((sum, child) => sum + countLeaves(child), 0); } console.log(\"leaf values:\", countLeaves(body)); // Recursive conditional type: all keys as dot paths, handy for typed config lookupstypePaths<T> = Textends object ? { [KinkeyofT & string]: K | `${K}.${Paths<T[K]>}` }[keyofT & string] : never; const config = { server: { port: 3000, tls: { enabled: false } }, retries: 2 }; typeConfigPath = Paths<typeof config>; const p1: ConfigPath = \"server.tls.enabled\"; console.log(p1); const date: Json = newDate(); // a Date is not JSONconst p2: ConfigPath = \"server.host\"; // no such path Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(21,7): error TS2322: Type 'Date' is not assignable to type 'Json'. Type 'Date' is not assignable to type '{ [key: string]: Json; }'. Index signature for type 'string' is missing in type 'Date'. src/main.ts(22,7): error TS2820: Type '\"server.host\"' is not assignable to type '\"retries\" | \"server\" | \"server.port\" | \"server.tls\" | \"server.tls.enabled\"'. Did you mean '\"server.port\"'? $ node src/main.ts leaf values: 5 server.tls.enabled Very deep recursive types make tsc slow and give long error messages. Keep them for shared building blocks, such as a typed config reader, not for every object. 🧮"],[26,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/7correct C1 An interface needs 2 members and an object has those plus 3 extra. How many extra members does tsc ignore when the object comes from a variable? Extra members are ignored for variables. Answer: 3 Only fresh literals are checked for extras. C2 How many values does \"GET\" | \"POST\" | \"PUT\" | \"DELETE\" allow? Count the literals. Answer: 4 One per member. C1 A tuple type [row: number, col: number, note?: string]. What is the largest allowed length? The optional element counts when present. Answer: 3 Allowed lengths are 2 and 3. C2 A Log tuple is [level, ...parts: string[]]. How many parts does [\"error\", \"payment\", \"timeout\"] have? Everything after the level. Answer: 2 3 − 1 = 2 C1 How many leaf values does { a: 1, b: [true, null], c: { d: \"x\" } } have? Count values that are not arrays or objects. Answer: 4 1, true, null and \"x\". C2 How many dot paths does Paths give for { server: { port: 3000 }, retries: 2 }? Each key, plus nested paths. Answer: 3 \"server\", \"server.port\" and \"retries\". C1 A function has 2 overload signatures and 1 implementation signature. How many signatures can callers see? The implementation signature is hidden. Answer: 2 Only the overloads are visible from outside. 🎯"],[26,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 A class has every member of an interface but does not say implements. Can it be used as that interface? Structural typing. The name and the implements clause do not matter, the members do. Q2 What is the type of let m = \"GET\"? let may change. let widens literals. const m = \"GET\" would keep \"GET\". Q3 Which type must be checked before you can use the value? The safe top type. any allows everything without checks, which hides mistakes. Q1 Why does { name: \"x\", to: \"y\", report } passed directly to a Reporter parameter fail, when the same object in a variable passes? Freshness. Only a literal written in place is checked for unknown fields. Q2 What does as const do to { method: \"GET\" }? No widening. It is compile time only, Object.freeze would be needed at run time. Q3 With noUncheckedIndexedAccess, what is the type of arr[0] for arr: string[]? The index may not exist. You must check it or use optional chaining. Q4 Can (a: Animal) => void be assigned to a variable of type (d: Dog) => void? Parameters flip. That is contravariance of parameter types. Q1 What does out T in interface Producer<out T> declare? Covariance. tsc reports an error if T is used as an input inside the interface. Q2 What does a return type of this in a builder method give? Fluent subclasses. header() on AuthRequestBuilder still returns AuthRequestBuilder. Q3 With exactOptionalPropertyTypes, is { retries: undefined } a valid { retries?: number }? Missing is not undefined. This matters for APIs where sending a field as undefined differs from leaving it out. Q1 What is the type of concat([1], [\"a\", true]) with [...A, ...B] as the return type? Variadic tuples keep positions. Each position keeps its own type. Q2 Which value is not a valid Json in the recursive Json type? Only JSON values. A Date is an object with methods, not a plain JSON object of Json values."],[26,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[26,"fits","Does it fit the shape?","Write fits(obj, keys) that returns true when obj is a non null object that has every key in keys, like a structural type check at run time. Extra keys are fine. Check for an object first, then use every with in. function fits(obj, keys) { returntypeof obj === \"object\" && obj !== null && keys.every(k => k in obj); } This is what structural typing means: required members must be present, extras are allowed. 🌿 Medium"],[26,"leaves","Count JSON leaves","Write countLeaves(value) that counts every value in a JSON tree that is not an array or an object. null counts as a leaf. Recursion: a leaf is 1, a container is the sum of its children. function countLeaves(value) { if (value === null || typeof value !== \"object\") return1; const children = Array.isArray(value) ? value : Object.values(value); return children.reduce((sum, child) => sum + countLeaves(child), 0); } The recursive Json type and the recursive function have the same shape. 🔥 Hard"],[26,"getpath","Read a dot path","Write getPath(obj, path) that follows a dot path such as \"server.tls.enabled\" and returns the value, or undefined if any step is missing. Split on dots and walk with reduce, stopping at null or undefined. function getPath(obj, path) { return path.split(\".\").reduce((cur, key) => (cur == null ? undefined : cur[key]), obj); } With the Paths type, a wrong path is a compile error. At run time the function still handles missing steps safely. 🧠 Expert"],[26,"lookup","A safe dictionary lookup","Write lookup(map, key, fallback) that returns map[key] only if the key is the object's own property, otherwise the fallback. Keys such as \"toString\" that come from the prototype must give the fallback. Object.hasOwn checks only the object's own keys. function lookup(map, key, fallback) { returnObject.hasOwn(map, key) ? map[key] : fallback; } This is the run time version of what noUncheckedIndexedAccess forces you to think about: a key may be missing."],[26,"lookup","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. Structural typing: shapes, not namesIn Java or C#, a class is a Reporter only if it says implements Reporter. TypeScript is structural: any value with the right members fits, whatever it is called. This matches how JavaScript code is really written, with plain objects passed around. Literal types and wideningA value can have a very exact type. const m = \"GET\" has the type \"GET\", a literal type. A let may change later, so TypeScript widens it to string. Object properties are widened too. as const stops widening and makes everything readonly. any, unknown and neverany turns checking off for a value and everything you reach through it, so mistakes spread silently. unknown accepts any value too, but you must check it before use. Function compatibility and varianceWhen is one function type usable as another? Fewer parameters is fine, because JavaScript ignores extra arguments. A callback that returns a value is fine where void is expected. Parameter types are where it gets subtle. Index signatures and the strictest optionsAn index signature, { [key: string]: number } or Record<string, number>, describes objects used as dictionaries. By default TypeScript pretends every key exists. Two options, not included in strict, make it honest. Tuples: fixed length arrays with namesA tuple is an array with a fixed length and a type per position. Elements can have labels, be optional, or be a rest of many. Overloads and thisOverloads give one function several typed call signatures, so the return type follows the argument type. Recursive typesA type may refer to itself. That is how trees are described: JSON, file systems, menus, nested config. Recursive conditional types go further and compute types such as every dot path in an object."],[26,"comic","How the checker thinks","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember TypeScript compares shapes, widens let and object values, wants unknown checked before use, and compares function parameters in the opposite direction to return values."],[26,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🧪A test team Replaced a heavy mocking library with plain objects that match the interface. Structural typing made them valid test doubles with no casts. 🐛An API client Typed every response as unknown and checked it once. A renamed field in the backend became a clear error instead of an undefined value deep in a report. 🔁A retry helper Used variadic tuple types, so wrapping any function with retries kept its exact arguments and return type. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"How the TypeScript type system decides\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 10Welcome This chapter explains how the TypeScript checker decides: structural typing, widening, unknown, variance, index signatures, tuples, overloads and recursive types. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[27,"","TypeScript project setup: tsconfig, modules and builds","The groups of tsconfig options and an option TypeScript 7 removed, import rules under nodenext with an animated resolver, type only imports, #/ subpath imports, erasable alternatives to enums and parameter properties, interface and namespace merging, isolatedDeclarations, and a two package monorepo built with tsc -b. · tsconfig · module resolution · nodenext · bundler · verbatimModuleSyntax · import type · subpath imports · erasableSyntaxOnly · type stripping · declaration merging · namespace · isolatedDeclarations · project references · tsc -b · TypeScript 7 🟨 JavaScript›🔷 TypeScript›Chapter 28 of 31 ⚙️ Step 7 · Topic 6 of 7 · Advanced"],[27,"top","","Most TypeScript problems at work are not about types but about setup: imports that cannot be found, enums that Node refuses, an old tsconfig that TypeScript 7 rejects. This chapter covers tsconfig in depth, ES modules and module resolution, type only imports, #/ subpath imports, erasable syntax for running .ts files directly, declaration merging, isolatedDeclarations and project references. ⏱️ About 22 min read📚 7 parts🎬 2 animations🔷 5 type checked examples🧪 2 real test runs🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 7 calculations🧪 4 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Test projects are usually set up by copying a config from somewhere. This chapter explains what each setting does, so import errors and \"works on my machine\" problems can be fixed instead of worked around. 1tsconfig.json in depth 2ES modules and module resolution 3Subpath imports instead of ../../.. 4Erasable syntax and running .ts files directly 5Declaration merging and namespaces 6isolatedDeclarations and fast declaration files 7Project references for big repositories Lesson 1 of 7"],[27,"tsconfig","tsconfig.json in depth","🧪 QA essentialTest projects often copy an old tsconfig. Knowing which options matter avoids hours of strange import errors. Every TypeScript project is driven by tsconfig.json. It answers four questions: which files to check (include, exclude, files), how strict to be, what JavaScript to produce, and how imports are found. Since TypeScript 6.0 a fresh project already defaults to strict: true, module: \"esnext\", target: \"es2025\" and types: []. Group Options you will meet Files include, exclude, rootDir, outDir Checking strict, noUncheckedIndexedAccess, exactOptionalPropertyTypes, noImplicitOverride Output target, declaration, sourceMap, noEmit Modules module, moduleResolution, verbatimModuleSyntax, paths Environment lib, types, skipLibCheck Sharing extends (a base config, or several since 5.0), references Old options do not live forever. TypeScript 6.0 deprecated a long list and TypeScript 7.0 removed them, so an old config stops the build: An old option that TypeScript 7.0 no longer accepts · 🔷 tsc finds 1 error 📄 src/main.ts const ok = 1; console.log(ok); Terminal $ npx tsc Terminal output tsconfig.json(15,5): error TS5102: Option 'baseUrl' has been removed. Please remove it from your configuration. Use '\"paths\": {\"*\": [\"./*\"]}' instead. Lesson 2 of 7"],[27,"esm","ES modules and module resolution","🧪 QA essentialPlaywright and Vitest projects break most often on imports: missing extensions, CommonJS against ES modules, and types imported as values. The module and moduleResolution options tell tsc which rules the real runtime will use to find files. With nodenext it copies Node exactly: ES module imports need the file extension, and packages are found through their exports field. With bundler it copies Vite, webpack and esbuild, which allow imports without extensions. 🎬 Try it"],[27,"esm","How tsc and Node find an import under nodenext","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. import { total } from \"./price.ts\"; import { total } from \"./price\"; import { total } from \"#/lib/price.ts\"; import { z } from \"zod\"; Import Where it looks Found Result Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 4 verbatimModuleSyntax keeps imports exactly as written, so anything that is only a type must say so with import type or an inline type marker. Tools that strip types one file at a time then know which imports to delete. ES module imports under module nodenext · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors 📄 src/main.ts import { total, typeItem } from\"./price.ts\"; // type is erased, total staysimport { total as sum } from\"./price\"; // no extension: Node cannot find this fileimport { ItemasRow } from\"./price.ts\"; // a type imported like a valueconst cart: Item[] = [{ sku: \"PEN-1\", price: 49 }, { sku: \"BAG-2\", price: 899 }]; console.log(\"total\", total(cart)); 📄 src/price.ts exportinterfaceItem { sku: string; price: number } exportfunction total(items: Item[]): number { return items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0); } Terminal $ npx tsc Terminal output src/main.ts(2,30): error TS2835: Relative import paths need explicit file extensions in ECMAScript imports when '--moduleResolution' is 'node16' or 'nodenext'. Did you mean './price.js'? src/main.ts(3,10): error TS1484: 'Item' is a type and must be imported using a type-only import when 'verbatimModuleSyntax' is enabled. Runs in module moduleResolution Node.js nodenext (or node20 since 5.9) follows module Vite, webpack, esbuild esnext or preserve bundler Lesson 3 of 7"],[27,"subpath","#️⃣Subpath imports instead of ../../..","🧪 QA essential Deep test folders end up with imports like ../../../lib/price. Node's subpath imports fix that with an imports field in package.json, and both Node and tsc understand it without extra tools. TypeScript 6.0 added support for the short #/ prefix that Node recently allowed. Subpath imports that start with #/ (TypeScript 6.0) · 🧪 Tests run 📄 package.json { \"name\": \"shop-tests\", \"type\": \"module\", \"imports\": { \"#/*\": \"./src/*\" } } 📄 src/tests/cart.test.ts // #/ points at the src folder, so deep files never need ../../..import { total, typeItem } from\"#/lib/price.ts\"; const items: Item[] = [{ sku: \"PEN-1\", price: 49 }, { sku: \"PEN-2\", price: 51 }]; console.log(total(items) === 100 ? \"PASS total of two pens\" : \"FAIL\"); Terminal $ npx tsc && node src/tests/cart.test.ts Terminal output tsc: no type errors PASS total of two pens The older paths option in tsconfig only teaches tsc where files are. Node itself does not read it, so a separate plugin was needed at run time. Subpath imports work in both places with one setting. Lesson 4 of 7"],[27,"erasable","Erasable syntax and running .ts files directly","🧪 QA essentialRunning test files with plain node, without ts-node or a bundler, is now common, and these three features are what breaks it. Node.js 23.6 and later run .ts files directly by replacing types with spaces, without checking them. That only works for TypeScript features that leave no JavaScript behind. Enums, namespaces with values and constructor parameter properties produce real code, so Node refuses them. erasableSyntaxOnly (TypeScript 5.8) makes tsc flag them early. erasableSyntaxOnly marks code that Node cannot strip · 🔷 tsc finds 3 errors 📄 src/main.ts // Node.js runs .ts files by deleting the types. Code that needs real JavaScript output cannot be deleted.enumStatus { Pass, Fail } // generates an object at run timeclassClient { constructor(private baseURL: string) {} // parameter property: generates this.baseURL = baseURL } namespace Legacy { exportconst v = 1; } // generates an object too// The erasable alternativesconstStatus2 = { Pass: 0, Fail: 1 } asconst; typeStatus2 = (typeofStatus2)[keyoftypeofStatus2]; classClient2 { baseURL: string; constructor(baseURL: string) { this.baseURL = baseURL; } } const s: Status2 = Status2.Fail; console.log(s, newClient2(\"http://localhost:3000\").baseURL, Status, Client, Legacy); Terminal $ npx tsc Terminal output src/main.ts(2,6): error TS1294: This syntax is not allowed when 'erasableSyntaxOnly' is enabled. src/main.ts(5,15): error TS1294: This syntax is not allowed when 'erasableSyntaxOnly' is enabled. src/main.ts(8,11): error TS1294: This syntax is not allowed when 'erasableSyntaxOnly' is enabled. This is also why the earlier chapters prefer as const objects over enums. They behave the same, work in Node, Deno and Bun without a build step, and the JavaScript output is exactly what you wrote. Lesson 5 of 7"],[27,"merging","Declaration merging and namespaces","TypeScript merges some declarations that share a name. Two interface blocks become one, which is how libraries let you add fields to their types. A function and a namespace can merge too, giving a function with extra properties. Namespaces come from before ES modules. In new code, use modules instead, and keep namespaces for reading older code and type declarations. Declaration merging and namespaces · 🔷 tsc finds 1 error 📄 src/main.ts // Two declarations of the same interface merge into oneinterfaceTestInfo { title: string } interfaceTestInfo { retries: number } const info: TestInfo = { title: \"checkout works\", retries: 2 }; // Adding a field to a global type, as test libraries do declare global { interfaceWindow { __TEST_USER__?: string } } // A function and a namespace with the same name merge: callable, with extra membersfunction money(cents: number): string { return (cents / 100).toFixed(2); } namespace money { exportconst currency = \"INR\"; } console.log(info.title, info.retries, money(4950), money.currency); const wrong: TestInfo = { title: \"only a title\" }; export {}; Terminal $ npx tsc Terminal output src/main.ts(18,7): error TS2741: Property 'retries' is missing in type '{ title: string; }' but required in type 'TestInfo'. This file is only checked here, not run. The namespace produces real JavaScript, so Node's type stripping refuses it, exactly as the erasable syntax part above explained. Lesson 6 of 7"],[27,"isolated","isolatedDeclarations and fast declaration files","A library publishes .d.ts files that describe its exports. Normally only tsc can write them, because it must infer return types. With isolatedDeclarations (TypeScript 5.5) every export states its type, so faster tools such as esbuild, oxc or swc can write the declarations one file at a time, in parallel. isolatedDeclarations: every export states its type · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts exportinterfaceItem { sku: string; price: number } exportfunction total(items: Item[]): number { // explicit return type: finereturn items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0); } exportfunction withTax(amount: number) { // return type must be inferred: not allowedreturn amount * 1.18; } exportconstVAT = 0.18; // a literal is easy to read: fineexportconst defaults = { currency: \"INR\", vat: VAT }; // an object made of other values: not allowedconst local = (n: number) => n * 2; // not exported, so nothing to declare console.log(total([{ sku: \"A\", price: 10 }]), withTax(100), local(2), defaults.currency); Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(8,10): error TS9013: Expression type can't be inferred with --isolatedDeclarations. src/main.ts(12,49): error TS9013: Expression type can't be inferred with --isolatedDeclarations. $ node src/main.ts 10 118 4 INR Lesson 7 of 7"],[27,"refs","Project references for big repositories","In a large repository with many packages, checking everything every time is slow. Project references split the code into projects that build separately and in order. tsc -b (build mode) checks only what changed since the last build. 🎬 Animated diagram"],[27,"refs","A monorepo with project references","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 5 Project references: build only what changed · 🧪 Tests run 📄 packages/core/tsconfig.json { \"compilerOptions\": { \"composite\": true, \"target\": \"esnext\", \"module\": \"nodenext\", \"strict\": true, \"outDir\": \"dist\", \"rootDir\": \"src\" }, \"include\": [ \"src\" ] } 📄 packages/app/tsconfig.json { \"compilerOptions\": { \"target\": \"esnext\", \"module\": \"nodenext\", \"strict\": true, \"outDir\": \"dist\", \"rootDir\": \"src\" }, \"references\": [ { \"path\": \"../core\" } ], \"include\": [ \"src\" ] } 📄 packages/app/src/main.ts import { slug } from\"../../core/dist/index.js\"; console.log(slug(\" Add To Cart \")); Terminal $ npx tsc -b packages/app --verbose node packages/app/dist/main.js npx tsc -b packages/app --verbose Terminal output --- first build --- Project 'packages/core/tsconfig.json' is out of date because output file 'packages/core/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo' does not exist Building project 'packages/core/tsconfig.json'... Project 'packages/app/tsconfig.json' is out of date because output file 'packages/app/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo' does not exist Building project 'packages/app/tsconfig.json'... add-to-cart --- second build, nothing changed --- Project 'packages/core/tsconfig.json' is up to date because newest input 'packages/core/src/index.ts' is older than its output Project 'packages/app/tsconfig.json' is up to date because newest input 'packages/app/src/main.ts' is older than its output The second build checked nothing, because no input changed. TypeScript 7.0, the native rewrite, also adds --builders to build independent projects at the same time, and --checkers to split checking across CPU cores. 🧮"],[27,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/7correct C1 An import statement has 3 imported names and 2 of them are types. With import type markers, how many names remain in the JavaScript? Types are erased. Answer: 1 3 − 2 = 1 C2 A test file is 4 folders below src. How many \"../\" parts does a relative import to src/lib need? One per folder level. Answer: 4 A #/lib import needs none. C1 erasableSyntaxOnly found an enum, a parameter property and a namespace. How many errors? One per construct. Answer: 3 Each produces real JavaScript. C2 A monorepo has 10 packages and one changed with 2 packages depending on it. How many does tsc -b rebuild? The changed one plus dependents. Answer: 3 1 + 2 = 3 C1 The VS Code build took 125.7 s with TypeScript 6 and 10.6 s with TypeScript 7. About how many times faster is that, to one decimal place? Divide old by new. Answer: 11.9 125.710.6 ≈ 11.9 C2 With --checkers 4 on a 4 core machine, if checking took 20 s on one thread and splits perfectly, how many seconds does it take? Divide by four. Answer: 5 204 = 5 s Real projects get somewhat less than perfect splitting. C1 A tsconfig extends two bases. The first sets strict true, the second sets strict false. Later entries win. What is strict? The last one wins. Answer: false Options from later configs in the extends array override earlier ones. 🎯"],[27,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 Which file configures a TypeScript project? ts plus config. tsc -p . reads it. Q2 Under module nodenext, which import works for src/price.ts from src/main.ts? Node does not guess. allowImportingTsExtensions lets tsc accept the .ts extension. Q3 What does import type tell the tools? Erased. Nothing remains of it in the JavaScript. Q1 Why does Node refuse an enum in a .ts file it runs directly? Erasable syntax. erasableSyntaxOnly reports enums, value namespaces and parameter properties. Q2 What does the package.json field \"imports\": { \"#/*\": \"./src/*\" } do? Subpath imports. Unlike tsconfig paths, Node reads it too. Q3 Two interfaces named TestInfo declare title and retries. What is TestInfo? Declaration merging. An object must then have both fields. Q4 Which moduleResolution copies Vite and esbuild? Bundlers allow imports without extensions. classic and node10 were deprecated in 6.0 and removed in 7.0. Q1 What does isolatedDeclarations require? Declarations per file. Then tools other than tsc can write .d.ts files fast. Q2 What happens in TypeScript 7.0 with \"baseUrl\": \".\" in tsconfig? Deprecated in 6.0. The error suggests using paths instead. Q3 What does tsc -b skip? Build mode. It compares inputs with the .tsbuildinfo file. Q1 A project needs \"composite\": true when? References need it. composite makes sure declarations and build info are produced. Q2 Why can a value namespace not be stripped by Node? Real JavaScript. A namespace with only types would be erasable."],[27,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[27,"resolve","Resolve a #/ import","Write resolveSubpath(spec, map). If spec starts with a key of map that ends in * (such as \"#/*\"), replace that prefix with the value without its *. Otherwise return null. Strip the * from both sides and swap the prefix. function resolveSubpath(spec, map) { for (const [key, target] ofObject.entries(map)) { const prefix = key.slice(0, -1); if (key.endsWith(\"*\") && spec.startsWith(prefix)) return target.slice(0, -1) + spec.slice(prefix.length); } returnnull; } This is the core of how Node and tsc read the imports field. 🌿 Medium"],[27,"ext","Add the missing extension","Write fixImport(spec) for imports under nodenext. Relative specs (starting with ./ or ../) that have no extension get .ts added. Package names and specs that already end in .ts, .js or .json stay the same. Check relative first, then test the ending with a regex. function fixImport(spec) { const relative = spec.startsWith(\"./\") || spec.startsWith(\"../\"); if (!relative || /\\.(ts|js|json)$/.test(spec)) return spec; return spec + \".ts\"; } This is the change most projects need when they move to nodenext. 🔥 Hard"],[27,"extends","Merge extended configs","Write mergeConfigs(list) that merges an array of tsconfig objects. compilerOptions are merged key by key, with later configs winning. Other top level keys are replaced by later values. Start with empty compilerOptions and use Object.assign for that key only. function mergeConfigs(list) { const out = { compilerOptions: {} }; for (const cfg of list) { for (const [k, v] ofObject.entries(cfg)) { if (k === \"compilerOptions\") Object.assign(out.compilerOptions, v); else out[k] = v; } } return out; } This is how extends with an array of bases behaves since TypeScript 5.0. 🧠 Expert"],[27,"order","Order project builds","Write buildOrder(refs) where refs maps each project to the projects it references. Return the projects in an order where every project comes after the ones it references. Visit projects and references in the order given. A depth first visit that adds a project after its references. function buildOrder(refs) { const done = newSet(); const out = []; const visit = p => { if (done.has(p)) return; done.add(p); for (const r of refs[p] || []) visit(r); out.push(p); }; Object.keys(refs).forEach(visit); return out; } tsc -b sorts projects the same way before building them."],[27,"order","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. tsconfig.json in depthEvery TypeScript project is driven by tsconfig.json. It answers four questions: which files to check (include, exclude, files), how strict to be, what JavaScript to produce, and how imports are found. ES modules and module resolutionThe module and moduleResolution options tell tsc which rules the real runtime will use to find files. With nodenext it copies Node exactly: ES module imports need the file extension, and packages are found through their exports field. Subpath imports instead of ../../..Deep test folders end up with imports like ../../../lib/price. Node's subpath imports fix that with an imports field in package.json, and both Node and tsc understand it without extra tools. Erasable syntax and running .ts files directlyNode.js 23.6 and later run .ts files directly by replacing types with spaces, without checking them. That only works for TypeScript features that leave no JavaScript behind. Declaration merging and namespacesTypeScript merges some declarations that share a name. Two interface blocks become one, which is how libraries let you add fields to their types. A function and a namespace can merge too, giving a function with extra properties. isolatedDeclarations and fast declaration filesA library publishes .d.ts files that describe its exports. Normally only tsc can write them, because it must infer return types. Project references for big repositoriesIn a large repository with many packages, checking everything every time is slow. Project references split the code into projects that build separately and in order. tsc -b (build mode) checks only what changed since the last build."],[27,"comic","Setting up so everything finds everything","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Match module settings to the real runtime, mark type only imports, prefer subpath imports to deep relative paths, write erasable TypeScript so Node can run it, and split big repositories with project references."],[27,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🎭A Playwright suite Moved from ts-node to running specs with plain node and erasableSyntaxOnly, and replaced its enums with as const objects. 🧭A test utilities folder Replaced ../../../helpers imports in 300 files with #/helpers imports, so moving a test no longer breaks its imports. 🏗️A monorepo with 40 packages Uses project references and tsc -b in CI, so a change in one package re-checks only the packages that depend on it. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"TypeScript project setup: tsconfig, modules and builds\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 9Welcome This chapter sets up TypeScript projects: tsconfig, ES modules and resolution, subpath imports, erasable syntax, merging, isolated declarations and project references. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[28,"","What is new in TypeScript: 5.0 to 7.0","A release table from 5.0 to 7.0, const type parameters, using with an animated cleanup order, JSON import attributes, NoInfer and closure narrowing, inferred type predicates and regex checks, truthiness and never initialised checks, iterator helpers, import defer, 6.0 runtime types and deprecations, and the TypeScript 7 compiler with parallel checking. · TypeScript 5.0 · TypeScript 5.5 · TypeScript 6.0 · TypeScript 7.0 · using · Symbol.dispose · import attributes · NoInfer · inferred type predicates · Temporal · getOrInsert · RegExp.escape · native compiler · --checkers · what is new 🟨 JavaScript›🔷 TypeScript›Chapter 29 of 31 🆕 Step 7 · Topic 7 of 7 · Advanced"],[28,"top","","TypeScript changes quickly, and each release removes a little more friction. This chapter walks from TypeScript 5.0 to 7.0: const type parameters, using for automatic cleanup, import attributes, NoInfer, filters that narrow by themselves, regex and truthiness checks, the ES2025 and Temporal types of 6.0, and the native, parallel TypeScript 7 compiler, each checked by the real compiler and run on Node.js. ⏱️ About 22 min read📚 7 parts🎬 2 animations🔷 9 type checked examples🧪 1 real test run🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 7 calculations🧪 3 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Several of these features exist mainly to prevent the bugs test code is full of: leaked browsers, forgotten function calls in conditions, wrong regex patterns and null rows in filtered data. 1Three years of TypeScript in one page 2TypeScript 5.0: const type parameters 3TypeScript 5.2 and 5.3: using and import attributes 4TypeScript 5.4 and 5.5: smarter inference 5TypeScript 5.6 to 5.9: fewer silent mistakes 6TypeScript 6.0: the bridge release 7TypeScript 7.0: the native compiler Lesson 1 of 7"],[28,"overview","Three years of TypeScript in one page","TypeScript ships a new version every few months, and each brings small features that save real time. This chapter goes release by release from 5.0 (2023) to 7.0 (2026). Every example is checked by the TypeScript 7 compiler and run on Node.js 26. 🎬 Animated diagram"],[28,"overview","From TypeScript 5 to the native TypeScript 7","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 6 Version Headline features 5.0 standard decorators, const type parameters, extends with several configs, moduleResolution bundler, verbatimModuleSyntax 5.1 to 5.3 unrelated getter and setter types, using declarations, decorator metadata, import attributes 5.4 and 5.5 NoInfer, narrowing in closures, inferred type predicates, regex checking, isolatedDeclarations 5.6 and 5.7 always truthy checks, iterator helper types, never initialised variables, rewriteRelativeImportExtensions 5.8 and 5.9 erasableSyntaxOnly, require of ES modules under nodenext, import defer, module node20, a short tsc --init 6.0 new defaults, target es2025, Temporal and upsert types, #/ subpath imports, many deprecations 7.0 native compiler in Go, parallel checking, deprecated options removed Lesson 2 of 7"],[28,"ts50","TypeScript 5.0: const type parameters","Before 5.0, a generic function that should keep exact strings needed callers to write as const. A const type parameter does it for every caller. 5.0 also introduced standard decorators, covered in the projects chapter. const type parameters (5.0) · 🔷 tsc finds 1 error, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // TypeScript 5.0: a const type parameter infers literal types without \"as const\" at every callfunction roles<constTextendsreadonlystring[]>(list: T): T { return list; } function rolesOld<Textendsreadonlystring[]>(list: T): T { return list; } const exact = roles([\"admin\", \"qa\", \"viewer\"]); // readonly [\"admin\", \"qa\", \"viewer\"]const loose = rolesOld([\"admin\", \"qa\", \"viewer\"]); // string[]typeRole = (typeof exact)[number]; // \"admin\" | \"qa\" | \"viewer\"const me: Role = \"qa\"; console.log(exact, loose, me); const typo: Role = \"qaa\"; // caught, because the literals were kept Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(12,7): error TS2322: Type '\"qaa\"' is not assignable to type '\"admin\" | \"qa\" | \"viewer\"'. $ node src/main.ts [ 'admin', 'qa', 'viewer' ] [ 'admin', 'qa', 'viewer' ] qa Lesson 3 of 7"],[28,"ts52","TypeScript 5.2 and 5.3: using and import attributes","🧪 QA essentialBrowsers, pages and connections that are always closed, even when an assertion fails, stop leaks that slow down whole test runs. using is explicit resource management from a JavaScript proposal that TypeScript supports since 5.2 and Node.js runs natively. When the block ends, normally or by an error, the object's [Symbol.dispose]() runs. For test code that opens browsers, database connections or temp folders, this replaces fragile try and finally blocks. using and await using: cleanup that cannot be forgotten (5.2) · 🔷 tsc passes, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // TypeScript 5.2 and Node.js: using calls [Symbol.dispose]() when the block ends, even after an errorfunction openDb(name: string) { console.log(`open ${name}`); return { name, query: (sql: string) => `${name}: ${sql}`, [Symbol.dispose]() { console.log(`close ${name}`); } }; } asyncfunction startBrowser() { console.log(\"browser started\"); return { title: async () => \"Shop\", async [Symbol.asyncDispose]() { console.log(\"browser closed\"); } }; } function report() { using main = openDb(\"orders\"); using audit = openDb(\"audit\"); console.log(main.query(\"SELECT 1\")); thrownewError(\"assertion failed\"); // both connections still close, newest first } try { report(); } catch (e) { console.log(\"test failed:\", (e asError).message); } { awaitusing browser = await startBrowser(); // await using waits for an async cleanup console.log(\"title:\", await browser.title()); } console.log(\"done\"); Terminal $ npx tsc && node src/main.ts Terminal output tsc: no type errors $ node src/main.ts open orders open audit orders: SELECT 1 close audit close orders test failed: assertion failed browser started title: Shop browser closed done 🎬 Try it"],[28,"ts52","What using does when a test fails","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. function report() { using main = openDb(\"orders\"); using audit = openDb(\"audit\"); console.log(main.query(\"SELECT 1\")); thrownewError(\"assertion failed\"); } Running Open resources Cleanup queue Console Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 5 TypeScript 5.3 added import attributes. with { type: \"json\" } tells the runtime the file is JSON, which Node requires for JSON imports, and TypeScript infers the type of the data from the file. Import attributes for JSON test data (5.3) · 🔷 tsc finds 1 error 📄 src/main.ts // TypeScript 5.3: import attributes tell the runtime what kind of file it is loadingimport users from\"./users.json\"with { type: \"json\" }; const admins = users.filter((u) => u.role === \"admin\"); // the JSON's shape is inferred console.log(admins.map((u) => u.name)); console.log(users[0]?.email.toUpperCase()); console.log(users[0]?.phone); // no such field in the file 📄 src/users.json [ { \"name\": \"Asha\", \"email\": \"asha@shop.test\", \"role\": \"admin\" }, { \"name\": \"Ravi\", \"email\": \"ravi@shop.test\", \"role\": \"customer\" } ] Terminal $ npx tsc Terminal output src/main.ts(8,23): error TS2339: Property 'phone' does not exist on type '{ name: string; email: string; role: string; }'. Lesson 4 of 7"],[28,"ts54","TypeScript 5.4 and 5.5: smarter inference","🧪 QA essentialFiltering test data and writing locator or log patterns are daily work. These checks catch the mistakes early. NoInfer<T> marks an argument that must match T but must not help decide what T is. Without it, a wrong default such as \"safari\" would simply widen T to include it. 5.4 also keeps narrowing inside callbacks created after the last change to a variable. NoInfer and narrowing in closures (5.4) · 🔷 tsc finds 1 error, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // TypeScript 5.4: NoInfer<T> stops one argument from deciding what T isfunction pickBrowser<Textendsstring>(supported: T[], fallback: NoInfer<T>): T { const wanted = process.env.BROWSERasT | undefined; return wanted && supported.includes(wanted) ? wanted : fallback; } console.log(pickBrowser([\"chromium\", \"firefox\", \"webkit\"], \"chromium\")); // Narrowing now survives inside callbacks created after the last assignmentfunction label(id: string | number) { if (typeof id === \"number\") id = `#${id}`; return [\"a\", \"b\"].map((s) => `${s}-${id.toUpperCase()}`); // id is known to be a string here } console.log(label(7)); pickBrowser([\"chromium\", \"firefox\"], \"safari\"); // without NoInfer, T would quietly widen to include \"safari\" Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(15,38): error TS2345: Argument of type '\"safari\"' is not assignable to parameter of type '\"chromium\" | \"firefox\"'. $ node src/main.ts chromium [ 'a-#7', 'b-#7' ] TypeScript 5.5 infers type predicates: rows.filter(r => r != null) finally gives an array without null. It also checks regular expression literals, so typos in patterns are found before a test runs. Inferred type predicates (5.5) · 🔷 tsc passes, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // TypeScript 5.5: filter with a simple check now narrows the array type by itselfconst rows = [{ id: 1, total: 450 }, null, { id: 2, total: 99 }, undefined]; const present = rows.filter((r) => r != null); // { id: number; total: number }[] console.log(present.map((r) => r.total)); // no \"possibly null\" error any more// The inferred function is a real type predicate you can reuseconst isBig = (r: { total: number } | null) => r !== null && r.total > 100; const isRow = (r: { id: number; total: number } | null | undefined) => r != null; const checked = rows.filter(isRow); console.log(checked.length, isBig(rows[0] ?? null)); Terminal $ npx tsc && node src/main.ts Terminal output tsc: no type errors $ node src/main.ts [ 450, 99 ] 2 true Regular expressions are checked too (5.5) · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors 📄 src/main.ts // TypeScript 5.5 checks the syntax of regular expression literalsconst orderId = /^ORD-(?<num>\\d{6})$/; const match = \"ORD-104233\".match(orderId); console.log(match?.groups?.num); const broken = /^ORD-(?<num>\\d{6})\\k<numb>$/; // a back reference to a group that does not existconst flags = /cart/gg; // a flag written twice Terminal $ npx tsc Terminal output src/main.ts(6,38): error TS1532: There is no capturing group named 'numb' in this regular expression. src/main.ts(7,22): error TS1500: Duplicate regular expression flag. Lesson 5 of 7"],[28,"ts56","TypeScript 5.6 to 5.9: fewer silent mistakes","5.6 reports conditions that can never be false, such as a function used without calling it. This is a classic bug in test helpers: if (isLoggedIn) is always true. 5.6 also typed the iterator helper methods such as filter, map and toArray on iterators. Always truthy checks and iterator helpers (5.6) · 🔷 tsc finds 2 errors, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // TypeScript 5.6: conditions that are always true or always false are errorsfunction isLoggedIn(): boolean { returntrue; } if (isLoggedIn()) console.log(\"called: fine\"); if (isLoggedIn) console.log(\"forgot the ()\"); // a function is always truthyconst role = \"customer\"; if (/admin/) console.log(\"forgot .test(role), so this always runs for \" + role); // a regex literal is always truthy// TypeScript 5.6 also types the iterator helper methods that Node.js supportsconst fast = [120, 455, 80, 999, 300].values().filter((ms) => ms < 400).map((ms) => `${ms} ms`).toArray(); console.log(fast); Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(5,5): error TS2774: This condition will always return true since this function is always defined. Did you mean to call it instead? src/main.ts(8,5): error TS2872: This kind of expression is always truthy. $ node src/main.ts called: fine forgot the () forgot .test(role), so this always runs for customer [ '120 ms', '80 ms', '300 ms' ] 5.7 reports variables that are never assigned anywhere, even when they are used inside a function, where older versions stayed quiet. 5.8 added erasableSyntaxOnly for running .ts files directly, and 5.9 added import defer, which loads a module but runs it only when first used. Variables that are never initialised (5.7) · 🔷 tsc finds 1 error, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // TypeScript 5.7: a variable that is never assigned anywhere is now reported inside functions toolet total: number; function report() { return total.toFixed(2); } // before 5.7 this compiled, because it is inside a functionlet baseURL: string; function url(path: string) { return baseURL + path; } // fine: baseURL is assigned below before use baseURL = \"http://localhost:3000\"; console.log(url(\"/health\")); try { console.log(report()); } catch (e) { console.log(\"crashed:\", (e asError).message); } Terminal $ npx tsc $ node src/main.ts Terminal output src/main.ts(3,28): error TS2454: Variable 'total' is used before being assigned. $ node src/main.ts http://localhost:3000/health crashed: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'toFixed') import defer (5.9) · Needs a runtime that supports the import defer proposal, so it is not run here // TypeScript 5.9: import defer loads the module but runs it only on first useimport defer * as reports from\"./heavy-reports.ts\"; exportfunction onExportClick() { return reports.buildPdf(); // heavy-reports.ts runs here, the first time, not at startup } Lesson 6 of 7"],[28,"ts60","TypeScript 6.0: the bridge release","🧪 QA essentialTemporal ends date bugs in test data, and RegExp.escape makes locators built from user text safe. TypeScript 6.0 (March 2026) is the last version of the compiler written in JavaScript. It changes the defaults of a new project to strict: true, module: \"esnext\" and target: \"es2025\", and adds types for runtime features that Node.js and browsers already ship. Map.getOrInsert, RegExp.escape and Temporal (6.0) · 🔷 tsc passes, then Node.js runs it 📄 src/main.ts // TypeScript 6.0: types for ES2025 and newer runtime features that Node.js already hasconst hits = newMap<string, number>(); for (const word of [\"pass\", \"fail\", \"pass\", \"pass\"]) { hits.set(word, hits.getOrInsert(word, 0) + 1); // Map.getOrInsert (the \"upsert\" proposal) } console.log(hits); const userInput = \"price (in $)?\"; const safe = newRegExp(RegExp.escape(userInput)); // escape text before putting it in a regex console.log(safe.test(\"What is the price (in $)?\")); // The Temporal date and time API (esnext.temporal types)const release = Temporal.PlainDate.from(\"2026-03-23\"); const nextCheck = release.add({ months: 6 }); console.log(release.toString(), \"->\", nextCheck.toString(), release.until(nextCheck).total({ unit: \"days\" }), \"days\"); Terminal $ npx tsc && node src/main.ts Terminal output tsc: no type errors $ node src/main.ts Map(2) { 'pass' => 3, 'fail' => 1 } true 2026-03-23 -> 2026-09-23 184 days 6.0 also deprecates options that TypeScript 7 removes: target: \"es5\", downlevelIteration, moduleResolution node10 and classic, the amd, umd and systemjs module formats, baseUrl, outFile, and the old module Foo {} namespace syntax. \"ignoreDeprecations\": \"6.0\" silences the warnings while a project migrates. Lesson 7 of 7"],[28,"ts70","TypeScript 7.0: the native compiler","TypeScript 7.0 is the compiler ported to Go. The language is the same, but builds are typically 8 to 12 times faster, and the VS Code code base went from 125.7 seconds to 10.6 seconds. Checking can run on several CPU cores. TypeScript 7.0, the native compiler · 🧪 Tests run 📄 src/b.ts import { a } from\"./a.ts\"; exportconst b: string = a; Terminal $ npx tsc --version npx tsc -p . --checkers 2 npx tsc -p . --target es5 Terminal output Version 7.0.2 --- check with 2 parallel checkers --- src/b.ts(2,14): error TS2322: Type 'number' is not assignable to type 'string'. --- target es5 is gone --- tsconfig.json(3,15): error TS5108: Option 'target=ES5' has been removed. Please remove it from your configuration. New in 7.0 Use --checkers N split type checking across N workers --builders N build N referenced projects at the same time --singleThreaded turn parallelism off, for debugging or small CI machines Tools that call the compiler from JavaScript, such as some lint rules and code generators, have no stable API in 7.0 yet. The @typescript/typescript6 package keeps the 6.0 compiler available as tsc6 until 7.1. 🧮"],[28,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/7correct C1 Three resources are opened with using in one block. How many dispose calls run when the block ends? One per resource. Answer: 3 Each using declaration registers one cleanup. C2 Map.getOrInsert counts [\"pass\", \"fail\", \"pass\", \"pass\"]. What is the count for pass? Count the passes. Answer: 3 pass appears 3 times. C1 A build took 125.7 s and now takes 10.6 s. How many seconds are saved per build? Subtract. Answer: 115.1 125.7 − 10.6 = 115.1 s C2 CI runs 40 type checks a day, each saving 115.1 s. About how many minutes a day is that, to the nearest whole number? Multiply, then divide by 60. Answer: 77 40 × 115.1 = 4604 s 460460 ≈ 77 min C1 How many days are there from 2026-03-23 to 2026-09-23? Count month by month: 31 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 31. Answer: 184 8 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 31 + 23 = 184 Days left in March, then April to August, then 23 days of September. C2 A const type parameter keeps [\"admin\", \"qa\", \"viewer\"]. How many members does the Role union have? One per literal. Answer: 3 \"admin\" | \"qa\" | \"viewer\". C1 A 4 core CI machine checks a project in 24 s single threaded. With --checkers 4 and 75 percent efficiency, about how many seconds does it take? Ideal time divided by efficiency. Answer: 8 244 = 6 s ideal 60.75 = 8 s 🎯"],[28,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 What does using conn = openDb() guarantee? Explicit resource management. It calls conn[Symbol.dispose](). Q2 Which TypeScript version is the native compiler written in Go? The newest. 6.0 is the last JavaScript based compiler. Q3 What does with { type: \"json\" } on an import do? Import attributes. Node requires it for JSON imports in ES modules. Q1 What is the type of [1, null, 2].filter(x => x !== null) since TypeScript 5.5? Inferred type predicate. The arrow function is inferred as x is number. Q2 Why is if (isLoggedIn) an error in TypeScript 5.6? Always true. The fix is isLoggedIn(). Q3 What does NoInfer<T> on the fallback parameter prevent? Inference source. T is decided only by the supported list. Q4 In what order are two using resources disposed? Last in, first out. audit closed before orders in the example. Q1 What does \"ignoreDeprecations\": \"6.0\" do? Temporary. In 7.0 the deprecated options are errors and cannot be silenced. Q2 What does --checkers 4 do in TypeScript 7? Parallel checking. --builders does the same for referenced projects. Q3 What does RegExp.escape(text) return? Safe patterns. Use it before putting user text into new RegExp(). Q1 Which option was deprecated in 6.0 and removed in 7.0? Old output targets. Also baseUrl, outFile and moduleResolution node10. Q2 Why might a tool still need @typescript/typescript6? Programmatic API. Language services and code generators may call the compiler from JavaScript."],[28,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[28,"stack","A disposable stack","Write runWith(names) that \"opens\" each name in order by pushing \"open NAME\" to a log, then closes them in reverse order by pushing \"close NAME\", and returns the log. This is what several using lines do. Map to open lines, then loop over a reversed copy. function runWith(names) { const log = names.map(n => `open ${n}`); for (const n of [...names].reverse()) log.push(`close ${n}`); return log; } Cleanup in reverse order means later resources, which may depend on earlier ones, close first. 🌿 Medium"],[28,"upsert","Count with getOrInsert","Write countAll(words) that returns a plain object of counts. Use a Map with getOrInsert if you like, then convert it with Object.fromEntries. m.getOrInsert(w, 0) returns the old count or inserts 0. function countAll(words) { const m = newMap(); for (const w of words) m.set(w, m.getOrInsert(w, 0) + 1); returnObject.fromEntries(m); } Upsert removes the \"check, then set a default\" dance that often hides off by one bugs. 🔥 Hard"],[28,"escape","Find text safely","Write countMatches(haystack, needle) that counts how many times needle appears in haystack, treating it as plain text even if it contains characters such as (, $ or .. Use RegExp.escape. Build the regex from the escaped needle with the g flag. function countMatches(haystack, needle) { return (haystack.match(newRegExp(RegExp.escape(needle), \"g\")) || []).length; } Without escaping, \".\" matches every character and \"(in $)\" is not even valid. 🧠 Expert"],[28,"deprecated","Find options TypeScript 7 removed","Write removedOptions(compilerOptions) that returns the names of problems, in this order of checks: \"baseUrl\" if set, \"outFile\" if set, \"target es5\" if target is \"es5\" (any case), \"moduleResolution node10\" if it is \"node10\" or \"node\", and \"moduleResolution classic\" if it is \"classic\". One if per rule, in the given order. Lower case strings before comparing. function removedOptions(o) { const out = []; if (o.baseUrl !== undefined) out.push(\"baseUrl\"); if (o.outFile !== undefined) out.push(\"outFile\"); if (String(o.target).toLowerCase() === \"es5\") out.push(\"target es5\"); const mr = String(o.moduleResolution).toLowerCase(); if (mr === \"node10\" || mr === \"node\") out.push(\"moduleResolution node10\"); if (mr === \"classic\") out.push(\"moduleResolution classic\"); return out; } A small script like this can scan every tsconfig in a monorepo before the TypeScript 7 upgrade."],[28,"deprecated","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. Three years of TypeScript in one pageTypeScript ships a new version every few months, and each brings small features that save real time. This chapter goes release by release from 5.0 (2023) to 7.0 (2026). Every example is checked by the TypeScript 7 compiler and run on Node.js 26. TypeScript 5.0: const type parametersBefore 5.0, a generic function that should keep exact strings needed callers to write as const. A const type parameter does it for every caller. 5.0 also introduced standard decorators, covered in the projects chapter. TypeScript 5.2 and 5.3: using and import attributesusing is explicit resource management from a JavaScript proposal that TypeScript supports since 5.2 and Node.js runs natively. When the block ends, normally or by an error, the object's [Symbol.dispose]() runs. TypeScript 5.4 and 5.5: smarter inferenceNoInfer<T> marks an argument that must match T but must not help decide what T is. Without it, a wrong default such as \"safari\" would simply widen T to include it. 5.4 also keeps narrowing inside callbacks created after the last change to a variable. TypeScript 5.6 to 5.9: fewer silent mistakes5.6 reports conditions that can never be false, such as a function used without calling it. This is a classic bug in test helpers: if (isLoggedIn) is always true. 5.6 also typed the iterator helper methods such as filter, map and toArray on iterators. TypeScript 6.0: the bridge releaseTypeScript 6.0 (March 2026) is the last version of the compiler written in JavaScript. It changes the defaults of a new project to strict: true, module: \"esnext\" and target: \"es2025\", and adds types for runtime features that Node.js and browsers already ship. TypeScript 7.0: the native compilerTypeScript 7.0 is the compiler ported to Go. The language is the same, but builds are typically 8 to 12 times faster, and the VS Code code base went from 125.7 seconds to 10.6 seconds. Checking can run on several CPU cores."],[28,"comic","Small releases, big savings","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Use using for cleanup, const type parameters and NoInfer for precise generics, let filters narrow by themselves, fix 6.0 deprecation warnings, then enjoy the speed of TypeScript 7."],[28,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🎭A Playwright team Wraps every extra browser context in await using, so contexts close even when an expect fails, and the suite stopped running out of memory. 📅A booking test suite Replaced hand written date maths with Temporal, and the tests stopped failing on the last day of each month. ⚡A large monorepo Moved to TypeScript 7 after fixing 6.0 deprecations, and its CI type check dropped from minutes to seconds. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"What is new in TypeScript: 5.0 to 7.0\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 9Welcome This chapter covers what is new in TypeScript from 5.0 to 7.0, each feature checked by the real compiler and run on Node. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[29,"","Modern JavaScript: ES2020 to ES2027","The yearly release process and a table of editions, ES2020 to ES2023 features for safer access and copies, ES2024 and ES2025 grouping, iterator helpers, Set methods and safer regular expressions, the newest features such as using and await using, Error.isError and base64, Temporal for dates, ages, time zones and durations, feature detection and the proposals coming next, and modernising an old helper with a check that its results did not change. · ES2020 · ES2021 · ES2022 · ES2023 · ES2024 · ES2025 · ES2026 · ES2027 · DisposableStack · getOrInsert · feature detection · optional chaining · nullish coalescing · toSorted · groupBy · iterator helpers · RegExp.escape · Promise.try · using · Symbol.dispose · Temporal · latest JavaScript 🟨 JavaScript›🧠 Expert and latest›Chapter 30 of 31 🆕 Step 8 · Topic 1 of 2 · Expert"],[29,"top","","What every yearly edition added and why it matters: optional chaining and nullish coalescing, change by copy arrays, groupBy, iterator helpers and Set methods, RegExp.escape and Promise.try, the new using declarations for automatic clean up, Temporal for dates and time zones, the ES2026 edition with upsert and base64, DisposableStack, and how to check for the features that are finished but not shipped yet. ⏱️ About 22 min read📚 7 parts🎬 2 animations▶ 9 runnable examples🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 16 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 6 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Modern features make test code shorter and safer: defaults that respect 0, test data that is never changed by accident, and browsers and servers that always get closed. 1How JavaScript grows every year 2ES2020 to ES2023: safer access and copies 3ES2024 and ES2025: grouping, iterators, safer regexes 4ES2026 and ES2027: using, Error.isError, upsert and more 5Temporal: dates done right 6What comes next, and how to check before you use it 7Real world scenario: modernise safely Lesson 1 of 7"],[29,"yearly","How JavaScript grows every year","Every June, Ecma publishes a new edition of the standard: ES2020, ES2021 and so on. A feature joins only after it reaches stage 4 at TC39, which needs two shipping browser engines and a full test suite. Engines often ship features months before the edition that lists them, so \"can I use it\" depends on your browsers and Node.js version, not the year on the label. Edition Highlights you will meet in real code ES2015 (ES6) let and const, arrow functions, classes, modules, promises, template literals, destructuring, Map and Set ES2017 async and await, Object.entries, padStart ES2020 ?., ??, BigInt, Promise.allSettled, globalThis, dynamic import ES2021 ||=&&=??=, replaceAll, Promise.any, numeric separators, WeakRef ES2022 class fields and #private, top level await, .at(), Object.hasOwn, error cause ES2023 findLast, toSorted, toReversed, toSpliced, with ES2024 Object.groupBy, Map.groupBy, Promise.withResolvers, the v regex flag ES2025 iterator helpers, Set methods, RegExp.escape, Promise.try, import attributes, Float16Array ES2026 (June 2026) Error.isError, Array.fromAsync, Uint8Array base64 and hex, Map and WeakMap getOrInsert (upsert), Iterator.concat, JSON.parse source text and JSON.rawJSON, Math.sumPrecise Finished for ES2027 using and await using with DisposableStack, Temporal, Atomics.pause, iterator chunks and windows, Iterator.zip, iterator includes and join The \"Finished for ES2027\" row comes from TC39's list of finished proposals, checked in October 2026. Being finished means the design will not change, but a feature can still be missing from your Node.js or browser. The last part of this chapter shows how to check. Check support on caniuse.com or MDN's compatibility tables, and set your TypeScript target and lint rules to match the oldest browser or Node.js you support. Lesson 2 of 7"],[29,"es2020","ES2020 to ES2023: safer access and copies","🧪 QA essentialThese features appear in nearly every modern test file and page object. Optional chaining and nullish coalescing removed most of the \"cannot read properties of undefined\" crashes from everyday code. The ES2023 change by copy methods return a new array and leave the original alone, which makes test data safe to reuse. ES2020 and ES2021: ?., ??, BigInt and friends · ▶ Runs const order = { id: 7, customer: { name: \"Asha\", address: null }, discount: 0 }; // Optional chaining: stop at null or undefined instead of throwing console.log(order.customer?.address?.city); // undefined, no crash console.log(order.items?.[0], order.notify?.()); // works for indexes and calls too// Nullish coalescing: a default only for null and undefined, not for 0 or \"\" console.log(order.discount || 10, order.discount ?? 10); // BigInt for whole numbers beyond 2^53 console.log(2 ** 53 + 1, 2n ** 53n + 1n); // Logical assignment (ES2021)const settings = { retries: 0, browser: null }; settings.retries ||= 3; // 0 is falsy, so it becomes 3 settings.browser ??= \"chromium\"; console.log(settings); console.log(1_000_000 + 0.000_5, \"a-b-c\".replaceAll(\"-\", \"/\")); console.log(typeof globalThis); Output undefined undefined undefined 10 0 9007199254740992 9007199254740993n { retries: 3, browser: 'chromium' } 1000000.0005 a/b/c object ES2022 and ES2023: at, hasOwn, error cause, change by copy · ▶ Runs const runs = [{ id: 1, ok: true }, { id: 2, ok: false }, { id: 3, ok: true }, { id: 4, ok: false }]; console.log(runs.at(-1).id); // ES2022: count from the end console.log(Object.hasOwn(runs[0], \"ok\")); // a safer hasOwnPropertytry { try { JSON.parse(\"{bad\"); } catch (e) { thrownewError(\"Could not read the config\", { cause: e }); } // ES2022 } catch (e) { console.log(e.message, \"<-\", e.cause.name); } // ES2023: find from the end, and \"change by copy\" methods that never touch the original console.log(runs.findLast(r => !r.ok).id, runs.findLastIndex(r => r.ok)); const byIdDesc = runs.toSorted((a, b) => b.id - a.id); console.log(byIdDesc.map(r => r.id), runs.map(r => r.id)); console.log(runs.toReversed()[0].id, runs.with(0, { id: 99 })[0].id, runs.toSpliced(1, 2).length, runs.length); Output 4 true Could not read the config <- SyntaxError 4 2 [ 4, 3, 2, 1 ] [ 1, 2, 3, 4 ] 4 99 2 4 ?? against || matters in tests: a retry count of 0 or an empty search string are real values, and || silently replaces them with the default. Lesson 3 of 7"],[29,"es2024","ES2024 and ES2025: grouping, iterators, safer regexes","🧪 QA essentialgroupBy summarises test results in one line, and RegExp.escape makes text matching in tests safe. Grouping a list, a promise you resolve from outside, lazy iterator helpers, real set operations and a safe way to put user text into a regular expression all arrived in the last two editions. ES2024: groupBy, withResolvers, the v flag · ▶ Runs const tests = [ { name: \"login\", status: \"passed\", ms: 840 }, { name: \"search\", status: \"failed\", ms: 120 }, { name: \"checkout\", status: \"failed\", ms: 2310 }, { name: \"profile\", status: \"skipped\", ms: 0 }, ]; // Group a list into an object, or a Map, by any ruleconst byStatus = Object.groupBy(tests, t => t.status); console.log(Object.keys(byStatus), byStatus.failed.map(t => t.name)); const bySpeed = Map.groupBy(tests, t => (t.ms > 1000 ? \"slow\" : \"fast\")); console.log(bySpeed.get(\"slow\").map(t => t.name)); // A promise you can resolve from the outsideconst { promise, resolve } = Promise.withResolvers(); setTimeout(() => resolve(\"done\"), 10); console.log(await promise); // Well formed strings and the v flag for set operations in character classes console.log(\"ok\".isWellFormed(), \"bad \\uD800\".isWellFormed()); console.log(\"Héllo Wörld\".match(/[\\p{L}--[a-z]]/gv)); // letters that are not plain a to z Output [ 'passed', 'failed', 'skipped' ] [ 'search', 'checkout' ] [ 'checkout' ] done true false [ 'H', 'é', 'W', 'ö' ] Iterator helpers are lazy. Arrays do each step for every element before the next step starts. An iterator chain pulls one value at a time through all the steps, and stops the moment take has enough. That is why the example below can work on an endless list of test ids without running forever. 🎬 Try it"],[29,"es2024","Iterator helpers pull one value at a time","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. function* ids() { let n = 1; while (true) yield `TC-${n++}`; } ids().filter(id => id.endsWith(\"7\")) .take(2) .toArray(); toArray asks for Generator makes filter says Result Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 6 ES2025: iterator helpers, Set methods, RegExp.escape, Promise.try · ▶ Runs // Iterator helpers: lazy map, filter, take on any iterator (chapter 8)function* ids() { let n = 1; while (true) yield`TC-${n++}`; } console.log(ids().filter(id => id.endsWith(\"7\")).take(2).toArray()); // Set methods (chapter 8) console.log([...newSet([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]).difference(newSet([\"b\"]))]); // RegExp.escape: put user text into a pattern safelyconst userText = \"Price (incl. tax) $5.00?\"; console.log(RegExp.escape(userText)); console.log(newRegExp(RegExp.escape(userText)).test(`Total: ${userText}`)); // Promise.try: run a function that may throw or return a promise, and always get a promiseconst readConfig = () => { thrownewError(\"missing file\"); }; awaitPromise.try(readConfig).catch(e => console.log(\"caught:\", e.message)); // Duplicate named groups and inline modifiers in regular expressionsconst date = /(?<y>\\d{4})-(?<m>\\d\\d)|(?<m>\\d\\d)\\/(?<y>\\d{4})/; console.log(date.exec(\"2026-10\").groups.y, date.exec(\"10/2026\").groups.y); console.log(/^(?i:hello) World$/.test(\"HELLO World\"), /^(?i:hello) World$/.test(\"hello WORLD\")); // Float16Array and Math.f16round for compact numbers (machine learning, graphics) console.log(Math.f16round(1.337), newFloat16Array([1.337])[0]); Output [ 'TC-7', 'TC-17' ] [ 'a', 'c' ] \\x50rice\\x20\\(incl\\.\\x20tax\\)\\x20\\$5\\.00\\? true caught: missing file 2026 2026 true false 1.3369140625 1.3369140625 Import attributes for JSON · Runs in chapter 10 // ES2025: import attributes and JSON modulesimport config from\"./playwright.settings.json\"with { type: \"json\" }; const { default: users } = awaitimport(\"./users.json\", { with: { type: \"json\" } }); new RegExp(userText) breaks or misbehaves when the text contains ( ) . ? $. RegExp.escape fixes that, which matters for tests that search a page for product names or prices. Lesson 4 of 7"],[29,"es2026","ES2026 and ES2027: using, Error.isError, upsert and more","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightNode.jsusing makes sure browsers, servers and temp data are always cleaned up, even when a test fails. The June 2026 edition, ES2026, is a collection of small tools that remove common helper functions from real projects: Error.isError tells you whether a value is a real error, even one made in another frame or a vm context, where instanceof Error gives the wrong answer. Array.fromAsync collects an async iterable, such as pages of API results, into one array. Uint8Array base64 and hex methods replace the old btoa tricks and Buffer calls for tokens, file uploads and signatures. Upsert: map.getOrInsert(key, value) and getOrInsertComputed(key, fn) read a key or add it in one step, the classic cache pattern. Iterator.concat joins several iterables lazily, and JSON.parse source text access lets a reviver read the original text of a number, so long ids survive as BigInt. Math.sumPrecise adds a list of numbers without the rounding drift of a loop. It is in the standard but not yet in Node.js 26, so it appears in the last part. The headline feature for ES2027, already finished and already shipping in Node.js 24 and later, is explicit resource management. A variable declared with using is cleaned up automatically when its block ends, in reverse order, even when an error is thrown. It replaces long try and finally blocks around browsers, servers, files and connections. 🎬 Try it"],[29,"es2026","using: clean up in reverse order, even after an error","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. { using db = openDb(); using browser = openBrowser(); runTest(); // throws } // the error arrives here Running Open resources Log Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 6 ES2026 and using: Error.isError, fromAsync, base64, upsert and more · ▶ Runs // Explicit resource management: \"using\" calls Symbol.dispose when the block ends,// even if an error is thrown. Great for browsers, servers, temp files and DB connections.function openBrowser(name) { console.log(`open ${name}`); return { name, [Symbol.dispose]() { console.log(`close ${name}`); } }; } function runTest() { using browser = openBrowser(\"chromium\"); using other = openBrowser(\"firefox\"); console.log(`testing with ${browser.name} and ${other.name}`); thrownewError(\"assertion failed\"); } try { runTest(); } catch (e) { console.log(\"test failed:\", e.message); } // Async version: await using with Symbol.asyncDisposeasyncfunction startServer() { return { url: \"http://localhost:4173\", async [Symbol.asyncDispose]() { console.log(\"server stopped\"); } }; } { awaitusing server = await startServer(); console.log(\"server at\", server.url); } // Error.isError works across frames and realms, unlike instanceof console.log(Error.isError(newTypeError(\"x\")), Error.isError({ message: \"fake\" })); // Array.fromAsync collects an async iterableasyncfunction* pages() { yield [1, 2]; yield [3]; } console.log((awaitArray.fromAsync(pages())).flat()); // Base64 and hex built into Uint8Arrayconst bytes = newTextEncoder().encode(\"QA rocks\"); console.log(bytes.toBase64(), bytes.toHex().slice(0, 8), newTextDecoder().decode(Uint8Array.fromBase64(\"UUEgcm9ja3M=\"))); // Map.getOrInsertComputed, Iterator.concatconst cache = newMap(); console.log(cache.getOrInsertComputed(\"token\", () => \"t-123\"), cache.size); console.log(Iterator.concat([1, 2], newSet([3])).toArray()); // JSON.parse can read the original text of a number, so big ids survive console.log(JSON.parse('{\"orderId\": 90071992547409931}', (k, v, ctx) => (k === \"orderId\" ? BigInt(ctx.source) : v))); Output open chromium open firefox testing with chromium and firefox close firefox close chromium test failed: assertion failed server at http://localhost:4173 server stopped true false [ 1, 2, 3 ] UUEgcm9ja3M= 51412072 QA rocks t-123 1 [ 1, 2, 3 ] { orderId: 90071992547409931n } When the clean up steps are only known at run time, collect them in a DisposableStack. Each adopt or defer adds a step, and they all run in reverse order. If both the test and a clean up step fail, nothing is lost: the two errors are joined in a SuppressedError. DisposableStack and SuppressedError for test set up · ▶ Runs // DisposableStack: collect clean up steps as you go, run them all in reverse at the endfunction setUpSuite() { using stack = newDisposableStack(); const server = stack.adopt({ port: 4173 }, s => console.log(\"stop server on\", s.port)); stack.defer(() => console.log(\"delete temp folder\")); const user = stack.adopt({ id: 42 }, u => console.log(\"delete test user\", u.id)); console.log(\"suite ready:\", server.port, user.id); return stack.move(); // hand the clean up to the caller instead of running it now } const cleanup = setUpSuite(); console.log(\"tests run here\"); cleanup.dispose(); console.log(\"disposed:\", cleanup.disposed); // Errors in clean up are not lost: they are wrapped in a SuppressedErrortry { using a = { [Symbol.dispose]() { thrownewError(\"close failed\"); } }; thrownewError(\"test failed\"); } catch (e) { console.log(e.name, \"|\", e.error.message, \"| hid:\", e.suppressed.message); } Output suite ready: 4173 42 tests run here delete test user 42 delete temp folder stop server on 4173 disposed: true SuppressedError | close failed | hid: test failed These features ship in Node.js 24 and later and in current browsers, with TypeScript support for using. The page you are reading ran them on Node.js 26. If your users run older browsers, check support or use a transpiler. Lesson 5 of 7"],[29,"temporal","Temporal: dates done right","🧪 QA essentialDate and time zone edge cases are a classic source of bugs and of wrong expected values in tests. The old Date object mixes up time zones, months that start at 0 and objects that change in place. Temporal replaces it with clear types: PlainDate for a calendar date, PlainDateTime without a zone, ZonedDateTime with a zone, Instant for an exact moment and Duration for lengths of time. Every object is immutable. Temporal: dates and times that behave · ▶ Runs const { PlainDate, PlainDateTime, ZonedDateTime, Duration, Instant } = Temporal; // Dates without times or time zones: perfect for test data such as birthdays and due datesconst due = PlainDate.from(\"2026-01-31\"); console.log(due.add({ months: 1 }).toString()); // clamps to the end of February console.log(due.dayOfWeek, due.daysInMonth, due.inLeapYear); // Exact age check, for an \"18 or older\" ruleconst birthday = PlainDate.from(\"2008-10-21\"); const today = PlainDate.from(\"2026-10-20\"); console.log(\"age:\", birthday.until(today, { largestUnit: \"years\" }).years); // Time zones done right: the same meeting in Kolkata and Berlinconst meeting = ZonedDateTime.from(\"2026-03-29T10:00[Asia/Kolkata]\"); console.log(meeting.withTimeZone(\"Europe/Berlin\").toString()); // Durations you can add, compare and roundconst timeout = Duration.from({ minutes: 1, seconds: 30 }); console.log(timeout.total(\"milliseconds\"), Duration.compare(timeout, { seconds: 60 })); // Objects are immutable and comparisons are explicitconst a = PlainDateTime.from(\"2026-10-20T09:00\"); console.log(PlainDateTime.compare(a, a.add({ minutes: 5 })), a.toString()); console.log(Instant.from(\"2026-10-20T09:00:00Z\").epochMilliseconds); Output 2026-02-28 6 31 false age: 17 2026-03-29T06:30:00+02:00[Europe/Berlin] 90000 1 -1 2026-10-20T09:00:00 1792486800000 Date bugs are some of the most common bugs testers find: month ends (31 January plus one month), leap years, daylight saving changes, and users in other time zones. Temporal makes the expected values in your tests easy to compute correctly. Lesson 6 of 7"],[29,"next","What comes next, and how to check before you use it","🧪 QA essentialTest code runs on CI images, old browsers and devices, so checking support prevents \"works on my machine\" failures. A feature you read about may not exist where your code runs. Old phones, a company laptop with an old browser, or a CI image with an older Node.js all lag behind. Professionals do not guess from version numbers. They detect the feature at run time, or they let tools such as TypeScript's lib setting, ESLint and Babel check it at build time. Which of the newest features this Node.js has · ▶ Runs // Feature detection: ask the runtime instead of guessing from a version numberconst checks = { \"using (ES2027)\": () => { newFunction(\"{ using x = null; }\")(); returntrue; }, \"Temporal (ES2027)\": () => typeofTemporal === \"object\", \"Error.isError (ES2026)\": () => typeofError.isError === \"function\", \"Map getOrInsert (ES2026)\": () => typeofMap.prototype.getOrInsert === \"function\", \"Math.sumPrecise (ES2026)\": () => typeofMath.sumPrecise === \"function\", \"iterator chunks (ES2027)\": () => typeofIterator.prototype.chunks === \"function\", \"Iterator.zip (ES2027)\": () => typeofIterator.zip === \"function\", \"iterator join (ES2027)\": () => typeofIterator.prototype.join === \"function\", }; for (const [name, check] ofObject.entries(checks)) { let ok; try { ok = check(); } catch { ok = false; } console.log((ok ? \"yes \" : \"not yet \") + name); } console.log(\"loop sum:\", [0.1, 0.2, 0.3].reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0)); Output yes using (ES2027) yes Temporal (ES2027) yes Error.isError (ES2026) yes Map getOrInsert (ES2026) not yet Math.sumPrecise (ES2026) not yet iterator chunks (ES2027) not yet Iterator.zip (ES2027) not yet iterator join (ES2027) loop sum: 0.6000000000000001 The \"not yet\" lines are features that TC39 has finished but V8 in Node.js 26 has not shipped. When they arrive, they will remove more small helpers from real projects: splitting test ids into batches for parallel workers, pairing names with timings in a report, and adding up money without floating point drift. Coming next, shown with the results the proposals define · Not in Node.js 26 yet // Finished for ES2027 (iterator helpers) or ES2026 (sumPrecise), but not in Node.js 26 yet.const ids = [\"TC-1\", \"TC-2\", \"TC-3\", \"TC-4\", \"TC-5\"]; ids.values().chunks(2).toArray(); // [[\"TC-1\",\"TC-2\"], [\"TC-3\",\"TC-4\"], [\"TC-5\"]] batches for workers ids.values().windows(2).toArray(); // [[\"TC-1\",\"TC-2\"], [\"TC-2\",\"TC-3\"], ...] neighbours to compareconst names = [\"login\", \"cart\"], times = [812, 2310]; Iterator.zip([names, times]).toArray(); // [[\"login\", 812], [\"cart\", 2310]]Iterator.zipKeyed({ name: names, ms: times }).toArray(); // [{ name: \"login\", ms: 812 }, ...]newSet([\"chromium\", \"firefox\"]).values().join(\" | \"); // \"chromium | firefox\"newSet([\"chromium\", \"firefox\"]).values().includes(\"webkit\"); // falseMath.sumPrecise([0.1, 0.2, 0.3]); // 0.6, a plain loop gives 0.6000000000000001 Proposal Stage in October 2026 What it would give you Deferring module evaluation (import defer) 3 Load a module at start up but run its code only when you first use it, so apps start faster Source phase imports, import text 3 Import a WebAssembly module or a text file directly Decorators 2.7 Standard @decorator syntax. TypeScript already supports it (chapter 18) Math.clamp 2 Keep a number between a minimum and a maximum Follow new features on the TC39 proposals page on GitHub, MDN's browser compatibility tables and the V8 blog. A proposal at stage 3 is safe to learn, stage 4 is safe to plan for, and \"ships in every browser you support\" is safe to use without a fallback. Lesson 7 of 7"],[29,"scenario","Real world scenario: modernise safely","🧪 QA essential The situationAn old test helper written in 2016 is hard to read. You want to rewrite it with modern features without changing what it returns. Modernise an old helper and prove nothing changed · ▶ Runs // Old style helper from a 2016 test suitefunction oldSummary(results) { var copy = results.slice(); copy.sort(function (a, b) { return b.ms - a.ms; }); var groups = {}; for (var i = 0; i < results.length; i++) { var s = results[i].status; if (!groups.hasOwnProperty(s)) groups[s] = []; groups[s].push(results[i].name); } var slowest = copy.length > 0 ? copy[0].name : \"none\"; var owner = results.length && results[0].meta && results[0].meta.owner ? results[0].meta.owner : \"unknown\"; return { failed: groups.failed || [], slowest: slowest, owner: owner }; } // The same helper with modern featuresconst newSummary = results => ({ failed: Object.groupBy(results, r => r.status).failed?.map(r => r.name) ?? [], slowest: results.toSorted((a, b) => b.ms - a.ms)[0]?.name ?? \"none\", owner: results[0]?.meta?.owner ?? \"unknown\", }); const runs = [ { name: \"login\", status: \"passed\", ms: 840, meta: { owner: \"team-auth\" } }, { name: \"checkout\", status: \"failed\", ms: 2310 }, { name: \"search\", status: \"failed\", ms: 120 }, ]; for (const input of [runs, []]) { const before = oldSummary(input), after = newSummary(input); console.log(JSON.stringify(after), \"| same as old:\", JSON.stringify(before) === JSON.stringify(after)); } Output {\"failed\":[\"checkout\",\"search\"],\"slowest\":\"checkout\",\"owner\":\"team-auth\"} | same as old: true {\"failed\":[],\"slowest\":\"none\",\"owner\":\"unknown\"} | same as old: true The rewrite is a third of the size. Running both versions on the same inputs, including an empty list, and comparing the results is a simple form of characterisation testing: it proves the behaviour stayed the same before you delete the old code. 🧮"],[29,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 What is [4, 8, 15, 16, 23, 42].at(-2)? Second from the end. Answer: 23 The last is 42, the one before is 23. C2 How many groups does Object.groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], n => n % 3) create? Remainders. Answer: 3 1 and 4 give 1, 2 and 5 give 2, 3 gives 0. Three keys: 0, 1 and 2. C1 What is Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER, written as a power of 2 minus 1? Give the exponent. Doubles have 53 bits of precision. Answer: 53 253 − 1 = 9,007,199,254,740,991 C2 Someone born on 2008-10-21. How old are they on 2026-10-20, in whole years? One day short of the birthday. Answer: 17 On 2026-10-21 they turn 18. One day earlier they are still 17. C1 A Duration of 1 minute 30 seconds is how many milliseconds? Seconds times 1000. Answer: 90000 90 × 1000 = 90000 C2 10:00 in Kolkata (UTC+5:30) on 29 March 2026 is what hour in Berlin, which moves to UTC+2 that day? Go through UTC. Answer: 6:30 10:00 minus 5:30 is 04:30 UTC. 04:30 plus 2:00 is 06:30 in Berlin. C1 How many editions from ES2015 to ES2025, counting both? One per year. Answer: 11 2025 − 2015 + 1 = 11 C2 The modern summary helper has 5 lines of body, the old one 13. By what percent is it shorter, rounded? (old minus new) over old. Answer: 62 (13 − 5) ÷ 13 × 100 ≈ 61.5 Rounded: 62. 🎯"],[29,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/16correct Q1 What is 0 ?? 5? Only null and undefined trigger the default. 0 is a real value, so ?? keeps it. 0 || 5 would give 5. Q2 What does user?.address?.city give when address is null? It stops early. Optional chaining returns undefined as soon as it meets null or undefined. Q3 Which method sorts without changing the original array? Change by copy. toSorted returns a new sorted array. Q1 What does [10, 20, 30].at(-1) return? Negative counts from the end. .at(-1) is the last element. Q2 What does Object.groupBy(list, fn) return? Map.groupBy returns a Map. Each key holds the items that produced it. Q3 Why use RegExp.escape(text) before new RegExp(text)? Special characters. Without escaping, user text can change the meaning of the pattern. Q4 Which edition added Error.isError, Array.fromAsync and Map getOrInsert? The June 2026 edition. They reached stage 4 in time for ES2026. using and Temporal finished later and are listed for ES2027. Q1 Two resources are declared with using in the same block. In which order are they disposed? Like a stack. Later resources may depend on earlier ones, so they close first. Q2 PlainDate.from(\"2026-01-31\").add({ months: 1 }) gives? February is short. Temporal clamps to the last valid day by default. Q3 Which is true about Temporal objects? No surprise changes. Every Temporal method returns a new value. Q4 What does new Map().getOrInsertComputed(\"token\", () => \"t-1\") return the first time? Read or add in one step. The callback runs only when the key is missing, which makes it the standard cache pattern. Q5 Both the test and a dispose step throw inside a using block. What does the catch block receive? Neither error is lost. e.error is the newer error from clean up and e.suppressed is the one it hid. Q1 Why can JSON.parse lose digits of a large id like 90071992547409931? Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER. A reviver with the source text can turn it into a BigInt instead. Q2 Why is Error.isError(x) better than x instanceof Error in some cases? iframes and vm contexts. Each realm has its own Error constructor, which breaks instanceof. Q3 Why prefer feature detection over checking the Node.js or browser version? Ask, do not guess. Engines ship features at different times, and flags or polyfills can add or remove them. Q4 What does settings.retries ||= 3 do when retries is 0? Logical OR assignment. Use ??= if 0 is a valid value you want to keep."],[29,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[29,"safe","Safe access with defaults","Write shippingCity(order) that returns order.customer.address.city, or \"unknown\" if any part is missing. An empty string city should be kept as it is. ?. at every step and ?? for the default. function shippingCity(order) { return order?.customer?.address?.city ?? \"unknown\"; } With ||, the empty string would wrongly become \"unknown\". 🌿 Medium"],[29,"copy","Sort without side effects","Write topSlow(results, n) that returns the names of the n slowest results (largest ms first) without changing the results array. toSorted instead of sort. function topSlow(results, n) { return results.toSorted((a, b) => b.ms - a.ms).slice(0, n).map(r => r.name); } The second half of the test checks the original order, which sort would have broken. 🔥 Hard"],[29,"group","Write groupBy yourself","Write groupBy(list, keyFn) that works like Object.groupBy: return an object whose keys are the results of keyFn(item, index) and whose values are arrays of items in their original order. (out[key] ??= []).push(item) creates the array the first time. function groupBy(list, keyFn) { const out = {}; list.forEach((item, i) => { (out[keyFn(item, i)] ??= []).push(item); }); return out; } The built in version returns an object with no prototype, so keys like \"constructor\" are safe too. 🧠 Expert"],[29,"dispose","Clean up like using","Write async withResources(openers, fn). openers is a list of async functions, each returning a resource with an async close(). Open them in order, call fn(resources), and always close every opened resource in reverse order, even when fn or a later opener throws. Return what fn returns, or rethrow its error. Keep a list of what actually opened, and close it in a finally block. asyncfunction withResources(openers, fn) { const opened = []; try { for (const open of openers) opened.push(await open()); returnawait fn(opened); } finally { for (const r of opened.toReversed()) await r.close(); } } This is exactly what await using does for you, and why it closes only resources that were successfully opened."],[29,"dispose","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. How JavaScript grows every yearEvery June, Ecma publishes a new edition of the standard: ES2020, ES2021 and so on. A feature joins only after it reaches stage 4 at TC39, which needs two shipping browser engines and a full test suite. ES2020 to ES2023: safer access and copiesOptional chaining and nullish coalescing removed most of the \"cannot read properties of undefined\" crashes from everyday code. The ES2023 change by copy methods return a new array and leave the original alone, which makes test data safe to reuse. ES2024 and ES2025: grouping, iterators, safer regexesGrouping a list, a promise you resolve from outside, lazy iterator helpers, real set operations and a safe way to put user text into a regular expression all arrived in the last two editions. ES2026 and ES2027: using, Error.isError, upsert and moreThe June 2026 edition, ES2026, is a collection of small tools that remove common helper functions from real projects: Temporal: dates done rightThe old Date object mixes up time zones, months that start at 0 and objects that change in place. What comes next, and how to check before you use itA feature you read about may not exist where your code runs. Old phones, a company laptop with an old browser, or a CI image with an older Node.js all lag behind. Professionals do not guess from version numbers. Real world scenario: modernise safelyThe rewrite is a third of the size. Running both versions on the same inputs, including an empty list, and comparing the results is a simple form of characterisation testing: it proves the behaviour stayed the same before you delete the old code."],[29,"comic","One small release every year","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Know which edition a feature came from, check that your browsers and Node.js support it, and prefer the safer modern form."],[29,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🌍A travel site Moved its booking calendar to Temporal and fixed a long standing bug where flights shown in the user's time zone jumped a day around daylight saving changes. 🧪A test framework Wraps its browser, server and database fixtures with Symbol.asyncDispose so a failing test can never leave a browser running on a CI machine. 🧾A billing service Uses JSON.parse with source text access so 18 digit invoice ids no longer lose their last digits. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Modern JavaScript: ES2020 to ES2027\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 9Welcome This chapter walks through what each edition from ES2020 to ES2026 added, the features finished for ES2027 such as using and Temporal, and how to check what your runtime supports. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[30,"","Expert JavaScript: performance, security and test quality","Counting steps and benchmarking with a warm up and median, listener leaks and weak references, memoize, pipe, strategy, observer, builder and factory patterns, worker threads and Web Workers, XSS and prototype pollution with a security checklist, property based testing, mutation testing and order dependent flaky tests, and balancing CI shards by duration. · performance · Big O · benchmark · memory leak · WeakMap · design patterns · test data builder · worker threads · Web Workers · XSS · prototype pollution · security testing · property based testing · mutation testing · flaky tests · CI sharding · expert JavaScript 🟨 JavaScript›🧠 Expert and latest›Chapter 31 of 31 🏆 Step 8 · Topic 2 of 2 · Expert"],[30,"top","","The habits that separate experts: choosing the right algorithm and measuring it fairly, finding memory leaks, design patterns for apps and tests, worker threads, XSS and prototype pollution, property based and mutation testing, flaky tests, and a faster CI pipeline. ⏱️ About 20 min read📚 7 parts🎬 2 animations▶ 12 runnable examples🧪 1 real test run🏋️ 4 hands-on exercises🎯 12 quiz questions🧮 8 calculations🧪 7 QA essential partsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 🧪 For QA engineers Senior testers judge the tests as well as the product: they check performance and memory, try security payloads, measure test quality with mutants, hunt flaky tests and keep the pipeline fast. 1Performance: count first, then measure 2Memory: leaks and weak references 3Patterns experts reach for 4Workers: real parallel work 5Security every JavaScript developer should test 6Test quality: properties, mutants and flaky tests 7Real world scenario: a faster CI pipeline Lesson 1 of 7"],[30,"performance","Performance: count first, then measure","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightLighthousePerformance checks in CI catch slow pages and slow APIs before users do. Most slow JavaScript is slow because of the algorithm, not the engine. A loop inside a loop over the same list does about n² ÷ 2 steps, while a Set lookup does about n. Count steps to compare ideas, and measure time only to confirm. O(n²) against O(n): count the steps · ▶ Runs // Count the work instead of the time: the count does not depend on the machinefunction hasDuplicateNested(ids) { let steps = 0; for (let i = 0; i < ids.length; i++) for (let j = i + 1; j < ids.length; j++) { steps++; if (ids[i] === ids[j]) return { found: true, steps }; } return { found: false, steps }; } function hasDuplicateSet(ids) { let steps = 0; const seen = newSet(); for (const id of ids) { steps++; if (seen.has(id)) return { found: true, steps }; seen.add(id); } return { found: false, steps }; } for (const n of [10, 100, 1000, 10000]) { const ids = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => `ORD-${i}`); // no duplicates: the worst case console.log(`n=${n}`.padEnd(8), \"nested:\", String(hasDuplicateNested(ids).steps).padStart(9), \" set:\", hasDuplicateSet(ids).steps); } Output n=10 nested: 45 set: 10 n=100 nested: 4950 set: 100 n=1000 nested: 499500 set: 1000 n=10000 nested: 49995000 set: 10000 When you do measure, warm the code up first so the JIT compiler can optimise it, run it several times and take the median. One run on a laptop with a browser open is noise. Measure with performance.now, a warm up and the median · ▶ Runs const ids = Array.from({ length: 5000 }, (_, i) => `SKU-${i}`); const wanted = Array.from({ length: 5000 }, (_, i) => `SKU-${i * 2}`); const idSet = newSet(ids); function bench(name, fn, rounds = 5) { fn(); // warm up so the JIT compiler can optimiseconst times = []; for (let r = 0; r < rounds; r++) { const start = performance.now(); fn(); times.push(performance.now() - start); } const median = times.toSorted((a, b) => a - b)[Math.floor(rounds / 2)]; console.log(name.padEnd(14), `${median.toFixed(2)} ms`); // times depend on the machine, so this page shows x.xxreturn median; } const slow = bench(\"array.includes\", () => wanted.filter(id => ids.includes(id)).length); const fast = bench(\"set.has\", () => wanted.filter(id => idSet.has(id)).length); console.log(\"Set is at least 10 times faster:\", slow / fast > 10); Output array.includes x.xx ms set.has x.xx ms Set is at least 10 times faster: true In the browser, the Performance panel of DevTools shows long tasks, layout work and memory over time. Lighthouse and the Core Web Vitals (LCP, INP and CLS) are what users actually feel, and Playwright can read them with page.evaluate during a test. Lesson 2 of 7"],[30,"memory","Memory: leaks and weak references","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightLeaks show up in long test runs and soak tests, and testers are often the first to see them. JavaScript frees memory that nothing can reach any more. A leak happens when something you forgot still points at old data: a listener that was never removed, a cache that only grows, or a timer that keeps running. In a long test run or a single page app that stays open all day, small leaks add up. A listener leak and the AbortSignal fix · ▶ Runs // A page object that adds a listener every time it is created and never removes itconst bus = newEventTarget(); let count = 0; const listeners = () => count; classLeakyBanner { constructor() { bus.addEventListener(\"cart\", () => { this.items = (this.items ?? 0) + 1; count++; }); } } for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) newLeakyBanner(); bus.dispatchEvent(newEvent(\"cart\")); console.log(\"leaky: one event ran\", listeners(), \"handlers\"); // The fix: tie every listener to an AbortSignal and abort it when the object is doneclassBanner { #controller = newAbortController(); constructor() { bus.addEventListener(\"cart\", () => { this.items = (this.items ?? 0) + 1; count++; }, { signal: this.#controller.signal }); } destroy() { this.#controller.abort(); } } count = 0; const banner = newBanner(); banner.destroy(); bus.dispatchEvent(newEvent(\"cart\")); console.log(\"fixed: after destroy, the event still reaches\", listeners() - 3, \"new handlers\"); Output leaky: one event ran 3 handlers fixed: after destroy, the event still reaches 0 new handlers WeakMap, WeakSet and WeakRef hold objects without keeping them alive. Use them to attach extra data to objects you do not own, such as DOM nodes. WeakMap and WeakRef: memory that cleans itself · ▶ Runs // WeakMap keys do not keep objects alive, so extra data dies with the objectconst meta = newWeakMap(); let element = { id: \"checkout-button\" }; meta.set(element, { clicks: 3 }); const ref = newWeakRef(element); element = null; // drop the only strong referenceawaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, 0)); // let the current job finish globalThis.gc(); // force garbage collection (needs --expose-gc) console.log(\"after gc:\", ref.deref()); // A plain Map would keep the object alive for as long as the Map livesconst cache = newMap(); let page = { url: \"/cart\" }; cache.set(page, \"rendered\"); page = null; awaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, 0)); globalThis.gc(); console.log(\"Map still holds:\", [...cache.keys()]); Terminal $ node --expose-gc main.mjs Output after gc: undefined Map still holds: [ { url: '/cart' } ] To test for a leak, repeat an action many times (open and close a dialog 50 times), force garbage collection and compare heap snapshots. Playwright can open a CDP session in Chromium and call HeapProfiler.collectGarbage and Performance.getMetrics. Lesson 3 of 7"],[30,"patterns","Patterns experts reach for","🧪 QA essentialBuilders, factories and page objects make large test suites readable and safe to run in parallel. A pattern is a known shape for a common problem. These four appear in application code and in test frameworks alike. Memoize, pipe, strategy and observer · ▶ Runs // Memoize: cache the result of a pure functionfunction memoize(fn) { const cache = newMap(); return (...args) => cache.getOrInsertComputed(JSON.stringify(args), () => fn(...args)); } let calls = 0; const shippingCost = memoize((pin, kg) => { calls++; return kg * 40 + (pin.startsWith(\"56\") ? 0 : 60); }); console.log(shippingCost(\"560001\", 2), shippingCost(\"560001\", 2), shippingCost(\"110001\", 1), \"calls:\", calls); // Pipe: build a function out of small stepsconst pipe = (...steps) => input => steps.reduce((value, step) => step(value), input); const slug = pipe(s => s.trim(), s => s.toLowerCase(), s => s.replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, \"-\"), s => s.replace(/^-|-$/g, \"\")); console.log(slug(\" Summer Sale: 50% OFF! \")); // Strategy: swap behaviour without if and else chainsconst discounts = { none: total => total, percent10: total => total * 0.9, flat100: total => Math.max(0, total - 100), }; for (const name ofObject.keys(discounts)) console.log(name.padEnd(9), discounts[name](800)); // Observer: subscribers are told about changesfunction createStore(state) { const subscribers = newSet(); return { get: () => state, set(patch) { state = { ...state, ...patch }; subscribers.forEach(fn => fn(state)); }, subscribe(fn) { subscribers.add(fn); return () => subscribers.delete(fn); }, }; } const store = createStore({ items: 0 }); const stop = store.subscribe(s => console.log(\"badge shows\", s.items)); store.set({ items: 1 }); stop(); store.set({ items: 2 }); console.log(\"final state\", store.get()); Output 80 80 100 calls: 2 summer-sale-50-off none 800 percent10 720 flat100 700 badge shows 1 final state { items: 2 } Debounce and throttle are the two patterns behind every search box, autosave and scroll handler. Debounce waits for a pause, throttle allows at most one call per time window. The animation shows debounce on a search box. 🎬 Try it"],[30,"patterns","Debounce: wait until the typing stops","Press Play, or step with the arrows, and watch each box change. const search = debounce(q => fetchResults(q), 300); input.addEventListener(\"input\", e => search(e.target.value)); Time Key typed Timer Request sent Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 4 For tests, the two most useful patterns are the page object from chapter 9 and the test data builder. A builder gives every field a valid default, so each test states only what it is about. Builder and factory for test data · ▶ Runs // Test data builder: sensible defaults, change only what the test is aboutclassUserBuilder { #user = { name: \"Test User\", email: \"test.user@example.test\", role: \"customer\", verified: true, country: \"IN\" }; static aUser() { returnnewUserBuilder(); } withRole(role) { this.#user.role = role; returnthis; } unverified() { this.#user.verified = false; returnthis; } from(country) { this.#user.country = country; returnthis; } build() { return structuredClone(this.#user); } } const admin = UserBuilder.aUser().withRole(\"admin\").build(); const newUser = UserBuilder.aUser().unverified().from(\"DE\").build(); console.log(admin); console.log(newUser); // Factory with a counter so every record is unique, which keeps parallel tests apartlet seq = 0; const makeOrder = (overrides = {}) => ({ id: `ORD-${++seq}`, items: [{ sku: \"PEN\", qty: 1 }], status: \"new\", ...overrides }); console.log(makeOrder(), makeOrder({ status: \"paid\" }).id); Output { name: 'Test User', email: 'test.user@example.test', role: 'admin', verified: true, country: 'IN' } { name: 'Test User', email: 'test.user@example.test', role: 'customer', verified: false, country: 'DE' } { id: 'ORD-1', items: [ { sku: 'PEN', qty: 1 } ], status: 'new' } ORD-2 Unique values from a counter or crypto.randomUUID() keep tests that run in parallel from clashing over the same email or order id. Lesson 4 of 7"],[30,"workers","Workers: real parallel work","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightJestVitestTest runners use workers to run files in parallel, which is why tests must not share state. JavaScript runs your code on one thread, so a long calculation blocks everything else. Worker threads in Node.js and Web Workers in the browser run code on separate threads and talk to the main thread with messages. Split heavy work across worker threads · ▶ Runs import { Worker } from\"node:worker_threads\"; // The worker counts primes in its own range on its own threadconst workerCode = ` const { parentPort, workerData } = require(\"node:worker_threads\"); const { from, to } = workerData; let count = 0; for (let n = Math.max(2, from); n < to; n++) { let prime = true; for (let d = 2; d * d <= n; d++) if (n % d === 0) { prime = false; break; } if (prime) count++; } parentPort.postMessage({ from, to, count }); `; const runWorker = range => newPromise((resolve, reject) => { const worker = newWorker(workerCode, { eval: true, workerData: range }); worker.once(\"message\", resolve); worker.once(\"error\", reject); }); const size = 500_000, parts = 4; const ranges = Array.from({ length: parts }, (_, i) => ({ from: i * size / parts, to: (i + 1) * size / parts })); const results = awaitPromise.all(ranges.map(runWorker)); for (const r of results) console.log(`${r.from}..${r.to}: ${r.count} primes`); console.log(\"total:\", results.reduce((sum, r) => sum + r.count, 0)); Output 0..125000: 11734 primes 125000..250000: 10310 primes 250000..375000: 9860 primes 375000..500000: 9634 primes total: 41538 The two threads share no variables. Everything goes through messages, and each message is a copy made with the structured clone algorithm, the same one structuredClone() uses. Big binary data can be transferred instead of copied, and a SharedArrayBuffer can be shared on purpose (chapter 8). 🎬 Animated diagram"],[30,"workers","The main thread and a worker talk with messages","Press Play, or step with the arrows, to follow the story one stage at a time. ↔ Swipe sideways to see the whole diagram This is the whole picture. Press ▶ Play to walk through it step by step. 0 / 4 Web Workers in the browser · Runs in a browser // main.js in the browser: keep the page responsive while a big CSV is parsedconst worker = newWorker(newURL(\"./parse-worker.js\", import.meta.url), { type: \"module\" }); worker.postMessage(file); // file from an <input type=\"file\"> worker.onmessage = ({ data }) => renderTable(data.rows); // parse-worker.js self.onmessage = async ({ data: file }) => { const text = await file.text(); const rows = text.split(\"\\n\").map(line => line.split(\",\")); self.postMessage({ rows }); }; Use workers for heavy CPU work such as parsing, hashing, image work or big calculations. For waiting on the network or disk, plain async code is already enough. Test runners use the same idea: Jest, Vitest and Playwright run test files in parallel workers. Lesson 5 of 7"],[30,"security","Security every JavaScript developer should test","🧪 QA essentialSecurity checks such as XSS payloads and auth rules belong in every regression suite. Cross site scripting (XSS) happens when user text is turned into HTML. The safe defaults are textContent, framework templates that escape by default, and a Content Security Policy header that blocks inline scripts. Cross site scripting: innerHTML against textContent · ▶ Runs in the browser 📄 page.html <h2>Reviews</h2> <divid=\"unsafe\"></div> <divid=\"safe\"></div> 📄 script.js // A review typed by a user, coming back from the APIconst review = 'Great pen <img src=\"x\" onerror=\"alert(document.cookie)\">'; // Unsafe: innerHTML turns the text into real elements, including the attacker's handler document.querySelector(\"#unsafe\").innerHTML = review; console.log(\"unsafe has an onerror image:\", document.querySelector(\"#unsafe img[onerror]\") !== null); // Safe: textContent shows the text exactly as typed document.querySelector(\"#safe\").textContent = review; console.log(\"safe has an onerror image:\", document.querySelector(\"#safe img\") !== null); console.log(\"safe shows:\", document.querySelector(\"#safe\").textContent); // A QA check you can run on any page: no inline handlers in user contentconst risky = [...document.querySelectorAll(\"[onerror], [onload], [onclick]\")].map(el => el.closest(\"[id]\").id); console.log(\"risky containers:\", risky); Output unsafe has an onerror image: true safe has an onerror image: false safe shows: Great pen <img src=\"x\" onerror=\"alert(document.cookie)\"> risky containers: [ 'unsafe' ] Prototype pollution happens when code copies keys from untrusted JSON into objects. The key __proto__ reaches the shared prototype and changes every object in the program. Prototype pollution and how to block it · ▶ Runs // A naive deep merge, often written for config files and API bodiesfunction merge(target, source) { for (const key in source) { if (typeof source[key] === \"object\" && source[key] !== null) { target[key] ??= {}; merge(target[key], source[key]); } else target[key] = source[key]; } return target; } const body = JSON.parse('{\"theme\": \"dark\", \"__proto__\": {\"isAdmin\": true}}'); // sent by an attacker merge({}, body); console.log(\"every object is now admin:\", ({}).isAdmin); deleteObject.prototype.isAdmin; // undo for the rest of the demo// The fix: skip dangerous keys, or use objects without a prototypeconstBLOCKED = newSet([\"__proto__\", \"constructor\", \"prototype\"]); function safeMerge(target, source) { for (const key ofObject.keys(source)) { if (BLOCKED.has(key)) continue; const value = source[key]; target[key] = typeof value === \"object\" && value !== null ? safeMerge(target[key] ?? Object.create(null), value) : value; } return target; } const settings = safeMerge({}, body); console.log(\"after safeMerge:\", ({}).isAdmin, settings.theme); Output every object is now admin: true after safeMerge: undefined dark Risk What to check XSS Type <img src=x onerror=alert(1)> into every field and check it shows as text Secrets No API keys in the browser bundle or in git. Read them from environment variables eval No eval or new Function on user input Dependencies Run npm audit, keep a lock file and use npm ci in CI Auth Every API checks the token and the role, not just the UI (chapter 21) Cookies Session cookies use HttpOnly, Secure and SameSite Lesson 6 of 7"],[30,"quality","Test quality: properties, mutants and flaky tests","🧪 QA essentialThese techniques measure how good a test suite really is, which is the core skill of a senior tester. Example based tests check the inputs you thought of. Property based testing generates hundreds of inputs and checks a rule that must always hold. Libraries such as fast-check also shrink a failure to the smallest input. The seed makes every failure repeatable. Property based testing from scratch · ▶ Runs // A tiny property based tester: random inputs from a fixed seed, so a failure can be replayedfunction mulberry32(seed) { return () => { seed = (seed + 0x6d2b79f5) | 0; let t = Math.imul(seed ^ (seed >>> 15), 1 | seed); t = (t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), 61 | t)) ^ t; return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296; }; } function check(name, gen, property, { runs = 200, seed = 42 } = {}) { const rand = mulberry32(seed); for (let i = 0; i < runs; i++) { const input = gen(rand); if (!property(input)) return console.log(`✗ ${name} failed on run ${i + 1} with`, input); } console.log(`✓ ${name} held for ${runs} inputs`); } // The code under test: split a bill into n shares in paise, so no money is lostconst splitBuggy = (paise, n) => Array(n).fill(Math.round(paise / n)); const splitFixed = (paise, n) => Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => Math.floor(paise / n) + (i < paise % n ? 1 : 0)); const bill = rand => ({ paise: Math.floor(rand() * 100000), n: 1 + Math.floor(rand() * 6) }); const sumIsKept = split => ({ paise, n }) => split(paise, n).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) === paise; const fair = ({ paise, n }) => { const s = splitFixed(paise, n); returnMath.max(...s) - Math.min(...s) <= 1; }; check(\"buggy split keeps the total\", bill, sumIsKept(splitBuggy)); check(\"fixed split keeps the total\", bill, sumIsKept(splitFixed)); check(\"fixed split is fair\", bill, fair); Output ✗ buggy split keeps the total failed on run 1 with { paise: 60110, n: 3 } ✓ fixed split keeps the total held for 200 inputs ✓ fixed split is fair held for 200 inputs Coverage tells you which lines ran, not whether a test would notice a bug. Mutation testing changes the code on purpose and checks that some test fails. Stryker does this for JavaScript and TypeScript projects. Mutation testing: do the tests notice a change · ▶ Runs // The code under testconst source = \"total => total >= 499\"; // Mutants: small changes a careless edit could makeconst mutants = [ [\"original\", source], [\">= to >\", source.replace(\">=\", \">\")], [\">= to <=\", source.replace(\">=\", \"<=\")], [\"499 to 500\", source.replace(\"499\", \"500\")], [\"499 to 498\", source.replace(\"499\", \"498\")], ]; // Two test suites for freeShipping(total)const weakSuite = [[1000, true], [10, false]]; const strongSuite = [[1000, true], [10, false], [499, true], [498.99, false]]; function score(suite) { let killed = 0; for (const [name, code] of mutants.slice(1)) { const fn = eval(code); const caught = suite.some(([input, want]) => fn(input) !== want); if (caught) killed++; else console.log(\" survived:\", name); } return`${killed} of ${mutants.length - 1} mutants killed`; } console.log(\"weak suite:\", score(weakSuite)); console.log(\"strong suite:\", score(strongSuite)); Output survived: >= to > survived: 499 to 500 survived: 499 to 498 weak suite: 1 of 4 mutants killed strong suite: 4 of 4 mutants killed A flaky test passes and fails without any change to the code. The common causes are shared state between tests, fixed sleeps instead of waiting for a condition, test order, time and time zones, and real network calls. Running a test on its own is the quickest way to find an order problem. A test that passes only in order · 🧪 Tests run 📄 test/cart.test.js import { test } from\"node:test\"; import assert from\"node:assert/strict\"; import { cart, add, total } from\"../src/cart.js\"; test(\"adds items\", () => { add(\"PEN\", 100); add(\"BOOK\", 400); assert.equal(total(), 500); }); test(\"applies the coupon\", () => { cart.coupon = \"SAVE10\"; // relies on the items left over by the first test assert.equal(total(), 450); }); 📄 check.sh"],[30,"quality","Test quality: properties, mutants and flaky tests","say() { printf '\\n$ %s\\n'\"$*\"; } say \"node --test # the whole file, in order\"node --test --no-warnings --test-reporter=spec | grep -E '^(✔|✖)' say \"node --test --test-name-pattern=coupon # the same test on its own\"node --test --no-warnings --test-reporter=spec --test-name-pattern=coupon | grep -E '^(✔|✖)|actual:|expected:' true Terminal $ sh check.sh Terminal output $ node --test # the whole file, in order ✔ adds items ✔ applies the coupon $ node --test --test-name-pattern=coupon # the same test on its own ✖ applies the coupon ✖ failing tests: ✖ applies the coupon actual: 0, expected: 450, The fix for the test above is to reset the cart in a beforeEach and let each test add its own items. Track flaky tests in a list, fix them quickly, and never leave retries on to hide them. Lesson 7 of 7"],[30,"scenario","Real world scenario: a faster CI pipeline","🧪 QA essentialPlaywrightCI The situationThe end to end suite runs on three CI machines, but one machine always finishes long after the others. You have the timings from the last run. Balance CI shards with last run timings · ▶ Runs // Timings from the last CI run, read from the JUnit reportconst timings = { \"checkout.spec\": 310, \"search.spec\": 95, \"login.spec\": 60, \"cart.spec\": 120, \"profile.spec\": 45, \"orders.spec\": 180, \"payments.spec\": 240, \"admin.spec\": 70, \"wishlist.spec\": 40, \"returns.spec\": 150, }; const files = Object.keys(timings).sort(); // Naive: spread files by position, which is what --shard style splitting doesfunction byPosition(n) { const shards = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []); files.forEach((f, i) => shards[i % n].push(f)); return shards; } // Balanced: longest file first, always into the shard with the least work so farfunction byDuration(n) { const shards = Array.from({ length: n }, () => ({ files: [], total: 0 })); for (const f of files.toSorted((a, b) => timings[b] - timings[a])) { const lightest = shards.reduce((min, s) => (s.total < min.total ? s : min)); lightest.files.push(f); lightest.total += timings[f]; } return shards.map(s => s.files); } const report = (name, shards) => { const totals = shards.map(s => s.reduce((sum, f) => sum + timings[f], 0)); console.log(name.padEnd(12), \"shard seconds:\", totals.join(\" / \"), \"| CI waits\", Math.max(...totals), \"s\"); }; report(\"by position\", byPosition(3)); report(\"by duration\", byDuration(3)); console.log(byDuration(3).map((s, i) => `shard ${i + 1}: ${s.join(\", \")}`).join(\"\\n\")); Output by position shard seconds: 215 / 450 / 645 | CI waits 645 s by duration shard seconds: 445 / 420 / 445 | CI waits 445 s shard 1: checkout.spec, search.spec, wishlist.spec shard 2: payments.spec, cart.spec, login.spec shard 3: orders.spec, returns.spec, admin.spec, profile.spec Splitting by position put the slow checkout and payments files on the same machine. Sorting by duration and filling the lightest shard first cuts the wait by more than three minutes with the same three machines. This greedy method is called longest processing time first, and it is what tools such as Knapsack Pro and CircleCI test splitting do. 🧮"],[30,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out what the code gives, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 A nested duplicate check on 1000 ids with no duplicates. How many comparisons? Use n × (n − 1) ÷ 2. Each pair once. Answer: 499500 1000 × 999 ÷ 2 = 499,500 C2 Benchmark runs took 12, 9, 30, 10 and 11 ms. What is the median? Sort them first. Answer: 11 Sorted: 9, 10, 11, 12, 30. The middle value is 11. C1 With a Set, the same 1000 ids need how many lookups? One per id. Answer: 1000 n = 1000 C2 A suite kills 18 of 24 mutants. What is the mutation score in percent? Killed over total. Answer: 75 18 ÷ 24 × 100 = 75 C3 A test fails 3 times in 200 CI runs. What is its flake rate in percent? Failures over runs. Answer: 1.5 3 ÷ 200 × 100 = 1.5 C1 ₹1000.00 is split between 3 people in paise so nothing is lost. What is the largest share, in paise? Floor, then hand out the remainder. Answer: 33334 100000 ÷ 3 = 33333 remainder 1 One person gets one extra paisa: 33334. C2 Test files take 310, 240, 180, 150 and 120 seconds. Longest first into 2 shards, always into the lighter one. How long does CI wait, in seconds? Track both totals. Answer: 540 310 goes to A, 240 to B. 180 goes to B (420), 150 to A (460), 120 to B (540). Totals 460 and 540. CI waits for the larger. C1 The scenario pipeline waits 645 s by position and 445 s by duration. How many seconds does balancing save on every run? Difference. Answer: 200 645 − 445 = 200 🎯"],[30,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/12correct Q1 Which is the safe way to show text a user typed? Text, not HTML. textContent never turns the text into elements or scripts. Q2 Why take the median of several runs in a benchmark? Noise. The median ignores outliers such as a garbage collection pause. Q3 What is a flaky test? Unreliable. Flaky tests teach people to ignore red builds, so fix them quickly. Q1 Searching an array of n items for each of n other items is roughly? A loop in a loop. Using a Set makes each lookup O(1) on average, so the total becomes O(n). Q2 What does passing { signal } to addEventListener let you do? AbortController. One abort() call can remove many listeners at once. Q3 What is a test data builder for? Defaults. Tests become shorter and stay valid when new required fields are added. Q4 When are worker threads the right tool? CPU bound. Waiting on I/O is already non blocking with async code. Q1 How does JSON with a \"__proto__\" key pollute every object? target[\"__proto__\"] is the prototype. Skip that key or use Object.create(null) objects. Q2 Your tests have 100% line coverage but a mutant survives. What does that mean? Ran is not checked. Add a test at the boundary that the mutant changed. Q3 Why should a property based test use a fixed or printed seed? Repeatable. fast-check prints the seed and path of every failure for this reason. Q1 A test passes in the full file but fails when run alone. The most likely cause? Order dependency. Reset state in beforeEach so each test sets up what it needs. Q2 Why does a WeakMap keyed by a DOM node not leak when the node is removed? Weak reference. A plain Map would keep the node and its data in memory forever."],[30,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real code in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[30,"memo","Memoize a pure function","Write memoize(fn) that returns a function which calls fn only once for each set of arguments and returns the cached result after that. Treat arguments as equal when JSON.stringify of them is equal. Use JSON.stringify(args) as the key of a Map. function memoize(fn) { const cache = newMap(); return (...args) => { const key = JSON.stringify(args); if (!cache.has(key)) cache.set(key, fn(...args)); return cache.get(key); }; } Memoize only pure functions. A function that reads the clock or the network would return stale results. 🌿 Medium"],[30,"debounce","Debounce a search box","Write debounce(fn, ms). The returned function waits ms milliseconds after the last call and then calls fn once with the latest arguments. Earlier calls inside the wait are dropped. Clear the previous timer on every call, then start a new one. function debounce(fn, ms) { let timer; return (...args) => { clearTimeout(timer); timer = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), ms); }; } Testers meet debounce often: a search box that waits before calling the API needs a wait in the test, never a fixed sleep shorter than the debounce. 🔥 Hard"],[30,"escape","Escape HTML","Write escapeHtml(text) that replaces &, <, >, \" and ' with &amp;, &lt;, &gt;, &quot; and &#39;, so the text is safe inside HTML and attributes. One regular expression with a lookup object. Replace every character in a single pass so & is not escaped twice. function escapeHtml(text) { const map = { \"&\": \"&amp;\", \"<\": \"&lt;\", \">\": \"&gt;\", '\"': \"&quot;\", \"'\": \"&#39;\" }; return text.replace(/[&<>\"']/g, ch => map[ch]); } Replacing one character at a time in separate passes can escape the ampersands you just added. 🧠 Expert"],[30,"shards","Balance test shards","Write balance(timings, n). timings maps file names to seconds. Sort files by time, longest first (ties by name A to Z), and put each one into the shard with the smallest total so far (the lowest index on a tie). Return an array of n arrays of file names. Keep a running total for each shard and pick the smallest with a strict less than, so ties go to the lowest index. function balance(timings, n) { const shards = Array.from({ length: n }, () => ({ files: [], total: 0 })); const files = Object.keys(timings).sort((a, b) => timings[b] - timings[a] || a.localeCompare(b)); for (const f of files) { let best = shards[0]; for (const s of shards) if (s.total < best.total) best = s; best.files.push(f); best.total += timings[f]; } return shards.map(s => s.files); } This is the longest processing time rule. It is simple and usually lands within a few percent of the best possible split."],[30,"shards","Summary","The one idea to keep from each lesson. Open a lesson again when a line does not make sense yet. Performance: count first, then measureMost slow JavaScript is slow because of the algorithm, not the engine. A loop inside a loop over the same list does about n² ÷ 2 steps, while a Set lookup does about n. Count steps to compare ideas, and measure time only to confirm. Memory: leaks and weak referencesJavaScript frees memory that nothing can reach any more. A leak happens when something you forgot still points at old data: a listener that was never removed, a cache that only grows, or a timer that keeps running. Patterns experts reach forA pattern is a known shape for a common problem. These four appear in application code and in test frameworks alike. Workers: real parallel workJavaScript runs your code on one thread, so a long calculation blocks everything else. Worker threads in Node.js and Web Workers in the browser run code on separate threads and talk to the main thread with messages. Security every JavaScript developer should testCross site scripting (XSS) happens when user text is turned into HTML. The safe defaults are textContent, framework templates that escape by default, and a Content Security Policy header that blocks inline scripts. Test quality: properties, mutants and flaky testsExample based tests check the inputs you thought of. Property based testing generates hundreds of inputs and checks a rule that must always hold. Libraries such as fast-check also shrink a failure to the smallest input. The seed makes every failure repeatable. Real world scenario: a faster CI pipelineSplitting by position put the slow checkout and payments files on the same machine. Sorting by duration and filling the lightest shard first cuts the wait by more than three minutes with the same three machines."],[30,"comic","Habits of an expert","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember Good code is fast because of its design, cleans up after itself, treats user input as data, and comes with tests that would fail if it broke."],[30,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🛒An online shop Replaced a nested loop that matched cart items to offers with a Map and cut checkout time from 2 seconds to 40 ms on large carts. 📊A dashboard team Found a listener leak after QA kept the app open for a full day and memory grew past 2 GB. AbortController fixed it. 🏦A payments company Added property based tests to its rounding code and found a split that lost one paisa, before it reached real customers. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"Expert JavaScript: performance, security and test quality\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 9Welcome This chapter covers expert topics: performance, memory, design patterns, workers, security, and how to judge the quality of your tests. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[31,"","Cheatsheet","The key idea of every lesson, short code examples, tables, tips, common mistakes and exam notes from the JavaScript and TypeScript chapters on one page, from values, types and functions to objects, async code, the DOM, Node, testing and the TypeScript type system. Search it, filter it, print it or download the PDF. · cheatsheet · cheat sheet · formula sheet · revision · PDF · download · quick reference · let const var · closures · this · promises · async await · event loop · array methods · destructuring · modules · TypeScript types · generics · Jest · Mocha"],[31,"","Step 1Foundations","1.1"],[31,"js-getting-started","Getting Started with JavaScript","Open chapter What JavaScript is, where it runs, how to set up Node.js, how to print with the console, how a page loads scripts and how to read the errors you will meet every day. Key ideas What JavaScript is and where it runs JavaScript is the programming language of the web. Every modern browser has a JavaScript engine built in, so the same code runs on a phone, a laptop and a smart TV. Outside the browser, Node.js , Deno and Bun run JavaScript on servers, laptops and CI machines. Setting up Node.js and an editor To follow along on your own machine you need two things: Node.js and a code editor. Every example on this page also runs inside your browser with the ✏️ Edit and run button, so you can start right now. Your first program A JavaScript program is a list of statements that run from top to bottom. console.log prints whatever you give it. Several values are printed with a space between them. The console toolbox The console is your first debugging tool and the place where test runners print what they find. It can do far more than log . JavaScript in a web page In a browser, JavaScript can read and change the page. The page is a tree of objects called the DOM (Document Object Model). document.querySelector finds an element with a CSS selector, the same kind of selector a test tool uses to find a button. How the engine runs your code Running a script happens in a few steps, and knowing them explains several surprises you will meet later. Reading error messages Errors are not a failure on your part. They are the fastest way to learn what the program really did. Every error has a name , a message and a stack trace that lists where it happened. Real world scenario: summarise a test run The script below does exactly that with only what this chapter covered: an array of objects, a loop, a template literal and the console. Formulas Check your setup in a terminal node -v # which Node.js is installednpm -v # npm comes with Node.jsnode# opens the REPL, type .exit to leavenode hello.js # runs a file Lesson: Setting up Node.js and an editor Your first program console.log(\"Hello, Brainy Bytes!\"); console.log(\"2 + 3 =\", 2 + 3); console.log(`Today we learn ${\"JavaScript\"}`); Lesson: Your first program Measuring how long something takes console.time(\"build list\"); const items = []; for (let i = 0; i < 100000; i++) items.push(i * 2); console.timeEnd(\"build list\"); console.log(\"items:\", items.length); Lesson: The console toolbox JavaScript changes the page <html> <head><title>Shoptitle>head> <body> <h1id=\"title\">Welcomeh1> <buttonid=\"buy\">Buy nowbutton> <pid=\"msg\">p> body> html> Lesson: JavaScript in a web page The same typo without strict mode let total = 0; totl = 10; // sloppy mode silently creates a global variable console.log(total, totl); Lesson: How the engine runs your code Reading an error message const user = { name: \"Asha\" }; console.log(user.name.toUpperCase()); console.log(user.address.city); Lesson: Reading error messages A first automated check with the built in test runner exportfunction summarize(results) { const summary = { passed: 0, failed: 0, skipped: 0 }; for (const r of results) summary[r.status]++; return summary; } Lesson: Real world scenario: summarise a test run Tables Table: name · what it is Name What it is ECMAScript The official standard. JavaScript is the language that follows it. A new edition comes out every June: ES2015 (also called ES6), ES2016 and so on up to ES2026. TC39 The committee that decides new features. A proposal moves through stages 0 to 4 and joins the standard at stage 4. Engine The program that runs the code: V8 (Chrome, Edge, Node.js, Deno), SpiderMonkey (Firefox) and JavaScriptCore (Safari, Bun). Runtime An engine plus extra tools. A browser adds the page (DOM), fetch and storage. Node.js adds files, network servers and processes. Lesson: What JavaScript is and where it runs Table: method · use it for Method Use it for log, info normal messages, written to standard output warn, error"],[31,"js-getting-started","Getting Started with JavaScript","problems, written to the error stream, shown in yellow and red in the browser table arrays of objects shown as a table, perfect for test data group, groupEnd indenting related lines, like a suite and its tests count how many times a line ran, for example retries assert prints an error only when the condition is false time, timeEnd how long a piece of code took Lesson: The console toolbox Table: error · usual cause Error Usual cause SyntaxError The code is not valid JavaScript: a missing bracket or quote. Nothing in the file runs. ReferenceError A name that does not exist, often a typo, or a variable used before let declared it. TypeError Reading a property of undefined or null, or calling something that is not a function. RangeError A number out of range, such as new Array(-1), or endless recursion. Lesson: Reading error messages Tips and traps Remember A project can pin its Node.js version in a file called .nvmrc or in the engines field of package.json, so every developer and every CI machine uses the same one.Lesson: Setting up Node.js and an editor In the exam A line break right after return makes JavaScript insert a semicolon there. The function returns undefined and the object below is never reached. Always start the returned value on the same line as return.Lesson: Your first program Remember Write new code as modules and you get strict mode for free. Every .mjs example on this page is strict.Lesson: How the engine runs your code 1.2"],[31,"js-how-it-runs","How a JavaScript Program Runs Inside the Engine","Open chapter Follow one program from a text file to the output: loading, the scanner and its tokens, the parser and its syntax tree, the creation phase and hoisting, Ignition bytecode, the call stack and the heap, the JIT compilers Sparkplug, Maglev and TurboFan, the event loop and how modules are linked. Type your own code into two step by step simulators. Key ideas The journey of a JavaScript program When you press Run, your .js file is only text. Two programs work together to turn that text into output. The engine understands the JavaScript language. Step 1: loading the source text The engine cannot read files or download anything itself. The runtime fetches the text and hands it over. Step 2: the scanner cuts text into tokens The first thing the engine does with the text is scanning (also called lexing or tokenising). It reads the characters from left to right and groups them into tokens , the smallest pieces that mean something. Step 3: the parser builds a syntax tree The parser reads the tokens and checks them against the grammar of JavaScript. If they fit, it builds an abstract syntax tree (AST): a tree of nodes such as VariableDeclaration , BinaryExpression and CallExpression . Step 4: scopes and the creation phase Before a script or a function runs, the engine sets up an execution context for it. This happens in two phases. Step 5: Ignition turns the tree into bytecode V8 does not run the tree directly. Its interpreter, Ignition , first turns the tree into bytecode : a list of small, simple instructions. Bytecode is much smaller than machine code, so it starts fast and saves memory on phones. Step 6: running: the call stack, the heap and garbage collection While the bytecode runs, the engine uses two areas of memory. Step 7: hot code gets compiled: Sparkplug, Maglev and TurboFan Interpreting bytecode is quick to start but slower to run. So V8 watches which functions run often and compiles them to machine code while the program is running. Step 8: the runtime, Web APIs and the event loop The engine runs one piece of code at a time on one thread. So how does a page wait for a server without freezing? It does not wait. Modules: load, link, then evaluate An ES module is not run the moment it is read. The runtime and the engine go through three phases for the whole graph of files. Real world scenario: a frozen page on sale day The cart runs a long synchronous calculation over every item. While it runs, the call stack is never empty, so the event loop cannot run click handlers, timers or painting. The code below measures this with a timer that should fire after 50 ms. Formulas The invisible wrapper around a CommonJS file // Node.js wraps every CommonJS file in a function before the engine sees it.// That is where require, module, exports, __filename and __dirname come from.const { wrapper } = require(\"node:module\"); console.log(wrapper[0] + \"...your file...\" + wrapper[1]); console.log(typeof require, typeof module, typeof exports); console.log(__filename.endsWith(\"main.cjs\")); console.log(\"arguments of the wrapper:\", arguments.length); Lesson: Step 1: loading the source text One typo on line 4 stops line 1 too console.log(\"line 1: this never prints\"); const user = { name: \"Asha\" }; console.log(\"line 3: neither does this\"); if (user.name === \"Asha\" { console.log(\"hello\"); } Lesson: Step 3: the parser builds a syntax tree The real bytecode V8 makes for one function function addTax(price, rate) { const tax = price * rate; return price + tax; } console.log(addTax(100, 0.18)); Lesson: Step 5: Ignition turns the tree into bytecode The same orders the simulator shows, run for real console.log(\"A sync\"); setTimeout(() => console.log(\"B timer\"), 0); Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"C promise\")); console.log(\"D sync\"); setTimeout(() => { console.log(\"E timer\"); Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"F promise inside the timer\")); }, 0); setTimeout(() => console.log(\"G second timer\"), 0); Lesson: Step 8: the runtime, Web"],[31,"js-how-it-runs","How a JavaScript Program Runs Inside the Engine","APIs and the event loop Tables Table: piece · what it does · real examples Piece What it does Real examples Engine Scans, parses, compiles and runs the code, and cleans up memory V8 (Chrome, Edge, Node.js, Deno), SpiderMonkey (Firefox), JavaScriptCore (Safari, Bun) Runtime Loads files, provides the event loop and the extra APIs A browser tab, Node.js, Deno, Bun, Electron apps, Cloudflare Workers Extras from a browser document, fetch, setTimeout, localStorage, events The Web APIs Extras from Node.js fs, http, process, setTimeout, fetch Built on the libuv library Lesson: The journey of a JavaScript program Table: step · java · javascript Step Java JavaScript Who compiles first You run javac, which writes a .class file of bytecode Nobody. You ship the source text and the engine compiles it when it loads What runs it The JVM: class loader, bytecode verifier, interpreter, JIT, garbage collector The engine: parser, Ignition interpreter, Sparkplug, Maglev and TurboFan, garbage collector When errors show up Type and syntax errors at compile time Syntax errors when the file is parsed, type errors only when the line runs Threads Many threads by default One thread per page or process, plus an event loop. Workers add more threads. Python for comparison CPython compiles your .py to bytecode (.pyc) and interprets it. Since Python 3.13 it can also try an experimental JIT. Lesson: The journey of a JavaScript program Table: token kind · examples from let total = price * 2; // tax Token kind Examples from let total = price * 2; // tax Keyword let Identifier (name) total, price Punctuator (operator) =, *, ; Literal 2, and strings such as \"pen\" Thrown away spaces, line breaks (it remembers where they were) and the comment // tax Lesson: Step 2: the scanner cuts text into tokens Table: declaration · after the creation phase · read before its line Declaration After the creation phase Read before its line function f() {} the whole function works var x = 1 undefined gives undefined let x = 1, const x = 1 uninitialised (TDZ) ReferenceError class A {} uninitialised (TDZ) ReferenceError const f = () => {} uninitialised (TDZ) ReferenceError, so arrow helpers must be defined before use Lesson: Step 4: scopes and the creation phase Table: instruction · meaning in plain words Instruction Meaning in plain words Ldar a1 Load argument 1 (rate) into the accumulator Mul a0, FBV[0] Multiply argument 0 (price) by the accumulator. FBV[0] is a feedback slot where V8 writes down the types it saw Star0 Store the accumulator in register r0 (that is tax) Ldar r0 Load r0 back into the accumulator Add a0, FBV[1] Add price to it, and record the types in feedback slot 1 Return Give the accumulator back to the caller Lesson: Step 5: Ignition turns the tree into bytecode Table: tier · used for · how it works Tier Used for How it works Ignition Everything, at first Interprets bytecode and collects type feedback Sparkplug Functions that are warm Turns bytecode into machine code almost one to one, no optimisation Maglev Functions that are hot Uses the feedback to make good machine code quickly TurboFan The hottest code, such as tight loops Uses the feedback to make the fastest code, and takes the longest to compile Lesson: Step 7: hot code gets compiled: Sparkplug, Maglev and TurboFan Tips and traps Remember You never call the compiler in JavaScript, but it is still there. V8 compiles in the background while your page runs. That is why the same code can get faster after it has run a few thousand times.Lesson: The journey of a JavaScript program Remember Line breaks matter to the scanner because of automatic semicolon insertion. If a line ends where a statement could end, a semicolon is added. That is why return on its own line followed by an object returns undefined (chapter 1 shows it).Lesson: Step 2: the scanner cuts text into tokens Remember Firefox and Safari use the same idea with different names. SpiderMonkey has a baseline interpreter, a baseline compiler and the Warp"],[31,"js-how-it-runs","How a JavaScript Program Runs Inside the Engine","optimiser. JavaScriptCore has LLInt, Baseline, DFG and FTL.Lesson: Step 7: hot code gets compiled: Sparkplug, Maglev and TurboFan 1.3"],[31,"js-values-types","Variables, Types and Operators","Open chapter let and const, the seven primitive types, why 0.1 + 0.2 is not 0.3, how JavaScript converts values by itself, the three kinds of equality and every operator, including ?. and ??. Key ideas Variables with let and const A variable is a name for a value. Modern JavaScript has two keywords for creating one. The types of value Every value in JavaScript is one of eight types. Seven are primitives , simple values that cannot be changed. Everything else is an object . Numbers, NaN and BigInt JavaScript has one number type for whole numbers and decimals. It stores them in binary, so some decimals cannot be stored exactly. Type conversion and coercion Conversion is when you change a type on purpose. Coercion is when JavaScript does it for you behind the scenes. Both follow fixed rules. Equality: ==, === and Object.is JavaScript has three ways to ask \"are these the same\". Operators Operators combine values. Most of them you know from maths. A few are special to JavaScript and very handy. Real world scenario: clean up data from an API Every tool from this chapter is here: ?? for missing values, ?. for the optional discount, explicit Number conversion, and a check with Number.isNaN for anything that went wrong. Formulas var leaks out of blocks, let does not for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {} console.log(\"var i after the loop:\", i); // var leaks out of the blockfor (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) {} console.log(typeof j); // let stays inside the block Lesson: Variables with let and const Money without rounding errors // Work with money in the smallest unit (paise or cents) to avoid floating point errorsconst pricePaise = 129950; // ₹1299.50const qty = 3; const totalPaise = pricePaise * qty; console.log(totalPaise / 100); const fmt = newIntl.NumberFormat(\"en-IN\", { style: \"currency\", currency: \"INR\" }); console.log(fmt.format(totalPaise / 100)); Lesson: Numbers, NaN and BigInt Converting on purpose console.log(Number(\"42\"), Number(\"\"), Number(\"42px\"), Number(\" 7 \")); console.log(parseInt(\"42px\"), parseFloat(\"3.5kg\"), parseInt(\"0x1F\"), parseInt(\"101\", 2)); console.log(String(42), (42).toString(2), `${42}`); console.log(Boolean(\"\"), Boolean(\"0\"), Boolean(0), Boolean([])); Lesson: Type conversion and coercion ==, === and Object.is console.log(5 == \"5\", 5 === \"5\"); console.log(0 == false, 0 === false); console.log(null == undefined, null === undefined); console.log(NaN === NaN, Object.is(NaN, NaN)); console.log(0 === -0, Object.is(0, -0)); const a = { id: 1 }, b = { id: 1 }, c = a; console.log(a === b, a === c); console.log(JSON.stringify(a) === JSON.stringify(b)); Lesson: Equality: ==, === and Object.is Tables Table: type · examples · typeof Type Examples typeof string \"Asha\", 'ok', `Hi ${name}` \"string\" number 42, 3.14, NaN, Infinity \"number\" bigint 9007199254740993n \"bigint\" boolean true, false \"boolean\" undefined a variable with no value yet, a missing property \"undefined\" null an empty value put there on purpose \"object\" (a famous old bug) symbol Symbol(\"id\"), a unique key \"symbol\" object {}, [], functions, dates, maps \"object\" or \"function\" Lesson: The types of value Table: group · operators Group Operators Arithmetic + - * / % **, and ++ and -- to add or take away one Assignment = += -= *= /= %= **= Comparison === !== < > <= >= Logical && (and), || (or), ! (not). They return one of their values, not just true or false. Nullish ?? uses the right side only when the left is null or undefined Optional chaining ?. stops and gives undefined instead of crashing on a missing value Logical assignment ||= &&= ??= assign only when needed Conditional condition ? a : b Lesson: Operators Tips and traps Remember Names are case sensitive and use camelCase: totalPrice, isLoggedIn, maxRetries. Constants that never change across the whole project are often written as MAX_RETRIES.Lesson: Variables with let and const In the exam const does not freeze an object. It only stops the name from being reassigned. Use Object.freeze(obj) when the contents must not"],[31,"js-values-types","Variables, Types and Operators","change either.Lesson: Variables with let and const Common mistake typeof null is \"object\" and typeof [] is \"object\" too. Check for null with value === null and for arrays with Array.isArray(value).Lesson: The types of value Remember Store money in the smallest unit as a whole number and format it only for display. Never add up prices as decimals.Lesson: Numbers, NaN and BigInt In the exam Values read from an input field, a URL or an environment variable are always strings. process.env.HEADLESS set to \"false\" is truthy. Compare it with the text: process.env.HEADLESS === \"true\".Lesson: Type conversion and coercion 1.4"],[31,"js-control-flow","Control Flow, Loops and Errors","Open chapter if and switch, every kind of loop, the forEach and await trap, throwing and catching errors with causes and cleanup, and the polling and retry loops that power auto-waiting in every test tool. Key ideas Making decisions with if and else An if statement runs a block only when a condition is truthy. else if adds more conditions, checked from the top, and the first one that matches wins. switch and lookup tables switch compares one value against many cases with === . Cases without a break or return fall through into the next one, which is useful for shared results and a classic bug otherwise. Loops Loops repeat work. Pick the loop by what you are repeating over. Loops with async steps Test code is full of steps that take time: open a page, click, wait for a response. These steps return promises and are written with await (chapter 11 explains how they work). Loops and await have one trap that breaks many first test suites. Throwing and catching errors Code signals a problem by throwing an error. The error travels up through the functions that called it until a try block catches it. If nothing catches it, the program stops. Polling and retry loops Two loops sit at the centre of every test framework. You will rarely write them yourself, but knowing how they work explains timeouts, flaky tests and the options every tool offers. Real world scenario: data driven password checks This is data driven testing : the logic of the check is written once and the cases live in a table. Adding a case means adding one row. Formulas An object lookup instead of a switch constICONS = { passed: \"✅\", failed: \"❌\", broken: \"❌\", skipped: \"⏭️\" }; const icon = status => ICONS[status] ?? \"❓\"; console.log(icon(\"passed\"), icon(\"broken\"), icon(\"flaky\")); Lesson: switch and lookup tables Looping over objects and indexes const scores = { asha: 82, ravi: 67, mei: 91 }; for (const [name, score] ofObject.entries(scores)) { console.log(`${name}: ${score >= 70 ? \"pass\" : \"fail\"}`); } const tests = [\"login\", \"search\", \"checkout\"]; for (const [index, name] of tests.entries()) { console.log(index + 1, name); } Lesson: Loops Tables Table: loop · use it when Loop Use it when for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) you need the counter, or a fixed number of repeats for (const x of list) you want each value of an array, string, Map or Set. The usual choice. for (const key in obj) you want the keys of a plain object. Avoid it on arrays. while (condition) you do not know how many times, such as polling until something is ready do { } while (condition) the body must run at least once Lesson: Loops Tips and traps Remember Order matters. Put the most specific condition first. If total >= 500 came before total >= 999, a ₹1200 order would wrongly pay ₹40.Lesson: Making decisions with if and else Common mistake A while loop whose condition never becomes false runs forever and freezes the page or the test. Every polling loop needs a timeout, as shown later in this chapter.Lesson: Loops Remember Retry infrastructure problems (a 503 from a test server, a slow container). Never retry to hide a real bug. A test that only passes on the third try is reporting something.Lesson: Polling and retry loops 1.5"],[31,"js-functions","Functions, Scope and Closures","Open chapter Declarations and arrows, defaults, rest and options objects, scope and hoisting, closures, how this is decided, higher order functions such as memoize and debounce, and recursion on the call stack. Key ideas Declaring functions A function is a named, reusable piece of code. It takes parameters , does some work and returns a value. A function without a return returns undefined . Function expressions and arrow functions A function can also be written as an expression and stored in a variable. The shorter arrow function syntax is the most common form in modern code and in test files. Scope, hoisting and the temporal dead zone Scope decides where a name can be seen. JavaScript looks for a name in the current block, then in each enclosing block and function, out to the global scope. This chain is fixed by where the code is written , which is called lexical scope. Closures A closure is a function together with the variables it could see when it was created. Those variables stay alive as long as the function does, even after the outer function has returned. Closures give you private state without classes. this, call, apply and bind this is decided by how a function is called, not where it is written. Arrow functions are the exception: they never get their own this and use the one from the surrounding code. Higher order functions A higher order function takes a function as an argument or returns one. You already used one: array.map(fn) . They let you write the shape of a task once and plug in the details. Recursion and the call stack A recursive function calls itself on a smaller piece of the problem. It needs a base case that stops the calls. Every call waits on the call stack until the one it made returns. Real world scenario: a test data factory Formulas The classic loop closure bug const viaVar = []; for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) viaVar.push(() => i); console.log(viaVar.map(f => f())); // one shared i, already 3const viaLet = []; for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) viaLet.push(() => j); console.log(viaLet.map(f => f())); // let makes a new j for every turn Lesson: Closures Mocha needs function, not arrows, to use this { \"name\": \"mocha-this\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"private\": true, \"scripts\": { \"test\": \"mocha\" } } Lesson: this, call, apply and bind Tables Table: form · notes Form Notes function name() {} Hoisted, so it can be called before its line. Has its own this. const f = function () {} Not hoisted. Has its own this. const f = () => {} Not hoisted. No own this or arguments: it uses the ones around it. Cannot be called with new. Lesson: Function expressions and arrow functions Table: call · this is Call this is obj.method() obj, the object before the dot fn() undefined in strict code (the global object in old sloppy code) fn.call(x, a, b), fn.apply(x, [a, b]) x, chosen by you for this one call fn.bind(x) a new function where this is always x new Fn() the new object being created arrow function whatever this was where the arrow was written Lesson: this, call, apply and bind Tips and traps Remember When a function has more than two or three parameters, take one options object and destructure it. Calls read like labels (launch({ headless: true, slowMo: 50 })) and the order stops mattering. Playwright, Cypress and Jest all configure this way.Lesson: Declaring functions Remember Declare variables at the top of the block where you need them, with const or let. Then hoisting never surprises you.Lesson: Scope, hoisting and the temporal dead zone In the exam With var, every function created in the loop shares one variable, which has its final value by the time they run. let creates a new binding for each turn. This was a famous interview question before let existed.Lesson: Closures Remember If a Mocha or Cypress test uses this, write both the describe and the test with function. Everywhere else, arrows are fine.Lesson: this, call, apply and bind"],[31,"step-2","Step 2Working with data","2.1"],[31,"js-objects-arrays","Objects and Arrays","Open chapter Objects and their tools, destructuring, spread with shallow and deep copies, arrays, sorting without surprises, map, filter, reduce and friends, and grouping data for reports. Key ideas Objects An object groups related values under names called keys or properties . Almost everything you test is an object: a user, an API response, a configuration, a page element. Destructuring Destructuring pulls values out of objects and arrays into variables in one line. It can rename, give defaults, skip items and collect the rest. Spread, copies and immutability The spread syntax ... copies the items of an array or the keys of an object into a new one. When keys repeat, the later one wins, which makes it perfect for \"defaults plus overrides\". Arrays An array is an ordered list. Indexes start at 0, length is the number of items and at(-1) reads from the end. Array methods: map, filter, reduce and friends These methods replace most hand written loops. Each one takes a function and calls it for every item. Grouping, counting and unique values Reports need data grouped and counted. ES2024 added Object.groupBy and Map.groupBy . reduce still handles any custom shape, and a Set removes duplicates. Real world scenario: verify a sorted product listing The check found two real problems in this data: the second USB Hub at ₹1,299 comes after Keyboard at ₹2,499, so the order breaks at position 3, and USB Hub is listed twice. Formulas Array and object matchers in Vitest exportfunction parseCards(texts) { return texts.map(text => { const [, name, price] = text.match(/^(.*) ₹([\\d,]+)$/); return { name, price: Number(price.replaceAll(\",\", \"\")) }; }); } exportconst isSortedAsc = list => list.every((x, i) => i === 0 || list[i - 1] <= x); Lesson: Real world scenario: verify a sorted product listing Tables Table: changes the array · returns a new array or value Changes the array Returns a new array or value push pop shift unshift splice sort reverse fill slice concat map filter flat flatMap toSorted toReversed toSpliced with Lesson: Arrays Table: method · returns · typical use Method Returns Typical use map new array, same length turn elements into their text, products into prices filter new array, only matching items failed tests, items in stock reduce one value built up step by step totals, counts, grouping find, findLast the first (or last) matching item, or undefined the row for one SKU findIndex its index, or -1 where a sort order breaks some, every true or false, and they stop early \"any error shown\", \"all prices positive\" flatMap map, then flatten one level one row per quantity Lesson: Array methods: map, filter, reduce and friends Table: matcher · passes when Matcher Passes when toEqual(list) same items, same order, compared deeply toContainEqual(item) at least one item deeply equals item expect.arrayContaining([...]) the listed items appear, in any order, others allowed expect.objectContaining({...}) the listed keys match, other keys allowed toHaveLength(n) the length is n Lesson: Real world scenario: verify a sorted product listing Tips and traps Remember Object.entries and Object.fromEntries together let you use every array method on an object: turn it into pairs, transform them, turn them back.Lesson: Objects In the exam Spread makes a shallow copy: the outer object is new, but nested objects and arrays are shared. Use structuredClone for a full deep copy.Lesson: Spread, copies and immutability Common mistake sort() without a compare function sorts as text, so 100 comes before 9. For numbers always pass (a, b) => a - b. It also changes the original array. ES2023 added toSorted, toReversed, toSpliced and with, which leave the original alone.Lesson: Arrays Remember [...new Set(list)] is the shortest way to remove duplicates from a list of strings or numbers. For objects, compare a key, as the scenario below does.Lesson: Grouping, counting and unique values 2.2"],[31,"js-strings-dates-json","Strings, Regex, Numbers, Dates and JSON","Open chapter String methods and Unicode, comparing text read from a page, regular expressions in code and in assertions, Math and Intl number formatting, Intl for dates, plurals, lists and characters in every language, Date traps and the new Temporal API, and JSON for APIs and fixture files. Key ideas Working with strings A string is text. Strings are immutable : every method returns a new string and the original never changes. Indexes start at 0. Regular expressions A regular expression describes a pattern of text. It is written between slashes, followed by optional flags . Math and number formatting The Math object holds rounding, min and max, powers and random numbers. Intl.NumberFormat turns numbers into the exact text a user in each country expects. Intl: one app, every language and country A shopping app used in Mumbai, Chicago and Berlin must show the same price, date and sentence in three different ways. Writing those rules by hand is how bugs like \"1 items\" or \"$12,34,567\" reach production. Dates, time zones and Temporal The Date object stores one moment as milliseconds since 1 January 1970 UTC. It has well known traps, which is why the new Temporal API was designed. It reached stage 4 in 2026 and is listed for the ES2027 edition. JSON JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is the text format of almost every API. JSON.stringify turns a value into JSON text and JSON.parse turns text back into a value. Real world scenario: validate an order API response Formulas The string methods you use daily const title = \" Wireless Mouse, Silent Click \"; const t = title.trim(); console.log(`[${t}]`, t.length); console.log(t.toUpperCase(), \"|\", t.toLowerCase()); console.log(t.includes(\"Mouse\"), t.startsWith(\"Wire\"), t.endsWith(\"Click\")); console.log(t.indexOf(\"Silent\"), t.slice(0, 8), t.slice(-5), t.at(0)); console.log(t.split(\", \")); console.log(t.replace(\"Click\", \"Clicks\"), \"|\", \"a-b-c\".replaceAll(\"-\", \"/\")); console.log(\"7\".padStart(3, \"0\"), \"ab\".repeat(3), \"Total\".padEnd(8, \".\") + \"₹99\"); Lesson: Working with strings Validate an order API response { \"name\": \"order-api\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"private\": true, \"scripts\": { \"test\": \"jest\" } } Lesson: Real world scenario: validate an order API response Tables Table: need · method Need Method remove spaces at the ends trim, trimStart, trimEnd search includes, startsWith, endsWith, indexOf, search cut out a part slice(start, end) (negative counts from the end), at change replace, replaceAll, toUpperCase, toLowerCase split and join split(separator) and the array's join pad and repeat padStart, padEnd, repeat Lesson: Working with strings Table: piece · means Piece Means \\d \\w \\s a digit, a word character, whitespace (capitals mean \"not\": \\D) [abc] [^abc] [a-z] one of, none of, a range * + ? {6} {2,4} repeat: 0 or more, 1 or more, optional, exactly 6, 2 to 4 ^ $ \\b start, end, word boundary ( ) (?<name> ) (?: ) capture group, named group, group without capturing a|b a or b (?=x) (?!x) followed by x, not followed by x (lookahead) flags g i m s u v y all matches, ignore case, multi line, dot matches new lines, unicode, unicode sets, sticky Lesson: Regular expressions Table: tool · what it formats · real example Tool What it formats Real example Intl.NumberFormat numbers, currencies, percentages, units, compact numbers ₹12,34,567.89 in India, $1,234,567.89 in the US Intl.DateTimeFormat dates and times in a chosen time zone a delivery slot shown in the customer's own zone Intl.RelativeTimeFormat \"yesterday\", \"in 3 hours\" \"Order placed 2 days ago\" Intl.PluralRules which plural form a number needs \"1 item\" and \"2 items\", or Arabic with six forms Intl.ListFormat \"A, B, and C\" or \"A oder B\" \"Ships with Chrome, Firefox, and Safari\" Intl.DisplayNames names of countries, languages and currencies a country dropdown in the user's language Intl.Segmenter characters, words and sentences as people see them a character counter for a Hindi or emoji message Intl.Collator sorting and comparing text like a human"],[31,"js-strings-dates-json","Strings, Regex, Numbers, Dates and JSON","\"résumé\" next to \"resume\", \"apple\" before \"Zebra\" Lesson: Intl: one app, every language and country Table: javascript value · what json does with it JavaScript value What JSON does with it objects, arrays, strings, numbers, booleans, null kept undefined, functions, symbols dropped from objects, turned into null in arrays Date becomes an ISO string and comes back as a string NaN, Infinity become null BigInt throws a TypeError Map, Set become {} Lesson: JSON Tips and traps Common mistake length counts UTF-16 code units, not visible characters. Emoji and some accents count as 2 or more. Use [...str].length for characters, normalize(\"NFC\") before comparing and localeCompare for human sorting.Lesson: Working with strings Common mistake Date months start at 0 (January is 0, December is 11), invalid dates such as 30 February roll over silently, and methods without \"UTC\" use the computer's time zone, so the same test can pass in Bengaluru and fail on a CI server in Frankfurt.Lesson: Dates, time zones and Temporal 2.3"],[31,"js-collections-iterators","Map, Set, Iterators and Generators","Open chapter Map for lookups with any key, Set with the new union and difference methods, weak collections, the iteration protocol and lazy iterator helpers, generators, async generators for paginated APIs, binary data with ArrayBuffer, typed arrays, DataView and Atomics, and symbols. Key ideas Map A Map stores key and value pairs, like an object, with three differences that matter: keys can be any value (objects, numbers, functions), it remembers insertion order, and it has a size . Set and set operations A Set holds unique values. Adding a value twice keeps one copy. ES2025 added the operations from school maths, so comparing two lists no longer needs nested loops. WeakMap, WeakSet and WeakRef Weak collections hold objects without keeping them alive. When nothing else uses the object, the garbage collector may remove it and its entry disappears. They cannot be listed or counted, because their contents can change at any moment. Iterators and iterator helpers An iterable is anything with a Symbol.iterator method: arrays, strings, Maps, Sets, NodeLists and arguments. That method returns an iterator , an object whose next() hands out { value, done } one at a time. for...of , spread and destructuring all use it. Generators and async generators A generator function ( function* ) can pause at every yield and continue later from the same place. Calling it runs nothing. It returns an iterator, and each next() runs the body up to the next yield . Binary data: ArrayBuffer, typed arrays and DataView Most JavaScript works with text and objects, but files, images, network packets, audio and cryptography work with bytes . An ArrayBuffer is a block of raw memory with a fixed size. You never read it directly. Symbols A symbol is a unique value used as a hidden key. Two symbols with the same description are still different, unless they come from the shared registry with Symbol.for . Symbol keys are skipped by Object.keys and JSON.stringify . Real world scenario: compare a menu and walk every page Set operations produce the missing and unexpected lists in one line each, and duplicates are easy to spot once you compare each item's first position with its own. The async generator hides the paging, so the count is a plain loop. Tables Table: method · returns Method Returns a.union(b) everything in either a.intersection(b) only what is in both a.difference(b) in a but not in b a.symmetricDifference(b) in exactly one of them a.isSubsetOf(b), isSupersetOf, isDisjointFrom true or false Lesson: Set and set operations Tips and traps Remember Use a plain object for fixed shapes such as a user or a config. Use a Map for lookups that grow at run time: caches, counters, \"element to data\" tables. getOrInsert and getOrInsertComputed (ES2026) replace the \"get, check, set\" dance.Lesson: Map"],[31,"step-3","Step 3Structure and tooling","3.1"],[31,"js-oop","Prototypes, Classes and the Page Object Model","Open chapter How objects borrow from prototypes, classes with fields, getters, static and private members, inheritance with extends and super, custom errors, composition, and the Page Object Model that most UI test frameworks are built on. Key ideas Objects and prototypes Every object in JavaScript has a hidden link to another object called its prototype . When you read a property the object does not have, JavaScript follows that link and looks there, then on that object's prototype, and so on. Classes A class is a template for objects that share the same shape and methods. It has a constructor that sets up each new object, methods shared through the prototype, fields that every object gets, getters and setters that look like properties, and static members ... Private fields and encapsulation A field or method whose name starts with # is private . Only code inside the class body can read or change it. It does not show up in Object.keys , in JSON or in the console of other code, and any attempt to read it from outside is a syntax error. Inheritance with extends and super class Dog extends Animal makes Animal.prototype the prototype of Dog.prototype . A Dog gets every Animal method and can override any of them. Inside the subclass, super(...) calls the parent constructor, and super.method() calls the parent version of a method. Composition over inheritance Deep class trees get hard to change: a fix in a parent class affects every child. Many teams prefer composition , where an object is built from small parts it holds or mixes in. The Page Object Model The Page Object Model (POM) is the most common design pattern in UI automation. Each page or component of the app gets a class. The class holds the locators and offers methods with business names such as loginAs or addToCart . Real world scenario: test data and an API client A builder class starts from sensible defaults, and each test changes only what matters to it. Every built user gets a fresh email, so tests never clash. Tables Table: member · lives on · example Member Lives on Example field each object items = [] method the prototype, shared add(name, price) getter or setter the prototype, used like a property get total() static the class itself Cart.fromJSON(json) Lesson: Classes Tips and traps Remember new User(\"Asha\") does four things: it creates an empty object, links it to User.prototype, runs User with this set to the new object, and returns it.Lesson: Objects and prototypes Common mistake In a subclass constructor, super() must run before the first use of this. Forgetting it throws a ReferenceError as soon as the class is used.Lesson: Inheritance with extends and super 3.2"],[31,"js-modules-tooling","Modules, npm, TypeScript and Tooling","Open chapter ES modules and CommonJS, npm with package.json and version ranges, TypeScript run straight in Node.js and checked with tsc, ESLint and Prettier, configuration from environment variables, and how a real test project is laid out. Key ideas ES modules: import and export A module is a file with its own scope. Nothing inside it is visible to other files unless it is exported , and nothing from outside is available unless it is imported . CommonJS and how the two mix Before ES modules, Node.js used CommonJS : require() to load and module.exports to share. Older packages, Jest setups, Mocha and many Selenium projects still use it, so you will read both styles. npm and package.json npm installs packages from the npm registry into node_modules and records them in package.json . The lock file ( package-lock.json ) pins the exact version of every package, so every laptop and CI machine installs the same thing. TypeScript TypeScript is JavaScript with types added. You describe the shape of your data once, and the type checker warns you about wrong property names, missing fields and wrong value types while you type, long before a test runs. ESLint and Prettier ESLint reads your code without running it and reports likely bugs and risky habits: unused variables, typos in names, == where === was meant, an async function that never awaits. Rules live in eslint.config.js . Environment variables and config The same tests must run against a laptop, a staging server and sometimes production. The parts that change, such as the base URL, user names and secrets, come from environment variables in process.env . Real world scenario: lay out a test project Source code lives in src . Tests are split by kind under tests . Each kind gets its own npm script, so a developer runs npm run test:unit in seconds while CI runs every script. Formulas CommonJS: require and module.exports function slugify(text) { return text.toLowerCase().trim().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, \"-\"); } module.exports = { slugify }; Lesson: CommonJS and how the two mix The type checker finds bugs before any test runs { \"compilerOptions\": { \"target\": \"es2024\", \"module\": \"nodenext\", \"strict\": true, \"noEmit\": true, \"erasableSyntaxOnly\": true }, \"include\": [\"src\"] } Lesson: TypeScript ESLint reads the code and reports problems { \"name\": \"linted\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"private\": true, \"type\": \"module\", \"scripts\": { \"lint\": \"eslint .\" } } Lesson: ESLint and Prettier Configuration from environment variables # Never commit real secrets. Keep .env in .gitignore and use CI secrets instead. TEST_ENV=staging BASE_URL=https://staging.shop.example TEST_USER=qa.bot@shop.example TEST_PASSWORD=not-a-real-password HEADLESS=true Lesson: Environment variables and config Separate scripts for each kind of test { \"name\": \"shop-tests\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"private\": true, \"type\": \"module\", \"scripts\": { \"test:unit\": \"node --test --test-reporter=spec tests/unit/\", \"test:api\": \"node --test --test-reporter=spec tests/api/\" } } Lesson: Real world scenario: lay out a test project Tables Table: syntax · meaning Syntax Meaning export const x, export function f named export, imported with the same name in braces export default ... one main value per file, imported with any name import { a as b } rename while importing import * as ns every export as one object await import(\"./x.js\") load later, only when needed Lesson: ES modules: import and export Table: · es modules · commonjs ES modules CommonJS Load import, loaded before the code runs require(), a normal function call Share export module.exports Chosen by .mjs, or .js with \"type\": \"module\" .cjs, or .js without it Top level await yes no Where you see it Playwright, Vitest, browsers, new code older Node.js code, Jest by default, Mocha Lesson: CommonJS and how the two mix Table: syntax · meaning Syntax Meaning let n: number a type annotation interface User { name: string; age?: number } the shape of an object, ? marks optional type Status = \"passed\" | \"failed\" a"],[31,"js-modules-tooling","Modules, npm, TypeScript and Tooling","union: one of these values function first<T>(list: T[]): T a generic: works for any type T value as string tell the checker you know better (use rarely) Lesson: TypeScript Tips and traps Remember Many teams avoid default exports: named exports keep the same name in every file, which makes search and automatic refactoring reliable.Lesson: ES modules: import and export Common mistake The error Cannot use import statement outside a module means a file with import is being treated as CommonJS. Add \"type\": \"module\" to package.json or rename the file to .mjs.Lesson: CommonJS and how the two mix Remember This is a first look. The TypeScript module, chapters 23 to 26, goes much further: narrowing and generics, mapped and conditional types, classes and decorators in real projects, and test automation written in TypeScript.Lesson: TypeScript Common mistake Everything in process.env is a string. \"false\" is truthy, so compare with === \"true\" or !== \"false\", and turn numbers into numbers. Never commit passwords or tokens: add .env to .gitignore and use your CI tool's secret store.Lesson: Environment variables and config Remember A typical layout shop-tests/ package.json, package-lock.json, tsconfig.json, eslint.config.js .env.example names of the variables, no real values src/ helpers, API clients, test data builders pages/ page objects tests/unit/ fast checks of pure functions tests/api/ HTTP requests against a running server tests/e2e/ browser tests with Playwright or Cypress playwright.config.ts Lesson: Real world scenario: lay out a test project"],[31,"step-4","Step 4Async and the platform","4.1"],[31,"js-async","Asynchronous JavaScript: Promises, async and await","Open chapter How one thread handles slow work with the event loop, callbacks, promises, async and await, running promises together, cancelling with AbortController, and waiting with retries and polling instead of fixed sleeps. Key ideas One thread and the event loop JavaScript runs your code on one thread : one line at a time. Slow work such as a network request, a timer or reading a file is handed to the browser or Node.js, and your code keeps going. When the slow work finishes, a callback is queued. Callbacks The first way to handle async work was to pass a function to call later. Node.js callbacks receive the error first and the result second. Promises A promise is an object that stands for a result that is not ready yet. It starts pending , then becomes fulfilled with a value or rejected with an error, and never changes again. then runs when it fulfils, catch when it rejects and finally either way. async and await async before a function makes it return a promise. Inside it, await pauses that function until a promise settles and gives you its value, while the rest of the program keeps running. Errors become normal exceptions you catch with try and catch . Running many promises: all, allSettled, race, any Four helpers combine several promises into one. They differ in what counts as done and what happens on a failure. Retries, polling and waiting the right way Tests and scripts often wait for something that happens at an unknown time: an element appears, a job finishes, a service recovers. A fixed sleep guesses the time. It wastes time when the guess is too long and fails when it is too short. Real world scenario: a link checker mapLimit starts 3 workers that take the next URL as soon as they are free. AbortSignal.timeout stops slow requests, and catching each error turns it into a row in the report instead of stopping the run. Formulas Which line prints first console.log(\"1 start\"); setTimeout(() => console.log(\"5 timeout\"), 0); Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"3 promise\")); queueMicrotask(() => console.log(\"4 microtask\")); console.log(\"2 end\"); Lesson: One thread and the event loop A missing await hides a real failure { \"name\": \"await-bug\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"private\": true, \"type\": \"module\", \"scripts\": { \"test\": \"node --test --test-reporter=spec\" } } Lesson: async and await Tables Table: queue · what goes there · when it runs Queue What goes there When it runs Microtasks promise callbacks (then, code after await), queueMicrotask right after the current code, all of them, before anything else Tasks (macrotasks) setTimeout, setInterval, clicks, network and file events one per turn of the loop, after the microtasks are empty Lesson: One thread and the event loop Table: helper · fulfils when · rejects when · good for Helper Fulfils when Rejects when Good for Promise.all all fulfil the first one rejects steps that must all succeed Promise.allSettled all have finished, either way never reports: check every link, list each result Promise.race the first one settles the first one settles with an error timeouts Promise.any the first one fulfils all reject (AggregateError) the fastest of several mirrors Lesson: Running many promises: all, allSettled, race, any Tips and traps Remember Most Node.js APIs now have promise versions: import { readFile } from \"node:fs/promises\". For an old callback API, util.promisify(fn) creates one.Lesson: Callbacks Common mistake A rejected promise that nobody catches is an unhandled rejection. In Node.js it crashes the process, and in test runners it often shows up as a confusing failure in a different test.Lesson: Promises 4.2"],[31,"js-dom-browser","The DOM and the Browser","Open chapter How the page becomes a tree of objects, finding elements with CSS selectors and XPath, choosing stable locators, changing the page safely, events and delegation, forms and validation, shadow DOM, iframes, observers, the rendering pipeline with requestAnimationFrame, storage and cookies. Key ideas The DOM tree When a browser loads HTML, it builds the DOM (Document Object Model): a tree of objects, one for each element, piece of text and comment. JavaScript reads and changes the page only through this tree, and so does every UI automation tool. Selectors and locators A CSS selector describes elements by tag, id, class, attribute and position. The same selector works in document.querySelector , in your stylesheet and in every test tool. Changing the page Elements can be changed in place: their text, classes, attributes and children. textContent sets plain text and is always safe. innerHTML parses HTML, so putting user input into it can run an attacker's code, a bug called cross site scripting (XSS). Events Clicks, key presses, input changes and form submits are events . addEventListener(type, handler) runs your function each time one happens. Forms and validation Form fields hold their current value in value (or checked for checkboxes). HTML attributes such as required , type=\"email\" and min give free validation, which JavaScript reads through checkValidity() and each field's validity . Shadow DOM, iframes and observers Web components hide their inner elements inside a shadow root . A normal querySelector from the document cannot see inside it. An iframe holds a whole separate document, often for payment forms, maps or embedded editors. How the browser draws the page Changing the DOM does not change the screen straight away. The browser collects your changes and then runs its rendering pipeline , usually 60 times a second, between tasks of the event loop (chapter 2). Storage and cookies Browsers keep data for a site in three main places. localStorage keeps strings until they are deleted. sessionStorage keeps them until the tab closes. Real world scenario: check an order table Reading the header cells first turns each row into an object keyed by column name, so the check keeps working if a column is added or moved. The footer does match the rows as shown, so a check of the total alone would have missed this bug. Formulas Shadow DOM and iframes <html><body> <user-cardname=\"Asha\">user-card> <iframeid=\"pay\">iframe> body>html> Lesson: Shadow DOM, iframes and observers Tables Table: method · returns Method Returns getElementById(\"x\") one element or null querySelector(css) the first match or null querySelectorAll(css) a NodeList of every match, possibly empty el.closest(css) the element itself or its nearest ancestor that matches parentElement, children, nextElementSibling neighbours in the tree Lesson: The DOM tree Table: locator · example · stability Locator Example Stability Role and name (what a user sees) getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Add to cart\" }) best: survives layout and styling changes Label or text getByLabel(\"Email\"), getByText(\"Deals\") good, changes with the wording Test id [data-test=\"product\"], getByTestId good, made for tests Short CSS .card .price fair, breaks when classes change Long CSS or XPath by position /html/body/main/article[3]/button poor, breaks with any layout change Lesson: Selectors and locators Table: property · reads or writes Property Reads or writes textContent all text, including hidden elements, fast innerText only the text a user can see, with layout applied (what Selenium's getText() returns) innerHTML the HTML inside the element value what is typed in an input, select or textarea Lesson: Changing the page Table: tool · shadow dom · iframes Tool Shadow DOM Iframes Playwright pierced automatically by most locators page.frameLocator(\"#pay\").getByRole(...) Selenium element.getShadowRoot() driver.switchTo().frame(...), then back with defaultContent() Cypress .shadow() or includeShadowDom"],[31,"js-dom-browser","The DOM and the Browser","needs a helper or plugin, same origin only Lesson: Shadow DOM, iframes and observers Table: tool · when your code runs · typical use Tool When your code runs Typical use requestAnimationFrame just before the next paint smooth animations, measuring after a change IntersectionObserver when an element enters or leaves the visible area lazy images, infinite scroll, \"seen\" analytics ResizeObserver when an element changes size charts that redraw to fit MutationObserver after DOM changes, as a microtask waiting for elements, as shown above requestIdleCallback when the browser has nothing else to do sending analytics, warming caches Lesson: How the browser draws the page Tips and traps Common mistake Events created by scripts have isTrusted set to false, and element.click() skips the checks a real user goes through: it works on hidden or covered buttons. That is why Playwright and Selenium move a real mouse and check that the element is visible and not covered before clicking, and why { force: true } clicks can hide real bugs.Lesson: Events Common mistake Cookies marked HttpOnly cannot be read from JavaScript at all, which protects session tokens from XSS. Test tools read them through the browser instead, for example context.cookies() in Playwright.Lesson: Storage and cookies 4.3"],[31,"js-http-node","HTTP, fetch and Node.js Servers","Open chapter What a request and a response look like, fetch and URLs, logging in with tokens and the difference between 401 and 403, writing a REST API with Express, CORS and preflight requests, real time updates with Server-Sent Events and WebSockets, the Node.js core modules, and a mock API that UI tests can control. Key ideas How HTTP works Browsers, apps and test tools talk to servers with HTTP . The client sends a request : a method, a path, headers and sometimes a body. The server sends back a response : a status code, headers and usually a body. Both are plain text you can read. fetch and URLs fetch(url, options) sends a request and returns a promise for the response. It is built into browsers and Node.js. Read the body once with res.json() , res.text() or res.blob() . Authentication: 401 against 403 Most APIs need you to prove who you are. A common pattern: log in once, receive a token , then send it on every request in the Authorization: Bearer ... header. Browsers usually keep the session in a cookie instead. Writing a server with Express On the server side, Node.js answers requests. Express is the most used library for it: you declare routes (a method plus a path) and a handler that receives the request and builds the response. CORS and the same origin policy A browser keeps websites apart. A page from https://shop.example may freely send requests to other sites, but by default its JavaScript may not read the answers from a different origin (scheme, host and port together). Real time: Server-Sent Events and WebSockets Normal HTTP is \"ask, then get one answer\". Live order tracking, chat, stock prices and multiplayer games need the server to push updates the moment they happen. Asking again every few seconds (polling) works but wastes requests and is always a little late. Node.js core modules Node.js comes with modules for files, paths, processes and more. Test suites use them to read test data, write reports and run other tools. Real world scenario: a mock API for UI tests The /__test routes exist only in the mock. A test resets the state, sets up exactly the products it needs and can inject a 503 to check that the page shows a friendly error and a retry button. A delay shows whether loading spinners appear. Tables Table: method · meaning · safe to repeat Method Meaning Safe to repeat GET read yes POST create, or run an action no PUT replace completely yes PATCH change some fields usually not DELETE remove yes Lesson: How HTTP works Table: status · group · ones you see every day Status Group Ones you see every day 1xx information 101 Switching Protocols (WebSockets) 2xx success 200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content 3xx redirect 301 Moved Permanently, 302 Found, 304 Not Modified 4xx the client got it wrong 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized, 403 Forbidden, 404 Not Found, 409 Conflict, 422 Unprocessable, 429 Too Many Requests 5xx the server failed 500 Internal Server Error, 502 Bad Gateway, 503 Service Unavailable, 504 Gateway Timeout Lesson: How HTTP works Table: console message · what it usually means Console message What it usually means No 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header is present The server did not list your origin, or the request failed before CORS headers were added (often a 500 or a proxy error) Response to preflight request does not pass access control check The OPTIONS answer is missing the method or a header your request uses The value of the 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header must not be the wildcard '*' when the credentials mode is 'include' Requests that send cookies need the exact origin, never * Lesson: CORS and the same origin policy Table: module · used for Module Used for node:fs/promises read and write files and folders node:path join and split file paths the same way on Windows, macOS and Linux node:child_process run other programs node:crypto random ids (randomUUID) and hashes node:test, node:assert the built in test runner (chapter 20) Lesson: Node.js core modules Tips and traps Common"],[31,"js-http-node","HTTP, fetch and Node.js Servers","mistake fetch only rejects when there is no response at all: a refused connection, a DNS error or an abort. A 404 or 500 still fulfils, so always check res.ok or res.status.Lesson: fetch and URLs Remember Playwright can do the same without a server by intercepting requests with page.route(), and Cypress with cy.intercept(). A mock server is still useful when mobile apps or several services need the same fake.Lesson: Real world scenario: a mock API for UI tests"],[31,"js-http-node","Step 5JavaScript in depth","5.1"],[31,"js-engine","How JavaScript really works","Open chapter What the engine does with your code: execution contexts and the call stack, hoisting and the scope chain, the five rules of this, the Node.js event loop phase by phase, V8 hidden classes and inline caches, and generational garbage collection. Key ideas Execution contexts and the call stack Every time a function is called, the engine creates an execution context : a record of its variables, its scope chain and its this . Contexts sit on the call stack . The one on top is running, and when it returns it is popped off. Creation phase, hoisting and the scope chain Before a function runs, the engine scans it and sets up its bindings. var names start as undefined , function declarations are ready to call, and let , const and class exist but sit in the temporal dead zone until their line runs. this in depth: five rules and a precedence The value of this is decided by how a function is called , not where it is written. Five rules cover every case, and when several apply, the one higher in this list wins. The Node.js event loop, phase by phase Chapter 11 showed the browser view: microtasks first, then one task. Node.js splits the task side into phases that run in a fixed order on every turn: timers (setTimeout and setInterval), poll (finished I/O such as files and sockets) and check (setImmediate). Inside V8: JIT, hidden classes and inline caches V8, the engine in Chrome and Node.js, first turns your code into bytecode and runs it with the Ignition interpreter. Functions that run often are compiled to machine code by the optimising compilers ( Sparkplug , Maglev and TurboFan ). Garbage collection JavaScript frees memory for you. V8 uses a generational collector because most objects die young. New objects go into the small young generation , which is cleaned often with a fast copying \"scavenge\". Real world scenario: a crash only in the retry helper The helper calls action() as a plain function, so this is undefined inside submit . Wrapping it in an arrow or binding it keeps the page object as this . The team also added a lint rule, @typescript-eslint/unbound-method , which flags this mistake in review. Tips and traps Remember Do not tune for the engine before you measure (chapter 31). Shapes matter in tight loops over millions of objects, such as parsing a large log file, and almost never in test code.Lesson: Inside V8: JIT, hidden classes and inline caches 5.2"],[31,"js-errors-debugging","Errors, debugging and logging","Open chapter Handle failure like an expert: the built in error types, custom errors with codes and causes, errors in async code and unhandled rejections, when to return a result instead of throwing, real debuggers and the Playwright trace viewer, structured logs with request ids, and source maps. Key ideas The error types and building your own Every error has a name , a message and a stack . The built in types tell you what kind of mistake happened, which narrows the search before you read a single line of code. Errors in async code A try block only sees errors thrown while it is on the call stack. A callback that runs later has left it already. With await , a rejected promise turns into an ordinary throw at that line, which is the main reason async and await made error handling simple. Throw or return: errors as values Throwing is right for things that should never happen. For failures you expect, such as a user typing a bad coupon, returning a result object keeps the flow simple and forces the caller to look at both cases. Debugging tools console.log is fine for a quick look, but it hides deep objects and its output is easy to misread. Node.js gives you precise tools for inspecting values. Structured logging Plain text logs are hard to search across many servers or parallel tests. Structured logs write one JSON object per line with a level, a message and fields. Source maps TypeScript, bundlers and minifiers change your code before it runs, so stack traces point at generated files. A source map is a file that maps each generated position back to the original line. Browsers use them automatically in DevTools. Real world scenario: triage a red pipeline Error names and messages carry enough signal to sort most failures automatically. Connection errors and 5xx responses point at the environment, timeouts at timing or locators, assertion errors at the product, and TypeErrors in test code at the tests ... Formulas An unhandled rejection stops Node.js asyncfunction save() { thrownewError(\"DB down\"); } save(); // nobody awaits or catches this promise setTimeout(() => console.log(\"this line never prints\"), 10); Lesson: Errors in async code Source maps point the trace at the TypeScript line exportfunction lineTotal(price: number, qty: number): number { if (qty > 10) { thrownewRangeError(`qty ${qty} is over the limit of 10`); } return price * qty; } lineTotal(99, 12); Lesson: Source maps Tips and traps Remember Check the code or the class, never the message text. Messages change with translations and wording fixes, codes do not.Lesson: The error types and building your own Remember Upload source maps to your error tracker, such as Sentry, so production errors show the real file and line. Keep them off the public web server if your source code is private.Lesson: Source maps 5.3"],[31,"js-regex","Regular expressions in depth","Open chapter Go beyond the basics of chapter 7: how the backtracking engine finds a match, greedy and lazy quantifiers, lookarounds and backreferences, every flag and the lastIndex trap, unicode letters in every language, replacing and masking, a sticky tokeniser, ReDoS, and testing patterns with tables. Key ideas How the regex engine matches JavaScript uses a backtracking engine. It tries the pattern at the first position of the text and, when a step fails, goes back to the last choice it made and tries another. If nothing works, it moves one character on and starts again. Lookarounds and backreferences A lookaround checks what comes before or after a position without including it in the match. (?=x) and (?!x) look ahead, (?<=x) and (?<!x) look behind. A backreference such as \\1 or \\k<name> matches the same text a group already matched. Flags in depth and the lastIndex trap Flags change how the whole pattern works. The g and y flags also make a regex stateful : it remembers lastIndex , the position after the last match. Unicode and every language [a-z] only knows English. With the u or v flag, \\p{L} matches a letter in any script, \\p{Script=Devanagari} one script, and \\p{Emoji} emoji. The u flag also treats an emoji as one character instead of two halves. Replacing, masking and splitting replace and replaceAll can use $1 , $<name> and $& in the replacement text, or a function that receives every group and returns the new text. ReDoS and safe patterns Some patterns make the engine try an exponential number of ways to split the input. ^(a+)+$ on a long run of \"a\" that ends with \"!\" never finishes in practice. An attacker who can send such input to a server can freeze it with one request. Validation patterns and testing them A regex can check the shape of text, not its meaning. A date pattern accepts 31 February, and no practical email pattern follows the full standard. Use a regex for the shape, then real code for the rest, such as Temporal.PlainDate.from for dates. Real world scenario: mine the access logs One pattern with named groups turns each line into an object. Lines that do not match are counted instead of silently dropped, which tells you when the log format changes. The same idea works for test runner output, browser console logs and CI logs. Formulas Test a regex with a table of cases exportconst orderId = /^ORD-\\d{6}$/; exportconst price = /^₹(?:0|[1-9]\\d{0,2}(?:,\\d{3})*|[1-9]\\d*)(?:\\.\\d{2})?$/; Lesson: Validation patterns and testing them Tips and traps Remember When you can say exactly what is allowed, a negated class such as [^<]* or [^\"]* is clearer and much faster than .*?, because the engine never needs to backtrack.Lesson: How the regex engine matches Common mistake Never reuse a regex with the g flag for test() across different strings or calls. The second call starts at the old lastIndex and can fail on text that clearly matches. Drop the g flag for yes or no checks.Lesson: Flags in depth and the lastIndex trap Common mistake Danger signs: a quantifier inside a quantified group, such as (a+)+, (\\w*)* or (a|aa)+. Safe habits: limit input length first, prefer specific classes, and run the linter rule regexp/no-super-linear-backtracking from eslint-plugin-regexp.Lesson: ReDoS and safe patterns 5.4"],[31,"js-functional","Functional programming in JavaScript","Open chapter Write code that is simple to test: pure functions with side effects pushed to the edges, immutability and copy on change updates, currying, partial application and composition, lazy pipelines, recursion on real JSON trees, and an API validator built from tiny functions. Key ideas Pure functions and side effects A pure function returns the same output for the same input and changes nothing outside itself: no global variables, no network, no clock, no random numbers. Immutability Data that never changes cannot be broken by another part of the program. const only stops a variable from being reassigned. Object.freeze stops an object from changing, but only one level deep. Currying, partial application and composition Currying turns f(a, b, c) into f(a)(b)(c) . Fixing the first few arguments, called partial application , creates specialised functions from a general one. Composition builds a function out of smaller ones: pipe runs them left to right, compose right to left. Eager and lazy collections Array methods are eager : each step creates a full new array. Iterator helpers from ES2025 are lazy : they pass one item at a time through all steps and stop as soon as take has enough. On large data that can be thousands of times less work. Recursion on real data JSON responses, the DOM and file systems are trees, and recursion is the natural way to walk a tree. Very deep recursion can overflow the stack, and a trampoline avoids that by returning a function instead of calling it, so a loop does the calls one at a time. Real world scenario: a validator built from tiny functions Each rule is a small pure function that returns a list of problems, and shape and listOf combine them. Tips and traps Remember This copy on change style is how React, Redux and many state libraries know something changed: a new object means new data, so a simple === check is enough.Lesson: Immutability 5.5"],[31,"js-metaprogramming","Objects in depth and metaprogramming","Open chapter Change how objects behave: property descriptors, getters and setters, freeze, seal and preventExtensions, well known symbols such as toPrimitive and util.inspect.custom, Proxy and Reflect for validation, logging and retries, tagged templates for safe HTML and SQL, and decorators. Key ideas Property descriptors, getters and setters Every property has a descriptor with flags. writable allows changes, enumerable shows it in Object.keys , loops and JSON, and configurable allows deleting it or changing its flags. Accessor properties use get and set functions instead of a value. Well known symbols Built in operations look for special symbol keys on your objects. Symbol.toPrimitive controls conversion to numbers and strings, Symbol.hasInstance controls instanceof , Symbol.toStringTag the type label, and Symbol.iterator (chapter 8) makes an object ... Proxy and Reflect A Proxy sits in front of an object and can intercept almost anything done to it: reading, writing, deleting, in , listing keys, calling and new . Each handler function is a trap . Tagged template literals A tag is a function placed before a template literal. It receives the fixed text pieces and the values separately, so it can escape values, build safe queries or change the text. Decorators A decorator is a function written as @name before a class, method, field or accessor. It receives the original and can replace or wrap it. Test frameworks use decorators for steps, retries, timeouts and dependency injection. Real world scenario: a fluent API client Each property read adds a part to the path, and the verb at the end sends the request. The same trick is used by real clients for large APIs. Pair it with TypeScript types (chapter 24) so the editor suggests only valid paths. Tips and traps Remember This is why a hidden property can be on an object you are testing but missing from JSON.stringify and toEqual comparisons. Use Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors when an object behaves strangely.Lesson: Property descriptors, getters and setters Common mistake Proxies add a cost to every operation and can surprise people reading the code. Use them in tools and test frameworks, not in hot paths of application code.Lesson: Proxy and Reflect 5.6"],[31,"js-node-deep","Node.js in depth","Open chapter The parts of Node.js that power real tools: EventEmitter and async event iteration, streams, pipeline and backpressure for huge files, buffers, encodings, hashes and webhook signatures, the built in SQLite database, child processes and signals, and a tested command line tool for CI. Key ideas Events and EventEmitter Much of Node.js is built on EventEmitter : servers, sockets, streams, child processes and test runners. Listeners register with on or once , and emit calls them in order, synchronously. Streams and backpressure A stream moves data in small chunks, so a 5 GB log file never has to fit in memory. Readable streams produce data, Writable streams consume it, and Transform streams change it on the way. Buffers, encodings and crypto A Buffer is a fixed block of bytes, a Uint8Array with extra methods. Text needs an encoding to become bytes: in UTF-8, \"é\" takes 2 bytes and \"₹\" takes 3, so byte length and string length differ. The built in SQLite database Node.js ships with node:sqlite , a full SQL database that runs in memory or in a file with no server and no install. It is ideal for seeding test data, checking reports, and learning SQL alongside JavaScript. Processes, signals and graceful shutdown spawn starts another program and streams its output as it arrives, which suits long runs such as a test suite or a dev server. Programs are stopped with signals : SIGINT when you press Ctrl+C and SIGTERM when CI or Docker stops a job. Building and testing a command line tool util.parseArgs reads flags with no extra package, readline reads the file line by line, styleText adds colour that respects NO_COLOR , and the exit code tells CI the result. The bin field in package.json turns the file into a command after npm install . Tips and traps Common mistake An \"error\" event with no listener throws and can crash the process. Every emitter you create, and every stream and socket you use, needs an error listener or a pipeline that handles errors for you.Lesson: Events and EventEmitter Remember Always use prepared statements with ? placeholders. Test data such as O'Brien then works, and so does an SQL injection test that tries ' OR '1'='1.Lesson: The built in SQLite database"],[31,"step-6","Step 6Testing with JavaScript","6.1"],[31,"js-unit-testing","Unit Testing: node:test, Jest, Vitest and Mocha","Open chapter Write, run and organise unit tests with the four most used JavaScript runners. Mocks, spies and fake timers, many ways to run a suite (filters, tags, shards, reporters, coverage, CI), unit tests for web UI code with jsdom and Testing Library, and for API code with supertest and nock. Key ideas What a unit test is A unit test checks one small piece of code, usually one function or one class, on its own. It runs in milliseconds, needs no browser, server or database, and tells you exactly which piece broke. Mocks, spies, stubs and fake timers Real code calls other things: an email service, the clock, the network. A unit test swaps them for test doubles so the test is fast and predictable and never sends a real email. Jest Jest is the most used JavaScript test runner. It has matchers built in ( expect(x).toBe(y) ), table driven tests with test.each , mocks for whole modules, fake timers, snapshots and coverage. By default it uses CommonJS. Vitest and Mocha Vitest has almost the same API as Jest ( vi.fn instead of jest.fn ) but runs ES modules and TypeScript with no set up and is very fast. It is the natural choice for Vite, Vue and modern React projects. Running tests in different ways The same suite is run differently depending on the moment: everything before a merge, one file while you fix it, only smoke tests after a deploy, split across machines in CI. Every runner has options for this. Unit testing web (UI) code UI components can be unit tested without a real browser. jsdom is a browser written in JavaScript that runs inside Node.js: it has document , events and forms, but draws nothing. Unit testing API code API code has two sides to test. Incoming : your routes. supertest sends requests straight into an Express app, without opening a port, and checks the status, headers and body. Outgoing : calls your code makes to other services. Real world scenario: turn a bug report into a test First write a test that reproduces the bug and watch it fail. A failing test proves the test can see the bug. Formulas A first unit test suite with node:test exportfunction cartTotal(items, { coupon } = {}) { if (!Array.isArray(items)) thrownewTypeError(\"items must be an array\"); const subtotal = items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price * i.qty, 0); const discount = coupon === \"SAVE10\" ? subtotal * 0.1 : 0; returnMath.round((subtotal - discount) * 100) / 100; } exportfunction freeShipping(total) { return total >= 499; } Lesson: What a unit test is Step 1: a test that reproduces the bug fails { \"name\": \"bug-4711\", \"version\": \"1.0.0\", \"private\": true, \"type\": \"module\", \"scripts\": { \"test\": \"node --test --test-reporter=spec\" } } Lesson: Real world scenario: turn a bug report into a test Tables Table: double · what it does · example Double What it does Example Stub returns a fixed answer the stock service always says 7 Spy records how it was called was send called once, with which arguments Mock a stub and a spy together, often replacing a whole module jest.mock(\"./mailer\") Fake a small working version an in memory database, a fake clock Lesson: Mocks, spies, stubs and fake timers Table: matcher · passes when Matcher Passes when toBe(x) same value, or same object (Object.is) toEqual(x) same contents, deeply toMatchObject(x) contains at least these properties toBeCloseTo(x, digits) decimals are close enough toThrow(x) the function throws a matching error resolves, rejects for promises, always with await toHaveBeenCalledWith(...) a mock was called with these arguments Lesson: Jest Table: · node:test · jest · vitest node:test Jest Vitest Mocha Install nothing one package one package plus Chai and Sinon Assertions node:assert expect expect Chai Mocks mock.fn jest.fn vi.fn Sinon ES modules and TS yes needs set up yes yes Seen in libraries, small tools React, Node.js APIs Vite, Vue, new projects WebdriverIO, Selenium suites Lesson: Vitest and Mocha Table: goal · node:test · jest · vitest Goal node:test Jest Vitest Mocha one file node --test file"],[31,"js-unit-testing","Unit Testing: node:test, Jest, Vitest and Mocha","jest path vitest run path mocha file by name or tag --test-name-pattern, tags -t \"name\" -t \"name\" --grep only this one test.only with --test-only test.only test.only it.only skip or todo test.skip, test.todo test.skip, test.todo same it.skip rerun on save --watch --watch vitest (default) --watch split over machines --test-shard=1/3 --shard=1/3 --shard=1/3 by file lists stop at first failure no flag yet --bail --bail=1 --bail report for CI --test-reporter=junit jest-junit reporter --reporter=junit --reporter xunit coverage --experimental-test-coverage --coverage --coverage with c8 Lesson: Running tests in different ways Tips and traps Remember Good unit tests are fast, independent of each other and of the order they run in, repeatable on any machine, and named so that a failure explains itself: \"takes 10% off with SAVE10\" beats \"test 3\".Lesson: What a unit test is Common mistake Mock what is slow or outside your control, not the code under test itself. A test where everything is mocked only proves that the mocks work.Lesson: Mocks, spies, stubs and fake timers Common mistake A test.only committed by mistake silently skips the rest of the suite in some runners. Add a lint rule or run CI with --forbid-only (Mocha, Playwright) to block it.Lesson: Running tests in different ways Remember Passing stockService into createApp is what makes the route easy to test. Code that creates its own clients inside every function is much harder to test, so testable design starts with how dependencies are passed in.Lesson: Unit testing API code 6.2"],[31,"js-api-testing","Automated API Testing with JavaScript","Open chapter Test one real shop API with four tools: fetch and node:test for a CRUD flow, Ajv for JSON Schema checks, Vitest and supertest for negative and boundary tables, Jest for auth and stock, Playwright request with a web server, response time percentiles, and a full order flow that cleans up after itself. Key ideas What an API test checks An API test sends real HTTP requests to a running service and checks the answers. It sits in the middle of the test pyramid: much faster and steadier than a browser test, and it checks the real routes, validation, database and auth together, which unit tests ... Testing a running server: a CRUD flow The most direct API test starts the server, sends requests with fetch and checks each answer. Listening on port 0 lets the system pick a free port, so many suites can run side by side. Checking the shape with JSON Schema Checking every field by hand gets long, and it misses fields you did not think of. A JSON Schema describes the allowed shape once: required fields, types, ranges and whether extra fields are allowed. Negative, boundary and data driven tests Most API bugs hide in input the developer did not expect. Negative tests send wrong data on purpose and check that it is refused with a clear 400, not saved and not answered with a 500. A table of cases with it.each keeps dozens of them readable. Authentication, state and conflicts Real APIs need a login and change data as they go. Log in once in beforeAll , keep the token, and send it with each request. API testing with Playwright Playwright is known for browsers, but its request fixture is also a complete API client. It shares the base URL and config with UI tests, and its webServer option starts the app before the tests and stops it afterwards. Response times A correct answer that takes five seconds is still a bug. Measure each request and look at percentiles , not the average: p95 is the time under which 95% of requests finish. One very slow request barely moves the average but is exactly what a user notices. Real world scenario: buying the last two mugs Subtests with t.test name each step, so a failure says exactly where the flow broke. The last step deletes the product the test created, so the shared environment stays clean for everyone else. Tables Table: check · example Check Example Status code 201 after create, 404 for a missing id, 400 for bad input Headers Content-Type: application/json, Location, Cache-Control Body values the created product has the name that was sent, trimmed Body shape every product has an integer id and a positive price (JSON Schema) Side effects the stock really went down, the deleted item is really gone Negative cases missing fields, wrong types, boundaries, broken JSON Security 401 without a token, 403 or 404 for someone else's data Speed 95% of requests under 200 ms Lesson: What an API test checks Table: number · meaning Number Meaning p50 (median) a typical request p95, p99 the slow tail that real users hit every day error rate failed requests divided by all requests throughput requests handled per second Lesson: Response times Tips and traps Remember This chapter tests one small shop API, written with Express in shop-api.js, with four different tools. It is the same app as in chapter 13, with login and orders added.Lesson: What an API test checks Common mistake Tests that depend on each other stop at the first failure and cannot run alone. Use this style for one flow on purpose. For everything else, each test creates its own data.Lesson: Testing a running server: a CRUD flow Remember supertest's .auth(token, { type: \"bearer\" }) sets the Authorization header for you. The same suite can point at a deployed server by passing a URL string instead of the app: request(\"https://staging.shop.example\").Lesson: Authentication, state and conflicts Common mistake Times measured on a laptop or a shared CI machine vary a lot. Use generous limits in functional suites to catch big slowdowns, and leave real load"],[31,"js-api-testing","Automated API Testing with JavaScript","testing to tools such as k6 against a production like environment.Lesson: Response times 6.3"],[31,"js-ui-automation","UI Automation: Playwright, Selenium, Cypress and WebdriverIO","Open chapter Drive a real browser against a small demo shop. Playwright locators, auto-waiting assertions, strict mode, network mocking, page objects, fixtures and retries, all run for real, Selenium WebDriver with explicit waits and a page object, run for real in Chrome, plus the same tests in Cypress and WebdriverIO and a sharded CI set up. Key ideas The tools and how they drive a browser A UI automation tool starts a real browser and acts like a user: it opens pages, types, clicks and reads what is shown. The four big JavaScript tools differ in how they talk to the browser, and that explains most of their strengths and limits. Playwright: locators and web first assertions A Playwright test receives a page , finds elements with locators and checks them with web first assertions . A locator is a recipe, not an element: it is looked up again on every use, so it never goes stale. Controlling the network Playwright sees every request the page makes. page.route can answer a request with fake data or an error, so you can test states that are hard to produce for real: a broken API, an empty list, a very long name. Page objects, fixtures and retries As a suite grows, repeated steps move into page objects (chapter 9) and fixtures . A Playwright fixture prepares something a test needs, here a logged in shop, and passes it in as a parameter. Tests become short and read like a test case. Selenium WebDriver Selenium WebDriver sends each command to a driver program (chromedriver, geckodriver, safaridriver), which controls the browser. Every call is a network round trip and returns a promise, so every line is awaited. Cypress and WebdriverIO Cypress runs inside the browser, in the same loop as your app. Commands such as cy.get are not promises: Cypress queues them and runs them in order, retrying each query and assertion until it passes. Real world scenario: a UI suite in CI Sharding splits the tests across four machines. fail-fast: false lets every shard finish, so one failure does not hide others. The report is uploaded even when tests fail, and the traces inside it show exactly what the browser saw. Formulas Strict mode stops an ambiguous locator import { test, expect } from\"@playwright/test\"; test(\"a locator that matches two elements\", async ({ page }) => { await page.goto(\"/\"); await page.getByLabel(\"Email\").fill(\"asha@shop.example\"); await page.getByLabel(\"Password\").fill(\"s3cret\"); await page.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Sign in\" }).click(); await page.getByTestId(\"product\").first().getByRole(\"button\").click(); await expect(page.getByRole(\"status\")).toHaveText(\"\"); // which status? }); Lesson: Playwright: locators and web first assertions Tables Table: · playwright · selenium webdriver · cypress Playwright Selenium WebDriver Cypress WebdriverIO Talks to the browser its own protocol (CDP for Chromium, patched Firefox and WebKit) W3C WebDriver through a driver program, BiDi coming runs inside the browser next to your app WebDriver or BiDi Browsers Chromium, Firefox, WebKit every major browser, also real Safari and mobile through Appium Chromium family, Firefox, WebKit (experimental) all, plus mobile through Appium Waiting auto-waits for every action and assertion explicit waits you write retries commands and assertions auto-waits on actions Languages JS, TS, Python, Java, .NET JS, Java, Python, C#, Ruby JS, TS JS, TS Async style await everything await everything chained commands, no await await everything Extras API testing, traces, network mocking, parallel by default Grid for many machines, the longest history time travel debugger, network stubs huge plugin set, mobile and desktop apps Lesson: The tools and how they drive a browser Table: need · selenium Need Selenium Find By.id, By.css, By.xpath, By.name, By.linkText Wait until.elementLocated, elementIsVisible, elementTextIs, titleIs, urlContains Act click(), sendKeys(), clear(), driver.actions() for hover and drag Frames and windows switchTo().frame(), switchTo().window(),"],[31,"js-ui-automation","UI Automation: Playwright, Selenium, Cypress and WebdriverIO","switchTo().alert() Many machines Selenium Grid: new Builder().usingServer(gridUrl) Lesson: Selenium WebDriver Tips and traps Remember The error lists both matches and suggests a precise locator. Fix it by narrowing: getByText(/^Order \\d+ placed$/), getByRole(\"status\").filter({ hasText: \"Order\" }) or a test id.Lesson: Playwright: locators and web first assertions Common mistake Retries keep a pipeline green while you investigate, they are not a fix. With trace: \"on-first-retry\", Playwright records a trace of the retry: every action, DOM snapshot, network call and console message, which usually shows the cause in minutes.Lesson: Page objects, fixtures and retries Common mistake Avoid driver.manage().setTimeouts({ implicit }) together with explicit waits. Mixing the two makes waits add up in surprising ways. Pick explicit waits.Lesson: Selenium WebDriver"],[31,"js-ui-automation","Step 7TypeScript","7.1"],[31,"ts-basics","TypeScript fundamentals","Open chapter Start TypeScript the modern way: tsc to check and Node.js to run, a strict tsconfig, everyday types and inference, typed functions and overloads, interface and type, unions and narrowing, enums against as const objects, the strict options, and typed results from untyped JSON. Key ideas What TypeScript is and how it runs TypeScript is JavaScript with type annotations. The compiler tsc reads the types and reports mistakes before the code runs. The types are then erased : at run time it is plain JavaScript. Everyday types Most of the time TypeScript infers the type from the value, so you annotate function parameters, public APIs and empty containers, not every variable. Use readonly for data that must not change, ? for optional properties, and tuples for fixed length lists. Functions Annotate parameters, and let the return type be inferred or write it for public functions. Optional parameters use ? , defaults give both a type and a value, and rest parameters are typed as arrays. interface and type Both interface and type name an object shape. Interfaces can be extended with extends and are merged when declared twice, which suits public object shapes. Type aliases can name anything: unions, tuples, functions and combinations with & . Unions and narrowing A union such as string | number means \"one of these\". Before you use a union, you narrow it with a check that TypeScript understands: typeof , instanceof , in , equality, or a shared literal field. Inside each branch, TypeScript knows the exact type. Enums and their modern alternative An enum creates a real object at run time, so it is not erasable and fails with erasableSyntaxOnly and with Node.js type stripping. The modern alternative is a plain object marked as const plus a union type made from its values. Strict options \"strict\": true turns on a family of checks. The two that catch the most bugs are strictNullChecks , which makes you handle undefined and null , and noImplicitAny , which stops untyped parameters. Real world scenario: typed results from untyped JSON JSON.parse really returns unknown data, whatever its declared type. A type predicate such as x is Result checks it at run time and tells TypeScript the result, so everything after the filter is fully typed and the bad rows are counted instead of breaking the ... Formulas tsc finds the mistakes, Node.js runs the code anyway // TypeScript is JavaScript plus types. The types are checked by tsc and then removed.function lineTotal(price: number, qty: number): number { return price * qty; } const total = lineTotal(40, 3); const wrong = lineTotal(\"40\", 3); // a string where a number is expectedconst name: string = total; // a number where a string is expected console.log(total, wrong, name); Lesson: What TypeScript is and how it runs Tables Table: type · use it for Type Use it for string number boolean bigint symbol the primitive values null undefined \"no value\", checked strictly with strict string[] or Array<string> lists of one type [string, number] tuples: fixed positions \"a\" | \"b\" literal unions: a fixed set of allowed values unknown outside data: you must check it before use any switches checking off, avoid it never a value that cannot exist, such as the return of a function that always throws Lesson: Everyday types Tips and traps Remember Playwright, Vitest, Jest (with a transform), Cypress and WebdriverIO all accept TypeScript tests. Most new automation projects are written in TypeScript because locators, fixtures and API responses become typed and the editor completes them.Lesson: What TypeScript is and how it runs Common mistake A project without strict lets most of these bugs through, so TypeScript gives a false sense of safety. Turn it on from day one. When moving an old JavaScript project, turn on checkJs and fix files one folder at a time (chapter 25).Lesson: Strict options 7.2"],[31,"ts-advanced","Advanced TypeScript types","Open chapter The type tools experts use: generics and constraints, typeof, keyof and indexed access, the utility types, mapped and conditional types with infer, template literal types, satisfies, exhaustive checks with never, assertion functions, branded types, and API routes whose types follow the path. Key ideas Generics A generic is a type parameter, written <T> , that is filled in when the function or type is used. It keeps the connection between inputs and outputs: first of a number list is a number. A constraint with extends says what T must at least have. typeof, keyof and indexed access typeof value turns a value into a type, keyof T gives the union of its keys, and T[K] reads the type of one property. Together they let a function accept only real keys and return the right type for each one. Utility types TypeScript ships with type helpers that change existing types instead of writing new ones by hand. Mapped and conditional types A mapped type loops over keys: { [K in keyof T]: … } . It can rename keys with as and add or remove readonly and ? . Template literal types Template literal types describe patterns of strings, built from other string types. They type routes, CSS values, event names and test ids, and the compiler checks every literal you write. satisfies An annotation such as const x: Record<string, Project> replaces the type of the value with the wider annotation, so the exact keys are lost. satisfies checks the value against the type but keeps the precise type that was inferred. Exhaustive checks with never With a discriminated union, a switch on the shared field narrows each case. Passing the leftover value to a function that takes never makes the check exhaustive : add a new kind to the union and every switch that forgot it becomes a compile error. Assertion functions and branded types An assertion function with asserts value is T narrows the type after the call, or throws. Test helpers such as expect(x).toBeDefined() cannot narrow types, but your own assertion functions can. Real world scenario: typed API routes A conditional type with infer pulls :id out of the route string, and the Routes map gives the response type. A missing parameter or a typo such as stok is now a compile error. Tables Table: helper · gives Helper Gives Partial<T>Required<T> every property optional, or every property required Pick<T, K>Omit<T, K> only some keys, or all but some keys Record<K, V> an object with keys K and values V Readonly<T> every property readonly ReturnTypeParametersAwaited the return, the arguments, the value inside a promise NonNullableExcludeExtract remove null and undefined, remove or keep union members Lesson: Utility types Tips and traps Remember Generics are why Playwright's test.extend<{ cart: CartPage }>() knows the type of every fixture, and why response.json() in a typed client can return your response type (chapter 26).Lesson: Generics Common mistake Look at the leaked line: spreading an object with extra fields into a typed variable is allowed, so a password can slip into a \"public\" object. Types describe what must be there, not what must be absent. API tests must still check that secret fields are not in the response.Lesson: Utility types Remember playwright.config.ts uses defineConfig for the same reason. With your own config objects, satisfies gives typo checking and auto complete at the same time.Lesson: satisfies 7.3"],[31,"ts-projects","TypeScript classes and real projects","Open chapter TypeScript at the scale of a real project: access modifiers, abstract classes and interfaces, TypeScript private against #private, standard decorators for steps and retries, declaration files and module augmentation, checking JavaScript with JSDoc, moving a project to TypeScript, and a typed page object hierarchy. Key ideas Classes in TypeScript TypeScript adds access modifiers to classes. public is the default, protected allows subclasses, and private allows only the class itself. Decorators TypeScript 5 supports the standard decorators proposal. A method decorator is a function that receives the original method and a context object, and returns a replacement. Decorators stack: the one closest to the method wraps first. Declaration files and augmentation A declaration file ( .d.ts ) contains only types. It describes JavaScript code that has no types of its own. Most npm packages ship one, and others have one in @types/… . Checking JavaScript with JSDoc You can get most of the safety of TypeScript without renaming a single file. With checkJs , or // @ts-check at the top of a file, TypeScript reads types from JSDoc comments and reports the same errors. Moving a project to TypeScript, and the tools around it Real world scenario: a typed page object hierarchy An abstract path forces every page to declare its URL, protected el() keeps raw locators inside page objects, and returning DashboardPage from loginAs makes the next step in a test auto complete. In a real project, PageLike is Playwright's Page type. Formulas Declaration files and module augmentation // An old plain JavaScript helper with no typesexportfunction summarise(results) { const failed = results.filter(r => !r.ok).map(r => r.name); return { total: results.length, failed }; } Lesson: Declaration files and augmentation A safe path from JavaScript to TypeScript # Step 1: check JavaScript without changing it# tsconfig.json: \"allowJs\": true, \"checkJs\": false, \"strict\": false# Step 2: turn on checkJs for one folder at a time with // @ts-check at the top of files# Step 3: rename files to .ts, starting with leaf modules (utils, test data builders)# Step 4: add types to public functions, page objects and API clients# Step 5: turn on \"strict\": true, fix errors folder by folder# Step 6: add tsc --noEmit to CI so new code cannot add type errorsnpx tsc --noEmit # fails the CI job on any type errornpx tsc --noEmit --watch # keep checking while you edit Lesson: Moving a project to TypeScript, and the tools around it Tips and traps Remember This is how some frameworks add steps to reports. In Playwright the built in way is await test.step(\"Pay with card\", async () => …), and a decorator like @step can call it for you so every page object method shows up as a step in the HTML report and the trace viewer.Lesson: Decorators Remember Custom Playwright matchers are typed by augmenting PlaywrightTest.Matchers, and custom Cypress commands by augmenting Cypress.Chainable in a .d.ts file.Lesson: Declaration files and augmentation Common mistake Path aliases such as @pages/login in paths are only understood by tsc and by tools that read tsconfig. Plain Node.js does not resolve them. Use package.json\"imports\" with #pages/* instead, which works everywhere.Lesson: Moving a project to TypeScript, and the tools around it 7.4"],[31,"ts-testing","Test automation in TypeScript","Open chapter Test automation written in TypeScript: why typed tests fail faster, unit tests with node:test, Vitest and Jest, type tests with expectTypeOf, Playwright with typed page objects and fixtures, a typed API client that checks responses with zod, the type check in CI, and typed test data builders. Key ideas Why write tests in TypeScript Test code is code. It has typos, wrong arguments and missing await s, and in JavaScript you only find them when the test runs, often in CI, often after a slow browser start. TypeScript finds them while you type. Unit tests in TypeScript Node.js 24 and later remove the types and run .ts files directly, so node --test needs no build step. Vitest reads TypeScript too, without any set up. In both cases the runner only strips the types and does not check them, so run tsc as its own step. Type tests Shared helpers, builders and API clients are used by hundreds of tests, so their types are part of their contract. expectTypeOf from Vitest (and the expect-type package it is built on) asserts on types instead of values. Nothing runs. Playwright in TypeScript Playwright runs TypeScript without any set up, and most Playwright documentation is written in it. Two things become much better with types. Page objects can limit their inputs, for example to real product names. A typed API client with run time checks TypeScript types disappear at run time, so they cannot check JSON from a server. A schema library such as zod does both jobs at once. TypeScript in CI Put the type check in front of everything slow. It needs no browser and no server, and it fails in seconds. Real world scenario: typed test data builders Each builder holds the defaults in one place and gives every object a fresh id, so tests stay independent. Overrides are Partial<T> , so a test only writes the fields it cares about, and a misspelt field or a wrong role does not compile. Formulas node:test runs .ts files directly exportinterfaceLine { sku: string; price: number; qty: number } exporttypeCoupon = \"SAVE10\" | \"SAVE20\"; constRATES: Record<Coupon, number> = { SAVE10: 0.1, SAVE20: 0.2 }; exportfunction cartTotal(lines: Line[], coupon?: Coupon): number { const sum = lines.reduce((total, l) => total + l.price * l.qty, 0); returnMath.round(sum * (1 - (coupon ? RATES[coupon] : 0))); } Lesson: Unit tests in TypeScript Tips and traps Common mistake Removing types is not checking types. A test file full of type errors still runs green under node --test, Vitest or @swc/jest. Only tsc (or ts-jest) reports them. That is why every example on this page runs tsc first.Lesson: Unit tests in TypeScript Remember user is an option fixture. A project in playwright.config.ts can set use: { user: adminUser } and every test in that project logs in as the admin. TypeScript checks that the value has the right shape.Lesson: Playwright in TypeScript 7.5"],[31,"ts-type-system","How the TypeScript type system decides","Open chapter Why does one object fit and an almost identical one fail? This chapter explains the rules the checker follows: structural typing and the excess property check, literal types and widening, any, unknown and never, function compatibility and variance, index signatures with the strictest options, tuples, overloads and this, and recursive types. Key ideas Structural typing: shapes, not names In Java or C#, a class is a Reporter only if it says implements Reporter . TypeScript is structural : any value with the right members fits, whatever it is called. This matches how JavaScript code is really written, with plain objects passed around. Literal types and widening A value can have a very exact type. const m = \"GET\" has the type \"GET\" , a literal type . A let may change later, so TypeScript widens it to string . Object properties are widened too. as const stops widening and makes everything readonly. any, unknown and never any turns checking off for a value and everything you reach through it, so mistakes spread silently. unknown accepts any value too, but you must check it before use. Function compatibility and variance When is one function type usable as another? Fewer parameters is fine, because JavaScript ignores extra arguments. A callback that returns a value is fine where void is expected. Parameter types are where it gets subtle. Index signatures and the strictest options An index signature , { [key: string]: number } or Record<string, number> , describes objects used as dictionaries. By default TypeScript pretends every key exists. Two options, not included in strict , make it honest. Tuples: fixed length arrays with names A tuple is an array with a fixed length and a type per position. Elements can have labels, be optional, or be a rest of many. Overloads and this Overloads give one function several typed call signatures, so the return type follows the argument type. Recursive types A type may refer to itself. That is how trees are described: JSON, file systems, menus, nested config. Recursive conditional types go further and compute types such as every dot path in an object. Tables Table: type · accepts · you can use it as Type Accepts You can use it as any everything anything, with no checks unknown everything nothing, until you narrow it never nothing anything, since it cannot happen Lesson: any, unknown and never Table: option · effect Option Effect noUncheckedIndexedAccess obj[key] and arr[i] may be undefined exactOptionalPropertyTypes retries?: number may be missing, but not set to undefined Lesson: Index signatures and the strictest options Tips and traps Remember The excess property check is a typo detector, not a security feature. As the advanced chapter showed with the leaked password, a value from a variable may carry extra fields. API tests must still check that secret fields are absent.Lesson: Structural typing: shapes, not names Remember Methods written as method(x: T): void in an interface are still checked loosely (bivariantly) for compatibility with old code. Writing them as properties, method: (x: T) => void, gets the strict check.Lesson: Function compatibility and variance Remember Prefer a union parameter or a generic when they are enough. Overloads are best when different inputs give clearly different outputs, as in parse above or document.createElement.Lesson: Overloads and this 7.6"],[31,"ts-config-modules","TypeScript project setup: tsconfig, modules and builds","Open chapter Most TypeScript problems at work are not about types but about setup: imports that cannot be found, enums that Node refuses, an old tsconfig that TypeScript 7 rejects. This chapter covers tsconfig in depth, ES modules and module resolution, type only imports, #/ subpath imports, erasable syntax for running .ts files directly, declaration merging, isolatedDeclarations and project references. Key ideas tsconfig.json in depth Every TypeScript project is driven by tsconfig.json . It answers four questions: which files to check ( include , exclude , files ), how strict to be, what JavaScript to produce, and how imports are found. ES modules and module resolution The module and moduleResolution options tell tsc which rules the real runtime will use to find files. With nodenext it copies Node exactly: ES module imports need the file extension, and packages are found through their exports field. Subpath imports instead of ../../.. Deep test folders end up with imports like ../../../lib/price . Node's subpath imports fix that with an imports field in package.json, and both Node and tsc understand it without extra tools. Erasable syntax and running .ts files directly Node.js 23.6 and later run .ts files directly by replacing types with spaces, without checking them. That only works for TypeScript features that leave no JavaScript behind. Declaration merging and namespaces TypeScript merges some declarations that share a name. Two interface blocks become one, which is how libraries let you add fields to their types. A function and a namespace can merge too, giving a function with extra properties. isolatedDeclarations and fast declaration files A library publishes .d.ts files that describe its exports. Normally only tsc can write them, because it must infer return types. Project references for big repositories In a large repository with many packages, checking everything every time is slow. Project references split the code into projects that build separately and in order. tsc -b (build mode) checks only what changed since the last build. Formulas An old option that TypeScript 7.0 no longer accepts const ok = 1; console.log(ok); Lesson: tsconfig.json in depth Subpath imports that start with #/ (TypeScript 6.0) { \"name\": \"shop-tests\", \"type\": \"module\", \"imports\": { \"#/*\": \"./src/*\" } } Lesson: Subpath imports instead of ../../.. Tables Table: group · options you will meet Group Options you will meet Files include, exclude, rootDir, outDir Checking strict, noUncheckedIndexedAccess, exactOptionalPropertyTypes, noImplicitOverride Output target, declaration, sourceMap, noEmit Modules module, moduleResolution, verbatimModuleSyntax, paths Environment lib, types, skipLibCheck Sharing extends (a base config, or several since 5.0), references Lesson: tsconfig.json in depth Table: runs in · module · moduleresolution Runs in module moduleResolution Node.js nodenext (or node20 since 5.9) follows module Vite, webpack, esbuild esnext or preserve bundler Lesson: ES modules and module resolution Tips and traps Remember This is also why the earlier chapters prefer as const objects over enums. They behave the same, work in Node, Deno and Bun without a build step, and the JavaScript output is exactly what you wrote.Lesson: Erasable syntax and running .ts files directly 7.7"],[31,"ts-whats-new","What is new in TypeScript: 5.0 to 7.0","Open chapter TypeScript changes quickly, and each release removes a little more friction. This chapter walks from TypeScript 5.0 to 7.0: const type parameters, using for automatic cleanup, import attributes, NoInfer, filters that narrow by themselves, regex and truthiness checks, the ES2025 and Temporal types of 6.0, and the native, parallel TypeScript 7 compiler, each checked by the real compiler and run on Node.js. Key ideas Three years of TypeScript in one page TypeScript ships a new version every few months, and each brings small features that save real time. This chapter goes release by release from 5.0 (2023) to 7.0 (2026). Every example is checked by the TypeScript 7 compiler and run on Node.js 26. TypeScript 5.0: const type parameters Before 5.0, a generic function that should keep exact strings needed callers to write as const . A const type parameter does it for every caller. 5.0 also introduced standard decorators, covered in the projects chapter. TypeScript 5.2 and 5.3: using and import attributes using is explicit resource management from a JavaScript proposal that TypeScript supports since 5.2 and Node.js runs natively. When the block ends, normally or by an error, the object's [Symbol.dispose]() runs. TypeScript 5.4 and 5.5: smarter inference NoInfer<T> marks an argument that must match T but must not help decide what T is. Without it, a wrong default such as \"safari\" would simply widen T to include it. 5.4 also keeps narrowing inside callbacks created after the last change to a variable. TypeScript 5.6 to 5.9: fewer silent mistakes 5.6 reports conditions that can never be false, such as a function used without calling it. This is a classic bug in test helpers: if (isLoggedIn) is always true. 5.6 also typed the iterator helper methods such as filter , map and toArray on iterators. TypeScript 6.0: the bridge release TypeScript 6.0 (March 2026) is the last version of the compiler written in JavaScript. TypeScript 7.0: the native compiler TypeScript 7.0 is the compiler ported to Go. The language is the same, but builds are typically 8 to 12 times faster, and the VS Code code base went from 125.7 seconds to 10.6 seconds. Checking can run on several CPU cores. Formulas Import attributes for JSON test data (5.3) // TypeScript 5.3: import attributes tell the runtime what kind of file it is loadingimport users from\"./users.json\"with { type: \"json\" }; const admins = users.filter((u) => u.role === \"admin\"); // the JSON's shape is inferred console.log(admins.map((u) => u.name)); console.log(users[0]?.email.toUpperCase()); console.log(users[0]?.phone); // no such field in the file Lesson: TypeScript 5.2 and 5.3: using and import attributes TypeScript 7.0, the native compiler import { a } from\"./a.ts\"; exportconst b: string = a; Lesson: TypeScript 7.0: the native compiler Tables Table: version · headline features Version Headline features 5.0 standard decorators, const type parameters, extends with several configs, moduleResolution bundler, verbatimModuleSyntax 5.1 to 5.3 unrelated getter and setter types, using declarations, decorator metadata, import attributes 5.4 and 5.5 NoInfer, narrowing in closures, inferred type predicates, regex checking, isolatedDeclarations 5.6 and 5.7 always truthy checks, iterator helper types, never initialised variables, rewriteRelativeImportExtensions 5.8 and 5.9 erasableSyntaxOnly, require of ES modules under nodenext, import defer, module node20, a short tsc --init 6.0 new defaults, target es2025, Temporal and upsert types, #/ subpath imports, many deprecations 7.0 native compiler in Go, parallel checking, deprecated options removed Lesson: Three years of TypeScript in one page Table: new in 7.0 · use New in 7.0 Use --checkers N split type checking across N workers --builders N build N referenced projects at the same time --singleThreaded turn parallelism off, for debugging or small CI machines Lesson: TypeScript 7.0: the native compiler"],[31,"step-8","Step 8Expert and latest","8.1"],[31,"js-modern","Modern JavaScript: ES2020 to ES2027","Open chapter What every yearly edition added and why it matters: optional chaining and nullish coalescing, change by copy arrays, groupBy, iterator helpers and Set methods, RegExp.escape and Promise.try, the new using declarations for automatic clean up, Temporal for dates and time zones, the ES2026 edition with upsert and base64, DisposableStack, and how to check for the features that are finished but not shipped yet. Key ideas How JavaScript grows every year Every June, Ecma publishes a new edition of the standard: ES2020, ES2021 and so on. A feature joins only after it reaches stage 4 at TC39, which needs two shipping browser engines and a full test suite. ES2020 to ES2023: safer access and copies Optional chaining and nullish coalescing removed most of the \"cannot read properties of undefined\" crashes from everyday code. The ES2023 change by copy methods return a new array and leave the original alone, which makes test data safe to reuse. ES2024 and ES2025: grouping, iterators, safer regexes Grouping a list, a promise you resolve from outside, lazy iterator helpers, real set operations and a safe way to put user text into a regular expression all arrived in the last two editions. ES2026 and ES2027: using, Error.isError, upsert and more The June 2026 edition, ES2026, is a collection of small tools that remove common helper functions from real projects: Temporal: dates done right The old Date object mixes up time zones, months that start at 0 and objects that change in place. What comes next, and how to check before you use it A feature you read about may not exist where your code runs. Old phones, a company laptop with an old browser, or a CI image with an older Node.js all lag behind. Professionals do not guess from version numbers. Real world scenario: modernise safely The rewrite is a third of the size. Running both versions on the same inputs, including an empty list, and comparing the results is a simple form of characterisation testing : it proves the behaviour stayed the same before you delete the old code. Formulas Import attributes for JSON // ES2025: import attributes and JSON modulesimport config from\"./playwright.settings.json\"with { type: \"json\" }; const { default: users } = awaitimport(\"./users.json\", { with: { type: \"json\" } }); Lesson: ES2024 and ES2025: grouping, iterators, safer regexes Tables Table: edition · highlights you will meet in real code Edition Highlights you will meet in real code ES2015 (ES6) let and const, arrow functions, classes, modules, promises, template literals, destructuring, Map and Set ES2017 async and await, Object.entries, padStart ES2020 ?., ??, BigInt, Promise.allSettled, globalThis, dynamic import ES2021 ||=&&=??=, replaceAll, Promise.any, numeric separators, WeakRef ES2022 class fields and #private, top level await, .at(), Object.hasOwn, error cause ES2023 findLast, toSorted, toReversed, toSpliced, with ES2024 Object.groupBy, Map.groupBy, Promise.withResolvers, the v regex flag ES2025 iterator helpers, Set methods, RegExp.escape, Promise.try, import attributes, Float16Array ES2026 (June 2026) Error.isError, Array.fromAsync, Uint8Array base64 and hex, Map and WeakMap getOrInsert (upsert), Iterator.concat, JSON.parse source text and JSON.rawJSON, Math.sumPrecise Finished for ES2027 using and await using with DisposableStack, Temporal, Atomics.pause, iterator chunks and windows, Iterator.zip, iterator includes and join Lesson: How JavaScript grows every year Table: proposal · stage in october 2026 · what it would give you Proposal Stage in October 2026 What it would give you Deferring module evaluation (import defer) 3 Load a module at start up but run its code only when you first use it, so apps start faster Source phase imports, import text 3 Import a WebAssembly module or a text file directly Decorators 2.7 Standard @decorator syntax. TypeScript already supports it (chapter 18) Math.clamp 2 Keep a number between a minimum and a maximum Lesson: What comes next, and how to check"],[31,"js-modern","Modern JavaScript: ES2020 to ES2027","before you use it Tips and traps Remember Check support on caniuse.com or MDN's compatibility tables, and set your TypeScript target and lint rules to match the oldest browser or Node.js you support.Lesson: How JavaScript grows every year Common mistake new RegExp(userText) breaks or misbehaves when the text contains ( ) . ? $. RegExp.escape fixes that, which matters for tests that search a page for product names or prices.Lesson: ES2024 and ES2025: grouping, iterators, safer regexes Remember Follow new features on the TC39 proposals page on GitHub, MDN's browser compatibility tables and the V8 blog. A proposal at stage 3 is safe to learn, stage 4 is safe to plan for, and \"ships in every browser you support\" is safe to use without a fallback.Lesson: What comes next, and how to check before you use it 8.2"],[31,"js-expert","Expert JavaScript: performance, security and test quality","Open chapter The habits that separate experts: choosing the right algorithm and measuring it fairly, finding memory leaks, design patterns for apps and tests, worker threads, XSS and prototype pollution, property based and mutation testing, flaky tests, and a faster CI pipeline. Key ideas Performance: count first, then measure Most slow JavaScript is slow because of the algorithm, not the engine. A loop inside a loop over the same list does about n² ÷ 2 steps, while a Set lookup does about n . Count steps to compare ideas, and measure time only to confirm. Memory: leaks and weak references JavaScript frees memory that nothing can reach any more. A leak happens when something you forgot still points at old data: a listener that was never removed, a cache that only grows, or a timer that keeps running. Patterns experts reach for A pattern is a known shape for a common problem. These four appear in application code and in test frameworks alike. Workers: real parallel work JavaScript runs your code on one thread, so a long calculation blocks everything else. Worker threads in Node.js and Web Workers in the browser run code on separate threads and talk to the main thread with messages. Security every JavaScript developer should test Cross site scripting (XSS) happens when user text is turned into HTML. The safe defaults are textContent , framework templates that escape by default, and a Content Security Policy header that blocks inline scripts. Test quality: properties, mutants and flaky tests Example based tests check the inputs you thought of. Property based testing generates hundreds of inputs and checks a rule that must always hold. Libraries such as fast-check also shrink a failure to the smallest input. The seed makes every failure repeatable. Real world scenario: a faster CI pipeline Splitting by position put the slow checkout and payments files on the same machine. Sorting by duration and filling the lightest shard first cuts the wait by more than three minutes with the same three machines. Formulas Cross site scripting: innerHTML against textContent <h2>Reviewsh2> <divid=\"unsafe\">div> <divid=\"safe\">div> Lesson: Security every JavaScript developer should test Tables Table: risk · what to check Risk What to check XSS Type <img src=x onerror=alert(1)> into every field and check it shows as text Secrets No API keys in the browser bundle or in git. Read them from environment variables eval No eval or new Function on user input Dependencies Run npm audit, keep a lock file and use npm ci in CI Auth Every API checks the token and the role, not just the UI (chapter 21) Cookies Session cookies use HttpOnly, Secure and SameSite Lesson: Security every JavaScript developer should test Tips and traps Remember In the browser, the Performance panel of DevTools shows long tasks, layout work and memory over time. Lighthouse and the Core Web Vitals (LCP, INP and CLS) are what users actually feel, and Playwright can read them with page.evaluate during a test.Lesson: Performance: count first, then measure Remember Unique values from a counter or crypto.randomUUID() keep tests that run in parallel from clashing over the same email or order id.Lesson: Patterns experts reach for Remember The fix for the test above is to reset the cart in a beforeEach and let each test add its own items. Track flaky tests in a list, fix them quickly, and never leave retries on to hide them.Lesson: Test quality: properties, mutants and flaky tests"],[32,"","Interview Questions","JavaScript and TypeScript questions from developer and automation interviews: types, closures, this, prototypes, the event loop, promises, TypeScript generics and narrowing, unit and API tests, Playwright and framework design, with output questions run on Node.js. · interview · interview questions · viva · FAQ · job preparation · var let const · hoisting · closure · this · event loop · microtask · Promise.all · debounce · deep clone · interface vs type · any vs unknown · generics · utility types · mocks and spies · auto waiting · flaky tests · fixtures JavaScript · Interview Questions 🎤"],[32,"","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","The JavaScript and TypeScript questions asked in developer and automation tester interviews, from types, scope and closures to the event loop, TypeScript generics, unit tests, API tests and Playwright. Every output question was run with Node.js and the printed result is the one shown in the answer. ✍️ Read the question and write your own answer in the box, as you would say it in the interview. 💡 Stuck? Open the hint. It points you in the right direction without giving the answer away. ✅ Open the answer to compare. You get the model answer, key points and a detailed explanation. 🎯 Rate yourself. Your notes and ratings stay in this browser, so you can filter to the ones to review. 🎤"],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","Write your answer first, as you would say it to the interviewer. Open the hint only if you are stuck, then compare with the model answer and the explanation. 0/86known ⭐ Most important topics for interviews 🔢 Types and equality == against ===, truthy and falsy values, typeof null and floating point maths open most rounds. 🧩 Scope and closures var, let and const, hoisting, the temporal dead zone and closures in loops are asked at every level. 🎯 this and prototypes How this is decided, arrow functions, bind, and how classes sit on top of prototypes. ⏳ Async and the event loop Promises, async and await, microtasks against macrotasks, and output questions that mix them. 🔷 TypeScript interface against type, any against unknown, generics, narrowing and utility types. 🧪 Unit and API tests Mocks, spies, fake timers, Jest against Vitest, and checking API responses. 🎭 Playwright Auto waiting, locators, fixtures, page objects, network mocking and flaky tests. 🧠 Coding rounds Debounce, deep clone, flatten, group by, retry with backoff and promise helpers written from scratch. 0 of 86 answers opened, 0 known, 0 to review Types and values Q1 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptTypes and values What is the difference between var, let and const? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Compare three things: scope, hoisting and whether you can assign again. Your answer Model answer var is function scoped, is hoisted and starts as undefined, and can be declared twice. let and const are block scoped and sit in the temporal dead zone until their line runs. let can be assigned again, const cannot, although an object held in a const can still be changed. var: function scope, hoisted as undefined let: block scope, can be reassigned const: block scope, no reassignment, contents can still change Detailed explanation Use const by default and let when the value really changes, such as a counter. Avoid var in new code. A const only fixes the binding, not the value. const user = { name: \"Asha\" } followed by user.name = \"Ravi\" works. Use Object.freeze if the object itself must not change. Follow up questions the interviewer may ask What is the temporal dead zone? Why does var in a for loop with setTimeout print the same number? How did you do? Q2 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptTypes and values What is the difference between == and ===? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One of them converts types before it compares. Your answer Model answer === is strict equality. It is true only when the type and the value are the same. == is loose equality. It converts the values to a common type first, so \"5\" == 5, 0 == \"\" and null == undefined are all true. Detailed explanation Use === everywhere. The one common exception is value == null, which is a short way to test for both null and undefined. NaN is not equal to anything, not even itself, with either operator. Use Number.isNaN(x) or Object.is(x, NaN). Follow up questions the interviewer may ask What does Object.is do differently from ===? How did you do? Q3 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptTypes and values Name the primitive types in JavaScript. What does typeof null return and why? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first There are seven primitives. One typeof result is a famous old bug. Your answer Model answer The primitives are string, number, bigint, boolean, undefined, symbol and null. Everything else is an object, including arrays and functions. typeof null returns \"object\", which is a bug from the first version of JavaScript that was kept for compatibility. 7 primitives: string, number, bigint, boolean, undefined, symbol, null typeof null is \"object\", a historic bug Use Array.isArray for arrays Detailed explanation To check for null, compare directly with x === null. To check for an array, use Array.isArray(x), since typeof [] is also \"object\". typeof a function gives \"function\". Primitives are copied by value and are immutable. Objects are passed as references, so two variables can point at the same object. How did you do? Q4 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptTypes and values"],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","Which values are falsy in JavaScript? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first There are eight of them, and an empty array is not one. Your answer Model answer false, 0, -0, 0n, \"\", null, undefined and NaN. Every other value is truthy, including \"0\", \"false\", [] and {}. Detailed explanation This matters in tests that read values from the page. if (count) is false for a count of 0, which is often a real value. Compare against what you mean, for example count !== undefined. How did you do? Q5 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptTypes and values What is the temporal dead zone, and what does this code print? console.log(a); var a = 1; console.log(b); let b = 2; ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first let is hoisted too. Think about what happens when you touch it before its line. Your answer Model answer It prints undefined for a and then throws ReferenceError: Cannot access 'b' before initialization. Both declarations are hoisted, but var a starts as undefined while let b stays uninitialised until its line runs. The time between the start of the scope and that line is the temporal dead zone. Detailed explanation The dead zone exists so that mistakes show up as errors instead of silent undefined values. It applies to let, const and class declarations. Even typeof b throws inside the dead zone, although typeof of an undeclared name returns \"undefined\". How did you do? Q6 🌿 Medium🖨️ OutputTypes and values What does this print? console.log(\"1\" + 2); console.log(\"1\" - 2); console.log(+\"1\" + 2); console.log([] == false); console.log(null == 0); console.log(\"a\" * 2); ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Plus joins text if either side is a string. Minus always converts to numbers. Your answer Model answer It prints 12, -1, 3, true, false and NaN on separate lines. Detailed explanation \"1\" + 2 joins text and gives \"12\". \"1\" - 2 converts to numbers and gives -1. +\"1\" + 2 converts first with the unary plus, so it is 3. [] == false is true because both sides become the number 0. null == 0 is false because null is only loosely equal to undefined. \"a\" * 2 cannot be a number, so it is NaN. 12 -13truefalseNaN How did you do? Q7 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptTypes and values What is the difference between || and ?? when setting a default? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One looks at falsy values, the other only at two specific values. Your answer Model answer a || b returns b when a is any falsy value. a ?? b returns b only when a is null or undefined. So 0 || 10 is 10 but 0 ?? 10 is 0. Detailed explanation Use ?? for config and test data where 0, an empty string or false are valid values, for example const retries = options.retries ?? 2. With ||, a deliberate retries: 0 would turn into 2. ??= assigns only when the left side is null or undefined. How did you do? Q8 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptTypes and values Why is 0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3 false, and how do you compare money in tests? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Numbers are stored in binary floating point. Your answer Model answer All numbers are 64 bit IEEE 754 floating point values, and 0.1 and 0.2 have no exact binary form, so the sum is 0.30000000000000004. Compare with a tolerance such as Math.abs(a - b) < Number.EPSILON, or keep money in whole paise or cents as integers. Detailed explanation Test frameworks have helpers for this: expect(total).toBeCloseTo(0.3) in Jest and Vitest. For money, the safest design is integers in the smallest unit, or BigInt for very large values, and formatting only for display with Intl.NumberFormat. Integers are exact up to Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER, which is 2 to the power 53 minus 1. How did you do? Q9 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptTypes and values What is the difference between a shallow copy and a deep copy? How do you make each? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Think about what happens to nested objects. Your answer Model answer A shallow copy creates a new top level object but shares the nested objects, for example { ...obj } or Object.assign({}, obj). A deep copy copies every level. Use structuredClone(obj),"],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","which also handles dates, maps, sets and circular references. Detailed explanation JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj)) is an old trick for deep copies, but it drops undefined and functions, turns dates into strings and fails on circular references. In tests, a shallow copy of shared test data is a common source of tests that affect each other. One test changes user.address.city and the next test sees the change. Follow up questions the interviewer may ask What can structuredClone not copy? How did you do? Q10 🔥 Hard⌨️ CodingTypes and values Write a function deepClone for plain objects and arrays without using structuredClone. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Recursion: copy primitives as they are, and build a new array or object for the rest. Your answer Model answer Return primitives and null as they are. For an array, map each item through deepClone. For an object, create a new object and copy each own key through deepClone. Detailed explanation A follow up is circular references. Keep a WeakMap from originals to copies, and return the stored copy when you see the same object again. For dates, maps and sets you would add special cases, which is exactly what structuredClone already does. function deepClone(value, seen = newWeakMap()) { if (value === null || typeof value !== \"object\") return value; if (seen.has(value)) return seen.get(value); const copy = Array.isArray(value) ? [] : {}; seen.set(value, copy); for (const key ofObject.keys(value)) copy[key] = deepClone(value[key], seen); return copy; } How did you do? Functions and closures Q11 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptFunctions and closures What is a closure? Give a real use. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first A function remembers something from where it was created. Your answer Model answer A closure is a function together with the variables from the scope where it was created. The inner function keeps access to those variables even after the outer function has returned. Uses include private state, counters, memoisation, and factories such as a function that builds a logger with a fixed prefix. Detailed explanation Example: function counter() { let n = 0; return () => ++n }. Each call to counter() creates a new n that only the returned function can reach. In test code, a helper such as makeUser() with an id counter inside a closure gives every test a unique id without a global variable. Follow up questions the interviewer may ask Can closures cause memory leaks? How did you do? Q12 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptFunctions and closures What is the difference between call, apply and bind? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Two of them call the function now. One returns a new function. Your answer Model answer call(thisArg, a, b) calls the function now with arguments listed one by one. apply(thisArg, [a, b]) calls it now with an array of arguments. bind(thisArg, a) does not call it. It returns a new function with this and any given arguments fixed. Detailed explanation Today the spread syntax covers most uses of apply: Math.max(...numbers). bind is still common for event handlers and callbacks that need a fixed this. How did you do? Q13 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptFunctions and closures What is a pure function and why do testers like them? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Same input, same output, and nothing else changes. Your answer Model answer A pure function always returns the same output for the same input and has no side effects. It does not change its arguments, global state, files or the network. Pure functions are easy to test because you only check the return value, with no set up or mocks. Detailed explanation A good design keeps the logic pure and pushes side effects to the edges. For example, a calculateTotal(lines, coupon) function is pure, and a separate function reads the cart from the API and calls it. How did you do? Q14 🌿 Medium🖨️ OutputFunctions and closures What does this print, and how do you fix it? const out = []; for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) setTimeout(() => out.push(i)); for (let j ="],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","0; j < 3; j++) setTimeout(() => out.push(j)); setTimeout(() => console.log(out.join(\" \"))); ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first How many i variables are there with var, and how many with let? Your answer Model answer It prints 3 3 3 for the var loop and 0 1 2 for the let loop. With var there is one i for the whole loop, and by the time the timers run it is 3. With let each pass of the loop gets its own j. Detailed explanation Before let existed the fix was an IIFE that passed i as a parameter. Today, use let. The timers run after the loop because setTimeout callbacks are tasks, which wait until the current script has finished. 333012 How did you do? Q15 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptFunctions and closures How is the value of this decided inside a function? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first It depends on how the function is called, not where it is written. Arrow functions are the exception. Your answer Model answer For a regular function, this is set by the call. obj.method() gives obj, a plain call gives undefined in strict mode, new gives the new object, and call, apply and bind set it explicitly. Arrow functions have no own this and use the one from the surrounding code. Method call: the object before the dot Plain call: undefined in strict mode new: the new object call, apply, bind: what you pass Arrow: this from the outer scope Detailed explanation A classic bug is passing a method as a callback: setTimeout(page.click, 100) loses page. Fix it with an arrow function, setTimeout(() => page.click(), 100), or with page.click.bind(page). Do not use arrow functions as object methods when they need this, because they will not get the object. How did you do? Q16 🌿 Medium⌨️ CodingFunctions and closures Write a debounce(fn, ms) function and say where you would use it. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Each call cancels the previous timer and starts a new one. Your answer Model answer Keep a timer in a closure. Every call clears the old timer and starts a new one, so fn runs only when the calls stop for ms milliseconds. Use it for search boxes, window resize handlers and auto save. Detailed explanation Throttle is the related helper. It runs fn at most once every ms milliseconds, which suits scroll handlers. To test a debounced function, use fake timers: vi.useFakeTimers(), call it several times, vi.advanceTimersByTime(300), then assert it ran once. function debounce(fn, ms) { let timer; returnfunction (...args) { clearTimeout(timer); timer = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), ms); }; } Follow up questions the interviewer may ask Write throttle How would you unit test debounce? How did you do? Q17 🌿 Medium⌨️ CodingFunctions and closures Write a memoize function that caches results by argument. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first A Map inside a closure, keyed by the argument. Your answer Model answer Keep a Map in a closure. On each call, return the cached result if the key is present, otherwise compute it, store it and return it. Detailed explanation For several arguments, build a key such as JSON.stringify(args), which works for simple values. Only memoise pure functions, since a cached result must not depend on time or outside state. Caches that never shrink can grow without limit. Real code often uses an LRU cache with a maximum size. function memoize(fn) { const cache = newMap(); return arg => { if (!cache.has(arg)) cache.set(arg, fn(arg)); return cache.get(arg); }; } How did you do? Q18 🔥 Hard🖨️ OutputFunctions and closures What does this print? \"use strict\"; const cart = { name: \"cart\", regular() { returnthis?.name; }, later() { const arrow = () => this.name; return arrow(); }, }; console.log(cart.regular()); const f = cart.regular; console.log(f()); console.log(cart.later()); ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Check each call: with a dot, without a dot, and the arrow function. Your answer Model answer It prints cart, undefined and cart. Detailed explanation cart.regular() is a method call, so this is cart. const f = cart.regular"],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","copies the function without the object, and in a module or strict mode a plain call has this as undefined, so this?.name is undefined. later() uses an arrow function created inside regular, so it keeps the this of that call, which was cart. cart undefined cart How did you do? Q19 🔥 Hard⌨️ CodingFunctions and closures Write curry(fn) so that curry(add3)(1)(2)(3) and curry(add3)(1, 2)(3) both return 6. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Collect arguments until you have fn.length of them. Your answer Model answer Return a function that gathers arguments. When it has at least fn.length arguments, call fn with them. Otherwise return another function that adds more arguments to the ones so far. Detailed explanation fn.length is the number of declared parameters, not counting rest or default parameters. Currying is used to build small configured helpers, such as const getJson = request(\"GET\"). function curry(fn) { returnfunction curried(...args) { return args.length >= fn.length ? fn.apply(this, args) : (...more) => curried.apply(this, [...args, ...more]); }; } How did you do? Objects and classes Q20 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptObjects and classes What is the prototype chain? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Where does JavaScript look when a property is not on the object itself? Your answer Model answer Every object has a hidden link to another object, its prototype. When you read a property that the object does not have, JavaScript looks at the prototype, then at its prototype, and so on until it reaches null. Methods of a class live on ClassName.prototype and are shared by all instances through this chain. Detailed explanation Object.getPrototypeOf(obj) reads the link and Object.create(proto) makes a new object with a chosen prototype. obj.hasOwnProperty(\"x\"), or better Object.hasOwn(obj, \"x\"), tells you if a property is on the object itself. Writing a property always creates it on the object itself. It never changes the prototype. Follow up questions the interviewer may ask How do classes relate to prototypes? How did you do? Q21 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptObjects and classes Are JavaScript classes real classes as in Java? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first What does a class create behind the scenes? Your answer Model answer A class is mostly cleaner syntax over constructor functions and prototypes. class Page {} creates a function, and its methods go on Page.prototype. There are real differences too: classes always run in strict mode, cannot be called without new, are in the temporal dead zone before their line, and support #private fields and static blocks. Detailed explanation Inheritance with extends links the prototypes, so LoginPage.prototype has BasePage.prototype as its prototype. super.method() calls the parent version. Unlike Java, there is no method overloading. A second method with the same name replaces the first. How did you do? Q22 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptObjects and classes What is the difference between a #private field and a TypeScript private field? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One is checked by the engine at run time, the other only by the compiler. Your answer Model answer A #private field is enforced by the JavaScript engine. Code outside the class cannot read it, and it does not show up in Object.keys or JSON.stringify. TypeScript private is only a compile time check. After the types are removed it is an ordinary property that anyone can read. Detailed explanation Use #private for values that must not leak, such as tokens in an API client. TypeScript private is fine for keeping a clean public interface inside a team. How did you do? Q23 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptObjects and classes When would you use a Map instead of a plain object? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Think about key types, order, size and inherited keys. Your answer Model answer Use a Map when keys are not strings, such as objects or numbers, when keys are added and removed often, when you need the size, or when keys come from users. A Map keeps insertion"],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","order, has size, and has no inherited keys like constructor. Use plain objects for fixed records such as a user or a config, and for JSON. Detailed explanation WeakMap holds its keys weakly, so it does not stop them from being garbage collected. It is useful for caching data about DOM elements or objects you do not own. Set stores unique values, which is the quickest way to remove duplicates: [...new Set(list)]. How did you do? Q24 🔥 Hard🖨️ OutputObjects and classes What does this print? const config = Object.freeze({ city: \"Pune\", address: { city: \"Pune\" } }); config.city = \"Delhi\"; config.address.city = \"Mumbai\"; console.log(config.city); console.log(config.address.city); ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first freeze is shallow. Your answer Model answer It prints Pune and Mumbai. Detailed explanation Object.freeze blocks changes to the top level properties only. The assignment to config.city is ignored (in strict mode it would throw a TypeError). config.address still points to an ordinary object that is not frozen, so its city changes. To freeze every level, write a deep freeze that calls Object.freeze on every nested object. PuneMumbai How did you do? Arrays and strings Q25 🌱 Easy🖨️ OutputArrays and strings What does this print? console.log([10, 1, 25, 2].sort()); console.log([10, 1, 25, 2].sort((a, b) => a - b)); ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Without a compare function, sort compares items as text. Your answer Model answer It prints [ 1, 10, 2, 25 ] and then [ 1, 2, 10, 25 ]. Detailed explanation The default sort converts items to strings and compares them by UTF-16 code units, so \"10\" comes before \"2\". Pass a compare function such as (a, b) => a - b for numbers. sort changes the array in place. toSorted() returns a new sorted array and leaves the original unchanged. [ 1, 10, 2, 25 ] [ 1, 2, 10, 25 ] How did you do? Q26 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptArrays and strings Explain map, filter and reduce with one test related example each. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One changes each item, one keeps some items, one combines them into one value. Your answer Model answer map returns a new array with each item changed, for example results.map(r => r.name). filter returns the items that pass a test, for example results.filter(r => !r.passed). reduce combines all items into one value, for example results.reduce((ms, r) => ms + r.duration, 0). Detailed explanation None of them change the original array. Always give reduce a starting value, otherwise it throws on an empty array. forEach returns nothing and is for side effects. find returns the first match and some and every return booleans. How did you do? Q27 🌱 Easy⌨️ CodingArrays and strings Remove duplicates from an array of numbers. Then remove duplicate users by email. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first A Set for simple values, a Map keyed by email for objects. Your answer Model answer For simple values use [...new Set(list)]. For objects, build a Map keyed by email and take its values: [...new Map(users.map(u => [u.email, u])).values()], which keeps the last user for each email. Detailed explanation To keep the first one instead, filter with a Set of emails seen so far. Both approaches are O(n), compared with O(n squared) for filter with indexOf. const unique = [...newSet([3, 1, 3, 2, 1])]; // [3, 1, 2]function uniqueBy(list, key) { const seen = newSet(); return list.filter(item => !seen.has(item[key]) && seen.add(item[key])); } How did you do? Q28 🌱 Easy⌨️ CodingArrays and strings Reverse the words of a sentence, and check whether a string is a palindrome ignoring case and spaces. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first split, reverse, join. Your answer Model answer Words: s.trim().split(/\\s+/).reverse().join(\" \"). Palindrome: clean the string with s.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]/g, \"\"), then compare it with its reverse. Detailed explanation For strings with emoji or accented letters, split(\"\") breaks characters apart. [...s] splits by code points, which is safer, and"],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","Intl.Segmenter splits by what a reader sees as one character. const reverseWords = s => s.trim().split(/\\s+/).reverse().join(\" \"); function isPalindrome(s) { const clean = s.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]/g, \"\"); return clean === [...clean].reverse().join(\"\"); } How did you do? Q29 🌱 Easy⌨️ CodingArrays and strings Split an array of 10 test files into chunks of 4 for parallel workers. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first A loop that steps by the chunk size and slices. Your answer Model answer Loop from 0 in steps of size and push list.slice(i, i + size). Ten files in chunks of 4 give chunks of 4, 4 and 2. Detailed explanation Real test runners shard by time, not by count, so that each worker finishes at about the same moment. Playwright uses --shard=1/3, and Jest and Vitest have the same option. function chunk(list, size) { const out = []; for (let i = 0; i < list.length; i += size) out.push(list.slice(i, i + size)); return out; } How did you do? Q30 🌿 Medium⌨️ CodingArrays and strings Flatten a nested array such as [1, [2, [3, [4]]]] without using flat. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Recursion with reduce, or a stack. Your answer Model answer Reduce over the array. When an item is an array, flatten it recursively and add its items, otherwise add the item itself. The built in answer is arr.flat(Infinity). Detailed explanation For very deep arrays a recursive solution can overflow the stack. A loop with an explicit stack avoids that: pop an item, push the children of arrays back on the stack, and collect the rest. function flatten(arr) { return arr.reduce((out, item) => Array.isArray(item) ? out.concat(flatten(item)) : out.concat([item]), []); } // flatten([1, [2, [3, [4]]]]) gives [1, 2, 3, 4] How did you do? Q31 🌿 Medium⌨️ CodingArrays and strings Group test results by status so that you get an object such as { passed: [...], failed: [...] }. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first There is a built in for this since ES2024, and you can also write it with reduce. Your answer Model answer Use Object.groupBy(results, r => r.status), or write it with reduce: for each result, push it into the array for its status and create the array the first time. Detailed explanation Map.groupBy does the same but returns a Map, which is useful when the keys are not strings. Object.groupBy returns an object with a null prototype. const byStatus = Object.groupBy(results, r => r.status); function groupBy(list, keyOf) { return list.reduce((groups, item) => { (groups[keyOf(item)] ??= []).push(item); return groups; }, {}); } How did you do? Q32 🌿 Medium⌨️ CodingArrays and strings Find the first character that does not repeat in \"automation\". ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Count first, then walk the string again. Your answer Model answer Count every character in a Map, then loop over the string again and return the first character with a count of 1. For \"automation\" the answer is \"u\". Detailed explanation This is O(n) time. A nested loop that checks every character against every other one would be O(n squared). A Map keeps insertion order, so you could also loop over the map entries. function firstUnique(s) { const count = newMap(); for (const c of s) count.set(c, (count.get(c) ?? 0) + 1); for (const c of s) if (count.get(c) === 1) return c; returnnull; } How did you do? Async and the event loop Q33 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptAsync and the event loop Explain the event loop, microtasks and macrotasks. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One thread, one call stack, and two queues with different priorities. Your answer Model answer JavaScript runs on one thread with one call stack. When the stack is empty, the event loop first runs every microtask, such as promise callbacks and queueMicrotask, then takes one macrotask, such as a timer or an I/O callback, runs it, and empties the microtask queue again. That is why a resolved promise callback always runs before a setTimeout(fn, 0). Call stack runs to completion All microtasks run before the next task Timers"],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","and I/O are tasks Rendering happens between tasks Detailed explanation The browser renders between tasks, so a long task or an endless chain of microtasks freezes the page. In Node.js, process.nextTick callbacks run even before promise callbacks. For testers this explains why awaiting is required: an action that returns a promise has not finished when the next line starts. How did you do? Q34 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptAsync and the event loop What is the difference between Promise.all, allSettled, race and any? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Ask for each one: when does it settle, and what happens when one promise fails? Your answer Model answer all resolves with every value when all succeed and rejects as soon as one fails. allSettled always waits for all of them and gives a status and value or reason for each. race settles with the first promise to settle, success or failure. any resolves with the first success and rejects with an AggregateError only if all fail. Detailed explanation In tests, use all for set up calls that must all work, allSettled to clean up many resources and report every failure, and race to add a timeout. A common Playwright pattern uses Promise.all to start waiting for a response and click at the same time, although const r = page.waitForResponse(...) before the click is clearer. How did you do? Q35 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptAsync and the event loop How long does each version take, roughly? const wait = ms => newPromise(r => setTimeout(r, ms)); asyncfunction sequential() { await wait(100); await wait(100); } asyncfunction parallel() { awaitPromise.all([wait(100), wait(100)]); } ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Where does each version start the second wait? Your answer Model answer sequential takes about 200 ms because the second wait starts only after the first ends. parallel takes about 100 ms because both waits start before either is awaited. Detailed explanation Start independent work first and await later, or use Promise.all. In API test set up, creating ten users in parallel can be ten times faster than awaiting each one in turn, as long as the server can handle it. How did you do? Q36 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptAsync and the event loop How do you handle errors in async code? What happens with an unhandled rejection? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first try and catch work with await. A promise nobody catches is a different story. Your answer Model answer With await, wrap the code in try and catch, just like synchronous code. With promise chains, add .catch(). A rejected promise that nobody handles fires an unhandledrejection event in the browser, and in Node.js 15 and later it crashes the process by default. Detailed explanation A try block only catches errors from promises that are awaited inside it. try { doAsync() } catch {} without await catches nothing. In test runners an unhandled rejection usually fails the run, sometimes in a different test from the one that caused it, which makes it hard to trace. How did you do? Q37 🔥 Hard🖨️ OutputAsync and the event loop What does this print? console.log(1); setTimeout(() => console.log(2), 0); Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(3)).then(() => console.log(5)); console.log(4); ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Synchronous code first, then all microtasks, then timers. Your answer Model answer It prints 1, 4, 3, 5, 2. Detailed explanation 1 and 4 are synchronous. Then the microtask queue runs: the first then logs 3, and the then chained on it is queued as a new microtask and logs 5 before any timer. Only after that does the timer callback log 2. 14352 How did you do? Q38 🔥 Hard🌍 ScenarioAsync and the event loop Why does await inside forEach not wait, and what do you use instead? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first What does forEach do with the promise your callback returns? Your answer Model answer forEach ignores the return value of its callback, so the promises from the async callbacks are thrown away and the code after forEach runs at once. Use for...of with"],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","await to run one after another, or await Promise.all(items.map(async item => ...)) to run them in parallel. Detailed explanation This bug is common in test clean up code: users are deleted in a forEach, the test ends before the requests finish, and the next test sees leftover data. The no-floating-promises lint rule from typescript-eslint catches some forms of it. How did you do? Q39 🔥 Hard⌨️ CodingAsync and the event loop Write retry(fn, { attempts, delay }) that retries an async function with exponential backoff. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first A loop with try and catch, and a wait that doubles each time. Your answer Model answer Loop up to attempts times. Return the result when fn succeeds. On failure, rethrow if it was the last attempt, otherwise wait delay * 2 ** i milliseconds and try again. Detailed explanation Only retry errors that can be temporary, such as a 503 or a network reset, and never a 400. Add random jitter to the delay so that many clients do not retry at the same moment. In tests, retrying hides real problems. Prefer waiting for a condition, such as Playwright expect.poll, over retrying whole actions. asyncfunction retry(fn, { attempts = 3, delay = 100 } = {}) { for (let i = 0; i < attempts; i++) { try { returnawait fn(); } catch (err) { if (i === attempts - 1) throw err; awaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, delay * 2 ** i)); } } } How did you do? Q40 🔥 Hard⌨️ CodingAsync and the event loop Add a timeout to any fetch call, and write a general withTimeout(promise, ms). ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first For fetch there is a ready made signal. For any promise, race it against a timer. Your answer Model answer For fetch, pass signal: AbortSignal.timeout(5000), which aborts the request after 5 seconds with a TimeoutError. For any promise, use Promise.race with a promise that rejects after ms, and clear the timer when the race ends. Detailed explanation The difference matters: aborting a fetch really stops the request, while racing only stops waiting for it, and the work carries on in the background. function withTimeout(promise, ms) { let timer; const timeout = newPromise((_, reject) => { timer = setTimeout(() => reject(newError(`timed out after ${ms} ms`)), ms); }); returnPromise.race([promise, timeout]).finally(() => clearTimeout(timer)); } How did you do? Q41 🧠 Expert🖨️ OutputAsync and the event loop What does this print? asyncfunction helper() { console.log(\"B\"); } asyncfunction run() { console.log(\"A\"); await helper(); console.log(\"C\"); } run().then(() => console.log(\"D\")); console.log(\"E\"); ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first An async function runs synchronously until its first await. Your answer Model answer It prints A, B, E, C, D. Detailed explanation Calling run() logs A right away, then helper() logs B. The await pauses run and gives control back, so the script logs E. In the microtask queue, the rest of run continues and logs C, and the then on the promise that run() returned logs D once run has finished. ABECD How did you do? Q42 🧠 Expert🖨️ OutputAsync and the event loop In Node.js, what does this print? setTimeout(() => console.log(\"timeout\"), 0); Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"promise\")); process.nextTick(() => console.log(\"nextTick\")); console.log(\"sync\"); ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Node.js has an extra queue that runs even before promise callbacks. Your answer Model answer It prints sync, nextTick, promise, timeout. Detailed explanation After the synchronous code, Node.js empties the process.nextTick queue first, then the promise microtask queue, then moves on to the timers phase of the event loop. The order between setTimeout(fn, 0) and setImmediate in the main script is not guaranteed, which is why this example does not use setImmediate. sync nextTick promise timeout How did you do? Q43 🧠 Expert⌨️ CodingAsync and the event loop Write your own promiseAll(list) that behaves like Promise.all. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Keep results in order of the input, not in"],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","order of finishing, and count how many are done. Your answer Model answer Return a new promise. For each item, wrap it with Promise.resolve, store its value at its own index, and count finished items. Resolve when the count reaches the length, reject on the first failure, and resolve at once for an empty list. Detailed explanation Using the index rather than push is the key detail, because promises finish in any order. Promise.resolve lets the list contain plain values as well as promises. function promiseAll(list) { returnnewPromise((resolve, reject) => { const results = []; let done = 0; if (list.length === 0) return resolve(results); list.forEach((item, i) => { Promise.resolve(item).then(value => { results[i] = value; if (++done === list.length) resolve(results); }, reject); }); }); } How did you do? Engine and runtime Q44 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptEngine and runtime What is hoisting? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Declarations are set up before the code runs. Which ones get a value right away? Your answer Model answer Before code runs, the engine creates every declared name in its scope. Function declarations are hoisted with their body, so you can call them before their line. var names are hoisted as undefined. let, const and class are hoisted but stay in the temporal dead zone until their line. Detailed explanation Function expressions and arrow functions assigned to variables follow the rule of the variable, so const f = () => {} cannot be called before its line. How did you do? Q45 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptEngine and runtime What is the difference between cookies, localStorage and sessionStorage? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Think of lifetime, size and whether the server sees them. Your answer Model answer Cookies are small, are sent to the server with every request and can be HttpOnly so that JavaScript cannot read them, which makes them right for session tokens. localStorage holds a few megabytes per origin until it is cleared. sessionStorage is the same but lasts only for one tab. Neither storage is sent to the server. Detailed explanation In UI tests you can skip the login screen by setting storage or cookies. Playwright saves both with storageState after one login and reuses them in every test. How did you do? Q46 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptEngine and runtime What is the difference between ES modules and CommonJS? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first import against require, and when each one is resolved. Your answer Model answer CommonJS uses require and module.exports, loads files synchronously and is Node.js only. ES modules use import and export, are the standard for browsers and Node.js, are resolved before the code runs, support top level await and always run in strict mode. Exports in ES modules are live bindings, not copies. Detailed explanation In Node.js, a file is an ES module when it ends in .mjs or the nearest package.json has \"type\": \"module\". Modern Node.js can also require() an ES module that has no top level await. In ES modules there is no __dirname. Use import.meta.dirname instead. How did you do? Q47 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptEngine and runtime If Node.js is single threaded, how does it handle many requests at once? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Who does the waiting while JavaScript keeps running? Your answer Model answer Your JavaScript runs on one thread, but slow operations such as network and file access are handed to the operating system or to the libuv thread pool. While they run, the event loop keeps serving other requests, and the callbacks run on the main thread when the results arrive. CPU heavy work blocks everything, so it belongs in worker_threads or a separate process. Detailed explanation This is why Node.js suits I/O heavy servers and test tools that drive browsers and APIs, and why a synchronous loop that runs for two seconds stops every other request for those two seconds. How did you do? Q48 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptEngine and runtime What is event delegation, and what are bubbling and capturing? ✍️"],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","Step 1. Write your answer first Events travel down to the target and back up. Your answer Model answer An event first travels from the window down to the target (capturing), then back up to the window (bubbling). Event delegation puts one listener on a parent and uses event.target.closest(selector) to find which child was clicked. It works for items added later and needs fewer listeners. Detailed explanation addEventListener(type, fn, { capture: true }) listens in the capturing phase. event.stopPropagation() stops the event travelling further, which can break delegated handlers and analytics, so use it with care. How did you do? Q49 🌿 Medium🌍 ScenarioEngine and runtime What is CORS, and why does an API call work in Postman but fail in the browser? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Who enforces the rule, the server or the browser? Your answer Model answer CORS is a browser rule. A page can only read a response from another origin if that server allows it with headers such as Access-Control-Allow-Origin. For some requests the browser first sends an OPTIONS preflight. Postman and Node.js are not browsers, so they do not apply CORS, which is why the same call works there. Detailed explanation The server has to fix it by sending the right headers for the right origins. A wildcard origin cannot be combined with credentials. When testing, check the preflight response as well as the real one. How did you do? Q50 🔥 Hard🌍 ScenarioEngine and runtime What causes memory leaks in JavaScript, and how would you find one? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first The garbage collector frees what can no longer be reached. What keeps things reachable by mistake? Your answer Model answer Common causes are global variables, timers and intervals that are never cleared, event listeners that are never removed, caches and maps that only grow, closures that hold large objects, and DOM nodes removed from the page but still referenced in JavaScript. Find them with heap snapshots in Chrome DevTools or node --inspect, comparing snapshots taken before and after repeating an action. Detailed explanation In long test runs, a leak in the test code itself, such as a listener added in every beforeEach, can slow the suite down until it times out. WeakMap, WeakRef and AbortController for removing listeners help avoid leaks. How did you do? Modern JavaScript Q51 🌱 Easy🖨️ OutputModern JavaScript What does this print? const user = { id: 1, nick: null }; console.log(user.address?.city); const { name = \"Guest\", nick = \"none\" } = user; console.log(name); console.log(nick); ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Optional chaining stops at null or undefined. Destructuring defaults apply only to undefined. Your answer Model answer It prints undefined, Guest and null. Detailed explanation user.address?.city stops at the missing address and gives undefined instead of throwing. In the destructuring, name is missing, so the default \"Guest\" is used. nick is null, which is a real value, so its default is not used. undefinedGuestnull How did you do? Q52 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptModern JavaScript Name some recent JavaScript features you have used. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Think of array methods that do not change the array, grouping, sets and promises. Your answer Model answer Good examples are structuredClone, at(-1), Object.hasOwn, toSorted and toReversed that return new arrays, Object.groupBy, Promise.withResolvers, set methods such as union and intersection, iterator helpers such as .map and .take on iterators, and using declarations for clean up. Detailed explanation Interviewers mostly want to see that you follow the language and that you know which runtime supports what. Mention how you check, for example MDN browser tables and the Node.js version in CI. How did you do? Q53 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptModern JavaScript What is a generator, and where could it help in test code? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first A function that can pause and resume, giving one value at a time. Your"],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","answer Model answer A generator function, written function*, returns an iterator. Each yield hands out one value and pauses the function until the next value is asked for. It is useful for lazy or endless sequences, such as unique test emails, and async generators with for await are good for reading paged API results one page at a time. Detailed explanation Example: function* ids() { let n = 1; while (true) yield n++ } gives a new id every time you call next(), without building an endless list. How did you do? TypeScript Q54 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptTypeScript What is TypeScript, and what does it change at run time? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Ask what is left after the code is compiled. Your answer Model answer TypeScript is JavaScript with a static type system. The compiler checks your code and then removes the types, so at run time it is ordinary JavaScript and the types change nothing. Its value is finding mistakes before the code runs, plus better auto complete and safer refactoring. Detailed explanation Because the types are removed, they cannot check data that arrives at run time, such as API responses or JSON files. That needs a run time check, for example a zod schema. Node.js 24 and later can run .ts files directly by removing the types, but it does not check them. tsc does the checking. How did you do? Q55 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptTypeScript What is the difference between interface and type? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Both describe object shapes. Which one can be reopened, and which one can describe unions? Your answer Model answer Both can describe objects and both can be extended. An interface can be declared again to add members, which is called declaration merging, and is how libraries let you add custom matchers or globals. A type alias can name any type, including unions, tuples, primitives, mapped types and conditional types. Detailed explanation A common team rule is interface for object shapes and public APIs, and type for unions and computed types. The choice rarely matters much. Being consistent matters more. How did you do? Q56 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptTypeScript Explain any, unknown and never. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One turns checking off, one forces you to check first, and one has no values at all. Your answer Model answer any turns type checking off for that value, so anything is allowed. unknown also accepts any value, but you must narrow it, for example with typeof or a schema, before you use it. never is the type with no values. It is the return type of a function that always throws, and the type left after every case of a union has been handled. any: no checks, spreads silently unknown: safe, must narrow before use never: no possible value, used for exhaustive checks Detailed explanation Use unknown for data from outside, such as JSON.parse results, caught errors and API bodies. Use never for exhaustive checks: in the default branch of a switch, const check: never = value fails to compile if someone adds a new union member and forgets to handle it. How did you do? Q57 🌿 Medium⌨️ CodingTypeScript What is type narrowing? Show a user defined type guard. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first TypeScript follows your if statements. Your answer Model answer Narrowing is how TypeScript refines a broad type inside a branch, using checks such as typeof, instanceof, in, equality with a literal, and Array.isArray. A user defined type guard is a function that returns value is SomeType, so calling it in an if narrows the value. Detailed explanation A discriminated union, where every member has a literal kind or status field, is the cleanest pattern: a switch on that field narrows each case automatically. interface ApiError { status: number; message: string } function isApiError(x: unknown): x is ApiError { returntypeof x === \"object\" && x !== null && \"status\"in x && \"message\"in x; } try { await api.createUser() } catch (e) { if (isApiError(e)) console.log(e.status) } How did you do? Q58 🌿 Medium⌨️"],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","CodingTypeScript What are generics? Write a typed first function and a typed API helper. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first A type parameter is like a function parameter, but for types. Your answer Model answer Generics let a function, class or type work with many types while keeping the link between input and output. function first<T>(list: T[]): T | undefined returns a string for a string array and a User for a user array. Constraints such as T extends { id: number } limit which types are allowed. Detailed explanation In test code, a helper such as getJson<T>(url) lets each test say what it expects. Note that this only tells the compiler what to assume. Combine it with a schema if the data really needs checking. function first<T>(list: T[]): T | undefined { return list[0]; } asyncfunction getJson<T>(url: string): Promise<T> { const res = await fetch(url); if (!res.ok) thrownewError(`${res.status} for ${url}`); return res.json() as Promise<T>; } const user = await getJson<{ id: number; name: string }>(\"/api/users/1\"); How did you do? Q59 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptTypeScript Name the utility types you use most and what each one does. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Think of making fields optional, picking some, dropping some and building a lookup. Your answer Model answer Partial<T> makes every field optional, Required<T> makes every field required, Readonly<T> blocks changes, Pick<T, K> keeps some fields, Omit<T, K> drops some, Record<K, V> builds an object type with given keys, and ReturnType, Parameters and Awaited read types from functions and promises. Detailed explanation In tests, Partial<User> is the type for overrides in a test data builder, and Omit<User, \"id\"> is the body of a create request where the server sets the id. How did you do? Q60 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptTypeScript Should you use an enum or a union of string literals? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One of them creates code at run time. Your answer Model answer A union such as type Status = \"passed\" | \"failed\" | \"skipped\" exists only at compile time, works with plain JSON strings and is removed completely. An enum creates a real object at run time. Many teams prefer unions, or an as const object when they also need the values at run time. Detailed explanation Node.js type stripping and the erasableSyntaxOnly option reject enums, because they cannot simply be removed. Numeric enums also accept any number, which is a known weak spot. An as const object gives both: const Status = { Passed: \"passed\", Failed: \"failed\" } as const and type Status = (typeof Status)[keyof typeof Status]. How did you do? Q61 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptTypeScript What does strict: true turn on, and which part matters most? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Most of the value comes from one check about null. Your answer Model answer strict turns on a group of checks, including strictNullChecks, noImplicitAny, strictFunctionTypes, strictPropertyInitialization and useUnknownInCatchVariables. strictNullChecks matters most: without it, null and undefined can go anywhere and the most common run time error is not caught. Detailed explanation noUncheckedIndexedAccess is not part of strict but is worth adding. It makes list[0] possibly undefined, which matches reality. On an existing project, turn on strict and use // @ts-expect-error on old lines to fix them over time, rather than waiting until everything is clean. How did you do? Q62 🌿 Medium🖨️ OutputTypeScript This TypeScript compiles. What does it print when run? classSession { constructor() { this.token = \"secret\"; } } // In TypeScript the class reads: class Session { private token = \"secret\" }const s = newSession(); console.log(Object.keys(s)); console.log(s.token); ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Types are removed. Which one is the real run time check? Your answer Model answer It prints [ 'token' ] and then secret. Detailed explanation TypeScript private is removed with the types, so token is an ordinary property that Object.keys lists. The"],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","cast to any only silences the compiler. A #token field would be hidden from Object.keys and could not be read from outside at all. [ 'token' ] secret How did you do? Q63 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptTypeScript What do keyof, typeof and indexed access types do? What is the type of Env here? const config = { dev: { url: \"http://localhost:3000\" }, staging: { url: \"https://staging.shop.test\" }, prod: { url: \"https://shop.test\" }, }; type Env = keyof typeof config; ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first typeof reads the type of a value, and keyof lists the keys of a type. Your answer Model answer typeof config gives the type of the value config. keyof gives a union of its keys. So Env is \"dev\" | \"staging\" | \"prod\". An indexed access type such as (typeof config)[\"dev\"] reads the type of one property. Detailed explanation This keeps lists of allowed values in one place. Adding a new environment to the object automatically adds it to the Env type, so a test run with --env=qa can be checked against it. How did you do? Q64 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptTypeScript What does satisfies do, and how is it different from a type annotation? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Check the value against a type, but keep what TypeScript learned about the value. Your answer Model answer value satisfies Type checks that the value matches the type but keeps its own, more exact type. A type annotation const x: Type = value replaces the exact type with Type. With satisfies, a config object is checked against Record<Env, Config> and you still get auto complete for each key. Detailed explanation Example: const routes = { login: \"/login\", cart: \"/cart\" } satisfies Record<string, string>. A misspelt key is still caught when used, because routes keeps its two exact keys. With an annotation, routes.anything would be allowed. How did you do? Q65 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptTypeScript Does this compile? Why? interface Credentials { email: string; password: string } function login(c: Credentials) {} const input = { email: \"a@shop.test\", password: \"x\", remember: true }; login(input); login({ email: \"a@shop.test\", pasword: \"x\" }); ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first TypeScript compares shapes, not names. Fresh object literals get an extra check. Your answer Model answer The first call compiles and the second does not. TypeScript is structurally typed, so input is accepted because it has every field that Credentials needs, and the extra field is fine. A fresh object literal passed directly gets an excess property check, so the misspelt pasword is reported. Detailed explanation Structural typing means two types with the same shape are interchangeable even if they have different names. The excess property check only applies to object literals written right where the type is expected, which is where typos are most likely. How did you do? Q66 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptTypeScript What is a .d.ts file, and how do you add a custom Playwright matcher or Cypress command to the types? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Types for code that has none, and adding to an interface that already exists. Your answer Model answer A declaration file contains only types. It describes JavaScript that has no types of its own, and most packages ship one or have one in @types. To add to an existing type, use declaration merging: reopen the interface inside declare global or declare module, for example Cypress.Chainable for a custom command, or the matchers interface for a custom Playwright expect. Detailed explanation Playwright also has expect.extend({...}), which returns a new expect with your matchers fully typed, so many teams no longer need to write the declaration by hand. How did you do? Q67 🔥 Hard🌍 ScenarioTypeScript The API returns JSON. Is const user = (await res.json()) as User safe? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Who checks the JSON at run time? Your answer Model answer No. The as assertion only tells the compiler to trust you. Nothing checks the data, so if the API sends price as a string, the code compiles and fails"],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","later in a confusing place. Parse the body with a schema library such as zod: const user = UserSchema.parse(await res.json()), and get the type from the same schema with z.infer. Detailed explanation For API testing this is a big win. One schema checks every response in every test, and the error message names the exact field that broke the contract. How did you do? Q68 🧠 Expert📘 ConceptTypeScript What are mapped types and conditional types? What does infer do? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One loops over keys. The other is an if statement for types. Your answer Model answer A mapped type builds a new object type by looping over keys, for example { [K in keyof T]: T[K] | null }. A conditional type picks a type based on a check, T extends U ? X : Y. Inside a conditional type, infer captures part of a type, for example T extends Promise<infer V> ? V : T unwraps a promise. Detailed explanation Most built in utility types are written this way: Partial is a mapped type, and ReturnType uses infer. Conditional types distribute over unions, so NonNullable<string | null> is string. A practical use is building the type of every page object method name that returns a promise, so a step logger can only wrap real async methods. How did you do? Unit and API testing Q69 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptUnit and API testing Jest or Vitest: what is the difference and which would you pick? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Same style of API. Think about modules, TypeScript and speed. Your answer Model answer Both have the same describe, it and expect style and similar mocks. Vitest is built on Vite, supports ES modules and TypeScript with no set up and is usually faster in watch mode. Jest has the larger existing user base and needs a transformer such as ts-jest or SWC for TypeScript and extra steps for ES modules. For a new project, Vitest is a common pick. Detailed explanation Node.js also has a built in runner, node:test, which needs no package at all and suits small libraries and API test suites. How did you do? Q70 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptUnit and API testing What is the difference between a mock, a stub and a spy? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One watches, one replaces with canned answers, one also checks how it was called. Your answer Model answer A spy wraps a real function and records calls without changing what it does. A stub replaces a function and returns canned values. A mock is a replacement that also carries expectations about how it is called, such as how many times and with which arguments. In Jest and Vitest, vi.fn() and jest.fn() can act as all three, and vi.spyOn(obj, \"method\") creates a spy. Detailed explanation Mock at the edges of your code, such as the network, the clock and random numbers, not internal functions. Too many mocks make tests pass while the real code is broken. Reset mocks between tests with vi.restoreAllMocks() or the restoreMocks config option. How did you do? Q71 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptUnit and API testing How do you test code that uses setTimeout or the current date without waiting? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Take control of the clock. Your answer Model answer Use fake timers. vi.useFakeTimers() or jest.useFakeTimers() replaces the timer functions and Date. Then vi.advanceTimersByTime(5000) runs everything due in the next 5 seconds instantly, and vi.setSystemTime(new Date(\"2026-01-01\")) fixes the date. Call vi.useRealTimers() afterwards. Detailed explanation With async code, use the async versions such as advanceTimersByTimeAsync, so promise callbacks between timers also run. Playwright has the same idea for the browser with page.clock. How did you do? Q72 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptUnit and API testing What do you check in an API test besides the status code? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Think of the whole response and what changed on the server. Your answer Model answer Check the status code, the response time, important headers such as content-type and caching, the body against a schema, the exact values"],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","that matter, and the side effect, for example by reading the created item back. Also cover negative cases: missing and invalid fields, wrong types, unauthorised and forbidden users, not found, and duplicates. Detailed explanation In JavaScript, API tests are often written with Playwright request, supertest for Express apps, or plain fetch with node:test or Vitest. Keep tests independent by creating their own data and cleaning it up. How did you do? Q73 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptUnit and API testing How do you write an API test in Playwright, and why mix API calls into UI tests? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first There is a built in fixture that shares cookies with the browser. Your answer Model answer Use the request fixture: const res = await request.post(\"/api/users\", { data: user }), then expect(res).toBeOK() and expect(await res.json()).toMatchObject({...}). In UI tests, use API calls to create test data and log in quickly, and keep the browser only for the part you are really testing. Detailed explanation page.request shares cookies with the page, so data created through it belongs to the logged in user. This cuts minutes from large suites and makes UI tests less flaky. How did you do? Q74 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptUnit and API testing How do you unit test a function that calls fetch? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Replace the network, not the function under test. Your answer Model answer Make the network predictable. Options are stubbing the global with vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", vi.fn().mockResolvedValue(new Response(JSON.stringify(data)))), intercepting requests with a library such as MSW or nock, or passing the fetch function in as a parameter. Then assert on the result and on the request that was sent. Detailed explanation MSW is often the best choice because it intercepts at the network level, so the real fetch code runs, and the same handlers can be reused in the browser and in component tests. Test the error paths too: a 500, a timeout and invalid JSON. How did you do? Playwright and UI automation Q75 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptPlaywright and UI automation What is auto waiting in Playwright, and why are sleeps a bad idea? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Actions and assertions both wait, but in different ways. Your answer Model answer Before an action such as click, Playwright waits until the element is attached, visible, stable, enabled and receives events. Web first assertions such as await expect(locator).toHaveText(\"Paid\") retry until they pass or time out. A fixed sleep either wastes time or is too short on a slow day, which makes tests slow and flaky. Detailed explanation Never use page.waitForTimeout in real tests. Wait for something you can see or a response you expect, such as page.waitForResponse(\"**/api/orders\"). A common mistake is expect(await locator.textContent()).toBe(\"Paid\"), which reads once and does not retry. How did you do? Q76 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptPlaywright and UI automation Which locators do you prefer in Playwright, and why? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Locators that a user would recognise. Your answer Model answer Prefer user facing locators: getByRole with an accessible name, getByLabel, getByPlaceholder and getByText. Use getByTestId where there is no good accessible name. Avoid long CSS or XPath paths tied to the page structure, which break when the layout changes. Detailed explanation Locators are lazy and strict. They find the element again at each action, and an action fails if a locator matches more than one element, which surfaces ambiguous selectors early. Narrow with filter({ hasText }) or by chaining from a parent. How did you do? Q77 🌿 Medium🏗️ DesignPlaywright and UI automation How do you design page objects in a TypeScript Playwright framework? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Keep locators and actions in one place, keep assertions in tests. Your answer Model answer Create one class per page or component that takes the Playwright Page, defines its locators as readonly fields and exposes"],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","actions in business language, such as login(user) or addToCart(product). Keep most assertions in the tests. Return the next page object from actions that navigate, and provide page objects to tests through fixtures. Detailed explanation Types help a lot: a Product union means addToCart(\"Pencil\") does not compile, and returning DashboardPage from login makes the next step auto complete. Prefer small component objects, such as a header or a cart, over one huge page object. How did you do? Q78 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptPlaywright and UI automation What are Playwright fixtures, and how are they better than beforeEach? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Tests ask for what they need by name. Your answer Model answer A fixture is set up code that a test requests by name in its parameters, such as { page, request, shop }. You add your own with test.extend<{ shop: ShopPage }>({...}). Each fixture sets up, hands its value to the test with await use(value) and cleans up after. Fixtures run only for tests that use them, can depend on other fixtures, can be scoped to the worker, and are fully typed. Detailed explanation With beforeEach, every test in the file pays for every set up step, and values are passed through shared variables. Option fixtures, declared with { option: true }, can be changed per project in the config, for example to run the same suite as an admin and as a customer. How did you do? Q79 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptPlaywright and UI automation How do you mock or change network responses in Playwright? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Intercept the route. Your answer Model answer Use page.route(pattern, handler). The handler can route.fulfill({ json }) to return fake data, route.abort() to simulate a network failure, or route.fetch() followed by fulfill to change a real response. This lets you test empty lists, errors and slow responses that are hard to create on a real server. Detailed explanation Use page.waitForResponse to assert that a request was made, and routeFromHAR to replay recorded traffic. Keep at least some tests against the real backend, since mocked tests cannot find contract changes. How did you do? Q80 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptPlaywright and UI automation How does Playwright run tests in parallel, and what do your tests need to be safe in parallel? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Workers, isolation, and data. Your answer Model answer Playwright runs test files in parallel worker processes, and with fullyParallel: true it also splits tests inside a file. Each test gets a fresh browser context, so cookies and storage are isolated. Tests must still not share server side data: each should create its own users and orders, or use unique names, and must not depend on the order of tests. Detailed explanation Use --shard=1/4 to split the suite across CI machines and merge the reports afterwards. Use test.describe.configure({ mode: \"serial\" }) only when tests really cannot be separated. How did you do? Q81 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptPlaywright and UI automation How do you avoid logging in through the UI in every test? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Log in once and save what the browser remembers. Your answer Model answer Log in once in a set up project, save the cookies and storage with page.context().storageState({ path }), and point the other projects at it with use: { storageState: path }. Keep one real UI login test so the login page itself is still covered. Detailed explanation For different roles, save one state file per role and create a fixture or project for each. If the session expires during long runs, log in through the API in the set up project, which is faster than the UI. How did you do? Q82 🔥 Hard🌍 ScenarioPlaywright and UI automation A UI test passes locally but fails in CI one time in ten. How do you investigate and fix it? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Gather evidence first, then look at timing, data and environment. Your answer Model answer First collect evidence: turn on traces, screenshots and videos on"],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","failure, and run the test many times, for example with --repeat-each=20 and several workers, to reproduce it. Then check the usual causes: missing awaits, reading values instead of using web first assertions, shared test data between parallel tests, order dependence, animations, and timing differences such as a slower CI machine. Fix the cause and keep retries only as a safety net. Detailed explanation The Playwright trace viewer shows every action, the DOM before and after, network calls and console logs, which usually points to the cause quickly. Track flaky tests over time and quarantine with a ticket rather than ignoring them. Retries that hide flakiness let real bugs through. How did you do? Scenarios and design Q83 🌿 Medium🌍 ScenarioScenarios and design This test passes, but the feature is broken. Why? test(\"order is paid\", async ({ page }) => { await page.goto(\"/orders/42\"); expect(page.getByTestId(\"status\")).toHaveText(\"Paid\"); }); ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first What does the test function return before the check has finished? Your answer Model answer The assertion is not awaited. expect(...).toHaveText() returns a promise, so the test function finishes before the check runs, and the failure is either lost or reported against another test. Add await before the assertion. Detailed explanation Missing awaits are one of the most common causes of false passes and flaky tests. The typescript-eslint rule no-floating-promises and the Playwright lint rule missing-playwright-await catch them in review, before they reach CI. How did you do? Q84 🔥 Hard🏗️ DesignScenarios and design Design a JavaScript or TypeScript test automation framework for a web shop. What layers and folders would it have? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Separate what you test, how you reach the app, and the data. Your answer Model answer Use TypeScript with Playwright Test. Layers: tests organised by feature, page and component objects, an API client layer used for set up and API tests, fixtures that wire everything together, typed test data builders, config per environment, and utilities. Add lint and tsc --noEmit as a fast CI gate, tagging for smoke and regression, parallel runs with sharding, and HTML reports with traces on failure. Detailed explanation A typical layout is tests/, pages/, api/, fixtures/, data/, config/ and playwright.config.ts. Explain the reasons as well as the folders: API set up for speed, fixtures for isolation, types to catch mistakes early, and schemas to catch contract changes. Follow up questions the interviewer may ask How would you add visual tests? How do you run it against several environments? How did you do? Q85 🔥 Hard🌍 ScenarioScenarios and design Your team has a 600 file JavaScript test suite. How would you move it to TypeScript without stopping work? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Small steps that each keep the build green. Your answer Model answer Add a tsconfig with allowJs and checkJs off, then turn on checkJs or // @ts-check folder by folder and fix what it finds. Rename shared helpers and page objects to .ts first, since every test benefits. Then rename test files over time, turn on strict with @ts-expect-error on old problems, and run tsc --noEmit in CI from day one so nothing gets worse. Detailed explanation Avoid a big bang rewrite. Agree that new files are TypeScript, track the remaining JavaScript files as a number that only goes down, and add JSDoc types where a rename is not worth it yet. How did you do? Q86 🔥 Hard🌍 ScenarioScenarios and design How would you test a search box that waits 300 ms after typing before it calls the API? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Two levels: the unit with fake timers, and the page with network checks. Your answer Model answer Unit level: with fake timers, call the debounced handler several times, advance time by 299 ms and assert no call, then advance 1 ms more and assert exactly one call with the last value. UI level: type quickly with Playwright, wait"],[32,"interview","JavaScript and TypeScript Interview Questions","for the search response with page.waitForResponse, count the requests with a route handler, and assert that the results match the final text. Detailed explanation Also cover clearing the box, typing slowly so that two searches happen, an empty result, a server error, and responses arriving out of order, where an older slow response must not overwrite newer results. How did you do? No questions match these filters."]]};
